{"repo":{"slug":"dacode","name":"dacode","path":"/repos/dacode","registered_at":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},"counters":{"epic":9,"task":218,"decision":21,"doc":42},"epics":[{"id":"dacode:E1","repo":"dacode","title":"scaffolding","description":"the dacode board's §5 milestone M0","status":"closed","references":[]},{"id":"dacode:E2","repo":"dacode","title":"engine + TUI (local)","description":"the dacode board's §5 milestone M1","status":"deferred","references":[]},{"id":"dacode:E3","repo":"dacode","title":"agents","description":"the dacode board's §5 milestone M2","status":"deferred","references":[]},{"id":"dacode:E4","repo":"dacode","title":"server + web UI","description":"the dacode board's §5 milestone M3","status":"open","references":[]},{"id":"dacode:E5","repo":"dacode","title":"extensions","description":"the dacode board's §5 milestone M4","status":"open","references":[]},{"id":"dacode:E6","repo":"dacode","title":"polish + scale + debug","description":"the dacode board's §5 milestone M5","status":"open","references":[]},{"id":"dacode:E7","repo":"dacode","title":"native nodes","description":"the dacode board's §5 milestone M6","status":"open","references":[]},{"id":"dacode:E8","repo":"dacode","title":"Follow-ups","description":"the dacode board's §6 open follow-ups, not tied to a milestone","status":"open","references":[]},{"id":"dacode:E9","repo":"dacode","title":"Spec deltas","description":"the dacode board's A-register design deltas","status":"open","references":[]}],"tasks":[{"id":"dacode:U1","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Size-table re-measure","body":"**U1 · Size-table re-measure.** `docs/SIZE.md` + the README extract mix\n      `rustc 1.95.0` figures with three rows refreshed on 1.98; neither\n      reproduces on the installed `1.98.1`. Needs `tools/size.sh` at `z`, `s` and\n      `3` with the toolchain recorded the way `docs/RESULTS.md:12` does. Check\n      first whether the 8-bit AVR / 16-bit MSP430 toolchains are even installed.\n      Also re-check `docs/SIZE.md`'s `flat`-at-`s`/`3` cells (2 446 → 1 302 and\n      3 550 → 1 304 in the WIP) — the one edit in `5d4a253` that moved a number\n      for code the WIP did not touch.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U2","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Positional-capture optimization — the biggest remaining win","body":"**U2 · Positional-capture optimization — the biggest remaining win.** Three\n      redundant passes, not one: `direct.rs:499-516` validates via `IgnoredAny`,\n      then a checked `from_utf8` over the whole span (`:513`), then\n      `RawJsonVisitor` has **no `visit_borrowed_str`**, so `visit_str` round-trips\n      through `String` into `crate::validate` **again**. At 853 KB, **98% of\n      capture's overhead over the ignore floor is that second validation**. A\n      trusted already-validated constructor takes capture from 160 → ~410 MiB/s\n      (2.6×). Fix all three: add `visit_borrowed_str`, drop the `:513`\n      `from_utf8`, add the debug-asserted constructor. **Audit number/string\n      strictness first** (`direct`'s `MAX_DEPTH=128` is already stricter than\n      `strict`'s `STACK_MAX=256`). Keep the path `unsafe`-free — it is today;\n      `direct.rs:220`'s neighbouring `from_utf8_unchecked` is *not* the model.\n      **⚠️ Do not extend the trusted constructor to the *pool* path.** There the\n      validate is **not** redundant: a faithful-parser `Doc` can `to_json` to\n      invalid JSON. Assuming otherwise was the med-severity bug found and fixed\n      during `90def19`'s review — trusting pool-path bytes re-opens it. Note also\n      that `validate` re-checks UTF-8 for non-ASCII (`strict.rs:519`), which is\n      the fourth pass in the chain.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U3","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`crate::validate` allocates per call","body":"**U3 · `crate::validate` allocates per call** (`api.rs:276` builds a fresh\n      `StrictParser`) — 20 allocations for one 873 KB validate, contradicting\n      `api.rs:162-163`'s \"allocates nothing but the output\". A reused pre-sized\n      parser is 0 allocations and ~11% faster.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U4","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Field-skip path is 26% slower than `serde_json`'s","body":"**U4 · Field-skip path is 26% slower than `serde_json`'s** (417 vs 530 MiB/s at\n      853 KB). `direct.rs:443-447` routes `{`/`[` through `deserialize_any` +\n      serde's `MapAccess` instead of a dedicated skip. Capture inherits this as\n      its floor, so fixing it lifts the `raw_value` story too.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U5","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Borrowed `&RawJson` form?","body":"**U5 · Borrowed `&RawJson` form?** Owned-only (`Box<str>`) is right for\n      store-and-re-emit events. Add a borrowed zero-copy form only if a hot\n      transient splice path appears.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U6","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`stream` deserializer capture","body":"**U6 · `stream` deserializer capture** — currently a clean error. Wire it\n      byte-exact only if dacode uses `stream::from_slice_with` for events.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U7","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Fuzz the `RawJson` capture path","body":"**U7 · Fuzz the `RawJson` capture path** before a release bumps the version\n      dacode pins.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U8","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"dacode will depend on `dacode-json` with `features = [\"raw_value\"]`","body":"**U8 · dacode will depend on `dacode-json` with `features = [\"raw_value\"]`** once the\n      protocol crate exists (M1). Until then `RawJson` has no in-tree consumer.\n      ✅ **DISCHARGED 2026-09-20 by M1.2's commit**: `dacode-json` is the first of the\n      protocol crate's four dependencies, `raw_value` on, entering through a path\n      entry in the root `[workspace.dependencies]` (doc 01 §1.9's corrected\n      mechanism — `[patch]` cannot rewrite a never-published crate), and `RawJson`\n      is re-exported flat from `dacode_protocol` so no client needs its own\n      dacode-json edge for a public field type (tracker f130). The golden fixtures\n      pin its byte-exact splice — `snapshot.json` deliberately carries a\n      reversed-key raw value so a canonicalizing encoder *fails* the suite.\n\n#### Measured baseline — what may be quoted, and what may not\n\n[B1](appendices/B-decisions.md) and delta\n[A16](appendices/A-design-deltas.md) both justify the hybrid strategy with these\nnumbers, so they are recorded here rather than left in a chat log. Source:\n`benches/rawjson.rs` at `90b21f9`.\n\nHost `darwin/arm64`, `rustc 1.98.1`, `[profile.bench] opt-level=3 lto=\"fat\"\ncodegen-units=1`, criterion 0.5.1, `sample_size(30)`, corpus\n`corpus::geo_int(_, 0xA7C5)`.\n\n| 853 KB payload | splice | re-encode (tree) | ignore floor | tree |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| | **13.9 µs / 57–59 GiB/s** | 1.57–1.90 ms / 462–532 MiB/s | 2.00 ms / 417 MiB/s | 7.85 ms / 106 MiB/s |\n\n- Splice is **≈110× faster than re-encoding** (112–118× across four runs) against\n  `serde_json`'s own serializer over the same tree — the strongest opponent\n  measured, hence the conservative quote. **≈130×** against `dacode-json`'s\n  serializer over that tree. `dacode-json` driving a foreign enum is **8–15%** slower\n  than `serde_json` driving its own.\n- The win is **per-byte, not per-value**: marginal ratio 141× (2.3→35 KB) then\n  129× (35→853 KB).\n- **Capture is 1.2–1.5× cheaper than building a tree** (1.24/1.24/1.50×) and stays\n  byte-exact where the tree is not; **2 inherent allocations vs the tree's\n  114 080**.\n- Native-DOM fairness: per-turn re-emit from a retained pool doc (`Value::to_json`)\n  **5.26 ms** vs `serde_json::to_vec` of a retained tree **1.66 ms** vs the splice\n  **13.9 µs** = **378×**. One-time acquisition: `parse + to_json` **8.10 ms**,\n  `parse + RawJson::from_doc` **11.29 ms**.\n\n**Methodology constraint — both arms of any ratio must come from one build.** A\nsingle edit to the bench file moved an in-process measurement 14.43 → 17.57 µs.\n`tools/isolate.sh` and a shared criterion process agree to ~0.3% *within* a build.\n\n**Do NOT quote these — each was retracted during review:**\n\n- \"25% slower in the shared criterion process\"\n- \"10–15%\" (the correct figure is 8–15%)\n- \"capture costs 2.6× a tree\" — the 2.6× is capture vs the *ignore floor*, and\n  98% of that gap is the redundant second validate, not the copy\n- \"capture inherently allocates 22 objects\" — 2 are inherent, 20 belong to the\n  redundant pass\n- group-(a) MiB/s compared against group-(b) MiB/s — different denominators\n  (output-byte vs input-byte)\n- anything about `serde_json`'s own `RawValue` — its `raw_value` feature is off\n  and was never measured\n- the *total* ratio climbing ~50× → ~130× as evidence of scaling — that is the\n  splice arm's ~53 ns loop floor falling away (61% of its 2 KB time, 0.4% at\n  853 KB)\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **DISCHARGED 2026-09-20 by M1.2's commit**: `dacode-json` is the first of the","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U9","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The field-skip gap deserves a row in dacode-json's `docs/RESULTS.md`","body":"**U9 · The field-skip gap deserves a row in dacode-json's `docs/RESULTS.md`** if it\n      is real and stays — `benches/rawjson.rs` group `rawjson_capture_de` is\n      currently the only place it is visible.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U10","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Malformed tool-arg JSON repair is a separate axis from B5","body":"**U10 · Malformed tool-arg JSON repair is a separate axis from B5.** forge\n      carries a distinct 3 694-LoC `forge_json_repair` crate (parser + schema\n      coercion) for models that emit almost-valid JSON in `args`. dacode will\n      likely need some of it regardless of how B5 resolves — ✅ **and B5 resolved 2026-09-17\n      with this recorded as a carve-out rather than part of it**, so this item is now the\n      *only* owner of the JSON-repair axis. Belongs in\n      [14-tools](part3-capabilities/14-tools.md), not\n      [13-providers](part3-capabilities/13-providers.md).\n      ⚠️ **Still open after A5 applied 2026-09-18, and deliberately so — A5's own entry\n      excludes it and §6 now says so in the spec rather than only in the appendix.** A5\n      landed the dialect machinery (escaping, demotion, one XML grammar, the allowlist, the\n      never-scrub-inside-code rule), and *almost-valid JSON in `args`* is a different\n      failure on a different axis: it is not about how a tool call is transported but about\n      repairing a payload that arrived. Folding it in would have put a 3 694-LoC parser\n      concern inside the provider layer, which is exactly the misplacement this item\n      exists to prevent. **It stays doc-14's**, and doc 14 is still a migrated summary, so\n      the axis has no plan-side home until M1 deepens it.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U11","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Snapshot pruning policy","body":"**U11 · Snapshot pruning policy.** No reference harness prunes checkpoint\n      storage — growth is unbounded in all four. §4.6 needs a retention rule\n      (by age, by count, or drop-on-session-close). Carried into B8 — ✅ **signed 2026-09-17,\n      and its recommendation specs a retention policy** as one of the two problems no\n      harness solved, so what remains is the §4.6 edit when doc 12 is deepened at M2.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U12","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`bash:readonly` is a misleading name for a deny-list","body":"**U12 · `bash:readonly` is a misleading name for a deny-list.** The `reviewer`\n      built-in agent is granted it under `mode = \"plan\"` and described as\n      \"Read-only code review; never edits\" — not enforceable by patterns. Either\n      document it as advisory (opencode does) or enforce read-onlyness where it\n      is enforceable (`write`/`edit` path denial) and leave `bash` advisory.\n      Carried into B7 — ✅ **signed 2026-09-17 taking the second option**: enforce where it\n      is enforceable, document `bash` as advisory, and do not merely re-word the claim.\n      **U98** is how it could later be enforced for real rather than documented.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U13","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Verify a maintained Rust ACP crate exists","body":"**U13 · Verify a maintained Rust ACP crate exists** before M5 planning. The B9\n      estimate (2 500–3 500 LoC) assumes hand-written serde types; a usable crate\n      cuts ~1 k LoC. **B9 signed 2026-09-17 and kept this as its one open input**, so it is\n      owed before M5 planning and not a day earlier.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U14","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Verify `libzstd` cross-compile cost","body":"**U14 · Verify `libzstd` cross-compile cost** before M1. The `zstd` crate links\n      C, which touches the\n      [31-ci-milestones](part6-quality/31-ci-milestones.md) matrix; `ruzstd` is\n      decode-only so it cannot replace it for writes. If the matrix cost is\n      ~~painful, `lz4_flex` (pure Rust) is the fallback and B12 reopens.~~ ✅ **B12 resolved\n      2026-09-17 — `zstd` accepted as the dependency policy's *first class-1 exception* —\n      which changes this item's meaning instead of closing it: it is now the *condition on\n      the exception*, not a reason to reopen it.** Verify the matrix cost before the\n      dependency lands. If it is painful the fallback is `flate2` + `rust_backend`, and\n      because A15's discriminator stays codec-agnostic that is a **config change, not a §14\n      wire break** — so \"reopens\" was always too strong, and is now wrong twice over.\n      ✅ **A15 applied 2026-09-18, and this item is deliberately NOT discharged by it.**\n      It is the *condition on* B12's exception rather than a consequence of the\n      transcription, so it stays open until the matrix cost is actually measured. The\n      mechanism it relies on did get sharper: the codec is named in **`LogHeader`** rather\n      than only in config, so a fallback affects new logs and every existing log still\n      decodes from its own header — which makes \"a config change, not a wire break\" true\n      in a stronger sense than when this was written, since a reader now learns the codec\n      from the file rather than from the config in force when it happens to be read.\n      ⚠️ **Measured 2026-09-19: this cannot be done yet, though it renders as if it can.**\n      `attention.py` shows it P0 / `review` / \"gates M1\", which reads as agent-actionable\n      now. Three prerequisites are absent, each checked rather than assumed: `zstd` is\n      nowhere in the tree (zero `Cargo.lock` entries, zero manifest mentions — M0 ships no\n      external dependencies at all); the only installed Rust target is the host\n      `aarch64-apple-darwin`, with no cross linker installed for anything else; and most\n      decisively [doc 31](part6-quality/31-ci-milestones.md) states the cross-compile\n      matrix **does not exist**. This item asks for the *matrix* cost, which is a CI\n      property — a local build cannot stand in for it. Doc 31 §31.1 defines the matrix as\n      Tier-1 **macOS arm64/x64 and Linux arm64/x64**, with Tier-2 **Windows** best-effort;\n      the host target covers only the first of those four, so even a hand-run local check\n      would measure one cell and generalise from it. **So this stays open and un-actionable\n      until (a) `zstd` lands as a dependency and (b) the matrix exists to measure\n      against.** Do not attempt it earlier: a host-only `cargo build` yields a number that\n      looks like an answer and is not, which is worse than leaving the item open. Note the\n      vocabulary gap this exposes — the action types are `review`/`ack`/`vote`/`session`,\n      with no \"blocked on infrastructure\", so an item that is correctly open but cannot yet\n      be started renders identically to one that can. Same class as **U93**, which renders\n      `review` but needs a user decision. **Attached to M1.4** (§5's sub-step table,\n      2026-09-19), which is where prerequisite (a) is satisfied and `zstd` enters the\n      tree; prerequisite (b) is doc 31's and has no milestone at all, so this item stays\n      open *after* M1.4 rather than closing with it.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U15","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Pick the templating approach","body":"**U15 · Pick the templating approach.** §5.4 workflow `when` conditions and §5.6\n      prompt partials both need one; forge uses handlebars compiled at build time\n      via `include_dir!`. Genuinely open — A12 adds it to §16.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U16","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`06-session-organization` §6.2 contradicts §6.3 — DONE 2026-09-18, via A14","body":"**U16 · `06-session-organization` §6.2 contradicts §6.3 — DONE 2026-09-18, via\n      A14.** (\"not in the agent event log\" vs \"organization changes are events\").\n      B13's Fix-1 resolves it; ~~the doc edit is still owed~~ **the doc edit landed**.\n      ✅ **B13 signed 2026-09-17, so Fix-1 is decided**: durable\n      log events that every client folds, **never part of `Context`**, with the store's\n      mapping tables a rebuildable projection and the log authoritative on conflict.\n      ~~What remains is the doc 06 edit at M2.~~ **Doc 06 §6.2 now carries Fix-1\n      verbatim, and arrived *earlier* than M2** — it is an expanded doc, so correcting\n      a known-false sentence in it is not a deepening and hard rule 1 does not apply.\n      **The fix also needed a carve-out Fix-1's own wording did not cover:** membership\n      is a projection, but a *name* is not — it appears in no session log, so no scan can\n      rebuild it. §12's `org.json` is authoritative for names and is the one file in that\n      layout which is not a projection; the conflict rule is stated where both are\n      described (names → the registry, membership → the log).","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **B13 signed 2026-09-17, so Fix-1 is decided**: durable","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U17","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Project auto-derivation breaks in worktree sandboxes — DONE 2026-09-18, via A14","body":"**U17 · Project auto-derivation breaks in worktree sandboxes — DONE 2026-09-18,\n      via A14.** `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` returns the worktree path, so every\n      sandboxed session splits into its own project. Must map back through\n      `--git-common-dir`. B13's Fix-2; ~~the doc edit and a test are owed~~ **the rule is\n      normative in §4.7 and the test is in §14**; doc 06 §6.2 carries the plan-side\n      account. ✅ **B13 signed 2026-09-17, so Fix-2 is decided** ~~and this is purely an\n      M2 implementation item now~~ → **it became a spec item instead, deliberately:**\n      B13 assigned it to doc 06, but the spec may not cite a plan doc (U91) and a rule\n      §14 must test has to be normative somewhere.\n      It gained a neighbour the same day: **B8 routes feature worktrees through §5.4\n      workflow instructions**, so more sessions than before will run inside a worktree and\n      hit this derivation.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      account. ✅ **B13 signed 2026-09-17, so Fix-2 is decided** ~~and this is purely an","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U18","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Rename sweep — DONE","body":"**U18 · Rename sweep — DONE.** The `dacodec` → `dacode-json` rename landed\n      upstream (`e739d6c`) and in `candroid-fw` (`ac3615f`), and this repo was\n      swept with it: 136 mechanical substitutions across 27 files, ordered so the\n      Rust module path takes the underscore (`dacode_json::`) and the package name\n      takes the hyphen (`dacode-json`) in manifests, the repo URL and prose.\n      [Appendix E](appendices/E-dacode-json-cheatsheet.md) and\n      [02](part1-foundations/02-json-strategy.md) were renamed — both were named\n      after the old crate — and `tools/agents-sync.py` regenerated the four\n      `.opencode` adapters from the corrected sources.\n      **Two things the sweep could not do**, both recorded in A11/A16:\n      - **Apply A16/A11 to §13.** That is a spec change gated on B1, not a rename.\n      - **Make `dacode_json::Value` exist.** `.agents/roles/_rust.md` still names\n        that type because §13 still does; it now carries an inline caveat pointing\n        at B1 so a coding agent cannot silently write code against it.\n      `.agents/implement.md`'s checklist line now reads \"JSON via dacode-json\",\n      which is correct on the name; its \"no serde in protocol/core\" half is still\n      §13-as-written and is B1's to re-read.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U19","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Deepen [22-tui](part4-hosts-clients/22-tui.md)","body":"**U19 · Deepen [22-tui](part4-hosts-clients/22-tui.md)** (36-line summary, M1)\n      with the specifics this pass established. ✅ **DONE 2026-10-04 at M1.1g** — doc 22 is\n      deepened (520 lines, R-a–R-h) and §22.16's table discharges the checklist item by\n      item: the scrollbox (§22.9, R-f), the widget-tree hit test (§22.7, R-d), event\n      coalescing (§22.9), the framework rationale with the rejected alternatives\n      (§22.13), and the supporting-crate checks (`pulldown-cmark` deferred with M1.9 as\n      owner; `similar` not needed — the viewer renders a preview, it does not compute a\n      diff; `syntect` unneeded at this row). The two bullets that became normative in §10\n      at A9 are carried as R-d/R-e with crossterm's *measured* command set beside them.\n      **Scheduled: M1.1** (§5's sub-step\n      table, 2026-09-19) — doc 22 is one of the eight M1 docs no 🧠 session owns, so\n      nothing but M1 starting holds it. ▶️ **RELEASED 2026-09-19: M1 started.** It sits in\n      M1.1's just-in-time slot\n      immediately ahead of **M1.11**, and it carries more weight than the row it is\n      listed in, because **doc 23 now waits on U93** (see that item: M1.11's row keeps\n      doc 23's marker until the exit-code question is settled) *(true when written; U93\n      ruled 2026-10-04 and doc 23's marker flipped with the ruling)*, so doc 22 is the only\n      client-side doc the hosts pass can be written against. A9's mouse and paste rules are\n      normative in §10, which makes the spec this item's source rather than the survey\n      below:\n      - **opencode is not a framework reference** — its TUI is TypeScript\n        (SolidJS via `@opentui/core`). What ports is *architecture*: fold into a\n        store, **events coalesced in ≤16 ms batches → one render pass per burst**\n        (`context/sdk.tsx:53-80`), a `scrollbox` with `stickyStart=\"bottom\"`\n        (`routes/session/index.tsx:1168-1185`), streaming markdown as its own\n        renderable (`:1684-1693`), a native diff renderable with theme-coloured\n        line-number cells (`:2454-2478`), per-tool bespoke widgets with a\n        `GenericTool` fallback (`:1702-1780`), and theme auto-detect **before\n        first paint** (`app.tsx:242`).\n      - **Mouse handling comes from oh-my-pi**, not opencode: SGR\n        `?1000h ?1003h ?1006h` enabled only while a fullscreen overlay holds the\n        alt screen (`tui.ts:88,:2775`), `parseSgrMouse` (`mouse.ts:39`), a\n        `MouseRoutable` interface (`:107`), 0-based row/col hit-testing. crossterm\n        already parses SGR, so what dacode must build is the **widget-tree hit\n        test** over ratatui layout rects.\n      - **Ratatui has no scrollbox** — build one: viewport offset plus a\n        \"stick to bottom unless the user scrolled up\" flag, and virtualize (render\n        only visible rows) for long transcripts.\n      - **Framework rationale**: `ratatui` + `crossterm` are pure Rust, no\n        ncurses, and satisfy [Appendix F](appendices/F-c-dependencies.md) as-is.\n        Record *why*, so the choice is not relitigated. Rejected: `notcurses`\n        and `ncurses` (C), `termion` (Unix-only), `cursive` (can pull an ncurses\n        backend).\n      - Supporting crates to check against Appendix F: `pulldown-cmark` (pure\n        Rust) for streaming markdown, `similar` (pure Rust) for diffs, `syntect`\n        **only** with `default-features = false, features = [\"regex-fancy\"]`.\n      ✅ **Two of these bullets are now normative in the spec rather than survey\n      notes, because A9 applied 2026-09-18.** The mouse rule (SGR `?1000h ?1003h\n      ?1006h`, enabled *only* while a fullscreen overlay holds the alt screen,\n      0-based row/col hit-testing) and **collapse-on-paste** (10 lines / 1000 chars,\n      an indivisible chip backed by a paste map, expanded on submit, a host hook to\n      intercept, bracketed-paste buffering under a size cap) are both in §10, with the\n      *reason* each is conditional — a terminal that always captures the mouse takes\n      scroll-back and selection away from the user's own terminal, and a big bracketed\n      paste can be misread as a burst of keystrokes. §10 also records that both are\n      **TUI rules and not shared client behaviour**, so §9.3 correctly carries neither.\n      **What this item still owes is unchanged and is the larger part**: the scrollbox\n      (ratatui has none), the widget-tree hit test (crossterm parses SGR but nothing\n      maps it onto ratatui layout rects), event coalescing, and the framework rationale.\n      **When doc 22 is deepened its source is now §10, not this survey** — which is the\n      point of landing the specifics normatively first. Doc 22 stays a summary until\n      M1.1 (hard rule 1); it is now three deltas out of date rather than one.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      with the specifics this pass established. ✅ **DONE 2026-10-04 at M1.1g** — doc 22 is","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U20","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Slash-command completion, including skills","body":"**U20 · Slash-command completion, including skills.** opencode does **not**\n      complete skills; forge does but with a `:` prefix. dacode uses **`/`** as\n      the common prefix. Required: a completion source spanning built-in\n      commands, agent names, skills and file paths (`@path`); and a **collision\n      rule** so `/skill_name` can never shadow a built-in command — define the\n      namespace precedence and fail config load on a collision rather than\n      resolving it silently. Check against the §9.2/§10 command list (`/agent`,\n      `/model`, `/mode`, `/compact`, `/fork`, `/cancel`, `/prompt`, `/rename`,\n      `/serve`, `/serve`). Belongs in\n      [22-tui](part4-hosts-clients/22-tui.md) and\n      [17-config-agents-skills](part3-capabilities/17-config-agents-skills.md).","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U21","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`tools/tasks.py` is mandatory","body":"**U21 · `tools/tasks.py` is mandatory** — was only stated for the orchestrator.\n      Now in `.agents/roles/implementer.md` (a \"Tracking (mandatory)\" section)\n      and `.agents/roles/reviewer.md` (\"Tracking hygiene (part of the verdict)\"),\n      and `.agents/implement.md` names the tool instead of saying \"the task\n      tracker\". Adapters regenerated.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U22","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Explicit-imports rule added","body":"**U22 · Explicit-imports rule added** — `.agents/roles/_rust.md` now denies\n      `clippy::wildcard_imports`, with `use super::*` in `#[cfg(test)]` as the\n      only exception. Verified the other three guidelines were already present:\n      1000-line file cap (`_rust.md:149`), avoid-nesting/early-return (`:13`),\n      and the `unwrap`/`expect` deny-lint set (`:24-38`) — the latter extended\n      with the \"external library leaves no fallible path\" case and what counts as\n      an acceptable reason.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U23","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Still deferred: `TODO.md` as an enforced exit gate across the agent config","body":"**U23 · Still deferred: `TODO.md` as an enforced exit gate across the agent\n      config.** The reviewer and implementer roles now mention it, but the full\n      contract — every delivery ends with the board updated, debt/bugs become new\n      entries, task splits and mid-task extra steps are reflected, and the\n      **orchestrator checks it as a gate before sign-off** — needs\n      `.agents/config/agent-rules.yaml`, `.agents/orchestrator.md` and\n      `.agents/roles/orchestrator.md` updated too. Do it once this file's\n      conventions have survived a real implementation cycle.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U24","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"A16 applied — design §13 rewritten","body":"**U24 · A16 applied — design §13 rewritten** as §13.1–§13.5 (tiers · per-surface\n      table · the serde rule re-read as *third-party only* · byte-exactness as a\n      correctness property · `Codec` + conventions). The `_rust.md` caveat naming\n      the non-existent `dacode_json::Value` is **deleted**, and the unqualified\n      \"serde types never leak\" rule was corrected to *third-party* at the other\n      sites carrying it: `AGENTS.md` ×2, the `dacode` skill ×2,\n      `.agents/implement.md` ×3, `.agents/reviewer.md`,\n      `.agents/roles/implementer.md`, `agent-rules.yaml` ×4. Adapters\n      regenerated; `agents-sync.py --check` clean.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U25","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"A15 must not be applied before B12 resolves — its codec discriminator enumerates `zstd` by name —","body":"**U25 · ⚠️ A15 must not be applied before B12 resolves — its codec discriminator\n      enumerates `zstd` by name — ✅ ANSWERED 2026-09-17, option 2 taken; open now only as\n      a transcription obligation.** A15 was signed off as **mechanism only**: the\n      discriminator, the allowlist, the ~1 KiB floor, the level, the `compress` knob and the\n      searchability trade are approved, while the codec name was left to B12 — ✅ **which resolved the same day to `zstd`,\n      accepted as the policy's first class-1 exception, so the hazard this item names is\n      gone and the item closes**. ~~The instruction survives it, deliberately: the variant\n      list should still be restated codec-agnostically, exactly as proposed here, with the\n      actual codec named in config — because a wire value that *names* a codec makes the\n      **next** switch a §14 compatibility event even though this one is settled.~~\n      ✅ **DISCHARGED 2026-09-18 by A15's transcription — and the instruction's own\n      answer had a hole worth keeping.** It said \"codec-agnostically, with the actual\n      codec named **in config**\"; config alone would orphan every record already written\n      the moment the codec changed, because the log is append-only and existing records\n      are never re-encoded. What landed is `plain | compressed` with the codec in\n      **`LogHeader`** — a file-level property in the file's header, which is where A1 had\n      already put `format_version`. Its variant list also kept `flat | flat+compressed`,\n      and **`flat` is not a field encoding at all** but a record *representation*, so A15\n      removed it from the per-field enum and left it reserved as §13.5's future binary\n      `Codec`. The diagnosis this item made was right and survives: a wire value naming a\n      codec makes the *next* switch a §14 compatibility event. The prescription was\n      incomplete, and the reason is general — **a per-field tag cannot carry a per-file\n      property safely.** A15's banner and Appendix A's footer\n      both carried that instruction, and **B12 gained an instrument** the same day (B18's\n      T1 benches measure codec throughput and ratio over a real session log), so the\n      measurement that was stalled is now merely not-yet-run — and it now tunes the\n      *level*, not the codec. Original entry follows. A15 proposes `plain | zstd | flat | flat+zstd`\n      as a **per-field serialized discriminator**, so the codec name lands on the\n      wire. If B12 settles on `flate2` / `lz4_flex` / `brotli` instead, those enum\n      values change — a compatibility event under §14, not a comment fix. Either\n      hold A15 until B12's measurement is done, or restate the discriminator\n      codec-agnostically (`plain | compressed | flat | flat+compressed`) with the\n      actual codec named in config. **This also disturbs Appendix A's suggested\n      application order**, which currently groups A15 with A1/A14 as\n      \"protocol/storage shapes\" ahead of the engine deltas.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      enumerates `zstd` by name — ✅ ANSWERED 2026-09-17, option 2 taken; open now only as","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U26","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`zstd` is still assumed in three more places now B12 is reopened —","body":"**U26 · `zstd` is still assumed in three more places now B12 is reopened — ✅ CLOSED\n      2026-09-17 without an edit, because B12 resolved *to* `zstd`.** The three places\n      (doc 05 §5.3's heading and `[DECISION B12]` line, the README's \"selective zstd\n      compression\") are **correct again** rather than pending, which is the one satisfying\n      way to close a sweep item. Only the `[DECISION B12]` marker itself should be updated\n      when doc 05 is next touched, to record that the decision is taken and on what\n      condition (U14). Original entry follows.\n      [05](part2-engine/05-event-log-storage.md) §5.3 — its *heading* is\n      \"Selective compression (zstd)\", plus `:39,:51,:61,:63` and the\n      `[DECISION B12]` line — and [README](README.md):48 (\"selective zstd\n      compression\"). Doc 02 §2.4's cell was corrected during the B1 pass; these\n      were left alone because B12 is open and there is no answer to write yet.\n      Sweep all of them when B12 resolves. Doc 05 is already `expanded`, so this\n      is *not* blocked by the just-in-time rule.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"**U26 · `zstd` is still assumed in three more places now B12 is reopened — ✅ CLOSED","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U27","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The spec's bare `Json` alias is now defined but not yet renamed —","body":"**U27 · The spec's bare `Json` alias is now defined but not yet renamed —\n      ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-18 on both halves, by A1 and then A2.** A1's\n      transcription renamed §3.2's `ToolCall { args: Json }` → `args: RawJson`, so\n      one of the two sites this item named is gone. ~~**What remains is A2's**: §4.3's\n      `perm_scope(&self, args: &Json)` / `call(..., args: Json, ...)` still spell the~~\n      ✅ **A2 renamed both the same day**, and `concurrency(&self, args: &RawJson)`\n      arrived already spelling the real type. **With no bare `Json` left anywhere in\n      the spec, §13.2's alias note was dropped rather than narrowed a third time** —\n      verified by `rg '\\bJson\\b' docs/dacode-design.md` returning nothing outside\n      `RawJson` and `dacode-json`. What replaced the note is the rule it was covering\n      for: opaque sub-JSON is always spelled `RawJson`, and spelling it out at each\n      site is the point, since a type the spec never defines is a defect even when a\n      prose note covers for it. Original entry follows. §4.3's\n      `perm_scope(&self, args: &Json)` / `call(..., args: Json, ...)` still spell the\n      alias, and §13.2's note is therefore **narrowed rather than dropped** — it now\n      cites only §4.3's two signatures and says explicitly that §3.2 names `RawJson`\n      directly. Dropping the note before A2 lands would leave two signatures spelling\n      a type the spec never defines, which is the original defect. §4.3's `ToolSpec`\n      comment was already corrected to \"(canonical RawJson, §13.2)\". Original entry\n      follows. §13.2\n      states that where the spec writes `Json` for opaque sub-JSON it means\n      `RawJson`, which removes the self-contradiction without touching unsigned\n      deltas. But §3.2's `ToolCall { args: Json }` and §4.3's\n      `perm_scope(&self, args: &Json)` / `call(..., args: Json, ...)` still spell\n      the alias. **A1 and A2 own those signatures** — when they land, name\n      `RawJson` explicitly and drop the alias note from §13.2.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-18 on both halves, by A1 and then A2.** A1's","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U28","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The design doc has no versioning convention — SETTLED 2026-09-12","body":"**U28 · The design doc has no versioning convention — SETTLED 2026-09-12**,\n      while applying A17, which is what made it load-bearing. **The version moves\n      at milestone boundaries only; applying a delta moves the `Date` field.**\n      Per-delta bumps (v0.2, v0.3, …) would make the number encode \"how many\n      deltas have landed\" — which Appendix A's applied-deltas table already\n      records, and better — and a delta edits one section rather than changing\n      whether the doc is coherent enough to be cited as a stable reference.\n      Recorded as a one-line convention directly under the spec's `Status` field,\n      so the next session reads it instead of re-litigating it. `v0.1` therefore\n      stands until M0 completes; A16 and A17 moved the date only.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U29","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"A17 applied — the dependency policy is normative at design §2.3","body":"**U29 · A17 applied — the dependency policy is normative at design §2.3.** §2 was\n      restructured into §2.1 crate tree · §2.2 internal dependency rules ·\n      §2.3 external dependency policy, matching the `### N.M` convention §13\n      adopted when A16 landed. Codec-agnostic: only TLS is named as the one\n      unavoidable class-1 dependency, so nothing B12 or A15 has yet decided is\n      enshrined in the spec. Appendix F is now the *register §2.3 points at*\n      rather than a proposal, and carries a banner saying the spec wins on any\n      disagreement about what the policy is. A13/B11 content (in-tree vendoring,\n      `dacode-store`, `dacode-introspect`) was deliberately kept out — both are\n      unsigned and would have ridden along on A17's approval.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U30","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The spec's zero-markdown-link convention was undocumented, and deltas keep proposing text that violates it — DONE, both halves","body":"**U30 · The spec's zero-markdown-link convention was undocumented, and deltas\n      keep proposing text that violates it — DONE, both halves.** The convention is\n      written down (Appendix A's banner, plus `check-links.py`'s `NO_LINK_FILES`\n      comment) and now machine-enforced rather than merely documented; see U50 for\n      the enforcement and its negative tests. Original entry follows. `docs/dacode-design.md` contains\n      **no** links at all — the plan cites the spec, never the reverse — but\n      nothing wrote that down, and Appendix A's proposed edits are *plan* text,\n      so       several carry links to appendices and feature docs (A17's own bullet\n      said \"a row in `[Appendix F](…)`\"). Caught it by grepping before transcribing;\n      the next applier will not be so lucky. Now recorded as a warning banner in\n      Appendix A's header with the `grep -c '](' docs/dacode-design.md` check\n      (must return 0). **Worth promoting to `tools/check-links.py`** as a hard\n      failure if the spec ever gains a link, since the convention is exactly the\n      kind a machine should hold.\n      **This entry then reproduced the bug it describes, which generalises f19:**\n      its first draft quoted the offending shape bare, and `check-links` resolved\n      it as a real link — 1 broken. Writing *about* a machine-readable form makes\n      you an instance of it, whether the form is the summary marker (f19, which\n      moved the depth count 27 → 28) or a link target. Neutralise it: backticks\n      for a link shape, and never the full phrase for the marker. Both\n      `check-links.py` and the depth grep already exclude inline code, so\n      backticking is the supported escape.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U31","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`tools/tasks.py check` cannot express a standing goal, so a long-lived one keeps the gate permanently red — FIXED in the tool","body":"**U31 · `tools/tasks.py check` cannot express a standing goal, so a long-lived\n      one keeps the gate permanently red — FIXED in the tool.** `check` exited\n      non-zero for any goal not `done`/`wontfix`, while *debt* was printed but\n      explicitly non-blocking. The design phase has a legitimately standing goal —\n      the decision pass over the remaining B-decisions and A-deltas — open for\n      weeks. A gate that is always red gets ignored, which is the failure mode the\n      mandatory-checker rule exists to prevent. The workaround (recording in that\n      goal's `verify` field that `OPEN g14` is the expected steady state) is now\n      retired: **`--status standing` exists**, `check` prints it as `STAND` and\n      exits 0, and g14 is set to it.\n      **Four properties it was written to keep, because a status that only\n      suppresses a gate is worse than no status.** (1) A standing goal is still\n      *printed* — dropping it from the output is how a long-running programme stops\n      being looked at. (2) It still needs a `verify`, holding the per-delivery\n      condition rather than a completion test; `TBD` blocks. (3) **Only goals may\n      be standing** — applied to a task it is just a way to dodge the gate, so both\n      `set` and `check` refuse it (`check` is the backstop because the tracker is a\n      file in the worktree and can be hand-edited). (4) **It needs a `note` saying\n      why it cannot close**, the same rule `blocked` already had. Review found this\n      one: as first written, marking a goal standing was *cheaper* than marking it\n      done — `done` demands evidence and a decided verify, standing demanded\n      nothing and still turned the gate green. An exemption from a gate that costs\n      less than satisfying the gate is not an exemption, it is a bypass.\n      Six cases negative-tested: refusal on a task and on a finding, a hand-edited\n      standing task, a standing goal with `TBD` verify, and a standing goal with no\n      note both via `set` and hand-edited.\n      **⚠️ This does NOT resolve §16.6, and must not be read as prototyping an\n      answer to it.** §16.6 is the same gap in the *product* — design §4.8's\n      completion gate reddens while a `Goal` is unclosed, with only `Blocked` and\n      `WontFix` exempt — and inventing a `TaskStatus::Standing` there would exceed\n      B15's signed-off text. What the tool fix contributes is **evidence**: the\n      four properties above are what a working standing status turned out to need,\n      which is a better input to §16.6 than the abstract argument was. Recorded\n      against f31 in the tracker.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U32","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§3.7's \"No gap, no duplicate\" was asserted, not earned — FIXED by A18","body":"**U32 · §3.7's \"No gap, no duplicate\" was asserted, not earned — FIXED by A18.**\n      that would earn it lives only in a summary-depth plan doc.** §4.1 picks \"a\n      broadcast channel per session\" and stops there; `tokio::sync::broadcast`\n      **drops the oldest messages when a receiver lags** (`RecvError::Lagged`),\n      so the primitive the spec already chose is lossy under a slow consumer and\n      §3.7's flat claim is false as written.\n      [07-session-registry](part2-engine/07-session-registry.md):16 does call for\n      \"a bounded queue and explicit drop/backpressure policy … a slow client must\n      not stall the engine\", but that is not where an implementer looks for a\n      correctness invariant. **A18's replacement §3.7 text is now in the spec**,\n      along with the two limits that turned out to be one invariant: the\n      in-memory replay window (5 000) must exceed the subscriber queue depth\n      (256), or a client lagging far enough to be dropped also falls outside\n      cheap replay and pays a disk read to recover. Note this was *not* an\n      argument for unbounded queues — those let one stalled browser tab apply\n      backpressure to the engine, trading recoverable staleness for an\n      unrecoverable hang. §4.1 gained the one-sentence bounded-channel rule and\n      §14 gained the two tests that can actually fail on it (fold-idempotence\n      over `seq`, and a lagged subscriber that must resync to the same folded\n      state) — the §14 half went slightly beyond A18's literal proposal, because\n      an untested invariant is how §3.7 acquired a false guarantee to begin with,\n      and it is recorded as a scoping decision in A18's banner.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U33","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Decide the HTTP version deliberately — new, owed by M3","body":"**U33 · Decide the HTTP version deliberately — new, owed by M3.** B4's resolution\n      surfaced it. Browsers cap concurrent connections per origin (~6 under\n      HTTP/1.1). That is usually discussed as an SSE problem, but it bites\n      whichever live transport is chosen: several dashboard tabs can exhaust the\n      budget against §8.2's HTTP endpoints, which carry the files, diffs and\n      blobs the \"large payloads are HTTP\" split depends on. Serving HTTP/2\n      removes the cap. Small decision, but it should be *made* rather than\n      inherited from whatever the HTTP crate defaults to — recorded in\n      [B4](appendices/B-decisions.md)'s banner and\n      [19](part4-hosts-clients/19-server-gateway.md).","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U34","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The default TUI is in-process, and doc 19's framing hides it","body":"**U34 · The default TUI is in-process, and doc 19's framing hides it.** §8.1's\n      four process models mean plain `dacode` touches `SessionRegistry` directly\n      (§4.1) and receives events over the per-session broadcast channel — no\n      socket, no serialization. Only `attach` and the web UI have a transport\n      question at all. Doc 19's now-resolved `[DECISION] WS vs SSE` block invited\n      the wrong mental model (every client behind a wire); when 19 is deepened at\n      M3, lead with the locality split, then the transport. **Still open**, and\n      not a doc-19 edit only: §8.1 lists the four process models but no section\n      says in so many words that the *default* TUI never touches a socket, which\n      is why the question reads as bigger than it is.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U35","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"For notification latency, the transport is close to the least of it","body":"**U35 · For notification latency, the transport is close to the least of it.**\n      WS and SSE are both push over a persistent connection and both are\n      sub-millisecond on localhost, against a provider round-trip measured in\n      seconds. What actually decides it: **A1's two-tier model** (live deltas\n      never persisted, so the hot path avoids §3.6's fsync on\n      `TurnFinished`/`ToolResult`/request-answer) — ✅ **normative since 2026-09-18,\n      so this is no longer a prediction about a delta but a property of §3.2/§3.6** —\n      the **bounded fan-out** above,\n      and **client-side ≤16 ms coalescing** (the doc-22 deepening item in §6's\n      session-3b list). Worth saying plainly whenever this question recurs,\n      because \"WS vs SSE\" reads like the latency decision and is not.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U36","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Survey + capture — DONE","body":"**U36 · Survey + capture — DONE.** Two `explore` subagents over the four reference\n      checkouts, merged into\n      [task-tracking-and-gating](../../.agents/knowledge/reference-harnesses/task-tracking-and-gating.md)\n      (the third topic digest, after sandboxing-and-checkpoints and\n      dialects-and-acp), indexed from that directory's README and from\n      [Appendix C](appendices/C-harness-learnings.md). B15 + A19 + doc 32 written;\n      both milestone tables and §4's counts updated in the same change.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U37","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`Verify::Command` is an exec surface — FIXED, in both places rather than only logged","body":"**U37 · ⚠️ `Verify::Command` is an exec surface — FIXED, in both places rather\n      than only logged.** The gate has the engine **run an argv taken from a\n      model-authored task item** when that item is closed: arbitrary command\n      execution driven by model data. A model that writes\n      `verify: command \"curl … | sh\"` and then closes the item gets it run, and if\n      the gate fired automatically it would get run *without an approval prompt*.\n      Found while writing this board entry, i.e. **after** B15 was drafted, so it\n      was patched into the proposal *and* the dossier before sign-off rather than\n      left as a note: **§4.8 now states** that `Verify::Command` goes through the\n      §4.4 permission engine exactly as `bash` does — same `PermScope::Exec`, same\n      deny-list, same §4.7 sandbox when one is active — and that **the gate never\n      auto-approves its own verification command**; a `verify` needing approval\n      blocks the close. B15's banner names it as the part of the decision with a\n      security consequence. Worth keeping as the lesson: a defect found after\n      drafting goes back into the artifact, not just into the tracker.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U38","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`tools/tasks.py` already hit the exact failure mode §4.8 now ships — escalated to the spec's §16.6, still unresolved","body":"**U38 · ⚠️ `tools/tasks.py` already hit the exact failure mode §4.8 now ships —\n      escalated to the spec's §16.6, still unresolved.** Its `check` cannot express\n      a standing goal, so the legitimately long-lived g14 keeps the gate\n      permanently red (finding f21) — and a gate that is always red gets ignored,\n      which is the failure the mandatory-checker rule exists to prevent. §4.8 as\n      signed off reddens `check` while a `Goal` is unclosed with only `Blocked` and\n      `WontFix` exempt, so **the product now has this repo's bug**. Found while\n      applying, not before. **Not patched**, because inventing a\n      `TaskStatus::Standing` would exceed the signed-off text; recorded as open\n      decision **§16.6** with the note that it must settle before the gate is\n      implemented and that it is a §14 compatibility event either way.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U39","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Gating strength — SETTLED as \"hard but bounded\"","body":"**U39 · Gating strength — SETTLED as \"hard but bounded\".** It was the one\n      genuinely open fork in B15 and the one flagged *low confidence, taste*:\n      lifetime and context-visibility were both settled by evidence (all four\n      harnesses append-only; oh-my-pi's fold is principle 2 already implemented),\n      but how *hard* the gate is was not. Taken as recommended on sign-off, which\n      is a real departure — every gate in the reference set is soft and bounded,\n      and forge's is off by default. **What is still not derived is the cap:**\n      `task.reminders_max` defaults to 3 because that is oh-my-pi's number. The cap\n      is the entire difference between a gate and a hang, so the built-in-tools\n      brainstorm owes a deliberate value (§5 above).","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U40","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`.dacode/plans/` format — SETTLED as markdown, and §12 now says so","body":"**U40 · `.dacode/plans/` format — SETTLED as markdown, and §12 now says so.**\n      Following forge's write-once `plan_create` precedent, because a board a human\n      reviews in a merge request should be a diff-friendly document. §12 records\n      the consequence that was the actual reason to decide: **`plans/` is\n      deliberately absent from the `.dacode/.gitignore` that dacode writes**, since\n      a promoted board is a reviewed repository artifact and not scratch state —\n      that distinction is what keeps §4.8's two tiers on the right side of the\n      cross-session-memory non-goal. It also notes that if the format ever becomes\n      one the user edits by hand, comment-preserving writes become a requirement\n      (B10's concern).","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U41","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Subagent write scope still needs a `PermScope` answer — §4.8 states the rule but not the mechanism","body":"**U41 · Subagent write scope still needs a `PermScope` answer — §4.8 states the\n      rule but not the mechanism.** A subagent may update items it owns (a `task`\n      spawn carries the parent item id) and may not modify the parent's `Goal`s or\n      `Gate`s — a deliberate departure, since both harnesses with subagents deny\n      them the tool entirely. **§4.8 now states the rule and §4.3 records that\n      `task` carries the parent id**, but §4.4 still has no scope for \"may write\n      task items\", and nothing says where ownership is enforced. Either a new\n      `PermScope` or an explicit note that `todo` is `PermScope::None` with\n      ownership enforced in the fold. Belongs to the built-in-tools brainstorm.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U42","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The `gate` word collision — RESOLVED by renaming §5.4's attribute","body":"**U42 · The `gate` word collision — RESOLVED by renaming §5.4's attribute.** The\n      separable bullet was accepted with the rest of B15: §5.4's workflow step\n      attribute is now **`approve`** (which is literally what it does — *\"pause for\n      approval of the plan\"*), leaving `Gate` unambiguous as a task kind. §5.4 also\n      gained one sentence saying *why*, so the rename is not silently undone by\n      someone who remembers the old name. Cost: one word in one TOML example plus\n      that sentence.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U43","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Unrelated, found while reading M0's docs: [01](part1-foundations/01-workspace-scaffolding.md) contradicted this board on the crate count — FIXED","body":"**U43 · Unrelated, found while reading M0's docs: [01](part1-foundations/01-workspace-scaffolding.md)\n      contradicted this board on the crate count — FIXED.** It told M0 to scaffold\n      \"the ten crates of §2 *(plus the new `dacode-store` and `dacode-introspect`)*\",\n      i.e. twelve, while §5 above says ten with both deferred per B11. An\n      implementer following the doc rather than the board would have created two\n      empty crates that B11's own recommendation argues against (\"a crate boundary\n      with one implementation is indirection without payoff\"). Corrected to name\n      **ten**, with B11 cited as the pending decision and both deferral dates\n      recorded. **B11 is deliberately left unsigned** — this is a consistency fix\n      that removes a wrong instruction, not a resolution. Doc 01 keeps its summary\n      marker, so §4's depth count is unchanged at 28.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U44","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The `agents-index.py` staleness was re-confirmed as the benign fixpoint, and was *not* chased","body":"**U44 · The `agents-index.py` staleness was re-confirmed as the benign fixpoint, and\n      was *not* chased.** `--check` exits 1 (\"index is stale\"). Regenerating to\n      diagnose changed **only** the root `AGENTS.md` row's last-commit and lag\n      (`a3f85b3`/15 → `27ddad0`/6) and the integrity hash — zero content, and this\n      session touched no `AGENTS.md` at all. So it is the self-staling fixpoint the\n      trap above describes, not drift from this change. **Reverted rather than\n      committed**, per that trap's \"do not chase `--check` to green by\n      re-committing\"; worth regenerating at the next milestone boundary, when a\n      commit is happening anyway.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U45","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"B15 signed off, A19 applied — design §4.8 is normative","body":"**U45 · B15 signed off, A19 applied — design §4.8 is normative.** All five\n      recommendation points and all four consequences, none redirected. Ten spec\n      sites edited: new §4.8, §3.2 `TaskUpdated`, §3.5 `Snapshot.tasks`, §4.2's\n      gate in the pseudocode *and* its special-tool list, §4.3's `todo` entry,\n      §5.4's `approve` rename, §9.2's unified tree, §10's sidebar, §12's\n      `plans/<board>.md`, §14's two engine tests. Version stayed **v0.1** and the\n      `Date` moved to 2026-09-13, per the convention under the spec's `Status`\n      field. Status flips across both appendices, doc 32 and this board in the same\n      change.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U46","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"A19's own `Depends on` claim was wrong, and I repeated it to the user before checking","body":"**U46 · ⚠️ A19's own `Depends on` claim was wrong, and I repeated it to the user\n      before checking.** It said *\"A1 must land first — the tier marker is what\n      lets §3.2 say `TaskUpdated` is durable.\"* The spec has **no tier concept at\n      all** today: §3.6 persists *every* `Envelope`, deltas included, which is\n      precisely the defect A1 exists to fix. So §4.8 declaring durability in prose\n      is consistent both before and after A1, and A1 was never the blocker. **The\n      real blockers were three dangling references** — to a §4.5 fingerprint (A4),\n      a B14 `Segment`, and an A3 guard set — none of which exist yet. Caught by\n      reading the target sections before editing rather than trusting the delta's\n      own dependency line. **Generalises: a delta's `Depends on` is a claim, not a\n      fact; verify it against the spec as it stands.** Corrected in A19's banner\n      (scoping decision 2) and in its detail body, struck rather than deleted.\n      ⚠️ **The premise is now historical, recorded so this entry is not read as a\n      present-tense claim about the spec**: A1 applied on 2026-09-18, so §3.2 *does*\n      have a tier concept and §3.6 no longer persists every `Envelope`. The lesson is\n      unaffected and the item stays open as a lesson, not as a description of the\n      current spec.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U47","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§4.8 was applied *self-contained*, which is a deliberate debt on A3 and A4","body":"**U47 · §4.8 was applied *self-contained*, which is a deliberate debt on A3 and\n      A4.** Rather than hold a signed-off decision behind three unsigned ones, §4.8\n      states its own caching and gating requirements in its own words (\"never\n      rewrites bytes already sent … a provider's cached prefix survives\") instead\n      of citing constructs that do not exist. **This is A16's move** — it defined\n      §13.2's `Json` alias locally rather than editing unsigned §3.2/§4.3\n      signatures. The debt: when A3 and A4 land they must tighten §4.8's wording\n      into real cross-references, and **A3 must reconcile its guard set with a gate\n      already sitting in §4.2's pseudocode**. Recorded in A19's banner and in\n      Appendix A's application-order paragraph, so whoever applies A3 sees it.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U48","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"A1 can no longer be applied by editing only the variants that existed when it was drafted —","body":"**U48 · A1 can no longer be applied by editing only the variants that existed when\n      it was drafted — ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-18, by A1's application, and the warning\n      generalised better than expected.** `TaskUpdated` is classified `Durable`, and\n      because the tier landed as a **rule** (*`Durable` iff the fold consumes it*,\n      §3.2) rather than a list, the variant A19 added and the `finding` variant B17\n      will add both classify themselves with no edit to A1 — which is the durable form\n      of the fix this item was asking for. §4.8's prose claim (\"it is durable\") is now\n      a property of the enum. The \"before any fixture\" rule held rather than being\n      waived: the crates are still stubs. Original entry follows. A19 added\n      `TaskUpdated` to §3.2's `Event` enum, so A1's\n      tier marker has one more variant to classify (durable — §4.8 says so in prose\n      in the meantime). That does *not* weaken the \"A1 before any fixture is\n      written\" rule: no fixture exists yet because there is no code, and\n      `TaskUpdated`'s will be written after A1 like every other.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      it was drafted — ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-18, by A1's application, and the warning","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U49","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§16.6 has no B-number, so a sign-off sweep of Appendix B will miss it","body":"**U49 · §16.6 has no B-number, so a sign-off sweep of Appendix B will miss it.**\n      Applying A19 put a genuinely open decision in the spec's §16 (standing goals)\n      but not in Appendix B's table, which is where the user signs decisions off.\n      Adding a B16 mid-application would have been a fourth artifact for one\n      question, and §16 is the spec's own list of open decisions, so it is the right\n      home — but the discoverability gap is real. **A12 (the §16 bookkeeping pass)\n      owns reconciling the two lists**; until then §1's sign-off paragraph says to\n      read §16 as well as Appendix B. Flagged in B15's banner, Appendix A's\n      applied-deltas row, and doc 32.\n      ⚠️ **Narrowed 2026-09-18, not closed.** The *question* §16.6 asks is answered —\n      `TaskStatus::Standing` is normative in §4.8, goals only, landed inside A1's\n      pass. What remains is purely the discoverability gap this item is about: §16's\n      text still lists item 6 as open, deliberately, so A12 removes it in one piece\n      rather than piecemeal. Until it does, `attention.py` renders §16.6 as an open\n      vote (**U95**'s false positive, accepted by the user 2026-09-18), and a reader\n      of §16 alone would not know the answer landed in §4.8.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U50","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`tools/check-links.py` should fail if the spec ever gains a markdown link — DONE","body":"**U50 · `tools/check-links.py` should fail if the spec ever gains a markdown\n      link — DONE.** Still owed from the A17 pass (f20), and A19 was the fourth\n      delta whose proposed text had to be checked by hand for link shapes before\n      transcribing. Verified again that pass: `grep -c '](' docs/dacode-design.md`\n      → 0, and the paste-ready §4.8 block was checked programmatically. A machine\n      should hold this convention; four manual checks was the evidence.\n      **Now enforced:** `check-links.py` carries a `NO_LINK_FILES` constant and\n      fails on any link in a listed file, run **regardless of the roots given** —\n      narrowing the scope must not switch a repo invariant off. Code regions are\n      stripped first, so the backticked escape U30 documents still works. A listed\n      file that has gone missing is itself reported, so a rename cannot silently\n      drop the convention. `attention.py`'s hygiene line now delegates to it\n      instead of keeping its own substring count, because the two would have\n      disagreed the first time the spec backticked a link shape. Four cases\n      negative-tested, not just run green: a real link fails; a backticked shape\n      passes; a fenced one passes; a missing file is reported.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U51","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`tools/attention.py` — one prioritized view of everything needing the user's decision. BUILT","body":"**U51 · `tools/attention.py` — one prioritized view of everything needing the\n      user's decision. BUILT.** Reads all five sources (Appendix B's summary\n      table, Appendix A's index, the spec's §16, this file's §6 unchecked items,\n      the 🧠 brainstorm gates) plus the task tracker's open debt and user-gates,\n      and renders one table to stdout and `.agents/cache/ATTENTION.md`\n      (**gitignored** — a rendering of committed state, never a source of it).\n      Priority is **derived, not hand-assigned**: from which milestone the item\n      gates, via the doc→milestone map in\n      [31-ci-milestones](part6-quality/31-ci-milestones.md) (M0/M1 → P0, M2/M3 →\n      P1, M4+ → P2, none → P3), so it stays honest as the board changes. The\n      `Do` column separates **vote** / **measure** / **session** / **review** /\n      **ack**, because \"sign off\" is not one thing — B12 needs a benchmark, four\n      items need a sitting-down design session, and neither is a vote.\n      `--max-priority N` narrows, `--json` is machine-readable, `--check` exits 1\n      on any P0 so it can gate a delivery. Added to `AGENTS.md`'s Key Files table\n      and to its committing checklist, so an agent reports \"nothing needs your\n      input\" only after running it.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U52","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The count it reported is itself a finding worth acting on: 65 open items, 14 of them P0","body":"**U52 · The count it reported is itself a finding worth acting on: 65 open items,\n      14 of them P0.** Four need a design *session*, one needs a *measurement*, 26\n      need a vote, 33 are unowned follow-ups. That is not a list a person holds in\n      their head, and the P0 set is mostly M1's blockers — so **M0 is still the\n      right next step** (it is gated on nothing), but M1 cannot start until a chunk\n      of that 14 is cleared. Worth deciding whether to batch the votes (B3, B5,\n      B7, B14 are all `accept`-recommended at high confidence and all gate M1) in\n      one pass rather than one at a time.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U53","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`attention.py` scrapes markdown, so it breaks silently when the docs' shapes change","body":"**U53 · `attention.py` scrapes markdown, so it breaks silently when the docs'\n      shapes change.** Four bugs were found by running it against the real docs and\n      spot-checking rows against known ground truth, not by reading the code: doc\n      numbers are zero-padded in Appendix B's `Gates` column (`05`) but not in the\n      milestone table (`5`), which silently reported \"gates no milestone\" for\n      B3/B12/B13; a `re.S` regex swallowed the file so only 1 of 4 brainstorm gates\n      appeared; B12's signal is written `**measured** decision`, so matching raw\n      markdown mislabelled the one decision needing a benchmark as a vote; and\n      A-deltas were held behind *already-resolved* B-decisions. **Generalises: a\n      doc-scraping tool needs its output checked against ground truth, not just a\n      zero exit code.** The durable fix would be machine-readable front matter on\n      the appendix tables instead of scraping prose — worth doing if this tool\n      survives another milestone.\n      **An eighth instance was found while rendering B19 (session 11), and it is the\n      first one that misinforms rather than merely mis-sorts.**\n      `why_from_confidence` tests `\"low\" in confidence.lower()` — a *substring* match\n      over the whole cell — so B19's three-way cell (`high (prevention must precede\n      creation) / med (the args check) / low (how wide the injected set goes)`)\n      rendered as **\"confidence marked LOW — genuinely your taste, not the\n      evidence\"**. That is not a wrong priority, it is a wrong *characterisation*:\n      B19's central claim is forced by the append-only log, and telling the reader it\n      is a taste call invites them to skip the evidence. Two defects in one line —\n      the substring match, which would also fire on \"follow\", \"below\" or \"allow\" in\n      any future cell, and the assumption that a cell carries one confidence when\n      this appendix's own convention is a leading value with qualified sub-parts (B17\n      and B18 already do this and escape only because neither says \"low\").       **Fixed both ways at once, because tokenising alone is not enough.** The\n      matcher now takes `\\b(high|med|low)\\b` *tokens* and reads the **first** as the\n      overall level, saying so explicitly when a later sub-part is lower — which\n      keeps B2's `med (facts) / low (taste)` signal instead of flattening it. But\n      token order still cannot express B12, whose cell reads \"was high → open\": the\n      first token there is a history lesson, not a verdict. So confidence also\n      became a **declared** field — `confidence-lead:` in Appendix B's\n      `attention-meta` block, absence meaning \"trust the first token\", with a\n      hygiene check that every declared id exists and every value is one of\n      high/med/low/open, mirroring `action:`. **That is the durable fix this entry\n      has recommended three times, applied to the field that just mislabelled a\n      decision**, and it is the eighth instance arguing for extending the same\n      treatment to the rest of the appendix tables.\n      **And a ninth instance landed while writing this very entry, which is the\n      cleanest one yet.** §6 items derived their action type from three keywords in\n      their own prose — `\"sibling\"`, `\"dacode-json\"`, `\"json crate\"` — and the\n      paragraph above used the word \"sibling\" about an unrelated defect, which\n      reclassified *this item* from `review` to `ack`. An entry documenting how\n      prose-scraping misfires, misfiring on its own wording, is U75 and U79 a third\n      time. **Fixed by declaration, not by rewording**: §6's header now carries an\n      `attention-meta` block with an `action:` line per non-default item, the\n      heuristic is deleted, and a hygiene check validates every declared id and\n      value. Rewording to dodge a regex is what U79 named as the scraper's cost made\n      visible; deleting the regex is the only fix that does not leave the trap armed\n      for the next author. The same pass replaced one more substring test in the\n      tracker-gate classifier (`\"user\" in …`, which would also fire on\n      \"superuser\") with a word-boundary match.\n      **A sixth instance was found the same way (U78), and the durable fix is now\n      partly applied.** `gates:` in Appendix A and the `U` ids in §6 below are both\n      *declared* fields with a hygiene check that fails when a declaration names\n      something absent or leaves a hole — following the `action:` precedent U75\n      set. So the direction is settled and cheap to extend; what remains is the\n      rest of the appendix tables, still scraped. The lesson held for the sixth\n      time: it was found by comparing the tool's output against §1 of this board,\n      not by its exit code.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U54","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§16.6 has no B-number and `attention.py` is currently the only thing that catches it","body":"**U54 · §16.6 has no B-number and `attention.py` is currently the only thing that\n      catches it.** The tool special-cases §16 items 1–5 as duplicates of Appendix\n      B's decisions (A12 reconciles those) and surfaces 6+ as new. That is a\n      heuristic keyed to a count, so adding a §16 item that *does* duplicate a\n      B-decision would either hide it or double-report it. **A12 should give §16\n      items their B-numbers** and retire the heuristic.\n      *2026-09-18:* §16.6's **answer** landed in §4.8 (`Standing`, inside A1's pass),\n      but this item is unchanged and stays open, because what it is about is the tool\n      and the §16 text — and both are A12's. Until A12 strikes item 6 the heuristic\n      still fires on it, which is **U95**'s accepted false positive.\n      🔧 **NARROWED 2026-09-18 — the tool half is built; only A12's markers remain.**\n      `parse_spec_16` no longer decides by position alone. `classify_spec16` reads each\n      item's **own text**, in precedence order: a resolution marker (`✅` or `~~`) makes\n      it *resolved*; a `B<n>` reference makes it *Appendix B's*, so it is not new\n      information for the user; only an item with neither is an open vote. The positional\n      `n ≤ 5` rule survives **as a counted fallback**, and a new hygiene line reports how\n      many items still rely on it — currently five, named — because **an invisible\n      heuristic is one nobody retires**. So A12's job is now mechanical and self-checking:\n      write a marker or a B-number into each item, watch that count reach zero, and the\n      fallback can be deleted. Two properties this buys that position could not: an item\n      answered **in place** stops rendering as a vote without being renumbered or struck\n      (which is exactly §16.6's case, and why the false positive was stuck), and an item\n      numbered above 5 that *does* duplicate a B-decision is suppressed on content rather\n      than surfacing as new purely because of where it sat. Verified by\n      `tools/attention.py --self-test` — 11 classification cases plus fallback counting —\n      which was itself checked by breaking the resolved-marker branch and confirming it\n      fails on the four cases that depend on it. **Stays open** until A12 writes the\n      markers and the fallback is removed.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U55","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The live task tree already works in server mode and needs nothing","body":"**U55 · The live task tree already works in server mode and needs nothing.** It is\n      folded into `Snapshot.tasks` (§3.5) and the fold is shared code in\n      `dacode-protocol`, so TUI, web, extensions and a remote `attach` all render\n      the same tree with **no new protocol surface**. That is principle 1 paying\n      for itself, and it is worth stating plainly because the question reads as\n      bigger than it is.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U56","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"CLOSED 2026-09-19 by A7 — all three gaps, and the answer to (1) is not the one this item guessed at","body":"**U56 · ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-19 by A7 — all three gaps, and the answer to (1) is not\n      the one this item guessed at.** *The promoted board is the real gap*: a board is a\n      file in the repo, not session state, so a browser talking to a remote `serve` (§8.1)\n      cannot read it from disk.\n      1. ~~**No screen for it.**~~ → **It has a screen and a route, and the route is\n         repo-scoped rather than project-scoped.** §8.2 carries `GET /api/plans` and\n         `GET`+`PUT /api/plans/:board`, resolving against the **server's** cwd and\n         returning `409` outside a repository rather than writing into whatever directory\n         happens to be current; §9.2 carries a **config editor** screen that includes\n         boards. **The reasoning that settled it: a route follows the file**, and §12 puts\n         the file at `<repo>/.dacode/plans/<board>.md`. So the \"nearest existing concept is\n         §6's **projects**\" reading survives — but as **presentation** (§9.2 groups boards,\n         and a board is cross-session by nature so it is not a tab of the session view)\n         rather than as a second routing axis. Nothing had to be decided about projects\n         after all, which is why this closed instead of needing a vote.\n      2. ~~**No write path.**~~ → **`.dacode/plans/` is the third of §8.5's three writable\n         classes**, beside user config and repo config, exactly as caveat #1 required.\n         ⚠️ **What neither the caveat nor this item anticipated is that the allowlist also\n         has to refuse *keys*, not only paths**: §8.5 rejects the exec-bearing parts of a\n         writable file (`[[extensions]]`' `cmd`, a hook's `run`/`post`) because §9.2 renders\n         model-authored markdown on the authenticated origin, so a script reaching that\n         origin could otherwise turn one transcript into persistent command execution as the\n         server's user. Boards are markdown and unaffected, but the rule lives in the same\n         list they do. **And a board save is conditional**: `If-Match` on the `ETag` it read,\n         `412` on mismatch — because §4.8's promotion writes the same file a UI saves, and\n         \"a human reviews the diff\" is not a reconciliation mechanism for a clobber (the diff\n         shows the result, not the bytes that were overwritten).\n      3. ~~**`promote` needs a controller answer.**~~ → **Answered as two checks that do\n         not substitute for each other.** The act is session-scoped (the item belongs to one\n         session's tree) while its effect is not (a committed repo file), so §8.4 gates it\n         on the **controller of the session owning the item** *and* §8.5/§8.2 bound the\n         write. **The asymmetry this item did not state:** from the model a promotion is a\n         **write through §4.4** (§4.8's own `Verify::Command` rule is the precedent — the\n         task subsystem does not get to touch the filesystem outside the permission engine),\n         while from a browser it is **not a tool call at all**, so §4.4 is not in the path\n         and the allowlist is the bound instead. Neither door inherits the other's check.\n      ⚠️ **What A7 recorded rather than fixed, and which gap 3 depends on:** §3.3 has **no\n      task-mutation `Command`**, so the browser path to `promote` does not exist in the\n      protocol yet — **U103**. This item is closed because the *design* questions it raised\n      are answered; U103 is the wire gap underneath them and stays open with the task-tree\n      brainstorm as owner.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"**U56 · ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-19 by A7 — all three gaps, and the answer to (1) is not","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U57","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"B16 must be read with B10 or it settles B10 by accident — DISSOLVED, not resolved","body":"**U57 · B16 must be read with B10 or it settles B10 by accident — DISSOLVED,\n      not resolved.** The warning was well-founded: signing B16 as drafted *would*\n      have settled B10 by accident. It was discharged by **narrowing the policy\n      instead of by deciding both** — nothing ports to Rust, so no YAML crate can\n      enter the tree and the choice the coupling existed to force cannot arise.\n      **B10 is now free-standing** and can be signed on its own merits whenever.\n      Two facts closed the other half: `agents-sync.py` *writes* YAML as well as\n      parsing it, because the generated `.opencode/agent/*.md` frontmatter is\n      **opencode's** format and not ours to choose; and Python has **no stdlib TOML\n      writer** (`tomllib` is read-only, verified on 3.14.3), so moving the tracker\n      would trade PyYAML for `tomlkit` — a lateral swap in a tool already slated for\n      retirement. **PyYAML is permanent**, which is also why `agent-rules.yaml` does\n      not convert early.\n      *Original entry, kept because the audit trail is the rule:* three of the five\n      Python tools parse YAML, so porting them either adds a YAML crate (`serde_yaml`\n      is deprecated upstream and no YAML crate is in\n      [Appendix F](appendices/F-c-dependencies.md)) or moves `agent-rules.yaml` and\n      the tracker to TOML — which is B10's subject and would reuse `toml_edit`,\n      already in Appendix F's clean list and already required by §5.1. **Every clause\n      of that is now moot except the `toml_edit` fact**, which survives as the reason\n      a TOML config would be cheap if one were ever wanted.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U58","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Two of the five Python tools are prototypes of §4.8, so they should be retired, not ported — CONFIRMED, with both line counts stale","body":"**U58 · Two of the five Python tools are prototypes of §4.8, so they should be\n      retired, not ported — CONFIRMED, with both line counts stale.** `tasks.py`\n      (~~261~~ **298** lines) *is* the task tree; `attention.py` (~~642~~ **860**) is\n      a view over it. Porting either builds a second implementation of a spec'd\n      product feature, and under §2.4 neither is ported: both stay Python and are\n      **deleted** when their trigger fires. ~~**Retiring `tasks.py` is still blocked on\n      §16.6**~~ ✅ **that blocker is discharged 2026-09-18**: §4.8 now carries\n      `Standing` — goals only, exempt from a red `check` and from reminders — so the\n      defect found in `tasks.py` is fixed in the product spec and would not be\n      inherited. What remains before retirement is the M2 dogfooding trigger, not a\n      design gap. The repo-side half is fixed (`--status standing`, 2026-09-14), which\n      was *evidence for* §16.6 and is now also the shape §4.8 adopted. `attention.py`'s\n      §4.8-view half dogfoods; its appendix/§6-scraping half has no product analogue\n      and never will, so it is permanent.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      §16.6**~~ ✅ **that blocker is discharged 2026-09-18**: §4.8 now carries","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U59","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`check-links.py` may be the one tool that should stay Python permanently — IT DOES, and it won by default rather than on its own argument","body":"**U59 · `check-links.py` may be the one tool that should stay Python\n      permanently — IT DOES, and it won by default rather than on its own argument.**\n      Decided before M0 as this item asked. Under §2.4 *every* authoring tool stays\n      a script, so `check-links.py` is not a special case any more; its reasoning is\n      nonetheless preserved in §2.4 as **the one genuine exception class** — tooling\n      that must run on a checkout that has not been built — because it holds\n      independently of how B16 came out. If link checking needed a compiled\n      workspace, the docs could not be validated until the code builds, which\n      inverts the order the design phase depends on. This item is also why §2.4's\n      final paragraph existed as a proposal; it landed as the scope boundary instead.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U60","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`attention.py` shipped a false-positive priority and an edit silently deleted a function — both caught only by running it","body":"**U60 · `attention.py` shipped a false-positive priority and an edit silently\n      deleted a function — both caught only by running it.** The milestone\n      heuristic matched a bare `M1` in prose (\"M0 is still the right next step\")\n      and then, after tightening, still matched `all gate M1)` at the end of a\n      74-character parenthetical, promoting an item to P0 on a sentence about\n      *other* items; fixed by requiring a short bracketed tag or a gating\n      preposition. Separately, an edit whose match was a single `def` line replaced\n      the signature and orphaned the body after a `return`, so\n      `earliest_milestone` was called but never defined — **`ast.parse` passed**,\n      because Python cannot see a `NameError` at parse time and dead code after\n      `return` is legal. **A syntax check is not a smoke test.** Recorded as f33's\n      companion lesson: run the tool and check its output against known ground\n      truth, every time.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U61","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§7.1 hooks are a template-into-shell seam, and it is the same class of defect as the `Verify::Command` one that was caught for §4.8","body":"**U61 · ⚠️ §7.1 hooks are a template-into-shell seam, and it is the same class of\n      defect as the `Verify::Command` one that was caught for §4.8.** `run` gets\n      \"the envelope on stdin **and templated args**\", with the example\n      `notify-send 'dacode' '{{ session.title }}: {{ reason }}'` — a *string*\n      interpolated into a shell command. The example's own fields are\n      client-controlled rather than model-controlled, so state the risk precisely:\n      the **mechanism is general**, hooks fire on `QuestionAsked` and\n      `PermissionRequested` (§7.1's second example does exactly this), and the\n      natural fields to interpolate there — the question `prompt`, a tool name, an\n      error message — are **model-authored**. A hook author writing\n      `{{ prompt }}` has written a command-injection bug and the spec gives them no\n      reason not to. **Fix: argv, not a shell string; no interpolation into the\n      command at all.** The envelope is already on stdin, which is the complete\n       mechanism — so *delete* templated args rather than escape them.\n       ~~Separately, `post` ships the **whole `Envelope`** to an arbitrary URL with no\n       redaction rule, while §8.2 redacts secrets on `/api/config`; that asymmetry\n       should be closed or explained.~~ ✅ **The second half is closed — B19/A23,\n       2026-09-16.** §7.1 now requires a `post` body to be redacted before it leaves\n       and says why the local `run` path is *not*; §8.2 names the four projection\n       endpoints plus `/api/config` as the redaction points. Cite §7.1/§8.2 rather\n       than restating the argument. **The first half stands and is still A22 item 1.**\n       Belongs in [24](part5-outreach/24-extensions-channels.md)\n       **Now delta A22, item 1.** Owed by M4 — but the delta itself can land any\n       time, and should, because it is three lines of spec text. ✅ **A22 signed 2026-09-17\n       with item 1 confirmed — amended in one particular:** `run` takes an argv array with no\n       shell, and **templating survives into argv elements** rather than being deleted, since\n       with no shell there is nothing to inject into and the convenience is free. A hook that\n       genuinely wants `sh -c` must ask for it explicitly, which is the point of making it\n       visible.\n      ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-18 by A22 item 1, in exactly the amended form recorded above.**\n      §7.1's `run` is now an **argv array executed without a shell**, with templating\n      surviving **into argv elements** — so `{{ session.title }}` expands to one argument\n      whatever it contains, and a title holding `; rm -rf ~` is an argument rather than a\n      second command. A hook that genuinely wants a shell must write `[\"sh\",\"-c\",…]`, which\n      is the point: the dangerous case becomes visible in the config file where a reviewer\n      can see it, instead of being the default behaviour of every hook that interpolates a\n      field. `when` keeps its expression language because an expression is *evaluated* and\n      never executed. **§7.1 also states why this is a rule and not an example-level\n      detail**, which is the part this item argued for and the spec previously lacked: the\n      example's own fields are client-controlled, but the natural fields to interpolate on\n      `QuestionAsked`/`PermissionRequested` — the prompt, a tool name, an error message — are\n      **model-authored**, so `\"{{ prompt }}\"` in a shell string is a model-reachable\n      command-injection bug and nothing about the field name warns the author. §14 has the\n      test (fire a hook whose template expands to `; rm -rf ~`, assert one argv element and\n      no second process). **Note the fix kept the convenience this item proposed deleting**:\n      deleting templating would have been safer and less useful, and the argv form makes\n      both available at once, which is the only combination worth having.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"       should be closed or explained.~~ ✅ **The second half is closed — B19/A23,","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U62","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`AllowPattern` lets an extension widen permissions without limit, and §8.4 grants extensions that power on purpose —","body":"**U62 · ⚠️ `AllowPattern` lets an extension widen permissions without limit, and\n      §8.4 grants extensions that power on purpose — ✅ FIXED 2026-09-18, not merely\n      documented.** §3.3's\n      `ResolvePermission { decision: AllowPattern(String) }` injects a caller-chosen\n      glob; §4.4 puts session grants at the **top** of the resolution order, above\n      agent, project and user config; §8.4 says extensions may answer\n      permissions *\"regardless of control\"* because that is their purpose. Compose\n      the three and a Telegram bridge answering one prompt can grant `**` for the\n      session — i.e. any connected bridge is effectively root-equivalent, which is\n      not what §8.3's \"no users, no roles\" model intends. **Two separable fixes:**\n      constrain `AllowPattern` to a **subset of the scope actually requested** (the\n      request is in `PermissionRequested`, so this is checkable), and split the\n      `Hello` capability so *answer questions* and *grant permissions* are\n      different flags. Belongs in [16](part3-capabilities/16-permissions.md) and\n      [24](part5-outreach/24-extensions-channels.md). **Now delta A22, item 2.** The\n      `AllowPattern` half is owed by M1 (the permission engine); the capability split by M4.\n      ⚠️ **Both halves changed owner on 2026-09-17.** A22's item 2 was **redirected to\n      permissive for v1**, so the split does not arrive from there; and **B20's resolution\n      needs it for remote principals specifically**, which is now **A25's §8.4 half** —\n      argued on its own merits (a process on another machine is not a local bridge), with\n      local permissiveness untouched. The `AllowPattern` bound is likewise reserved rather\n      than applied. Do not close this item on A22's account; it moved.\n      ✅ **Answered 2026-09-17 — and not the way this item proposed.** A22's item 2 was\n      **redirected**: the user's call is a **permissive v1** (*\"I want that the first version\n      of harness will be permissive\"*), so neither the subset bound nor the capability split\n      is applied.~~\n      ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-18 by A22 — and the annotation above is now wrong on the point\n      that mattered, so it is corrected rather than left standing.** The user was asked the\n      question this item's own text raised and **redirected it back**: the `AllowPattern`\n      **subset bound IS applied** (§4.4), while the capability split stays **reserved**\n      (§7.2's `Hello` flags, granted by default in v1). The argument that settled it is\n      *this item's* argument rather than a new one: **permissive** describes the user's\n      experience, and the bound does not touch it — a user at their own terminal can still\n      grant `**` explicitly. What the bound constrains is a **client acting on the user's\n      behalf**, which is precisely the escalation this item found: without it, §3.3's\n      `AllowPattern(String)` + §4.4 putting session grants at the top of the resolution\n      order + §8.4 letting extensions resolve permissions compose into \"one bridge\n      answering one prompt can grant `**` for the session\". It is mechanically checkable at\n      resolution time because the request is already in `PermissionRequested { scope }`, a\n      pattern outside it is an `Ack Err`, and §14 has the test. What remains open is not\n      this item but **A25's M5 half**, which must argue capabilities for a *remote*\n      principal on its own merits using the flags §7.2 now reserves.\n      What the 2026-09-17 annotation replaced them with is the part this item was actually about — the\n      composition stops being *implied*: §8.4 must state the trust model explicitly (a\n      connected extension is as trusted as the local terminal, and may answer questions,\n      resolve permissions and grant session-wide patterns, deliberately), and `Hello`\n      **reserves** the two flags so v2 can tighten without a wire break. ~~**The escalation\n      this item found is therefore documented and made reversible, not fixed** — carry it as\n      an accepted risk with a named owner (§8.4's text), not as closed work.~~ **Superseded\n      by the closure above: both halves of what this item asked for now exist** — the bound\n      applied and the trust model written down — so there is no accepted risk left to\n      carry. §8.4 stating the model explicitly is the half that survived the redirect\n      unchanged, and it survived because it was never the *escalation* fix, it was the\n      **reviewability** fix: a trust model implied by three separately-written sections\n      composing badly is one nobody can audit.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      §8.4 grants extensions that power on purpose — ✅ FIXED 2026-09-18, not merely","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U63","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"B7's honesty problem has a bigger sibling nobody has flagged: §4.4's main `bash` allow-list is advisory for exactly the same reason","body":"**U63 · ⚠️ B7's honesty problem has a bigger sibling nobody has flagged: §4.4's\n      main `bash` allow-list is advisory for exactly the same reason.**\n      ✅ **ACKED BY THE USER 2026-10-01 (g129 iii), on the M1.1f measurement below rather than on B7's\n      original one.** ⚠️ **The ack discharges the item and does NOT land §4.4's advisory wording** — that\n      stays **B7's owed transcription** beside A12's §16.3 half, deliberately not folded into A28, since\n      advisory-versus-enforced is a judgement B7 owns and this measurement is *input* to it rather than a\n      substitute. What the ack settles is that the allow-list is a **guardrail against accidents and not\n      against an adversary**, and that §4.7's worktree is the boundary; what B7's transcription still owes\n      is saying so in §4.4 with the reason now measured (§16.10 of doc 16 carries it in full). B7\n      establishes that `bash:readonly`'s deny-list cannot enforce read-onlyness. The\n      identical argument applies to `bash = { allow = [\"cargo *\", \"git diff*\"],\n      deny = [\"git push*\"] }` — a shell command is a *program*, and a glob over its\n      text cannot decide what it does: `git diff; git push`, `cargo run` invoking\n      anything, a shell function, `$(...)`. Yet §4.4 presents it as a control while\n      only `bash:readonly` gets the caveat. **The real boundary is the worktree\n      sandbox (§4.7) plus the OS**, and §4.4 should say so — the glob layer is a\n      guardrail against *accidents*, not against an adversary. **Widen B7's scope\n      to cover §4.4**, rather than fixing the smaller instance and leaving the\n      larger one asserted. **B7's scope is now widened to cover §4.4**, deliberately\n      instead of folding it into A22 — advisory-versus-enforced is a judgement B7\n      owns. Owed by M1; B7 already gates doc 14. ✅ **B7 signed 2026-09-17 with the widened\n      scope intact**, so §4.4's main allow-list gets the same advisory wording and the same\n      pointer to where the real boundary is (§4.7's worktree plus the OS). Two things this\n      sign-off added that bear on this item: the OS sandbox ships as an **inert stub** in v1,\n      so \"the worktree plus the OS\" is *the worktree* until the stub is filled; and **U98**\n      records the BPF `execve`-interception route, which is the mechanism that would make the\n      glob layer unnecessary rather than honest.\n      ⚠️ **M1.1f measured a STRONGER mechanism than the one this item was filed on (2026-10-01, tracker\n      f306), and it removes the comfort dacode's own design offers.** Every example above is *shell*-level\n      (`git diff; git push`, `$(...)`, a shell function) — but `PermScope::Exec { cmd: Vec<String> }` is an\n      argv array and §7.1's hooks are executed **without a shell**, so dacode already has the property\n      whose absence B7 and this item both blamed. **It does not help.** Globbing a flattened argv against\n      `deny = [\"rm -rf *\"]`, **four of five semantically identical invocations evade** —\n      `[\"rm\",\"-r\",\"-f\",…]`, `[\"rm\",\"--recursive\",\"--force\",…]`, `[\"rm\",\"-fr\",…]`, `[\"/bin/rm\",\"-rf\",…]` —\n      with no shell, subshell or pipeline involved, and the result is **matcher-independent** across all\n      five measured arms: the defect is in *globbing an argv*, not in the glob. Two further measurements\n      close off the cheap fixes: the join is **not injective** (`[\"git\",\"status\"]` and `[\"git status\"]`\n      both space-join to `\"git status\"` and both satisfy an allow rule), and **neither** a\n      first-element-only **nor** a last-element-only join denies `rm -rf /tmp/x` at all. **So the advisory\n      wording B7 owes §4.4 must give *this* reason rather than the shell one** — the pattern language\n      cannot *name* an argv — and the alternative that can (match `cmd[0]` plus a parsed-flag model) is\n      **doc 14's**, being a claim about the `bash` tool rather than about the resolver. Full table in\n      [16](part3-capabilities/16-permissions.md) §16.10. **This is the evidence for the ack, not a new\n      item**, and it is what M1.7's Step 0 puts in front of the user.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **ACKED BY THE USER 2026-10-01 (g129 iii), on the M1.1f measurement below rather than on B7's","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U64","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Sub-agent permission inheritance caps the *mode* but not the *scopes*","body":"**U64 · Sub-agent permission inheritance caps the *mode* but not the *scopes*.**\n      §5.3 says a child \"cannot exceed parent's mode\" — and mode is only\n      `Plan|Ask|AutoEdit|Yolo`. The allow/deny **lists** are per-agent config, so a\n      child agent whose own config carries `edit.allow = [\"**\"]`, spawned from an\n      `auto-edit` parent scoped to `src/**`, is capped by neither. The rule should\n      be **intersection of scopes, not `min(mode)`**. Belongs in\n      [11](part2-engine/11-subagents.md) and [16](part3-capabilities/16-permissions.md).\n      **Now delta A22, item 3.** Owed by M2, when sub-agents are built. ✅ **A22 signed\n      2026-09-17 with item 3 confirmed**, and the reason it survives a *permissive* v1 is\n      worth keeping: intersection is not a restriction on the operator, who can set the\n      parent wide — it is what makes §5.3's **existing promise** true for scopes and not only\n      for modes. B7's dissolution of its sandbox half stands (the sandbox is an immutable\n      session attribute, so there is no per-agent key to intersect).\n      ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-18 by A22 item 3.** §5.3 now states inheritance as an\n      **intersection** — the child's effective scopes are its own resolved scopes ∩ the\n      parent's, and its mode is `min(child, parent)` — with the failure case named in the\n      spec rather than only here (a child whose own agent file carries `edit.allow = [\"**\"]`\n      under a parent scoped to `src/**` was constrained by neither), and with the reason it\n      is not a restriction on the operator, who can set the parent wide. §14 has the test:\n      spawn exactly that child and assert a write outside `src/**` is denied — the case a\n      mode-only cap passes. **What stays owed is the plan-side write-up**, in\n      [11](part2-engine/11-subagents.md) and [16](part3-capabilities/16-permissions.md),\n      both still summaries; the spec is normative, so M2's build has its rule.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      **Now delta A22, item 3.** Owed by M2, when sub-agents are built. ✅ **A22 signed","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U65","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`?token=` leaks by design, and the transcript is the leak vector","body":"**U65 · `?token=` leaks by design, and the transcript is the leak vector.** §8.3\n      accepts the token as a query parameter on first visit. It then sits in browser\n      history, in any server/proxy log, and in the `Referer` of **any outbound link\n      the UI renders** — and §9.2 renders model-authored markdown, so a transcript\n      containing `http://evil/` is a click away from shipping the token. Cheap\n      fixes, none of them mentioned: `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`,\n      `history.replaceState` to strip the parameter once the cookie is set, and\n      `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"` on rendered links. Related and also unmentioned:\n      §8.2's `/api/sessions/:id/file` returns arbitrary repo content **on the\n      authenticated app's origin**, which is a stored-XSS delivery path unless it is\n      forced to `text/plain` + `Content-Disposition` under a CSP. Belongs in\n      [19](part4-hosts-clients/19-server-gateway.md) and\n      [21](part4-hosts-clients/21-web-ui.md). **Now delta A22, item 4.** Owed by M3 —\n      nothing here exists before `serve` does. ✅ **A22 signed 2026-09-17 with item 4\n      confirmed in full** — it is the one item the permissive-v1 answer does not touch,\n      because it costs no capability: it stops dacode's *own* bearer token leaving through\n      the `Referer` of a page that renders model-authored markdown.\n      **⚠️ Not fixed by A23, and it is worth being precise about why.** B19 named\n      this item, U61 and identifier privacy as one family — *an identifier or a\n      secret placed into a channel that outlives the decision to put it there* — and\n      that principle is now normative prose in §4.3 rather than an argument repeated\n      in three places, so cite it instead of restating it. But the family splits on\n      **who chose the channel**: A23 covers egress the *engine* performs (projection\n      endpoints, hook `post` bodies), while a token in a URL and then in a `Referer`\n      is egress the *browser* performs on the user's behalf, which no server-side\n      redaction rule reaches. ~~Every fix listed above is still owed, and all of them\n      are still A22 item 4.~~ ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-18 by A22 item 4 — every fix listed above\n      landed.** §8.3 now carries `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer` on every response and\n      documents `?token=` as the **bootstrap-only** convenience it is, stripped from the URL\n      once the cookie is set; §9.2 carries `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"` as a **renderer**\n      rule rather than a per-view one, so it covers any surface rendering model output and\n      not only the transcript; and §8.2's `/api/sessions/:id/file` and `/blob/:blob_id` are\n      served as `text/plain` with `Content-Disposition: attachment` under a CSP forbidding\n      inline script, closing the stored-XSS path this item named. §8.3 also records *why*\n      none of this was part of the permissive trade-off: it costs no capability, and what\n      stops leaking is dacode's **own** bearer token. **The distinction this item drew is\n      why it closed cleanly** — A23 covers egress the *engine* performs, this covered egress\n      the *browser* performs, and only a client-side rule reaches the second.\n\n#### Correctness gaps\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      nothing here exists before `serve` does. ✅ **A22 signed 2026-09-17 with item 4","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U66","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The append-only event log has no version record —","body":"**U66 · The append-only event log has no version record — ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-18,\n      folded into A1's transcription, which is where this item said it belonged.**\n      The cheap answer was taken: line 1 of `events.jsonl` is now a **`LogHeader`**\n      record — `{\"type\":\"LogHeader\",\"data\":{\"format_version\":1,\"created\":<unix millis>}}`\n      — and **not** an `Envelope`, so it carries no cursor and needs no tier. It is\n      written once at creation and never rewritten, so it costs the append-only\n      property nothing. Two details worth keeping: `format_version` is **the same\n      counter §7.2's `Hello` sends**, so a log and a wire version cannot drift apart;\n      and §12's layout text records that the header has no index entry, the first\n      `index.bin` record being the first event. ⚠️ **It reworded a sentence A24 had\n      landed the day before** — the cursor's \"equals line number + 1\" became\n      \"derivable by arithmetic from the record's position\", because a header on line 1\n      shifts any position-derived arithmetic. That *strengthens* U67's fix rather than\n      undoing it: a fixed `+1` was exactly the weld to the JSONL representation U67\n      was closed to remove. Original entry follows. §14 pins the wire format\n      with golden fixtures and §7.2 sends `Hello { protocol_version }` to\n      extensions — but nothing stamps a version into `events.jsonl` itself. A log\n      written by one build and read by a later one has no way to know what it is\n      looking at, which is a strange gap in the one artefact the design calls the\n      source of truth. A header record at log head is the cheap answer; a field on\n      `Envelope` is the expensive one. **A1 is the natural place**, since it is\n      already re-shaping `Envelope`/`Event`, and it is *cheaper before the first\n      fixture exists* than after. **Owed by M1**, and in practice by A1.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"**U66 · The append-only event log has no version record — ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-18,","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U67","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"CLOSED 2026-09-17, discharged by [A24](appendices/A-design-deltas.md) rather than by the M1 doc-05 pass it was owed to","body":"**U67 · ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-17, discharged by [A24](appendices/A-design-deltas.md)\n      rather than by the M1 doc-05 pass it was owed to.** `Cursor = line number + 1`\n      (§3.6) welded the cursor to the JSONL representation, and\n      [05](part2-engine/05-event-log-storage.md) plus B11 both plan a SQLite store by\n      M3, where the equivalence is simply false. The recommended restatement landed\n      almost verbatim — *the cursor is assigned by the store and is strictly\n      increasing; for the JSONL store it happens to equal line number + 1* — in §3.6\n      and in doc 05's `JsonlStore` row, with the addition that it is \"a property of\n      this backend and not an invariant another backend inherits\".\n      ⚠️ **The quoted `+1` half was itself reworded a day later, by U66's\n      `LogHeader`**: a header record on line 1 shifts any position-derived\n      arithmetic, so §3.6 now says the cursor is *derivable by arithmetic from the\n      record's position* rather than naming an offset. The invariant this item was\n      closed to establish is untouched and is in fact less representation-bound than\n      before. **Doc 05's `JsonlStore` row still says `+1` and is owed the same\n      rewording when M1 touches it** — it is an expanded doc, so hard rule 1 does not\n      forbid the fix, it merely was not in A1's scope.\n      **It closed early because B21 made it unavoidable rather than merely desirable.**\n      B21's index *is* `cursor → byte offset`, which is only worth having if the cursor\n      is not already the line number — so applying A24 as scoped would have left the\n      spec asserting an equivalence its own new index contradicts. Worth noting as a\n      pattern: the item was owed by M1, and a decision landed it in the design phase,\n      because **a new mechanism can retroactively invalidate an old sentence nobody was\n      re-reading**. A24's banner records it as scoping decision 1.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"**U67 · ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-17, discharged by [A24](appendices/A-design-deltas.md)","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U68","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"A token-estimate under-run has no first-class failure path —","body":"**U68 · A token-estimate under-run has no first-class failure path — ✅ DONE\n      2026-09-18, via A4, and closing it exposed a hole three items composed.**\n      §4.5 compacts at 80 % of the window using \"provider-reported usage from the last\n      turn plus a heuristic for the delta since\" — an estimate that can be low precisely\n      when the delta is large (a big tool result). The consequence is a provider\n      context-overflow **mid-turn, after the tool work is already paid for**, and\n      nothing in §4.2 or §6 says what happens then. ~~Needs an explicit path: treat\n      overflow as retryable, compact, and re-issue.~~ **That prescription could not be\n      transcribed as written, and the reason is worth keeping: A4's own amendment (a)\n      made compaction opt-in and *not* v1's default, so \"compact and re-issue\" is a\n      branch rather than the answer.** What landed is a retryable overflow with **two\n      branches** — compact-and-re-issue when compaction is enabled, otherwise terminate\n      through §4.2's guard set **with a remedy the user can act on** (`/compact`, a\n      hand-over, a fork) — stated in §6 as a provider-level path and in §4.5 as the\n      estimate's consequence, with §6 recording why overflow is *not* a 429 (backing off\n      and retrying the same request cannot succeed; the request itself is too large).\n      **The composition is the finding:** amendment (a) defers compaction, A3 landed the\n      hand-over as a *slot* not a mechanism, and this item's fix assumed compaction — so\n      v1 had no automatic response to a filling context and both candidates were\n      unavailable. Closing U68 therefore forced v1's honest default into the spec:\n      **stop and tell the user.** ⚠️ **Which makes U96 load-bearing** — two normative\n      sections now cite an undesigned mechanism. ~~Belongs in\n      [09](part2-engine/09-context-caching.md) and [10](part2-engine/10-compaction.md)~~\n      **The plan-side write-up is still owed by both**, and both are still summaries\n      (hard rule 1). ~~should be in A3's guard set~~ ✅ **it terminates through the guard\n      set**, which A3 had already landed. **Owed by M1** — discharged in the spec.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"**U68 · A token-estimate under-run has no first-class failure path — ✅ DONE","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U69","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Tool output is bounded on disk but unbounded in the fan-out","body":"**U69 · Tool output is bounded on disk but unbounded in the fan-out.** §4.3 caps\n      stored output at `max_output_bytes` with head/tail truncation and a blob —\n      good — but `ToolOutputDelta` streams uncapped into the per-session broadcast\n      channel and (pre-A1) into the log. A `bash` command emitting hundreds of MB of\n      stdout floods every subscriber's 256-envelope queue and makes §3.7's\n      drop-oldest fire constantly for reasons unrelated to a slow client. Needs a\n      volume/rate cap on the delta stream, distinct from the storage cap.\n      **Owed by M1** — the loop and the fan-out are both built there. **Scheduled:\n      M1.9** (§5's sub-step table, 2026-09-19), the loop row, because the cap is a rule\n      about *what a tool call may emit* rather than a property of the channel — the\n      channel it protects is M1.4's fan-out, and §3.7's drop-oldest stays the\n      slow-client remedy rather than becoming the volume valve. ▶️ **The deferral moves\n      this to the end of M1, and the interim exposure is nil rather than merely accepted** —\n      which is worth stating because the opposite looks true at a glance. The cap protects a\n      channel **M1.4** builds, so M1.4 does ship a fan-out whose only relief is drop-oldest;\n      but nothing can flood it until a tool exists to emit through, and the tool layer is\n      **M1.10**, i.e. the same deferred session this item is already scheduled against. So\n      M1.2–M1.8 build a bounded-fan-out channel with no unbounded producer attached.\n      ⚠️ **The condition that would break this**: if any tool ships before the loop session\n      — a `bash` or `fetch` written early to exercise the provider layer, say — this item\n      moves with it and becomes owed before that commit, not before M1.9.","status":"in_work","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U70","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§4.6's \"before the first mutating tool call of each assistant turn\" is ambiguous once tools run in parallel","body":"**U70 · §4.6's \"before the first mutating tool call of each assistant turn\" is\n      ambiguous once tools run in parallel.** §4.2 runs eligible calls concurrently,\n      so \"first\" is not well-defined and, more importantly, the snapshot must\n      complete before **any** of them start. Restate as a per-turn barrier. A2\n      (which replaces `parallel_safe` with a concurrency policy) should state the\n      interaction explicitly — it currently does not. **Owed by M1**, since A2 is an\n      M1 delta, even though checkpoints themselves are M2.\n      ⚠️ **Verified 2026-09-19: the assignment was missed, and this item's own\n      prescription needs refining before it can be applied.** A2 landed (`e5641d3`)\n      with **zero** added lines mentioning §4.6 or checkpoints, and A2's Appendix A\n      entry never mentions §4.6, checkpoint, snapshot or U70 — so the\n      inbound-assignment sweep could not have caught it: that sweep greps *the two\n      appendices*, and this item lives in TODO §6. The gap is load-bearing rather than\n      stylistic. §14.1 requires a test asserting \"**two `edit`s on different paths\n      overlap** — assert both were in flight at once\"; §4.3 makes `edit` `Keyed` on\n      paths, and `Keyed`'s own definition is \"the call **mutates** a resource it *can*\n      name from its arguments.\" So two mutating calls provably run concurrently and\n      §4.6's \"the first mutating tool call\" names no well-defined call. A snapshot taken\n      while another mutating call is already writing captures a half-mutated tree — a\n      corrupt restore point, not a slow one.\n      **Why \"restate as a per-turn barrier\" is not directly applicable:** this item\n      predates A3, which made dispatch *incremental* — \"a call is dispatched as soon as\n      it is complete in the stream, not when the stream ends.\" At turn start the engine\n      cannot know which later calls will mutate, so a literal per-turn barrier means\n      snapshotting before *every* turn including read-only ones, contradicting §4.6's\n      \"before the first *mutating* tool call.\" The available fix is to align §4.6 with\n      the boundary §3.6 already defines for the fsync — \"on the `ToolCall` that owns a\n      side effect, **before that side effect starts**\" — so the snapshot completes\n      before the first mutating call's side effects begin and every other mutating call\n      of that turn waits on it. §3.6's boundary list does **not** currently include the\n      checkpoint. ⚠️ Naming hazard for whoever writes it: the spec uses `Snapshot` for\n      the *fold* result (§3.5/§3.7, and `Snapshot.epoch` in §3.6) and \"snapshot\" for the\n      *shadow-git working-tree copy* (§4.6). They are unrelated, and §3.6's lone\n      \"snapshot\" hit is the former — so a grep for the checkpoint in §3.6 looks like it\n      found something and did not. **Now needs a decision as well as a transcription.**\n      **Scheduled: owed before\n      M1.9** (§5's sub-step table, 2026-09-19) — the barrier is an engine rule the loop\n      implements, so it cannot be written against an undefined one, while checkpoints\n      themselves stay M2 and doc 12 keeps its marker. ▶️ **The deferral moves this *with*\n      M1.9 rather than ahead of it, and to the end of M1** — so it is no longer one of the\n      two decisions blocking the way in, it is the *opening question of the loop session*,\n      which is where its analysis (incremental dispatch against a per-turn barrier) belongs\n      anyway. Nothing in M1.2–M1.8 implements §4.6, and checkpoints are M2, so no code is\n      written against the undefined barrier in the interim. **U93 overtakes it** as the first\n      decision M1 asks for.","status":"in_work","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U71","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`cost_usd: Option<f64>` will be wrong, not merely imprecise, until A4 lands —","body":"**U71 · `cost_usd: Option<f64>` will be wrong, not merely imprecise, until A4\n      lands — ✅ DONE 2026-09-18, via A4, taking the first of the two options this item\n      offered.** With prompt caching a turn has four rates — input, cache-write,\n      cache-read, output — and §3.2's `Usage` had no cache fields, so any cost computed\n      silently priced cached reads at full input rate. **A wrong cost number is worse\n      than an absent one**, because it will be believed. ~~Either A4 first, or `cost_usd`\n      stays `None` until it lands.~~ **A4 landed, and `Usage` is now a real struct with\n      all four counters** — which it had to become anyway, since `TurnFinished` and\n      `SubAgentFinished` had both been naming a type that existed nowhere in the spec\n      (the fourth instance of that class in two days; see the tracker's `f77`/`f79`).\n      §6 now states that cost is computed from **four rates matched to those four\n      counters**, with the argument for putting them on the *event* rather than leaving\n      them a §6 computation detail: a cached read priced at the input rate overstates\n      exactly the saving the cache exists to deliver. A provider reporting no cache\n      counters yields zeros, and a zero is honest — it says nothing was cached, which for\n      such a provider is true. **A second consequence this item did not predict:** the\n      four counters are what make the cache hit rate *measurable rather than inferred*,\n      which is what §14.2's benchmarking tier and **B18's T2** both need — so T2 gained\n      its input fields as a side effect of a cost-accuracy fix. (Floats for money is a\n      lesser, separate quibble, and **stays open as such** — it is not part of this item\n      and A4 did not address it.) **Owed by M1** — discharged in the spec.\n\n#### Wording that is doing more work than it can carry\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      lands — ✅ DONE 2026-09-18, via A4, taking the first of the two options this item","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U72","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The \"no cross-session memory\" non-goal is now load-bearing and imprecise","body":"**U72 · The \"no cross-session memory\" non-goal is now load-bearing and imprecise.**\n      §4.8's promoted `.dacode/plans/` boards, §5.6's `AGENTS.md` assembly and §12's\n      `index.json` are all cross-session state, and §4.8 already has to argue its\n      way out of the contradiction. The actual rule the design follows is better and\n      should simply be stated: **nothing accumulates without a human-reviewed\n      diff.** \"No memory\" is the slogan; that is the principle. A12 (the §16/§1\n      bookkeeping pass) is the right place.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U73","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§5.4's six-line workflow example uses two different addressing schemes","body":"**U73 · §5.4's six-line workflow example uses two different addressing schemes** —\n      `{{ steps.plan.result }}` and `{{ steps[2].result }}` — which is cosmetic on\n      its own but is a symptom of the already-tracked \"pick the templating approach\"\n      follow-up. Whichever template language is chosen, the example should use one\n      form.\n\n#### Process observations — about the plan, not the design\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U74","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The meta-risk is now that analysis depth has outrun feedback","body":"**U74 · The meta-risk is now that analysis depth has outrun feedback.** 18\n      decisions, 21 deltas, ~2 900 lines of appendix, a 1 000-line board — and zero\n      lines of Rust. The *quality* is high and the counter-evidence discipline is\n      genuinely unusual (retracted benchmark figures, a corrected delta dependency,\n      a defect patched back into the artefact rather than only logged). But every\n      decision so far has been made against surveys and reasoning, never against a\n      running system, and that is the one input the process cannot supply to itself.\n      **Concrete recommendation: batch-sign the four high-confidence M1 blockers\n      (B3, B5, B7, B14 — all `accept`-recommended at high confidence) in one pass\n      and scaffold M0 now.** M0 is gated on nothing. Its value is not the code; it\n      is that \"verification is design consistency\" becomes \"does it compile\".\n      (This overlaps the existing \"worth deciding whether to batch the votes\" item\n      above — recorded again because an independent reviewer reached it from the\n      other direction.)","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U75","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The backtick escape did not protect `attention.py`'s action matcher — FIXED by declaring the action type instead of detecting it","body":"**U75 · ⚠️ The backtick escape did not protect `attention.py`'s action matcher —\n      FIXED by declaring the action type instead of detecting it.** The matcher is\n      gone: Appendix B now carries an `attention-meta` HTML-comment block with one\n      `action:` line per non-vote decision (exactly one today, `B12=measure`),\n      absence means `vote`, and two new hygiene checks validate it — an `action:`\n      line naming a decision that does not exist is reported rather than silently\n      ignored, and an A/B id in a **Gates** cell is reported instead of being read\n      as a plan-doc number. `norm_docs` also strips A/B ids now, so the\n      mis-derivation cannot happen even if the authoring error recurs. Both checks\n      were negative-tested (deliberately broken, confirmed to fail, restored), not\n      just run green. **This is the \"machine-readable front matter\" fix that the\n      entry below argued for four times — done for the one field that kept\n      misfiring, not for the whole table.** The original finding follows, kept as\n      the audit trail.\n      *Original:* **the backtick escape did not protect the action matcher,\n      and this pass reproduced the bug to find out.** Writing B18 mislabelled it\n      `measure` on the first run, because `action_for_decision` scans a decision's\n      **detail section** for the phrases B12 uses to mark itself — and B18's detail\n      necessarily *quotes* B12 while explaining that it unblocks it. This is the\n      f19/f20/f33 class again (\"writing about a machine-readable form makes you an\n      instance of it\"), but with a new wrinkle: the documented escape is inline\n      code, and **this matcher strips `` ` `` before matching** (a deliberate fix,\n      because B12's own signal is emphasised). So backticking is *not* an escape\n      here. Worked around by rewording B18 to avoid the literal phrases, with a\n      note in its detail saying why. **The durable fix is the one already named\n      for the appendix tables: machine-readable front matter instead of scraping\n      prose** — an `action: vote` field cannot be triggered by a quotation. Same\n      run, a second instance of the same class: putting *\"unblocks B12\"* in the\n      **Gates** column made `norm_docs` read `B12` as plan-doc **12** — the\n      checkpoints doc — and derive its milestone from that. **Rule: the Gates cell\n      takes doc numbers only; B-numbers belong in prose.** Both were caught by\n      checking the tool's output against known ground truth, not by its exit code\n      — f35's lesson, third confirmation. **And then this very entry became a\n      fourth**: its first draft named that doc's milestone inside a short\n      parenthetical, which is precisely the shape f35 taught the milestone\n      heuristic to match, so an item gating nothing was promoted to P1. Four\n      instances across three different matchers is no longer a run of bad luck —\n      it is the argument for machine-readable front matter, made four times.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U76","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§6 has grown into a ~600-line append-only stream with no item ids — DONE","body":"**U76 · §6 has grown into a ~600-line append-only stream with no item ids —\n      DONE**, which was the same failure mode §16.6 and §4.8's gate are about: a\n      list that is always long gets skimmed, and then a real finding is invisible\n      inside it — exactly how §16.6 was lost. `attention.py` was the right first\n      response; stable ids were the next, and every item below now carries one\n      (`U1`–`U79`), so an item can be *cited and closed* rather than restated.\n      The cost this entry predicted was real and is handled: an id is another\n      mechanically-read form, so **it sits inside the leading bold run, after the\n      checkbox**, and flipping `[ ]` to `[x]` cannot change it. `attention.py`\n      reads the id instead of the line number it used to synthesise — line numbers\n      move on every edit, which is why citing `§6:1071` was already useless by the\n      next session. Two hygiene checks guard it: ids must be **unique across closed\n      items too** (a reused number silently re-points an old citation), and every\n      item must have one. Both negative-tested. **Never renumber**: a new item takes\n      the next unused number wherever it sits, so gaps are expected and meaningful.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U77","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Committing is now a required step of the delivery cycle","body":"**U77 · Committing is now a required step of the delivery cycle**, not\n      explicit-ask-only. The old rule (\"never run `git commit` … without explicit\n      instruction\") meant a finished delivery could sit uncommitted until the user\n      noticed — which is exactly what happened to the B1 pass. Changed at eight\n      sites: `.agents/roles/_common.md` (the preamble every agent carries),\n      `.agents/roles/orchestrator.md` loop step 7, `.agents/orchestrator.md` loop\n      step 8 **and** its Review Exemptions paragraph — which *contradicted* step 8,\n      saying docs \"must be committed cleanly\" while step 8 said \"only when\n      explicitly asked\" — `AGENTS.md` (Never-Do list, plus a retitled \"Committing —\n      a required step of the cycle\" section), `agent-rules.yaml` `never_do`,\n      `.agents/skill-base.md`, `.agents/implement.md`'s checklist, and a new\n      \"Committing\" section in `.agents/roles/implementer.md`. All 5 adapters\n      regenerated. **Who commits is now explicit:** the orchestrator, after dual\n      review, for code; a directly-invoked worker once its own verification\n      passes; read-only roles (reviewers, explore) never.\n- **Deliberately NOT relaxed:** `git push`, `git reset`, `--amend`, force-push,\n      branch deletion, `git config` edits and `--no-verify` all remain\n      explicit-instruction-only. A local commit is reversible; those are not, or\n      they bypass a gate. The user asked to drop \"explicitly asked\" for *commits*\n      — this is the narrower reading, flagged at the time rather than assumed.\n- **`t25` is still deferred, but only its `TODO.md` half.** The exit-gate contract\n  now has its commit step written down; what remains owed is the orchestrator\n  refusing sign-off until the board reflects the delivery, which still waits\n  for one real implementation cycle.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U78","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`attention.py` ranked three M1 blockers as if they gated nothing — FIXED, and it is the sixth instance of U53","body":"**U78 · `attention.py` ranked three M1 blockers as if they gated nothing —\n      FIXED, and it is the sixth instance of U53.** Answering \"what is next\" meant\n      reading the prioritized view, and the view contradicted §1 of this board:\n      **A2, A3 and A4 all rendered P3, \"no open decision dependency\"**, while §1\n      names all three under *Blocking M1*. A delta inherited its milestone from the\n      still-open decisions blocking it, so a delta depending on nothing inherited\n      nothing and fell to the bottom of the view. A1 escaped only because B3\n      happens to block it. `--max-priority 1` therefore hid three M1 blockers, and\n      the P0 count was understated by seven.\n      The same rule was wrong in the other direction on three more rows: **A7, A10\n      and A15** took the milestone of whatever decision blocks them, which answers\n      when a *prerequisite* is needed rather than when the delta's own subject\n      matter is owed. A15 edits the storage sections (doc 05, M1) but rendered M3\n      because the codec decision blocking it is an M3 concern.\n      **Fixed by declaring, not detecting** — the move U75 made for action types.\n      Appendix A gained an `attention-meta` block carrying a `gates:` line for the\n      nine deltas derivation got wrong, under the rule *a delta is owed by the\n      milestone of the plan docs covering the spec sections it edits*. Absence\n      still means \"inherit from the decisions blocking it\", so the change is\n      additive. **A14 was the ninth, and it was caught in review, not by me** — §1\n      above names it as an M1 blocker, the rule agrees (it adds durable events to\n      the protocol sections, and doc 03 is M1), and it had been left inheriting M2\n      from B13. **A13 deliberately opts out of the rule** and the block says why:\n      it edits an M0-covered section but its subject is two crates B11 defers to M3\n      and M5, so the heuristic would have raised the priority of work that cannot\n      usefully happen yet. The rule is a heuristic, and where it disagrees with the\n      substance the substance wins — recorded rather than silently encoded.\n      In isolation the declarations moved P0 from 29 to 36 at a constant total of\n      93, which is the evidence that rows moved rather than vanished; the delivered\n      tree reports **90 open items at 36 P0**, because this pass also closed four\n      (U30, U31, U50, U76) and opened one (U79).\n      **Two hygiene checks guard it, both negative-tested:** a `gates:` line naming\n      an absent or already-applied delta is reported, and — the actual regression\n      guard — **any open delta left with no milestone at all is reported**, which\n      is the failure that was silent before. Broken three ways (unknown id,\n      out-of-range milestone, removed declaration), confirmed to fail, restored.\n      **Method note, because it is U53's lesson a sixth time:** this was found by\n      comparing the tool's output against §1 above, not by its exit code.\n      `attention.py` exited 0 and printed a confident, wrong table.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U79","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Raised in priority by B16: the tools are now *permanent*, and permanent untested code is worse than transitional untested code","body":"**U79 · ⚠️ Raised in priority by B16: the tools are now *permanent*, and\n      permanent untested code is worse than transitional untested code.** §2.4 says\n      authoring tooling is not awaiting a port, so there is no future Rust rewrite\n      that inherits `cargo test` for free — this is the only implementation these\n      checks will ever have, and §14 now records the gap in the spec rather than\n      leaving it implicit. *Original entry:* `tools/` has no test runner, so every check is verified by hand and\n      the verification is not repeatable.** This pass added **eighteen** negative\n      and edge-case probes across three tools — three ways to break the new\n      `gates:` declarations, four cases for the no-link convention, two for the §6\n      ids, six for the `standing` status, and three more that the review round\n      forced (a reported line number, a run from another directory, a FAIL row whose\n      detail contradicted it) — and every one was an inline shell one-off: mutate a\n      tracked file, run, eyeball, restore. None of it is committed, so the next\n      person who edits `attention.py` gets no regression signal at all, and the\n      *only* reason the defects it finds are believed is that someone re-ran the\n      experiment. **That dual review then found six defects in the new code and five\n      doc claims that did not reproduce — after all eighteen probes had passed — is\n      the argument for a runner, and for review, in one sentence.**\n      Two concrete costs already paid, both now in `.agents/pitfalls-live.md`:\n      **P-LIVE-001** (`$?` after a pipe reports the last command, which twice\n      reported a failing gate as passing) and **P-LIVE-002** (`set -e` aborted a\n      negative-test script before its restore step, leaving a deliberately-corrupted\n      tracked file on disk). Both are symptoms of asserting on exit codes in shell\n      rather than in a harness.\n      The evidence that this matters is the bug record itself: **U53** lists four\n      `attention.py` defects, **U75** a fifth, **U78** a sixth — all found by\n      running the tool against ground truth, none by a test, and each one a\n      *silently wrong* table rather than a crash. A doc-scraping tool fails by\n      looking plausible.\n      **Shape it should take, and the constraint that decides the language:** these\n      tools must stay runnable **before the workspace builds**, or the docs cannot be\n      validated during the design phase — which is the same argument **U59** makes\n      for `check-links.py` staying Python permanently. So a Python test runner over\n      `tools/` (fixtures + expected table rows + expected exit codes), not Rust\n      `xtask` tests. Interacts with **B16/A20**: if the tools move to `xtask`, the\n      runner moves with them, and `tasks.py`/`attention.py` are the two B16 says to\n      *retire and dogfood* rather than port. Cheap enough to do now, and it pays for\n      itself the first time a regex is edited.\n      **This entry is its own seventh data point, and it happened while writing it.**\n      The first draft placed a milestone tag after a gating preposition, and\n      `milestone_in_prose` matched the construction, promoting an item that gates no\n      milestone to **P0**. U75 records the same thing happening to itself; U53\n      records it happening four times before that. Reworded rather than left wrong —\n      but note what rewording means: **prose is being written to satisfy a regex**,\n      which is the scraper's cost made visible.\n      **Describing the trigger reproduces it, and here the backtick escape does not\n      help.** Quoting the offending two words in the paragraph that reports them\n      re-armed the matcher — twice, because the correction quoted them again. That is\n      the U30/f19 rule a third time: describing a machine-readable form makes the\n      description an instance of it. Unlike `check-links.py` and the depth grep,\n      `milestone_in_prose` receives text with only `**` stripped — **inline code is\n      not removed before matching** — so backticking a milestone construction is\n      *not* an escape in this tool. U30's claim that backticking is \"the supported\n      escape\" holds for the two matchers it names and not for this one.\n      The durable fix is the one U53 names: §6 items declare a milestone the way\n      Appendix A's deltas now do, instead of having one inferred from a sentence —\n      and until then, a §6 entry that names a milestone must expect to be ranked by\n      it.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U80","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§2.4 requires an authoring-tool disposition register, and it has no permanent home — DONE, as [Appendix G](appendices/G-authoring-tools.md)","body":"**U80 · §2.4 requires an authoring-tool disposition register, and it has no\n      permanent home — DONE, as [Appendix G](appendices/G-authoring-tools.md).**\n      The recommended option was taken: a new appendix mirroring\n      [F](appendices/F-c-dependencies.md)'s shape — policy banner declaring itself\n      the register §2.4 points at, a class test, an enforcement section, the ledger\n      with struck-not-deleted rows, and the evidence. Five tool rows plus\n      `agent-rules.yaml`, transcribed from B16's dossier rather than re-decided.\n      **No spec edit was needed**, which is the symmetry that settled the fork:\n      §2.3 does not name Appendix F either, so F declares itself §2.3's register and\n      G declares itself §2.4's. Three consequences worth naming:\n      - **G carries no summary marker and counts as expanded** (§4 moved 41 → 42\n        content docs, 12 → 13 expanded, 29 summaries unchanged). That is a deliberate\n        asymmetry with doc 33, and §4 now records the distinction: a register of\n        *already-decided* policy holds no speculative depth, which is what hard\n        rule 1 is actually about.\n      - **Doc 01 points at G rather than carrying the table**, so there is one\n        register and not two copies to drift. Updated in §1, §3's A20 row and §5's\n        M0 entry, plus B16's banner and resolved-table row and A20's scoping\n        decisions 5 and 6 — all five of which asserted, in the present tense, that\n        the table still lived in the dossier.\n      - **The enforcement gap is now visible and still open — U81.**\n      *Original entry, kept because the audit trail is the rule:* The spec says every authoring tool carries a recorded\n      disposition — permanent, or retire on a named trigger — \"in a register\n      maintained alongside the implementation plan\", deliberately mirroring how §2.3\n      points at [Appendix F](appendices/F-c-dependencies.md) for class-1\n      dependencies rather than inlining the ledger. **That register does not exist.**\n      The confirmed table currently lives in B16's dossier, which is an *audit-trail\n      document*: it is struck through, superseded in place, and read for reasoning\n      rather than consulted as current state. A table that is the normative register\n      for a spec requirement should not live in a file whose convention is that its\n      content is historical.\n      Two consequences if it is left there. **A new tool has nowhere to be\n      registered**, so §2.4's enforcement (\"a tool with no row is out of policy\") is\n      unenforceable — the same shape as a dependency policy with no allowlist. And\n      **the retirement triggers are not where they fire**: `agents-sync.py` retires\n      at the M2 dogfood migration and `tasks.py` retires onto §4.8 once §16.6\n      settles, but neither M2's task list nor §16.6's resolution will think to look\n      inside B16's dossier. (M2's entry was patched to carry its trigger; that is a\n      workaround, not the register.)\n      **Options:** a new Appendix G mirroring F's shape (ledger, struck-not-deleted,\n      one row per tool with disposition + trigger + the §2.4 class it belongs to);\n      or a section in [01](part1-foundations/01-workspace-scaffolding.md) when it is\n      deepened at M0, since that is already where B16's \"on sign-off\" note sends the\n      table. **Recommend the appendix**, for the reason F won: a register consulted\n      by a CI gate should not be inside a milestone doc that is read once. Cheap\n      either way, and it should land before M0 scaffolds `xtask`, since M0 is when a\n      second class of tooling first exists to register.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U81","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Appendix G's register has no mechanical check, so §2.4's \"a tool with no row is out of policy\" is still prose","body":"**U81 · Appendix G's register has no mechanical check, so §2.4's \"a tool with\n      no row is out of policy\" is still prose.** G.2 states the gap and specifies\n      the check rather than leaving it implicit: enumerate the executables under\n      `tools/` and require each to appear in G.3's first column, **both directions**\n      — an unrowed tool *and* a row naming an absent tool, the second being what\n      catches a deletion that skipped the register. `attention.py`'s hygiene block\n      is the natural home; it already runs five doc-shape checks, three of which\n      validate a declared field (Appendix A's `gates:` markers, Appendix B's\n      `action:` markers, §6's ids), and this would be a fourth of that kind. The\n      matching is **filename-against-table-cell, not prose-scraping** — which is the\n      rule U53/U75/U78 argued for six times. Cheap, negative-testable in one pass\n      (add a dummy script, confirm FAIL, remove), and it pays for itself the first\n      time someone drops a helper into `tools/`. **Interacts with U79:** the check\n      would be more permanent untested Python, so if U79's runner lands first, this\n      becomes one of its fixtures instead of another hand-verified probe. The CI\n      half rides with the skeleton [31](part6-quality/31-ci-milestones.md) specifies.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U82","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Reading doc 01 against the spec found two instructions an M0 implementer would have followed wrongly — FIXED, both the U43 class","body":"**U82 · Reading doc 01 against the spec found two instructions an M0\n      implementer would have followed wrongly — FIXED, both the U43 class.** Neither\n      is a deepening, so doc 01 keeps its summary marker and §4's count is unchanged.\n      1. **\"Workspace `Cargo.toml` with the ten crates of §2\" stopped being true when\n         A20 put `xtask` in §2.1's tree.** The tree is ten `dacode*` crates *plus*\n         `xtask` = **eleven members**, and `xtask` is not one of B11's two deferred\n         crates — so the existing \"gains an eleventh only if B11 resolves the other\n         way\" sentence was about a twelfth. An implementer following the doc would\n         have scaffolded ten and created no `xtask`, which is precisely the crate\n         §2.4 exists to specify. Restated as *members* vs *crates*, with the\n         previous wording recorded rather than silently replaced.\n      2. **The deny-lint list omitted `clippy::unreachable`.** It named four lints\n         where [04](part1-foundations/04-error-model.md), §5 above and\n         `.agents/roles/_rust.md` all name five. The doc that *sets up* the gate\n         (Part I.1) was the one place the set was incomplete, so M0 would have\n         shipped a weaker lint than the three docs that cite it. Corrected, with a\n         line saying why the count matters.\n      **Generalises, and it is the third instance:** a doc at summary depth is still\n      read as an instruction by whoever implements its milestone, so *factual*\n      drift in it is a defect even though *depth* is deliberately deferred. U43 was\n      the first (twelve crates vs ten), the §2.4 pointer the second. When a delta\n      edits a spec section a summary doc cites, the citing doc needs a consistency\n      pass in the same change — A20 edited §2.1 and §2.2 and did not.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U83","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The JSON crate's future repo — ANSWERED 2026-09-16: it is `dacode/dacode-json`, and there is no second rename","body":"**U83 · The JSON crate's future repo — ANSWERED 2026-09-16: it is\n      `dacode/dacode-json`, and there is no second rename.** Answering \"how does\n      `dacode-json` enter the tree\" (M0's scoping question d) the user first named\n      `https://github.com/dacode/dacode-codec`; asked whether that meant a rename of\n      the crate, the answer was *\"sorry, my bad, it's `dacode-json`\"*. So\n      **`t34`'s original target stands** — right about the org *and* the repo — and\n      its supersession note is withdrawn. **Asking was worth one round trip.** Had\n      the `codec` name been acted on, it would have meant a second sweep of the U18\n      class (498 occurrences across 78 files in the JSON repo, 136 across 27 here),\n      touching `src/raw.rs:70`'s wire-visible serde sentinel `TOKEN`, and U8 puts\n      the first in-tree consumer at M1's protocol crate — i.e. the window in which\n      it is free was already closing. Nothing was renamed on the strength of a URL,\n      which is the only reason this was a question rather than a defect.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U84","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Identifier privacy — the repo-side instruction is landed, the product-side rule is [B19](appendices/B-decisions.md)","body":"**U84 · Identifier privacy — the repo-side instruction is landed, the\n      product-side rule is [B19](appendices/B-decisions.md).** The user's note:\n      *\"obfuscating any ids in the commands via shell vars or specially crafted\n      file names.\"* Split deliberately, because the two halves have different\n      authority. **The instruction half needed no sign-off** — it governs the\n      agents working on *this* repository, so it is now a bullet in\n      [`.agents/roles/_common.md`](../../.agents/roles/_common.md), the shared\n      preamble every generated agent carries, and all five adapters were\n      regenerated. **The product half is a decision**, and B19 is where it lives:\n      the spec has exactly one redaction concept (§8.2's \"secrets redacted\" on\n      `/api/config`) and no general one, while ids flow into three surfaces that\n      retain or export them — the append-only log (§3.6), the provider transcript\n      *including the cached prefix* (§13.4), and the OS process list. The\n      load-bearing argument is that **redaction is a rewrite and the log is\n      append-only**, so prevention before the event is created is the only control\n      that works — which is why it belongs in §4.3/§4.4 and not in a doc. Same\n      family as U61 and U65, and the reasoning that made U37 patch\n      `Verify::Command` into §4.8 instead of logging it.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U85","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Remote agent execution and swarms — captured as [B20](appendices/B-decisions.md), which is *not* vote-ready","body":"**U85 · Remote agent execution and swarms — captured as\n      [B20](appendices/B-decisions.md), which is *not* vote-ready.** The user's\n      note: a sandboxing feature to come later, plus agent execution on another\n      host, *\"so, a swarm of agents could participate in one workflow.\"* What\n      makes this cheaper than it sounds is that the spec already describes most of\n      a swarm member: §8.1's `attach <url>` is a remote client over the same\n      protocol, §7.2 makes extensions ordinary clients, §5.3 already says a\n      child's **only outbound channel is its final message**, and §3.7 already\n      makes a lossy channel safe. So the recommendation is to reuse the protocol\n      and never build a second one — **but a sixth reference survey is owed\n      first**, because what the four harnesses do about distributed execution is\n      unknown, and the\n      [sandboxing-and-checkpoints](../../.agents/knowledge/reference-harnesses/sandboxing-and-checkpoints.md)\n      digest's openclaw-Docker finding is evidence about *sandboxing*, not about\n      swarms, and must not be quoted as the other. Declared `action: session` in\n      Appendix B's `attention-meta` block, so `attention.py` renders it as a\n      session rather than a vote — the second non-vote in the appendix after B12,\n      for the opposite reason.\n      **Four things must not wait for the survey**, because all four are\n      wire-format questions that are free before golden fixtures exist and\n      expensive after (A1's argument): a session id able to name another host\n      (decide *with* A1), §8.4's capability split (U62 widened), the executing\n      host recorded in §4.8's `Verify::Command` evidence, and **one `seq` assigner\n      per session log stated as an invariant** — §3.7's resync assumes a single\n      assigner and §3.6 welds `Cursor` to the JSONL line number (U67), so two hosts\n      appending to one session breaks both, and gap-detection-from-`seq` cannot tell\n      a dropped envelope from a concurrent assigner.\n      ⚠️ **The user's ordering — sandboxing first — is the correct one.** B7\n      establishes that the glob allow-list is advisory, and B7's part 4 with U63\n      put the real boundary at *the §4.7 worktree plus the OS* — a worktree bounds\n      **where writes land**, not **what a process may do**, so it contains an edit\n      and not a `curl | sh`. Remote execution also removes the mitigation the local\n      case quietly leans on, that the blast radius is the user's own machine, so\n      remote execution without an OS-level sandbox is remote arbitrary code\n      execution *by design*. B7's reserved `SandboxBackend` seam is what B20 spends.\n      And B19 must settle first: a swarm on N hosts retains every identifier in N\n      append-only logs.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U86","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Sandboxing polarity — ANSWERED 2026-09-16, and the answer is sharper than the question: the sandbox is a *session attribute fixed at creation*, `yolo` is a *mode toggled during the session*, and the TUI shows both as colour","body":"**U86 · Sandboxing polarity — ANSWERED 2026-09-16, and the answer is sharper\n      than the question: the sandbox is a *session attribute fixed at creation*,\n      `yolo` is a *mode toggled during the session*, and the TUI shows both as\n      colour.** Amended into [B7](appendices/B-decisions.md) *before* sign-off; the\n      flagged ambiguity is resolved. Original entry follows. The user's note: *\"sandboxing\n      should have opt-in configuration. `yolo` is opt-out.\"* Captured as a dated\n      amendment banner plus a **fifth part of B7's recommendation** (\"Four parts\"\n      → five), following the B4/B6/B1 precedent of amending an unsigned entry\n      rather than reinterpreting a signed one. **The reading taken is two axes, both\n      explicit, neither a silent default:** the sandbox is off unless configured —\n      which the evidence already supports, since openclaw's ships `mode: \"off\"` and\n      §4.7's worktree is *already* per-session opt-in via `dacode --sandbox`\n      (§11) — and `yolo` is how a user deliberately opts out of the guardrails,\n      with §4.4's default staying `ask`.\n      Three consequences, the third of which is **new work**:\n      1. **The honesty fix extends to the mode indicator.** A `yolo` *and*\n         unsandboxed session must be announced on screen, where §4.7 already shows\n         the branch — B7's own theme (never imply a guarantee the design does not\n         keep) applied to the status line instead of to `bash:readonly`.\n      2. **B20's sandbox prerequisite became a rule.** Opt-in *locally*, where the\n         blast radius is the user's own machine; **mandatory remotely** — a member\n         on another host either has a sandbox configured or is refused. Opt-in\n         configuration and a swarm are only compatible because the requirement\n         tightens with distance.\n      3. ⚠️ **Layered config could silently drop a parent's sandbox — U64 with\n         higher stakes, owed to A22 item 3, and note the conditional.** §4.4's\n         `[permissions]` block is documented as \"global defaults, overridable per\n         agent and per session\" and its resolution order puts **agent config above\n         project and user config**, so an agent-level value wins. §5.3 says a child\n         *inherits* cwd/sandbox today and §4.4 has **no sandbox key yet**, so the\n         hazard is one this amendment creates the moment sandboxing becomes\n         per-agent configurable — not one that exists. U64/A22 item 3 already fixes\n         scopes by **intersection, not `min(mode)`**; whatever sandbox key B7 adds\n         must be inside what intersects and never something a child may relax.\n      ~~**Still open because of one flagged ambiguity:** whether `yolo` *is* the\n      sandbox's off switch (no separate flag) or a second axis.~~ ✅ **Resolved: two\n      axes, confirmed — and the distinction that settles it is *when each is\n      decided*, not only what it controls.** The sandbox is set at\n      `CreateSession { sandbox: Option<SandboxSpec> }` and is **not mutable**; the\n      mode is changed by `SetPermMode` / `/mode` at any time. **Both shapes are\n      already in §3.3 and §4.7, so this ratifies the spec and needs no edit** —\n      what it adds is a prohibition: the sandbox must never also become a layered\n      `[permissions]` value under §4.4, because that is exactly what would make it\n      per-agent and reopen consequence 3.\n      **Two consequences now recorded rather than open.** (i) Consequence 3 is\n      **dissolved**: a child inherits the session's sandbox (§5.3), there is no\n      per-agent key to weaken, and **A22 item 3 / U64 does not need the sandbox\n      added to its intersection rule**. (ii) A session started unsandboxed\n      **cannot be retro-contained** — containment means forking a new session, and\n      adding a `SetSandbox` command would undo the decision.\n      **The indicator's form is settled as colour, and one caveat travels with it:\n      U89.**","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      sandbox's off switch (no separate flag) or a second axis.~~ ✅ **Resolved: two","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U87","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"A verification command written into a plan doc could never fire, and the gate it guarded fails silently — FOUND running M0.2's negative tests, fixed in the same pass","body":"**U87 · ⚠️ A verification command written into a plan doc could never fire,\n      and the gate it guarded fails silently — FOUND running M0.2's negative tests,\n      fixed in the same pass.** Doc 04 §4.8 documented\n      `grep -L 'workspace = true' crates/*/Cargo.toml` as the check for a member\n      that forgot to inherit `[workspace.lints.clippy]`. **It cannot fire**: every\n      manifest also carries `version.workspace = true`, `edition.workspace = true`\n      and three more, so the pattern always matches and no file is ever listed. It\n      was written into the doc, read by two reviewers, and never executed until\n      there was a workspace to execute it against.\n      **What it was guarding is worse than a style drift.** With the `[lints]`\n      stanza deleted from a member, adding an `unwrap()` to that crate produced\n      **zero clippy errors** under `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets`: the\n      entire deny set vanishes for that member and every gate stays green, because\n      no rustc lint says \"you forgot to inherit the lints\". Inheritance is opt-in\n      per member and its absence is silent — which makes doc 01 §1.4's \"a missing\n      stanza is a review finding\" insufficient on its own, with no CI provider to\n      run anything (decision e).\n      **Fixed three ways:** the command is anchored on the stanza\n      (`grep -A1 '^\\[lints\\]' \"$f\" | grep -q '^workspace = true'`) and corrected in\n      doc 04 with the vacuous form named; the check is now **`xtask check-lints`**,\n      a committed subcommand with a golden test rather than a one-liner nobody runs;\n      and it earned its keep immediately by catching a real defect in the\n      scaffolding it was written for — `crates/dacode/src/main.rs` was missing the\n      relaxation block. Recorded as **P-LIVE-004**, with P-LIVE-003 beside it (the\n      zsh word-splitting trap that malformed two generated manifests).\n      **Generalises, and it is the third time:** a verification command in a doc\n      must be *executed* once before it is trusted — a check that has never fired is\n      not known to be able to. P-LIVE-001 (`$?` after a pipe reporting a failing\n      gate as passing) and f17 (`cargo fmt --check` reporting 0 hunks because\n      `cargo metadata` had failed) are the same family: **a zero from a tool that\n      did not really run is not a clean result.** It is also U79's argument with a\n      new data point — the tools are permanent, and permanent unverified checks are\n      worse than none, because they are believed.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U88","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The class-1 dependency scan cannot attribute, so it breaks the moment a real C dependency arrives — and the first one is M1's TLS","body":"**U88 · ⚠️ The class-1 dependency scan cannot attribute, so it breaks the\n      moment a real C dependency arrives — and the first one is M1's TLS.** ✅ **DONE\n      2026-09-17 — `tools/dep-audit.py` landed before M1's first external dependency,\n      as this item required.** It attributes each class-1 marker to its **introducer**\n      (`cc ← ring ← dacode-providers` → row `ring`, never `cc`), keys\n      `gates/class1-allowlist.txt` on that introducer and cross-checks the crate is\n      named in Appendix F §F.3's *table rows* (not its prose, so a name listed only to\n      be forbidden does not count), and prints the chain. New `gates/class1-exempt.txt`\n      holds §F.1's class-2 platform bindings (`windows-sys`), reported not rowed. The\n      scan is **wide** (`--target all --all-features --locked`) so a cfg-gated or\n      optional class-1 dep cannot hide and the committed resolve is never rewritten;\n      markers are pinned `name@version` so a two-version crate cannot make\n      `cargo tree -i` ambiguous; and **any cargo failure exits non-zero rather than\n      auditing an empty graph**. Both suppression surfaces are guarded:\n      `validate_allowlist` rejects rowing `cc`/`cmake`/`bindgen`; `validate_exempt`\n      rejects a build tool, a non-`*-sys` name (so `ring` cannot be exempted), an\n      exempt+allowlisted contradiction and a §F.3 class-1 debt (**a fifth rule arrived\n      with U94 below: a row Appendix F §F.1's class-2 register does not name**); and\n      attribution fails an exempt crate caught *introducing* a build tool (it compiles C,\n      so it is class-1). Carries an Appendix G row (§2.4) and a `--self-test` (U79's first\n      committed test over `tools/`) replaying committed `cargo tree` recordings *and*\n      building a throwaway offline workspace to drive the real cargo glue (**two parts\n      when written; U94 added two more — the committed Appendix F and gates files are\n      validated against the gate's own rules, and `live_audit` is driven against a\n      throwaway repo, since it is the only place the registers are wired**) —\n      **mutation-tested**: dropping any of `--target all`/`--all-features`/`--locked`/\n      the `@version` pin/the ASCII `|-- ` mapping/any exempt guard fails it.\n      **Deviations from this item's literal text:** the committed fixture is recorded\n      `cargo tree` *output*, not a \"fixture lockfile\" — `Cargo.lock` carries no\n      normal-vs-build edge kinds, which the marker definition needs; and the exempt set\n      is a new `gates/` file, not folded into the allowlist, because the allowlist\n      mirrors §F.3 *debt* while exempt mirrors §F.1 *class-2*. **Dual-reviewed four\n      rounds** — round 1 found two high-severity silent-pass holes (a swallowed\n      `cargo tree -i` failure → exit 0; the host/default-feature blind spot), round 3\n      the unguarded exempt route — all closed and mutation-locked. ~~One narrow residual\n      is **U94**~~ ✅ **U94 closed 2026-09-17** by the §F.1 class-2 mirror; its entry\n      follows this one. Original entry follows. Found by\n      *running the M1 scenario* rather than by reading the command, which is U87's\n      lesson one step earlier: with `ring` resolved and its predicted allowlist row\n      present, doc 01 §1.5's scan still prints **`cc`** — ring's build dependency —\n      and fails. `--prefix none` throws the tree away, so the output is a bare list\n      of names with nothing saying who pulled each one in. The only way to silence\n      that with the flat scan is to allowlist `cc` itself, **which then blinds the\n      gate to every future `cc`-based dependency** — turning a policy check into a\n      formality exactly when it starts having something to check.\n      **The allowlist must key on the introducer, not on the marker**, which means\n      `cargo tree -i <marker> -e normal,build` per marker and a real script rather\n      than a shell pipeline; per §2.4 a script under `tools/` needs an\n      [Appendix G](appendices/G-authoring-tools.md) row, and per U79 it needs tests,\n      because permanent untested Python is the debt B16 made permanent. Doc 01 §1.5\n      already anticipated the script (\"if either check ever needs to become a\n      script, that script needs a row there\"); U88 is the trigger arriving.\n      **Recommended shape, on the reliability-first principle:** attribute, do not\n      list. A `tools/dep-audit.py` that takes the marker set from `cargo tree`, runs\n      `cargo tree -i <marker> -e normal,build` for each, and requires the **top-level\n      introducer** — not the marker — to be rowed in `gates/class1-allowlist.txt` and\n      Appendix F §F.3, printing the chain (`cc ← ring ← dacode-providers`) so a\n      reviewer sees who is responsible rather than a bare `cc`. Needs an Appendix G row\n      (§2.4) and tests (U79), **including a committed fixture lockfile carrying a known\n      `*-sys`** — the fire path cannot be tested against the live tree, which has no\n      class-1 dependency and will not have one until M1.\n      **Must land before M1's provider HTTP dependency, not after it** — a gate that\n      is wrong when the first class-1 row is added teaches everyone to route around\n      it. ~~Until then the scan is correct and empty, and its fire path is verified on\n      synthetic input only, which doc 01 §1.5 now says plainly.~~ **Superseded by the\n      delivery: the fire path is verified on committed `cargo tree` recordings *and* a\n      throwaway offline cargo workspace built live in `--self-test` — not synthetic\n      input alone — and doc 01 §1.5 now says so.**\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      moment a real C dependency arrives — and the first one is M1's TLS.** ✅ **DONE","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U89","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The sandbox/`yolo` indicator is colour-only, and colour is a fragile channel for a *security* state","body":"**U89 · The sandbox/`yolo` indicator is colour-only, and colour is a fragile\n      channel for a *security* state.** Settled by the user with U86: *\"in TUI mode\n      we can inform the modes (sandbox/yolo) in colors, not necessary to have\n      explicit name it.\"* Recorded as a decision, not relitigated — but B7's whole\n      subject is honesty about what is actually enforced, and a state that is\n      encoded only in colour is a state that can be present and unseen. Four\n      concrete cases: a **monochrome or low-colour terminal** (and `TERM=dumb`,\n      which a `run -p` pipeline may well set), a **colour-blind user**, a\n      **screenshot or pasted transcript** — which is how a session gets discussed\n      after the fact, and the one place the state would most need to survive — and\n      the **web UI** (§9.2, mobile-first), where the TUI's palette does not\n      automatically carry over.\n      **Cheapest mitigations, none of which adds a printed label to the status\n      line:** colour plus a distinct **glyph** (so the two states differ in shape as\n      well as hue), and the explicit name available **on demand** — `/status`, or a\n      hover/tooltip in the web UI — so it is checkable without being permanent\n      screen furniture. A third option is to name it only at the **transition**,\n      i.e. when `SetPermMode` moves a session into `yolo`, which is the moment the\n      user is looking.\n      **Recommended variant, the cheapest that survives all four cases: colour *plus*\n      one distinct glyph** (`⚡` yolo, `▣` sandboxed — a single cell, so the state\n      survives monochrome, colour-blindness and a screenshot), the explicit name on\n      `/status` and as a web-UI tooltip, and a one-line announcement **at the\n      transition** into `yolo`, which is the moment attention is already on the status\n      line. Colours come from the theme, with a defined fallback for themes that omit\n      them.\n      Belongs in [22-tui](part4-hosts-clients/22-tui.md) and\n      [21-web-ui](part4-hosts-clients/21-web-ui.md) when each is deepened, and the\n      glyph half is cheap enough to decide now. **Interaction:** U19 already asks for\n      doc 22 to be deepened with theme auto-detect *before first paint*; a\n      security-state colour belongs in that same theme work rather than being\n      hardcoded, and both must survive a theme that does not define it.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U90","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Opt-in injection of live usage and context statistics back into the prompt — a new feature requested with B20's Q8 answer, and it has one hard constraint that decides its mechanism","body":"**U90 · Opt-in injection of live usage and context statistics back into the\n      prompt — a new feature requested with B20's Q8 answer, and it has one hard\n      constraint that decides its mechanism.** The ask: *\"to have an option where\n      harness could inject current usage/context back as a prompt (i.e. live\n      statistics/hints for the model). opt-in feature.\"* Sound idea with real\n      precedent — oh-my-pi injects goal-mode counters per turn and forge pushes a\n      `system_reminder` on its End lifecycle (f23, f25) — and ⚠️ **per-turn numbers\n      are exactly the volatile data §13.4 forbids in the cached prefix**: \"anything\n      volatile — timestamps, per-request ids, tool ordering — stays out of it\". A token\n      count that changes every turn, written into the stable prefix, would destroy the\n      prompt cache on every turn — the very thing B18's headline metric measures.\n      **So the mechanism is decided by the constraint rather than by taste:** it rides\n      in an **appended, droppable B14 segment** (or a `system_reminder`-shaped\n      message), never in the prefix. f25 is precedent for the exclusion as well as the\n      injection — oh-my-pi *deliberately* keeps its volatile counters out of reviewer\n      and export context because they change every turn. **Two second-order questions\n      for whoever builds it:** a model that can see its own budget changes behaviour\n      (which is the point), but the hint then becomes prompt surface that B18's T2\n      overhead number must count; and §4.5's compaction threshold interacts with a hint\n      that says \"you are at 78 %\".\n      **Recommended mechanism:** a `system_reminder`-shaped message appended per turn\n      behind `[context] usage_hints = true`, carrying **counters only** — context %\n      used, session tokens, cost so far, turns used / `max_turns`, and §4.8's gate\n      state, which is rendered anyway. **No timestamp**, because §13.4 names timestamps\n      as the volatile case; counters change too, but they sit *outside* the prefix so\n      nothing is invalidated. Being appended and droppable, compaction can drop it and\n      lose nothing, and f25's exclusion rule applies: it stays out of exported and\n      reviewer context. Belongs in\n      [09](part2-engine/09-context-caching.md) and [10](part2-engine/10-compaction.md),\n      and needs B14's segment index to exist first.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U91","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The spec cites the plan in exactly one place, and no gate can catch it —","body":"**U91 · The spec cites the plan in exactly one place, and no gate can catch\n      it — ✅ FIXED 2026-09-17 (Fix 1, reword spec-internal).** §13.5's lone\n      `(Appendix B, B12)` → `(§3.6, §12)`, mirroring §2.3 (`:121`), which already\n      records this same codec openness spec-internally as `(§3.6, §12)` and never\n      named B12 — so the fix restores a pattern the spec follows one section\n      earlier rather than inventing one. The information is not orphaned: §2.3 plus\n      the plan's B12 / doc 33 / doc 05 all track it, and §16 (Open decisions) does\n      not list the codec, so nothing was relying on the §13.5 citation. **Fix 2**\n      (relax the Appendix A banner to permit stable B-number citations) was coherent\n      but not taken — it reverses a convention A17 followed deliberately and invites\n      more spec→plan citations, the thing the rule exists to prevent. Verified:\n      `check-links.py --conventions-only` green and the citation grep over the spec\n      returns 0. Original entry follows. Design **§13.5** ends *\"The log's\n      compression codec is deliberately **not** named here; it is still open\n      (Appendix B, B12)\"* — the spec naming a plan appendix and a decision id. Cited by section rather than by line, per\n      this file's own rule: the A23 pass moved that sentence 96 lines down while\n      leaving it untouched, which is the demonstration. Appendix A's own\n      banner states the rule the other way round (*\"the plan cites the spec; the spec\n      never cites the plan\"*), and A17's application followed it deliberately,\n      writing *\"a debt register maintained alongside the implementation plan\"* rather\n      than naming Appendix F. So this is an **inconsistency between two applied\n      deltas**, not a rule nobody wrote down. It is invisible to every gate: it is\n      prose and not a link, so `check-links.py --conventions-only` passes while\n      truthfully reporting zero markdown links. Landed via A16.\n      **Fifth instance of the U43/U82/f43/U87/f48 doc-consistency class**, and the\n      first in the spec rather than in a plan doc. Found by grepping the spec for\n      B-number citations *before* writing A23's text — i.e. by checking a convention\n      rather than by reading for it, which is the only reason it surfaced, and the\n      same method that found f48 and U87.\n      **Two coherent fixes, and the choice is the user's:** reword §13.5 into\n      spec-internal terms (\"the log's compression codec is deliberately not named\n      here; it is still open\"), or relax the convention and write it down — a B-number\n      is a stable identifier, and §13.5 cannot resolve its own openness from inside\n      the spec. **Not fixed in the A23 commit**: different section, one coherent\n      change per commit. A23 itself added no B- or U-number citation, so the count\n      did not grow.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      it — ✅ FIXED 2026-09-17 (Fix 1, reword spec-internal).** §13.5's lone","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U92","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Appendix B's resolved-entries table has no mechanical check, and it was already wrong in two ways","body":"**U92 · Appendix B's resolved-entries table has no mechanical check, and it was\n      already wrong in two ways.** Found while adding B21's row. **(a) B19 was missing\n      entirely** — it was signed off on 2026-09-16 *and* its delta A23 applied, yet the\n      table that B4's rule exists to keep as the audit trail never gained a row for it.\n      **(b) The footer read \"13 of 18 decisions remain open\"** when 21 decisions exist\n      and 14 are open: B19, B20 and B21 were added to the summary table without the\n      footer being recomputed, so it had been stale since B19 arrived. Both fixed in the\n      B21 pass, and the correction is stated in the footer rather than made silently.\n      **Why it matters more than a count.** That table is the only place a resolution is\n      recorded *with its date and what applied it* — the summary table above it is struck\n      prose, and the detail banner is long-form. A reader auditing \"what has actually\n      been decided\" reads the table, and it was missing the most recent entry. It also\n      survived a full sign-off-and-apply cycle: `attention.py` ran green throughout,\n      because its hygiene block validates **ids and markers** (`action:`, `confidence-lead:`,\n      `gates:`, Gates cells) and never cross-checks the resolved table against the summary\n      table's struck rows. Same family as **U81** (Appendix G's register has no mechanical\n      check, so §2.4's \"a tool with no row is out of policy\" is still prose) — a policy\n      stated in prose and enforced by nothing.\n      **Cheap check, if wanted:** `attention.py` already parses both tables, so asserting\n      *every struck summary row has a resolved-table row* and *the footer's open count\n      equals total minus resolved* is a few lines and would have caught both. Not done\n      here: it is a change to a tool, which per U79 needs tests, and that is a larger\n      unit of work than the B21 pass it was found in.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U93","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"A refused lock has no exit status, and \"session already held\" is an operational state rather than a failure","body":"**U93 · A refused lock has no exit status, and \"session already held\" is an\n      operational state rather than a failure.** Surfaced by applying A24, not by\n      reading for it. §3.6 now guarantees a second opener **fails fast with the holder\n      named and `attach` offered** — but §11 documents exit codes *only* for `run`\n      (`0 done, 2 needs input, 3 blocked on permission, 1 error`), and B21's sub-answer\n      5 says the commands that actually hit the lock are **`session fork` and resume**,\n      neither of which has any documented exit code. So a refusal falls through to a\n      generic `1`, indistinguishable from a crash.\n      **Why that is worth a code of its own.** `run` already got **3** for exactly this\n      shape of case: \"blocked on permission\" is not an error, it is a state a caller\n      must branch on, and CI cannot tell it from a bug without a distinct status. A held\n      session is the same thing — *retryable, somebody else's fault, and the remedy is\n      known* (`attach`, or wait). A script that forks sessions in a loop would otherwise\n      have to parse stderr to learn whether to retry.\n      **Not invented here.** Assigning a number is a small design decision and §11 has\n      no slot for it; the honest fix is to extend §11's exit-code table to the other\n      subcommands and give a held session its own value in the same edit, rather than\n      bolting a code onto a section that documents one command. **Owed by M1**, when the\n      CLI is built and doc [23](part4-hosts-clients/23-cli.md) is deepened — and it is\n      cheap to settle then because nothing has shipped to stay compatible with.\n      Related and also undocumented: what `session fork` should do when the *parent* is\n      held, since forking a live session is the case B21's sub-answer 5 names first.\n      ⚠️ **Corrected 2026-09-19 — this is not a gap M1 can cheaply fill, and the item\n      cites the decision it would overturn without noticing.** B21's sub-answer 5 is\n      quoted above for *which* commands hit the lock, but not for *what it decided*:\n      \"Both: error plus `attach`.\" Under §11's mapping `Error` → 1, so the \"generic `1`\"\n      this item calls indistinguishable from a crash is the **signed** outcome rather than\n      an omission — and giving a held session its own code therefore *overturns B21*,\n      which is the user's decision and not an M1 detail. **A second consequence, also\n      unstated here:** §11's four codes are \"a mapping from `IdleReason`, not a separate\n      vocabulary,\" and a refused lock cannot be an `IdleReason` at all, because that enum\n      says why an *agent loop* went idle (`AgentIdle { reason, guard }`) while a refused\n      lock means no loop was ever entered **in this process** — on `session fork` no child\n      exists yet, and on resume the session is on disk but held elsewhere, so in neither\n      case is there an idle loop to report a reason for. So the code cannot be added to the\n      existing mapping; §11 would have to grow a second vocabulary for pre-session failures,\n      ending the property that the exit-code space has one source of truth. That is a design\n      change, not the \"small design decision\" this item calls it. Still owed by M1 and\n      still cheap *relative to post-ship*, but it needs the user first — and doc 23 still\n      carries its depth marker, so nothing here may be deepened yet (hard rule 1).\n      **Scheduled: owed before M1.11** (§5's sub-step table, 2026-09-19), the hosts row,\n      which is where §11's exit-code table and doc 23's deepening both land. ▶️ **The\n      deferral promotes this to the FIRST decision M1 will ask for** — U70 travels *with*\n      M1.9 to the end of the milestone, and M1.11 does not, so this is now the earliest\n      user-shaped hole in the schedule rather than the later of two. It is also **narrower\n      than the row it gates**: §11's four existing codes (`0/1/2/3`) are signed and cover\n      `run`, so **`run -p` and the TUI are buildable without it** and M1.11 splits rather\n      than       blocks. ⚠️ **And it collides with M1.1's own row, which no gate can see**: doc\n      [23](part4-hosts-clients/23-cli.md) is one of the **eight** ungated docs M1.1 may\n      deepen, while M1.11's row says *\"Doc 23 keeps its depth marker until then\"* — i.e.\n      until U93 settles. Read as a prohibition on *touching* doc 23, M1.11's row would leave\n      the hosts pass with no CLI doc at all, since `run -p`, `serve`, `session fork` and\n      `attach` are all doc 23's and none of them is blocked. **So the two rows reconcile the\n      other way, and hard rule 5 is what settles it**: doc 23 is deepened in M1.1's\n      just-in-time slot *except* for the exit-code table, which is written as **pending on\n      U93 with this item named**, and **the marker does not flip** — a doc with a\n      load-bearing hole is not \"expanded\", and §4's count is a claim about believed depth\n      rather than about word count (the rule §4 states for Appendix G). The marker flip is\n       then U93's own one-line edit, and the count moves 27 → 26 when the exit-code space\n       actually has one source of truth again.\n       ✅ **CLOSED 2026-10-04 — ruled by the user: arm (b), value `4`.** A held session\n       gets its own exit code, overturning B21's sub-answer 5's *exit-code* half while\n       keeping its machinery (fail fast, holder named, `attach` offered — §3.6's\n       refusal, untouched). **Design §11** carries the value with the one-source-of-truth\n       property restated rather than abandoned: `0–3` remain the `IdleReason` mapping;\n       `4` is the *single* pre-session refusal, one value with its own one-sentence\n       rule, not a growing family — the composite edit this item's \"honest fix\" paragraph\n       proposed, taken as proposed. **Doc 23 §23.5**'s pending shape became the ruled\n       record and **the marker flipped** — the count moved **20 → 19**, not the 27 → 26\n       this entry predicted (that numeral was already stale at HEAD: five markers\n       flipped between the prediction and the ruling, which is the recorded-numeral trap\n       class and why the closure cites the measured move). **B21's Appendix B row and\n       its sub-answer 5 carry the supersession annotations** (struck, not deleted). The\n       related sub-question — what `session fork` does when the *parent* is held — is\n       **deferred to M3's fork row with the owner named** (doc 23 §23.6): the ruling\n       settles the exit-code half only, and under either behavior answer the code is\n       `4`. **No code moved**: `exit.rs`'s six-arm mapping is untouched and `4`'s first\n       producer is M3's `session fork`/resume — `run` creates a new session and cannot\n       hit the lock, so nothing in the tree emits `4` by design. Landed as M1.11's tail\n       (tracker t334/g149); the §1 banner of this date is the narrative.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"       ✅ **CLOSED 2026-10-04 — ruled by the user: arm (b), value `4`.** A held session","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U94","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"A `*-sys` crate that links vendored C *without* a `cc`/`cmake`/`bindgen` build dependency is invisible to the class-1 gate's exempt guard —","body":"**U94 · A `*-sys` crate that links vendored C *without* a `cc`/`cmake`/`bindgen`\n      build dependency is invisible to the class-1 gate's exempt guard — ✅ CLOSED\n      2026-09-17 (route B, the §F.1 mirror check; no spec change).** `dep-audit.py` now\n      reads Appendix F §F.1's class-2 row as a *register* and `validate_exempt` rejects\n      any `gates/class1-exempt.txt` row that register does not name. Exempting a crate\n      that links vendored C is therefore a reviewed Appendix F edit asserting *\"compiles\n      nothing, the symbols come from the OS\"* rather than a one-line gates-file change:\n      the graph cannot contradict that claim, but a reviewer with the crate's `build.rs`\n      open can, and now has a diff to do it in. **Both axes are resolved by name, not by\n      position** — the row is the one whose class cell defines class 2, the cell is the\n      one whose header reads `Example` — and the cell must be a bare list of names, prose\n      in it being refused rather than mined for tokens so a negation (*never\n      `openssl-sys`*) cannot register the crate it forbids. Anything unparsable (a\n      missing §F.2 heading; a reworded, duplicated or prose-bearing row; a retitled\n      column) exits non-zero instead of auditing against an empty set, because with the\n      exempt file empty at M0 an unparsed register would let the mirror check pass\n      unread.\n      **Dual review round 1 returned `changes_requested` from both reviewers, and the\n      primary found a HIGH this pass had missed:** the register cell was read by column\n      *index*, so inserting one column into §F.1's table repointed it onto that column's\n      prose and an undocumented `vendored-sys` exemption passed the live gate with\n      `--self-test` still green — the silent-pass class U88's review had already found\n      twice. Header resolution is the fix, and the shifted-column fixture now carries a\n      bare backticked name so the case detects the *junk-register* shape and not merely\n      an empty one. Round 1 also closed: the \"enforced in both directions\" overclaim\n      (only exempt ⊆ §F.1 is checked, and the converse is deliberately not — `libc`);\n      the absence of any case driving `live_audit`, the only place the two registers are\n      wired, so a swapped argument survived (`--self-test` gained a fourth part for it);\n      Part C substituting an empty set for a missing gates file; Appendix G's row still\n      calling `--self-test` two parts; and §1's stale \"U88 … the only work an agent can\n      start unaided\" clause. The `Example` column was *not* renamed — the other two rows'\n      cells genuinely are examples — so the class-2 row's own cell points at the register\n      paragraph instead.\n      **Round 2: the secondary signed off; the primary returned `changes_requested`\n      again — one MED, four LOWs, all closed.** The MED was an incomplete sweep: U88's\n      own §6 entry still called U94 an open residual and still counted `validate_exempt`\n      at four rules and `--self-test` at two parts, i.e. the same stale claims corrected\n      in §1, §5 and Appendix G, missed in the entry that introduced them. The LOWs: one\n      failure message covering four distinct refusals, so the parser now returns *why*\n      and the plausible edit \"`libc` and `windows-sys`\" prints `prose is left once they\n      are lifted out: 'and'` rather than \"names no crates\"; `_table_blocks`' post-loop\n      flush was load-bearing but untested (a table butting against the next heading now\n      has a fixture); doc 01's inline `--self-test` gloss still named two of four parts;\n      and `_is_separator` treats an empty first cell as a divider without saying so.\n      Round 2 independently re-derived every round-1 fix by mutation, and agreed with\n      declining the optional `§F.1 ∩ §F.3` contradiction check — `validate_exempt`'s §F.3\n      rule already rejects such a crate where it matters, at the exemption.\n      **Round 3: the primary signed off (`ok`) with two non-blocking LOWs, both fixed\n      rather than banked** — one because it was the round-1 HIGH in miniature: the arity\n      guard was one-sided, so a class-2 row *longer* than its header (an escaped or stray\n      pipe) read cell 1 and silently registered whatever sat there. Row and header must\n      now agree, and the fixture that pins it asserts the junk name is refused rather\n      than merely that parsing failed.       The other was coverage: the arity guard, the\n      \"names no crates\" reason and `_is_separator`'s empty-first-cell rule were all\n      unexercised, so each now has a case. `f1_class2_names`' docstring no longer states\n      how many refusals it has — the numeral was stale before it was committed, and a\n      count in prose that drifts is P-LIVE-006's class.\n      **Round 4 confirmed all four code fixes and then caught this entry overclaiming:**\n      the sentence above said the empty-first-cell rule \"now has a case\" when it had a\n      docstring, i.e. P-LIVE-006 committed in the same breath as citing it. The case now\n      exists (`_F1_EMPTY_FIRST_CELL`: an empty first cell in a *later* row reads as a\n      divider, discarding the table and the class-2 row with it, so the gate refuses) and\n      is mutation-proven.\n      **Verified:** fourteen mutations, each caught by the case that owns it and two of\n      them by more than one (mirror rule neutered; ambiguity guard neutered; empty-set\n      refusal neutered; the forbidden `\\Z` fallback restored; column index hardcoded;\n      bare-list guard dropped; `live_audit` unpack swapped; `f3_names` passed as the\n      class-2 register; a real gates file hidden; the post-loop flush deleted; the\n      no-match refusal reason dropped; the empty-cell reason dropped; the arity guard\n      made one-sided; the empty-first-cell divider rule neutered) · five live-path runs,\n      each mutating a *committed* file from a shell and reverting it, so they are\n      evidence and not a re-runnable check: undocumented `vendored-sys` row → 1,\n      reworded §F.1 row → 1, prose in the register cell → 1 naming the leftover `'and'`,\n      *documented* `windows-sys` row → 0, control → 0. Their re-runnable equivalents are\n      in `--self-test` — Part D drives `live_audit` against a throwaway repo for the\n      first, second and fourth shapes, and case 12 pins the third at unit level · §F.3's\n      parsed name-set unchanged across the shared table-parser extraction ·\n      `--self-test`, `check-links.py` and `attention.py` green. Route A (widening `MARKER_TOOLS` to\n      `nasm`/`vcpkg`) was **not** taken: it is the genuine §2.3/§F.1 spec change, and a\n      crate vendoring a precompiled `.a` evades any tool-name list, so it would have\n      added scope without closing this hole. Docs: §F.1 states the mirror, the format\n      contract for its register cell, *and* the limit (it confirms an exemption is\n      documented, not that it is true); doc 01 §1.5 and the exempt file's header no longer\n      call that file's review the only guard.\n      **The mis-filing is why it sat:** §1 described U94 as \"an Ask-First §2.3/§F.1 spec\n      change, not agent work\", which is true of route A only — corrected there in the\n      same commit. Original entry follows. Demonstrated\n      end to end in U88's fourth review round, and **out of U88's scope by\n      construction**: U88 fixed *attribution* (keying the allowlist on the introducer),\n      while the *marker set* — what counts as class-1 — is design §2.3 / Appendix F\n      §F.1's normative mechanical test (\"a build dependency on `cc`/`cmake`/`bindgen`,\n      or a `*-sys` companion\"). A crate whose `build.rs` emits\n      `cargo:rustc-link-lib=static=…` with no tool build-dep, or that vendors a\n      precompiled `.a`, still matches `*-sys` by name so it *is* flagged when unrowed —\n      but once its name is in `gates/class1-exempt.txt` it is graph-indistinguishable\n      from a genuine `windows-sys`, so `validate_exempt` has no contradicting evidence\n      and only review of that file catches it. (`validate_exempt`'s other rules — and\n      the attribution guard that fails an exempt crate caught introducing a build tool —\n      close every route that *does* leave evidence; this is the one that leaves none.)\n      **Two routes, both Ask-First:** widen `MARKER_TOOLS` (e.g. `nasm`, `vcpkg`) — a\n      §2.3/§F.1 spec change — or enforce the mirror §F.1 already claims by requiring\n      every exempt row to also appear in §F.1's class-2 cell (~10 lines in\n      `dep-audit.py`, reusing the `f3_register_names` technique), which makes an\n      undocumented `-sys` exemption impossible without a reviewed Appendix F edit.\n      **Recommended: the §F.1 mirror check** — no spec change, closes the route\n      mechanically. `gates/class1-exempt.txt`'s header and doc 01 §1.5 both name this\n      residual rather than paper over it. **Not urgent:** triggering it needs a\n      deliberate, reviewed, wrong edit to one file, and no class-1 dependency exists\n      until M1.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      build dependency is invisible to the class-1 gate's exempt guard — ✅ CLOSED","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U95","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`tools/attention.py` has no state for \"decided but not yet applied\", and the decision pass exposed it in both directions at once","body":"**U95 · `tools/attention.py` has no state for \"decided but not yet applied\", and the\n      decision pass exposed it in both directions at once.** A struck index row means\n      *resolved* to the tool, and resolved means gone — so fifteen approved deltas whose\n      transcription is still owed left the prioritized view entirely. Meanwhile design\n      §16 item 6, decided the same day, still renders as an open **P0 vote**, because\n      `parse_spec_16` lists every numbered item in §16 and has no notion of a resolved one;\n      only A12's §16 pass removes it. The one view that exists to catch \"recorded in the\n      spec but absent from Appendix B\" — which is how §16.6 went missing in the first place\n      — now **under-reports owed work by fifteen items and over-reports open votes by\n      one**. Same missing state, two symptoms. **Interim, stated so nobody is misled:**\n      §3 above is the application queue and the only carrier of it, and §16.6's P0 row is a\n      known false positive until A12 lands. *2026-09-18: the false positive is now\n      doubly false — §16.6's answer (`Standing`) is normative in §4.8, so the P0 vote\n      the tool renders is not merely decided but **applied**, and A12's job is only the\n      strike. The user accepted that interim state explicitly rather than letting A1\n      edit §16 piecemeal, on the reasoning that §16 is a list of open decisions and\n      removing one item out of band is how A12's bookkeeping pass ends up wrong.* **Fix:** teach the tool the third state — an\n      `approved:` marker in Appendix A's `attention-meta` block rendering as `do: apply`,\n      and a resolution marker `parse_spec_16` skips. Cheap, and this is the tool whose whole\n      job is to be trusted.\n      🔧 **HALF FIXED 2026-09-18 — the §16 half, not the delta half.** The second clause of\n      that fix is built: `parse_spec_16` now classifies from each item's **own text**\n      (a `✅`/`~~` marker resolves it, a `B<n>` reference makes it Appendix B's, only an\n      item with neither is a vote), with the old positional `n ≤ 5` rule kept as a\n      **counted fallback** that a new hygiene line reports by name — see **U54**, which\n      this narrows rather than closes. So §16.6's phantom P0 vote becomes removable the\n      moment A12 writes a marker into it, with no renumbering and no out-of-band strike.\n      **The first clause is still missing and is the larger half**: nothing renders\n      \"approved but transcription owed\" as `do: apply`, so the five items still owed\n      remain invisible to this tool and §3's table stays their only carrier. That half is\n      unchanged, and this item stays open for it. Found by the pass itself: Appendix A's `gates:` lines for the\n      nine struck deltas had to be deleted because the hygiene block rightly rejects an id\n      that no longer resolves, which is what made the invisibility concrete.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U96","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"A3's hand-over resume is signed but has no mechanism, and it is now v1's answer to a filling context","body":"**U96 · A3's hand-over resume is signed but has no mechanism, and it is now v1's\n      answer to a filling context.** ⚠️ **Still open after A3 applied 2026-09-18, and\n      deliberately so — A3 gave it a slot, not a mechanism.** `GuardKind::HandOver` now\n      exists in §3.2, so a bail-out has somewhere to report through and the termination is\n      visible to a client; §4.2 names it in the guard table with its config key marked\n      *pending* and says in terms that the resume's contents, durability, whether bail-out\n      is a guard or a tool call, and its meeting with `Fork` and §3.6's single-writer lock\n      are **not specified there**. Inventing any of them would have pre-empted the 🧠\n      session the sign-off explicitly did not bypass, and would have created an `Event`\n      variant needing a golden fixture for a design nobody has done. **One of the five\n      sub-questions below got easier while this was open**: \"how the next session *finds*\n      it\" pointed at B13's virtual folders, and **A14 landed those** — the org registry,\n      `Org { op: OrgOp }` and a folder creatable *before* its sessions exist, which is\n      precisely the shape a hand-over needs. So the discovery half now has a mechanism to\n      point at; the other four do not.\n      🔺 **Escalated by A4 the same day, and this is the item's real change of status: it\n      went from aspirational to LOAD-BEARING.** A4's amendment (a) made compaction opt-in\n      and off by default, and named the hand-over as **v1's answer to a filling context**;\n      §6's new overflow path then offers it as one of the remedies a user gets when a\n      session terminates on context exhaustion. So **two normative spec sections now cite\n      a mechanism nobody has designed.** That is still the right call — the alternative\n      was inventing it, which would have pre-empted the 🧠 session the sign-off explicitly\n      did not bypass and created an `Event` variant needing a fixture for a design that\n      does not exist — but it changes what the brainstorm is for. **It is no longer\n      refining an idea; it is filling a slot the spec points at**, and until it does, v1's\n      honest behaviour is the one A4 wrote down: stop and tell the user, with `/compact`,\n      a hand-over and a fork offered as remedies, only the last of which exists.\n      **Owner: the 🧠 agentic-loop\n      brainstorm**, with the built-in-tools session deciding whether bail-out is a tool —\n      **M1.9 and M1.10** in §5's sub-step table (2026-09-19), which is why the hand-over\n      is the one M1 gap no amount of surrounding-part building closes.\n      Original entry follows. The user's words, signed as part of A3: *\"we would have\n      some extra (opt-in) instructions for the model with provided usage (context) that could\n      change the length of the session (earlier bailing out with hand over resume for the\n      next session)\"*. It matters more than an amendment usually does because **A4's\n      compaction became opt-in and later in the same breath** — so *hand over instead of\n      compact* is what v1 does when the window fills. **Open, and deliberately not\n      answered here:** is bail-out a *guard* in A3's set or a model-initiated tool call; is\n      the resume durable (an `Event` variant, which needs A1's tier marker and a golden\n      fixture) or a file under `.dacode/plans/`; what it contains (§4.8's task tree plus A4's\n      structured summary are the obvious payload); how it meets `session fork` and **B21**'s\n      single-writer lock, since resuming into a new session is a fork-shaped operation; and\n      how the next session *finds* it — which is **B13**'s virtual folders, a feature that\n      spans sessions being exactly the case that motivated them. **Owner: the 🧠 agentic-loop\n      brainstorm**, with the built-in-tools session deciding whether bail-out is a tool.\n      ▶️ **Refined by the user the same day (2026-10-05) — the mechanism's arm structure:**\n      **model-initiated bail-out if the model can do it** (A3's original vision —\n      usage/context in the model's instructions, the model bails out early and writes the\n      handover), **with an agent-side hard threshold as the fallback** that triggers\n      handover-summary generation when the model does not. **Both arms under one option,\n      enabled by default** — the alternates being running to the end (option off) or\n      relying on compaction, *including a server-side variant* (the option's compaction\n      mode; what \"server-side\" precisely names — provider-side context management vs a\n      `dacode-server` mechanism — is a session question, recorded verbatim from the user).\n      This answers the filed \"guard vs tool\" question **in shape: both** — the model's\n      initiative first, the guard as backstop — while leaving the session to decide the\n      summary's writer/contents/durability, the fork/lock mechanics, the config surface's\n      naming, and whether the model-initiated arm is a tool (M1.10's half, as filed). The\n      survey's measured thresholds (the-agentic-loop.md Part 9) are the fallback\n      threshold's candidates.\n      ▶️ **User steer, 2026-10-05, recorded ahead of the session so it is not lost: lean\n      AWAY from compaction and INTO the hand-over — \"alert the user, have a handover\n      summary, then start a new session.\"** This strengthens the A3/A4 position rather\n      than changing it (hand-over was already v1's answer; compaction already\n      opt-in-and-later), and it gives the session a concrete flow shape: the alert is\n      **user-facing** rather than a silent model bail-out, the summary is the payload, and\n      the outcome is a **new session** rather than a compacted continuation of the same\n      one. It does not answer the filed mechanism questions — trigger (threshold guard vs\n      model bail-out vs both), the summary's writer/contents/durability, the fork/lock\n      meeting, discovery — those stay the session's — but it does tilt \"guard vs tool\"\n      toward *user-in-the-loop either way*. One mapping worth naming for the session: the\n      steer rides existing slots (`GuardKind::HandOver` + `IdleReason` is the alert an\n      event-sourced client renders; B13's virtual folders — A14 landed them — are the\n      discovery; the new-session creation is fork-shaped, which is why B21's lock is\n      already in the filed questions), and the survey's measured compaction thresholds\n      ([the-agentic-loop](../../.agents/knowledge/reference-harnesses/the-agentic-loop.md)\n      Part 9) become the **alert** thresholds under this steer — the trigger machinery is\n      the same; the response differs. The steer is *against the field's grain* (all five\n      harnesses compact; none hands over) and *with the spec's* — green field, chosen\n      deliberately.","status":"deferred","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":"      are **not specified there**. Inventing any of them would have pre-empted the 🧠","references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U97","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Partial compaction is a candidate, not a feature, and it fights the cache","body":"**U97 · Partial compaction is a candidate, not a feature, and it fights the cache.**\n      A4's second amendment, in the user's words: *\"it could be partial compaction (by\n      stripping some later tools output that doesn't affect the following conversation, but\n      requires some experiments while could affect caching)\"*. Two hazards are why it stays\n      a candidate: stripping earlier tool output **rewrites bytes already sent**, which §4.8\n      forbids for context rendering and which A4's own append-only rule exists to prevent —\n      so it must go through the truncate-and-re-emit resync path, never a byte-rewrite — and\n      it **destroys the prompt-cache prefix from the cut point onwards**, trading a cache\n      miss for context space. Whether that trade pays is precisely what **B18's T2\n      cache-hit-rate metric** measures, and B18 was signed the same day, so the instrument\n      exists. **Owner: A4's application plus B18-T2. Do not adopt before the number** — that\n      is B18's publication rule applied to a design choice rather than to a claim.\n      ⚠️ **Still open after A4 applied 2026-09-18, which is the correct outcome and not a\n      miss.** A4's half of the ownership is **discharged**: §4.5 now records partial\n      compaction as a **candidate, not an adopted feature**, with both hazards named in\n      the spec rather than only here — it rewrites bytes already sent, so it must go\n      through the truncate-and-re-emit resync path and never a byte-rewrite; and it\n      destroys the cache prefix from the cut point onwards, trading a cache miss for\n      context space, a trade §14.2's hit-rate metric is the instrument for. What remains\n      is **B18-T2's half, which is a measurement and cannot be discharged by prose**: the\n      number does not exist, so the feature is not adopted. Two things changed its\n      prospects while it was open. ~~**§14.2 does not exist yet** — A21 is signed but\n      unapplied — so the metric this item waits on is currently cited by section number\n      rather than by content, and **A21 must place it**.~~ ✅ **A21 applied 2026-09-18 and\n      §14.2 exists, and it names this gate explicitly**: partial compaction \"is recorded\n      there as a candidate rather than a feature because it trades a cache miss for context\n      space, and that trade is exactly what hit rate measures; adopting it before the\n      number exists would be guessing at the one quantity the design says matters.\" And\n      **`Usage` now carries the four\n      counters** (A4, closing U71), so T2 has the cache-read and cache-write inputs a hit\n      rate is computed from, which it did not have when this item was written — **plus\n      `ExchangeRecord`** (A10's carve-out), which is what the rate is computed *from* at\n      all. **So everything this item was waiting on now exists except the measurement\n      itself**, which is the only part prose cannot substitute for. The item\n      closes when T2 produces a number and partial compaction is either adopted with it or\n      dropped.\n      ▶️ **User steer, 2026-10-05: \"compaction … I don't really like that.\"** Recorded as a\n      strong prior **against** adoption at this item's B18-T2 gate. The gate itself is\n      unchanged — the number still decides adopt-or-drop — but the number now has to be\n      good enough to *overcome a stated user preference*, and the user may close this item\n      early at the M1.9 session by ruling the drop outright. The steer travels with U96's:\n      alert-the-user → handover summary → new session is the preferred context-growth\n      answer, and compaction is at most the opt-in exception, not the mechanism the design\n      leans on.\n","status":"in_work","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U98","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The sandbox seam has a named v2 route, supplied by the user at B7's sign-off: intercept `execve` through a BPF filter","body":"**U98 · The sandbox seam has a named v2 route, supplied by the user at B7's\n      sign-off: intercept `execve` through a BPF filter.** *\"For sandbox variant, we can\n      intercept execve via bpf filters and there we can capture exact program path and,\n      optionally, parameters. Similar to `sandboxed-api` built on top of `sandbox2`\"*\n      (both Google; github.com/google/sandboxed-api,\n      developers.google.com/code-sandboxing/sandbox2). **Why this is more than an\n      implementation note: it answers B7's own central argument instead of working around\n      it.** A glob over a command *string* cannot decide what the command does — that is\n      why §4.4's allow-list and `bash:readonly` are advisory, and why U12/U63 exist. The\n      kernel at `execve` sees the **resolved binary path and its argv**, so `git diff; git\n      push`, `bash -c …`, a shell function, an alias and a symlink all stop being evasions.\n      It is therefore the mechanism that makes read-onlyness **enforced** rather than\n      documented — i.e. it *retires* the honesty fix — and it is what **B20**'s remote\n      members actually need, since B7's stub means its \"sandboxed or refused\" rule has\n      nothing to point at today.\n      **Two costs to settle before it is anything but a lead.** *(a)* **Dependency policy:**\n      Rust BPF loaders are `aya` (pure-Rust loader) and `libbpf-rs` (links `libbpf`), and the\n      filter itself is compiled at build time — which is §2.3/§2.4 territory and a *different*\n      question from linking C at runtime, but needs an answer and possibly an Appendix F row.\n      *(b)* **`sandboxed-api` and `sandbox2` are C++**, so they are the source of the\n      technique and not a candidate dependency — **the lead is the mechanism, not the\n      library.** Also worth noting: BPF-based interception is Linux-specific, so a macOS\n      arm of the same seam wants `sandbox-exec`/Seatbelt or Endpoint Security, and the\n      `SandboxBackend` seam B7 reserves is exactly the place that per-OS split belongs.\n      ⚠️ **2026-10-01, and this item gains a *predecessor* obligation rather than a new one: the user made\n      the permission matcher adoptable by this route a condition of ruling g128.** Ruling arm 5\n      (hand-rolled, `Domain`-parameterised, +0 packages) came with *\"the pattern should be adoptable for\n      our strict sandbox as well (similar to google's sandbox2/sandboxed-api, where we can filter on linux\n      syscalls via ebpf)\"* — i.e. **this item's mechanism is now a design constraint on M1.7's matcher, not\n      only a v2 lead.** Four consequences are written into doc 16 §16.5 so M1.7 builds to them rather than\n      discovering them: the matcher is a **pure predicate over `(Domain, pattern, subject bytes)`** with no\n      `PermScope` in its signature, so a sandbox evaluator can call it with a path lifted out of a syscall\n      argument; ⚠️ **`Domain`'s variants must not be named for §4.4's config keys** (`Path`, not `Edit`) —\n      a sandbox filter needs the same *matching* semantics over subjects no TOML key names, and renaming\n      after M1.3 publishes is a wire-adjacent change; the pattern language must be able to name a\n      syscall-level subject, which is this item's own argument (at `execve` the kernel sees the resolved\n      path and argv, so §16.10's evasions stop being evasions); and ⚠️ **\"adoptable\" cannot mean the glob\n      runs in the filter** — a seccomp-BPF program executes in the kernel over raw register values and\n      cannot call a userspace matcher, so adoptable means the *vocabulary* is shared and the matcher is\n      callable from the **userspace half** of a notify-based filter (seccomp user notification, or an eBPF\n      LSM hook). Anything arguing otherwise is arguing about a different mechanism. **Verify (added):**\n      whoever fills the stub finds a matcher it can reuse without a rename, and if it cannot, the reason is\n      recorded here rather than worked around by a second matcher — two pattern languages in one product is\n      the §16.4 defect one layer up.\n      **Owner: whoever fills the stub — M5 at the earliest, and B20's design session should\n      read this before it assumes container containment.**\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U99","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"CLOSED 2026-09-19 by A7 — both halves of the channel are normative, and the adjacent question this item queued was answered once as asked","body":"**U99 · ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-19 by A7 — both halves of the channel are normative, and\n      the adjacent question this item queued was answered once as asked.** *A folder\n      rename reaches no client but the one that issued it, and the fix is §8.2's, not the\n      protocol's.* A14 made the organization registry authoritative\n      for **names** (§12's `org.json`), because a name appears in no session log and so\n      cannot be folded; membership, which *is* folded, converges on every client as\n      principle 1 requires. Names do not. Every channel in §3.7 is per-session — an\n      `Envelope` carries a `session`, `Subscribe` names one — so a rename has nowhere to\n      travel, and its issuer learns it only from the `Ack`. §3.7's \"never silently stale\"\n      promise is therefore **scoped to a session**, with the registry named as the one\n      exception rather than left as a counterexample.\n      **Why it is narrow rather than a hole.** A client reads the trees when it renders\n      them, so visible staleness needs two clients holding a session list open at the\n      same moment as a rename. Nothing is *lost*: the registry is authoritative and the\n      next read is correct, which is different from §3.7's durable-event case where a\n      missed envelope needs a resync.\n      ~~**What closing it means, and why it is A7's, owed by M3.**~~ ✅ **Closed by A7\n      2026-09-19, and the shape is exactly the one prescribed here**: a freshness\n      channel — a registry version on the tree reads plus a hint on the socket — landed as\n      HTTP-surface work at §8.2 rather than in the replay contract. **The read half** is\n      `org_version` on all four tree reads (`GET /api/sessions?project=&tag=&folder=&q=`,\n      `/api/projects`, `/api/tags`, `/api/folders`), backed by a monotonic `version` stored\n      *in* `org.json` (§12) rather than derived from mtime, because mtime is not monotonic\n      across a rename-on-write, a restore from backup, or two hosts sharing a home\n      directory — and a freshness token that can go backwards silently makes a client that\n      trusts it stale forever. **The socket half** is §3.6's **third down-frame kind,\n      `Hint`**, and it is a *compatibility event*: additive, and deliberately **not** an\n      `Envelope` — no `session`, no `seq`, no cursor, never written, never replayed, safe\n      to lose, since the authoritative version travels on the read it prompts. It gets **no\n      golden fixture**, because §14.1 derives the fixture set from `Event`'s variants, and\n      §14.1's new bullet asserts the three properties instead.\n      ~~**A7 should answer one adjacent question at the same time, once rather than three\n      times: who may write a non-session resource from a browser.**~~ ✅ **Answered once,\n      as asked**: §8.4 now states **one rule for every non-session write** — the registry,\n      the config writes and a board write are the same act, *a client changed something\n      durable outside any session* — so v1's \"any authenticated principal\" covers all three\n      and tightening is one decision rather than three. ⚠️ **But the mechanism this item\n      named was wrong, and correcting it is why the answer is stronger.** It said the rule\n      should use \"`Hello`'s reserved capability flags\"; **`Hello` is not a `Command`** — it\n      is §7.2's extension handshake — so a flag there cannot gate a *browser*, and a tenant\n      cannot gate a *bridge*. §8.3 now names **two seams required to express one policy**:\n      the `Authenticator` for browser principals, `Hello`'s flags for extensions. Same\n      correction shape as U62's annotation under A22: the item's diagnosis was right and\n      its prescription was written before the mechanism was checked.\n      **Rejected, so it is not re-proposed later:** widening `Envelope.session` to make\n      the registry an event stream. It would re-open §3.6's cursor, lock and fencing\n      questions for a second stream whose contents are not append-only in the first\n      place, and it would touch every envelope one day after A1 settled them.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"**U99 · ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-19 by A7 — both halves of the channel are normative, and","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U100","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Sixteen types the spec names in a signature and defines only in a trailing comment. Fine for a document; not fine for a crate","body":"**U100 · Sixteen types the spec names in a signature and defines only in a\n      trailing comment. Fine for a document; not fine for a crate.** ✅ **CLOSED\n      2026-09-28 AS STALE RATHER THAN ACTIONED, on measurement and not on judgement\n      (tracker f262): THIRTEEN OF THE SIXTEEN ARE ALREADY DEFINED IN CODE.** A sweep\n      for each name as a real definition (`rg '^\\s*pub (enum|struct|type) <T>\\b' crates/`)\n      returns `TaskOp` `task.rs:21`, `TaskId` `id.rs:398`, `TaskTree` `task.rs:139`,\n      `PendingRequest` `snapshot.rs:281`, `TurnView` `snapshot.rs:183`, `ModelRef`\n      `resolve.rs:35`, `PermMode` `perm.rs:19`, `PermScope` `perm.rs:44`, `PermDecision`\n      `perm.rs:66`, `SandboxSpec` `command.rs:422`, `CheckpointId` `id.rs:432`,\n      `CloseReason` `turn.rs:105`, `Role` `turn.rs:19` — all in `dacode-protocol` except\n      `ModelRef`. **Absent: `ToolSpec`, `ToolCtx`, `OutputSink`** — and of those, this\n      item's own text already rules `ToolCtx`/`OutputSink` **not** defects (\"opaque handles\n      a client never matches on\"), while `ToolSpec`'s shape belongs to the built-in-tools\n      session (**M1.10 / doc 14, 🧠-gated**), which is precisely why doc 13 §13.4 landed\n      `CompletionTool` as the *encoded* form instead. **So the residual is zero actionable\n      items and the 🧠-gated one is named rather than dropped.** *Why it went stale*: filed\n      at session 16 while applying A2/A3/A4, i.e. **before M1.2 built the protocol crate**,\n      and never re-measured — the f96 class, since every hygiene check verifies that\n      references **resolve** and none checks that a list is still **absent from the code**.\n      ⚠️ **And one of its six \"defects\" was misclassified on the merits**, which is the half\n      that would have caused real damage: `CloseReason` is listed as failing A3's criterion\n      (\"a surface a client matches on\"), but its **own spec doc says the opposite** —\n      *\"**Display, not dispatch**: §11's exit codes map from `IdleReason` and nothing\n      branches on this\"* (§3.2, `docs/dacode-design.md:478-480`). Promoting it \"because\n      clients branch on it\" would have promoted it for a reason the spec forbids. **The\n      generalisation: a criterion applied to a *list* of names is only as good as the reading\n      of each name.** Found by the\n      type-definition sweep A3's pass established as a gate, which was looking for one\n      undefined type and found seventeen. Two of the seventeen were introduced by A4\n      itself and are fixed (`SegmentKind`, and `ModelInfo`/`Pricing` once the four-rate\n      cost rule made their shape load-bearing). The remaining sixteen are\n      **`TaskOp`, `TaskId`, `TaskTree`, `PendingRequest`, `TurnView`, `ModelRef`,\n      `PermMode`, `PermScope`, `PermDecision`, `SandboxSpec`, `CheckpointId`,\n      `CloseReason`, `Role`, `ToolSpec`, `ToolCtx`, `OutputSink`** — each currently\n      specified as `// Plan | Ask | AutoEdit | Yolo`, `// user | assistant`,\n      `// name, description, JSON schema` and so on.\n      **Not all of them should be promoted, and the criterion already exists.** A3\n      stated it when it defined `StopReason` and `IdleReason`: a type gets a real\n      definition when it is **\"a surface a client matches on, and a comment cannot be\n      matched\"**. By that test `PermMode`, `PermScope`, `PermDecision`, `Role`,\n      `CloseReason` and `TaskOp` are defects — clients branch on them, §4.4's\n      resolution order is written in terms of them, and a fold cannot match a comment.\n      `ToolCtx` and `OutputSink` are not: they are opaque handles a client never\n      inspects, and naming them without defining them is honest. The rest need the\n      judgement applied one at a time rather than in a batch.\n      **The cheapest promotion is `TaskOp`, and it is transcription rather than\n      invention:** §3.2's prose already enumerates its five values verbatim\n      (`add | set | close | block | promote`), exactly as it enumerated `IdleReason`'s\n      before A3 promoted that. **Why this is owed by M1 and not later:** doc 03 is the\n      protocol crate's doc and the crate cannot be written against trailing comments —\n      every one of these becomes a decision taken silently by whoever writes the first\n      `enum`, which is how a wire type ends up with variants nobody chose.\n      **Owner:\n      M1.1 / doc 03's deepening** ✅ **— and `M1.1` is defined as of 2026-09-19**: §5's M1\n      sub-step table, the doc-deepening row, whose eight docs include 03 and whose three\n      *withheld* docs (08, 14, 32) belong to the 🧠 rows. So this item is released by M1\n      starting and by nothing else. Doc 03 **is** already owed a marker correction for six\n      deltas' worth of variants and should apply A3's criterion to this list in the same\n      pass. Verified by the re-runnable sweep in the tracker (`f82`).\n      ⚠️ **The sweep was re-run 2026-09-19, after A21/A22/A8/A13 landed, and the list is\n      seventeen, not sixteen.** The addition is **`Preview`** — `PermissionRequested`'s\n      `preview: Option<Preview>` at §3.2 line 319, its only appearance in the spec. It is\n      wire-visible (§3.2 is \"Events (engine → clients)\"), so §14 owes it a golden fixture\n      and whoever writes the protocol crate invents its shape silently — this item's own\n      failure mode. But wire-visibility is *not* what distinguishes it: measured, **11 of\n      the sixteen are wire-visible too** and only 5 are internal, so `Preview` joins a\n      majority rather than standing out.\n      **What the re-run actually corrects is this item's gloss, and the correction is\n      larger than one type.** The item says each of the sixteen is \"currently specified as\n      `// Plan | Ask | AutoEdit | Yolo`, `// user | assistant` … and so on.\" Measured\n      against the spec, **eight of the seventeen carry no shape comment at all**:\n      `TaskId`, `ModelRef`, `SandboxSpec`, `CheckpointId`, `CloseReason`, `ToolCtx`,\n      `OutputSink` and `Preview`. So the \"each has a comment\" framing is wrong for nearly\n      half the list, not for the new entry — and the list is better described as *nine\n      types with a comment to promote and eight with nothing to promote from*, which is a\n      materially harder M1.1 job than this item implies.\n      **`Preview` is also not the worst of those eight, which is worth stating because the\n      opposite is the intuitive reading.** It is the only one of the eight with anything\n      resembling a *content* description: [16-permissions](part3-capabilities/16-permissions.md)\n      says \"with a `Preview` (e.g. the diff for an edit)\". The other seven have mentions\n      but not descriptions, and the distinction matters for whoever promotes them —\n      `ToolCtx`'s only mention is a **v2 proposal** (B7's `SandboxBackend` seam, \"gains\n      something like …\"), `OutputSink`'s is a signature restated in doc 14, `SandboxSpec`'s\n      is a usage cited in a B-decisions table row, and `TaskId`'s six hits include A9's\n      own note that it is \"currently undefined; it is in **U100**'s list\". **Three of the\n      eight — `ModelRef`, `CheckpointId` and `CloseReason` — have no mention outside the\n      spec and the board at all**, so there is nothing anywhere to promote from and each\n      must be designed rather than transcribed. U91's rule (the spec may not cite a plan\n      doc) means §3.2 cannot reach `Preview`'s description by reference either, so for the\n      purpose of writing the crate all eight are equally unspecified; what differs is that\n      one has a recorded intent, four have circumstantial mentions, and three have nothing.\n      **Owner and milestone are unchanged: M1.1 / doc 03's deepening**, applying A3's\n      criterion one type at a time. ▶️ **M1 started 2026-09-19, so this is released, and it\n      is now M1.1a — but with a carve-out this item does not contain.** ⚠️ **Four of the\n      seventeen are not M1.1's to promote**, because they sit on surfaces the same table\n      assigns elsewhere: **`TaskTree` and `TaskId`** are §4.8's task tree = **M1.10**'s\n      brainstorm deliverable (`TaskId` also owing a §4.3 scan-exclusion clause, per A9's\n      note, since it is tool-nameable via `todo`); **`ToolSpec`** belongs to **doc 14**,\n      which M1.1's own row *withholds*; **`CheckpointId`** is checkpoints = **doc 12, M2**.\n      Those four get §1's condition (1) — land the approved *shape*, mark brainstorm-owned\n      *content* pending with its owner named, invent nothing — which is what U100's own\n      warning asks for (*\"every one of these becomes a decision taken silently by whoever\n      writes the first `enum`\"*) rather than what a batch promotion would do. `TaskOp` is\n      the cheap one and stays in M1.1a. **Verified 2026-09-19**: the spec still has zero\n      `finding` variants and the sweep's seventeen are unchanged since `f82`.\n      ✅ **THIRTEEN OF THE SEVENTEEN PROMOTED 2026-09-19 (M1.1a), and the item is narrowed\n      rather than closed.** Now real types in the spec: `TaskOp`, `TaskId`, `TaskTree`,\n      `PendingRequest`, `TurnView`, `PermMode`, `PermScope`, `PermDecision`, `SandboxSpec`,\n      `CheckpointId`, `CloseReason`, `Role`, `Preview`. **Four remain undefined, each with a\n      reason rather than an omission**: `ToolSpec` is the tools crate's and is carved out to\n      M1.10 (its §4.3 shape comment stands until then); `ToolCtx` and `OutputSink` are\n      excluded by this item's own criterion (opaque handles a client never inspects) — and\n      §4.1 now says so for its whole block, which is what stops the next sweep re-finding\n      them; `ModelRef` is **reclassified** into that same category, since it is engine\n      state and `CompletionRequest` takes a plain `String`, so it was never a protocol type.\n      ⚠️ **The carve-out this item was given was too coarse in one direction, and applying\n      it literally would have blocked M1.2.** `TaskTree`, `TaskId` and `CheckpointId` were\n      assigned to M1.10/M2 as brainstorm- or milestone-gated, but §1's condition (1) is\n      *shape* versus *content*, and their shapes are transcription: §4.8 already defines\n      `TaskItem` with `parent: Option<TaskId>`, so a tree is a flat vec; and an **opaque\n      `CheckpointId(String)`** commits M2 to nothing while keeping the id out of §3.1's\n      ULID family — which is the point, because a ULID there would make it a candidate for\n      §4.3's scanned set as a side effect of a storage decision. What *is* still gated is\n      the content: which item kinds ship, `task.reminders_max`, the default tree, and what a\n      checkpoint id contains. **Three of the four needed design rather than transcription**\n      (`CloseReason`, `Preview`, `TurnView`) and are argued at their definitions; they are\n      the parts worth a second pair of eyes, since no gate can check that a variant set is\n      the right one.\n      **Four more undefined wire types were found beside the seventeen, and the sweep's\n      method could not have seen any of them**: `cmd_id` (a wire field named in three\n      places with **no type at all** — now `CmdId`, §3.1), `Ack` and `Hint` (shapes given in\n      prose, so a grep for capitalized names in signatures skips them), and `ContextInfo`\n      (used as a `Snapshot` field type while sharing its name with an `Event` variant, which\n      reads as defined and is not — an enum variant is not a type). Two supporting types came\n      with them (`CallView`, `PreviewKind`), and one **collision** was fixed rather than\n      promoted: §6's `ProviderMessage.role` used §3.2's `Role`, whose comment says\n      `user | assistant`, so an assembled buffer could not express a system prompt or a tool\n      result — `ProviderRole { System, User, Assistant, Tool }` is now §6's, never\n      wire-visible, and `TurnStarted { role: System }` is unconstructible. **The criterion\n      also needed widening, and the reason is §14.1**: a `Durable` variant's fields are\n      pinned by a golden fixture, so a wire-visible enum whose variants live in a comment\n      cannot be *fixtured* either. \"A comment cannot be matched\" understates it — a comment\n      cannot be matched **and** cannot be fixtured, and the second half is what forces\n      promotion of types no client branches on (`CloseReason` is the case in point).\n      **Four other undefined names the re-run surfaced were deliberately NOT added**,\n      because this item's own criterion excludes them: `AgentConfig`, `CheckpointStore`,\n      `EventLog` and `PermissionState` are §4.1 internal engine state, each with a\n      trailing comment — the \"opaque handles a client never inspects\" category that\n      `ToolCtx` and `OutputSink` already sit in. Likewise `Sandbox`/`SandboxSpec` and\n      `PendingRequests`/`PendingRequest` are internal-vs-wire pairs (§4.1 against §3.3\n      and §3.5), legitimately two types rather than one named twice.\n      ▶️ **M1.1a's doc-03 half landed 2026-09-19, and what is left of this item is one\n      type.** [03](part1-foundations/03-protocol.md) is deepened (marker flipped, §4's\n      count 27 → 26) and its §3.9 records the four still-undefined names with their owners\n      so that M1.2 does not invent them. Three of the four are **not defects**: `ToolCtx`,\n      `OutputSink` and `ModelRef` are the \"opaque handle a client never inspects\" category\n      this item's own criterion excludes, and design §4.1 now classifies its whole struct\n      block that way so the next sweep does not re-find them. **`ToolSpec` is the only\n      genuine remainder, and it is M1.10's / doc 14's rather than the protocol crate's** —\n      design §4.3's `ToolSpec.schema` is `RawJson`, so `dacode-protocol` sees bytes and\n      never the type. The item therefore stays **open with a narrowed owner** instead of\n      closing: closing it would drop the one name still undefined off attention's P0 list,\n      and `ToolSpec` is precisely the type whose shape the 🧠 session decides.\n      ✅ **The M1.5 collision this created is resolved (2026-09-24, M1.1d, tracker g85) and\n      `ToolSpec` is now *purely* M1.10's — the item's owner narrows again rather than\n      widening.** §6's normative `CompletionRequest` carried `tools: Vec<ToolSpec>`, which\n      made the provider row depend on a type whose shape the built-in-tools session owns,\n      and M1.5 could not be written without either touching that type or editing normative\n      §6. The user approved the edit: §6 now carries `Vec<CompletionTool>` — the *encoded*\n      form (`name`, `description`, `schema: RawJson`), defined beside `CompletionRequest`.\n      That is this item's own reasoning applied one crate over rather than contradicted: if\n      `ToolSpec.schema` is `RawJson` so the *protocol* crate sees bytes and never the type,\n      then an *adapter* sees the same bytes for the same reason, and §6's neighbouring\n      `messages` field already states the contract (`\"the engine builds it, the provider only\n      encodes it\"`). **Consequence for this item: `ToolSpec`'s shape stays exactly where it\n      was — M1.10 / doc 14, 🧠-gated — and no M1.5 surface depends on it any more**, so the\n      deferral the user chose is preserved instead of being quietly spent by a struct field.\n      Doc 13 §13.4 carries the ruling and the two arms rejected.\n      ⚠️ **The same pass ran f109's per-field walk over §3 for the first time and it found\n      four more**, which is f109's own claim confirmed rather than extended — see **U106**\n      (`Bytes`, plus the *fourth* blind spot in the sweep's method: a name that looks\n      imported) and **U107** (`blob_id` with no type, `LogHeader`, `GetSnapshot`, fold\n      totality).\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      trailing comment. Fine for a document; not fine for a crate.** ✅ **CLOSED","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U101","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§14 has no UI test tier at all, so two client rules are now normative with nothing that can fail on them —","body":"**U101 · §14 has no UI test tier at all, so two client rules are now normative\n      with nothing that can fail on them — ✅ DONE 2026-09-18, via A21, exactly as this\n      item assigned it.** **A21's split added a `Clients` bullet to §14.1 carrying both\n      properties as tests** — mouse tracking off unless an overlay holds the alternate\n      screen (assert the enable *and* disable sequences, and that neither appears\n      otherwise), and collapse-on-paste honouring the 10-line / 1000-char boundary with a\n      chip the cursor cannot enter. **The harness is deliberately not named, and that is\n      the part of this item's own reasoning that survived contact with the fix**: the TUI\n      crate does not exist and §9.1's framework decision is open, so §14.1 fixes the\n      *properties* — which outlive any harness — and leaves the backend to M1. **The web\n      UI's half stays open with it**, since §9.1 lands at M3; this item closes on the TUI\n      properties it named and not on UI testing generally. Original entry follows.\n      §14's layers are Protocol, Engine, Tools,\n      Server, Session log, Storage codec, Prompt caching, Providers, Extensions,\n      `xtask` and repository authoring tooling — **there is no TUI or web-UI tier**, and\n      A9 just made two rules normative that live entirely in a client:\n      - **Mouse tracking is enabled only while a fullscreen overlay holds the alt\n        screen** (§10). The failure is not a crash, it is a user who has permanently\n        lost scroll-back and text selection in their own terminal — the two things a\n        terminal does better than any UI — and it is invisible to every existing test\n        because nothing asserts on the escape sequences emitted.\n      - **Collapse-on-paste above 10 lines / 1000 chars into an indivisible chip**\n        (§10). The failure is a chip the cursor can enter and half-edit, which looks\n        like text and is not, so the model receives something the user never saw. The\n        thresholds are numbers, and numbers drift.\n      **Why this was recorded instead of fixed:** adding a UI test tier means choosing a\n      harness for a crate that does not exist yet (ratatui's `TestBackend` for the TUI,\n      and something else entirely for the web UI once §9.1's framework decision lands at\n      M3), which is a §14 restructuring of the kind **A21** does — and A21 is signed but\n      unapplied. Inventing a tier inside a two-section delta would have pre-empted it.\n      **Owner: A21's §14 split, or M1.1 if A21 lands first.** A21 should place these two\n      as the tier's first content rather than leaving them as the only two normative\n      client rules with no test. Related: **U19** still owes doc 22's deepening, whose\n      source is now §10 rather than the survey.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      with nothing that can fail on them — ✅ DONE 2026-09-18, via A21, exactly as this","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U102","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Revisit `ulid`'s dependency set when it changes — the class-2 exemption for `js-sys` is contingent on it, and nothing else would notice the contingency lapsing","body":"**U102 · Revisit `ulid`'s dependency set when it changes — the class-2 exemption\n      for `js-sys` is contingent on it, and nothing else would notice the contingency\n      lapsing.** ✅ **The user decided the fork 2026-09-19** (f70's option (a)): `ulid`\n      with `default-features = false, features = [\"serde\"]`, which drops four packages —\n      28 in the lock becomes 24 — among them `rand` and `chacha20`, plus ~15 lines\n      building the id from `SystemTime` and `getrandom` bytes rather than through\n      `Ulid::new()`. ~~`cpufeatures` is *not* one of the four: it stays, arriving via\n      `getrandom`, which is non-optional.~~ ⚠️ **That attribution was falsified on\n      re-measurement 2026-09-20 (tracker f113), while the arithmetic reproduced\n      *exactly***: in `ulid` 3.0.0 `getrandom` is a wasm32 *dev*-dependency and never\n      resolves for consumers, so the reduced set drops **seven** packages (`rand`,\n      `rand_core`, `chacha20`, `cpufeatures`, `getrandom`, `libc`, `r-efi`) and adds\n      back three (`serde` + its derive pair) — net four, 28 → 24. **Consequence,\n      decided by the user 2026-09-20: the ~15 lines' bytes come from `getrandom` as a\n      direct `dacode-protocol` dependency** — the fourth external crate, boundary edges\n      18 → 22 — which returns `getrandom`+`libc`+`r-efi` to the wide scan; `libc` is\n      class 2 per §F.1's own example row and never a marker per the gates file, `r-efi`\n       matches no marker, so no register row beyond the planned `js-sys` one. Appendix F\n       §F.3 carries the same correction. ⚠️ **And then the configuration itself was\n       corrected at M1.2's first compile (tracker f117, ratified the same day):\n       `features = [\"serde\"]` DOES NOT COMPILE** — `ulid` 3.0.0's serde module uses\n       `String` with no `extern crate alloc`, so it fails with E0433 under `no_std`,\n       and no 3.0.1 exists. The lockfile probes had *resolved* the configuration and\n       never *compiled* it — P-LIVE-012's gap in its purest form, found by the first\n       Rust this milestone wrote. Shipped instead: `ulid = { version = \"3\",\n       default-features = false }` with **no serde feature**, and the nine ULID-backed\n       ids carry a transparent private base32 serde adapter (`id.rs`'s `mod\n       ulid_serde`: `collect_str` over `Display` — the same bytes ulid's own serde\n       impl produces, so a future upstream fix re-enables the feature with the\n       fixtures proving byte-identity). Enabling `std` instead was rejected: it\n       re-adds the rand/chacha20 tail this very entry exists to keep out, overturning\n       the user's own decision. **The decision's substance, the wire bytes and the\n       boundary graph are all unchanged** (still four dependencies; serde enters the\n       tree via `dacode-protocol`'s own dep either way, so the consumer-set numbers\n       above stand as shipped — the ulid-only 28 → 24 arithmetic is now doubly\n       historical). The\n       configuration *is* the substance: §4.3/A23 makes an identifier a **correlator**,\n      not a secret, so cryptographic unpredictability is not required and the CSPRNG tail\n      is dead weight. Recorded in\n      [Appendix F §F.3's non-entries](appendices/F-c-dependencies.md), where the\n      dependency policy's evaluations live.\n      **The obligation the user attached to the decision, and why it needs a board item\n      rather than only an appendix note:** ~~`web-time` and `getrandom` are *non-optional*\n      in `ulid`, and they are what drag the wasm-bindgen tail into `cargo tree --target\n      all`.~~ **(f113, corrected 2026-09-20: `web-time` alone — a wasm32-only target\n      dependency — drags the wasm-bindgen tail in; `getrandom` never resolves from\n      `ulid` 3.0.0, whose copy of it is a wasm32 dev-dependency.)** That tail is the **only** reason `js-sys` needs the class-2 row the user\n      approved the same day (f71's option (a), which widened §F.1 and design §2.3 from\n      \"the OS\" to \"the platform\", since on `wasm32` the host runtime *is* the platform).\n      So if a future `ulid` makes ~~either~~ **`web-time`** optional, or a maintained fork drops ~~them~~ **it**, the\n      exemption should be **removed** — and an exemption for a crate no longer in the\n      tree is precisely the stale permission the register exists to prevent. No gate\n      catches it: `dep-audit.py` only reports a crate it *finds*, so a vanished `js-sys`\n      leaves its row silently intact. Same shape as **U14** (the condition on B12's zstd\n      exception) — substance in Appendix F, watch item here, because `attention.py`\n      surfaces §6 and not the appendix.\n      ~~**Note that vendoring `ulid` in-tree does not dodge the tail:** `getrandom` pulls\n      `js-sys` on wasm targets on its own~~ **(f113: also stale — `getrandom` 0.4's\n      `js-sys` is optional behind its `wasm_js` feature, so vendoring *would* dodge the\n      tail; it stays rejected because the user chose configuration over a fork)**, and the wide scan is a deliberate U88\n      decision. **Owner: whoever bumps `ulid`, checked at each M1 dependency bump.**\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U103","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§3.3 has no task-mutation `Command`, so the client→engine path to `promote` does not exist — and neither does the path to closing a `Verify::Manual` item","body":"**U103 · §3.3 has no task-mutation `Command`, so the client→engine path to\n      `promote` does not exist — and neither does the path to closing a\n      `Verify::Manual` item.** Found while transcribing A7's amendment that\n      \"`promote` from a browser is a controller action\". §8.4 now answers *who may*\n      (the controller of the session owning the item, and the write is bounded by\n      §8.5's allowlist plus §8.2's `If-Match`), but the question presumes a command\n      to issue and there is none: `promote` exists only as a `TaskOp` value on the\n      `TaskUpdated` **event** (§3.2), and the mutation path in the spec is the `todo`\n      *tool* (§4.3), which a browser does not have.\n      **The same gap blocks a rule §4.8 states normatively.** Closing a\n      `Verify::Manual` item \"requires a controller action (§8.4) recorded as its own\n      event, and the model cannot self-certify\" — so §4.8's non-self-certification\n      property is **unenforceable today**, because the only actor that can mutate the\n      tree is the model. That is a stronger statement than the missing `promote`\n      route, and it is the reason this is a P1 rather than a tidiness item.\n      **Why A7 recorded it instead of fixing it.** Adding a `Command` variant is a wire\n      change in a section A7 does not own, and the shape belongs to the **🧠 task-tree\n      brainstorm** (§5 holds it in the same session as the built-in tools), which owns\n      `TaskOp`'s promotion to a real type (**U100** lists it as the cheapest of the\n      sixteen). Inventing a variant here would have pre-empted that session — the U96\n      error. **Owner: the task-tree brainstorm, or M1.1/doc 03 if the brainstorm stays\n      deferred; owed before any UI can promote or close a manual gate.**\n      **Verify:** §3.3's `Command` enum carries a task-mutation variant whose shape the\n      brainstorm chose; §4.8's `Verify::Manual` close names the command that performs\n      it; `TaskOp` is a defined type rather than a trailing comment (U100's criterion);\n      a §14.1 test asserts an observer's promote is rejected and a controller's is not.","status":"deferred","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":"      change in a section A7 does not own, and the shape belongs to the **🧠 task-tree","references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U104","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"What a config write does to a *running* session is undecided, and a UI author will guess","body":"**U104 · What a config write does to a *running* session is undecided, and a UI\n      author will guess.** A7 landed the write surface (§8.2) and §8.2 states the part\n      it can: a write is visible to the next read. What it deliberately does not say is\n      whether a session **re-assembles** its prompt after an agent, skill or\n      `custom_rules` file changes underneath it — §5.6 owns assembly and states no\n      timing, and principle 3 says only that the prompt is assembled \"from files you can\n      read and version in git\".\n      **Why this is not free to answer.** An edit to §5.6's items 1–5 changes the\n      **fingerprinted prefix**, so applying it mid-session is a §4.5 resync\n      (truncate-and-re-emit from the longest stable prefix) rather than a silent\n      invalidation — and §13.4 makes the cached prefix's byte-identity a correctness\n      property. So the honest options are \"next turn, through §4.5's resync\" or \"next\n      session\", and picking one is a caching decision wearing a config-editing costume.\n      §8.2 records the constraint and defers the choice rather than implying one.\n      **Owner: M3 with the config editor, read together with §5.6/§4.5; the 🧠\n      agentic-loop session should see it, since §4.5's resync is its subject.**\n      **Verify:** §5.6 states when assembly re-reads the files and what a mid-session\n      change costs in §4.5's terms; §8.2's write bullet cites that answer instead of\n      deferring; a §14.1 test asserts the prefix is resynced rather than silently\n      changed (the prompt-cache bullet already tests byte-stability, so this extends it).\n      ▶️ **Annotated 2026-09-19 (M1.1a):** U103's owner line offers \"the task-tree\n      brainstorm, **or** M1.1/doc 03 if the brainstorm stays deferred\", and the\n      deferral is now scheduled rather than open-ended — the brainstorm *is* M1.10, inside\n      this milestone. So the shape stays the brainstorm's and what M1.1a owed is the\n      record: [03](part1-foundations/03-protocol.md) §3.4 now names the missing\n      task-mutation `Command` beside B17's missing `finding`, with the consequence that\n      **both arrive after M1.2 has fixtured the enum**, so both are additions with a\n      fixture and neither may be invented early.\n","status":"deferred","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":"      **Owner: M3 with the config editor, read together with §5.6/§4.5; the 🧠","references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U105","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"A missing code fence in the spec rendered 46 lines of §6 as code, and survived ~20 editing commits —","body":"**U105 · A missing code fence in the spec rendered 46 lines of §6 as code, and\n      survived ~20 editing commits — ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-19** (fix plus a standing gate,\n      one commit). `pub struct Pricing` had a closing ```` ``` ```` and no opening\n      ```` ```rust ````. CommonMark forbids an info string on a *closing* fence, so the\n      stray closer **opened** a block that ran to the next bare fence: §6's v1-implementation\n      list, the four-rate cost rule, the context-overflow path (the one U96 calls\n      load-bearing) and the whole `ExchangeRecord` definition — B11's signed M1\n      deliverable — all rendered as monospace text. **Found by accident, not by review**:\n      a fence-parity count taken while verifying the type promotions.\n      **Why nothing caught it.** Every gate this repo has is structural, and all of them\n      read the spec as *text*: `check_no_link_convention` counts `](`, `check_section_refs`\n      deliberately does **not** strip code regions, and `attention.py` counts markers.\n      None asks whether the markdown parses. ⚠️ **It also inverted `strip_fenced`**, which\n      toggles on *any* fence line, so the link resolver's view of that region was upside\n      down too — prose blanked as code, code read as prose. The spec carries no links by\n      convention, so nothing was actually mis-checked, and saying otherwise would be the\n      f96 class of overstatement. The §-reference count did **not** move when the fence was\n      fixed (412 before and after, measured), because that check never stripped code.\n      **Closed by `check_fences()` in `check-links.py`** — over *every* scanned file rather\n      than only `NO_LINK_FILES`, since a broken fence is a rendering defect wherever it\n      sits — and watched failing before it was trusted, four ways: the real defect\n      reintroduced, a closing fence deleted, an unclosed fence appended to a plan doc, and\n      the check run against `HEAD`'s spec (which it fails, naming the line). **The lesson is\n      the one this board keeps re-learning in a new costume**: a reviewer reading the spec\n      reads *prose*, and a rendering defect is invisible to that act — the reviewer of a\n      3 000-line spec is not going to notice that one region is monospace. Where a defect\n      class is invisible to the review that is actually performed, the gate is the only\n      thing that will ever find it. Appendix G's `check-links.py` row now says three\n      invariants and records why the third is not bound to the same file list.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      survived ~20 editing commits — ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-19** (fix plus a standing gate,","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U106","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`Bytes` appears in two spec signatures and is defined nowhere — and the crate the name borrows is an unregistered external dependency —","body":"**U106 · `Bytes` appears in two spec signatures and is defined nowhere — and the\n      crate the name borrows is an unregistered external dependency — ✅ CLOSED\n      2026-09-19, decided `Vec<u8>`.**\n      `ContentBlock::Image { media_type: String, data: Bytes }` (§3.4) and\n      `trait Codec { fn encode<T: Encode>(&T) -> Bytes; fn decode<T: Decode>(&[u8]) -> Result<T> }`\n      (§13.5). There is no `pub struct`/`type`/`enum Bytes` in the spec, and the sibling\n      `dacode-json` checkout does not export one either (verified against its `lib.rs`\n      re-export list), so the only `Bytes` in the ecosystem is **`bytes::Bytes`** — a crate\n      that appears in no dependency list, no [Appendix F](appendices/F-c-dependencies.md)\n      row, and no `Cargo.lock`. §13.2 fixes the *encoding* of that field (base64 via a\n      small serde module) and says nothing about the Rust type, so the two readings are\n      both available and they differ in what M1.2 types into `Cargo.toml`.\n      **Why every sweep missed it, and why that is a method finding rather than four\n      oversights.** U100's list came from a sweep that counts *names that look like types\n      in signatures*, and `Bytes` looks **imported** — it sits in the same visual class as\n      `String`, `Vec`, `PathBuf` and `RawJson`, three of which are std and one of which is\n      another crate's, so a reader's eye classifies it as resolved. f109 named three blind\n      spots (a wire field with no type, a shape given only in prose, a variant/type name\n      collision); this is a **fourth**: a name whose defining crate is *assumed* rather\n      than checked. ⚠️ The walk f109 prescribed **does** find it — it is per-field and\n      asks whether the type resolves to a `pub enum`/`struct`, which `Bytes` does not —\n      so the fix is what f109 already said: run the walk, do not re-run the sweep. The\n      walk had simply never been run over §3.4 or §13.5, both of which sit outside the\n      `Event`/`Snapshot` blocks the promotions touched.\n      **Recommendation: `Vec<u8>`, in both signatures.** Base64 encoding is a serde-module\n      property and works identically over a `Vec<u8>`; `bytes` would buy refcounted slicing\n      that nothing in a no-I/O crate needs yet (§3.4's `File` variant attaches by *path*\n      and the engine reads it, so the protocol never owns a large buffer by design).\n      ⚠️ **This is not a dependency-policy argument and must not be written as one**:\n      `bytes` is pure Rust, so §2.3's three-class table *permits* it and it would need no\n      Appendix F row — and §2.3's \"always chosen, never discovered\" line is about vendored\n      C, not about a fourth pure-Rust crate. What makes this the user's call is the\n      repository's own ask-first rule on adding *any* external crate, and the argument\n      against is that the benefit is unused today. Choosing `bytes` is legitimate; it would\n      be this milestone's **fourth or fifth** external crate, since §3.8's set is already\n      three or four rather than the two §5's M1.2 cell names.\n      **Owner: the user, ask-first, before M1.2 writes `ContentBlock`'s golden fixture.**\n      Doc 03 §3.9 G1 carries the same text for an implementer who reaches it from the crate\n      side.\n      **Verify:** §3.4 and §13.5 both name a type that exists; `cargo tree -p\n      dacode-protocol --depth 1` matches doc 03 §3.8's table exactly, with no fifth crate;\n      if `bytes` is chosen, its `gates/boundary-edges.txt` row lands in the same commit\n      (§2.3's same-commit rule) and its class is recorded in Appendix F §F.3's\n      non-entries.\n      ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-19 — the user took the recommendation: `Vec<u8>` in both\n      signatures.** Applied to §3.4 (`ContentBlock::Image.data`) and §13.5 (`Codec::encode`'s\n      return), with the reason written into §3.4 itself rather than left only here, so the\n      next reader meets the argument where the type is: this crate does no I/O, `File`\n      attaches by *path* with the engine reading it, so the only buffer the protocol ever\n      owns is one a caller handed it, and `bytes`' refcounted slicing would serve a\n      zero-copy path that does not exist — while making an external crate a dependency of\n      **all nine** crates that depend on this one (§2.2). §3.4 also records the property\n      that makes the decision cheap to reverse: the wire form is base64 either way\n      (§13.2/§13.5), so switching later is **not** a compatibility event. **No dependency\n      was added**, so no `gates/boundary-edges.txt` row and no Appendix F entry — and the\n      ask-first rule was satisfied by asking rather than by picking the free option and\n      calling it policy.\n      ⚠️ **Applying the fix found a sixth hole, in the same line.** §13.5's trait carries\n      `T: Encode` and `T: Decode`, two traits defined nowhere — invisible to the per-field\n      walk because it walks *fields* and not generic bounds, which is a fifth blind spot\n      beside f109's three and P-LIVE-013's. Recorded as doc 03 §3.9 **G6** and folded into\n      U107 below, with one measured fact that decides it: the sibling crate's `flat::typed`\n      API is driven by its **own `FlatSchema` trait, not serde** (`src/flat/typed.rs`), so\n      §13.5's reserved `flat` codec is a bounds change rather than a free swap.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      crate the name borrows is an unregistered external dependency — ✅ CLOSED","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U107","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Five spec corrections doc 03 §3.9 records — agent work, owed at or before M1.2, and each one a fixture or a build failure if it is left","body":"**U107 · Five spec corrections doc 03 §3.9 records — agent work, owed at or before\n      M1.2, and each one a fixture or a build failure if it is left.** ✅ **CLOSED\n      2026-09-20 — all five applied to the spec (plus G6's plan-side note in doc 02\n      §2.4), so M1.2 transcribes rather than invents.** G2: design §3.2 now reads\n      `Truncated { blob_id: String }` and the prose beside it says why a bare `String`\n      (storage handle, not a correlator; a newtype would add a wire-visible name while\n      buying no distinctness the scan could use). G3: design §3.6 states **one** header\n      shape `{format_version, codec?, created}` — the two former spellings are its plain\n      and compressed cases — with `codec: Option<String>` (config names the codec, not\n      the type system, per §13.5), the type in `dacode-protocol::wire` and a golden\n      fixture at M1.2. G4: design §3.1 states `Cursor(0)` is the legal \"from the start\"\n      value, and §3.7 step 5 carries the message-only bootstrap — `Subscribe { since:\n      Some(Cursor(0)) }` and fold, bounded because the log holds durable envelopes only;\n      `GetSnapshot` is an HTTP optimization, not a protocol operation, so no new\n      `Command` and no `Snapshot` down frame. G5: design §3.5 states the fold is\n      **total** (ignore what it cannot place, never panic, never invent a placeholder —\n      with the honesty and deny-set arguments), and §14.1 gained one test per case\n      (unknown-`CallId` result, unknown-turn finish, boundary with no open block). G6:\n      design §13.5's bounds are `serde::Serialize` / `serde::de::DeserializeOwned`, with\n      the `flat`-codec consequence stated beside them (a bounds change, not a second\n      impl — `FlatSchema` measured as scalar/`str`/`[str]` slots, no enums, no derive).\n      **Carry-overs, tracked where the work is rather than here:** the code halves\n      (compiling `blob_id`, the `LogHeader` type and fixture, the totality tests,\n      `codec.rs`) are M1.2's and sit in doc 03 §3.10's gate list; the `LogHeader`\n      *writer* and the replay side of §3.7 step 5 are M1.4's, now ordinary spec\n      transcription. Doc 03 §3.9's rows are struck with outcomes. The original entry follows as the audit trail.\n      None is a decision;\n      all five are the class f109 describes (a shape the spec gives in prose, or a rule it\n      assumes), and each has a recommendation already argued in doc 03 so that M1.2\n      transcribes rather than invents. **G6 joined after this item was filed** — it was\n      found applying U106's fix, which is the second time a correction discovered the next\n      hole in the line it touched.\n      **G2 — `ToolOutput::Truncated { blob_id }` has no type at all** (§3.2): the block is\n      not valid Rust as written, and the prose beside it says only that `blob_id` \"stays a\n      bare field rather than a newtype\", which rules out a newtype and names nothing.\n      Recommend `String` — it names a file inside the session directory (§12) and is\n      excluded from §4.3's scan by argument. This is f109's blind spot (1) recurring in the\n      same section that produced `CmdId`.\n      **G3 — `LogHeader` exists only as JSON, in two spellings that disagree** (§3.6):\n      `{format_version, created}` where it is defined, `{format_version, codec, created}`\n      fourteen lines later in the compression paragraph. Whether `codec` is mandatory, and\n      what a `compress = \"off\"` log writes, is unstated — and no crate owns the type,\n      though §3.6 is a protocol section while the writer is M1.4's `JsonlStore`. Recommend\n      `codec: Option<…>` defined in `dacode-protocol::wire` (so `format_version` has one\n      home beside §7.2's `Hello`, which shares the counter) and written by the store.\n      **G4 — `GetSnapshot` is an HTTP route that the replay contract treats as a protocol\n      operation.** §3.7's step 5 and §9.3 both open a resync with it, §3.6 claims §3.7's\n      contract is transport-independent, and a stdio extension (§7.2) has no HTTP channel —\n      so the contract as written is not executable by one of the three clients it names.\n      Beside it, \"from the beginning\" requires `Cursor(0)` while §3.1 says cursors start at\n      1. Recommend **no new `Command` and no `Snapshot` down frame**: a message-only client\n      subscribes from the start and folds, which is bounded because the log holds durable\n      envelopes only. What is owed is two sentences, not a variant.\n      **G5 — the fold's behaviour on an event it cannot apply is unspecified.** §3.5's\n      `CallView` note asserts the *inverse* property (an unpaired call means an incomplete\n      log) and §14.1 tests it, but nothing rules on a `ToolResult` whose `CallId` was never\n      seen or a `TurnFinished` for an unknown turn — both reachable from a truncated log or\n      a dropped envelope (§3.7 drops both tiers). Recommend the fold be **total**: ignore\n      what it cannot place, never panic, never invent a placeholder, since\n      [04](part1-foundations/04-error-model.md)'s deny set makes every easy answer a build\n      failure and this is shared code in every client that renders a session.\n      **G6 — `trait Codec`'s two bounds name traits that exist nowhere** (§13.5's\n      `T: Encode` / `T: Decode`). Found while applying U106's fix to the same line, and\n      invisible to the per-field walk because it walks *fields* and not generic bounds.\n      Recommend binding them to `serde::Serialize` / `serde::de::DeserializeOwned` and\n      saying so in §13.5: that is what the one implementation (`dacode-json`) needs and what\n      §13.3 already blesses for dacode's own types, while the alternative is two marker\n      traits with one implementor each. ⚠️ **Decide it with §13.5's own next sentence in\n      view** — the trait exists to leave room for a `flat`-based binary codec, and the\n      sibling crate's `flat::typed` API is driven by its **own `FlatSchema` trait rather than\n      serde** (measured in `src/flat/typed.rs`), so that codec is a bounds change and not a\n      free swap. It is an internal refactor with no wire effect (`Codec` is not wire-visible\n      and a log announces its own format in `LogHeader`), which is why deferring is cheap.\n      **Owner: M1.2 for G2, G5 and G6, M1.2 plus M1.4 for G3, and a spec correction with\n      M1.4's replay side for G4 ([07](part2-engine/07-session-registry.md));\n      [02](part1-foundations/02-json-strategy.md) is G6's plan-side home.**\n      **Verify:** each of the five has either a spec edit or a rule recorded in doc 03 with\n      a §14.1 test behind it; `Truncated { blob_id: String }` compiles; one `LogHeader`\n      shape with a fixture; §3.7 states the message-only bootstrap and `Cursor(0)`'s\n      legality; a fold test per unappliable-event case in G5; §13.5 names bounds that exist.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      M1.2, and each one a fixture or a build failure if it is left.** ✅ **CLOSED","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U108","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`TurnView` cannot hold a turn with more than one text block, and the fold rule as written destroys the first one —","body":"**U108 · `TurnView` cannot hold a turn with more than one text block, and the fold\n      rule as written destroys the first one — ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-19, option (a) applied.** Three spec sites assume one block and none\n      says so: §3.2's `TextEnded { turn, text }` carries \"the whole text block\"\n      (singular); §3.5's `TurnView.text: String` is commented \"the whole `TextEnded`\n      block\"; and §3.2's fold rule says the boundary **replaces** \"a block's accumulated\n      text\" — a phrase with no referent, since a single `String` field cannot distinguish\n      one block from the next. `TurnView.thinking: Option<String>` makes the same\n      assumption for reasoning.\n      **Why more than one block happens.** §6's `StreamChunk` is a *flat* sequence —\n      `Text`, `Thinking`, `ToolCallBegin/Args/End`, `Usage`, `Done`, `Failed` — with no\n      block index and no end-of-block chunk, so one completion may carry text, a tool call,\n      and more text; with interleaved thinking it carries `Thinking → Text → ToolCall →\n      Thinking → Text`. That is ordinary provider behaviour, not an edge case. §4.2 makes\n      each completion one turn, so the blocks land inside a single `TurnId`.\n      **The consequence is durable-state loss, not a rendering nit.** With one accumulator,\n      the second `TextEnded` of a turn replaces the first, and because deltas are `Live`\n      and never persisted (§3.2), nothing on disk can recover what was replaced. Replay\n      from the log reproduces the loss. And whoever writes `fold` first chooses between\n      replacing (loses a block) and appending (breaks the duplicate-delta self-heal §14.1\n      tests), and the fixture then pins whichever they picked — **U100's own failure mode,\n      \"a decision taken silently by whoever writes the first `enum`\", in a doc that had\n      already been promoted.**\n      **Three options.** **(a) Recommended: `TurnView.blocks: Vec<TurnBlock>`** with\n      `TurnBlock { Text(String), Thinking(String), Call(CallView) }`, and §3.2's rule\n      reworded from *replaces* to *closes the open block, replacing its accumulated text*\n      — a delta accumulates into the open block, `*Ended` closes it, and a delta after a\n      close opens a new one. **No block index is needed on the events**, which is the part\n      a reader will ask about: the close is authoritative, so the self-heal property\n      survives verbatim, and a *dropped* close is already covered by §3.7's gap detection\n      (a durable envelope lost from the live stream is a `seq` discontinuity, so the client\n      resyncs rather than folding a merged block). `text`/`thinking` become filters over\n      `blocks`, which is two lines of client code. **(b)** Keep `text: String` and require\n      the engine to merge a turn's text blocks before emitting `TextEnded` — no type\n      change, but the transcript loses where text sat relative to its calls, which is what\n      a review UI actually shows (\"I'll read X\" → call → \"X says Y\" collapses into one\n      paragraph above all calls), and the merge rule still has to be written into §3.2 or\n      the fold is ambiguous. **(c)** Add a block index to the four delta/boundary events\n      and make `text: Vec<String>` — widens four wire shapes to buy strictly less than (a).\n      **Blast radius is smaller than it looks, which is why now is the cheap moment.** No\n      `Event` variant changes, so **no event fixture changes**: only `Snapshot`/`TurnView`,\n      which is design §8.2's HTTP payload and not a log record, plus §3.2's wording and\n      §3.5's type. After M1.2 it is a wire change with a fixture diff and a §9.1 `protocol.d.ts`\n      regeneration; before M1.2 it is one enum.\n      **Owner: the user — it changes a signed shape — before M1.2 builds `TurnView`.\n      Recommendation (a); doc 03 §3.5 carries the do-not-build-against warning and §3.7 the\n      fixture consequence (none, which is the asymmetry that makes this cheap).**\n      **Verify:** §3.5's `TurnView` holds an ordered block list (or §3.2 states the merge\n      rule (b) requires); §3.2's fold rule says *closes* rather than *replaces* and the\n      self-heal argument still holds; a §14.1 test folds a turn whose stream is\n      text → call → text and asserts both text blocks survive with their order; the\n      duplicate-delta test still passes unchanged.\n      ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-19 — the user took option (a).** The question that prompted the\n      decision (*does this mean reverting work already done, and bringing it back later?*)\n      has a **measured** answer, and it is neither: nothing was reverted and nothing is owed\n      back. `TurnView` and `CloseReason` were added to the spec two commits earlier\n      (`bc52e50`) and had never been compiled, fixtured, generated into `protocol.d.ts`, or\n      rendered by a client — the spec carried `TurnView` at exactly **two** lines (its own\n      definition and `Snapshot.turns`'s comment) and `CloseReason` at two (`SessionClosed`\n      and its definition), both counted before editing. Both fixes are therefore **forward\n      edits**: `CallView` is unchanged and is *reused* as `TurnBlock::Call`, six of\n      `TurnView`'s eight fields survive, and the two replaced (`text`, `thinking`) become one\n      `blocks: Vec<TurnBlock>`.\n      **What landed.** §3.5's `TurnView` plus a new `TurnBlock { Text, Thinking, Call }` with\n      the reason in its doc comment; §3.5's \"**Four** types `Snapshot` names\" → five (counted,\n      not incremented); `Snapshot.turns`'s comment, \"merged text\" → \"ordered blocks\"; §3.2's\n      fold rule reworded from *replaces a block's accumulated text* to ***closes the open\n      block, replacing the text the deltas accumulated***, with the self-heal argument\n      restated for close-semantics and the two facts that make a block index unnecessary\n      (arrival order *is* block order; a dropped close is a `seq` discontinuity, so §3.7\n      resyncs rather than merging); a new §3.2 paragraph stating that **a turn may carry more\n      than one text block and more than one thinking block** and why (§6's `StreamChunk` is\n      flat and carries no end-of-block chunk, §4.2 makes each completion one turn);\n      `CallView`'s comment gaining **match a `ToolResult` by `CallId`, never by position**\n      (§4.2 dispatches concurrently, so results arrive in no order a block position\n      predicts); and §14.1's Protocol bullet gaining a **fourth** two-tier test — text →\n      call → text inside one completion, asserting both blocks survive in order with the call\n      between them, which the two single-block tests cannot see.\n      **Why it was cheap, recorded so the next shape review is scheduled the same way.** No\n      `Event` variant changed, so **no fixture existed to change**, and `Snapshot` is §8.2's\n      HTTP payload rather than a log record. The same question after M1.2 would have cost the\n      Rust struct, `fold`'s text handling, a route payload and M1.3's `protocol.d.ts` — and\n      after M1.11, a TUI that renders `.text`. That is the whole argument for reviewing a\n      designed type before the crate that fixtures it, and it worked.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      rule as written destroys the first one — ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-19, option (a) applied.** Three spec sites assume one block and none","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U109","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`CloseReason` has no variant for the commonest way a child session ends, and §5.3 never says whether it ends at all —","body":"**U109 · `CloseReason` has no variant for the commonest way a child session ends, and\n      §5.3 never says whether it ends at all — ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-19, `Completed` added.** The four variants each name a producer:\n      `User` an explicit `CloseSession`, `Superseded` a writer fence, `ParentGone` a parent\n      that closed, `Shutdown` a host that exited. **A sub-agent that finishes its task has\n      none of them** — §5.3 says the child's final message is returned as the tool result\n      and that the parent emits `SubAgentFinished`, but not whether the child session is\n      closed, by whom, or with what reason. If it is closed, every variant is wrong; if it\n      is not, child sessions accumulate in the session list forever with no state that says\n      they are finished.\n      **Why this is not symmetric with `Preview`.** Adding a `PreviewKind` later is additive\n      on the wire and clients fall back to `Other`; a close with no reason cannot be\n      rendered at all, and `SessionClosed` is `Durable`, so the gap is in the log rather\n      than in one client's rendering.\n      **Recommendation: add `Completed`** — \"the session finished the work it was spawned\n      to do\" — and one sentence in §5.3 closing a child session with it when its final\n      message is returned. ⚠️ **The doc comment must say it is child-specific**: a *primary*\n      session that finishes goes `AgentIdle(Done)` and stays open for more input (§4.2), so\n      emitting `Completed` there would make the session list say a session is over while the\n      engine is still accepting messages. §11's exit codes map from `IdleReason` and are\n      unaffected either way, which is exactly why nothing else would catch it.\n      **Owner: the user (a signed variant set), before M1.2's `SessionClosed` fixture;\n      §5.3's sentence is agent work and can ride with it.**\n      **Verify:** §3.2's `CloseReason` has a variant for every path that closes a session,\n      checked by enumerating the producers rather than by reading the list; §5.3 states when\n      a child session closes and with what reason; a §14.1 fixture covers the new variant;\n      a test asserts a primary session reaching `AgentIdle(Done)` is *not* closed.\n      ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-19 — `Completed` added, with the child-specific rule written into\n      §5.3 where the producer lives rather than only into a comment on the variant.** The\n      enum's five variants now each name a producer, established by **enumerating the paths\n      that close a session** rather than by reading the list: an explicit `CloseSession`, a\n      task that finished, a writer fence, a parent that closed, a host that exited. §5.3 now\n      states that a child session **is** closed when its final message is returned — which it\n      previously never said, so the alternative to a new variant was child sessions\n      accumulating in the session list forever with no state marking them finished. §5.3 also\n      carries the ⚠️ that makes the variant safe: a *primary* session that finishes goes\n      `AgentIdle(Done)` and stays open (§4.2), §11's exit codes map from `IdleReason` rather\n      than `CloseReason`, and the fold renders whatever it is given — so a leak would be\n      invisible except to a user reading a session list. §14.1's Engine bullet gains the\n      paired test (a primary session at `AgentIdle(Done)` emits **no** `SessionClosed`; a\n      sub-agent whose final message is returned closes with `Completed`). **`SessionClosed`'s\n      fixtures are writable at M1.2 with five variants**, which is the sequencing consequence\n      this item was filed ahead of U108 for.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      §5.3 never says whether it ends at all — ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-19, `Completed` added.** The four variants each name a producer:","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U110","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The user's stated end-state: serde derives in the shipped crates are *eventually* replaceable by a dacode-json-native typed layer — recorded so the intent survives, not scheduled","body":"**U110 · The user's stated end-state: serde derives in the shipped crates are\n      *eventually* replaceable by a dacode-json-native typed layer — recorded so the\n      intent survives, not scheduled.** Filed from the M1.2 dependency question\n      (2026-09-20): asked whether §13.3 rested on a misunderstanding (*\"I suppose we\n      would use serialize/deserialize from the dacode-json\"*) and whether serde could\n      later be drop-in replaced. **The clarification, measured in the sibling checkout\n      the same day:** today those are the same thing — dacode-json's typed layer *is*\n      serde (default features `[\"std\",\"serde\"]`; §13.3's own reason line says \"serde is\n      what the crate's public API is built from\"), so §13.3 as signed is accurate rather\n      than a misunderstanding. The serde-*free* surfaces are the dynamic layer\n      (`Doc`/`Value`/`RawJson` — no typed structs, no derives) and `flat::typed`, whose\n      `FlatSchema` was measured as **fixed-stride slots for scalars/`str`/`[str]` only —\n      no enums, no nesting, no tagging, and no derive macro yet** (`flat_struct!` is the\n      macro_rules stand-in; the module's own docs anticipate a `derive`-feature-gated\n      macro later). It cannot express `Event`/`Command` at all today. **The decision\n      taken now:** serde as a direct `dacode-protocol` dependency (M1.2), which M1.3's\n      JSON-Schema export needs anyway (schemars is serde-based, and §13.3 already\n      confines it to an offline generator that never ships). **The intent recorded for\n      later:** if dacode-json grows a native typed layer, the serde derives may be\n      replaced by it. **Preconditions, so a future session evaluates rather than\n      rediscovers:** (1) that layer covers §13.2's encoding conventions — adjacent\n      tagging, `Option` omitted, base64 bytes, canonical byte-stable output — with a\n      derive; (2) a JSON-Schema export route that does not need runtime serde; (3) a\n      sweep of every shipped crate's derives and `#[serde(…)]` attributes. **The safety\n      net is M1.2 itself:** all 80 golden fixtures must pass *byte-identical* through\n      such a migration (any diff is a compatibility event and proves the migration is\n      not semantics-preserving), the boundary diff and `dep-audit.py` show serde leaving\n      the shipped graph, and doc 03 §3.7's route-1 macro table means the `Event`/\n      `Command` enums and their derive emission live in exactly one place. A migration\n      would amend §13.3's *means* while keeping §13.2's per-surface split and §13.4's\n      byte-exactness intact — so it is a design delta with a fixture gate, not a\n      refactor nobody voted on. **Owner: nobody until a precondition exists; check when\n      dacode-json grows a derive feature.**\n      **Verify:** the item closes only on an explicit decision — either a design delta\n      applying the migration with the byte-identical-fixture gate named in its verify,\n      or the user withdrawing the intent. It must not be closed by silence.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U111","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Four `Durable` events had no fold destination —","body":"**U111 · Four `Durable` events had no fold destination — ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-20,\n      all four recommendations approved in-session and applied as A27.** Found by M1.2\n      Pass C1's **first fold walk**: writing `fold` variant by variant runs A1's tier rule\n      as a diagnostic — *every `Durable` variant must have a fold destination, or the\n      rule's own \"iff\" is false* — which is a stronger question than the per-field walk\n      behind U100/U107 asks (does each type *resolve*? → does each event *land*?). It ran\n      once, over one module, and found four, each a durable payload a bootstrapped or\n      replaying client would silently lack: **`UserMessage`** (a log-only transcript had\n      no user side, and §9.2's reconciliation line presupposed one) → `TurnBlock::User\n      { content, from }` appended per event, copied verbatim; **the `Set*` commands'\n      effects** (`SessionInfo.model`/`.perm_mode` were renderable per §9.2 and promised\n      \"all derived from the log\" per §3.5, with no producer anywhere) → `SessionCreated`\n      seeds both and a new `SettingsChanged { model?, agent?, perm_mode? }` carries\n      changes — the bundle's **one wire change, Event 30 → 31 (4 Live / 27 Durable)**;\n      **`SessionClosed`** (no fold effect, so no `Snapshot`-derived surface could say a\n      session is over — while §8.2's list annotation has always read \"(**status**, …)\"\n      and U109's own rationale was phrased in exactly those terms) → `SessionInfo.closed:\n      Option<CloseReason>`, first close wins; **`Compacted`** → `context.tokens_used =\n      after`, with §4.5's divider made explicitly a *live-rendering* affordance. Plus\n      **two fold rules stated in the spec because M1.3's TS client will re-implement\n      them**: openness is a convention, not a flag — the open block is the turn's\n      trailing same-kind block, exact under §6's flat stream (f124); and \"no open block\"\n      splits by turn existence — unknown turn ignored (totality), started turn **creates**\n      the block (the half log-only replay depends on — f126, which corrected G5's own\n      §14.1 transcription: my U107 edit had listed the creates-case as an ignore-case,\n      and the fold walk is what caught it — **the third time a correction discovered the\n      next hole in the line it touched**). §14.1 gained the user-turn replay test and the\n      creates-its-block positive. **Sequencing, recorded because it is the second time it\n      paid**: approved and applied pre-fixture, so the wire change cost zero golden diff;\n      after C2 it would have cost two goldens and a fold rewrite; after M1.3, a published\n      wire change. Registered as **A27** — the register's second entry to arrive already\n      applied, and the second from a board finding rather than a B-decision. Doc 03's\n      counts (§3.4) and fixture budget (§3.7: 80 → **82**) re-measured in the same pass.\n      **Verify:** design §3.2/§3.5/§4.5/§8.2/§14.1 carry the four destinations and the two\n      rules; doc 03's counts read 31/4/27 and its budget 82; Appendix A carries A27 in\n      the Index (struck), the applied table and a detail section; the code half (the\n      variant, the field, the fold effects, the tests) lands in M1.2's commit with t196's\n      gate list behind it; `check-links.py` exit 0 both modes.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"**U111 · Four `Durable` events had no fold destination — ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-20,","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U112","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`Checkpoint` is durable and unexposed: no §8.2 route, no `Snapshot` field, and a `Rollback` UI needs the ids from somewhere","body":"**U112 · `Checkpoint` is durable and unexposed: no §8.2 route, no `Snapshot` field,\n      and a `Rollback` UI needs the ids from somewhere.** Filed from U111's fold walk as\n      its one *verified non-fix*: `Checkpoint { id, turn }` is in the log (correctly —\n      audit and export need it, and §4.6's restore takes an id), the fold consumes\n      nothing but the cursor (correctly — `Snapshot` has no checkpoint list), and a live\n      client sees the events stream by. But §3.3's `Rollback` command carries a\n      `CheckpointId` the *client* must obtain: after a G4 message-only bootstrap the\n      client's fold has seen every `Checkpoint` envelope and kept none, and an HTTP\n      client has `/events` paging but no checkpoint-specific read. The gap is real and\n      **deliberately not filled here**: checkpoints are doc 12/M2, `CheckpointId`'s\n      *contents* are f107's carve-out (a storage decision — commit-ish or handle), and\n      the exposure shape (a `Snapshot.checkpoints` list? a §8.2 route over the\n      `CheckpointStore`? fold state after all?) depends on both. Inventing any of it\n      inside a protocol pass is the U96 error. **Owner: M2/doc 12, before any UI can\n      offer a restore.** **Verify:** doc 12 states where a client learns checkpoint ids;\n      whichever surface it chooses is spec'd before M2 builds `Rollback`'s UI side; this\n      item closes citing that text.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U113","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§13.5's `Codec::encode` is pinned infallible, but it is not total over this crate's own types — the fallback is reachable, measured","body":"**U113 · §13.5's `Codec::encode` is pinned infallible, but it is not total over\n      this crate's own types — the fallback is reachable, measured.** ✅ **CLOSED\n      2026-09-20 — the user took the recommendation the same session it was filed:\n      §13.5 now reads `encode → Result<Vec<u8>, CodecError>`** (and `decode` names\n      `CodecError` too, which the bare `Result<T>` never did), with the rationale in\n      the spec itself: the serializer underneath is fallible, the signature is not\n      total over this crate's own types (the non-UTF-8 `PathBuf` trigger, all three\n      carriers named), and the empty-buffer fallback an infallible signature forces\n      is a blank line in a log whose cursor is position-derived (§3.6) — one\n      record's silent corruption of every later cursor. **Applied to the crate in\n      the same pass**: `try_encode` deleted outright (one total API, no alias to\n      forget to prefer — the interim M1.4 obligation is **moot**, the store simply\n      propagates `encode`'s `Result`), `CodecError::Encode`'s variant doc corrected\n      (it claimed no crate type could fail), 22 mechanical call sites updated, and\n      the hazard is pinned by a permanent test —\n      `a_non_utf8_path_is_an_encode_error_not_an_empty_record`, whose **first**\n      assertion is `assert_ne!(encoded, Ok(Vec::new()), \"the empty-buffer fallback\n      is back\")`, so a reintroduced infallible wrapper fails by name. **The goldens\n      are byte-identical (md5 manifest over all 82, before == after)** — the\n      signature change cannot alter bytes for valid values, and the bless-less\n      fixture suite re-proved all 82 against fresh encodings. Primary reviewer\n      CONFIRMED the application (their finding 2(a) verbatim); 119 tests green.\n      The original entry follows as the audit trail. M1.2's codec pass\n      found the signature `fn encode<T: Serialize>(&T) -> Vec<u8>` cannot honestly be\n      infallible: dacode-json's serializer is fallible, and the dual review **empirically\n      reached the documented empty-buffer fallback** with a non-UTF-8 `PathBuf` — legal\n      on Unix, and carried by `SessionCreated.cwd`, `ContentBlock::File.path` and\n      `PermScope::Edit.paths` (`PathBuf`'s own `Serialize` impl rejects it). The crate\n      did the best thing available under the pinned signature: the fallback is\n      documented, `DacodeJsonCodec::try_encode` is the total path, and the doc comment\n      now names the trigger. **Why it still needs a ruling:** if a store ever appends\n      `encode`'s output, the empty buffer is a blank line in `events.jsonl`, and since\n      §3.6 makes the JSONL cursor position-derived, one blank line silently corrupts\n      every later position-derived cursor — §3.6's own \"corrupt in ordering rather than\n      bytes, the kind nobody notices\" class. And a non-UTF-8 path can never *decode*\n      back (JSON text is UTF-8), so the alternatives are a `Result`-returning `encode`\n      or engine-side rejection of non-UTF-8 paths at construction. **Recommendation:\n      amend §13.5 to `fn encode<T: Serialize>(value: &T) -> Result<Vec<u8>, CodecError>`**\n      — one line, pre-M1.4 (whose store is the first caller), and it retires both the\n      fallback and the trap; the alternative (reject non-UTF-8 paths in the engine) is\n      also legitimate but is M1.4/M1.6 work and leaves `encode`'s signature lying about\n      totality either way. **Interim obligation until ruled, recorded where M1.4 meets\n      it: `try_encode` on every append and broadcast path** (codec.rs's doc comment\n      carries the same sentence). **Owner: the user (§13.5 is signed spec); owed before\n      M1.4 writes its first log line.**\n      **Verify:** §13.5's signature and codec.rs agree; the empty-buffer fallback is\n      gone or the rejection rule is spec'd; M1.4's store calls the total path; a §14.1\n      test covers the non-UTF-8-path case whichever way the ruling goes.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      this crate's own types — the fallback is reachable, measured.** ✅ **CLOSED","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U114","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"A steering `UserMessage` injected inside an open text run silently breaks §3.2's convergence property, and no fold can repair it — the constraint belongs to the loop","body":"**U114 · A steering `UserMessage` injected inside an open text run silently breaks\n      §3.2's convergence property, and no fold can repair it — the constraint belongs to\n      the loop.** Found by the dual review of M1.2's fold: f124's trailing-block\n      convention was proven exact for **§6's producer chunks** (a same-kind run ends only\n      where another kind intervenes or the turn ends), but `UserMessage` is not a §6\n      chunk — nothing constrains §4.2's steering to place one at a block boundary. If it\n      lands between a text run's deltas and its `*Ended`: the live fold shows\n      `[Text(\"A\"), User, Text(\"AB\")]` (the boundary replaces only the *trailing* block,\n      so \"A\" survives twice) while the log-only fold shows `[User, Text(\"AB\")]` —\n      divergence on a legal stream, silently, and openness is unrepresentable in durable\n      state, so the fold has no repair. **The fix is one producer-side rule: close the\n      open block (emit its `*Ended`) before appending a mid-turn `UserMessage`** — or,\n      equivalently, place steering only at block boundaries. **Recorded in code where\n      M1.3's TS re-implementer inherits it** (`TurnBlock::User`'s and fold's\n      `user_message` doc comments, both citing this item), but the rule is §4.2's and\n      the loop session owns it. **Owner: the 🧠 loop session (M1.9) with doc 08/09; owed\n      a sentence in §4.2 before any engine emits steering — until then the constraint\n      exists only in the protocol crate's comments.**\n      **Verify:** §4.2 (or doc 08/09 under it) states the close-before-inject rule; a\n      §14.1 Engine test streams steering mid-run and asserts the live and log-only folds\n      converge; this item closes citing both.","status":"deferred","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":"      the loop session owns it. **Owner: the 🧠 loop session (M1.9) with doc 08/09; owed","references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U115","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`AgentConfig` and `ModelRef` have no producer anywhere in M1, and M1.4 has to construct both","body":"**U115 · `AgentConfig` and `ModelRef` have no producer anywhere in M1, and M1.4\n      has to construct both.** Design §4.1 lists them as *resolved* config on the\n      `Session` aggregate, and §4.1's own paragraph makes them engine-internal opaque\n      handles whose fields are an implementation choice — which is correct and is not\n      the problem. The problem is that **resolution is layered config discovery**, and\n      that is `dacode-config`, whose plan doc is\n      [17](part3-capabilities/17-config-agents-skills.md): §4's table puts it at **M2**\n      and **no M1 row builds it**. So M1.4 defines both types and populates them from\n      CLI flags plus built-in defaults, and M2 replaces that resolution rather than\n      adding to it. Recorded in [07](part2-engine/07-session-registry.md) §7.2's\n      per-field table, where the interim is visible beside the twelve fields that are\n      not interim. **Owed by M1**, and the reason is a specific failure rather than\n      tidiness: the next row is M1.5's providers, which needs to turn a `ModelRef` into\n      a §6 `ModelInfo` and a credential, and **a second, parallel way to name a model\n      appearing there is the defect this item exists to prevent** — it would be\n      invisible to every gate, since both ways would compile and both would work.\n      ⚠️ This is the f89/U70 class (an assignment nobody sweeps for) rather than a\n      design hole: §5's M1 rows were written before doc 17's milestone was consulted\n      from the engine side. **Verify:** M1.4's code names one resolution path and doc\n      07 §7.2 cites it; when doc 17 is deepened it states that it *replaces* M1.4's\n      interim rather than supplementing it, and this item closes citing that text.\n      ⚠️ **WIDENED at Pass C1 (2026-09-23), still open, and the widening is the item's own failure mode\n      arriving one row early.** The interim landed — `session.rs`'s `AgentConfig { name }` and\n      `ModelRef { provider, model }`, `pub(crate)`, both docs naming doc 17 as the replacement — and doc 07\n      §7.2 cites it, so the verify clause's first half is met. But building it established two things this\n      item did not carry. **(1) There are no built-in defaults to populate it from**, which is what §7.2's\n      \"populated from CLI flags and built-in defaults\" promised: §5.1's layer 1 says \"built-in defaults\n      (embedded)\" and names none, and §5.2 lists five built-in agents without marking one default, so\n      neither type has a `Default` impl and inventing one would hard-code a product decision nobody made.\n      **M1.11's CLI row is where a default becomes necessary**, and this item is where that obligation is\n      recorded. **(2) No protocol surface carries a provider at all**: `CreateSession.model:\n      Option<String>`, `SetModel.model: String` and `SessionCreated.model: Option<String>` are each one bare\n      string, and §5.2's `model = \"anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-6\"` is the *only* specified join — which the\n      protocol's own samples do not follow (`\"claude-sonnet\"`, in three samples across `command.rs` and\n      `event.rs`). So the string → `(provider, model)` split `ModelRef` asserts **has no specified\n      derivation**, and if Pass C2 invents one to populate the field while M1.5's provider row invents\n      another to consume it, that is precisely the second, parallel way to name a model this item exists to\n      prevent. **The split is added to this item with M1.5 named as the row that needs it**, because §6's\n      `Provider::id()` lookup is `ModelRef`'s first consumer; `AgentConfig` deliberately carries **no**\n      `model` field for the same reason, and how an agent file's `model` reconciles with a session's is doc\n      17's decision. Verify clause extended: this item now also closes only when the split has one specified\n      derivation cited by both M1.5's code and doc 17.\n      ✅ **The M1.5 side is now SPECIFIED *and built* — doc 13 §13.7 rules it and M1.5 Pass A landed it\n      (`crates/dacode-providers/src/resolve.rs`, 2026-09-24) — so the derivation exists in code and the item\n      stays open only on doc 17's citation.** ⚠️ **And the type is now single**: Pass A found that defining\n      `ModelRef` beside the derivation duplicated M1.4 C1's `pub(crate)` copy in `session.rs`, so core's was\n      deleted and its 14 references repointed at `dacode_providers::ModelRef` — `rg 'struct ModelRef' crates/`\n      returns exactly one hit. That is this item's own defect one level down (two identical structs compile and\n      both work, and no gate sees the drift); doc 13 §13.7 carries the ruling and its four reasons.\n      **What is still unspecified is the *inverse* join** — `(provider, model)` → string — which is why\n      `registry.rs:580-592` still leaves `SessionInfo.model` as `None` and its comment must keep saying the\n      *pair-to-string* direction is open rather than the split (the range was `:507-511` until M1.6 Pass B\n      moved it — U159's class, re-measured 2026-10-01). The rule: **split at the first `/`**, provider id\n      before it (matched against §6's `Provider::id()`), model id after it **verbatim and unsplit** — which is\n      why first and not last, since provider ids are a small closed set with no `/` while model ids routinely\n      contain one (`openrouter/openai/gpt-4o` → `(\"openrouter\", \"openai/gpt-4o\")`). **A bare string with no\n      `/` is REFUSED with an error naming the required form, and deliberately does not fall back to a default\n      provider** — that is this item's own finding (1) honoured rather than worked around, since inventing a\n      default would hard-code the product decision this item says nobody made; if **M1.11** lands a default\n      provider, the rule gains an optional branch and does not change shape. Doc 13 §13.7 also carries the\n      **inbound assignment** for M1.5's code pass that f89's lesson asks to be recorded where a sweep finds\n      it: the three protocol samples using bare `\"claude-sonnet\"` (across `command.rs` and `event.rs`) are\n      corrected to the joined form **in the same commit that lands `resolve.rs`**, having first verified they\n      are doc-comment samples and not golden fixtures, since a sample inside a fixture would be a wire change.\n      The credential interim is specified beside it on the same replace-not-supplement terms. **What remains\n      for closure is unchanged and still two citations**: M1.5's code citing §13.7, and doc 17 stating it\n      *replaces* that derivation.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U116","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§4.1 named a concrete map crate in prose, and the prose is now a choice rather than a requirement — but nothing pins which choice was made or when to revisit it","body":"**U116 · §4.1 named a concrete map crate in prose, and the prose is now a choice\n      rather than a requirement — but nothing pins which choice was made or when to\n      revisit it.** Design §4.1 said the registry is a `DashMap<SessionId,\n      Arc<Mutex<Session>>>`; measured during the M1.1b doc pass, `dashmap` 6.2.1 adds\n      **11 packages** (`crossbeam-utils`, `hashbrown`, `lock_api`, `once_cell`,\n      `parking_lot_core`,\n      `scopeguard`, `smallvec`, `cfg-if`, `libc`) for a map whose access pattern is\n      open/close/lookup, the hot path being *inside* one session. The user ruled the\n      spelling **illustrative** (2026-09-21, Q4), §4.1 gained a precision edit saying\n      the concrete type is an implementation choice with the criterion named, and\n      [07](part2-engine/07-session-registry.md) §7.3 records what M1.4 builds instead:\n      `std::sync::RwLock<HashMap<…>>`, plus the idiom that makes a `std` lock correct\n      here — *look up, clone the `Arc`, drop the guard, then lock the session* — and\n      the fact that no deny-lint catches a helper returning a guard to a caller that\n      awaits (`clippy::await_holding_lock` is not among doc 04's six), so that one is a\n      review item. **What is still owed is the revisit obligation, and it is the same\n      shape as U102's**: a decision taken on an access-pattern argument with no\n      measurement behind it, which no gate will notice going stale. The trigger is the\n      M1.8 bench row (registry lookup contention under a server hosting many sessions)\n      or [20](part4-hosts-clients/20-server-scaling.md)'s scaling pass, whichever comes\n      first; if either shows contention, sharding is additive and the spec edit already\n      permits it. **Verify:** M1.8's bench list names a registry-lookup case, or doc 20\n      records why one is unnecessary; this item closes citing whichever landed.\n      ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-24 (M1.1c), through the second arm with a substitution stated\n      rather than glossed: the record landed in [33](part6-quality/33-benchmarking.md)\n      §33.5, not doc 20** — doc 20 is an **M3** doc still at summary depth, which hard\n      rule 1 forbids deepening early, while §14.2's *where the detail lives* sentence\n      makes doc 33 the home of scorer detail. M1.8's bench list does **not** name a\n      registry-lookup case, and doc 33 §33.5's row 8 records why: the registry is\n      `pub(crate)` behind the visibility narrowing **both** C1 reviewers demanded (a\n      `pub Session` lets any dependent crate `session.log.take()` and drop the writer,\n      turning M8's \"call `shutdown`, not drop\" into a workspace-wide review obligation),\n      and widening it for a bench with no production caller would spend that safety\n      argument on a measurement nobody ships. **The revisit obligation does not die with\n      the closure**: it re-homes to §5's **M3** item, named there in terms — d6's\n      widening is the row that makes the registry benchable and therefore the row that\n      chooses between a registry-lookup-contention bench (memory-per-session beside it,\n      doc 33 §33.5's rows 7–8) and a recorded reason not to; if contention shows, §4.1's\n      precision edit already permits sharding additively.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-24 (M1.1c), through the second arm with a substitution stated","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U117","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§3.6 specified a per-field compression discriminator and never gave its on-disk shape — a durable format hole found by M1.4's opening walk, ruled and applied 2026-09-21","body":"**U117 · §3.6 specified a per-field compression discriminator and never gave\n      its on-disk shape — a durable format hole found by M1.4's opening walk, ruled\n      and applied 2026-09-21.** A15 landed the mechanism (discriminator\n      `plain | compressed`, config-driven allowlist over durable fields, ~1 KiB\n      floor, configurable level, one `compress` knob) and §14.1's Storage-codec\n      bullet requires **three** tests over it, which\n      [07](part2-engine/07-session-registry.md) §7.10 item 7 assigns to **M1.4**.\n      But no section said what a compressed field *looks like* inside a JSONL\n      record: §3.6's compressed example showed only the `LogHeader`, and A15's\n      banner recorded the two discriminator values and stopped. Since line 1 of a\n      log carries `format_version: 1`, whatever M1.4 wrote would have been pinned by\n      it — so the hole was a hard gate on Pass A rather than a detail to settle in\n      review. ⚠️ **Two things the walk established that no reading of §3.6 had.**\n      First, the allowlist's `ToolResult.output` names an *object*, not a string —\n      it is a `ToolOutput`, itself adjacently tagged — so the leaf that compresses is\n      `ToolOutput::Text.text`, while `Truncated` is already a blob and `Synthetic` is\n      under the floor. Second, **serde attributes are per type and not per call\n      site**, so no annotation on `Event` can produce a log-only shape: whatever the\n      ruling, the store owed its own write path for the targeted variants and a read\n      path accepting both forms. A 31-variant mirror is not that; a 2-variant one is,\n      with a test asserting its plain encoding is byte-identical to `Codec::encode`.\n      **Ruled (option 1 of four put to the user): a sibling `<leaf>_codec` key whose\n      absence means `plain`**, the compressed value being base64 of the zstd bytes.\n      The deciding property is that **an uncompressed record is then byte-identical\n      to the wire encoding of the same envelope**, so `compress = \"off\"` and\n      `JsonlStore`'s `\"blobs\"` default both yield a log any client can fold with no\n      store-side transform, §8.2's `/export` is wire bytes, and §14.1's fixture\n      corpus describes a log record as accurately as a socket frame. A tagged value\n      (`\"text\":{\"codec\":…,\"data\":…}`) loses all three by reshaping a field that was\n      never compressed, and additionally makes one field's JSON type depend on\n      config. The rejected third option was **deferring `allowlist` to M3's\n      `SqliteStore`**, where a field is a BLOB column and the shape question does not\n      arise — defensible on doc 05 §5.3's own framing (\"when storing in SQLite\"), but\n      it moves a §14.1-mandated test out of the row §14.1 puts it in, which is not a\n      call to take silently. **Applied to design §3.6** in the same pass: the leaf\n      resolution, both example lines, the three properties, and the log-surface-only\n      scope. [05](part2-engine/05-event-log-storage.md) §5.3 carries the plan-side\n      note.       **Closed the same day, on the U107/U113 pattern: the hole was a missing\n      spec rule and the rule is now written**, so M1.4 transcribes rather than\n      invents. What remains is ordinary row work and is carried by §5's M1.4 cell,\n      not by this item. **Verify:** design §3.6 carries the two example lines\n      and the sibling-key rule; M1.4 Pass A ships the §14.1 round-trip, the index\n      rebuild over a compressed log, and the no-`_codec`-outside-the-allowlist scan,\n      all three green under `cargo test -p dacode-core`.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U118","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"What a store does when `events.jsonl` ends mid-record was nowhere specified — ruled (b) by the user and applied to §3.6 and §12, 2026-09-21","body":"**U118 · What a store does when `events.jsonl` ends mid-record was nowhere\n      specified — ruled (b) by the user and applied to §3.6 and §12, 2026-09-21.** §3.6 fixes the durability\n      boundaries and §12 calls the log unrecoverable, but neither says what an\n      *opener* does when the tail is a partial line — reachable whenever a\n      `write_all` larger than the buffer is interrupted between syscalls, or a\n      crash lands mid-flush. Appending there would splice a new record onto the\n      fragment's line, and since the cursor is position-derived that silently\n      renumbers every later record, which is exactly §3.6's \"quiet\" failure\n      class. **A1 refuses the open** (`StoreError::TornTail`) rather than\n      repairing, because repair means rewriting a file the append-only rule\n      protects and nobody has ruled on it. The alternative — truncate to the\n      last newline, the same WAL move `index.bin` already gets — is defensible\n      (a fragment was never a record, so nothing readable is lost) and is what\n      a user would probably expect, but it is a *log* rewrite and needs a\n      decision rather than an implementer's default. **Gates:** nothing today;\n      a session that crashed mid-write cannot be reopened until it is answered.\n      **Ruled: option (b) — truncate to the last complete newline-terminated line,\n      fsync, proceed, and report the discarded byte count to the caller.** The user's\n      reasoning was that failing is the worse option and loses more, which is the\n      argument the item's own (a) row could not answer: §3.6's design has the *kernel*\n      release the lock on process death precisely so the next opener gets in, and an\n      opener that then cannot read the file makes a mid-write crash permanently brick\n      the session for `attach` and `run` alike. **What made (b) safe rather than\n      lossy is that the cursor is store-assigned on a *successful* append** — a\n      fragment was never acknowledged with one, so no client holds a reference to\n      anything removed. It is also §12's validate-and-truncate recovery for\n      `index.bin` applied to the file the index projects, so it is consistency rather\n      than a new idea. ⚠️ **The one thing this had to state explicitly, because\n      AGENTS.md's Never-Do list says \"never rewrite or drop event-log entries\":** an\n      *entry* is a complete record with an assigned cursor, so bytes that never became\n      one are not an entry and nothing readable is rewritten. A log corrupt *above* its\n      last complete line stays unrecoverable and gets no recovery path — §12's\n      asymmetry is untouched, and §3.6 now says so in terms rather than leaving the\n      truncation rule to be read as a general repair licence. **Applied to design §3.6**\n      (a new bullet beside the cursor rule, naming both behaviours it rules out and\n      why) **and §12** (the cross-reference beside `index.bin`'s own torn-tail\n      sentence). Recording the truncation *in* the log would need a new `Event`\n      variant — a wire change, ask-first, with M1.3's publish constraint live — so\n      the ruling deliberately stops at \"reported to the caller\". **Verify:** design\n      §3.6 and §12 both carry the rule; `JsonlStore::open` truncates rather than\n      refuses, surfaces the discarded byte count, and has a test per branch — a torn\n      tail recovers, a log corrupt above it still fails, and the recovered session's\n      next cursor continues from the last complete record.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U119","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Three doc claims M1.4 Pass A1 falsified by building against them — all three discharged 2026-09-21","body":"**U119 · Three doc claims M1.4 Pass A1 falsified by building against them — all three discharged 2026-09-21.**\n      (a) [05](part2-engine/05-event-log-storage.md) §5.1's `EventStore` list is\n      **four** methods and the store needs **five**: §3.6's fsync boundaries are a\n      requirement on *every* backend, so `flush` belongs on the trait — Pass B's\n      `Flush`-with-reply calls it through `dyn EventStore`, which is the only thing\n      that makes the pre-side-effect boundary *ordered*. §5.1 also gives\n      `append(session, Envelope)` with no return type; the cursor is store-assigned\n      (§3.6) and the actor broadcasts the envelope *after* the writer answers\n      ([07](part2-engine/07-session-registry.md) §7.4), so it returns the stored\n      `Envelope` and not a bare `Cursor`. (b) [07](part2-engine/07-session-registry.md)\n      §7.8's `fd-lock` row reads \"+6 — `rustix` 1.1.5, `libc`, `errno`, `bitflags`,\n      `cfg-if`\"; the committed resolve adds **17** packages and no `libc` — the\n      probe counted the unix tail and not `windows-sys`'s own\n      (`windows-link`, `windows-targets`, nine `windows_*_{gnu,gnullvm,msvc}`\n      crates, and **two** `windows-sys` versions: 0.59.0 via `fd-lock` directly and\n      0.61.2 via `errno`/`rustix`). The *marker* set §7.8 predicted is right\n      (`linux-raw-sys`, `windows-sys`), so the gate rows are unaffected; the count\n      is what is wrong, and `Cargo.lock` went 39 → 56. (c) `cargo deny check`\n      passes but **warns** on that duplicate `windows-sys` under\n      `multiple-versions = \"warn\"`; whether a cfg(windows)-only duplicate is worth\n      a `[bans]` skip is a policy call, not an implementer's. **All three landed\n      the same day.** (a) doc 05 §5.1 now lists **five** methods with\n      `append(session, Envelope) -> Envelope`, and says why `flush` must be on the\n      *trait* rather than on `JsonlStore` alone: a second backend arriving without\n      it would silently downgrade §3.6's strongest guarantee. (b) doc 07 §7.8's\n      `fd-lock` row is re-measured from the committed resolve rather than from the\n      probe — **+17**, not +6 — and `libc`/`cfg-if` are *not* new, because\n      `getrandom` already put them in the lock at M1.2. ⚠️ **The probe measured an\n      isolated crate, which is f135's own limitation arriving in a committed\n      number**: it is the third recorded measurement falsified by re-measuring\n      against the real tree, after f113 and f117. The report's `windows_*` count\n      was nine and the `Cargo.lock` diff says **eight**, so the row was written\n      against the diff and not against the report. (c) `deny.toml` gains a\n      version-pinned `skip` for `windows-sys` 0.59 with the reason in a comment:\n      `fd-lock` constrains `>=0.52,<0.60` while its own `rustix`/`errno` tail uses\n      0.61, so the duplicate can never unify, and a permanent warning is exactly\n      what that file's header says trains whoever runs it to ignore warnings.\n      Pinned by version so a *third* copy still warns; no gate notices a stale\n      skip, which is U102's shape again. **Verified:** doc 05 §5.1 names five\n      methods; doc 07 §7.8's row reads +17; `cargo deny check` exits 0 with the\n      duplicate warning gone rather than merely tolerated.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"imported checked","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U120","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`fd-lock`'s guard cannot live in a struct without `unsafe`, which changes what Pass B's writer thread should own","body":"**U120 · `fd-lock`'s guard cannot live in a struct without `unsafe`, which\n      changes what Pass B's writer thread should own. ⚠️ RULED 2026-09-21 — the\n      `unsafe` goes, and the authority is doc 04 §4.6 rather than a preference.**\n      §4.6 says the one place `unsafe` is plausible at all is a future binary codec\n      for the event log, *\"and it is not needed for M0–M1\"* — a signed claim in a\n      doc deepened during the M0 doc pass, so A1's three items contradict it rather\n      than exercising a judgement it left open. ⚠️ **Wording note for whoever next\n      edits this item**: an earlier draft said \"deepened at M0\", which\n      `attention.py`'s gating regex reads as `at M` + `0` and so classified this as\n      gating **M0** — a milestone that closed in 2026-09-16. The P0 is right and the\n      derivation was not, which is U116's trap arriving a second time from a\n      different preposition. **Gates:** M1 work — Pass A1 does not commit until\n      `store/lock.rs` is `unsafe`-free. Doc 07 §7.4 now carries the mechanism (two locals\n      in one scope), its consequence (the `Held` error crosses back over a oneshot\n      rendezvous), what it costs (the by-construction guarantee that a writer\n      cannot exist without the lock, replaced by the same kind of review obligation\n      §7.3 already accepts), and what must not be re-introduced to buy that\n      guarantee back. ✅ **Discharged the same day: `store/lock.rs` is\n      `unsafe`-free, and the ruling is now compiler-enforced rather than a\n      convention** — `crates/dacode-core/src/lib.rs` carries\n      `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` citing §4.6, so a regression is a build failure and\n      not a grep. `SessionLock` as an owning type is **deleted**; `store/lock.rs` is\n      two free functions (`open_lock_file` + `try_write`) and every caller holds the\n      `RwLock` and its guard as locals, which is doc 07 §7.4's snippet verbatim.\n      **One consequence worth keeping: `JsonlStore` became `Send + Sync`** — the\n      `&'static mut` field was what made it `!Sync` — and there is a test asserting\n      it, because Pass C's registry holds `Arc<tokio::sync::Mutex<Session>>` and a\n      `!Sync` store would have surfaced there as a confusing trait bound rather than\n      as a lock problem. `try_write(&mut self)`\n      returns `RwLockWriteGuard<'_, File>` borrowing the `RwLock` it came from, so\n      a type holding both the lock and its guard is self-referential and Rust has\n      no safe form of that. [07](part2-engine/07-session-registry.md) §7.4 says the\n      writer thread \"owns the `fd_lock::RwLockWriteGuard`\" as though that were a\n      field; it is not. **A1's `SessionLock`** pays for it with `Box::leak` +\n      two `Box::from_raw` + one `unsafe impl Send` (all three documented in\n      `store/lock.rs`, with the rejected alternative — a second dependency calling\n      `flock(LOCK_UN)` directly — named there). **Pass B has a cheaper route**:\n      inside a `std::thread` closure the `RwLock` and its guard are two *locals*,\n      which is exactly the shape `fd-lock`'s API assumes, and needs no `unsafe` at\n      all. If B takes it, `JsonlStore` stops owning the lock and A1's three\n      `unsafe` items can go — but then the refusal error has to travel back over\n      the channel B already builds, so `open` becomes a rendezvous rather than a\n      call. **Decide before Pass B, not during it**: the two shapes put the\n      `Held` error on different sides of a thread boundary, and\n      §14.1's second-opener test asserts on whichever one lands. **Verify:**\n      `store/lock.rs` either carries the `unsafe` with this item struck, or is\n      `unsafe`-free with the acquisition inside the writer thread.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      guarantee back. ✅ **Discharged the same day: `store/lock.rs` is","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U121","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`index.bin`'s on-open validation recovered a *crash* and not a *corruption*, and the difference was measured by M1.4 Pass A1's primary review rather than argued","body":"**U121 · `index.bin`'s on-open validation recovered a *crash* and not a\n      *corruption*, and the difference was measured by M1.4 Pass A1's primary\n      review rather than argued.** Doc 05 §5.5's invariant 3 said \"validate **the\n      last record** against the log and truncate to the last good one\", and the code\n      transcribed it faithfully — walking back from the tail to the first record\n      that validates and accepting everything before it **unlooked-at**, on a stated\n      assumption that \"the corruption model is a lost *suffix*\". That is true of a\n      crash, because the projection is written after the log, and false of a\n      corruption, which is not suffix-shaped. ⚠️ **The consequence inverts the\n      asymmetry §12 says decides which file is the truth**: a damaged offset in the\n      *middle* of the projection survives the tail check untouched and then\n      renumbers the log. Measured with three probes — one index record\n      `{seq:1, offset:0}` over a three-record log made the `LogHeader` cursor 1 and\n      turned a healthy log unbootstrappable (`range(Cursor(0))` → `CorruptRecord`);\n      a middle offset nudged one byte into its line was served as-is and reported\n      clean; and, sharpest, a **last** record whose offset names another *legal* line\n      start assigned the next append a cursor two past the log's real length, so two\n      cursors became phantoms nothing ever carried, `LogRecovery` reported clean at\n      every step, and the damage persisted across a reopen. That is §3.6's quiet\n      failure verbatim — *\"§3.7's gap detection would report phantom\n      discontinuities and clients would resync forever\"* — arriving from a\n      *rebuildable projection* in a single-writer session. **The existing tests\n      missed it because all three corruption fixtures were ones validation\n      rejects** (all-`0xff`, an offset past EOF, a mid-line offset as the last\n      record); nothing tested a corrupt offset that still looks legal, or a corrupt\n      record that is not last. **Ruled and applied to doc 05 §5.5**: the tail check\n      is necessary and not sufficient, and what closes the gap must stay cheap\n      enough for the index to be worth having — verifying every record at open costs\n      a seek each, and verifying them in one forward pass costs a full sequential\n      read, which is what a rebuild costs, so at that point the index has bought\n      nothing. The rule is therefore **O(1) verification at the two points of use**\n      (an `append` checks the last index record really names the log's last complete\n      line *before* assigning the next cursor; a `range` read compares the `seq`\n      inside the record it decoded against the one it was asked for) **plus an\n      in-memory arithmetic sweep** of the surviving prefix at open. **Verify:** all\n      three probes become tests asserting `tail()` and a clean `range(Cursor(0))`;\n      `cargo test -p dacode-core` green with open still sub-linear in the log size;\n      doc 05 §5.5's invariant 3 carries the rule. ✅ **Discharged in Pass A1's fix\n      round, and the primary review mutation-proved each layer itself rather than\n      accepting the claim** (M1 removes the `header_end` floor → probe A's two tests\n      fail; M2 removes the sweep → probe G's fails and nothing else; M3 removes the\n      decoded-`seq` compare; M4 removes the rebuild-and-retry → probe C's fails). All\n      three probes are tests in `store/corruption.rs`, each written against a fixture\n      the *old* code accepted — which is the property that separates them from the\n      three pre-existing corruption fixtures, all of which validation already\n      rejected and so proved nothing. **Sub-linearity is proved structurally rather\n      than by timing**: `sweep`'s signature takes no `File`, so it *cannot* perform\n      I/O, and `open_does_not_verify_every_record_against_the_log` uses probe C's\n      fixture to show open accepting a middle record `validates` would have rejected\n      in one seek, with `dropped == 0`. The implementer's refusal to write a timing\n      test was right — it would have measured the wrong axis and flaked. ⚠️ **One\n      consequence worth keeping: the fix found a second instance of the same class.**\n      `jsonl.rs`'s `until - window.from` was unguarded, and in **release** it does\n      not panic — `take` wraps, becomes `read_to_end`, and the limit `break`\n      truncates to the *right* record, so `read_range` returns `Ok` with exactly the\n      envelope asked for. A production caller accepts it, never learns the projection\n      lied, never triggers the rebuild, and leaves the damaged `index.bin` on disk.\n      `saturating_sub` routes the decreasing case into the count check; a `u64` sweep\n      of all eight production subtractions in `store/` found the other six provably\n      guarded (tracker f150).","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      doc 05 §5.5's invariant 3 carries the rule. ✅ **Discharged in Pass A1's fix","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U122","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"A log corrupt *above* its last complete line opens successfully, which is sound for a reader and unsafe for a writer — and nothing at M1.4 or M1.5 is otherwise required to notice","body":"**U122 · A log corrupt *above* its last complete line opens successfully, which\n      is sound for a reader and unsafe for a writer — and nothing at M1.4 or M1.5 is\n      otherwise required to notice.** Design §3.6 gives such a log \"no recovery\n      path\", and Pass A1 is faithful: the open succeeds, `LogRecovery.is_clean()`\n      reports true, and the failure surfaces as `StoreError::CorruptRecord` on the\n      `range()` that reaches it. **The primary review's disposition is that this\n      needs no ruling and must not change**, and the argument is worth keeping\n      because the obvious \"fix\" is worse: §3.6 and §12 both speak of *recoverability*,\n      not *openability*; decoding every record at open is O(log size) against §3.7's\n      fast resync; and refusing would convert a *partial* failure into a *total* one,\n      destroying the readable suffix that a read-only `attach`, an `export`, or a\n      client already holding its state can still serve — the same shape as §3.6's own\n      argument against refusing a torn tail. The failure is loud, typed and honest\n      (`CorruptRecord { path, seq, source }`, distinct from both a `Codec` error and\n      a torn tail, with a test asserting the bytes are still there). ⚠️ **What it\n      does leave is an obligation nobody currently carries.** Doc 07 §7.2's table\n      gives `Session` **no `Snapshot` field** and puts `context: Context` at **M1.6**,\n      so nothing in the M1.4 aggregate has to fold the log at open, and `append` never\n      consults decodability. The sequence that follows: resume a session whose record\n      200 is corrupt, send a message, watch the live UI work normally — while a stdio\n      extension's `Subscribe { since: Cursor(0) }` (§3.7 step 5, the *only* bootstrap\n      it has) fails forever, `GetSnapshot` fails with it so no HTTP client can resync\n      either, and the log keeps growing with events no new client can ever read. Not\n      tested either way: A1's test proves the *read* fails and never appends\n      afterwards. **The obligation, now in doc 07 §7.4: a session opened for writing\n      must have its log read to the tail before its first append, and a\n      `CorruptRecord` there must fail the open rather than be downgraded to a\n      warning.** From M1.6 this is free — a `Context` cannot be reconstructed without\n      a full read — which bounds the exposure but does not record it, and M1.5 sits\n      inside the window. **Verify:** doc 07 §7.4 carries the rule; Pass B's writer\n       implements it with a test that resumes over a corrupt record and asserts the\n       open fails; this item closes citing that test.\n       ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-23 at M1.4 Pass B** (tracker t223), citing\n       `store::writer::tests::the_same_corrupt_record_fails_a_writer_open_and_leaves_no_claim`.\n       The rule lives in `store/writer.rs`'s `read_to_tail`, called inside the writer\n       thread between the store's open and the first append, so a `CorruptRecord` there\n       fails the open over the same rendezvous a `Held` uses. **The asymmetry is tested\n       on both sides and the pair *is* the rule**: the sibling test\n       `a_log_corrupt_above_its_last_complete_line_still_opens_for_reading` asserts the\n       same bytes still open for reading, still report a clean `LogRecovery`, and still\n       serve their readable suffix three records at a time — so a future \"make the store\n       refuse at open\" edit fails one test and a future \"downgrade U122 to a warning\"\n       fails the other. The writer test also asserts nothing was appended over the damage\n       and that the failed open **released the claim**, so a refusal cannot lock out the\n       `attach`/`export` that §3.6's argument depends on. Both were mutation-proved:\n       deleting `read_to_tail` and replacing its `?` with `let _ =` each kill the writer\n       test by name (M2, M2b). The M1.5 window the item named is closed with it, since\n       the writer is the only path that opens a session for writing.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"       ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-23 at M1.4 Pass B** (tracker t223), citing","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U123","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`SessionCatalog`'s `index.json` has two writers and no lock, so the read-modify-write races *within one process* once Pass C lands concurrent callers — and a lost merge does not self-heal","body":"**U123 · `SessionCatalog`'s `index.json` has two writers and no lock, so the\n      read-modify-write races *within one process* once Pass C lands concurrent\n      callers — and a lost merge does not self-heal.** `SessionCatalog` is\n      `Clone + Send + Sync` with no internal synchronization; `put_meta` does a\n      read-merge-write of `index.json` and `list` *also* writes it (the\n      rebuild-when-missing-or-undecodable path). At A2 this is **latent, not\n      absent**: nothing outside `#[cfg(test)]` calls the store yet (debt d4's\n      `allow(dead_code)` is the marker). It goes live the moment Pass C's registry\n      puts a server thread answering `/api/sessions` (through `list`) beside the\n      engine's `put_meta` — two writers, one process, no lock. The outcome is a lost\n      *cache* update, not lost data (`meta.json`, written by one atomic replace,\n      still holds every session's truth-cache), **but it does not recover on its\n      own**: a stale `index.json` still decodes, `list` trusts a cache that decodes\n      rather than rescanning, so the missing entry returns only when a rebuild is\n      forced (the file deleted or corrupted) or a later `put_meta` re-merges *that*\n      session. The A2 fix round corrected two comments that claimed the opposite\n      (\"the next rebuild recovers it\", and \"in the deployed shape the race does not\n      arise\" — both false, the f148-B2 class of a comment asserting what the code\n      does not do); the module-doc and `put_meta`'s comment now state the real\n      recovery path and name Pass C as the owner. **Pass C owns the locking story**\n      (doc 07 §7.3's registry is the natural serialization point); solving it inside\n      A2 would pre-empt that pass (banner item 10). ~~**Verify:** Pass C either\n      serializes `index.json` at the registry, makes `list` read-only (only\n      `put_meta` writes), or has `list` reconcile against `scan_metas`, with a test\n      that two concurrent `put_meta`/`list` calls lose neither entry; this item\n      closes citing that test.~~ ✅ **Superseded by the 2026-09-23 ruling at the end of\n      this item: route 1 is rejected as a JSONL-shaped answer, route 2 does not fix the\n      defect, and the mechanism is M3's. The test in this clause stays the closing\n      condition; only its owner moved.** Same class as P-LIVE-009 / `jsonl.rs:341`.\n      ⚠️ **Narrowed and re-owned at Pass C1 (2026-09-23), and one of the three routes is not available.**\n      C1 was scoped to fix this and did not, deliberately: `SessionCatalog` is a **stateless clone over a\n      root** (`store/session.rs:54-57` — one `PathBuf`, no handle held between calls), so an internal\n      `Arc<Mutex<…>>` would serialize clones of *one* instance and not two separately-constructed catalogs\n      over the same root, which is exactly the shape this item names. A process-wide root→lock map would\n      cover it and is global state, which `_rust.md` forbids inventing. **Route 1 is therefore the only\n      in-process option and it is C2's registry** — this item's own text already said so (\"the registry is\n      the natural serialization point\"). ⚠️ **Two things C1 established that this item's verify text does\n      not yet account for.** (a) **Route 2 does not fix the defect**: making `list` read-only removes it as\n      a *second writer*, but a lost `put_meta` merge still leaves a stale-yet-decodable cache that `list`\n      trusts, so the \"does not self-heal\" half survives — and it changes a property the landed test\n      `the_index_rebuilds_when_missing_or_corrupt` pins (\"and the scan writes the cache back\"). (b) **No\n      in-process lock reaches the cross-process half** this item also names, and only route 3 (reconcile\n      against `scan_metas`) is correct across processes — but if `list` always rescans, `index.json` serves\n      nothing, and retiring it is a **design §12 edit** (§12's directory layout names it), which is\n      Ask-First. **So the route is a user decision, owed before C2 is dispatched, not an implementer's\n      choice.** ~~Verify text amended accordingly: C2 either serializes at the registry **and** records that\n      the cross-process half stays open with its exposure stated, or takes route 3 with the §12 edit\n      proposed first; this item closes citing whichever, with the residual filed rather than dropped.~~\n      ✅ **Superseded by the ruling immediately below — C2 builds no locking story at all, only a seam and\n      a trait-contract note.**\n      ✅ **RULED 2026-09-23 (user): take it easy — re-owned to M3, and no locking story is built at Pass C.**\n      The user's objection to route 1 is the one that decides it: **M3 generalizes the backend** (A7's\n      scaling axis, B11's `dacode-store` extraction, `SqliteStore` as the second implementor), and a\n      registry-level mutex around `index.json` is a *JSONL-shaped* answer baked into the engine. A sqlite\n      session list has its own transactional guarantees and needs no such lock, so the lock would become\n      dead weight nobody dares remove — and worse, it would *look* like the answer, so M3 might serialize\n      at the registry instead of fixing the backend's own concurrency. Building the mechanism now is what\n      would lead the wrong direction. **Two measurements make the deferral safe rather than merely\n      convenient:** `rg -n '\\.list\\(\\)|put_meta' crates/` returns **no production caller of either** — only\n      `SessionStore`'s trait declaration, `SessionCatalog`'s impl and its own tests, with `dacode-server`\n      still a 15-line stub — so the race's *consequence* (a stale session list somebody reads) is\n      unreachable until §8.2's `/api/sessions`, which is **M3**: the same row that must decide the\n      mechanism. Pass C2 does become `put_meta`'s **first** production caller, so it creates one writer\n      side, but the second (a reader) and the observer both arrive at M3. ⚠️ **That last clause was checked\n      and is weaker than it reads, so the qualification is recorded rather than smoothed over:** \"no reader\n      before M3\" is true of the *store trait* and not of the *feature*. The session list has an earlier\n      named consumer — doc 22's TUI layout carries a **sessions sidebar**, design §10's layout box is\n      `┌ sessions (toggle) ┬ transcript ┐` and §10 calls it \"the same component §9.2 specifies for the web\n      UI\" (whose sessions dashboard is a *list with live status chip*), doc 05 names `dacode session list`\n      as what `index.json` exists for, and design §11 ships it — and the TUI is **M1.11**. What keeps the\n      deferral sound is a *gap* rather than a schedule: **`Command` has no `ListSessions` variant** and\n      `Snapshot` carries exactly one `SessionInfo`, so no client can enumerate sessions over the protocol\n      today, and `dacode-tui` depends on `dacode-protocol` only (§2), so it cannot call `SessionStore::list`\n      directly either. That gap is **U136**, and it moves U123's owner from \"M3\" to \"**whichever row first\n      gives a client a session list**\" — M1.11 if U136 lands a protocol surface there, M3 if the sidebar is\n      scoped to registry-resident sessions. M1.11's row now carries the check. ⚠️ The first-caller fact\n      above is f160's shape and C2's review must treat it that way. **What C2 does instead, at zero mechanism\n      cost:** (i) funnel catalog access through one registry accessor, so M3 has a seam to put an answer\n      in without restructuring; (ii) state the concurrency **contract** on `SessionStore::list`/`put_meta`'s\n      trait docs — what they do and do not guarantee under concurrent callers — in the same shape Pass B's\n      review gave `EventStore::append` its caller obligation. A contract on the trait is backend-neutral\n      and is the part that generalizes; the *mechanism* is each backend's. **M3 then decides the mechanism\n      with the contract already written**, and route 3's §12 dimension (retiring a cache §12 names) is\n      argued there beside `SqliteStore` rather than pre-empted here. **Verify (amended twice):** the row\n      that first gives a client a session list — **M3**, or **M1.11** if U136 lands a protocol surface\n      there — either satisfies the trait contract in each backend or narrows the contract, with a test\n      that two concurrent `put_meta`/`list` calls lose neither entry; this item closes citing that test and\n       the contract text. C2's obligation is the seam plus the contract note, and it closes nothing here.\n      ⚠️ **C2's half landed 2026-09-23 and this item stays open, as its own Verify requires.** The seam is\n      one registry accessor for catalog access (`registry.rs`'s `catalog()`, which returns a reference and\n      never a guard); the contract is on `SessionStore::list`/`meta`/`put_meta`'s trait docs. **No mutex was\n      added around `index.json`** — `rg -n 'Mutex' crates/dacode-core/src/store/session.rs` → **0** — and\n      `rg -n 'locking story' crates/` → **0**, so both of U135(iv)'s stale pointers are re-owned rather than\n      merely deleted. ⚠️ **The first draft of the contract violated this item's own ruling, and the review\n      caught it rather than the author.** It stated the *mechanism* as the contract: `list` promised that \"a\n      stale cache that still decodes is trusted rather than rescanned … until the cache is deleted or\n      corrupted\", `meta` that \"a single `meta.json` is written by one atomic replace\", `put_meta` that it is\n      \"a read-modify-write of the session *list* … as well as a write of one `meta.json`\". **None of that is\n      true of M3's `SqliteStore`**, whose transactional list would not lose the merge at all — so the trait\n      asserted one backend's *defect* as a contract every backend must honour, which is precisely the\n      JSONL-shaped answer the ruling above rejected, arriving through the doc rather than through a lock.\n      Corrected: the trait now carries only the neutral half (a `list` concurrent with a `put_meta` **may**\n      miss that entry and the trait promises **nothing** about when or whether the miss repairs; a miss is a\n      stale *projection*, never lost log data; a read is atomic with respect to a `put_meta` of the same\n      session; no ordering is promised between two `put_meta` calls for one session; **different processes\n      race identically and no in-process mechanism reaches that half**), and every JSONL artifact name moved\n      to `SessionCatalog`'s module doc — moved, not deleted, and the primary verified it present at its new\n      home. **The lesson for M3 is the reason this is recorded on the item rather than in the review:** a\n      contract written on a trait *because* it must generalize is the one place a single implementation's\n      behaviour most easily gets promoted to a rule, and nothing in the gate matrix reads a trait doc.\n      ✅ **The conditional owner resolved 2026-10-04: U136 was ruled arm (b), so the mechanism\n      owner is M3 — unconditionally.** The row that first gives a client a session list is the\n      row that builds `dacode session list` (§11), sharing the catalog path with §8.2's\n      `/api/sessions`; M1.11's sidebar is the fold's tree and reads no catalog. The closing\n      condition is unchanged — the test that two concurrent `put_meta`/`list` calls lose neither\n      entry, plus the contract text — and it is M3's to satisfy.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U124","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The blob container's on-disk bytes were specified nowhere in design §3.6/§12, and M1.4 writes them — a durable format pinned with no spec backing","body":"**U124 · The blob container's on-disk bytes were specified nowhere in design\n      §3.6/§12, and M1.4 writes them — a durable format pinned with no spec\n      backing.** ✅ **RESOLVED 2026-09-22 — design §12 now pins the container.**\n      §3.6/§12 fix the *property* (a blob is \"always compressed and\n      self-describing\", \"each carries its own codec tag\", because it lives outside\n      the log and cannot inherit `LogHeader`) but never the *shape*. *(The quoted\n      \"always\" is the property as it stood when this item was filed; **U125 below\n      overturned it** the same day, so a blob is compressed *by policy* and plain\n      under `off`. The shape this item pinned is unaffected — only the codec tag's\n      value varies.)* A2's\n      `FsBlobStore` chose `{\"type\":\"BlobHeader\",\"data\":{\"codec\":\"zstd\"}}` + `\\n` then\n      the raw zstd frame — a tagged head record over the compressed bytes, following\n      the `LogHeader`/§13.5 convention so the head is self-describing and a future\n      codec is a new tag value. **Both reviewers ruled it sound and store-private**:\n      `BlobStore::get` returns the *decompressed* bytes and §8.2 serves those, so no\n      `Event`, wire frame or golden fixture pins the container and changing it is\n      **not** a compatibility event — AGENTS.md's \"ask first\" does not fire. It is\n      recorded in doc 05 §5.3's obligation 4 and in `blob.rs`. The §12 sentence the\n      primary asked for is **added**: it names the container (the tagged head record\n      over the payload, compressed or plain per U125), records it as **store-private**\n      (no `Event`/wire/fixture pins it, so changing it is not a compatibility event),\n      and states the two subtleties — a blob is the **only non-rebuildable** artifact\n      in a session directory, and `BlobHeader` carries no `format_version` (the `type`\n      tag does that job). Doc 05 §5.3's obligation 4 now cites §12 as normative rather\n      than owing it. **Verified:** design §12 names the shape; `check-links.py` exit 0\n      both modes after.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      backing.** ✅ **RESOLVED 2026-09-22 — design §12 now pins the container.**","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U125","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"RULED 2026-09-22 (option b): `compress = \"off\"` writes blobs plain too, and compression is a generalized *logging-backend* concern, not a `JsonlStore` one","body":"**U125 · ✅ RULED 2026-09-22 (option b): `compress = \"off\"` writes blobs plain\n      too, and compression is a generalized *logging-backend* concern, not a\n      `JsonlStore` one.** ✅ **And the code half landed the same day (tracker t219),\n      so the spec is no longer ahead of it.** The original wart: §3.6/§12 and doc 05 §5.3 all said blob\n      segments are **always** compressed (unconditional), so for `JsonlStore` — which\n      compresses only blobs — `\"off\"` and `\"blobs\"` produced identical bytes and the\n      knob's sole effect was `\"allowlist\"` vs not. **The ruling:** `\"off\"` means\n      *nothing* compresses, blobs included, so the three values are distinct for every\n      backend; and the policy is framed for **logging backends generally** — plain\n      JSONL ships today, with sqlite / clickhouse future backends each carrying its own\n      default. **The spec is corrected** (a user-directed change to A15's \"always\",\n      recorded as such, not a silent rewrite): design §3.6's allowlist bullet, its\n      `[storage]` knob comment and prose, and §12's blob artifact now say a blob is\n      compressed *by policy* and plain under `off`; doc 05 §5.3 records the ruling and\n      the backend generalization. ~~⚠️ **The code obligation remains and is the\n      immediate next task (tracker t219):** M1.4's `FsBlobStore` built the\n      unconditional reading (it does not consult the knob), so `blob.rs` must write a\n      plain blob under `\"off\"` (a `BlobHeader` naming `plain`, the raw payload), its\n      read path must accept both heads, and `traits.rs`'s `BlobStore` doc (\"always\n      compressed\") must be corrected — with a test that `\"off\"` writes an uncompressed\n      blob and reads it back. Until that lands the spec is ahead of the code: the\n      default `\"blobs\"` path is unaffected so nothing committed is wrong today, but\n      `compress=\"off\"` would still compress.~~ **Landed:** `FsBlobStore` carries the\n      policy, `Compress::compresses_blobs()` is the one place its blob half is read,\n      and `get` dispatches on the **head** rather than the config, because §12 makes a\n      blob the only non-rebuildable artifact so a policy change must not orphan the\n      blobs already on disk. **Closed by\n      `store::blob::tests::compress_off_writes_a_plain_blob_that_round_trips_byte_equal`**\n      (head is `{\"codec\":\"plain\"}`, payload byte-equal to the input, file length exact)\n      with `the_three_policy_values_write_distinguishable_blob_bytes` pinning the wart's\n      removal and `the_head_decides_how_a_blob_reads_and_not_the_stores_config` pinning\n      the read rule. ⚠️ **Applying the ruling found two spec sentences it had missed**\n      — §3.6's discriminator paragraph and §12's directory-layout block both still said\n      \"always compressed\" eleven lines from the corrected prose, so the transcription\n      source contradicted itself; both corrected here. **Verify:** ✅ met — `blob.rs`\n      consults the config, the named test asserts `\"off\"` → a plain blob that\n      round-trips byte-equal, `traits.rs`'s doc is corrected, and\n      `rg 'always compressed' crates/` returns only `compress.rs`'s quotation of the\n      overturned reading.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"**U125 · ✅ RULED 2026-09-22 (option b): `compress = \"off\"` writes blobs plain","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U126","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"User-initiated pause/resume that propagates through the agent tree — a global control with a per-agent tristate, requested for server mode (TUI and web UI)","body":"**U126 · User-initiated pause/resume that propagates through the agent tree —\n      a global control with a per-agent tristate, requested for server mode (TUI and\n      web UI).** ⚠️ **Distinct from everything the spec already calls \"pause\"** — the\n      name is taken twice and a third meaning must not collide: §4.2's\n      `StopReason::Paused` / `GuardKind::PauseContinuations` is the *provider's*\n      non-terminal pause (oh-my-pi's `pause_turn`, capped by\n      `loop.max_pause_continuations`), and §4.1's `CancellationToken` is *terminal*\n      (cancel keeps the partial turn with `stop: Cancelled` and cannot be undone).\n      This is a third thing: a **resumable user control**. The user's shape:\n      - **Tristate per agent: `PENDING_PAUSE` / `PAUSED` / `RESUMED`.** A pause is\n        global but reaches agents in order — **upper agents in the workflow are marked\n        `PENDING_PAUSE`**: they stop taking *new* input and wait for their children to\n        finish the current turn, rather than being halted mid-flight.\n      - **An agent is paused at one of two points**, and which one decides what is\n        queued: (a) **completing a tool run** (local actions) — the tool's output is\n        **queued for the next resumed turn**; (b) **awaiting the provider answer** — in\n        `PENDING_PAUSE` the agent still *receives* the answer but runs **no tool\n        invocation**, and the next turn is **queued for resume**.\n      - **On resume the order matters** — the user proposes **deepest sub-agents\n        first** and explicitly asks for other ideas. ⚠️ **Open design question, not a\n        settled rule**: deepest-first has an argument (a child's queued turn unblocks\n        its parent's wait), but so do shallow-first and queued-input-order; it\n        interacts with §5.3's parent-waits-for-child shape and with the (a)/(b)\n        queues. A design session must decide it.\n      Being observable, every transition must be an **`Event`** and the pause state a\n      **fold** of the log (principle 2), so a reconnecting *and* a replaying client\n      reconstruct who is paused/pending and what is queued — and the queue itself must\n      live somewhere durable-or-reconstructable, not only in a server map (the §8.4 /\n      doc 07 §7.7 lesson: state in the log cannot live only in a server map). Touches\n      the loop (§4.2), sub-agents (§5.3), the protocol (§3 — new `Pause`/`Resume`\n      commands + a pause-state event), the server (§8, propagation in server mode) and\n      both clients. Needs a **design session** — fold into or follow the M1.9 loop\n      brainstorm, since pause-at-a-turn-boundary is a loop concern; the resumption\n      order and the queue's durability are the two questions that session must answer.\n      **Verify:** the design records the tristate semantics, the two pause points, the\n      queue's home, the resumption order and the (non-colliding) name; §4.2/§5.3/§3\n      spec text or a delta follows; this item closes citing them.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U127","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Surface failed agents/sub-agents in the TUI and web UI with recovery actions — retry, change model, or pause/suspend (the pause propagating to the rest of the agents)","body":"**U127 · Surface failed agents/sub-agents in the TUI and web UI with recovery\n      actions — retry, change model, or pause/suspend (the pause propagating to the\n      rest of the agents).** The protocol already carries the seam: `Event::Error {\n      message, retryable }` (§3.2, `Live`) with doc 04 §4's rule that a client decides\n      on `retryable`; §6/doc 13 retry the provider on 429/5xx with backoff; and A4 made\n      a context overflow a *retryable* error with a defined path. What is **new** is the\n      **per-agent failure surface and its action menu**: when an agent or sub-agent\n      fails, the UI shows it as failed (not merely an inline error chip) and offers\n      (a) **retry**, (b) **change model** (→ U128), (c) **pause/suspend** — where pause\n      is U126's mechanism and **propagates to the rest of the agents**, so a failure in\n      one branch can freeze the workflow rather than let siblings run on. Open\n      questions for design: what `retry` re-runs (the provider call, the whole turn, or\n      the failed tool — §4.2's turn model and the `retryable` flag bound what is even\n      retryable); how a failed *sub-agent* surfaces to its parent (`SubAgentFinished`\n      carries the summary via the parent's `ToolResult` per §5.3 — does a failure ride\n      the same path, and does the parent retry or escalate?); whether retry is\n      automatic (provider-level, already specified) or only user-initiated (this item);\n      and the UI affordance (a failed-agent panel with per-agent actions, in both\n      clients). **Depends on U126 and U128**, so design the three together. Touches\n      §4.2 (the loop's error path), §6 (provider retry), §3 (`Event::Error`, possibly a\n      retry command), §5.3 (sub-agent failure), §9.2 (TUI) and §8.2 (web). **Verify:**\n      the design records the action set, what retry re-runs and the sub-agent failure\n      path; spec/protocol text follows; this item closes citing them.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U128","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Temporary model change per (sub-)agent session, for when a model is temporarily unavailable — driven from the UI by navigating to the agent and clicking its model name, effective on the next turn, with a cache warning","body":"**U128 · Temporary model change per (sub-)agent session, for when a model is\n      temporarily unavailable — driven from the UI by navigating to the agent and\n      clicking its model name, effective on the next turn, with a cache warning.**\n      ⚠️ **The protocol seam already exists — extend it, do not reinvent it**: §3.3\n      has `SetModel { session, model }` and A27 added `SettingsChanged { model?,\n      agent?, perm_mode? }` (§3.2), which folds into `Snapshot.model` (\"seeded by\n      `SessionCreated`, updated by `SettingsChanged`\"). A *session-level* model change\n      is therefore already specified. What is **new**: (a) **per-sub-agent** granularity\n      — a sub-agent is a child session (§5.3), so `SetModel` on the child's `SessionId`\n      may already reach it, but the *navigation* (pick an agent/sub-agent in the tree)\n      and the affordance (click the model name) are not built; (b) **\"temporary\"** —\n      revert-after-N-turns, revert-on-success, or just \"until changed back\"? The user's\n      framing (\"a model temporarily unavailable\") suggests an auto-revert once the\n      original is reachable, a behaviour the spec does not have; (c) **effective next\n      turn**, with a **warning that the new message carries no prompt cache for the new\n      model** — a §13.4 consequence (the cached prefix is per-model, so a switch starts\n      cold) and a UI affordance, not a protocol change; (d) it interacts with **U115**\n      (the interim `AgentConfig`/`ModelRef` M1.4 carries, whose replacement M2's doc 17\n      owes) — a per-sub-agent model override *is* a `ModelRef` question, so U128 and\n      U115 must be reconciled when doc 17 is deepened rather than designed twice.\n      Touches §4.1 (`Session.model`), §3.3 (`SetModel`), §5.3 (sub-agent config),\n      §13.4 (the cache warning), §9.2/§8.2 (the click affordance). Pairs with U127 (its\n      \"change model\" action). **Verify:** the design records the temporary/revert\n      semantics, the per-sub-agent path (reusing `SetModel`/`SettingsChanged`), the\n      cache warning and the U115/`ModelRef` reconciliation; spec/protocol text follows;\n      this item closes citing them.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U129","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"CLOSED 2026-09-23 at M1.4 Pass C1, on route 1 — `StoreError::UnsupportedCodec`'s `Display` describes one of its three provenances, and the config path is the one it gets wrong","body":"**U129 · ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-23 at M1.4 Pass C1, on route 1 — `StoreError::UnsupportedCodec`'s `Display` describes one of its three\n      provenances, and the config path is the one it gets wrong.** The variant's own\n      doc names all three — \"a log header, a blob head **or the config** names a\n      compression codec this build cannot read\" — but `error.rs`'s `Display` renders\n      it as *\"{path}: compressed with {codec}, which this build cannot read\"*. For a\n      *config* refusal nothing was compressed with that codec, nothing is being read,\n      and the build cannot **write** it; the path it names may not even exist. Found\n      while applying U125: `FsBlobStore::with_config` validated the codec\n      unconditionally, so `compress = \"off\"` + `codec = \"lz4\"` refused with that\n      message naming a `blobs/` directory that had not been created — fixed there by\n      gating validation on `Compress::compresses_blobs()`, which removes the worst\n      instance but not the wording. **The residual asymmetry is now principled but\n      still there**: each store validates what it would pin, so at §3.6's JSONL\n      default (`compress = \"blobs\"`) a `codec = \"lz4\"` config is *refused by the blob\n      store and accepted by the log store* — and `blobs` is the policy a user gets by\n      writing the bad codec and changing nothing else. Under `allowlist` both refuse.\n      **Pass C owns this**, because it is Pass C that loads `[storage]` once and hands\n      one `CompressConfig` to both stores (doc 07 §7.3): either validate the table at\n      config load so neither store is the first to notice, or split the `Display` on\n      provenance (\"the config names `{codec}`, which this build cannot write\"). Not\n      fixed here — t219's dispatch said do not pre-empt Pass B/C, and changing a\n      `StoreError` shape is Pass C's config-loading story. **Verify:** Pass C either\n      validates `[storage]` once at load with a test that a bad codec is refused\n      before either store is constructed, or the `Display` names the provenance; this\n      item closes citing whichever.\n      ✅ **Met by the first route, at Pass C1 (2026-09-23).** `storage_config::StorageConfig::new` is now the\n      single validation point: it refuses an unwritable `codec` or an out-of-range `level` **before either\n      store exists**, and hands the *same* `CompressConfig` to `jsonl_store_with` and\n      `FsBlobStore::with_config`, so neither store can be the first to notice and the asymmetry this item\n      named cannot arise. Closed citing\n      `storage_config::tests::an_unwritable_codec_is_refused_before_either_store_exists`, which asserts the\n      refusal under **all three** policies including `off` — the case where neither store-level check fires\n      — asserts the message names the config and the *write* side and contains neither \"cannot read\" nor\n      \"compressed with\", and asserts that no `events.jsonl` and no `blobs/` directory came into existence;\n      `a_validated_config_reaches_both_stores_as_one_value` is the control that makes those absence\n      assertions non-vacuous, and `the_boundary_and_the_store_check_agree_on_what_is_valid` pins the\n      boundary's verdicts to `CompressConfig::validate`'s over a 3 policies × 2 codecs × 6 levels matrix.\n      `StorageConfigError` is a second error type rather than a reuse of `UnsupportedCodec` precisely\n      because that variant's `Display` is the defect this item names. ⚠️ **Two residuals recorded with\n      owners rather than dropped** (M6: a corrected pointer whose work is not re-owned is worse than a stale\n      one). (i) The `Display` half is **not** fixed — `UnsupportedCodec` still renders a read refusal for its\n      config provenance, and both landed store-level `validate` call sites stay reachable by a caller that\n      assembles stores by hand (`FsBlobStore::with_config` is `pub`), so \"unreachable via `StorageConfig`\" is\n      a review claim and not a type-level one. (ii) The boundary validates **unconditionally**, which\n      *falsifies* the rationale t219 wrote at `store/blob.rs:101-109` (\"validating anyway would refuse a\n      config the log store accepts — Pass C hands one `CompressConfig` to both\"): `off` + `lz4` is refused\n      again, at the boundary, with better wording — the outcome this item asked for, in a comment that now\n      says the opposite of what Pass C does. C1 was forbidden from editing `store/`. **Both residuals are\n      U135's, with Pass C2 named as the owner.**\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"**U129 · ✅ CLOSED 2026-09-23 at M1.4 Pass C1, on route 1 — `StoreError::UnsupportedCodec`'s `Display` describes one of its three","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U130","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`atomic_write`'s crash-safety claim has no test and no longer has an owner","body":"**U130 · `atomic_write`'s crash-safety claim has no test and no longer has an\n      owner.** `store/mod.rs:137` documents that the temporary is `fsync`ed before\n      the `rename`, so a crash cannot make a durable rename public with empty\n      contents behind it — and that **no test fails if the write happens in place\n      instead**. A2 assigned that test to \"the fault injection doc 07 §7.10 items\n      4–5 assign to Pass B\", and Pass B falsified the assignment rather than\n      discharging it: items 4–5 are the *log writer's* `Flush`-with-reply boundary\n      and its shutdown drain, and nothing on that path goes through `atomic_write`.\n      Its only three callers are `blob.rs:170`, `session.rs:140` and\n      `session.rs:168` — `meta.json`, `index.json` and blobs — while the log and\n      `index.bin` are append-only and deliberately excluded from it (`mod.rs:129`).\n      What would actually kill it is an injection *at that function*: a failure\n      between its `sync_all` and its `rename`, or a `spawned.rs`-style process kill\n      raced across that window. **M1.9 owns it**, because M1.9 is the row that\n      builds crash-boundary fault injection (§14.1's Engine bullet: \"kill the\n      process … reopen, and assert\"), and because §7.10 item 4's full\n      crash-between-commit-and-side-effect form is already M1.9's. **Verify:** a\n      test that fails when `atomic_write` writes in place instead of through\n      temporary-plus-rename; this item closes citing it.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U131","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Two notes in `index.rs` assign an untestable injection to a pass that has landed, and doc 05 §5.5's invariant 2 (log line → fsync → index record) has no test that fails if it is reversed and no seam to write one against","body":"**U131 · Two notes in `index.rs` assign an untestable injection to a pass\n      that has landed, and doc 05 §5.5's invariant 2 (log line → fsync → index\n      record) has no test that fails if it is reversed and no seam to write one\n      against.** Both said the test needed \"Pass B's fault injection\". It does not,\n      and for the *same* structural reason in both cases — a `Box<dyn EventStore>`\n      wrapper wraps `JsonlStore`, it cannot get **inside** it:\n      - `index.rs:419` (`Index::sync`) — invariant 2's write order.\n        `JsonlStore::flush`'s two `sync` calls are **adjacent statements in one\n        method**, reached through a single `EventStore::flush` call, so no wrapper\n        Pass B's writer could hold gets between them. Only a crash *between* the\n        two fsyncs distinguishes the orders.\n      - `index.rs:341` (`Index::tail_is_consistent`) — the `records_end !=\n        expected_end` half, whose production reach is `Index::record`'s `write_all`\n        failing part-way. That is **three calls below the trait surface**, so the\n        same wrapper argument applies: Pass B landed four injected `EventStore`\n        doubles and none can make an inner `write_all` fail. The existing test\n        (`append_rebuilds_a_projection_whose_tail_disagrees_with_the_log`) injects\n        the desynchronization *directly*, which proves the recovery and not the\n        detection.\n\n      Reaching either window needs a `#[cfg(test)]` hook inside `JsonlStore` (in\n      `flush` for the first, around `Index::record`'s write for the second) or a\n      process kill raced into it. What *is* tested today is the **recovery** from a\n      reversed order (`an_index_ahead_of_the_log_is_validated_back_to_the_log`),\n      which is not the same claim and must not be read as one. **M1.9 owns both**,\n      with the hook as the cheaper candidate; Pass C may take them opportunistically\n      if U129's config-loading story already touches `JsonlStore::flush`. ⚠️ The\n      hook is a decision, not an implementation detail: it puts a `#[cfg(test)]`\n      branch in the crate's only durability path, which is why this item does not\n      simply add one — and why the two notes were corrected in place at Pass B\n      round 4 rather than left pointing at a pass that had already landed.\n      **Verify:** either the hook lands with tests that fail on a reversed write\n      order and on a partial `Index::record` write, or both claims are struck from\n      `index.rs` and doc 05 §5.5 as review-defended-only; this item closes citing\n      whichever.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U132","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"U122's read-to-tail holds the whole decoded log in memory at every writer open, and `EventStore` has no streaming form of `range` to avoid it","body":"**U132 · U122's read-to-tail holds the whole decoded log in memory at every\n      writer open, and `EventStore` has no streaming form of `range` to avoid it.**\n      `writer.rs`'s `read_to_tail` calls `EventStore::range(session, Cursor(0),\n      None)`, whose signature returns `Vec<Envelope>` (U119's method list), so the\n      transient is **O(log size) in memory and not only in time** — plus\n      decompression of every allowlisted leaf under `compress = \"allowlist\"`. Doc\n      07 §7.4 accepts the *time* cost explicitly (\"From M1.6 the read is free\") and\n      says nothing about the memory, and §3.7's fast-resync argument is about time\n      too. **Not a defect and not owed by Pass B**: the trait is right to be\n      synchronous and dyn-compatible (f138), and a paged read is expressible today\n      with `range(…, Some(limit))` in a loop at the cost of a re-seek per page.\n      **M3's `SqliteStore` owns the question**, because that is the second\n      implementor and the point at which the trait's shape stops being\n      `JsonlStore`'s private business; a long-lived session under `allowlist` is\n      the case that would make it urgent earlier. ⚠️ **Do not change the trait for\n      this inside M1.4** — Pass B did not, deliberately. **Verify:** M3 either adds\n      a streaming/iterator form to `EventStore`, or records why `Vec` stands and\n      `read_to_tail` pages; this item closes citing whichever.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U133","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§4.1's `EventLog` boundary is \"append + range read\"; Pass C1 landed the append half, and no landed surface supplies the read half while a writer lives","body":"**U133 · §4.1's `EventLog` boundary is \"append + range read\"; Pass C1 landed the\n      append half, and no landed surface supplies the read half while a writer lives.**\n      `Session.log` is `Option<LogWriter>`, which appends and flushes and nothing else.\n      Doc 07 §7.6 step 2 needs a range read (`Subscribe { since }` replays the stored\n      range before switching to live), and the obvious candidate is **destructive rather\n      than merely illegal**: a second in-process `JsonlStore::open` breaks the\n      caller-holds-the-lock contract, and it rewrites the *live* `index.bin` from a handle\n      that is not the writer's — `Index::open`'s `set_len` to the validated prefix plus a\n      write of the records it re-derived (`store/index.rs:260,288`) — inside invariant 2's\n      own documented window (`store/index.rs:407-419`: both files sit behind independent\n      `BufWriter`s, so a projection record *can* reach the disk before the log line it\n      names), which is exactly when its `validated_prefix` rejects records the writer\n      counts as written and truncates them as if they were crash debris. The log half is\n      **contingent, not guaranteed**, and C1 measured it rather than citing std line\n      numbers (the pinned 1.98.1 tree carries no `rust-src`): `BufWriter`'s capacity is\n      8192, an over-capacity `write_all` from an empty buffer is **one** inner write and\n      from a primed buffer two whose first carries only earlier *whole* records, so a torn\n      tail needs the kernel to short-write. ⚠️ **The route is settled and recorded so C2\n      transcribes rather than re-derives it: a third writer-queue request kind**,\n      `Range { since, limit, reply }`, answered by the writer thread between appends,\n      **chunked** with `limit` bounded by the fan-out depth so doc 07 §7.5's `ring > queue`\n      assertion also bounds chunk < ring, and served **ring-first**. That keeps §3.7's \"the\n      engine does not block on any client\" true in the only implementable sense — bounded\n      coupling, independent of client speed, a slow client stalling in its own pump — and\n      reads through the one handle that owns the buffer and the projection. A read-only\n      second handle is right for the *no-writer* case (§8.1's `attach`, §8.2's export) and\n      wrong here: it would have to re-solve the unflushed tail, the projection's\n      validation, and a second decoder for the compressed leaves. **Ring-only replay is not\n      an option** — §3.7 step 5 makes `Subscribe { since: Some(Cursor(0)) }` a stdio\n      extension's *only* resync, and §3.7's own bullet requires older ranges to be read\n      from the log file. Owner: **Pass C2**, which also owes doc 07 §7.4's \"two request\n      kinds\" sentence and `store/writer.rs`'s matching comment the precision edit (both\n      were scoped to durability boundaries; §7.4 carries the corrected text already).\n      **Verify:** C2's replay serves a cold range through the writer, with a test that a\n      `Subscribe { since: Some(Cursor(0)) }` against a log larger than the ring delivers\n      every durable envelope in order and folds to the same `Snapshot` as a full read; both\n      \"two kinds\" comments carry the third; this item closes citing that test.\n      ✅ **Closed 2026-09-23 at Pass C2**, citing\n      `replay::tests::a_full_replay_past_the_ring_delivers_every_envelope_in_order_and_folds_to_the_same_snapshot`.\n      The settled route was transcribed rather than re-derived: `Request::Range { since, limit, reply }`\n      is the writer's **third** queue kind (`store/writer.rs`), answered between appends and served\n      **ring-first** (`replay.rs`'s `cold_limit` returns `None` once the ring covers the cursor, so the\n      stored read is skipped rather than duplicated), **chunked** at `RANGE_CHUNK_MAX = FANOUT_QUEUE_DEPTH`\n      with `RANGE_CHUNK_MAX < REPLAY_RING_DEPTH` as a `const _: () = assert!(…)` in `store/writer.rs`\n      *and* a named test — so §7.5's `ring > queue` bounds `chunk < ring` mechanically, not by comment.\n      **No second in-process `JsonlStore` was opened**, which is what the destructive-read warning above\n      forbade. Both \"two kinds\" comments carry the third (doc 07 §7.4's heading and its new `Range`\n      bullet; `store/writer.rs`'s module doc), and the `Range` arm now states the dropped-reply rule\n      itself rather than inheriting it from `Append`'s (the primary's round-3 LOW-2: a guarantee cited\n      at a site that does not state it is a guarantee a reader cannot check). ⚠️ **The clamp is a *cost*\n      bound and not a correctness one** — removing it alone survives the whole suite, because the ring\n      loop's `seq < next` skip absorbs the overshoot and the stream stays contiguous and duplicate-free;\n      the comment that claimed it prevented silent data loss was false and is corrected at both sites.\n      The mutation that dies is `R6b`, which removes the clamp *and* the skip.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **Closed 2026-09-23 at Pass C2**, citing","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U134","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Design §4.2's per-turn `Cancel` and doc 07 §7.4's session-teardown token are the same field, and one token cannot do both","body":"**U134 · Design §4.2's per-turn `Cancel` and doc 07 §7.4's session-teardown token\n      are the same field, and one token cannot do both.** §4.2's `Cancel` \"triggers the\n      token\" for a *turn*: the partial turn is kept in the log with `stop: Cancelled` and\n      **the session continues**. §7.4's cancellation paragraph has the *same* token\n      starting session teardown — cancel → drain → explicit `flush` + `fsync` → drop the\n      guard — and lists \"the writer's shutdown message\" among the loops honouring it. A\n      turn cancel that reached the writer would release the session lock, which §3.6 holds\n      for the whole session. The obvious shape is a `child_token` per turn under a\n      session-lifetime parent (`tokio-util` 0.7.19 has `child_token`), with the writer\n      driven by the session one. **Owner: M1.9's loop row** — §4.2 is its section and the\n      turn boundary is its design — with **Pass C2 recording the shape it needs** rather\n      than choosing it, since C2 builds the actor's select. Filed rather than decided\n      because a hard-rule-1 doc (08) owns the loop and is still a migrated summary, so\n      settling it now would be designing a 🧠 row's content early. **Verify:** M1.9 either\n      lands two tokens with §4.2 and doc 07 §7.4 each citing its own, or records why one\n      suffices; this item closes citing whichever, and C2's actor names the token each of\n      its loops honours.\n      ⚠️ **Still open — only C2's half landed (2026-09-23), and the distinction is the\n      point.** C2 *recorded* the shape rather than choosing it, exactly as this item\n      required: `actor.rs`'s module doc carries a **token table** naming which token each\n      of its loops honours, so M1.9 inherits two labelled hooks instead of one ambiguous\n      field. The decision itself is untouched and still M1.9's, because doc 08 owns the\n      loop and is a hard-rule-1 migrated summary. **The round earned a rule worth keeping:**\n      the primary's round-2 MED found the pump *naming* a token it did not poll — three of\n      its five raced `.await`s were unpinned while the doc claimed all five honoured the\n      session token — and U134's obligation to \"name the token each loop honours\" is what\n      made that a defect rather than a gap. **A name that overstates is worse than silence**,\n      because the next pass reads the table instead of the code. `replay.rs` now carries a\n      five-row ledger naming the pinning test per site or \"review-defended\", and the two\n      review-defended sites are **U137**.","status":"deferred","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":"      settling it now would be designing a 🧠 row's content early. **Verify:** M1.9 either","references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U135","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Four edits to `store/` that Pass C1 was forbidden from making, one owner","body":"**U135 · Four edits to `store/` that Pass C1 was forbidden from making, one owner.**\n      C1's dispatch froze `crates/dacode-core/src/store/`, correctly — it kept a\n      comment-and-visibility pass from touching 150 landed tests — but that pushed three\n      known defects into a later pass, and an unowned deferral is the thing M6 warns about.\n      **(i) `store/blob.rs:101-109` now says the opposite of what Pass C does.** t219 gated\n      `FsBlobStore::with_config`'s validation on `compresses_blobs()` with the stated\n      rationale that validating anyway \"would refuse a config the log store accepts — Pass\n      C hands one `CompressConfig` to both\". `StorageConfig::new` validates\n      **unconditionally**, so `off` + `lz4` is refused again — at the boundary, with the\n      config as the named provenance, before either store exists, which is U129's actual\n      complaint and a better outcome. The store-level gate stays correct for a caller that\n      assembles stores by hand; what is stale is its premise about Pass C. One-line edit.\n      **(ii) Six sites carry a §3.6 misquote** — \"a reader must support every codec any\n      surviving log used\" where the design (`dacode-design.md:1030-1031`) says \"…while\n      still obliging a reader to support every codec any surviving log used\":\n      `store/error.rs:86-87`, `store/compress.rs:49-50` and `store/jsonl_tests.rs:801-802`\n      — **these three span a line break** — plus `store/jsonl.rs:145`,\n      `store/log_file.rs:160` and `store/log_file.rs:438`. ⚠️ **The sweep command matters,\n      and the two obvious ones are wrong**: a line-based grep of the full phrase returns\n      **3 of 6**, and a naive `rg -U` with `\\s+` between the words also returns **3**,\n      because the `///` and `//` prefixes sit inside the gap and `\\s` does not match them.\n      Two forms that reproduce six, both measured: `rg -n 'support every codec' crates/`\n      → **7** lines, the six misquotes plus `storage_config.rs:78`'s *correct* quotation\n      (the hit that must not be \"fixed\"); and the comment-tolerant multiline pattern\n      `rg -U --multiline-dotall 'a reader(?:\\s|/)*must(?:\\s|/)*support(?:\\s|/)*every(?:\\s|/)*codec(?:\\s|/)*any(?:\\s|/)*surviving(?:\\s|/)*log(?:\\s|/)*used' crates/`\n      → exactly **6** (`jsonl` 1, `jsonl_tests` 1, `compress` 1, `error` 1, `log_file` 2).\n      One inherited phrase, six sites, and `storage_config.rs` quoted it correctly only\n      because C1's review round sent the author back to the design's actual words:\n      P-LIVE-006's sweep-the-phrase rule, with the sweep deferred by a file freeze. **(iii) Split\n      `CompressConfig::validate` into a path-free value check plus a path-carrying\n      wrapper**, so `StorageConfig::new` calls it instead of duplicating its two predicates\n      (`ZSTD_CODEC` identity and `zstd::compression_level_range`). The duplication is\n      *exact* today and pinned by an agreement test over a 3 × 2 × 6 matrix, so it is safe\n      to land — but it is the A2 mirror's shape in miniature (a copied rule whose only\n       defence is a test), and the split also gives U129's residual `Display` half a natural\n       home. **(iv) Two comments in `store/session.rs` still assign the `index.json` locking story to\n       Pass C** — `:32` (\"**Pass C owns the locking story**\") and `:176` (\"the locking story is Pass\n       C's\") — and U123's 2026-09-23 ruling makes both stale in the same way Pass B's ten `Pass B`\n        sites were: C2 owes a *seam and a trait-contract note*, and the mechanism's owner is now\n        conditional on U136 (M3, or M1.11 — **resolved to M3 when U136 was ruled arm (b),\n        2026-10-04**). They were found by the sibling sweep of the ruling rather\n       than by anything C1 touched, and they are listed here because this item is the only durable\n       record of what the `store/` freeze deferred — leaving them out is M6's case, a corrected\n       pointer whose work has no owner. **Owner: Pass C2**, which lifts the freeze on its own account:\n       U123's seam half puts the concurrency contract on `SessionStore`'s trait docs, so it is editing\n       `store/` anyway. **Verify:** all four land in one sweep, with (ii) evidenced by a command\n       whose output is **six** — the comment-tolerant multiline pattern above, or\n       `rg -n 'support every codec' crates/` read as seven minus the one correct quotation —\n       and **not** by a line-based grep of the full phrase or a naive `rg -U`, both of which\n       return three and so report a clean half-sweep; plus the agreement test still passing\n       after (iii) with the duplication gone, and `rg -n 'locking story' crates/` returning only\n       text whose owner matches U123's ruling. This item closes citing the sweep and the\n      command that produced its count.\n      ✅ **Closed 2026-09-23 at Pass C2 — all four in one sweep, with the counts re-measured\n      after the last edit** (P-LIVE-015: a sweep that follows the edit stops at the file it\n      is editing, and this item's own warning is that the *obvious* commands report a clean\n      half-sweep). **(ii), the load-bearing one, evidenced by the two commands that reproduce\n      six and not by either that returns three:** before the edit, `rg -n 'support every codec' crates/`\n      → **7** (the six misquotes plus `storage_config.rs:78`'s *correct* quotation, which was\n      **not** \"fixed\") and the comment-tolerant multiline pattern → **6** across 5 files\n      (`jsonl` 1, `compress` 1, `error` 1, `log_file` 2, `jsonl_tests` 1) — both reproducing\n      this item's stated counts exactly. After: the multiline pattern → **0**, and the line\n      grep → **7** with every hit now a correct quotation. Neither wrong form was used. ⚠️ **The\n      correct quotation moved** in the same pass — `ConfigFault`'s extraction relocated it from\n      `storage_config.rs:78` to `store/compress.rs:52,210` — and the count held at 7, which is\n      the check that the sweep tracked the *phrase* rather than the site (P-LIVE-006). **(iii)**\n      split `CompressConfig::validate` into a path-free `check()` returning a `ConfigFault` plus\n      a path-carrying `validate()` wrapper, so `StorageConfig::new` calls it instead of duplicating\n      the two predicates, and **U129's residual `Display` half now has the home this item named\n      for it**; `the_boundary_and_the_store_check_agree_on_what_is_valid` still passes over its\n      3 × 2 × 6 matrix **with the duplication genuinely gone**, which is the only thing that made\n      the split safe to land. **(i)** `store/blob.rs`'s falsified t219 rationale is corrected —\n      the store-level gate stays (it is right for a caller that assembles stores by hand) and only\n      its false premise about Pass C went. **(iv)** `rg -n 'locking story' crates/` → **0**, and\n      `rg -n 'Mutex' crates/dacode-core/src/store/session.rs` → **0**, so U123's ruling is honoured\n      on both counts: the stale pointers are re-owned and **no mutex was added around `index.json`**.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **Closed 2026-09-23 at Pass C2 — all four in one sweep, with the counts re-measured","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U136","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The session list has no protocol surface, and three docs already assume one","body":"**U136 · The session list has no protocol surface, and three docs already assume one.**\n      Doc 22's TUI layout carries a **sessions sidebar**; design §10's layout box is\n      `┌ sessions (toggle) ┬ transcript ┐` and §10 calls it \"the same component §9.2 specifies for the\n      web UI\", where §9.2's sessions dashboard is a *list with live status chip*; doc 05 names\n      `dacode session list` as the consumer `index.json` exists for (\"if `dacode session list` needs more,\n      the index grows a second table\"); and design §11 ships `dacode session list|export|fork|delete`.\n      **But `Command` has no `ListSessions` variant** and `Snapshot` carries exactly one\n      `SessionInfo`, so no client can enumerate sessions over the protocol — and `dacode-tui` depends on\n      `dacode-protocol` **only** (design §2), so the TUI cannot reach `SessionStore::list` directly either.\n      The one route that exists is §8.2's `/api/sessions`, which is **M3**, while the TUI sidebar is\n      **M1.11**. Found while checking U123's deferral premise, and it is why that premise needed stating\n      precisely: the first production reader of `list` is whichever row gives a client the list, and that\n       row is not yet chosen — so U123's mechanism owner is conditional on this item. **Owed by M1.11's\n       opening**, which must pick one of two shapes rather than discover the choice while building a widget:\n      either a `ListSessions`-shaped protocol surface lands there (cheap *before* M1.3 publishes — §14\n      derives the fixture set from the enum, so a new `Command` variant self-registers, and B2's drift\n      check fires on the addition by design), or doc 22 and design §10 record that the sidebar shows\n      **registry-resident sessions only**, which nothing currently states and which is a real scoping\n      decision (a fresh `dacode` would then show an empty sidebar over a data root holding sessions).\n      ⚠️ Note the second shape does **not** retire the question: `dacode session list` (§11) still needs\n      the store, and U123's mechanism still travels with whoever builds that path. ▶️ **Re-measured at\n      M1.1g (2026-10-04), and the measurement changes both arms' weight** — doc 22 §22.11 carries the\n      full record: `Ack` is `Result<(), String>` (`command.rs:450-455`) so **no command returns data**\n      (the reply pattern is command-causes-events; `GetSnapshot` is HTTP-only by design,\n      `command.rs:307-310`), and the event stream is **per-session** (§3.7; the fan-out; the\n      `Envelope`), so a cross-session list has no stream to ride — arm (a) is a *new protocol concept*\n      (a data-carrying `Ack`, or a session-less event channel), not \"a new `Command` variant\n      self-registers\", and either form is a compatibility event. Arm (b) is refined by the same\n      measurements: §10's own three sources are all in one `Snapshot` (the session + `children`,\n      `tasks`, workflow steps), and the real list consumers already have non-protocol routes — §9.2's\n      dashboard over `/api/sessions` (M3) and §11's `dacode session list` reading `SessionCatalog`\n      (`store` is `dacode-core`'s one `pub` module, `lib.rs:80`). **Recommendation: arm (b)**, four\n      grounds in doc 22 §22.11; the ruling is the user's and lands as M1.11's Step 0 on the M1.7\n      precedent. **Verify:** either the protocol surface lands with doc 22 stating where the sidebar's\n      list comes from, or doc 22 and §10 carry the registry-resident scoping sentence; U123's owner is\n      then named as that row, and this item closes citing whichever landed.\n      ✅ **RULED ARM (B) BY THE USER (2026-10-04, \"let's pickup your recommendation\"), landed as\n      M1.11's Step 0 on the M1.7 precedent.** What landed, citing the Verify clause's second arm:\n      **design §10**'s sidebar bullet now reads *the current session with its sub-agents* and\n      carries the scoping sentence — no protocol surface enumerates sessions (`Command` has no\n      list variant; the event stream is per-session, §3.7), so the cross-session list belongs to\n      §9.2's dashboard over `/api/sessions` (§8.2) and §11's `dacode session list`, both reading\n      the session store directly; **doc 22 §22.11** is finalized as the ruling's record and\n      **doc 22's depth marker flipped with it** (§4: 21 → 20); **U123's mechanism owner is M3**,\n      the row that builds `dacode session list` and shares the catalog path with `/api/sessions`.\n      The filing's \"registry-resident sessions only\" phrasing is superseded by the M1.1g\n      re-measurement: the sidebar cannot see the registry either — `dacode-tui` depends on\n      `dacode-protocol` only — it sees the fold. The filing's own ⚠️ held: the second shape does\n      not retire the question, `dacode session list` still needs the store, and U123's mechanism\n      travels with M3.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **RULED ARM (B) BY THE USER (2026-10-04, \"let's pickup your recommendation\"), landed as","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U137","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Two of the replay pump's five raced `.await`s are unpinned, and the seam to pin them is public but unused","body":"**U137 · Two of the replay pump's five raced `.await`s are unpinned, and the seam to pin them is\n      public but unused.** `crate::replay`'s subscriber pump races `Session.cancel` at five points.\n      **Three are pinned by named tests** — `deliver`'s send (`a_stalled_subscribers_pump_ends_when_the_session_is_cancelled`),\n      the live `recv` (`a_live_phase_pump_also_ends_on_the_session_token`), and the live `send`\n      (`a_live_send_blocked_on_a_stalled_subscriber_ends_on_the_session_token`, which is the one that\n      matters most because it is the state the pump actually spends its life in: blocked on a subscriber\n      that will not drain). **Two are review-defended**: `read_cold`'s chunk read at `replay.rs:545-572`,\n      and the failure report's `tx.send(Err(..))` at `replay.rs:392-404`. Both sites say so in the code\n      and point at this item.\n      **Why they are unpinned, and what is *not* the obstacle.** The first needs a backend whose `range`\n      never answers; the second needs one that fails on a *later* chunk after ≥`SUBSCRIBER_QUEUE_DEPTH`\n      successful deliveries, so the report's send meets a **full** queue — reachable, because `read_cold`\n      delivers as it goes and `JsonlStore::read_range` returns `CorruptRecord` for a damaged *middle*\n      record (retried once, then surfaced). Neither double exists in the crate: `store::writer::backends`'\n      five are `#[cfg(test)]`-private to that module. ⚠️ **But the seam is public and a test can build one\n      with zero production surface** — `StoreFactory` (`store/writer.rs:150`), `OpenedStore` with both\n      fields `pub` (`:131-139`), `EventStore` (`store/traits.rs:30`) and `LogWriter::spawn` (`:302`) are\n      all `pub`, and `replay::subscribe` is `pub(crate)`, so a test in `crate::replay::tests` can spawn a\n      writer over a wedged double, wrap it in `Session::new` and drive `subscribe` directly. **The cost is\n      ~40–55 lines, and the file it would go in is 38 lines from `_rust.md`'s 1 000-line cap.**\n      ⚠️ **This is explicitly *not* U131**, whose first draft cited it as the precedent: U131 is invariant\n      2's write order — two adjacent `sync` calls *inside* `JsonlStore::flush` with no function boundary\n      between them at any visibility, so there is genuinely nothing to inject into. Here the boundary\n      exists, is `pub`, and is simply unused. **A wrong reason is what a later pass declines a cheap test\n      on**, which is why the correction is recorded rather than just made.\n      **Exposure while unpinned, bounded:** worst case is a **leaked pump task** — never a hang and never a\n      held session lock, because `RangeHandle`'s `mpsc::WeakSender` is what keeps `close_session` unaffected,\n      and *that* guarantee is pinned (`a_live_replay_pump_does_not_hold_the_writer_channel_open`, whose\n      mutation kills by assertion rather than by timeout).\n      **Owner: M3's backend row**, which is also the named remover of `replay.rs`'s dead-code exemption.\n      M3 gives `EventStore` a second implementor and extracts `dacode-store` (B11), which is where injectable\n      doubles stop being 40 hand-rolled lines in one consumer's test file and become a utility the second\n      implementor needs anyway; building them at C2 would put that utility where M3 cannot reuse it. **M1.9\n      is the wrong owner** — it reaches the actor but never a subscriber queue.\n      ⚠️ **Two things M3 inherits with this, because both currently exist only in a review transcript.**\n      **(a) The split must come first:** `replay_tests.rs` is at the cap minus 38, and the clean cut is a\n      `replay_cancel_tests.rs` holding the four pump-cancellation tests (`:584`, `:642`, `:704`, `:768`),\n      ~250 lines out — the same shape as `store/writer.rs`'s `mod backends` hoist and `actor_support.rs`.\n      **(b) A naive \"`range` always blocks\" wedge will deadlock `LogWriter::spawn` itself**, because U122's\n      `read_to_tail` calls `store.range(session, Cursor::new(0), None)` *during the open* — so the double\n      must block only on the **replay's** call (on `limit.is_some()`, or on the Nth call), or the test never\n      gets a writer to subscribe against.\n      **Verify:** M3 lands both tests naming them, or records why the exposure changed; both `replay.rs`\n      sites cite this item by id and are updated in the same change; the split lands in the same commit or\n      an earlier one, never after the cap is crossed.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U138","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The asker is woken before the `Ack` that tells the client its answer was accepted, so a client can see the asker's next event at a *lower* `seq` than the event that unblocked it","body":"**U138 · The asker is woken before the `Ack` that tells the client its answer was accepted, so a\n      client can see the asker's next event at a *lower* `seq` than the event that unblocked it.**\n      `actor.rs`'s `decide_answer`/`decide_resolve` consume the waiter and send on the oneshot during\n      **decide**, before the reply is sent at `apply` — and design §3.3's ordering rule is that the `Ack`\n      precedes the resulting events. The early decide is **sound and deliberate**: it is what makes\n      first-answer-wins race-free, since two concurrent answers must not both reach the waiter. What is\n      unstated is the consequence — the woken asker's next `emit` races `apply`'s, so its `ToolResult` can\n      land at a lower `seq` than the `QuestionAnswered` that unblocked it, **and before the answering\n      client's own `Ack`**. Found by the primary's round-2 review (L5) and recorded on `decide_answer`'s doc,\n      cross-referenced from `decide_resolve`, rather than left implicit.\n      **Not fixed at C2 because the fix is not local:** removing the entry in `decide` while deferring the\n      oneshot send to `apply` needs a **two-phase `PendingRequests` API**, which rewrites C1's\n      mutation-proved delivery methods for a caller that does not exist until M1.9. **Exposure at M1.4 is\n      nil**: there is no asker — the agent loop is M1.9's, so nothing is ever woken. **Owner: M1.9's loop\n      row**, which is the row that creates askers and therefore the first one for which the inversion is\n      observable. **Verify:** M1.9 either lands the two-phase API and a test that the asker's next event\n      carries a `seq` above the resolution that unblocked it, or records why the inversion is acceptable —\n      and this item closes citing whichever. ⚠️ Do not \"simplify\" the early decide away: it is load-bearing\n      for first-answer-wins, and the fix is to move the *send*, not the *decision*.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U139","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The registry's controller cache is seeded only for a session it created, so a *resumed* session's controller is unknown until something folds its log","body":"**U139 · The registry's controller cache is seeded only for a session it created, so a *resumed*\n      session's controller is unknown until something folds its log.** `Event::ControlChanged` is `Durable`\n      and folds to `Snapshot.controller` (doc 07 §7.7's first two spec facts, which are why the state lives\n      in the registry rather than a server map) — but `registry.rs`'s `OpenSpec::created_by` is the only\n      seed, so a session **reopened** over an existing log carries its last `ControlChanged` in records\n      nobody reads at open. A resumed session therefore starts with `controller: None`, and the first\n      `TakeControl` is not a *transfer* from the log's point of view.\n      **Why C2 did not close it:** reading the controller back needs a **fold at open**, and M1.4 has\n      nowhere to put one — doc 07 §7.2's field table is the schedule, and §7.9 forbids this row from\n      building the `Context` (M1.6) that a fold at open would populate. Recorded on `OpenSpec::created_by`'s\n      doc with its exposure stated rather than left as a silent gap. **Exposure at M1.4 is nil**: no client\n      exists, and `TakeControl` is allowed for any client in any state (§8.4's first rule), so a `None`\n      controller refuses nothing — it only fails to *notify* a previous controller that no longer exists.\n      **Owner: M1.6**, the context row, which is the first pass that folds a log at open and therefore the\n      first that can seed `Snapshot.controller` from it. **Verify:** M1.6 seeds the cache from the reopened\n      log's fold, with a test that a session closed under client A and reopened reports A as controller\n      until a `TakeControl` transfers it; this item closes citing that test.\n      ✅ **CLOSED at M1.6 Pass B (t276, 2026-10-01), Verify clause discharged verbatim.** `registry.rs`'s\n      `open_session` now seeds the actor's controller from the reopened log's fold — **R-h** (doc 09 §9.12):\n      `folded_controller`, a chunked **full forward fold from cursor 0 through `dacode_protocol::fold`**,\n      between `LogWriter::spawn` and `SessionActor::spawn`; **no hand-rolled `ControlChanged` scan** (one\n      fold implementation per design principle 2), and the backward-scan-from-tail alternative is **deferred\n      with an owner to M3's doc 20**, because `index.bin` carries `(seq, offset)` only and a backward read is\n      a new mechanism. The composition is `folded.or(created_by)` — the log outranks the claim (doc 07\n      §7.7). **The test this item named** is `registry_tests.rs`'s\n      `a_session_closed_under_client_a_and_reopened_reports_a_until_take_control_transfers_it`, with the\n      precedence pin `a_created_by_claim_does_not_outrank_the_logs_controller` beside it; the ruled error\n      arm (**a fold failure fails the open** — three reasons in `registry.rs`'s module header, the first\n      being that U122's `read_to_tail` has already refused any pre-existing corrupt record one statement\n      earlier) is pinned by `a_fold_chunk_read_failure_is_an_error_value_and_not_a_silent_unseeded_open`\n      over an injected `FailSecondRange` backend, and — at review round 2, through the private\n      `open_session_with` seam — **at the call site too**\n      (`an_open_whose_fold_fails_at_the_call_site_is_refused_and_releases_the_lock`, whose retry half also\n      pins the arm's M8-undo: the refused open shuts its writer down rather than dropping it).\n      Mutation-proved: dropping the fold, flipping the precedence, stubbing it to `Ok(None)`, swallowing\n      the chunk error, swallowing the call-site `?` (FM10) and truncating the chunk walk (FM13 — killed by\n      `the_fold_at_open_walks_every_chunk_and_finds_a_control_change_past_the_first`, **520** records) each die\n      by name. The fold's O(log-size) `Snapshot` transient is **U160** (M3's doc 20), filed beside this\n      closure. One consequence the fold cannot reach,\n      recorded on `OpenSpec::created_by`'s doc rather than left implicit: a session whose creator never\n      transferred control has **no** `ControlChanged` stored — §8.4's creator seed is memory-only — so its\n      resume still reports `None`; the fold reports what the log recorded, and the exposure stays nil\n      **only inside this item's own time box** — while `Mutating` commands are unserved the actor refuses\n      them anyway, but from **M1.9** a resumed never-transferred session would refuse `SendMessage` from\n      *everyone*, its creator included, until some client issues `TakeControl`; M1.9's row owns that\n      reading, named on `OpenSpec::created_by`'s doc (f169: the deferral has an owner rather than a\n      reassurance). `SessionActor::spawn`'s parameter was renamed `created_by` →\n      `control_seed`, because after this pass the old name was a false claim.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **CLOSED at M1.6 Pass B (t276, 2026-10-01), Verify clause discharged verbatim.** `registry.rs`'s","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U140","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`ModelInfo.window` has no specified source for a model nobody tabled, and M1.6 consumes it one row after M1.5 builds it","body":"**U140 · `ModelInfo.window` has no specified source for a model nobody tabled, and M1.6 consumes it one\n      row after M1.5 builds it.** §6 declares `window: u32` **non-optional** — commented *\"context window in\n      tokens (§4.5's threshold is a fraction of this)\"* — and §4.5's `compact_at` is a fraction of the model\n      window, so the context row reads a number the provider row must produce. Three spec facts collide:\n      **(1)** config resolution is **M2** ([17](part3-capabilities/17-config-agents-skills.md)), so at M1.5\n      there is no \"user-overridable table in config\" to consult, and U115's widening already established there\n      are **no built-in defaults** either (§5.1's layer 1 says \"built-in defaults (embedded)\" and names none);\n      **(2)** §6 promises `openai-compat` covers Ollama, vLLM, LM Studio and z.ai **\"via base URL\"**, which\n      means *arbitrary local model names* — so refusing any model absent from a seed table would break the\n      only reason that provider exists; **(3)** therefore `window` has no source for an untabled model, and\n      inventing one feeds §4.5's threshold a number nobody chose.\n      **The pricing half of this hole is CLOSED, from spec facts rather than a decision, and the route is worth\n      keeping.** §3.2's `TurnFinished` carries `cost_usd: Option<f64>`, so *absent* is expressible; read\n      against §6's own *\"a wrong cost is worse than an absent one, because it is believed\"*, the rule follows\n      without invention: **a model with no known pricing yields `cost_usd: None`, never `0.0`** — a zero cost\n      is a believed wrong number, `None` is the truth. That is recorded in doc 13 §13.11 and removes the\n      pressure to invent a price table. `window` is the half that stays open, because `u32` has no absent case.\n      **Three candidate answers, each somebody's decision:** **(a)** refuse an untabled model — honest, and\n      breaks §6's base-URL promise; **(b)** query the provider's `/v1/models` — OpenAI-compatible servers do\n      serve model ids, but **not** context windows or pricing, so it cannot fill `window`; **(c)** carry the\n      unknown — make `window` optional or add a documented conservative fallback, either of which is a\n      **normative §6 edit** and therefore Ask-First. The evidence points at (c), since §4.5 needs *a* number,\n      but the fallback value is a product decision with a two-sided failure: too small and compaction fires\n      constantly, too large and §4.5's guard never does.\n      **Not blocking M1.5's code**, and the reason is a build-order fact rather than a deferral: `complete()`\n      never reads `window` — only `models()` does, and M1.6 after it. So M1.5 can land everything doc 13\n      §§13.2–13.10 specifies (both clients, the dialect, the seam, the exchange record, the model-string\n      derivation) while this stays open, and the row that must answer it is **M1.6**, with M1.5's `models()`\n      as the surface the answer lands on. **It is also why doc 13 keeps its depth marker**: the doc-23/U93\n      precedent — a doc with a load-bearing hole is not \"expanded\", and flipping the marker would tell §4's\n      count, and every reader after it, that M1.5 opens with nothing pending.\n      ✅ **RULED BY THE USER 2026-09-24 — arm (c), verbatim: *\"U140. ok, as per your suggest\"*. The\n      sub-arm between (c)'s two forms is DERIVED FROM SPEC FACTS rather than chosen, and it is\n      `window: Option<u32>` and not a conservative fallback number.** Three facts, all in the spec\n      already: **(1)** §6 contains the idiom — `supports_cache_breakpoints` is commented *\"false ⇒ §4.5's\n      caching is inert, not an error\"*, so \"a capability is absent ⇒ the feature reading it is inert rather\n      than failing\" is the spec's own pattern, and a fallback number would make `window` the one capability\n      in that struct that **lies rather than abstains**; **(2)** A4's amendment made compaction opt-in and\n      **off by default**, so nothing consumes `window` by default in v1; **(3)** §6's overflow path is already\n      retryable-with-a-defined-route terminating through §4.2's guard set with a remedy, so an untabled model\n      that turns out to be too large is caught by a path that **exists** rather than needing an invented\n      number to pre-empt it. A fallback would also carry the two-sided failure this item names, while `None`\n      has neither side because it makes no claim. **M1.6 inherits an obligation, not a hole**: with `None`\n      there is no fraction to take, so M1.6 either treats `None` as \"no automatic compaction, rely on the\n      overflow path\" (consistent with A4, and the recommended reading) or requires an explicit token count in\n      config — a decision to make **with a consumer in hand**, which is why it was not made here.\n      ⚠️ ~~**THE RULING IS RECORDED BUT NOT LANDED, and that is why this item is still unchecked and doc 13\n      still carries its depth marker.**~~ The Verify clause below requires the §6 edit, `model.rs`'s field and a\n      test to land together, with the marker flip and §4's count change in that same commit — so recording the\n      ruling here without landing it is the *scheduled* half of the work, not a discharge. It ~~is~~ *was* tracker\n      **t255**, ~~the next session's first task,~~ and doc 13 §13.11 carries the full reasoning.\n      **Verify:** M1.6 (or M1.5, if the user rules earlier) lands one of (a)/(b)/(c) with the choice and its\n      reason recorded in doc 13 §13.11; if (c), the §6 edit lands in the same change and both `check-links`\n      modes plus `attention.py` exit 0. This item closes citing that text, and doc 13's marker flips in the\n      same commit. ✅ **The choice is made — (c), as `Option<u32>` — ~~so what remains is the landing, not the\n      decision~~ and the landing followed.**\n      ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-25 — LANDED at t255, one change carrying everything the Verify clause names.** The §6\n      text that landed: `pub window: Option<u32>,` commented *\"context window in tokens (§4.5's threshold is a\n      fraction of this); None ⇒ §4.5's fractional threshold is inert, not an error — the\n      supports_cache_breakpoints idiom below, not a fallback number (U140 ruled 2026-09-24 — doc 13, section\n      13.11; M1.6 consumes it)\"* — the inert-not-error precedent cited, as the ruling requires, because §6's own\n      `supports_cache_breakpoints` comment a few lines below is the same sentence with a different field. ⚠️ The\n      doc-13 citation is spelled \"section 13.11\" rather than with the § sign: `check-links.py`'s\n      `check_section_refs` resolves every bare § citation in the spec against the *spec's own* headings, so a\n      \"§13.11\" in §6's comment fails as a dangling reference (tracker f214 carries the finding, and the latent\n      mis-resolution of the pre-existing \"doc 13 §13.1\" cite beside it). §4.5's\n      trigger sentence gained the pointer clause (inert when the window is `None`, §6's overflow path the remedy —\n      a pointer, not a compaction policy; the policy stays M1.6's, with a consumer in hand, per the recommended\n      reading recorded in §13.11). `model.rs`'s field and docs match, and\n      `an_untabled_models_window_abstains_rather_than_guessing` constructs `window: None` asserting the\n      abstention *semantics* — `None ≠ Some(0)`, no default anywhere in the struct. Doc 13's depth marker flipped\n      and §4's count fell 24 → 23 in the same change; both `check-links` modes and `attention.py` exit 0. **The\n      no-seed property survives the ruling**: nothing in production code seeds a model table, a window or a\n      price — `None` is what an untabled model carries, and M1.6 inherits the consumer-side obligation §13.11\n      states.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **RULED BY THE USER 2026-09-24 — arm (c), verbatim: *\"U140. ok, as per your suggest\"*. The","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U141","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Every summary doc's header comment names a path that does not exist — 23 of 24 →","body":"**U141 · Every summary doc's header comment names a path that does not exist — 23 of 24\n      → **22 of 23** since t255, a migration artifact no gate reads.** Each `docs/implementation/**` doc carries\n      a first-line HTML comment naming its own path, and **22 of the 23** that still have\n      a depth marker name the **pre-split flat layout**:\n      `<!-- docs/implementation/14-tools.md -->` in a file that lives at\n      `docs/implementation/part3-capabilities/14-tools.md` *(the example was doc 13 until t255 flipped its\n      marker — the flip rewrites the whole first line, and every deepened doc carries its correct path, so\n      doc 13 gained its own as a consequence of the flip rather than as a partial sweep of this item; the\n      bulk fix below is still owed on the rest)*. Measured by comparing each header comment\n      against its own path, not by eyeballing one. **Cosmetic and mechanical, and filed because the\n      alternative is that it silently persists**: `check-links.py` validates relative links, in-page anchors\n      and §-references, and **never a header comment**, so nothing can fail on this; the ~~four~~ **six** docs\n      already deepened (01, 03, 04, 07, **13**, 33) carry correct paths *(the \"four\" was a miscount of the\n      five named, pre-existing and corrected here)*, which is how the discrepancy stays invisible — a\n      reader only ever sees the correct ones. ⚠️ **Do not fix it in one file**: correcting only the doc a\n      pass happens to touch is what produced the current split, and a partial sweep makes the artifact look\n      intentional. The fix is one command across all **26** in a single commit, and it must **not**\n      disturb the marker phrase in the same line, since §4's count greps that line — which is the only reason\n      this is worth a note rather than a `sed`. **Verify:** ~~every header comment equals its file's real path\n      (`for f in $(find docs/implementation -name '*.md'); do …`)~~ *(unexecutable as written — see the\n      correction below)* **every doc that HAS a header comment names its own real path, over the whole of\n      `docs/implementation` and not only the marker set**, comparing **the first whitespace-delimited token of\n      that comment** against the file's path — *not* the whole comment, because 23 of these docs carry the\n      depth marker inside the same `<!-- … -->` line after an em-dash, so a whole-comment comparison\n      mis-parses every one of them (`for f in $(find docs/implementation -name '*.md'); do …`);\n      **`README.md` recorded as carrying no header comment at all** so it fails loudly\n      rather than passing a `find`-loop vacuously; the two-form depth grep still agrees; and\n      `check-links.py` exits 0 in both modes. Owner: whichever pass next touches a batch of these docs, or a\n      housekeeping pass — **not** a single-doc pass.\n      ⚠️ **CORRECTED 2026-09-25, TWICE, AND THE SECOND CORRECTION IS THE ONE TO READ.** Both numbers this\n      item has carried were **true**: the original `23 of 24`, and the `22 of 23` t255's implementer wrote\n      when doc 13's marker flipped. What was false was the orchestrator's *round-1 \"correction\"* of them to\n      `23 of 23` / `27 of 43`, which overwrote a measured truth with a mis-parse, and the primary reviewer\n      caught it at round 2 by measuring independently and reporting a disagreement with the orchestrator. The\n      true counts, from a sweep **self-tested against known-good and known-bad inputs first** (doc 32 →\n      CORRECT, doc 13 → CORRECT, doc 14 → WRONG, doc 02 → WRONG, `README.md` → NO-COMMENT):\n      - **marker set**: at `7ff46fe`, **24** docs, **23** wrong, **1** correct — so `23 of 24` held. After\n        t255 flipped doc 13's marker and corrected its path, **23** docs, **22** wrong, **1** correct.\n      - **the one marker-carrying doc with a correct header** is\n        [32](part2-engine/32-task-tracking.md), and it is the whole mechanism: it is a *post-split* doc whose\n        first line is `<!-- docs/implementation/part2-engine/32-task-tracking.md — migrated summary; expand\n        during implementation. -->` — the path and the depth marker **in one comment**, separated by an\n        em-dash. Stripping `<!--`/`-->` and comparing the *whole* remainder to the path yields a mismatch, so\n        the doc that is actually correct is the one a careless sweep reports as wrong. **23** docs carry the\n        marker inside their header comment, which is why the mis-parse moves the count by a lot and not by\n        one.\n      - **whole of `docs/implementation`**: **43** `.md` files → **16** correct, **26** wrong, **1** with no\n        header comment (`README.md`). The 16 correct are the **15** no-marker docs (`00-overview`, the\n        **seven** appendices A–G, the **six** deepened 01/03/04/07/13/33, and this file — which is itself a\n        no-marker doc, the +1 an earlier draft of this bullet missed) plus doc 32, the one marker-carrying doc\n        with a correct header.\n      - **the four wrong-path docs that carry NO depth marker** — invisible to any count keyed on the marker\n        set, which is why the stated fix could never have satisfied the stated Verify — are\n        [02](part1-foundations/02-json-strategy.md),\n        [05](part2-engine/05-event-log-storage.md),\n        [06](part2-engine/06-session-organization.md) and\n        [31](part6-quality/31-ci-milestones.md). **22 marker + 4 marker-less = 26**, which is the set the\n        bulk fix covers; doc 32 needs nothing and `README.md` needs a decision, not a `sed`.\n      **The lesson is not the off-by-one, it is that the orchestrator's own sweep was never tested against an\n      input known to pass** — P-LIVE-019's rule, verbatim, in the session that was already citing it. When a\n      hand-written instrument disagrees with measurements that already agree with each other, the instrument is\n      the likelier defect, and debugging it costs one command against a file known to pass; here that command\n      was `head -1 docs/implementation/part2-engine/32-task-tracking.md`, and it was only run *after* a\n      reviewer disagreed. Two aggravating facts, both recorded as tracker f217 (whose\n      round-1 evidence is **retracted** and whose lesson is restated there): the false number was written\n      into both reviewers' dispatch prompts, and the secondary then **abandoned a correct round-1 measurement\n      to agree with it** — so putting the orchestrator's number in a reviewer's prompt converts an\n      independent check into a second transcription of the same error. The primary did the opposite and is\n      the reason this paragraph is true.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U142","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§6's nested bullets may not render as a list, and no gate here can see it — 21 sites, 6 of them pre-existing items","body":"**U142 · §6's nested bullets may not render as a list, and no gate here can see it — 21 sites, 6 of them\n      pre-existing items.** A `- [ ]` item's content column is 2, so a bullet written at **6** spaces sits at a\n      relative indent of 4 with no blank line separating it from the paragraph above it; the round-3 review\n      reports that markdown-it renders such bullets as **literal `- ` text inside the parent paragraph** rather\n      than as a nested list, and verified that by rendering rather than by reading. **Cosmetic and invisible by\n      construction**: this is P-LIVE-011's class exactly — `check-links.py` validates relative links, in-page\n      anchors, §-references and code-fence balance, and every one of those reads *text*; none parses markdown,\n      so no gate can fail on a list that does not render. **Measured, not transcribed** (`grep -c '^      - '`\n      over this file, all 21 inside §6): **21** six-space bullets, of which **17** are pre-existing across\n      **6** items (contiguous blocks beginning near lines 3837, 5175, 6197, 7489, 7556 and the 3855–3878 run)\n      and **4** were added by U141's t255 correction at lines 8044/8046/8054/8059. ⚠️ **Do not fix U141's four\n      alone**: that is U141's own instruction applied to a different artifact — correcting only the site a pass\n      happens to touch is what makes an inconsistency look intentional, and it would leave the file with two\n      competing bullet indents inside one section. **The fix is one house-wide pass** choosing an indent that\n      renders (2 or 4 spaces relative to the item's content column, or a blank line before the sublist) and\n      applying it to all 21, in a single commit, with the two-form depth grep still agreeing afterwards because\n      §4's count reads the same lines. **Verify:** all 21 sites use the chosen indent; a render of §6 shows\n      nested lists rather than literal `- ` text (name the renderer used — nothing in `tools/` does this today,\n      so the check is manual unless a markdown-parse gate is added); `check-links.py` exits 0 in both modes;\n      the depth grep is unchanged at **23** on both forms. Owner: a housekeeping pass, **not** a single-item\n      pass — the same ownership rule U141 carries. **Filed rather than fixed** because the alternative was\n      shipping a correction that quietly disagreed with its six siblings.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U143","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The canned test responder provides no *temporal* arrival separation, and two rows need it","body":"**U143 · The canned test responder provides no *temporal* arrival separation, and two rows need it.**\n      `crates/dacode-providers/src/test_responder.rs` writes a multi-element SSE body as one `write_all` per\n      element, which puts **one chunk frame per element** on the wire — and its pinned-bytes test asserts\n      exactly that. What it does **not** do is let a client *observe* the elements arriving separately, and a\n      comment in the file claimed otherwise until the review round falsified it. Two facts, both measured in\n      the fix round: tokio's `TcpStream::poll_flush` is a no-op (`tokio-1.53.1/src/net/tcp/stream.rs:1507-1511`,\n      its own words \"tcp flush is a no-op\"), so the three per-write flushes the responder carried did nothing\n      — **removing all three left every test green**; and every **runtime** driving the double is a\n      current-thread one, so `serve` writes head, chunks and terminator in one\n      uninterrupted poll. ⚠️ **That clause read \"every test driving the double is a bare `#[tokio::test]`\"\n      until Pass B2 falsified the *phrasing* while leaving the property intact** (session 30): B2's\n      streaming-pump tests hand-build a runtime on a dedicated observer `std` thread, because a runtime\n      timer cannot bound a spin inside a single poll (`.agents/pitfalls-live.md` P-LIVE-021) — that runtime\n      is **also** current-thread (`Builder::new_current_thread()`), so the no-separation property survives,\n      but \"a bare `#[tokio::test]`\" stopped being the only shape driving the double, and the responder's own\n      module doc was reworded in the same change. This is P-LIVE-006's shape inside one item: the *property*\n      was swept and re-verified, the *sentence that asserted it* was the stale half. A probe reading into a 64 KiB buffer got a whole three-element body in **one** read\n      (`first_read == total`, `reads == 1`) — the probe was temporary and is not in the tree, so what is claimed\n      is the property and not a byte count. **Filed rather than fixed** because no consumer at Pass B1\n      observes arrival and the double's own module rule is that nothing is pre-built for a later pass: adding a\n      `multi_thread` flavor and an inter-write yield with no test asserting the separation would be an\n      unobservable mechanism. **Who needs it:** design §6's `ExchangeRecord.first_byte_ms` — \"time to the first\n      streamed chunk\" — is **Pass D** (tracker t250, doc 13 §13.6's obligation 4), and a `first_byte_ms`\n      measured against this double as written is measured against a body that arrived all at once; doc 13\n      §13.13's item 10 also names \"the streaming pump\" as an M1.5 P-LIVE-016 qualifier, which is **Pass B2**\n      (tracker t257). **The fix is a test that asserts the separation, not a mechanism that hopes for it:**\n      `#[tokio::test(flavor = \"multi_thread\", worker_threads = 2)]`, a `yield_now().await` between the\n      responder's writes, and an assertion that the client held the first chunk before the last was written —\n      mutation-proved, since a concurrency property that survives the removal of its own mechanism is not\n      asserted (P-LIVE-016). **Verify:** such a test exists and fails when the inter-write yield is removed;\n      the responder's `writes` field doc carries the qualification rather than the old claim (done in the fix\n      round — re-check it survives); whichever row lands `first_byte_ms` cites this item instead of\n      re-deriving it. Owner: **Pass D** for the measurement, **t257** for the pump — whoever arrives first adds\n      the test, and **both are M1 work** (`ExchangeRecord` is an M1 deliverable by B11's signed condition).\n      ✅ **CLOSED AT Pass D (t250)** — the measurement row arrived first, exactly as this item's owner clause\n      anticipated. The responder grew `CannedResponse::sse_paced` (a runtime sleep between body writes — a real\n      delay, because a bare `yield_now` cannot guarantee separation across two worker threads), and\n      `exchange_tests::first_byte_ms_separates_from_total_ms_over_a_paced_stream` is the test this item\n      prescribed: `#[tokio::test(flavor = \"multi_thread\", worker_threads = 2)]`, driving a real provider\n      stream, asserting **on the separation** — `total_ms` spans the paced schedule while `first_byte_ms`\n      lands at a fraction of it. **Mutation-proved twice, per the verify clause**: deleting the inter-write\n      sleep collapses the schedule bound, and a recorder whose `note_stream_chunk` overwrites instead of\n      latching the first arrival makes `first_byte_ms` equal `total_ms` — each mutant dies on its own\n      assertion. The responder's `writes` field doc keeps the qualification (framing ≠ arrival timing) and\n      now cites the landed test; doc 13 §13.9 carries the extension. d14's stall-then-retryable sibling\n      rode the same extension in the same commit, as its \"cheap moment\" clause ruled.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **CLOSED AT Pass D (t250)** — the measurement row arrived first, exactly as this item's owner clause","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U144","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The XML grammar's tag set is specified nowhere, and it is prompt-side product data with no owner","body":"**U144 · The XML grammar's tag set is specified nowhere, and it is prompt-side product data with no\n      owner.**\n      ✅ **RULED 2026-09-30 AS AN ORCHESTRATOR RULING RECORDED IN DOC 13 — DESIGN §6 UNEDITED** (tracker\n      **g113**, on the survey at **f276**), which is the arm this item named and **g95** pre-decided:\n      *parameterize plus a surveyed default*, with **g90**'s seam already parameterized so the deferral\n      cost nothing. **The escalation condition did not fire** — g105 sent this back to the user only if the\n      survey *forced* a §6 edit, and it does not, because the survey shows divergence between model\n      families is handled by **dialect selection derived from the model** rather than by a product choice\n      about one grammar (oh-my-pi's dialect comes from `classifyModel().class` at\n      `packages/catalog/src/identity/dialect.ts:18-19`, not from a setting). So §6's \"**One** XML grammar\n      ships\" stays *literally true*: dacode ships one grammar plus a tag-set **parameter**, and admitting a\n      second dialect later is *additive*, not a contradiction. **THE DEFAULT, DELIBERATELY NARROW, AND EVERY\n      SPELLING CITED TO THE PROMPT THAT TEACHES IT RATHER THAN TO A PARSER THAT MERELY TOLERATES IT** — four\n      constructs: **(i)** an `invoke` element carrying the tool name in a `name` **attribute**, with one\n      child `parameter` element per argument (prompted at `dialect/xml.md:3-6`, rendered at\n      `dialect/xml.ts:64-71`); **(ii)** an **optional** `tool_calls` envelope around consecutive\n      invocations, with **both** wrapper spellings accepted because `anthropic.ts:24` sets `WRAPPER_TAGS` to\n      exactly `function_calls` and `tool_calls`; **(iii)** a `tool_response` element pair for results —\n      **a rendering target for in-band history, not a promotion trigger**, since `xml.md:21` forbids the\n      model to emit one; **(iv)** a `thinking` element pair (`xml.ts:52`). **Namespace:** every tag name is\n      accepted **both bare and `antml:`-prefixed**, which is the observed default and not an embellishment —\n      `anthropic.ts:26-43` carries the eight invocation-side names twice and `:44-57` the three reasoning\n      names. **Values are verbatim, not XML-escaped:** `xml.md:20` states the bodies are read by regex\n      delimiter-matching and **not** by a real XML parser, so the *only* reserved sequence inside a body is\n      that construct's own close delimiter. **WHY NARROW, WITH THE MECHANISM CITED:** the allowlist refuses\n      only **unknown** names — openclaw's is an injected *resolver* returning null\n      (`tool-call-repair/src/promote.ts:5-9`, `:20`, `:26`) and oh-my-pi's equivalent is a **prompt\n      sentence** (`xml.md:19`), i.e. advisory with no enforcement point at all — so a model quoting an\n      example that names a tool the session really has *passes* and becomes an invocation. Narrowness is the\n      only defence in that direction, exactly as this item recommended. The other side of the two-sided cost\n      is **accepted and disclosed, not solved**: a near-miss spelling is never promoted (silent capability\n      loss), mitigated by the set being a *parameter*, so widening is a config change and not a code change.\n      **EXCLUDED FROM THE DEFAULT, each a separate *dialect* in `factory.ts:14-26` and therefore not a tag\n      variant** — admitting them would make \"one grammar\" eleven: DSML's fullwidth-plus-ASCII doubling\n      (`deepseek.ts:33-40`), gemma's **asymmetric** open/close (`gemma.ts:14-16`), kimi's section markers\n      (`kimi.ts:15-19`), glm's `arg_key`/`arg_value` pair (`glm.ts:28-35`), hermes/qwen3's JSON-carrying\n      `tool_call` (`hermes.ts:15-16`, `qwen3.ts:15-16`), qwen-xml's self-closing attribute form\n      (`qwen-xml.ts:3-6`), forge's vendor-namespaced JSON wrapper\n      (`templates/forge-partial-tool-use-example.md:10-17` read by `tool_call.rs:206-211`). **THE REPAIR SIDE\n      DOES NOT WIDEN IT, per g105's rule, and the exclusion is measured:** openclaw's `tool-call-repair` is\n      repair by its own header (`index.ts:1`) and openclaw **prompts no XML tool grammar at all** — its\n      recognized spellings are **bracket**-based (`payload.ts:48`, `:159-161`, `:343`; `grammar.ts:2,4,6,8`).\n      And **two of the five harnesses have no in-band grammar whatsoever** (opencode, open-code-review —\n      zero hits on the same sweeps), so **the reference base for this ruling is three harnesses, not five.**\n      ⚠️ **THIS ITEM'S OWN \"INTERNALLY INCONSISTENT\" CHARGE IS *RESOLVED* RATHER THAN INHERITED, and the\n      resolution is the survey's most useful result:** the two lists sit at **different layers of a\n      three-layer scheme** — model **class** → **dialect** → **tagset**. `dialect.ts:30-31` is\n      `case \"qwen\": return \"qwen3\"`, so *both* spellings are correct at their own layer; and\n      `factory.ts:14-26` lists **eleven** dialects with **no `dsml`** *correctly*, because `dsml` is a\n      **tagset of the `xml` dialect** (`types.ts:59` declares it, `xml.ts:27` dispatches on it) and never a\n      dialect. The \"minimum viable subset\" row and the eleven-row inventory are therefore **both true**; the\n      notes conflated a *parameter* with a *member*. **THE SIBLING HOLE f236 FILED BESIDE THIS ONE IS RULED\n      IN THE SAME NODE:** the reserved control-token spelling set defaults to **SEVEN** (start, end, message,\n      channel, constrain, return, call) with the **contest cited and never merged** — six-vs-seven *inside*\n      oh-my-pi, whose `harmony-leak.ts:20-22` states the reason (\"`constrain` … is a real control token\n      even though it is not a leak signal on its own\"), and four-vs-seven *across* harnesses, explained by\n      the **action** rather than the size: oh-my-pi **escapes reversibly** over the *transport copy* for\n      provider-**validator acceptance** (`:18-20`, `:30-31`, `:34`) while openclaw **replaces\n      destructively** over *untrusted external content* (`external-content.ts:112`, `:293`, sources at\n      `:101-110`). **§6's bullet asks oh-my-pi's question**, so it takes oh-my-pi's set *and* action. Also\n      carried: escaping has **two forms** (`:33-35` raw, `:45-47` JSON with a doubled backslash and the\n      soundness argument at `:41-43`); the seven are a **subset of a larger reserved space**\n      (`docs/toolconv/harmony.md:31` records a bulk range `reserved_200014`–`reserved_201088`) so that gap\n      is a **reopen trigger, not a guess**; and **neither harness makes either escape set configurable**\n      (both are module-level consts, oh-my-pi's only knob a boolean at `openai-shared.ts:1708`), which is\n      what makes dacode's parameter load-bearing rather than decorative. **OWED TO PASS D, same commit as\n      `dialect.rs` (§2.3):** doc 13 records all of the above with its citations; §13.14's Open cell strikes\n      this item; the holdback marker set is **derived and recorded by Pass D from its own scanner** against\n      the ruled membership criterion (a delimiter belongs if recognizing it changes how the surrounding\n      bytes are classified) rather than received as a count; and **a model emitting a `tool_response` is\n      fabricating a tool result, which is a *noted non-goal* of this ruling** — §6 specifies no fake-result\n      defence and adding one would invent §6 scope. What follows is the item as filed.\n      Found while ruling the `sse.rs`/`dialect.rs` holdback seam (tracker g90), **before** dispatch\n      rather than at Pass D. Measured, not recalled: `rg` for the grammar finds the *phrase* — \"**One** XML\n      grammar with stream holdback\", the third bullet of design §6's dialect list, echoed by doc 13 §13.1's\n      `dialect.rs` layout line and §13.12's delta row (cited by **section**, not line: this pass edited doc 13\n      twice and moved every line number in it, which is how the first draft of this item came to cite two lines\n      that no longer carried the phrase) — and **zero tag spellings** anywhere: no literal, no example, no\n      pointer to a reference harness's grammar. So `dialect.rs` (Pass D, tracker t250) cannot be written\n      without choosing what a model must be *prompted* with, and that choice is U140's category — product data\n      nobody chose, which this crate's own `lib.rs` doc says nothing in production code may seed. It is\n      **two-sided** in the way U140 named: too narrow a grammar and a model emitting a near-miss spelling is\n      never promoted (silent capability loss), too permissive and prose that looks like a call becomes an\n      invocation — which is the failure §14's test #3 (the allowlist) defends, so the allowlist is the\n      mitigation and not the answer. ⚠️ The reference survey is **not** a source for the tag set either, and is\n      internally inconsistent about the variants it names: its \"minimum viable subset\" row lists\n      `anthropic/hermes/qwen/dsml` as reducing to one grammar \"with a tagset switch\", while the dialect\n      inventory it cites eleven from spells one `qwen3` and contains no `dsml` at all — so a pass that reads\n      the survey for a spelling inherits that inconsistency (this item's own first draft did, and printed a\n      \"four of eleven\" arithmetic that sums to fourteen).\n      **Why it is filed now:** g90 makes Pass B1's holdback mechanism take its delimiter set as a *parameter*,\n      so the deferral costs no rework — the mechanism is already shaped for whatever the tag set turns out to\n      be, and the reference survey records the upstream shape as \"a tagset switch\"\n      (`.agents/knowledge/reference-harnesses/dialects-and-acp.md:162-165`). **Which arm:** if the answer edits\n      design §6 it is **Ask-First** (AGENTS.md, and the g85 shape — g85 was filed because M1.5 could not be\n      written without touching a type the spec owned); if it is settled as doc 13's own reading with the tag set\n      recorded there, it is an orchestrator ruling. Pass D must decide **which** before writing `dialect.rs`\n      and must not default to the cheaper arm silently. **Verify:** a gate node closes before `dialect.rs`\n      exists, naming the arm taken and citing its precedent; doc 13 §13.14's Open cell carries it until then\n      (done in this pass — re-check); no tag literal exists in `sse.rs`'s production half, which is g90's\n      checkable form (held at B1: the only byte literals there are the BOM and SSE's own `data`/`event` field\n      names). Owner: **Pass D**, pre-code, so it is **M1 scope** — and Ask-First, i.e. the user's rather than\n      an orchestrator ruling, if the arm taken edits design §6.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **RULED 2026-09-30 AS AN ORCHESTRATOR RULING RECORDED IN DOC 13 — DESIGN §6 UNEDITED** (tracker","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U145","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The provider response-size bound is specified nowhere, and three buffers grow without it","body":"**U145 · The provider response-size bound is specified nowhere, and three buffers grow without it.**\n      ✅ **RULED UNDER THE USER'S DELEGATION AND LANDED 2026-09-27** (tracker g96, the same day's\n      third ruling after g94/g95) — the arm is neither of the two this item filed: not a\n      `[net_policy]` config key and not a bare constant, but a **`TransportLimits` struct with a\n      `Default`** in a new `limits.rs`, which is d12's shape — M2's doc 17 *substitutes* values and\n      never restructures. **[13](part3-capabilities/13-providers.md) §13.8's new \"The three\n      transport bounds\" subsection is the record**: the five-harness survey table with `path:line`\n      per row (every citation re-verified in the resolved checkouts before landing, per\n      P-LIVE-014 — three of the ruling's own needed correcting and the subsection names them), the\n      no-total-body-cap finding, and the internal/user-facing split. What landed: the bound is\n      **per undispatched SSE frame, not total body**, because a five-harness survey found **zero of\n      five** cap a general LLM response body and a total-body cap on a stream truncates a\n      legitimate long completion; the number (16 MiB) cites openclaw's\n      `MISTRAL_STREAM_BODY_MAX_BYTES` with the semantic difference stated rather than smoothed;\n      enforcement is the pump reading a new `SseDecoder::buffered()` after every body chunk —\n      **measurement put the cap on accumulated bytes rather than on d8's suggested chunk**: body\n      chunks are socket-read-sized (14 chunks, max 286 089 bytes, for a 1 MiB single-write frame\n      over §13.9's double), so a per-chunk cap at 16 MiB could never fire and the cross-chunk\n      accumulation is d8's actual concern; `FrameTooLarge` is non-retryable through `error.rs`'s\n      existing `retryable()` on §6's closed set; the cap fires proved against a stalled double,\n      mutation-proved with the tree restored md5-identical; and it is the one bound of the three\n      marked **internal-only** — doc 17 should *not* expose it, which on an all-`pub` `Clone`\n      struct is a **documented recommendation to M2, not a visibility restriction**, stated as\n      such. **d8 is discharged.** What follows is the item as filed.\n      Debt **d8** (Pass B1) recorded that `SseDecoder` imposes no length cap and named the reason: the bound\n      belongs with the body reader that owns the connection and its cancellation, because one bound placed on\n      the **chunk handed to the decoder** covers the line buffer, the data buffer and the frame count at once,\n      where a cap inside the decoder would need a number nothing supplies. Pass B2 **is** that body reader, and\n      it landed no bound — ruled before dispatch as tracker **g92** rather than discovered mid-pass, on the\n      measurement that the only size bound in the spec is design §4.3's `max_output_bytes`, a *tool*-output\n      config key for a different subsystem, and that doc 13 carries no response-size bound at all. So the\n      disclosure d8 made is now the whole of the position: **a broken or hostile provider streaming bytes with\n      no newline is bounded only by memory.** The mechanism is not the question — `http_body_util::Limited` is\n      one call away and `http-body-util` is already a declared edge — the *number* is, and a number written into\n      `transport.rs` would be product data nobody chose (U140's category, which this crate's own `lib.rs` doc\n      says nothing in production code may seed). **Which arm:** (a) a config key on the `[providers]`/`[net_policy]`\n      surface, which puts the choice with M1.7/M2 and leaves the crate unbounded until then; or (b) a constant\n      chosen now, which ships a product decision inside a mechanism file and needs a justification no doc\n      currently carries. **Ask-First either way**, because (a) edits a config surface another row owns and (b)\n      is the U140 shape that was ruled by the user, not by an orchestrator. **Verify:** ~~d8's tracker text\n      re-names its owner to this item; `transport.rs`'s \"Deliberately not here\" block cites this item rather\n      than an unnamed one; whichever row lands the bound states the arm taken and its precedent.~~ ✅ **all\n      three discharged** — the first two at Pass B2's fix round (session 30), the third by this landing:\n      doc 13 §13.8's limits subsection states the arm (`TransportLimits` with a `Default`) and its\n      precedent (d12's shape, U146's method), and `transport.rs`'s \"Deliberately not here\" block now records\n      that the two entries it carried for this item and U147 are gone *because the bounds landed*. Owner:\n      ~~the user~~ **closed**; the residual is doc 17's (M2) — the internal/user-facing split arrives there\n      as a documented recommendation, and the crate is bounded meanwhile.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **RULED UNDER THE USER'S DELEGATION AND LANDED 2026-09-27** (tracker g96, the same day's","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U146","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§6's retry numbers and jitter source are specified nowhere, and M1.9 needs them before M2's config surface exists","body":"**U146 · §6's retry numbers and jitter source are specified nowhere, and M1.9 needs them before M2's\n      config surface exists.** ✅ **RULED BY THE USER AND LANDED 2026-09-27** (tracker g94) — arm (a), with the\n      values living in `RetryPolicy::default()` rather than at M1.9's call site, so every caller gets them.\n      **[13](part3-capabilities/13-providers.md) §13.8's new \"The four backoff numbers\" subsection is the\n      record**: `retries: 2` / `base: 500 ms` / `cap: 30 s` / jitter 75–100 %, each with the four-harness\n      measurement it came from, the two choices made *against* that survey and why, the cap's inertness at the\n      default, and the jitter source's caveat plus its upgrade path. What follows is the item as filed. Design §6 requires \"backoff **with jitter** on 429/5xx\" and names **no** attempt\n      count, base delay, cap or jitter fraction; measured for Pass B2, no number appears in the spec, in\n      Appendix C, or in the reference-harness surveys. Unlike U145 this is not a defensive extra: **a retry loop\n      cannot exist without them**, so \"land nothing\" was not an available arm. Pass B2 therefore parameterised\n      — `RetryPolicy { retries, base, cap, … }` with **no `Default` impl**, because a default *is* a choice, and\n      a `JitterFn` parameter, because §13.2's declared set carries no RNG and `getrandom` is another member's\n      edge. That is the right shape and it leaves a collision rather than a gap: **M1.9's loop must construct a\n      `RetryPolicy` to call a provider at all, and the row that owns these numbers as config is M2's (doc 17).**\n      The `2` in \"each retry doubles the base\" and §6's own `429`/`500..=599` are the only numerals the\n      mechanism contains, and doubling is the one reading of \"backoff\" that needs no further number.\n      **Which arm:** (a) the user rules four numbers now and M1.9 hardcodes them until doc 17 arrives; (b) M1.9\n      invents them, which is the U140 failure with a config file as its alibi; or (c) M1.9 ships retry disabled\n      (`retries: 0`) and the first provider call a user makes is unretried, which is honest and visibly worse.\n      **Verify:** ~~a gate node closes before M1.9 wires a provider, naming the arm and the four numbers with\n      their source; `RetryPolicy` still carries no `Default` unless the ruling supplies one; whichever row picks\n      the jitter source states whether it needs an RNG edge (Ask-First, §2.3)~~ ✅ **all three discharged** —\n      g94 names the arm and the four numbers with their sources; the ruling *did* supply a `Default`, so the\n      second clause is satisfied by its own exception; and the jitter source states plainly that a real RNG would\n      need a `ring` edge (39 → 40, Ask-First) and records the upgrade path instead of taking it. Four tests pin\n      the values, the band, the variance and the clamp, each mutation-proved (an identity jitter, a constant\n      jitter, a scale-*up* jitter, a raised `retries` and a removed clamp all die). ⚠️ **Two of this item's own\n      claims were falsified by the ruling rather than by measurement, and both are the reason it is kept rather\n      than deleted**: \"no number appears … in the reference-harness surveys\" was true of the *surveys* and false\n      of the *harnesses* — all four are checked out beside this repo and three of them converge on 30 s, so the\n      numbers were measurable all along and nobody had looked; and \"M1.9 hardcodes them\" described an arm worse\n      than the one taken, since a `Default` gives Pass C, M1.9 and every test the same production policy instead\n       of three call sites inventing one each. Owner: ~~the user~~ **closed**; the residual is **d12**'s second\n       half — no config surface to override the default, which is doc 17's (M2).\n       ⚠️ **UPDATED 2026-09-28, AND IT CHANGES THE RESIDUAL FROM \"RECORDED\" TO \"APPROVED AND OWED\":** the\n       `ring::rand` jitter upgrade this item declined to take was **approved by the user** (tracker **g101**\n       item 5). One `ring` edge, boundary **39 → 40**, **zero new packages** (`ring` is already in the lock,\n       reached through `rustls::crypto::ring`). ⚠️ **It buys SPREAD QUALITY, NOT SECURITY** — `JitterFn`'s own\n       doc already rules that the source need not be cryptographic, so claiming otherwise would be the f96\n       class. It retires the 18-line hand-rolled xorshift's two disclosed weaknesses (26-bucket quantisation,\n       same-nanosecond cross-process collision) **plus U146's two surviving jitter-counter mutants**, which are\n       today defended only by a do-not-delete comment — the weaker defence of the two this repo has recorded.\n       **Conditions:** a §13.2 measurement and the boundary row land in the **same commit** as the edge (§2.3).\n       **No row owns it yet**, which is why this note is here rather than in a struck item: the sentence above\n       saying the upgrade path was \"recorded instead of taken\" was true when written and is now incomplete.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      config surface exists.** ✅ **RULED BY THE USER AND LANDED 2026-09-27** (tracker g94) — arm (a), with the","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U147","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"There is no request-exchange timeout, so a server that accepts and never answers holds the exchange open indefinitely","body":"**U147 · There is no request-exchange timeout, so a server that accepts and never answers holds the\n      exchange open indefinitely.**\n      ✅ **RULED UNDER THE SAME DELEGATION THAT RULED U145, AND LANDED 2026-09-27** (tracker g96). The arm\n      taken is the first of the three this item filed — a parameter rather than a constant or an unbounded\n      exchange — but **not** as a `RetryPolicy` sibling, which the item also contemplated and g96 rejects:\n      a retry policy governs *how many times we try* and a limits struct governs *how long one try may\n      take*, and folding the second into the first would make `RetryPolicy::default()` the place a reader\n      looks for a byte cap. It landed as **two** numbers in `TransportLimits`, because a headers-only bound\n      leaves d11's mid-stream sibling open: `exchange_timeout` (request → response headers) **120 s**, cited\n      to openclaw's cloud first-event default and labelled approximate, since a first-event bound fires\n      *later* than a headers bound so adopting its value for an earlier milestone is generous rather than\n      tight; and `stream_idle_timeout` (gap between body chunks) **300 s**, the survey's modal value at\n      2 of 5. opencode's 10 s `headerTimeout` is the only true headers-flavour default in the set and is\n      **rejected** for the same reason U146 rejected its 2 000 ms base — tuned for a warm cloud path, and\n      §6 names Ollama as an `openai-compat` target, where 10 s does not survive a cold model load. Both are\n      **user-facing** at M2's doc 17 surface. What landed beyond the ruling's numbers: enforcement on both\n      sides of the delivered-output boundary — the exchange timeout wraps each attempt's wait for headers\n      inside `send`'s retry loop (pre-headers, so returning strands nothing), the idle timeout is a fresh\n      per-chunk `tokio::time::timeout` in the pump ending the stream with a terminal `Err` item (post-\n      delivery, where re-issuing would duplicate output — the boundary is why the two sit where they do\n      although neither retries); **neither timeout is retryable** (§6's closed set — *\"Only 429 and 5xx are\n      retried\"* — and a timeout is not a status; all three new variants classify through `error.rs`'s\n      existing `retryable()`, pinned by test); **neither is abort-aware**, disclosed beside U148 rather\n      than left to differ silently from §6's backoff sleep — covering the in-flight exchange with the\n      signal is mechanically cheap from `send` but *which* signal arrives there is U148's Ask-First\n      question, and the pump needs no signal because §4.2 cancels a stream by dropping it, which kills the\n      idle wait itself. Both bounds are proved **to fire** against `test_responder`'s new stalled doubles\n      (a d9-disclosed extension), each mutation-proved with every restore md5-verified; P-LIVE-021's\n      discriminator puts all three firing tests on the **park** side, so runtime timers bound them and no\n      OS-timer observer was needed; U143 does not apply because the stall *is* the arrival separation.\n      **d11 is discharged — both halves, which is the argument for two numbers rather than one.**\n      **[13](part3-capabilities/13-providers.md) §13.8's limits subsection is the record**, survey table\n      included, with the three citation corrections the landing's re-verification made (P-LIVE-014).\n      What follows is the item as filed. Recorded as debt **d11** by Pass B2 and independently re-measured by its\n      review round: `Transport::send` awaits the hyper-util client with no bound, and §6 specifies none. The\n      disclosure matters more than the mechanism — the mechanism is a `tokio::time::timeout` around the\n      exchange, and `time` is already a declared feature of this crate. ⚠️ **The supporting claim Pass B2 first\n      wrote for this gap was false and the review round falsified it** (the P-LIVE-014 class, arriving in the\n      pass after the one that fixed four of them): `transport.rs` asserted that hyper-util's client builder \"has\n       no timeout method to set\" and enumerated a five-name \"whole setter list\", while the resolved\n       `hyper-util-0.1.21` builder carries **30** `&mut self` setters — reproducible with\n       `rg -U -c 'pub fn [a-z_0-9]+\\(\\s*&mut self' …/hyper-util-0.1.21/src/client/legacy/client.rs`, which\n       returns 30 against that file's **41** total `pub fn`s (the difference being `Client`'s own methods and\n       `Builder::{new,build,build_http}`) — including `timer` (`client.rs:1551`) and\n       `pool_timer` (`:1561`), neither `cfg`-gated, and `pool_idle_timeout` (`:1075`) — which the enumeration\n       itself listed — *is* a timeout setter. ⚠️ **This count took three passes to become reproducible, and the\n       reason is the lesson**: the review round reported \"roughly forty\" (which is the `pub fn` total, not the\n       setter count), the fix round reported **33**, and the second review round could reproduce neither — it\n       measured 41 / 35 / 30 across three patterns of its own (tracker f233 item 2, then f234). A multi-line\n       signature is matched or not depending on whether the pattern is `rg -U`, so **the number is only\n       meaningful with its command beside it**, which is P-LIVE-014's rule applied to a count rather than to a\n       citation. What all five measurements agree on is that a five-name \"whole setter list\" was wrong by ~6×\n       in a comment flagged *MEASURED, not assumed*. The true statement is\n       narrower and is what the code has carried since that round: there is no **request-exchange** timeout\n       setter — `timer`/`pool_timer` supply a *clock*, not a duration, and the `http2_keep_alive_*` pair\n       (`:1454`/`:1475`) is behind a feature this crate does not carry. The number is\n      the same category as U145's ~~and U146's~~ — product data nobody chose *(⚠️ U146 has since been\n      **ruled and landed**, so its four numbers *are* chosen and live in `RetryPolicy::default()`; this\n      item and U145 are the surviving two, and the interaction is now sharper rather than weaker — a\n      reader of doc 13 §13.8's ruled backoff numbers will reasonably ask why the exchange bound beside\n      them is still open)* — and it interacts with both: a\n      provider exchange bound and a retry bound are two numbers a user will expect to reason about together,\n      and one of them now has a value to reason from.\n      **Which arm:** ~~a per-exchange timeout as a `RetryPolicy` sibling parameter (no number invented, M1.9\n      blocked the same way), or a ruled constant, or leaving it unbounded and letting §4.2's `Cancel` be the only\n      escape — which is defensible for an interactive harness and is what the tree does today.~~ *(the first\n      arm, minus the `RetryPolicy` sibling — g96's rejection reason is in the ✅ closure above.)* **Verify:**\n      ~~d11's tracker text re-names its owner to this item; no comment in `transport.rs` enumerates a third-party setter\n      list without a `file:line` (P-LIVE-014's corrective); whichever row lands a bound says what it gates on,\n      per doc 13 §13.8's rule 3.~~ ✅ **all three discharged** — the re-name at session 30; the setter-list\n      comment is gone entirely with the bound's landing (nothing in `transport.rs` enumerates setters any\n      more, which satisfies the clause more strongly than adding cites would); and the landing row says what\n      each bound gates on — the exchange timeout and the idle bound both gate on **parked** socket reads\n      inside the test's own runtime (P-LIVE-021's timer-bounded side, classified per test in\n      `transport_limits_tests.rs`'s module doc), and the tests' `IO_BOUND` guards carry §13.8 rule 3's\n      statement beside them. Owner: ~~the user~~ **closed**; the residuals are **U148** (abort over an\n      in-flight exchange, Ask-First) and **d12**'s second half (the config surface, doc 17's at M2, to\n      which both timeouts arrive as user-facing recommendations).","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **RULED UNDER THE SAME DELEGATION THAT RULED U145, AND LANDED 2026-09-27** (tracker g96). The arm","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U148","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§6's `Provider::complete` signature has no way to receive §4.2's `Cancel`, so the abort-aware sleep §6 requires is unreachable as specified","body":"**U148 · §6's `Provider::complete` signature has no way to receive §4.2's `Cancel`, so the abort-aware\n      sleep §6 requires is unreachable as specified.**\n      ⚠️ **ARM RULED BY THE USER 2026-09-28 — (b). DO NOT RE-OPEN THE CHOICE; WHAT REMAINS IS THE CODE, AT\n      M1.9** (tracker **g101** item 3). The client is constructed **holding a token per session**, which keeps\n      §6's signature (**no spec edit**) and needs no new edge, moving the question to who owns the token's\n      lifetime — a session-scoped question M1.9 owns anyway. **Arms (a) and (c) are not taken and are\n      orthogonal to it**: (a) would edit §6, and (c) would add `tokio-util` as an edge (boundary 39 → 40) and\n      is only needed to get the real `CancellationToken` *type* rather than the landed `AbortSignal` alias.\n      ⚠️ **STILL UNRULED, AND DELIBERATELY NOT FOLDED INTO THIS CLOSURE:** whether a cap on **total retry\n      wall-clock** should exist at all. The measured worst case is **~361 s** (~420 s with a clamped\n      `Retry-After`) and **`Cancel` cannot interrupt any of it**. That is a *new number* and arguably a §6\n      question; nobody has ruled it and g101 records that it explicitly did not. **The checkbox stays open\n      because the code has not landed**, not because the arm is undecided. What follows is the item as filed.\n      §6 says the backoff sleep is **abort-aware** — \"a\n      cancellation during a backoff returns immediately rather than after the delay\" — and `lib.rs`'s\n      `Provider::complete` doc already states retry as *that method's* contract, never the caller's. But the\n      signature is `complete(&self, req: CompletionRequest)`: there is no channel for a cancellation to arrive\n      on. Dropping the returned `CompletionStream` does cancel the pump (Pass B2 tests that path), and it cannot\n      cancel a sleep **inside** `complete()`'s own future, which the caller is still awaiting. Pass B2 landed the\n      seam rather than the type — an `AbortSignal` alias for any boxed `Send` future resolving to `()` — because\n      §4.1's token type is `tokio_util::sync::CancellationToken` and `tokio-util` is **not** an edge of this\n      crate, so adding it is Ask-First (§2.3). ⚠️ Consequently one claim in that seam is **unmeasured and\n      labelled so in the code**: that `token.cancelled_owned()` satisfies the alias's `Send` bound. Nothing in\n      `tokio-util` compiles in this tree, so a citation would have been a P-LIVE-014 one; M1.9 is the row that\n      wires a token to a provider and therefore the row that finds out. **Which arm:** (a) `complete()` gains a\n      cancellation parameter — **a design §6 edit, so Ask-First**; (b) the client is constructed holding a token\n      per session, which keeps §6's signature and moves the question to who owns the token's lifetime; or\n      (c) `tokio-util` becomes an edge of this crate, which needs a boundary row and a §13.2 measurement.\n      **Verify:** a gate node closes before M1.9 wires a provider, naming the arm; the `Send` claim is measured\n      in the tree that can measure it rather than asserted in one that cannot; if (a) or (c), the §6 edit or the\n      §13.2 row lands in the same commit as the code (§2.3's same-commit rule). Owner: **the user** for the arm,\n      consumed by **M1.9** — **M1 scope**.","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U149","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§4.4's \"on every connection\" is not what the resolver seam can deliver, and an IP-literal `base_url` bypasses the egress check entirely","body":"**U149 · §4.4's \"on every connection\" is not what the resolver seam can deliver, and an IP-literal\n      `base_url` bypasses the egress check entirely.**\n      ✅ **CLOSED 2026-10-03 — arm (b)'s code landed at M1.7 Pass C** (`dto::require_name_host` +\n      `ProviderError::LiteralHostBaseUri`, both dto constructors fallible, the error naming the ruled\n      remedy; the detection byte-matches hyper-util's own literal check, verified against\n      `connect/dns.rs`/`connect/http.rs`/http-1.5's `authority.rs` in the registry checkout at review;\n      the only literal-host URIs in the crate are the six in the three refusal/detection test files,\n      none of which constructs a provider or connects; the declined connector-wrapper measurement was\n      not revived). d10 discharged with it. The item as filed follows.\n      ⚠️ **ARM RULED BY THE USER 2026-09-28 — (b). DO NOT RE-OPEN THE CHOICE; WHAT REMAINS IS THE CODE, AT\n      M1.7** (tracker **g101** item 4). The transport **refuses a literal-host `base_url` outright**, with an\n      error that names the remedy. Safe, and **no spec edit**. Against **(a)**, which would make *two*\n      enforcement points and cut §13.5's one-typed-point argument, and **(c)**, which is Ask-First, is *two*\n      spec sentences (§4.4's and §14's), and **documents the hole instead of closing it**.\n      ⚠️ **THE COST IS REAL AND MUST BE DISCLOSED IN THE ERROR, NOT SMOOTHED OVER:** §6 covers Ollama/vLLM\n      *\"via base URL\"* and `127.0.0.1` is a common spelling, so the refusal must say that **a hostname *is*\n      guarded and a literal is not** — `localhost` works, `127.0.0.1` does not. ⚠️ **AND ONE MEASUREMENT WAS\n      DECLINED, WHICH M1.7 MUST NOT QUIETLY UNDECLINE:** the orchestrator additionally recommended measuring a\n      **fourth** shape before settling — a connector wrapper feeding an already-literal host to the *same*\n      `EgressGuard`, which would satisfy §4.4 *and* preserve §13.5's one-point property that (b) sacrifices.\n      The user chose (b) **without** that measurement, so if M1.7 finds the one-point shape is cheap, that is a\n      **new arm needing a new ruling**, not a quiet supersession of this one. **The checkbox stays open because\n      the code has not landed**, not because the arm is undecided. What follows is the item as filed.\n      §4.4 states the IP check runs *after* resolution, **on\n      every connection**, on the resolved address, because \"a hostname check alone is what makes SSRF possible\".\n      Doc 13 §13.5 built the seam on that and quotes **design §14's own** Providers-tier bullet — \"the only test\n      that can fail on the IP check being **skipped** or ordered before resolution, which a hostname-level test\n      would pass either way\" — so the over-broad half is *spec* text that a plan doc repeats, not a plan-doc\n      invention. ⚠️ That attribution was corrected in the fix round (tracker f233, item 1): this item's first\n      draft assigned the claim to doc 13 and its Verify clause prescribed narrowing it *there*, which would have\n      meant rewriting a quotation of the spec inside a plan doc. Pass B2 measured the first half of the claim\n      false: hyper-util **skips the resolver** when the URI's host is already an IP literal\n      (`hyper-util-0.1.21/src/client/legacy/connect/http.rs:557-560`, its own comment \"If the host is already an\n      IP addr (v4 or v6), skip resolving the dns and start connecting right away\"), so a check placed at the\n      resolver — correctly, and structurally unable to run earlier — never sees such a connection. **The ordering\n      half holds and is proved**; the *skipped* half does not. ⚠️ **How exotic the case is was overstated in this\n      item's first draft and the correction changes an arm's cost** (found by the review round, tracker f234):\n      design §6 says only that `openai-compat` *\"covers OpenAI, OpenRouter, Groq, Ollama, vLLM, z.ai, etc. **via\n      base URL**\"* — it names Ollama and **no URL spelling**, and `11434` appears **zero** times in the spec\n      (measured repo-wide; the only three occurrences were the two this pass added to docs and one in\n      `transport.rs`). Ollama's own documented default is `http://localhost:11434`, and **`localhost` is a\n      hostname, so the landed seam *does* guard it**. Reaching the bypass needs a base URL spelled with a\n      **literal** (`http://127.0.0.1:11434`) — a common way to write one, and the spelling §4.4's own SSRF\n      example uses, but a *user-config* shape rather than a spec-named one. Recorded as debt\n      **d10** with owner **M1.7**, and Pass B2 deliberately did **not** add a second enforcement point: §13.5's\n      whole argument is that one *typed* point beats two *remembered* ones, and where the second lives is a seam\n      §13.1 reserves to the doc. No test in the crate uses a literal-host URI, so none can pass on the unguarded\n      path by accident — which is the property to preserve, not a gap to paper over. **Which arm:** (a) M1.7's\n      `[net_policy]` gains a URI-level check ahead of the connector, making two points and needing a rule for\n      which one is authoritative; (b) the transport refuses a literal-host `base_url` outright, which is safe and\n      breaks a *plausible user configuration* — not, as this item's first draft claimed, a case §6 names, since\n      §6 names no spelling and the Ollama default it would most often be written as (`localhost`) stays\n      permitted; or (c) §4.4's sentence is narrowed to \"on every connection whose host\n      required resolution\" — **a spec edit, so Ask-First**, and the weakest of the three because it documents the\n      hole instead of closing it — and note that arm (c) is **two** spec sentences, §4.4's and §14's, not one.\n      **Verify:** doc 13 §13.5 *annotates* the quotation with the measurement and points here rather than\n      rewriting it (landed in this pass — re-check it survives); the **narrowing itself is the user's**, being\n      spec text; d10's tracker text names this item; whichever arm is taken\n      lands with a test that fails on the literal path, since a hostname test passes either way and that is\n       exactly §14's stated reason for owning test #5. Owner: **the user** for the arm, consumed by **M1.7** —\n       **M1 scope**, and a security property rather than a nicety.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **CLOSED 2026-10-03 — arm (b)'s code landed at M1.7 Pass C** (`dto::require_name_host` +","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U150","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Design §6 says the transport copy arrives *already escaped*, while doc 13 §13.1 puts the escaping inside this crate — and `ProviderMessage.content` is opaque, so neither reading is implementable exactly as written","body":"**U150 · Design §6 says the transport copy arrives *already escaped*, while doc 13 §13.1 puts the\n      escaping inside this crate — and `ProviderMessage.content` is opaque, so neither reading is\n      implementable exactly as written.**\n      ✅ **RULED BY THE USER 2026-09-28 — ARM (a) IN ITS REFINED (a1) SHAPE** (tracker **g100**, with the\n      seam consequence ruled at **g103**): **§6 is the truth.** The transport copy arrives *already\n      escaped*, so the **engine** applies the escaping while it builds the copy — **no spec edit**, so this\n      was an orchestrator recommendation the user confirmed rather than an Ask-First. **The (a1)\n      refinement is what makes the arm cheap rather than a deferral:** the escaping *function* still lives\n      in `dialect.rs` inside `dacode-providers`, because it is provider knowledge shared by both clients\n      exactly as §13.1 argued — **what §13.1 got wrong is *who calls it*, not *where it lives*.** Only the\n      call site moves, to `dacode-core` at **M1.9**. Rejected: **(a2)** escaping moves *wholesale* to\n      `dacode-core`, which would have pushed a correctness fix for gpt-5.x/gpt-oss past a brainstorm-gated\n      row; **(b)** and **(c)**, both of which edit design §6, and (c) additionally needs a boundary\n      statement neither document has.\n      ⚠️ **THE CONSEQUENCE IS BIGGER THAN THE QUESTION LOOKED, AND IT IS g103's RULING:** under (a1)\n      **both** of Pass C's seams lose their caller, so **Pass D *removes* them rather than substituting\n      into them.** The argument turns on one word in §6 — if the *engine* escapes while building the copy,\n      then `complete()` **receives an escaped copy**, so a step inside `dto/` would either **double-escape\n      or be inert**, and neither is a substitution. The same holds for the demotion, which **g97\n      predicted** in terms. And the engine side is *strictly easier*, which is the positive argument: the\n      demotion strips `tool_calls` and re-expresses tool results, which over an opaque provider-shaped\n      `content` means parsing bytes §6 calls opaque — but **the engine holds the pre-shaping data**, so it\n      can demote *before* shaping and never parse anything. **(a1) is therefore the only arm that\n      satisfies the opacity rule without a re-parse.** Pass D deletes `TransportCopy`, `EscapeStep`,\n      `DemotionStep`, `ControlTokenSet`, `ToolCapability` and `with_transport_copy` from `dto/mod.rs` plus\n      the tests pinning them — **including the entry-point test, which dies because the thing it pinned no\n      longer exists**, and that is *said* here rather than left looking like lost coverage. §14's test #1\n      **splits** into a `dto/` byte-exactness half (an already-escaped copy is spliced to the wire\n      unchanged) and an `exchange.rs` half; test #2 becomes a **pure `dialect.rs` unit test**. *Why remove\n      rather than keep an inert default:* this repo's own precedent — at M1.4 Pass C2 \"the two attributes\n      that guarded nothing on their own were **removed**, since an individually redundant exemption reads\n      as load-bearing to whoever deletes it next.\" A seam whose caller will never exist is that shape.\n      **And the seam did its job even though it is being deleted**, which is the part worth recording:\n      arm (a1) cost a *deletion* plus two re-aimed tests instead of a rewrite of both adapters, because\n      g95 had made it parameterized. **Cost, named before dispatch rather than discovered:** those\n      functions have **no production caller until M1.9**, so Pass D needs a scoped **item-level**\n      dead-code exemption naming M1.9 as remover — **d6's shape a fourth time**; module-level is refused\n      per d5's discharge. **Owed to doc 13 §13.1 in the same commit (§2.3):** its `dialect.rs` line must\n      say which of §6's five rules the **engine** applies and which stay in this crate, since (a1)\n      *splits* them and the layout rule was written assuming they travel together. What follows is the\n      item as filed.\n      Found while ruling tracker **f236** (the file-ownership\n      contradiction that made Pass C undispatchable as scoped — see **g95**), i.e. found by *decomposition*\n      rather than by implementation, and filed pre-dispatch on U144's precedent rather than at Pass D.\n      Three measurements, all in the resolved sources rather than recalled:\n      **(1)** design §6's own field comment on `CompletionRequest.messages` reads *\"already escaped for\n      transport, never for the log\"* (`docs/dacode-design.md:2120`), and the struct doc two lines above says\n      *\"the engine builds it, the provider only encodes it\"* (`:2116-2117`);\n      **(2)** `ProviderMessage.content` is `RawJson` — *\"provider-shaped, so opaque here by design\"*\n      (`:2089`), landed as `model.rs:120`;\n      **(3)** doc 13 §13.1's layout block assigns *\"Harmony control-token escaping\"* to `dialect.rs`,\n      **inside `dacode-providers`**, and its second layout rule makes that escaping *shared by both clients*\n      rather than per-provider.\n      Read together, (1) places the transform **upstream of `complete()`** — on the engine, i.e. `dacode-core`\n      and therefore **M1.9**, a different crate and a different row — while (3) places it in this crate. And\n      (2) makes the provider-side reading costly rather than merely different: escaping a reserved spelling\n      that sits *inside* an opaque `RawJson` means reaching into provider-shaped bytes, which is not \"only\n      encodes it\", and no doc states a rule for when an adapter may parse bytes the spec calls opaque.\n      ⚠️ **This is not a doc typo and it is not cosmetic.** It decides *which crate* owns a correctness fix,\n      and it is the same shape the board records repeatedly at this milestone — the defect in a plan doc\n      rather than in the code that followed it — arriving here in the **spec and the plan doc disagreeing\n      with each other**, which is worse than either being wrong alone, because a worker can cite either and\n      look compliant. **g95 is what makes the deferral free**: Pass C leaves a *named, parameterized* seam\n      where the escaping step will be inserted into the transport copy (g90's pattern), so every arm below\n      is a substitution into that seam rather than a rebuild, and no `dto/` file may carry a control-token\n      literal meanwhile. **Which arm:** (a) §6's comment is the truth — the engine escapes, doc 13 §13.1's\n      `dialect.rs` line loses the escaping, and `dacode-providers` never escapes; an orchestrator ruling plus\n      a plan-doc edit, **no spec change**; (b) §13.1 is the truth — §6's \"already escaped\" comment and its\n      \"the provider only encodes it\" sentence are wrong, so **a design §6 edit, Ask-First**, and the crate\n      owes a stated rule for parsing an opaque `content`; or (c) a **split** — the engine escapes text it\n      owns and the provider escapes provider-shaped content — which needs a boundary statement neither\n      document currently has, and is therefore also Ask-First for the half that writes it into §6.\n      **Verify:** a gate node closes **before `dialect.rs` exists**, naming the arm and citing its precedent;\n      if (b) or (c), the §6 edit lands in the **same commit** as the code (§2.3's same-commit rule); Pass C's\n      seam survives whichever arm is taken, checked as g90's form is (no control-token literal in any `dto/`\n      file, the seam takes the token set as a parameter); and no `dto/` file parses `ProviderMessage.content`\n      in the meantime, since that would pre-empt (a) by implementing (b). Owner: **Pass D**, pre-code, so\n      **M1 scope** — and **Ask-First** if the arm taken edits design §6.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **RULED BY THE USER 2026-09-28 — ARM (a) IN ITS REFINED (a1) SHAPE** (tracker **g100**, with the","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U151","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§6's `ProviderMessage` cannot express a provider-specific *message-level* field, so `openai-compat`'s `tool_call_id` is unencodable for `ProviderRole::Tool` — and the mainstream case is the one that breaks","body":"**U151 · §6's `ProviderMessage` cannot express a provider-specific *message-level* field, so `openai-compat`'s\n      `tool_call_id` is unencodable for `ProviderRole::Tool` — and the mainstream case is the one that breaks.**\n      Found by Pass C's implementer while encoding the four roles, sustained by the primary review, and filed here\n      rather than left in a comment because **P-LIVE-017's rule is that a deferral recorded only in a comment names\n      no row that will ever read it**. ⚠️ **The code already cites this id** (`lib.rs:145-146`, `dto/anthropic/mod.rs:44`)\n      — it was cited before the item existed, which is the dangling-reference class this board's own id rule exists to\n      prevent, and is why the item is filed in the same commit as the code.\n      **The measurement:** `ProviderMessage` has exactly two fields, `role` and `content: RawJson` (`model.rs:114-121`,\n      transcribing §6:2087-2090). On the chat-completions wire `tool_call_id` is a **sibling of `content`**, not inside\n      it (forge `crates/forge_app/src/dto/openai/request.rs:25-47`), so there is nowhere to put it. Anthropic is\n      **unaffected** — its `tool_use_id` lives *inside* the `tool_result` block, which is inside `content`.\n      **The consequence, and why the implementer's first reading of it was wrong:** it initially argued this was not a\n      blocker because §6's `supports_tools: false` demotion re-expresses tool results as user messages anyway. The primary\n      overturned that: **the demotion fires only for `supports_tools: false`**, so for every tool-**capable**\n      `openai-compat` model — the mainstream case — the request as landed is rejected by the provider. Arm (iii) closes\n      the *minority* only. The landed encoding is pinned, not silent:\n      `all_four_roles_travel_as_themselves_and_the_tool_gap_is_pinned` asserts the exact body and its missing field.\n      *(That was the pin's **then**-name and it asserted the **gap**; at closure below it was renamed\n      `all_four_roles_travel_as_themselves_and_a_tool_result_carries_its_call_id` and re-aimed to assert the\n      chosen behaviour, per this item's own verify clause.)*\n      **Which arm:** (i) §6 gains a provider-specific message-level field — **Ask-First**, the g85 shape; (ii) the engine\n      keeps tool results as `User` messages carrying the provider's block shape, leaving `Tool` unread on this path;\n      (iii) Pass D's demotion re-expresses them, which §6 already requires for `supports_tools: false` — so the gap\n      **self-closes for the demoted case and stays open for a tool-capable one**; (iv) **the primary's, cheaper than (i)\n      and provider-neutral** — one opaque `RawJson` *sibling-fields* object spliced beside `role`/`content` and never\n      parsed, rather than an openai-named `tool_call_id: Option<String>`.\n      **Verify:** an arm is named with its precedent before M1.9 builds a history that reaches this path; if (i) or (iv),\n      the §6 edit lands in the **same commit** as the code (§2.3's rule); the pinning test is updated to assert the\n      *chosen* behaviour rather than the gap; and no `dto/` file parses `ProviderMessage.content` in the meantime, since\n      that would pre-empt U150's arm (a) by implementing (b). Owner: **Pass D or M1.9, whichever first builds a\n      tool-capable `openai-compat` history** — **M1 scope**, and **Ask-First** on arms (i) and (iv).\n      ✅ **CLOSED AT Pass D (t250) — ARM (iv), the user's own 2026-09-28 ruling (tracker g101), which is what\n      satisfies the Ask-First clause.** Pass D is the owner by this item's own trigger: its pinning-test rewrite\n      builds a tool-capable `openai-compat` history carrying a `tool_call_id`. **Every verify clause, discharged\n      in one commit (§2.3):** §6's `ProviderMessage` gained `sibling_fields: Option<RawJson>` — one opaque\n      object spliced beside `role`/`content`, never parsed — verified by `git diff docs/dacode-design.md`\n      carrying **only** that edit; `model.rs` carries the field and its contract; `dto`'s shared\n      `encode_message` splices the object's interior verbatim (byte-stable, no re-serialisation) in **both**\n      encoders, refusing a non-object at encode time rather than dropping it; the pinning test now asserts the\n      *chosen* behaviour — `all_four_roles_travel_as_themselves_and_a_tool_result_carries_its_call_id`, an\n      assistant `tool_calls` sibling and a tool result's `tool_call_id` both on the socket bytes — and no\n      `dto/` file parses `content` (the gate greps and the splice's mechanical interior strip are the checks).\n      ⚠️ **The closure is the sibling field's, NOT §14's test #2's**: the demotion landing beside it closes\n      only the `supports_tools: false` minority, exactly as g99/g101 warned — a pass that shipped test #2\n      alone would have *narrowed* this item, not closed it.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **CLOSED AT Pass D (t250) — ARM (iv), the user's own 2026-09-28 ruling (tracker g101), which is what","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U152","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§6's `StreamChunk` cannot amend a `ToolCallBegin`, so a tool name arriving *after* its call's first argument fragment is unrecoverable — and Pass C had to choose which way to lose it","body":"**U152 · §6's `StreamChunk` cannot amend a `ToolCallBegin`, so a tool name arriving *after* its call's first\n      argument fragment is unrecoverable — and Pass C had to choose which way to lose it.** Found in Pass C's round 4,\n      when the primary measured that the natural one-clause fix for an id-before-name provider silently dropped the real\n      name: the call opened nameless at the announcement and `apply`'s dedup discarded the name one chunk later, so\n      **the model's tool call vanished with no error and no text**. The landed rule is the dominant one instead — a\n      `name` *opens* a call, an `id` only *announces* it, and `ToolCallBegin{name:\"\"}` is minted late, immediately\n      before the fragment that needs it — which recovers the name in every ordering except \"id and arguments in the same\n      element, name in a later one\".\n      **Why this is a spec limit and not a `dto/` choice:** oh-my-pi applies a late name unconditionally\n      (`packages/ai/src/providers/openai-completions.ts:1310`, `if (incomingName) block.name = incomingName;`) because its\n      block is a mutable object it owns. dacode cannot: §6's `ToolCallBegin` carries `name` and is emitted **once** into\n      what §3.6 makes an append-only log, and `StreamChunk` has **no rename variant**. **Ruled NOT worth amending now**:\n      a `ToolCallRename` variant or a mutable call view is a protocol change carrying fold handling, a golden fixture and\n      an event-log **compatibility event** (§3.2/§13/§14), which is disproportionate for an unmeasured wire ordering\n      nobody has observed (d13 — no provider call has ever been made against a real endpoint here), and in the residual\n      case the only loss is the name, which §6's allowlist is *already specified* to refuse (\"otherwise it stays text\").\n      **Verify:** the item is reopened **only** on a durable trigger — *a provider is observed sending arguments before\n      the name* — and not on argument; if reopened, the arm is a §6 amendment and therefore **Ask-First**; until then\n      `dto/openai_compat/stream.rs`'s `## ⚠️ The §6 vocabulary limit` section is the record and must cite this item; and\n      the landed emit-late rule stays pinned by\n      `an_id_announces_a_call_and_a_later_name_still_opens_it`'s seven shapes. Owner: **nobody until the trigger fires** —\n      recorded so the choice is a decision and not an accident. **Not** covered by U151, which is a different type\n      (`ProviderMessage`) and the opposite direction (a field the spec cannot carry, not a chunk it cannot amend).\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U153","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`writer_tests.rs`'s bounded-queue test is *weakened* rather than skipped, and the blocker it recorded has been paid — so the stronger form is now owed work with no row that owns it","body":"**U153 · `writer_tests.rs`'s bounded-queue test is *weakened* rather than skipped, and the blocker\n      it recorded has been paid — so the stronger form is now owed work with no row that owns it.**\n      Found by Pass E's phrase sweep and sustained by both reviewers. `crates/dacode-core/src/store/writer_tests.rs:37`\n      is the module's own probe table, and it records that `the_request_queue_is_bounded_at…` ships in a\n      **weakened** form — the secondary's words, *\"not `#[ignore]`d — a WEAKENED test\"* — with the end-to-end\n      bounded-queue form deferred because it *\"needs a timeout that `tokio::time` (M1.5) would supply\"*.\n      **That blocker is gone**: Pass E landed `time` and `dacode-core` now declares it member-side\n      (`crates/dacode-core/Cargo.toml`), so nothing prevents the stronger form. ⚠️ **Two comments in the tree\n      now assert this item exists** — `:37` says the form is *\"owed as board work\"* and `:667` says it *\"is\n      carried as board work rather than left to this comment to remember\"* — so until this entry stands, both\n      are present-tense claims about a board that did not carry it. That is the f224 stale-cite class arriving\n      as a *forward* reference, which is worse: a stale cite points at something that was true, while a forward\n      one points at something nobody has done yet.\n      **Why it is not fixed in Pass E:** the pass was scoped test-conversion-only, and writing a new end-to-end\n      test is not a comment correction — it needs a writer double and a mutation proof, which is d14's shape\n      (a test-double *extension*, whose cost d9 already establishes). Absorbing it into a comment-only round\n      would have hidden real work inside a pass whose whole finding was that prose and code had drifted apart.\n      **Verify:** the end-to-end bounded-queue form is written and **mutation-proved** (a queue that does not\n      bound must fail it, with the selected/failed counts reported per P-LIVE-019); `writer_tests.rs:37`'s and\n      `:667`'s comments then name the landed test instead of pointing here; and the weakened form is either\n      deleted or its remaining distinct coverage stated, since a weakened test kept beside its strengthened\n      replacement reads as two tests and is one.\n      Owner: **whoever next extends `crates/dacode-core/src/store/writer_backends.rs`** — the row that needs a\n      backend gating `append` rather than `flush`, i.e. a **`GatedAppend` sibling of the existing `GatedFlush`**.\n      ⚠️ **Not d14's owner, and an earlier draft of this line said so wrongly**: d14 assigns \"whoever next extends\n      `test_responder` (Pass C or Pass D … if neither does, M1.9)\", which is a *providers* file and a different\n      extension. Its **fallback reasoning** does transfer — M1.9 is the first row where a user waits on writer\n      backpressure, so M1.9 is this item's fallback owner too — but the artifact does not. `StallPoint` and\n      stall-then-retryable are HTTP-provider vocabulary with zero occurrences in `dacode-core/src`.\n      No milestone gate, so **P3**; it is test *strength*, not a missing capability.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U154","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"One of Pass E's ten conversions is not a timeout at all, and its failure mode is a *silent false pass* rather than a hang — which is the one shape this milestone keeps finding and the only one no gate sees","body":"**U154 · One of Pass E's ten conversions is not a timeout at all, and its failure mode is a *silent\n      false pass* rather than a hang — which is the one shape this milestone keeps finding and the only one\n      no gate sees.** `crates/dacode-core/src/replay_tests.rs:604` waits with an unconditional\n      `tokio::time::sleep(SETTLE)` (`SETTLE` = 50 ms, `:58`) and then asserts `extra.is_none()` — an\n      **absence window**: it proves nothing arrived, so it can only ever be *widened*, never bounded. Nine of\n      the ten loops became `tokio::time::timeout` over a predicate, whose failure is loud (the bound elapses\n      and the message says what it was waiting for). This one's failure is the opposite: **if 50 ms is too\n      short on a loaded machine, the thing it waits to *not* happen has not happened yet, and\n      `assert!(extra.is_none())` passes vacuously.** The test then reports green while asserting nothing.\n      ⚠️ **This is not hypothetical for this repo.** M1.4 Pass C2 shipped a ~1.6 % flake with *every* gate\n      green, and the second flake it introduced pointed the **other** way — a bound spent by the *observer*\n      rather than consumed by the *observed*, failing 8/160 at eight concurrent workers with 1- and 2-worker\n      runs clean. A 50 ms absence window is the same coupling: its adequacy depends on host load, and the\n      CONTENTION gate is exactly the run that would expose it — but a *vacuous pass* is invisible to a gate\n      that counts failures, because there is no failure to count.\n      **Why Pass E did not fix it:** the pass was scoped to retiring yield loops, and this one is a genuine\n      fourth shape rather than a conversion (\"absence is not a predicate\", as the code comment says). The\n      primary mutation-probed the neighbouring dedupe predicate and recorded `SETTLE` = 50 ms as\n      *sufficient* — but \"sufficient on this host at this load\" is not a property, it is an observation, and\n      doc 13 §13.8 now records that this crate's own yield costs moved **33×** between milestones on one\n      machine with no load change.\n      **Verify:** either (a) the window is made *failable* — assert something that must become true within it,\n      so too short a window fails loudly instead of passing vacuously — or (b) its adequacy is measured across\n      the CONTENTION gate's load range and recorded beside `SETTLE` with the load stated, per §13.8's rule\n      that any bounded wait says **what it gates on**. Whichever is taken, a mutation must prove it: shortening\n      `SETTLE` toward zero has to *fail*, and if it does not, the assertion is vacuous today.\n      Owner: **whoever next touches `replay_tests.rs`** — which is **U137's split** (`replay_cancel_tests.rs`),\n      now the precondition for the next test added there anyway, since the file is **7 lines from the\n      1 000-line cap**. No milestone gate, so **P3**; the test still asserts a real property, it just cannot\n      currently fail for the reason it exists to catch.\n\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U155","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§6's `ExchangeRecord.response` is `RawJson` — one validated JSON document — and a v1 response body is SSE text, which is not one","body":"**U155 · §6's `ExchangeRecord.response` is `RawJson` — one validated JSON document — and a v1 response\n      body is SSE text, which is not one.** Found by Pass D while building `exchange.rs` (§13.6's emission\n      row): the field's type and the clients' own wire disagree, and no prior ruling covers it — §13.6's four\n      obligations bind *how* bodies are recorded (byte-exact, redacted) but never faced the container\n      question, and obligation 1 forbids inventing fields. **The disclosed interim, landed rather than\n      blocked** (the pass could not defer its own deliverable, and every arm below is a *reader* decision):\n      a body that **is** one JSON document is spliced verbatim (byte-identical to the wire — the strongest\n      form of obligation 2, and the case §13.4's caching-comparison argument is about, which lives on the\n      *request* side); a body that is **UTF-8 text but not JSON** — every SSE stream both v1 clients can\n      produce — is carried as a JSON **string** whose decode is the exact body bytes, a container and not a\n      re-serialisation (§3.6's \"bytes base64\" is the house precedent for container-encoding bytes a JSON\n      field cannot hold; the string form keeps the diagnostic copy human-readable, which is its whole\n      purpose); a body that is **neither** is refused with an `Exchange` error rather than lossily\n      converted — unreachable through both v1 clients (their frames are JSON inside ASCII SSE framing), and\n      loud rather than silent if a future provider breaks that. `exchange.rs`'s module header and doc 13\n      §13.6's Pass D addendum carry the shape; `an_sse_body_travels_in_the_lossless_string_container` and\n      `a_non_utf8_non_json_body_is_refused_rather_than_corrupted` pin both halves.\n      **Which arm:** (i) ratify the container — M5's analyzer decodes the string when the field is not an\n      object, and §6's comment gains one sentence (Ask-First: a normative edit); (ii) amend §6's field type\n      (bytes-with-encoding, or a document-or-text sum) — Ask-First, a protocol-adjacent shape change with a\n      golden-fixture question beside it; (iii) record the response as an assembled JSON array of the\n      frames' `data` documents, each spliced byte-exact — friendlier to the analyzer, but it drops the SSE\n      envelope bytes and so weakens \"the body received\" to \"the payloads received\", which needs the same\n      Ask-First as (ii). **Verify:** M5's analyzer row (doc 25) and M1.9's wiring name the shape they read\n      rather than rediscovering it; if (i), §6's comment edit lands in the analyzer's commit; the interim's\n      two pins above stay until whichever arm lands. Owner: **M5's analyzer row, with M1.9's wiring as the\n      first reader** — whoever touches the field first. It gates no milestone build, so `attention.py`'s\n      derivation shows it P3; the reason it still needs an owner rather than a shrug is that a silent reader\n      assumption is exactly how a diagnostic copy stops being diagnostic.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U156","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§4.5's `Segment.digest: [u8; 32]` names no hash function, and no hashing crate is a `dacode-core` edge — so `Segment` is not constructible and M1.6 cannot start","body":"**U156 · §4.5's `Segment.digest: [u8; 32]` names no hash function, and no hashing crate is a\n      `dacode-core` edge — so `Segment` is not constructible and M1.6 cannot start.** Filed by M1.1e's\n      field-by-field walk over §4.5; [09](part2-engine/09-context-caching.md) §9.5 carries the arms and the\n      measurement. **Measured, not recalled**: the design names no hash anywhere (`rg -n\n      'digest|SHA-256|sha2|blake3' docs/dacode-design.md` returns only §4.5's own field and its prose about\n      fingerprints); `dacode-core`'s nine normal edges are `fd-lock`, `serde`, `zstd`, `tokio`,\n      `tokio-util` plus the four in-workspace crates, and none hashes; the only hash-capable crate in the\n      lock is `ring`, which is class-1 and a `dacode-providers` edge; `dacode-json` exposes none (`rg -l\n      'sha256|blake3' ../../rust/json-tiers/src` is empty); and `dacode-protocol/tests/corpus.rs` only\n      *mentions* a sha256 in two comments (`:100`, `:630`) because it byte-compares — so there is no\n      in-tree implementation to reuse either. **This is U117's and U140's shape a third time**: a normative\n      field whose value has no specified source, found by walking a section rather than by building\n      against it.\n      ⚠️ **The reference this technique came from uses a 32-bit non-crypto hash, which is why the arms\n      differ in what they buy and not only in price.** oh-my-pi's two digests are both\n      `hash = ((hash << 5) - hash + c) | 0` over a `JSON.stringify` of a hand-projected field set\n      (`append-only-context.ts:311-334` per message, `:355-374` for the prefix, returning base36), and\n      their purpose is **change detection, not integrity** — `StablePrefix.build()` returns a bool\n      documented as \"true if the prefix actually changed (cache miss imminent)\" (`:67-76`). §4.5's 32-byte\n      field is a step *beyond* the reference, and only (a)/(b)/(c) fill it with a construction somebody\n      else has analysed.\n      **Arms** (doc 09 §9.5's table carries the costs and failure modes): **(a) `sha2` 0.10 —\n      recommended**; (b) `sha2` 0.11; (c) `ring::digest`, already in the lock but bringing class-1 C/asm\n      into a second crate against §2.3's pure-Rust default; (d) a wide in-crate fingerprint (four 64-bit\n      lanes with fixed keys, zero dependencies) — **the jitter-xorshift precedent applied to the wrong\n      problem**, since that one bought spread quality where a weak output was harmless and here a\n      false-equal digest silently skips a re-emit, leaving the provider prompting the model with stale\n      bytes; (e) narrow the spec's field to `[u8; 16]` or a `u64`, which matches the reference's actual\n      strength and is a design edit. **Ask-First under every arm**: (a)/(b) add an external crate, (e)\n      edits the spec.\n      **Measured for (a) and (b) in a scratch crate on the pinned 1.98.1 — compiled, not merely resolved**\n      (doc 13 §13.7's precedent, because a recalled package count is the f113/f117 class): each resolves\n      **10** packages; `cfg-if` and `libc` are already in this lock (**143**), so **net +8**; `sha2` 0.10.9\n      builds and `Sha256::finalize()` returns the 32 bytes §4.5 declares; the default feature tree has the\n      optional `asm` feature **off**, and `cargo tree -e build` on the probe returns the probe alone — i.e.\n      **zero build-dependency edges and no `cc`** — so no class-1 row is expected. The landing commit runs\n      `tools/dep-audit.py` anyway, because M1.8's `alloca` is the standing proof that reasoning about this\n      is how a marker arrives invisibly, and §33.7's rule assigns the authoritative in-tree delta to that\n      commit.\n      **Owner: the user (Ask-First), before M1.6's first commit.** **Verify:** the chosen arm's\n      implementation lands in `src/context/digest.rs` carrying the version tag doc 09 §9.4's R-b rules\n      (covering the construction *and* the target encoding, so a provider switch diverges every digest\n      rather than comparing across one); if (a)/(b), the dependency, its measured delta, `dep-audit.py`'s\n      verdict and any §2.3 gate row land in the same commit; if (e), §4.5's field edit lands in the same\n      commit as the code; and doc 09's depth marker flips with the closure, this being one of the two holes\n      holding it.\n      ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-30 at M1.6 Step 0b, arm (a), ruled by the user the same day** — the Verify clause's\n      four parts each discharged and re-measured by the orchestrator rather than taken from the worker's\n      report: (1) `crates/dacode-core/src/context/digest.rs` carries `segment_digest(encoding, projected) ->\n      [u8; 32]` over `Sha256`, with R-b's tag **as a digest input** in two halves (`DIGEST_TAG`, the const\n      naming the construction and the projected-field-set version, and the per-call target-encoding argument),\n      both hashed *before* the payload so a provider switch diverges every digest at once; (2) the dependency\n      and its §2.3 gate row landed in one tree — `sha2` 0.10 in the root manifest and `dacode-core`'s, the\n      lock **143 → 151 (+8, exactly the scratch estimate)**, `gates/boundary-edges.txt` **39 → 40** with the\n      one `dacode-core -> sha2` row, and doc 01 §1.6's verbatim diff empty; (3) `dep-audit.py` exits 0 with 8\n      attributions and `--self-test` 0, `cargo deny check` reports all four sections ok with **no new license\n      row**, and **no gate file needed an unpredicted row**; (4) the marker does **not** flip on this closure\n      alone, because U157's consumer half is still open — see that item.\n      ⚠️ **ONE PRE-FLIGHT CLAIM WAS FALSIFIED BY THE LANDING MEASUREMENT AND THE CONCLUSION SURVIVED**: doc 09\n      §9.9 said `cargo tree -e build` showed \"zero build-dependency edges\". The query was right and the\n      inference was not — `-e build` over a package shows *that package's* build edges and does not traverse\n      its normal dependencies', so the scratch probe's empty result proved nothing about the chain. Measured\n      in-tree, `generic-array` 0.14.7 **does** carry a `build.rs` and `[build-dependencies.version_check]`, so\n      `version_check` 0.9.5 enters the tree. It is a pure-Rust version probe, not `cc`/`cmake`/`bindgen` and\n      not `*-sys`-shaped, so no class-1 marker fires and `dep-audit.py` still exits 0 — **U137's shape: both\n      conclusions survived, the recorded reason did not.** The generalisation is now in §9.9: *a graph query's\n      scope is part of its answer.*","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **CLOSED 2026-09-30 at M1.6 Step 0b, arm (a), ruled by the user the same day** — the Verify clause's","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U157","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`ContextInfo.tokens_max` is `u32` while §6's `ModelInfo.window` is `Option<u32>`, so the context gauge has no way to say \"unknown\" — U140's abstention has no consumer-side representation","body":"**U157 · `ContextInfo.tokens_max` is `u32` while §6's `ModelInfo.window` is `Option<u32>`, so the\n      context gauge has no way to say \"unknown\" — U140's abstention has no consumer-side\n      representation.** Filed by the same walk; [09](part2-engine/09-context-caching.md) §9.7 carries the\n      arms. Both halves ~~are~~ **were** landed code rather than plans: ~~`ContextInfo { tokens_used: u32,\n      tokens_max: u32 }` at `dacode-protocol/src/snapshot.rs:316-321` and the `Event::ContextInfo` variant's\n      two inline `u32` fields at `event.rs:647-655`, both **fixtured at M1.2**~~ ⚠️ **corrected 2026-09-30 at\n      Step 0a, because Pass B's dispatch will be written from this item and a stale citation here is how\n      `window.unwrap_or(0)` comes back**: `tokens_max` is now **`Option<u32>`** in both places\n      (`snapshot.rs:325-332` with §13.5's `skip_serializing_if`, `event.rs:650-657`), `Default` is `None`\n      i.e. *unknown* rather than 0, and the shapes were fixtured at M1.2 so the change was a\n      fixture-regenerating one — measured benign, since `Some(n)` encodes byte-identically to the old `n`\n      under §13.5's omission convention, so **zero golden fixtures and zero corpus bytes moved** and doc 33\n      §33.8's two pinned sha256 values still hold; `window: Option<u32>` with `None` for a model\n      nobody tabled is U140's arm (c), landed at t255 and recorded in doc 13 §13.11, which assigns the\n      **consumer** side to M1.6. So the row that must read `window` cannot render it honestly: **`0` is a\n      number nobody chose** (U140's own category) and reads to every client as \"no room left\", while §4.5's\n      `compact_at` fraction is *inert* when `window` is `None` — a `0` gauge would therefore report a\n      session with an untabled model as full, which is the U129 shape (one value describing several\n      provenances) arriving in a meter rather than in an error message.\n      **Arms:** **(a) `tokens_max: Option<u32>` — recommended**, honest, and the meter renders \"unknown\";\n      (b) keep `0` as a sentinel — no change, but a number nobody chose and indistinguishable from full;\n      (c) do not emit `ContextInfo` when `window` is `None` — no change and honest by absence, but the meter\n      never appears for exactly the models that need watching, and §4.5's gauge is a `Durable` event clients\n      fold rather than a live convenience.\n      ⚠️ **The deadline is the reason this is filed rather than deferred, and it is the `TaskKind` case\n      arriving a second time.** Arm (a) is a **serialized protocol shape change**, so it regenerates a\n      golden fixture and is free *until M1.3 publishes the schema export and `protocol.d.ts`* — after which\n      it is a compatibility event. M1.3 is already ordered after M1.10 for an independent reason (B17's\n      `finding` variant), and M1.6 is before both, so the cheap window is open now and closes at the first\n      publication. **Ask-First** (AGENTS.md's list names changing a serialized protocol shape).\n      **Owner: the user (Ask-First), before M1.6 emits a gauge — and in any case before M1.3 publishes.**\n      **Verify:** if (a), the shape change, its regenerated fixture and the fold's handling of `None` land\n      in one commit whose message says \"compatibility event\" (§14.1's rule), and the meter's rendering of\n      \"unknown\" is named in doc 22/doc 21 rather than left to a client; if (b) or (c), doc 09 §9.7 records\n      the choice and why a `0`/absent gauge is not read as \"full\"; either way M1.6's emit path handles\n      `window: None` without inventing a number, with a test that an untabled model produces the chosen\n      representation, and doc 09's depth marker flips with the closure.\n      ✅ **CLOSED at M1.6 Pass B (t276, 2026-10-01) — the consumer half Step 0a could not close.** The\n      Verify clause, part by part: (1) the shape change landed at Step 0a inside the cheap window with zero\n      golden-fixture and zero corpus bytes moved (recorded above); (2) **M1.6's emit path handles\n      `window: None` without inventing a number** — `gauge.rs`'s `context_info(tokens_used, window)` passes\n      the window **straight through** to `tokens_max`, pinned by\n      `an_untabled_models_window_passes_through_as_none_and_never_as_zero` and by the producer→fold→meter\n      chain test `the_gauge_event_folds_to_a_snapshot_whose_meter_says_unknown`, and the\n      `Some(window.unwrap_or(0))` mutant — the exact defect this item exists to prevent — dies to both\n      (FG6 in `gauge_tests.rs`'s measured kill list); (3) **the meter's rendering of \"unknown\" is named in\n      doc 22/doc 21 rather than left to a client** — **R-i** (flagged to the user as reversible):\n      **`used/?`** with the percentage bar suppressed (e.g. `ctx 18.4k/?`), the omit-the-denominator\n      alternative (`ctx 18.4k`) recorded beside it so an overrule is a one-line edit, and `0`/a full bar\n      not an arm; both client docs **keep** their `migrated summary` markers, one clause being no\n      deepening; (4) **doc 09's depth marker flipped with the closure** — §4's count fell 23 → 22 in the\n      same change. The estimate beside it took the divisor from a survey of the reference harnesses\n      (three of five converge on 4 per token; the other two carry a real tokenizer, named and **refused**\n      — oh-my-pi's is an N-API binding unlinkable from a pure-Rust binary, open-code-review's is\n      `tiktoken-go` whose own *fallback* is `len(bytes)/4`, and a ported tokenizer would be an Ask-First\n      edge) rather than inventing one: **bytes**, **rounded up**, both biases on the safe side of §4.5's\n      under-run — citations in `gauge.rs`'s module header, R-j (no gauge field on `Context`) in doc\n      09 §9.12.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **CLOSED at M1.6 Pass B (t276, 2026-10-01) — the consumer half Step 0a could not close.** The","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U158","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`replay.rs`'s dead-code exemption claims the registry's remover, and its own comment two sentences earlier names a different one","body":"**U158 · `replay.rs`'s dead-code exemption claims the registry's remover, and its own comment two\n      sentences earlier names a different one.** Filed by M1.6 Pass A's review round 3 (2026-10-01),\n      which is also the round that read the same comment at round 2 and did not flag it — so this is a\n      defect that survived two readings by the reviewer who found it, and the reason it is filed rather\n      than fixed in place is that **`replay.rs` is not a file M1.6 owns**.\n      `crates/dacode-core/src/replay.rs:67-68` reads \"Same shape and same remover as `crate::registry`'s\n      — debt d6\". The registry's remover is **M1.9** (`registry.rs:75`: \"M1.9 removes this attribute when\n      the first production caller lands\"), while `replay.rs:55-58` says the **subscriber-facing half** has\n      no caller \"until **M3**'s `dacode-server` maps `Command::Subscribe`\" and adds \"M1.9's loop reaches\n      the actor but never a subscriber queue\"; the crate `AGENTS.md`'s exemption paragraph agrees with M3.\n      So the sentence is falsified **by the file's own text**, not by a measurement anyone had to\n      construct — which is the f96 class in its cheapest form: no gate reads a comment, and the two\n      clauses that contradict each other are eleven lines apart.\n      **Why it is a defect and not a nit:** d6's whole mechanism is that each exemption *names its\n      remover*, so a reader in M1.9 deleting the registry's attribute would read `replay.rs`'s claim as\n      licence to delete this one too — and f160's rule (the diff to review is every contract in the module\n      that was previously unexercised, not the removal) would then be applied to a module whose subscriber\n      half still has no caller at all. The failure is a silent widening of `replay`'s dead surface into a\n      build warning somebody \"fixes\" by widening visibility, which is the exact thing d6 exists to prevent.\n      **Owner: whoever next touches `replay.rs` — i.e. U137's split** (`replay_cancel_tests.rs`), which is\n      already the precondition for the next test added there and is M3's backend row. One clause fixes it:\n      \"same shape; remover **M3**, not registry's M1.9\". Recorded in the crate `AGENTS.md`'s obligations\n      table with the same owner, so the row that inherits `replay.rs` meets it in the map layer as well as\n      here.\n      **Verify:** `replay.rs`'s attribute comment names M3 and no longer claims the registry's\n      remover; the crate `AGENTS.md` row is struck; and `rg -n '^#!\\[cfg_attr\\(not\\(test\\)' crates/dacode-core/src`\n      still returns exactly 4 (the fix is a comment, not an attribute).\n      ✅ **CLOSED AT M1.11 PASS A (2026-10-04) — by a stronger fix than the one this item\n      foresaw.** The facade's first production callers deleted `replay.rs`'s module-level\n      exemption *and its comment block outright* (f160), so the false \"same remover as\n      `crate::registry`'s\" sentence no longer exists; the three `ReplayRing` inspectors the\n      block mis-attributed now carry their own item-level exemptions naming **M3** — the\n      correct remover, which is what the filing wanted stated. The Verify clause's \"exactly 4\"\n      count is overtaken by design: the module-level count is now **2** (`context`, `perm`)\n      because three exemptions were deleted with their first callers, which is f160's designed\n      outcome rather than a violation. The crate `AGENTS.md`'s obligations row is struck. Both\n      reviewers verified the tree side (the primary at round 2, the secondary at round 1); the\n      closure lands in the same commit as the deletion.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **CLOSED AT M1.11 PASS A (2026-10-04) — by a stronger fix than the one this item","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U159","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Nothing in `tools/` validates a `path:line` citation, and three of doc 09's thirteen had gone stale — including the one naming where U139's code lands","body":"**U159 · Nothing in `tools/` validates a `path:line` citation, and three of doc 09's thirteen had\n      gone stale — including the one naming where U139's code lands.** Filed by the Pass B pre-dispatch\n      read (2026-10-01), which corrected all four rather than working around them: `snapshot.rs:316-321`\n      (the `ContextInfo` struct is at `:325-332` since Step 0a added its doc paragraph), `event.rs:647-655`\n      (the variant's two fields are at `:647-654`), and `lib.rs:56-60` for the engine modules'\n      `pub(crate)` declarations (they are at `:69-75`). The fourth was not a citation but a **site claim**,\n      and it is the one that would have cost a worker a round: doc 09 §9.11 and the crate `AGENTS.md` both\n      said **U139's fold at open lands in `registry.rs`'s `seed_cache`**, which the code falsifies —\n      `seed_cache:507` builds a `SessionInfo` for `meta.json`, while the controller is the *actor*'s\n      (`actor.rs:311`, seeded at spawn from `created_by:298` and passed at `registry.rs:353`) and\n      `Session` has no such field by design (doc 07 §7.7). The real site is `open_session`, between\n      `LogWriter::spawn` and `SessionActor::spawn`; both docs now say so.\n      **Why it is a class and not four typos:** `tools/check-links.py` resolves every relative link and\n      in-page anchor (822 links, 562 §-references at this commit) and has **zero** `.rs` handling —\n      `rg -c '\\.rs' tools/check-links.py` exits **1** — so a citation rots whenever the cited file grows\n      above it and nothing reports it. The two files a dispatch is written from carry **81** such\n      citations between them (doc 09: **13**, `crates/dacode-core/AGENTS.md`: **68**, measured with\n      `rg -o` rather than `rg -c` so a line with two counts twice), and the ten remaining internal ones in\n      doc 09 were each re-measured in the same pass: **all ten resolve**. So the rate is 3 stale of 13 in\n      the smaller file, and the *site* claim is a separate and worse shape, because it is a claim about\n      what a named location **can hold** rather than about where a line moved to — and no tool can check\n      that at all. This is f96's class in a new place: the gates check that references *resolve*, never\n      that they *point where they say*.\n      **Owner: whoever next extends `tools/`** — a row of its own, not a milestone's. The honest scope is\n      (a) the cited file exists, (b) the cited line is not past EOF, and (c) where the prose names a\n      declaration, the cited line *is* one (`rg -n '^\\s*(pub(\\(crate\\))? )?(fn|struct|enum|mod|const|static)\\b'`\n      over the cited range). (c) catches all three of this pass's stale citations and none of the site\n      claim, so the site claim needs a **procedure** rather than a tool: a dispatch's pre-flight\n      re-measures every site its instruction names, which is what found this one.\n      **Verify:** either a citation check exists in `tools/`, is rowed in\n      [Appendix G](appendices/G-authoring-tools.md)'s register, exits non-zero on a deliberately moved\n      line, and both doc 09 and the crate `AGENTS.md` pass it; **or** the item is closed recording that\n      (a)–(c) are not worth a tool, with the manual sweep written into `.agents/roles/_search.md` as a\n      named pre-dispatch procedure so it is not rediscovered by the next pass.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U160","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"The fold at open accumulates an O(log-size) `Snapshot` transient at every resume, and R-h's chunking bounds the read, not this","body":"**U160 · The fold at open accumulates an O(log-size) `Snapshot` transient at every resume, and R-h's\n      chunking bounds the read, not this.** Filed by the primary review (2026-10-01), which mutation-proved\n      the fold's chunking (FM13) and then measured what the chunking does *not* reach: `registry.rs`'s\n      `folded_controller` reads in `RANGE_CHUNK_MAX` chunks — bounding each read's `Vec<Envelope>` — but\n      folds them all into one `Snapshot`, whose `turns`/`blocks` retain the log's owned text\n      (`snapshot.rs`'s fold targets: `Text(String)`, `Thinking(String)`, user content), so the transient is\n      **O(log size) in allocation at every open** and grows with the session. **This is a consequence of\n      R-h's ruling rather than of the implementation**: the ruled full forward fold through\n      `dacode_protocol::fold` (one fold implementation, design principle 2) is O(n) in time *and* O(n) in\n      transient allocation, where the backward-scan-from-tail alternative R-h deferred would have been\n      O(k) in both, for k = the number of `ControlChanged` records — which strengthens the case for that\n      deferral having an owner rather than dissolving it. **Not U132**: that item is scoped to\n      `read_to_tail`'s `Vec<Envelope>` and the `EventStore` trait's streaming shape, and its owner (M3's\n      `SqliteStore`) does not reach a `dacode-protocol` fold target. **Owner: M3's doc 20 (the scaling\n      row)** — the same row that owns the backward-scan mechanism, since the candidate answers are that\n      mechanism, a reduced-accumulation fold for cache-seeding reads (which must not become a second fold\n      implementation without answering principle 2), or a recorded accepted cost with a measured bound.\n      Exposure today is nil in practice: no client resumes sessions, and the longest log this row's tests\n      build is 520 records. **Verify:** doc 20 either lands the backward scan or a bounded seed fold and\n      closes this citing it, or records the O(n) transient as accepted with the measurement that made it\n      acceptable; this item closes citing whichever.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U161","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"Two landed `actor_tests.rs` tests observe a `ControlChanged` event and then read the controller, which is one `.await` away from failing — because `apply`'s publish *precedes* its control write","body":"**U161 · Two landed `actor_tests.rs` tests observe a `ControlChanged` event and then read the\n      controller, which is one `.await` away from failing — because `apply`'s publish *precedes* its\n      control write.** Filed from M1.6 Pass B's review round 3 (2026-10-01), which **confirmed it by\n      running rather than by reading**: inserting one `yield_now().await` in `actor.rs`'s `apply` between\n      the publish and the control write makes\n      `actor::tests::take_control_is_allowed_for_any_client_and_moves_control_durably` and\n      `actor::tests::release_control_is_the_controllers_alone` fail, and nothing else in the crate does\n      (**267 passed / 2 failed**, against a 269/0/4 baseline). The mechanism is that `apply` publishes\n      *inside* `emit` and writes `ctx.control` only after `emit` returns, so a subscriber that has already\n      received the envelope can still read the **previous** controller.\n      **Why it is filed and not fixed here: it is not a live flake.** Both tests are plain `#[tokio::test]`\n      (zero `flavor` attributes in `actor_tests.rs`) and `apply` has no `.await` between the two today, so\n      the ordering holds — but it holds by the *absence of a line* rather than by anything stated or\n      enforced, and the next `.await` added there silently breaks two tests in a different file. That is\n      P-LIVE-016's flake shape arriving as a review obligation, and it gets U137/U154/d16's disposition:\n      `actor_tests.rs` is not a file M1.6 owns, so the pass stated the invariant on `apply`'s own doc and\n      filed this rather than editing a neighbour.\n      ⚠️ **The same probe found the fence this pass *does* own, and round 2 fixed it.** Round 1's\n      `registry_tests.rs` `take_control` claimed a fence that did not exist (the primary's MED-2,\n      falsified by this very `yield_now`); it now ends with a second, no-op `TakeControl` whose `Ack` is\n      the fence, sound because the loop awaits `apply` inline before its next `recv`, so command *k+1*'s\n      `Ack` cannot precede command *k*'s write. Under the probe, `registry::tests` passes **15/15** while\n      the two `actor_tests.rs` tests still fail — which is what makes the one a fence and the other not.\n      **Owner: whoever next touches `actor.rs`'s `apply` or `actor_tests.rs`.** **Verify:** either both\n      tests fence on a later command's `Ack` the way `registry_tests.rs`'s `take_control` now does, or\n      `apply`'s ordering is made *structural* rather than incidental — writing `ctx.control` before the\n      publish inverts what an observer can see and needs its own argument, since `apply` writes control\n      only on `emit`'s `Ok` arm today; either way the `yield_now` probe between the publish and the write\n      leaves the whole crate green, and this item closes citing that run.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U162","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`Event::PermissionResolved` cannot carry §4.3's \"which of the two layers refused\", and the requirement is normative","body":"**U162 · `Event::PermissionResolved` cannot carry §4.3's \"which of the two layers refused\", and the\n      requirement is normative.**\n      ✅ **RULED BY THE USER 2026-10-01 — ARM (a) — AND LANDED AT M1.7 STEP 0, refined to `Option` by the\n      pass that built it.** `PermDecision::Deny { reason: Option<DenyReason> }` in\n      `crates/dacode-protocol/src/perm.rs`, five arms (`Rule`, `Mode`, `Timeout`, `NetHost`, `NetAddress`),\n      spec'd at §3.2 and §3.3 via **A28**, exported from `lib.rs`. **The refinement is the part worth\n      keeping**: the ruled non-optional field would have put a *diagnostic* on the **command** path, so\n      every client answering `ResolvePermission` with a denial would name an engine taxonomy whose only\n      correct value there is a constant — `None` **is** the answerer case, corroborated by\n      `PermissionResolved.by`, and no sixth `Client` arm is needed. **The register's FIRST golden diff**:\n      `{\"type\":\"Deny\"}` → `{\"type\":\"Deny\",\"data\":{}}`, because adjacent tagging emits `data` even when\n      every field inside it is omitted, so the pre-change bytes **no longer decode** (`missing field\n      data`); one inline assertion moved, one was added for `Some(NetAddress)` and mutation-proved\n      load-bearing, and **no golden file moved** because the 82-file corpus never carried a `Deny`.\n      `dacode-protocol` 123 → **125**, workspace 642 → **644**, `event.rs` **untouched at 998** — the\n      variant lives in `perm.rs` (188 lines), so doc 16 §16.13's cap warning was aimed at the wrong file\n      and is corrected there. `Cargo.lock` and `gates/` unmoved.\n      ⚠️ **ONE OF THE THREE REASONS GIVEN FOR ARM (a) WAS MEASURED FALSE, and it was the recommendation's\n      own stated ground**: that \"a `Snapshot` that folds decisions without events cannot see it\".\n      `fold.rs` handles `PermissionResolved` as `answered(snapshot, req)` — it **discards** the decision —\n      and `Snapshot` retains `perm_mode` and `pending` but no permission decisions at all, so arm (b) would\n      have satisfied §4.3 fully. The arm survives on the two reasons that are true (`dacode-core`'s\n      `PermissionResolution` carries a decision to its waiter without the event; a field on\n      `PermissionResolved` would have spent lines `event.rs` does not have). That is the board's recurring\n      class — *a deferral's reason is what the next pass acts on* — arriving in a recommendation one turn\n      old, and it is recorded rather than quietly dropped.\n      ⚠️ **THE VERIFY CLAUSE IS NOT FULLY MET, WHICH IS WHY DOC 16'S MARKER IS RETAINED.** Two parts\n      remain and both are M1.7's: §14.1's Providers-tier test #5 must gain an assertion that the two\n      layers are **distinguishable**, which cannot live in `dacode-providers` (no protocol edge) and lands\n      where `ProviderError::EgressRefused { host, addr }` maps to `NetAddress`; and the `None`/`by` pairing\n      must be **enforced** where `decide_resolve` already forwards a client's decision verbatim. Also\n      measured: swapping the `NetHost`/`NetAddress` **doc comments** survives 125 green tests (mutant B2),\n      so nothing detects an inverted description of §4.3's two layers — **review is its only control**, a\n      wording-pinning test is declined, and the risk decays at M1.7 once producers exist (**f313**, doc 16\n      §16.16). **Still open as U166**: an automatic decision has no `PermissionRequested`, so the `ReqId`\n      on its `PermissionResolved` names nothing — which `NetAddress` makes unavoidable, since the IP layer\n      refuses at *connect* time after `net` already allowed automatically. Design §4.3 states it twice over: *\"the resolved address is checked, not\n      just the hostname the model supplied\"* and *\"**Which of the two layers refused is recorded on\n      `PermissionResolved`**, so 'the host is not allow-listed' and 'the host resolved to a private range'\n      are distinguishable in the audit trail **and in the prompt shown to the user**\"*\n      (`dacode-design.md:1482-1488`). The landed event has exactly **three** fields — `req`, `decision`,\n      `by` (`crates/dacode-protocol/src/event.rs:535-544`) — and `PermDecision::Deny` carries **no\n      reason**, so the distinction has nowhere to live: two refusals that §4.3 requires a user to be able\n      to tell apart are the same envelope. **How it stayed hidden:** [13](part3-capabilities/13-providers.md)\n      §13.5 split the SSRF test deliberately and correctly — *\"M1.5 owns the enforcement point and the\n      test; M1.7 owns the predicate\"* — and then deferred the *recording* with \"which is M1.7's wiring, and\n      until then the test asserts the refusal itself\". Both halves were right and neither looked at the\n      event, so the deferral moved the gap rather than closing it. This is the **f89 / U70→A2\n      inbound-assignment class**: an obligation assigned to a future row that the future row's own doc\n      would have to re-derive, and it was re-derived only because M1.1f walked §4.4 field by field.\n      ⚠️ **It is the same class as U157** — a normative requirement a landed shape cannot express — and it\n      carries U157's deadline: **cheap only until M1.3 publishes** the schema export and `protocol.d.ts`,\n      after which it is a compatibility event rather than a shape choice. M1.3 sits *last* in §5's build\n      order by design (§1's ordering constraint on B17's `finding`), so now is the cheap moment and M1.7's\n      Step 0 is the last one before the row that needs it. **A3's typed `AgentIdle.guard` is the precedent\n      for the fix**, and its reasoning transfers exactly: an untyped reason string was rejected there\n      because principle 3 rules out a rendering a fold cannot match, and \"the host resolved to a private\n      range\" is a fact a client branches on (it changes the remedy shown), not prose. **Which arm:**\n      (a) `PermDecision::Deny` gains a typed reason — `Deny { reason: DenyReason }`, so the reason travels\n      with the decision wherever it is folded, at the cost of touching every `Deny` construction site and\n      its fixture; (b) `PermissionResolved` gains an `Option<DenyReason>` field beside `by`, which is\n      additive and omits when `None` per §13.5's convention, but puts the reason on the *event* rather than\n      on the *decision*, so a `Snapshot` that folds decisions without events cannot see it; (c) a separate\n      `EgressRefused` event, which makes the two layers structurally distinct at the cost of a fourth\n      durable variant and a fold branch. **Recommendation: (a)** — the reason is a property of the denial\n      and not of its delivery, §3.2's `PermDecision` already carries a payload variant\n      (`AllowPattern(String)`), so this is not the enum's first, and (b)'s `Snapshot` blindness is exactly\n      the \"a fold cannot match it\" failure A3 ruled out. **Verify:** the arm is ruled **before M1.7's first\n      commit** and before `event.rs` is touched — ⚠️ which is **998 of the ungated 1 000-line cap**, the\n      closest file in the workspace, so a reason-carrying `Deny` does not fit beside it and the row plans a\n      split or moves the prose to `snapshot.rs` (the decision M1.6 Pass B had to make and did); the golden\n      fixture changes **in the same commit** and is called out as the compatibility event it is; §14.1's\n      Providers-tier test #5 gains an assertion that the two layers are *distinguishable*, not only that\n      the private-range case is refused, since a test that only asserts the refusal passes on any of the\n      three arms and on none of them being implemented. Owner: **the user** for the arm, consumed by\n      **M1.7** — **M1 scope**, and the deadline is M1.3's publication rather than M1.7's commit.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **RULED BY THE USER 2026-10-01 — ARM (a) — AND LANDED AT M1.7 STEP 0, refined to `Option` by the","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U163","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"What a session grant does to the *other* pending permission requests is unspecified, and two harnesses do opposite things","body":"**U163 · What a session grant does to the *other* pending permission requests is unspecified, and\n      two harnesses do opposite things.** §4.4 defines the decision and its audit; §7.3 and doc 07 define\n      first-answer-wins delivery for *one* request; nothing says what an `AllowSession` or `AllowPattern`\n      means for the requests already waiting. Measured in the checkouts: **opencode auto-resolves every\n      pending request its new saved rules now cover** (`packages/core/src/permission.ts:250-259`) and\n      **cascades a reject to all pendings in the session** (`:237-245`) — two cross-request rules, both\n      load-bearing for a UI where several prompts are open at once. **Nil exposure at M1.7**, and the\n      reason is structural rather than incidental: nothing emits a `PermissionRequested` until §4.2's loop\n      exists, so at this row the pending set can only ever hold one entry, created by a test. From **M1.9**\n      the loop dispatches calls concurrently under §4.3's `Concurrency` policies, so several `ask`\n      decisions can be open at once and the question becomes observable — which makes this U139's shape\n      (a consequence recorded at the row that creates it, with the time box named) rather than a note.\n      ⚠️ **Doc 16 §16.2 states M1.7's behaviour explicitly so the row does not pick one silently**: each\n      pending is answered on its own, a new grant does not retro-resolve an older request, and a `Deny`\n      does not cascade. That is the *conservative* reading and the one that needs no new surface, but it\n      has a cost worth naming when the arm is chosen: a user who answers \"allow all `cargo *` for this\n      session\" while three `cargo` prompts are open still has to answer the other two. **Which arm:**\n      (a) as doc 16 states it — independent requests, no retro-resolution; (b) opencode's\n      auto-resolve-covered-pendings, which needs the new grant re-run against each waiting scope and a\n      rule for which `by` the synthesized `PermissionResolved` carries (the granting client, or `None` as\n      an engine resolution — the **event field's own** wording for the timeout case,\n      `event.rs:539-540`; ⚠️ this entry first cited §3.2 for it, which is the same misattribution the\n      primary review found in `perm.rs` at M1.7 Step 0, and §3.2's only `by` occurrences are the bare\n      uncommented declarations at `dacode-design.md:361,363`); (c) auto-resolve for `allow` and\n      independent answers for `deny`, on the argument that a denial is a decision about *one* call while a\n      grant is a decision about a class. **Verify:** M1.9 names the arm in doc 08 before the loop\n      dispatches concurrently, and a test pins it by opening two `ask` pendings and granting a pattern that\n      covers both — asserting either that the second resolves with the named `by`, or that it stays open,\n      whichever arm is taken; this item closes citing that test. Owner: **M1.9** — **M1 scope**.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U164","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§4.4's `deny_ip_ranges` vocabulary reads as exhaustive and is not, and one of the two omissions cannot be std-derived on the pinned toolchain","body":"**U164 · §4.4's `deny_ip_ranges` vocabulary reads as exhaustive and is not, and one of the two\n      omissions cannot be std-derived on the pinned toolchain.** §4.4 names four sets —\n      `[\"loopback\", \"private\", \"link-local\", \"metadata\"]` — \"or CIDR\". Measured while establishing that the\n      IP layer needs **zero dependencies** (doc 16 §16.8): `Ipv4Addr::is_shared` (CGNAT, `100.64.0.0/10`)\n      and `is_benchmarking` are **unstable** on `rustc 1.98.1` (`error[E0658]`, issue #27709), while\n      everything the four named sets need is stable — including `Ipv6Addr::is_unique_local`,\n      `is_unicast_link_local` and `to_ipv4_mapped`. So a resolved address in CGNAT space is **not\n      classifiable by any std name**, and `0.0.0.0/8` is omitted from the list as well. Both are reachable\n      by the exact case §4.4 exists to stop: an allow-listed hostname whose DNS answer an attacker\n      controls. **Two smaller findings from the same measurement, recorded rather than gated:**\n      `169.254.169.254` is `is_link_local()` and is **not** `is_private()`, so the four sets are not nested\n      the way a reader assumes and `link-local` is load-bearing rather than redundant beside `metadata`;\n      and `::ffff:127.0.0.1` is **not** `Ipv6Addr::is_loopback()` without `to_ipv4_mapped()` first, which\n      is a loopback bypass for any implementation that calls only the v6 predicates — the reason\n      openclaw's `net-policy` handles mapped and embedded forms explicitly\n      (`packages/net-policy/src/ip.ts:190`, whose own comment reads *\"including IPv4-mapped IPv6\"*). Both\n      are pinned by tests in doc 16 §16.8's trap list, so neither is a hole in the *code* — only the\n      vocabulary is. **Which arm:** (a) add `cgnat` and `this-host` to the named-set vocabulary — spec\n      text, so **Ask-First**, and it makes the list exhaustive against the ranges that matter; (b) leave\n      the vocabulary as written and require operators to spell `100.64.0.0/10` as CIDR, which works\n      (R-f hand-rolls a CIDR parser) but means the default configuration has a hole the named sets appear\n      to close; (c) treat the four names as a *floor* the engine always applies regardless of config, so\n      no spelling can remove loopback/link-local/private — the shape openclaw's approval-file floors take.\n      **Recommendation: (a) plus (c)** — (a) because a name an operator cannot write is a range an operator\n      cannot deny, and (c) because a *deny* list whose defaults are removable by omission is the same\n      inversion as §16.4's `.env`. **Verify:** folded into **g129**'s spec edit if the user wants one; if\n      not, doc 16 §16.8 names the two omissions as known and M1.7's `[net_policy]` tests pin whatever the\n      shipped default actually covers, so the gap is measured rather than assumed. Owner: **the user** for\n      the vocabulary, consumed by **M1.7** — **M1 scope**, low severity, and unlike U162 it does not\n      become more expensive later because no wire shape depends on it.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U165","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`tools/agents-index.py --check` has no fixpoint, so its exit code carries no information — and AGENTS.md's diagnosis of the red was wrong in the direction that hides the case firing most often","body":"**U165 · `tools/agents-index.py --check` has no fixpoint, so its exit code carries no information —\n      and AGENTS.md's diagnosis of the red was wrong in the direction that hides the case firing most\n      often.** Measured at M1.1f (2026-10-01, tracker f311) on a commit that touched **no** `AGENTS.md`:\n      the check exited 1, and regenerating produced a diff in **two** columns — the root doc's `lag`\n      **4 → 8** with its `rev` unchanged at `3436e81`, and `crates/dacode-core`'s `rev` `55bdc04 →\n      915e0d7` with its hash (`dae26a92`) and line count (787L) both unchanged. The mechanism is in the\n      tool: `lag = git rev-list --count <doc_rev>..HEAD -- <covered-dir> :!<doc>`\n      (`tools/agents-index.py:120`), and the **root** `AGENTS.md`'s covered directory is the repository\n      root, so *every* commit increments it. Regenerating and committing the index then moves HEAD, which\n      increments it again — **there is no clean tree at which `--check` exits 0**. AGENTS.md said the red\n      comes \"on the `rev` column alone\", which is false for the root row, and is why a reader following\n      that instruction sees a delta in a column the instruction says cannot move and then has to decide\n      whether the index is stale or the doc is. Corrected in the same change as this item.\n      **Why it is more than cosmetic:** the check exists to catch exactly one failure — a hash that was\n      never regenerated, which is what shipped at `a8bbd53` — and a gate that is always red is a gate whose\n      signal has to be recovered by a manual procedure (regenerate, diff, read the *hash* column). Three\n      consecutive sessions have now run that procedure and reverted the file, which is U44's shape, but a\n      procedure someone has to remember is the second-class gate the 2026-09-30 correction was made to\n      avoid — and this pass is the second time the rememberable half was a *sentence* rather than a\n      command. **Which arm:** (a) drop the derived, always-moving columns from `--check`'s comparison —\n      compare **hash, line count and `rev`** only and render `lag` as informational — which makes exit 0\n      reachable and leaves the check catching precisely the failure it exists for; (b) special-case the\n      root doc, whose covered directory is the repo root by construction, so its `lag` measures \"commits\n      since the last agent-instruction edit\" rather than the \"doc trailing its code\" the column claims;\n      (c) leave the tool alone and promote the regenerate-and-diff procedure into `tools/` as a wrapper\n      that prints the diagnosis, so the procedure is a command rather than a paragraph.\n      **Recommendation: (a), with (b)'s observation recorded beside it** — (a) is a small change that\n      restores a signal, and (b) alone would leave `rev` red after any doc-touching commit, which is U44's\n      benign case and still not exit 0.\n      ⚠️ **Arm (a) as first written is insufficient, and running the procedure is what showed it.** (a) said\n      to compare \"hash, line count **and `rev`**\" — but `rev` **cannot** record the commit that edits the\n      doc, because the index is generated *before* the commit that carries the edit. Measured at M1.7 Step 0\n      on a tree where `AGENTS.md` had just been committed at `cc3a594`: the committed index said `rev`\n      `3436e81` with hash `c57df08f` and 336L, regeneration produced hash `c57df08f` and 336L **unchanged**\n      and `rev` `cc3a594` — correct, and uncommittable without moving HEAD again. So `rev` is stale by\n      *construction* for exactly the doc the check exists to protect, and (a) would still exit 1 after any\n      `AGENTS.md` edit. **Corrected arm (a): compare hash and line count only** — the two columns that\n      describe *content* — and render both `rev` and `lag` as informational, since both are derived from\n      HEAD and neither can be current at a clean tree. ⚠️ Any arm changes a **gate's semantics**, so it is a decision\n      rather than a fix-in-passing, and whichever is taken must land with\n      [Appendix G](appendices/G-authoring-tools.md)'s row updated, that register being where §2.4 says a\n      tool's contract lives. **Verify:** `agents-index.py --check` exits **0** at a clean tree with no\n      `AGENTS.md` edit pending, exits **1** when a hash is stale — proved by editing a doc without\n      regenerating, then reverting — and Appendix G's row names what the check compares; this item closes\n      citing that pair of runs. Owner: **the user** for the arm — **no milestone impact**, and unlike U162\n      it does not become more expensive later.\n      ⚠️ **A sibling misrendering measured in the same pass, and it is `attention.py` rather than\n      `agents-index.py` — recorded here because both are one defect: a gate whose output is *derived* where\n      it should be *declared*.** This item renders **P0 \"gates M1\"** and it gates no milestone. The cause is\n      `_MILESTONE_GATING`'s second alternative (`tools/attention.py:143`), whose preposition list includes\n      `at`, so the provenance phrase *\"Measured at M1.1f\"* matches as a gating construction — the `\\b` after\n      `M1` holds because `.` is a non-word character, so a **sub-step label is indistinguishable from a\n      milestone**. ⚠️ **The naive fix is wrong, and that was measured rather than assumed**: excluding every\n      `M(\\d)\\.\\d` would suppress **58** matches in §6, most of which *are* genuinely gating — \"owed before\n      M1.9 wires a provider\" is U148's, and U148 is correctly P0 — so the heuristic's own stated preference\n      (\"under-triggering is the safe failure\") argues against it. The precise gap is narrower: **§6\n      milestones are detected from prose while §6 *actions* are declared**, and the `attention-meta` block\n      at §6's head already exists for exactly this purpose, its own text reading *\"Declared rather than\n      detected … applied to the third and last scraped field\"* — a claim this item falsifies, since the\n      milestone is a fourth scraped field and is still detected (deltas' milestones *are* declarable, at\n      `attention.py:342`). **So arm (d): add a `milestone:` line to that block**, mirroring `action:` and the\n      Appendix B `confidence-lead:` precedent, with a hygiene check that every declared id exists and every\n      value is in `MILESTONE_ORDER`. **Recommendation: (a) plus (d)** — (a) restores a signal to one gate,\n      (d) removes a class of false P0 from the other, and neither is the regex surgery the 58-match\n      measurement rules out.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U166","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"§4.4 logs *every* decision on `PermissionResolved` \"including automatic ones\", but an automatic decision has no `PermissionRequested` — so its `ReqId` names nothing","body":"**U166 · §4.4 logs *every* decision on `PermissionResolved` \"including automatic ones\", but an\n      automatic decision has no `PermissionRequested` — so its `ReqId` names nothing.** Found at M1.7 Step 0\n      (2026-10-01) while landing U162, and **U162's own shape makes it unavoidable rather than\n      theoretical.** Three pieces of normative text do not compose: §4.4's *\"Every decision is logged\n      (`PermissionResolved`), including automatic ones, so the audit trail is complete\"*; §4.3's *\"a\n      refusal is recorded on `PermissionResolved` like any other denial\"*; and §3.2's definition of the id\n      those events carry — *\"a `ReqId` names a request the **engine made of the user**\"*\n      (`dacode-design.md:279-281`, with `:233` glossing it \"question / permission request\"). For an\n      automatic decision the engine made no request of the user, so the resolution carries an id whose\n      referent was never emitted, and a client that folds the log sees a `PermissionResolved` for a\n      `PermissionRequested` that does not exist. **Why U162 forces it:** `DenyReason::NetAddress` is\n      produced by the IP layer at **connect** time, *after* the `net` decision already resolved\n      automatically as allow — so the denial that most needs recording is precisely one with no request\n      behind it. Nothing in the tree can hit this yet (`decide_resolve` only serves a client answer, which\n      always has a request), which is why it is filed rather than fixed. **Which arm:** (a) the engine\n      mints a `ReqId` for every decision and emits only the resolution, making `ReqId` a **correlation\n      handle** rather than the name of an emitted request — needs one spec sentence amending §3.2's\n      wording, and nothing else; (b) automatic decisions emit **both** events, so every id names a request\n      and the audit trail is uniform — but a `PermissionRequested` nobody was asked is a prompt a UI must\n      know not to render, and it doubles the log volume on the *common* path, since most decisions in an\n      `auto-edit` or `yolo` session are automatic; (c) automatic decisions are logged on a **new** event\n      (`PermissionChecked`), leaving `PermissionResolved` for real answers — which makes §4.4's \"recorded\n      on `PermissionResolved` like any other denial\" false as written, costs a durable variant and a fold\n      branch, and ⚠️ lands on `event.rs` at **998 of the ungated 1 000-line cap** (doc 16 §16.13's\n      corrected gate 2). **Recommendation: (a)**, because §4.4's requirement is that the *decision* is\n      recorded and (a) is the only arm that records it without paying on the common path — and the wording\n      it amends is a *definition* of an id type, not a property a client depends on. **Verify:** M1.7 names\n      the arm before it emits its first automatic decision; if (a), §3.2's `ReqId` sentence is amended in\n      the same commit as the code (§2.3's same-commit rule, applied to spec text) and a fold test pins that\n      a `PermissionResolved` with no matching `PermissionRequested` is *accepted* rather than treated as a\n      gap; if (b) or (c), the rendering rule and the fixture set move with it. Owner: **M1.7** — **M1\n      scope**, and unlike U162 it does not get more expensive at M1.3 unless the arm is (c).\n      ✅ **RULED arm (a) by the user 2026-10-03, and CLOSED at M1.7 Pass B in the same commit** — each\n      Verify-clause part annotated against a re-measurement: the arm was named *before* Pass B wrote the\n      minting (the ruling preceded the dispatch); §3.2's sentence is amended in this commit (the\n      correlation-handle wording, `dacode-design.md:279-283`); and the fold test is\n      `a_resolution_for_a_request_never_emitted_is_a_no_op` (`tests/fold.rs:602`, dacode-protocol\n      125 → 126). `check()` returns the minted `ReqId` beside the decision\n      (`check_mints_a_fresh_req_id_for_every_decision_u166`), and no `Event` variant was added — arm (c)'s\n      `event.rs` cap hazard never materialised.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"      ✅ **RULED arm (a) by the user 2026-10-03, and CLOSED at M1.7 Pass B in the same commit** — each","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U167","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`Domain::Path` does not fold case, so on a case-insensitive filesystem a `deny` is voided by spelling — `edit.deny = [\"","body":"**U167 · `Domain::Path` does not fold case, so on a case-insensitive filesystem a `deny` is voided\n      by spelling — `edit.deny = [\"**/.env\"]` does not deny `.ENV`, which the filesystem opens as\n      `.env`.** Found at M1.7 Pass A's review (2026-10-02) and **ruled arm (a) by the user the same\n      day** (R-h, doc 16 §16.15): the matcher stays non-folding, the asymmetry is documented at the site\n      (`pattern.rs`'s header), and this item is the filed hole. **Measured, not reasoned:** on this\n      machine's default APFS `python3 -c 'os.path.samefile(\".env\",\".ENV\")'` is `True` and `open(\".ENV\")`\n      reads a file created as `.env` — §16.4's *worse* direction, a voided `deny`. **Why folding is not\n      the fix:** on a case-sensitive filesystem (default Linux) folding converts the voided `deny` into\n      a widened `allow` (`src/*` permitting `SRC/secret`), so there is no single correct answer without\n      knowing which filesystem the paths are on. **And why the matcher cannot ask:** g128's consequence 1\n      makes it a pure predicate over `(Domain, pattern, subject bytes)` with no filesystem access — the\n      sandbox-adoptability constraint the user added — so the case-sensitivity must arrive from the caller\n      that supplies the `Domain`, which at M1.7 does not exist (Pass B's `scope.rs`) and which itself has\n      no filesystem knowledge until config exists. **The fix shape, when doc 17 builds it:** a\n      case-sensitivity flag on `Domain` (or a `PathInsensitive` variant) supplied by the config layer,\n      which is the only place that knows the filesystem the session's `cwd` is on. **Verify:** doc 17\n      cites this item when it designs `[permissions]`'s loading, and the matcher's non-folding test in\n      `pattern_tests.rs` is no longer the last word on the behaviour. Owner: **M2 / doc 17**.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U168","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`redact_url_secrets` is spec-named with no owning row","body":"**U168 · `redact_url_secrets` is spec-named with no owning row.** §4.4's `[net_policy]` block\n      names the key (`redact_url_secrets = true`, `dacode-design.md:1595`) and the behaviour paragraph\n      describes it (`:1655` — strip credentials embedded in a URL before it leaves, because such a URL\n      puts a secret into a provider's access log, a proxy's, and the `PermissionRequested` preview a UI\n      renders; redaction of a *copy about to leave*, which §8.2 permits). **Measured repo-wide at M1.7\n      Pass C's review: nothing outside the spec and one historical TODO note (`:2437`, A5's application\n      record) mentions it — no plan doc assigns the behaviour, no row owns it.** Found by the secondary\n      reviewer's must-not sweep (it verified the pass correctly *didn't* implement it, and noticed\n      nothing would ever assign it). The key is config vocabulary — doc 17's (M2) — and the behaviour\n      belongs to the rows that build the three egress paths §4.4 names: M1.9's loop (provider requests),\n      M1.10's `fetch` (🧠-gated), M4's hooks. **Verify:** doc 17 cites this item when it designs\n      `[net_policy]`'s loading, and each egress-path row cites it when it builds URL handling.\n      Owner: **M2 / doc 17** for the key, consumed by **M1.9/M1.10/M4** for the behaviour.\n","status":"deferred","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":"      M1.10's `fetch` (🧠-gated), M4's hooks. **Verify:** doc 17 cites this item when it designs","references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U169","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"`SessionCreated` and `WriterEpoch` have no producer anywhere, and `CreateSession.sandbox` has no landing place — doc 23 §23.4's `run -p` stream needs all three","body":"**U169 · `SessionCreated` and `WriterEpoch` have no producer anywhere, and `CreateSession.sandbox` has no landing place — doc 23 §23.4's `run -p` stream needs all three.** ✅ **CLOSED at the binary-bridge pass (2026-10-04)** — all three producers landed at the registry's open path (`open_session_with`, gated on `created_by: Some(_)` — the create/resume distinction `OpenSpec` already carried): `WriterEpoch { epoch: folded high-water + 1 }` then `SessionCreated` through the actor's emit path before `insert` (the corpus's own order at seq 1–2; origins `Some(created_by)`/`None` — the convention this pass establishes, pinned; `model` the canonical `provider/model` join, round-trip-pinned through `resolve`), and `CreateSession.sandbox` lands on `Session.sandbox` — §4.1's field taken one row early, a recorded supersession of the crate doc's M2 schedule (B7's inert seam: nothing reads it; an item-level exemption names the §4.7 worktree row as remover). The §23.4 sequence test is green — the integration test asserts the stream envelope-by-envelope, byte-identical to the log's prefix — and the sandbox decision is recorded at `engine.rs`'s `create_session` and both crate AGENTS.md files. Closes citing `crates/dacode/tests/run.rs`'s sequence assertion and `engine_tests.rs`' four producer tests.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"**U169 · `SessionCreated` and `WriterEpoch` have no producer anywhere, and `CreateSession.sandbox` has no landing place — doc 23 §23.4's `run -p` stream needs all three.** ✅ **CLOSED at the binary-bridge pass (2026-10-04)** — all three producers landed at the registry's open path (`open_session_with`, gated on `created_by: Some(_)` — the create/resume distinction `OpenSpec` already carried): `WriterEpoch { epoch: folded high-water + 1 }` then `SessionCreated` through the actor's emit path before `insert` (the corpus's own order at seq 1–2; origins `Some(created_by)`/`None` — the convention this pass establishes, pinned; `model` the canonical `provider/model` join, round-trip-pinned through `resolve`), and `CreateSession.sandbox` lands on `Session.sandbox` — §4.1's field taken one row early, a recorded supersession of the crate doc's M2 schedule (B7's inert seam: nothing reads it; an item-level exemption names the §4.7 worktree row as remover). The §23.4 sequence test is green — the integration test asserts the stream envelope-by-envelope, byte-identical to the log's prefix — and the sandbox decision is recorded at `engine.rs`'s `create_session` and both crate AGENTS.md files. Closes citing `crates/dacode/tests/run.rs`'s sequence assertion and `engine_tests.rs`' four producer tests.","note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U170","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"design §12 names the data root's path but not its resolution, and the binary-bridge pass ruled the resolution without the spec's word","body":"**U170 · design §12 names the data root's path but not its resolution, and the binary-bridge pass ruled the resolution without the spec's word.** `DACODE_HOME` overrides the root outright (doc 23 §23.4's ruling: a harness a test or a script drives must not touch the user's real sessions; the env var is the obvious shape), and the default is `~/.local/share/dacode` hand-resolved from the environment (no `dirs` crate — the measured dependency set adds nothing). **§12 owes a one-line spec note** naming the resolution rule so the next host (M3's server) does not re-invent it; until then the ruling lives in doc 23 §23.4 and `crates/dacode/AGENTS.md`. **Owner: the user, at the next §12 edit** — a spec change is the user's, and this row exists so `attention.py` renders the obligation (the pass's report claimed \"no unsigned decision was created\", which was wrong: this one was). **Verify:** §12 carries the resolution sentence and this closes citing it.\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U171","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E1","kind":"gate","title":"M0.1","body":"deepen [01](part1-foundations/01-workspace-scaffolding.md) and [04](part1-foundations/04-error-model.md), and settle the five scoping questions M0 inherits","status":"closed","verify":"docs only — review-exempt","evidence":"imported: parent milestone M0 complete","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"M0.1","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U172","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E1","kind":"gate","title":"M0.2","body":"workspace skeleton: eleven members, edition/resolver/toolchain pins, the deny-lint set as workspace lints","status":"closed","verify":"dual review","evidence":"imported: parent milestone M0 complete","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"M0.2","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U173","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E1","kind":"gate","title":"M0.3","body":"the gates: `cargo-deny` config, the class-1 `cargo tree` scan and its allowlist, the crate-boundary check","status":"closed","verify":"dual review","evidence":"imported: parent milestone M0 complete","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"M0.3","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U174","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E1","kind":"gate","title":"M0.4","body":"`xtask` as a member that ships nothing, with one real subcommand (`check-lints`) and its golden test. ~~plus `tracing` scaffolding~~ — dropped, doc 01 §1.10: a subscriber with no events to emit is untestable","status":"closed","verify":"dual review","evidence":"imported: parent milestone M0 complete","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"M0.4","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U175","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E2","kind":"gate","title":"M1.1","body":"deepen M1's docs — the **eight** no 🧠 session owns: [03](part1-foundations/03-protocol.md) protocol, [07](part2-engine/07-session-registry.md) registry, [09](part2-engine/09-context-caching.md) caching, [13](part3-capabilities/13-providers.md) providers, ~~[16](part3-capabilities/16-permissions.md) permissions~~ *(deepened at M1.1f, 2026-10-01 — **marker retained**: g128/g129/g130 and U162 are open)*, [22](part4-hosts-clients/22-tui.md) TUI, [23](part4-hosts-clients/23-cli.md) CLI, [33](part6-quality/33-benchmarking.md) benchmarking. **Withheld: 08, 14, 32** — those are the 🧠 rows' own deliverables, and deepening them early is precisely what §1's condition (2) forbids. Carries **U100** (promote the seventeen comment-only types under A3's criterion — *\"a surface a client matches on, and a comment cannot be matched\"* — noting that eight of the seventeen carry **no** shape comment at all), **U19**, **U63**'s doc-16 side, and doc 03's marker correction for six deltas' worth of variants. ⚠️ **But NOT all of U100, and this cell said so until 2026-09-19**: four of the seventeen sit on surfaces M1.1 does not own — **`TaskTree` and `TaskId`** are §4.8's task tree, which is **M1.10's** brainstorm deliverable (and `TaskId` needs a §4.3 scan-exclusion clause, A9's note, because it is tool-nameable via `todo`); **`ToolSpec`**'s home is **doc 14**, which this same row withholds; **`CheckpointId`** is checkpoints — **doc 12, M2**. Those four get §1's condition (1): land the approved *shape*, mark brainstorm-owned *content* pending with its owner named, invent nothing. `TaskOp` is the one promotion that is transcription rather than invention, §3.2's prose already enumerating its five values. **Taken just-in-time, one row ahead of the code that needs it**: **M1.1a** = doc 03 (ahead of M1.2), ~~then 07 (M1.4)~~ ✅ **M1.1b = doc 07 (2026-09-21)**, ~~then 33 (M1.8)~~ ✅ **M1.1c = doc 33 (2026-09-24)**, ~~then 13 (M1.5)~~ ✅ **M1.1d = doc 13 (2026-09-24) — deepened, ~~but its marker is DELIBERATELY RETAINED because U140 is open (§4 records why the count did not move)~~ *(marker flipped when U140 landed at t255, 2026-09-25; §4's count then moved 24 → 23 in that same change)***, ~~then 09 (M1.6)~~ ✅ **M1.1e = doc 09 (2026-09-30) — deepened, marker DELIBERATELY RETAINED because U156/U157 are open (§4 records why the count did not move; M1.1d was the first pass to leave it unchanged and this is the second)**, 16 (M1.7), 22 + 23 (M1.11) — ⚠️ **with doc 23 deepened *minus* its exit-code table, which stays pending on U93 and whose marker therefore does not flip** (see U93: a doc with a load-bearing hole is not \"expanded\"), so §4's count can fall by at most seven here and by the eighth when U93 lands. ✅ **M1.1g done 2026-10-04 — [22](part4-hosts-clients/22-tui.md) and [23](part4-hosts-clients/23-cli.md) are deepened and are M1.11's instruction, both markers DELIBERATELY RETAINED (doc 22's held by U136, doc 23's by U93; §4 records why the count did not move — and repaired its own one-commit-old header drift while doing so). Doc 22 rules R-a–R-h (the engine facade, markdown deferred to M1.9, the diff viewer over `Preview` with M1.10 inheriting the format constraint, crossterm's measured five-mode mouse superset, the 1 MiB paste cap, the scrollbox + coalescing, the `SendMessage` handler + the slash dispatch set, the Clients-tier harness) and carries U136's two arms re-measured with a recommendation; doc 23 scopes `run -p` as the live harness whose turn is M1.9's, lands the `IdleReason → ExitCode` mapping as a tested function, and carries U93's pending table as a shape. Both dependency sets measured in scratch crates (ratatui 0.30.2 + crossterm 0.29 = 74 packages; clap 4.6.7 = 21), `cargo deny` green, zero C markers. U19 closed by doc 22 §22.16.** ✅ **M1.1c done 2026-09-24 — [33](part6-quality/33-benchmarking.md) is deepened and its marker flipped (§4: 25 → 24), ~~so five docs remain (09, 13, 16, 22, 23) and the count can fall by at most four more here~~ *(superseded by t255: four remain (09, 16, 22, 23), at most three more here)* *(superseded again by M1.1e: three remain **to deepen** (16, 22, 23), while doc 09 is deepened with its marker held by U156/U157 — so the count can fall by at most three more here, plus a fourth when those two close)* — doc 23's marker still pending on U93 — superseding the M1.1b note's \"six remain / at most five more\" below, which is struck there rather than deleted.** Doc 33 is M1.8's instruction and adds what the spec delegates: the corpus (bless-pattern generator, drift-checked by the ordinary suite), the per-subject reachability table with U116's disposition, the measured criterion set, the baseline/environment convention §14.2's publication rule binds, and the hand-run gate standing in for doc 31 §31.1's absent CI. ✅ **M1.1b done 2026-09-21 — [07](part2-engine/07-session-registry.md) is deepened and its marker flipped (§4: 26 → 25), ~~so six docs remain and the count can fall by at most five more here~~ *(superseded by M1.1c above: five remain, at most four more)*, superseding the M1.1a note's own \"seven / at most six more\" below, which is struck there rather than deleted.** It is M1.4's instruction and adds what the spec omits: **which seven of §4.1's thirteen `Session` fields M1.4 can populate** (`agent`/`model` are interim, `sandbox`/`context`/`perm`/`checkpoints` belong to M2/M1.6/M1.7 — so `Session` *grows* across four rows rather than being filled in at one); **the blocking writer** B21's lock semantics require (one `std` thread per open session owning the `fd-lock` guard for its lifetime, because `try_write` takes `&mut self` and a per-append `spawn_blocking` cannot own a session-long guard); **an `Append`/`Flush` split** where the cursor round trip is what makes §3.6's pre-side-effect fsync *ordered* rather than best-effort; **the fan-out's two numbers as one invariant** (ring 5 000 > queue 256, a `const` assertion and a test rather than a comment); and **subscribe-then-read-then-dedupe** as the ordering that makes replay race-free, with the trap that replay must feed the *same* bounded queue. ⚠️ **It settled a plan-side \"or\" from a spec fact rather than choosing it** (doc 07 §7.7): the summary said controller state lives \"here or in the server session map\", and `ControlChanged` being **`Durable`** (`event.rs:692`) and folding to **`Snapshot.controller`** (`fold.rs:293`, design §3.5) means state in the log cannot live only in a server map — A7's §8.3 sentence (\"the engine sees a caller only as a `ClientId` and a controller status\") is the seam, and identity stays server-side. **Two holes filed: U115** (`AgentConfig`/`ModelRef` have no producer in M1 at all — resolution is `dacode-config`, whose plan doc 17 is **M2**, so M1.4 carries an interim the next row must replace) and **U116** (design §4.1 named `DashMap` in prose; measured at **+11 packages** for a map whose hot path is inside a session, so the user ruled it illustrative and §4.1 gained a precision edit saying the concrete type is an implementation choice). **Doc 07 §7.8 carries M1.4's measured dependency set** — compiled in a scratch crate on the pinned 1.98.1, not resolved, per f115/f117 — and with it the correction that M1.4's gate edits are **three rows across two files plus two Appendix F cells**, not the one `zstd` row the M1.4 cell implied. ✅ **M1.1a done 2026-09-19 — [03](part1-foundations/03-protocol.md) is deepened and its marker flipped (§4: 27 → 26), ~~so seven docs remain and the count can fall by at most six more here~~ *(superseded by M1.1b above: six remain, at most five more)*.** It carried the stale `[DESIGN DELTA]` tier marker (corrected: A1 landed the tier as a *rule*, not as a choice between two mechanisms), B3's owed note that the delta-vs-full question is subsumed by the tier split, A8's JSON-Schema correction restated as settled, and U100's four remaining names with their owners so M1.2 does not invent `ToolSpec`. **What it added that no row predicted is in §3.9: five holes in the protocol surface**, found by running f109's per-field walk over §3 for the first time — **U106** (one, `Bytes`, needs the user before M1.2 can fixture `ContentBlock`) and **U107** (four spec corrections, agent work). It also measured M1.2's dependency set as **three or four crates rather than the two this row's M1.2 cell names** (`serde` is entailed by §13.3 and appears in no list; `getrandom` depends on how U102's ~15 lines are written), and each adds a `gates/boundary-edges.txt` row in the same commit","status":"open","verify":"docs only — review-exempt","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"M1.1","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U176","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E2","kind":"gate","title":"M1.2","body":"`dacode-protocol`: `Event`/`Command`/`Envelope`, A1's tier marker, `fold()`, cursors, **golden fixtures for every variant** (§14.1 derives the set from the enum, so B17's `finding` self-registers when it arrives). M1's first external dependencies land here, so §2.3's same-commit rule fires: `dacode-json` with `raw_value` (**U8**), and `ulid`'s registration — the Appendix F §F.1 class-2 name and the `gates/class1-exempt.txt` row for `js-sys`, both deferred to *this* commit on purpose so the exemption is reviewed beside the crate that needs it. **U102** carries the revisit obligation, and no gate notices it lapsing. ~~⚠️ **[03](part1-foundations/03-protocol.md) §3.8 measures the set as three or four crates, not these two**~~ ✅ **Settled 2026-09-20 (session 20's pre-flight, all four user-approved): the set is FOUR crates — `dacode-json[raw_value]`, `serde[derive]`, `ulid[−std,+serde]`, `getrandom` — and `gates/boundary-edges.txt` goes 18 → 22.** f113's re-measure made `getrandom` forced rather than conditional (`ulid` 3.0.0's copy is a wasm32 dev-dep; the recorded \"non-optional\" claim was falsified and is corrected in U102/Appendix F/doc 03 §3.8), and **U110** records the user's end-state intent that serde derives may later be replaced by a dacode-json-native typed layer, fixture-gated. ⚠️ **One pre-step this cell did not predict (f114, user-approved route (a)): `dacode-json`'s unconditional `cc` build-dep is a class-1 marker `dep-audit.py` attributes to it — ✅ gated 2026-09-20 as json-tiers `33cf040` (`cc` optional behind the existing `cbench` feature, build.rs cfg-gated; verified there and from a consumer probe: the wide scan no longer lists `cc`)** — Appendix F §F.3's non-entry already described it as \"used only by the opt-in `cbench` baselines\", which the gating makes true in the graph and not only in prose. ~~Fixture budget settled at **80** goldens (§3.7: +`Snapshot`, +`LogHeader`).~~ **(U111 moved it to 82; and `ulid[−std,+serde]` above is pre-f117 — the serde feature does not compile under `no_std`, so the shipped config is `−std` alone with the id.rs adapter.)** **U106 is closed (`Vec<u8>`), so `bytes` is not a fifth.** ~~Doc 03 §3.9 also leaves five holes with owners here: **U107**~~ ✅ **U107 closed 2026-09-20 — G2–G6 applied to the spec, so this cell transcribes: `blob_id: String`, one `LogHeader` shape, `Cursor(0)` + the message-only bootstrap, total fold with a §14.1 test per case, serde bounds on `Codec`** ✅ **DONE 2026-09-20 — the crate landed under dual review (secondary APPROVE first pass; primary REQUEST CHANGES → eight fixes + a four-assertion rider → APPROVE), 114 tests green, all six of doc 03 §3.10's gates re-run at delivery. Built in five passes: A foundation types, B macro-table enums (route 1 — a variant with no tier or sample is a syntax error) + tagged-record frames (f119: `Ack`/`Hint`/`LogHeader` tagged, `Envelope`/`UpFrame` plain), C1 fold + the §14.1 suite, C2 the 82-golden corpus (declaration-order canonical, f131; bless-only regeneration; missing file fails) + xtask's tests/ extension (doc 04 §4.1's assignment discharged), then the review rounds. The fold walk mid-pass produced U111/A27 (four destinations, Event 31) and the review produced ~~U113 (encode's reachable fallback — user ruling owed before M1.4)~~ **U113 — ✅ ruled and applied the same day: `encode → Result`, `try_encode` deleted, goldens byte-identical** — and U114 (steering's close-before-inject constraint — M1.9's). First compile of the spec's shapes found f115/f117 (ulid's recorded serde feature does not compile — adapter landed) and f118 (fieldless variants cross the wire as one-key objects — fixtures pin it)**","status":"open","verify":"dual review ✅","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"M1.2","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U177","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E2","kind":"gate","title":"M1.3","body":"the JSON-Schema export generator in `xtask` (§2.4/§13.3 — **not** a `build.rs` in the protocol crate) plus `protocol.d.ts` generated in CI with a drift check that fails the build. ⚠️ **§1's ordering constraint**: publish *neither* before B17's `finding` variant lands, or its arrival becomes a post-hoc wire change instead of part of M1's first — which makes this row depend on M1.10 even though it is numbered before it","status":"open","verify":"dual review","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"M1.3","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U178","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E2","kind":"gate","title":"M1.4","body":"**the store and the session actor.** Event log + `JsonlStore`, **coded against the `dacode-core::store` traits** (`EventStore`/`SessionStore`/`BlobStore`) and not against the file format, per §8.2's scaling axis — `JsonlStore` is the *only* M1 implementation, and the crate is extracted at M3; `LogHeader`, A15's selective compression, B21's single-writer `flock` and `index.bin` sidecar, session persistence. Beside it §4.1's session actor and the registry ([07](part2-engine/07-session-registry.md)): the bounded per-subscriber fan-out with drop-oldest, A18's seq→gap→resync contract, `attach`/`Subscribe { since }` replay, and A25's `WriterEpoch` fencing. **`zstd` enters the tree here**, which is the precondition **U14** has been waiting on — though doc 31's cross-compile matrix still does not exist, so the *matrix* cost stays unmeasured even after this row. ⚠️ **Corrected 2026-09-21 at M1.1b: `zstd` is not this row's only gate edit, and the cell implied it was.** [07](part2-engine/07-session-registry.md) §7.8 carries the **measured** set (compiled on the pinned toolchain, not resolved — f115/f117) and with it the count: **three rows across two files plus two Appendix F cells** — `zstd-sys` as `class1-allowlist.txt`'s *first* row (§F.3's entry already reads \"accepted\"), and **`linux-raw-sys` + `windows-sys`** in `class1-exempt.txt` with their §F.1 class-2 namings, both arriving via `fd-lock` → `rustix` and neither predicted here. \"Exempt\" is not \"no gate work\": `dep-audit.py` rejects an exempt row §F.1 does not name (U94). The set is **four crates** — `tokio[rt, rt-multi-thread, sync, macros]`, `tokio-util[−default]` for §4.1's `CancellationToken`, `fd-lock`, `zstd` — with **`dashmap` declined** (U116: §4.1's spelling ruled illustrative, +11 packages for a map whose hot path is inside a session) and tokio's `fs`/`io-util`/`time` **excluded** by the blocking-writer ruling, which is also what keeps `bytes` out of the row except via `tokio-util`'s non-optional tail. ⚠️ **Two questions this row's opening settled rather than carried, 2026-09-21.** **U117** was a hard gate and is closed: §3.6 specified a per-field compression discriminator and never gave its on-disk shape, so anything written would have been pinned by `format_version: 1` — the sibling `<leaf>_codec` key with absence meaning `plain` is now spec, chosen because it is the only candidate under which an *uncompressed* log record is byte-identical to the wire encoding, and [05](part2-engine/05-event-log-storage.md) §5.3 carries the three implementation obligations that follow (the leaf is `ToolOutput::Text.text`, not the object the allowlist names; a **2-variant** store-side write path because serde attributes are per type and not per call site; a read path accepting both forms). **f120's second half resolves to \"no spec edit\"**: the compression *level* is on the field-encoding axis §13.5 puts in config, not on `Codec`'s record-representation axis, so the trait keeps its receiver-less methods and all 82 goldens are untouched — [07](part2-engine/07-session-registry.md) §7.9 records the derivation and Pass A replaces the question in `codec.rs:10-13` with the answer ⚠️ **Pass A1 is written and every gate re-run green by hand, but it is NOT committed and is under rework for U120** — the store skeleton, and the `codec.rs` question is now the answer (comment-only; `dacode-protocol` still 114 tests green with zero fixture diff). `dacode-core::store` holds the three **sync, dyn-compatible** traits (doc 05 §5.1/f138), `JsonlStore` (LogHeader on line 1, store-assigned cursors from 1 with the header offset, `Cursor(0)` = everything per G4, `Live` never written, `Codec::encode`'s `Err` propagated with **no empty-buffer fallback**), `index.bin` under §5.5's three invariants, and the `fd-lock` acquisition with doc 05's refusal message. **45 tests** (42 unit + 3 that spawn a second process, §14.1's two plus an orderly-release case). **`zstd` did NOT land here** — A2 owns it, so this commit's gate diff matches its own dependency: `gates/boundary-edges.txt` **22 → 23** (`dacode-core -> fd-lock`, doc 01 §1.6's verbatim diff empty), `class1-exempt.txt` += `linux-raw-sys` + `windows-sys` with their §F.1 class-2 namings, `class1-allowlist.txt` still empty, `dep-audit.py` + `--self-test` + `cargo deny check` all exit 0. **No tokio/tokio-util either** — Pass B's. Three items filed from building it: **U118** (a torn log tail has no specified remedy; A1 refuses the open), **U119** (doc 05 §5.1's method list and doc 07 §7.8's `fd-lock` package count are both measurably wrong), **U120** (`fd-lock`'s guard cannot live in a struct without `unsafe` — **ruled the same day against doc 04 §4.6, which says `unsafe` \"is not needed for M0–M1\", so A1's three items are being removed rather than reviewed**). ✅ **Pass A1 LANDED 2026-09-21 under dual review — four implementer rounds, three primary reviews.** Secondary **APPROVE** at round 1 with zero blockers (a 34-row transcription table from design §3.6/§3.7/§12/§14.1 and docs 04/05/07 to `path:line`). Primary **REQUEST CHANGES** → **REQUEST CHANGES (narrow)** → **APPROVE**, with the final verdict explicit that no blocker remained across all four rounds. `dacode-core::store` is ten files, **69 tests**, every file under the 1000 cap (largest `jsonl.rs` at 870), **zero `unsafe`** and `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` at the crate root so U120's ruling is a build failure rather than a convention. **The review earned its keep three times over.** (i) Its HIGH was demonstrated by *running* code, not argued: `validated_prefix` walked back from the tail and accepted everything before the first record that validated, on a stated assumption that \"the corruption model is a lost suffix\" — true of a crash, false of a corruption — so a damaged offset in the *middle* of the projection silently renumbered the log, inverting §12's asymmetry and minting phantom cursors with a **clean** recovery report. **The root cause was the doc, not the code**: §5.5's invariant 3 said \"validate *the last record*\" and the code transcribed it faithfully. (ii) It then dispositioned the question *I* had escalated better than I had framed it (U122). (iii) It caught an unguarded `u64` subtraction in the very expression its own proposed hardening fed, twice, and disclosed that it had missed it in both earlier passes. ⚠️ **Two lessons recorded rather than lost.** First, **the primary's context was the only available diff**: the implementer moved `index.rs`'s 301-line test module out to get under the cap and could not byte-diff the move against the original (`store/` is untracked, so there is no git baseline), offering instead an indentation-asserting extraction script, a `--list` diff, a green suite and eight mutations — none of which can distinguish \"moved\" from \"moved and quietly weakened\", because a dropped assertion leaves all of them green. The reviewer *had* round 1's file verbatim in context and diffed it assertion-by-assertion. Second, **\"unreachable today\" arithmetic is not a shrug when the release failure is silence rather than a crash** — `clippy::arithmetic_side_effects` is not among doc 04's six, so no gate ever catches the class (f150). **Gates re-run by the orchestrator, not inherited:** `fmt`/`check`/`clippy --workspace --all-targets` clean; `dacode-core` 69; `dacode-protocol` **114** unchanged with zero golden-fixture diff; `dep-audit.py` + `--self-test` + `cargo deny check` + `check-lints` exit 0; doc 01 §1.6's verbatim boundary diff **empty** at **23** edges; `Cargo.lock` 39 → **56**. ✅ **A2 LANDED 2026-09-22 under dual review** (secondary **APPROVE** round 1; primary **REQUEST CHANGES → APPROVE**; its one MED blocker was two false comments in `session.rs` claiming a self-heal the code does not have — corrected, the race filed as U123). Step 0 split `jsonl.rs`'s test module to `jsonl_tests.rs` in its own commit (`d3c24e0`) so the move had a git baseline A1's `index.rs` move lacked. `zstd` 0.13.3 entered the tree — the precondition **U14** waits on, **not** its discharge — and `dacode-core::store` is now **15 files / 112 tests** (110 + 2 spawned halves), largest `jsonl_tests.rs` at 897, zero `unsafe`. Selective compression per U117's ruled shape (`compress.rs`: leaf = `ToolOutput::Text.text`/`ThinkingEnded.text`, sibling `<leaf>_codec` with absence = `plain`, base64 zstd, floor 1024 / level 3 store-side per f136, a 2-variant log mirror pinned by a byte-identity test against `Codec::encode` — mutation-proved load-bearing); `FsBlobStore` (`blob.rs`, always-compressed self-describing blobs → U124) and `SessionCatalog` (`session.rs`, `meta.json` + `index.json`, `org.json` excluded per hard rule 1). Gate edits: `class1-allowlist.txt`'s **first** row `zstd-sys` (introducer never `cc`), `boundary-edges.txt` **23 → 25** (`dacode-core -> zstd` + `-> serde`, the serde edge forced by the mirror per §13.3 and adding zero packages), `Cargo.lock` **56 → 64** (+8). `dacode-protocol` still **114** with zero fixture diff. Three holes filed (**U123** the `SessionCatalog` race / Pass C, **U124** the blob container owes a §12 sentence, **U125** `compress=\"off\"` ≡ `\"blobs\"` for `JsonlStore`); doc 07 §7.8's `+9` → `+8` and its boundary banner corrected. ✅ **U125's code obligation landed 2026-09-22 (tracker t219), so U124 and U125 are both closed and the spec is no longer ahead of the code**: `FsBlobStore` carries the policy, `Compress::compresses_blobs()` is the one place its blob half is read, `off` writes a `plain` head over the raw payload, and `get` dispatches on the **head** rather than the config (a blob is §12's only non-rebuildable artifact, so a policy change must not orphan the ones already on disk). `dacode-core::store` is **118 tests** (116 + 2 spawned halves), `blob.rs`'s 9 → **15**. Dual review **REQUEST CHANGES → APPROVE** (secondary at round 2, primary at round 3 after **19** mutations on the final tree), and it earned its keep on the author's own additions rather than on A2's code: a capacity comment claiming neither branch reallocates, falsified by a 64 KiB incompressible payload (zstd emits *more* than it is given, so the reservation is now `zstd::zstd_safe::compress_bound` — reachable ungated through `zstd`'s own `pub use zstd_safe`, which the first replacement comment got wrong twice over by naming a `zstd::block` module that does not exist in 0.13.3 and an `experimental` feature that would not have helped); `with_config` validating a codec under `off` that nothing uses, which both emitted a false \"cannot be read\" message naming a directory that does not exist **and** disagreed with `JsonlStore::open_with` on the one `CompressConfig` Pass C hands both stores; and a surviving \"always compressed of their own accord\" in `compress.rs` itself. Applying the ruling also found **two design sentences `6e8e8f5` had missed** — §3.6's discriminator paragraph and §12's directory-layout block, eleven lines from the corrected prose, so the transcription source contradicted itself; both corrected. ✅ **Pass B LANDED 2026-09-23** (tracker t211/t222–t225) **under dual review — five implementer rounds, four primary reviews, secondary APPROVE at round 2.** Step 0 landed `tokio` 1.53.1 `[rt, rt-multi-thread, sync, macros]` + `tokio-util` 0.7.19 `[−default]` with `gates/boundary-edges.txt` **25 → 27** (`dacode-core -> tokio`, `-> tokio-util`, doc 01 §1.6's verbatim `tail -n +2` diff **empty** at 27) and **no class-1 or class-2 row** — `dep-audit.py` is clean at 6 attributions with zero new markers, so §7.8's \"no `mio`, no `socket2`, no `windows-sys` even under `--target all`\" **holds**. ⚠️ **But §7.8's two package counts do not reproduce against the committed tree, and are corrected there in this same change** (the f113/f117/U119b/f151 class, fourth occurrence): `Cargo.lock` **64 → 69 = +5**, not the +12 its two rows sum to. `tokio` alone is **+2** (`tokio`, `tokio-macros`) and `tokio-util` **+3** (`tokio-util`, `bytes`, `futures-sink`); seven of the twelve were already pinned at M1.2 — `proc-macro2`/`quote`/`syn`/`unicode-ident` by `serde_derive`, `pin-project-lite` and `futures-core` by `ulid`'s wasm tail (`web-time` → `js-sys` → `futures-util`). Separately, tokio's closure under `--target all` is **7** packages, not the recorded \"8 total\": the scratch probe's `cargo tree --prefix none` counted the probe crate itself as its own root line. `bytes` arrives via `tokio-util`'s non-optional tail whatever `io-util` does, so §7.8's Q1 gained a clause saying excluding `io-util` narrows the *route* rather than removing the package. **Code:** `store/writer.rs` (529) — one `std` thread per open session, the `fd-lock` guard and the store as **locals in the thread body** (U120's shape, zero `unsafe`, `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` intact), `Append`/`Flush` as request-with-reply so the **store** assigns every cursor, the oneshot rendezvous a `Held` travels back over, U122's `read_to_tail`, and §7.4's shutdown ordering (cancel → drain → explicit `flush` + `fsync` → drop the guard, the guard outliving the flush because it is declared first); `store/fanout.rs` (281) — `broadcast` cap **256** carrying **`Arc<Envelope>`**, `SeqTracker` as design §3.7 step 3's transport-independent check with `Lagged` demoted to a hint, and `ring > queue` as a `const` assertion **and** a test; plus `emit`, §7.4's tier routing, which lands here rather than in Pass C because an ordering nobody implements is an ordering nobody tests. `store` was **19 files / 145 lib tests** (143 + 2 spawned halves), largest `jsonl_tests.rs` at 897 with `writer_tests.rs` at 890, zero `unsafe` *(round-1 counts; superseded by the round-2 and round-3 tallies below)*. **✅ U122 closed** citing `the_same_corrupt_record_fails_a_writer_open_and_leaves_no_claim` and its read-only sibling; **✅ debt d4 struck** — the scoped `allow(dead_code)` is deleted and a non-test `cargo check -p dacode-core` is warning-free, which needed the writer's API to be `pub` rather than `pub(crate)` (it is the *safe* entry point: it acquires the guard itself, unlike `JsonlStore::open`). **16 mutations, all 16 killed**, and two of them earned their keep: reply-before-flush **survives** the file-based item-4 test the dispatch and §7.10 both prescribe, so §7.10 item 4 now carries the gated-flush injection that does fail on it, and deleting the explicit final flush **survives** a \"the tail is there\" test because `JsonlStore::drop` is a second net — so item 5 is three tests, the discriminating one asserting `shutdown` *reports* a failed `fsync`. ⚠️ **Two stale pointers falsified rather than discharged, both corrected where they were written:** §7.10 items 4–5 do **not** test `atomic_write`'s crash-safety (nothing on the writer path goes through it — the log and `index.bin` are append-only and it serves only `meta.json`/`index.json`/blobs), and they cannot reach between `JsonlStore::flush`'s two adjacent `sync` calls, so invariant 2's write order has no seam to inject into and stays review-defended; both `mod.rs` and `index.rs` said \"Pass B owes this\" and now say what is actually true. ⚠️ **Three things this pass chose that no doc pinned, recorded rather than buried:** `WRITER_QUEUE_DEPTH = 64` (§7.4 pins the queue as *bounded* and not its depth; with request-with-reply a single actor has one request in flight, so the depth is slack for a second producer), `StoreError::WriterGone` (a dead writer thread has no error to report through and doc 04 §4.2 forbids folding it into `Io`), and the writer's cancellation being **channel closure** rather than a `CancellationToken` poll — `tokio_util`'s token has no blocking API at all (measured against 0.7.19's source), so §7.4's \"the writer's shutdown message\" can only mean the sender dropping; the token stays Pass C's. Also a precision edit to §7.4's `Append` bullet, whose \"the actor never waits\" contradicted the request-with-reply ruling eleven lines below it. **Verification, measured:** `fmt --check` 0; `check --workspace --all-targets` 0 with zero warnings; `clippy --workspace --all-targets` 0 with **zero warning lines**; `test --workspace` **262 passed / 0 failed**; `dacode-protocol` **114** with `git status --porcelain crates/dacode-protocol` **empty**; `dep-audit.py` + `--self-test` + `cargo deny check` + `check-lints` all 0; `check-links.py` 0 in both real modes (714 links, 545 §-refs, 59 fence-balanced). **Not verified:** no cross-compile build, so **U14 stays open**; `atomic_write`'s crash-safety and invariant 2's write order remain untested for the reasons above; the end-to-end backpressure form of the bounded queue is untested because a non-hanging assertion needs `tokio::time`, which §7.8's Q1 puts at M1.5. ⚠️ **Pass B dual review round 1: BOTH reviewers REQUEST CHANGES** (secondary with a 25-row transcription table that found every normative claim discharged, so the *shape* was right; primary proving one real defect by running it). **One BLOCKER, and it was reachable only because this pass made it reachable.** `JsonlStore::append` validated its `session` *argument* against the store's session and never validated `envelope.session`; `serve` passes the writer's own captured session on every call, so the check always passed — and the primary's probe appended an envelope built for a **foreign** `SessionId` into this session's `events.jsonl` under **this** log's cursor. Design §12 makes that unrecoverable (the log is append-only) and `fold` keys the snapshot's identity off the envelope, so this session's subscribers would fold a `Snapshot` claiming to be another session while that session's own log never received the record: both §3.7 streams wrong, neither repairable. `StoreError::SessionMismatch`'s own doc at `error.rs:42` already read \"**The envelope** names a session this store does not own\" while its sole raise site checked the argument. It was **unreachable before this pass** — `JsonlStore::open` is `pub(crate)` with no non-test caller, literally what debt d4's `allow(dead_code)` recorded — so deleting d4 and widening `LogWriter` to `pub` made `LogWriter::append` the crate's public write route. The `pub` widening itself was verified genuinely forced and mutation-proved, and stands: `spawn` acquires the guard itself, so the *lock* half of the safety argument holds; it was simply silent on the *session* half. **Fixed in the store, not in `LogWriter`**, so every backend inherits it and M3's `SqliteStore` does not rediscover it; the obligation is now stated on `EventStore::append`'s trait doc (which stated no caller obligation about `envelope.session`) with the variant added to its `# Errors`, and `SessionMismatch`'s doc names both producers. Three tests (`traits.rs` store-level, `writer_tests.rs` at the writer and at `emit`), and two mutations kill all three by name. **Round 1 also found two surviving mutations on invariants §7.4 says no gate sees**, both now killed by a named test: `break`ing out of `serve`'s loop on an append `Err` (which drops every remaining reply, so queued callers get a `WriterGone` that is a lie and a queued `Flush` is never answered — turning §3.6's pre-side-effect boundary into \"the tool ran without its fsync\", silently) → `a_failed_append_does_not_abandon_the_requests_queued_behind_it`; and releasing the lock guard *before* the store drops, the exact inversion `writer.rs`'s own comment forbids → `the_store_drops_while_the_lock_claim_is_still_held`, whose probe sits in the backend's own `Drop` so it observes the moment exactly rather than racing it. ⚠️ **And the author's own comments were the blockers for the second consecutive pass — three new false claims written beside five corrected ones.** (i) `writer.rs` asserted `blocking_recv` \"panics inside an asynchronous execution context, so a runtime worker **or a `block_in_place`** would not do\": the `block_in_place` half is false, and the primary traced it — `future::block_on` gates on `try_enter_blocking_region()`, which returns `None` only when the context `is_entered()` (`context/blocking.rs:17-31`), and the multi-thread worker's `block_in_place` runs its closure inside `context::exit_runtime` (`multi_thread/worker.rs:505`), so on the `rt-multi-thread` runtime this row enables it does **not** panic. The conclusion (a `std` thread) is still right, for the real reason now written there: a writer lives as long as its session, so `block_in_place` would hold a worker out of the pool for the whole of it, and it cannot be used in a `current_thread` runtime at all (`task/blocking.rs:21-25`). All three re-verified against tokio 1.53.1's sources before the replacement was written. (ii) The `CancellationToken` surface was miscounted as \"three `async`/future methods\" where there are four — the enumeration is now dropped entirely, because the load-bearing half (\"**no** blocking API at all\", zero `rg -i blocking` hits over `cancellation_token.rs`) stands on its own. (iii) **P-LIVE-006 verbatim on my own §7.8 correction**: the sweep followed the edit, so the `Cargo.toml` comment written *beside* the corrected table still said \"Measured at **8 packages**\" (the closure is 7) and \"the **four** packages are `bytes`, `futures-core`, `futures-sink` and `tokio-util`\" (three are new; `futures-core` was pinned at M1.2 via `ulid`'s wasm tail, which the corrected row says in terms), and §7.8's own **Q3** six lines below the corrected row still read \"+4 packages\" and \"cheaper by four packages\". All four numbers fixed, and `writer.rs`'s reason-3 and §7.8-Q1 framing of `io-util`→`bytes` corrected to \"narrows the route rather than removing the package\". **M6's point is the one worth carrying: correcting a stale pointer without re-owning the work it assigned leaves the gap with NO owner, which is strictly worse than a stale one** — so **U130** (`atomic_write`'s crash-safety, M1.9), **U131** (invariant 2's write order has no seam to inject into, M1.9 with Pass C opportunistic) and **U132** (U122's read-to-tail is O(log size) in *memory*, not only time, and `EventStore` has no streaming `range`; M3) are filed with owners named, because f157/f158/f159 live in `.agents/cache/` which `.gitignore` excludes and are therefore not durable records. **M7:** the three *closed* entries that asserted the falsified claim (t219's, A2's, A1's) are struck-and-corrected in place per P-LIVE-006 — whose closing line is that reviewers should keep grepping closed entries, which is how the primary found them — and A2's `mod.rs:133` cite refreshed to `:137`. **M8:** `LogWriter`'s `Drop` doc claimed \"nothing is lost\", which holds only while the process outlives the detached thread; it now carries all three costs, including the one Pass C will actually hit — detaching releases the lock *later*, so a registry that drops a writer and immediately re-opens the session can get a spurious `Held`, and **Pass C's registry must call `shutdown`, not drop**. **L1/L3:** `read_to_tail`'s 22-line doc trimmed to the half §7.4 does not already carry (a near-verbatim spec transcription rots independently of the section it copies), and `spawn` now states that it is **not cancellation-neutral** — dropping the future at the rendezvous lets the thread run to completion and `report` a `LogRecovery` into a receiver already gone, so §3.6's \"report the discarded byte count rather than swallow it\" is swallowed on exactly that path. **The round-2 tests pushed `writer_tests.rs` to 1 211 lines, over the cap**, so the five injected backends moved to `writer_backends.rs` (~~946 + 312~~ → **945 + 319**, `wc -l` transcribed rather than recalled — the wrong pair was the f113/f117/U119b/f151 recorded-count class arriving again in prose written *beside* the correction that names it, and the same cell gave `writer_tests.rs` as both 946 and 945 eleven words apart) as a **pure move verified by `--list` diff**: all 20 test names byte-identical before and after. **Mutations: 20 run, 20 killed, 0 survivors** (16 from round 1 re-run on the final tree plus H1/H1b/R7/R11), every file md5-verified restored. **Counts after round 2** *(superseded by round 3 below)*: `store` was **20 files / 150 lib tests** (148 passed + 2 spawned halves; `writer` 20, `traits` 6), workspace **267 passed / 0 failed**, `dacode-protocol` still **114** with zero fixture diff, largest store file `writer_tests.rs` at 945, `cargo doc` back to exactly the **8**-warning baseline (all five new ones were `private_intra_doc_links`, removed by linking the public types instead of the private modules), zero `unsafe` with `forbid` intact, `rg allow(dead_code) store/mod.rs` empty, clippy `--workspace --all-targets` **zero warning lines** after `touch`ing the store so it genuinely recompiled. **Still NOT verified:** no cross-compile build (**U14 open**); U130/U131's two crash-boundary claims untested by construction; end-to-end backpressure of the bounded queue untested because a non-hanging assertion needs `tokio::time`, which §7.8's Q1 puts at M1.5; and three of `WriterGone`'s **six** `at:` sites unreachable through the public API — measured with `rg -n 'at: \"' crates/dacode-core/src/store/writer.rs`, which returns 6 (lines 270/314/318/341/345/381), not the \"four\" an earlier revision of this cell said: `PanickingAppend` reaches `append reply`, `flush reply` and `shutdown join`, while the two send-error sites and the open-rendezvous site are about the *sender* being gone rather than the thread dying, and are unreachable because `LogWriter` holds the only sender and `shutdown` consumes `self`. ⚠️ **Pass B round 3: secondary APPROVE, primary REQUEST CHANGES with every round-1/2 item marked DISCHARGED** (its move-diff verdict \"nothing was weakened\", its `pub`-widening verdict \"now safe on both halves, and each half is mutation-proved rather than argued\"), leaving five small items — two prose counts, one measured correctness asymmetry, two imprecise comments. **All five fixed, and every number below was measured rather than recalled**, which is the instruction the primary's closing line gave: `wc -l` and `rg 'at: \"'` answer both prose counts in seconds. **LOW-A was H1's class on the half H1's fix did not cover**: `emit`'s *live* branch broadcast a foreign-session envelope unchecked — the primary measured `emit(&writer, &fanout, text_delta(other))` returning `Ok` with a subscriber to `temp.session` receiving an envelope whose `session` was `other`, violating design §3.7's \"every channel described here is per-session\". It self-repairs (a live envelope carries no `seq`, so it never moves `Snapshot.cursor`, and §3.7's own remedy applies) and needs a caller bug to reach, but the asymmetry was the defect: the durable half had just become guarded by the store and the live half was guarded by nothing. Fixed at the head of `emit` so it covers both branches and makes `emit` total, with `emit_refuses_a_live_envelope_naming_another_session_and_broadcasts_nothing` as the mirror of the durable test; **mutations N1 (delete the guard) and N2 (move it onto the durable branch only, i.e. restore the asymmetry) both die on that test by name**. **LOW-C:** `SessionMismatch`'s `Display` still read \"the call named {named}\" while its own variant and field docs had been swept to cover the envelope producer — so for the new producer it *misattributed*, on user-facing text on the path U93 will map to an exit status. Now \"{named} disagreed\", true of both, and pinned by `a_session_mismatch_message_blames_neither_producer` (N3 kills it). No test pinned the old string, verified by `rg 'this store holds session'`. **LOW-B:** the `block_in_place` replacement objection was imprecise about the mechanism — tokio's multi-thread `block_in_place` *hands the worker core to a freshly spawned blocking-pool thread and steals it back* (`worker.rs:483-489`), so task-driving capacity is preserved and \"a worker out of the pool\" was the wrong cost; what a session-lifetime closure consumes is a **blocking-pool slot** (bounded by `max_blocking_threads`, default 512 at `builder.rs:306`). Replaced with tokio's own stronger objection quoted verbatim from `task/blocking.rs:27-30` (\"cannot be cancelled … will wait indefinitely for all blocking operations to finish\"), which is decisive for a writer that lives as long as a session and shorter than the paraphrase — all four source sites opened and read before the sentence was written, this being the *second* edit to that same comment. **MED-A/MED-B:** the two prose counts, corrected above. ⚠️ **The LOW-A test pushed `writer_tests.rs` to 1 005, over the cap**, so §7.10's items 4–5 moved to `writer_durability_tests.rs` — a second deliberate split, by responsibility this time (the request/routing contract vs. *when do bytes become durable and in what order does the writer stop*), with `mod backends` hoisted into `writer.rs` so both test files share it. Verified a pure move by `--list` diff: **all 21 names byte-identical**, only the module path changed for the 7 that moved. **Mutations: 23 run across the three rounds on the final tree, 23 killed, 0 survivors** (16 round-1 re-run, R7/R11/H1/H1b, then N1/N2/N3). **Counts after round 3, all transcribed from `wc -l` and `--list`:** `store` is **21 files / 152 listed lib tests** (150 passed + 2 spawned halves; `jsonl` 21, `writer` 21 across its two files, `error` 6, `traits` 6), largest store file **`jsonl_tests.rs` at 897** with `writer_tests.rs` 664, `writer_durability_tests.rs` 364 and `writer_backends.rs` 319, all 21 under 1 000. ⚠️ **Pass B round 4 — prose-only, primary as sole reviewer, and it discharged *everything* from rounds 1–3** (H1, M1–M8, L1/L2/L3, LOW-A/B/C, and both unasked structural changes: the second move \"assertion-for-assertion identical, nothing lost and nothing weakened\", the `mod backends` hoist \"CORRECT, conventional, and it *avoided* a widening … the narrower of the two available answers\"). It re-ran R7/R11/N1/H1-del and all four die, and its round-2 probe now reports `leaked_across_sessions = false`. Five items remained, all prose. **MED-C** was the substantive one and the measurement changed the prescribed fix: `emit_publishes_nothing_when_the_store_refuses_the_envelope` stopped testing what its name and doc said once LOW-A moved the refusal to `emit`'s head, upstream of the store — and re-running the mutations showed it **survives both H1-del (kills 2) and N1 (kills 1)** while dying on **M6 and M8**, so grouping it \"under LOW-A's\" as instructed would have installed a *fresh* unmeasured attribution in the row being corrected for having one. Renamed to `emit_publishes_nothing_when_it_refuses_a_foreign_envelope`, doc rewritten to name the head guard and state what it actually dies on, and the whole header table **re-derived from measured kill lists** — which meant running five mutations that had never been run (P1 open-before-lock, P3 caller's-cursor-stands, Q1 fsync-per-append, Q3 remove the `NotDurable` refusal, Q4 `WriterGone` shutdown arm returns `Ok`), and labelling the two rows that **cannot** be mutation-probed as such rather than inventing an attribution: the U122 read-only control half (nothing in the present code refuses such an open, so there is no mutation to remove) and the queue-depth const test. **MED-D:** `index.rs:341` was a **third** site assigning an injection to Pass B, 76 lines above the sibling round 2 corrected — `Index::record`'s `write_all` is three calls below the trait surface, so none of Pass B's four injected `EventStore` doubles can reach it, the same structural reason U131 gives. Corrected in place, and **U131 widened from one note to two** rather than opening U133, per the primary's explicit instruction. **LOW-D:** the whole `rg -n 'Pass B' crates/` cluster swept in one mechanical pass — 10 sites, including the worst (`jsonl.rs:405`'s \"U122 **is** Pass B's and is deliberately NOT implemented here\", a live obligation on an item closed two rounds earlier, now pointing at `read_to_tail`) and `traits.rs:102`'s \"this pass provides the mechanism and its test, **not the thread**\", false since the thread landed. Every surviving `Pass B` in `crates/` is now past tense and true. ⚠️ **And the sweep found a false claim in the instruction document itself, which is the round's real finding.** Doc 07 §7.4 and §7.10 item 5 both motivate the shutdown ordering with \"a `BufWriter` dropped without a flush **loses its buffer**\". **Measured false on the pinned toolchain**: a standalone probe on rustc 1.98.1 wrote 43 bytes to a `BufWriter<File>`, dropped it with no `flush` and no `sync_all`, and read all 43 back through a fresh handle — `BufWriter`'s own `Drop` impl flushes. §7.4 was also self-contradicting, relying on \"the writer's `Drop`-flush\" delivering the partial turn in the same sentence. Two consequences, both corrected: there are **three** nets under the tail, not the two round 2 recorded (`BufWriter::drop`, `JsonlStore::drop`, `serve`'s explicit flush) — so Q2a (remove one) and Q2b (remove both) **leave `shutdown_does_not_lose_the_buffer` passing**, and its real kill is M1 (the drain), which is what its table row now says; and what the explicit flush actually adds over `BufWriter::drop` is the **`fsync`** and the **reported error**, not the bytes. **The ordering and the explicit flush both stand; only the reason was wrong** — and a wrong reason is what a later pass \"simplifies\" away. Corrected at all seven sites (doc 07 §7.4 and §7.10, `writer.rs`'s module doc and `serve`, `jsonl.rs`'s `Drop`, `jsonl_tests.rs`, and the durability table/test docs). ⚠️ **Two process notes recorded rather than buried.** (i) The first attempt at measuring Q1/Q2 **hung and stranded a mutation on disk**: flush-per-append deadlocks the `GatedFlush` test, whose gate is only released after the flush request. Restored by hand and verified (`rg` for the mutation text, then 150 passed), and the re-run was scoped to one `--exact` test per mutation with a `subprocess` timeout and a `finally` restore. A mutation harness that can hang must time out per mutation and restore unconditionally. (ii) `rg -n 'Pass C' crates/` returns **41 lines across 15 files — 37 in `dacode-core` and 4 in `dacode-protocol`** (`wire.rs:40`, `event.rs:842`, `command.rs:571`, `turn.rs:221`), and those four mean **M1.2's** Pass C — the golden-fixture corpus, which landed — not M1.4's, so they are stale forward-references in the same class; ~~they are **not fixed here** because this row must leave `git status --porcelain crates/dacode-protocol` empty~~ ✅ **discharged 2026-09-23 by Pass C's comment-only pre-step (tracker t226), which is its own commit precisely so C1 and C2 can each assert an empty protocol porcelain again.** ⚠️ **The pre-step found a *second* falsehood inside the same `wire.rs` sentence, and it is the f162 sibling class rather than a typo**: the sentence listed `Hint` among \"the tagged fixtures\" when there is **no** `Hint` golden at all (`tests/fixtures.rs:34` says so in terms, and `wire.rs:323`'s `hint_is_a_tagged_record_with_the_specified_bytes` pins its bytes instead), and `UpFrame` has none either (`wire.rs:280`'s `up_frame_has_the_specified_shape`) — so the correction enumerates **all five** shapes the sentence's own preamble names, measured against the corpus (`wire/` 31/31 tagged, `command/` 21/21 tagged, `log/` 27/27 plain envelopes with the event tagged inside, `find -iname '*hint*'` = 0) rather than transcribed from the claim being corrected. The name collision between two different \"Pass C\"s is itself worth a future rename. ⚠️ The count was first recorded here as 35 and corrected by measurement — the fourth round of this pass produced a from-memory number beside a correction that was *about* from-memory numbers. **Counts, all transcribed from `wc -l` and `--list` after the edits:** `dacode-core` **150 passed + 2 ignored** (unchanged by the rename, which is the check that it changed no behaviour), workspace **269 passed / 0 failed**, `dacode-protocol` **114** with porcelain empty; `store` is 21 files, largest `jsonl_tests.rs` at **901**, with `writer_tests.rs` 696 / `writer_durability_tests.rs` 402 / `writer_backends.rs` 319 / `writer.rs` 596, none over 1 000 *(these five are the post-orchestrator-round-5 values — the BufWriter citation edits moved `jsonl_tests.rs` 900→901, the durability doc 392→402 and `writer.rs` 594→596; re-measured with `wc -l`, not carried over)*; `rg 'assign to Pass B' crates/` **empty**; `rg 'this store holds session' crates/` confirms LOW-C's neutral string intact. **Mutations: 30 distinct across four rounds** (16 + 4 + 3 + 7 new; counted by enumerating the ids in all six harness scripts rather than by arithmetic, because the first number written here was 28 and was itself the class this round is about), **all killed or explicitly labelled not-mutation-probeable, 0 unexplained survivors**. ✅ **Pass C was SPLIT into C1 and C2 (2026-09-23, user-approved) and C1 LANDED** — a comment-only pre-step first (`3b1efde`, t226: the four `dacode-protocol` \"Pass C\" comments disambiguated to M1.2's, so C1 and C2 can each assert an empty protocol porcelain again), then **C1 = the `Session` aggregate and the interim storage config** (t227), under dual review: both reviewers **REQUEST CHANGES → APPROVE**, and neither found a logic, ownership, async or crate-boundary defect — **every** finding was prose, visibility or a missing durable record, this milestone's dominant class arriving in a pass that wrote 1 321 lines (the three new files' `wc -l` plus `lib.rs`'s 11 added) and no concurrency. **Code:** `session.rs` (607) — nine fields, `pub(crate)`, exactly doc 07 §7.2's ✅ rows (`id`, `cwd`, `log`, `pending`, `cancel`, `children`, `parent`) plus U115's interim `agent`/`model`, and **no** `sandbox`/`context`/`perm`/`checkpoints`/`controller`; `log` is `Option<LogWriter>` because §7.4's `StoreFactory` builds the store *inside* the writer thread and `LogWriter::shutdown` consumes `self` while a registry lookup hands out `Arc` clones, so take-under-mutex is the only reachable close; `PendingRequests` as kind-tagged typed oneshot senders with first-answer-wins as a tested property and a cross-kind answer refused **without consuming** the waiter (`ReqId` is one key space for both kinds, so a cross-kind command is reachable from any client); `session_tests.rs` (311); `storage_config.rs` (392) — U129's route 1, one `CompressConfig` validated once **before either store exists** and handed to both, with no TOML parser and no `Default` on the interim types. **165 passed + 2 ignored** (`dacode-core`, from 150), workspace **284**, `dacode-protocol` still **114** with porcelain empty, **zero new dependencies** (`Cargo.lock` still 69, `gates/` untouched, boundary diff empty at 27), zero `unsafe`, largest new file 607, `cargo doc` at the exact 8-warning baseline. **Mutations, enumerated by id rather than by arithmetic** (this cell's own recorded-count class): the implementer's **seven** — FA1 displace-on-register, FA2 second-answer-`Ok`, FA3 wrong-kind answer consumes, FA3b the mirror arm, SC1 delete the boundary's codec check, SC2 delete its level check, SC3 narrow the level rule to `0..=15` — plus the primary's **two** (SS1 a `Rc<()>` field that compile-fails the Send/Sync pin; MD1 a `Default` at level 9 that dies on the default pin), **nine on the final tree, all killed, 0 survivors**. The primary's round 2 added three probes of its own: **SC3-old** (SC3 with the pre-widening `[3, i32::MAX]` axis, which proves the header's \"only through the widened axis\" claim — the agreement test *passes* and only `an_out_of_range_level_…` dies), **SC4-wider** (widen the boundary to `(start−1)..=(end+1)`, killed *only* by the agreement test at `level: -131073`, so the widened axis is load-bearing in both directions) and **DC1** (delete `session.rs:44`'s `allow(dead_code)` → **12** `dead_code` warnings from `cargo check -p dacode-core --lib`, i.e. the exemption is load-bearing and not decorative). ⚠️ **The primary also stranded a mutation in the *build cache* rather than on disk** — its restore used `shutil.copy2`, which preserves the backup's older mtime, so cargo's fingerprint reused the mutated binary and the first post-restore run reported a failure on a tree whose md5 already matched the pre-run snapshot. f163's sibling, and recorded in `.agents/pitfalls-live.md`: restore without metadata or `touch` after restoring, and never trust a post-mutation green run that did not rebuild. ⚠️ **The review's five MEDs were all the false-prose class, and the primary measured one false against the author's own probe**: `session.rs` claimed a record larger than the writer's buffer \"reaches the file through several `write` syscalls\", citing std line numbers that are **not openable on the pinned toolchain** (no `rust-src` component) — measured, an over-capacity `write_all` from an empty buffer is **one** inner write and from a primed buffer two whose first carries only earlier whole records, so the torn-tail window is *contingent on a kernel short-write*, not guaranteed. The conclusion survived on a stronger, **unconditional** mechanism the comment had not used (`Index::open`'s `set_len` plus its rewrite of the re-derived records from a second handle, inside invariant 2's own documented window), and the rewrite leads with that. Also from the review: the visibility narrowing (**both** reviewers, independently — a `pub Session` lets any dependent `session.log.take()` and drop the writer, making M8's \"call `shutdown`, not drop\" a workspace-wide review obligation rather than a crate-internal one) cost **two scoped `allow(dead_code)` exemptions** recording \"no production caller until C2\", the debt-d4 shape Pass B had just deleted, with C2 named as remover and f160's lesson applying to their deletion; and the pending map's **monotone growth in abandoned requests** plus its `Ok`-on-gone-receiver semantics are now documented on the type with C2's obligation named (an abandonment path or a `deregister`), because `Ok` says \"a live waiter was consumed\" and never \"the asker heard this\". **Three items filed with owners rather than left in comments** — **U133** (§4.1's range-read half has no landed provider while the writer lives; route settled as a chunked third writer-queue request kind, ring-first, because ring-only replay would break §3.7 step 5's stdio bootstrap), **U134** (§4.2's per-turn `Cancel` and §7.4's session-teardown token cannot be one token; M1.9's), **U135** (three `store/` edits the file freeze deferred: `blob.rs:101-109`'s falsified t219 rationale, a §3.6 misquote at **six** sites of which one spans a line break and evades a line-based `rg`, and the `CompressConfig::validate` split that retires the duplicated checks and houses U129's residual `Display`). **U129 closed** on route 1 with both residuals re-owned by U135; **U115 widened** (no built-in defaults exist to populate the interim, and **no protocol surface carries a provider**, so the string → `(provider, model)` split has no specified derivation and M1.5 is named as the row that needs it — ✅ **discharged at M1.5 Pass A, 2026-09-24: doc 13 §13.7 specifies it and `resolve.rs` implements it**); **U123 narrowed** (route 2 does not fix the lost merge, no in-process lock reaches the cross-process half, and route 3's honest form is a §12 edit — so the route is a **user decision owed before C2 is dispatched**). Doc 07 gained **five** precision-edit hunks in the same change per §7.9's rule (§7.2's heading count, its falsified `log` cell, its \"built-in defaults\" sentence, §7.4's \"two kinds\", and §7.4's cancellation paragraph), and the row-vs-field count sweep reached **six** sites — four in this file, `docs/implementation/README.md:51`, and doc 07 §7.2's heading (the last already counted as one of the five hunks above, so the two tallies overlap by one). The README site was missed by the first pass and caught **independently by both reviewers** — the primary's round-2 LOW-3 and the secondary's round-2 finding 1 — which is P-LIVE-015's mechanism from the outside: the author's sweep followed the edit and stopped at the file it was editing. **Not verified, plainly:** no cross-compile build (**U14 stays open**); ~~`PendingRequests`' behaviour under a real actor select loop is C2's~~ ✅ **discharged at C2** — the actor drives it through a real `select`, and the abandonment path U135's sibling paragraph owed landed as `deregister(req)` (see the C2 block below); the two new `allow(dead_code)` exemptions mean a non-test build compiles these modules without exercising them, exactly as d4's did. ~~**Remaining: C2**~~ ✅ **C2 LANDED 2026-09-23 — M1.4's last pass; its narrative follows the struck scope list.** Its scope, for the record: (the registry and actor, §7.6's subscribe-then-read-then-dedupe with the `SeqTracker` seed it owes, U123's **seam and trait-contract note only** (the locking story is **not** C2's to build: a registry-level mutex would be a JSONL-shaped answer baked into the engine ahead of the row that generalizes the backend. Its owner is now *conditional* — **M3**, or **M1.11** if U136 lands a protocol surface for the session list there — and the \"no production caller of `list` before M3\" premise holds for the *store trait* only, not for the feature: doc 22's TUI sidebar, design §10's layout box and §11's `dacode session list` all name an earlier consumer, and what defers it is the *gap* U136 files, namely that `Command` has no `ListSessions` and `dacode-tui` may depend on `dacode-protocol` only), U133's `Range` request kind, U134's token shape, U135's three `store/` edits, M8's \"call `shutdown`, not drop\", the **pending map's abandonment path** — an event that makes an entry consumable or a `deregister(req)`, so the map mirrors the fold's pending set, documented as owed at `session.rs:355-372` and C2's because C2 is what emits — and the removal of C1's two dead-code exemptions, which is a review event per f160 and debt d5 — ~~plus, when it next touches `dacode-protocol`, the **four stale \"Pass C\" comments there that mean M1.2's Pass C, not this one**: `wire.rs:40`, `event.rs:842`, `command.rs:571`, `turn.rs:221`, left unfixed only because Pass B had to keep that crate's porcelain empty~~ ✅ **the four are discharged by Pass C's comment-only pre-step (t226), landed ahead of C1 so C1 and C2 can each assert an empty protocol porcelain again — and `rg -n 'Pass C' crates/dacode-protocol` now returns only the four disambiguated sites, all reading \"M1.2's Pass C\"**). ✅ **Pass C2 LANDED 2026-09-23** (tracker t228), under dual review — **secondary APPROVE at round 2, primary REQUEST CHANGES → REQUEST CHANGES → REQUEST CHANGES → APPROVE at round 4, with a fifth prose-only round for its four NITs.** Six new files: `registry.rs` (646) — §7.3's settled shape `std RwLock<HashMap<SessionId, Arc<tokio Mutex<Session>>>>`, **lookup returns the `Arc` and never a guard**, and the registry is the lock holder; `actor.rs` (785) — a `select` loop with **decide → reply → apply** so design §3.3's `Ack`-before-resulting-events holds, §8.4's control gate with `TakeControl` ungated for any client and an extension exemption that rides a capability on the sink rather than a `Command` (so §7.7's \"cannot gate a browser\" is structural, not a convention); `replay.rs` (659) — §7.6's subscribe-**first** handshake taken under the same session mutex `emit` holds across publish+retain, which is what makes it race-free rather than merely ordered; plus their three test files and `store/writer.rs`'s **third** request kind. `dacode-core` **165 → 208 passed + 4 ignored**, workspace **284 → 327**, `dacode-protocol` still **114** with porcelain empty, **zero new dependencies** (`Cargo.lock` still **69**, `gates/` untouched, boundary diff empty at **27**), zero `unsafe`, largest file `replay_tests.rs` at **962**. **Mutations: 26 ids, 22 killed by assertion, 4 declared survivors — all four judged legitimate by the primary.** ⚠️ **The finding worth carrying is not in the code: this pass shipped a flake, and the gate matrix as written cannot see one.** Round 1 was green on every gate and the primary's first `cargo test -p dacode-core` exited **101** — it measured **10 failures in 628 runs (~1.6%)** across three tests. Both root causes were the same shape: **a cross-task ordering asserted through an observation point that is not a happens-before edge** (two tests read `events.jsonl` directly instead of through the flushing helper, because `apply` broadcasts *inside* `emit` and flushes only after; a third asserted an empty subscriber queue the instant `send` returned, which holds only while `writer.append().await` happens to yield). ⚠️ **And fixing it introduced a second flake in the opposite direction.** The re-fence that converted a hang into an assertion bounded a wait on `LogWriter::shutdown`, whose work runs on a **blocking-pool thread** doing a real drain + flush + `fsync` — so the bound was spent by the *observer* rather than consumed by the *observed*, and the primary measured **8/160 failures at 8 concurrent workers** while 1- and 2-worker runs were clean. **The two flakes were load-sensitive in opposite directions** (the first needed a *fast* writer reply and reproduced at 1 worker; the second needed a *starved* blocking pool and reproduced only at ≥4), which is why one repeat gate would not have caught both. Fixed: the bound raised 25× on that loop alone with the reason written down, and the failure message rewritten to name **two** causes and the discriminator between them — after which the primary re-ran **1020 runs across five load levels, 0 failures**, and 0/240 at the same 8-worker saturation that had produced 8/160. **The gate matrix gains two additions as a result (tracker f182/f184):** a REPEAT gate (N≥200 at 1 worker) and a CONTENTION gate (≥4 concurrent whole-suite workers) over whichever modules carry cross-task ordering, **derived per pass rather than hardcoded** — with the detection floor stated (1−(1−p)^N: 96% at p=1.6%, 63% at p=0.5%, 18% at p=0.1%), so a green REPEAT reads as \"not detected at this N\" and never as \"absent\". ⚠️ **The other recurring finding is that a deferral's *reason* is what the next pass acts on, and twice the reason was false.** (i) Round 2 asserted the pump \"has no way to bound an `.await` without tokio's `time` feature\" — while the bounded-yield-plus-`is_finished` pattern appeared **four times in that same diff**. (ii) Round 3 justified leaving two cancel races untested by claiming a test would need production surface that does not exist; measured, `StoreFactory`, `OpenedStore` (both fields `pub`), `EventStore` and `LogWriter::spawn` are all `pub`, so a test-local double costs ~40 lines and **zero** production surface — and the cited precedent (U131) was a false analogy, since U131 is two adjacent `sync` calls with no function boundary at any visibility. Both conclusions survived; **both recorded reasons did not**, and a wrong reason is what a later pass declines a cheap test on. Filed as **U137** with M3 as owner. The generalisation, now in `.agents/pitfalls-live.md`: *\"cannot be done without X\" has two flavours needing different greps — a missing **mechanism** (grep the current diff for the pattern) and a missing **seam** (grep the crate for the visibility of the named boundary)* — and for the seam flavour the question is not \"is it `pub`\" but \"**can a `#[cfg(test)]` child of the module that needs it reach it**\", which `SessionRegistry`'s own `pub(crate)` answers *no* for and is why `registry_tests.rs` spawns child processes by name instead of living in `crates/dacode-core/tests/`. ⚠️ **A third false-prose finding was in a comment this pass had just written about U123, and it was the ruling's own subject:** the first draft of the `SessionStore` trait-doc contract stated the *mechanism* as the contract (\"a stale cache that still decodes is trusted rather than rescanned\", \"one atomic replace\", \"a read-modify-write of `index.json`\"), none of which is true of M3's `SqliteStore` — so the trait promoted one backend's defect to a rule every backend must honour, which is precisely the JSONL-shaped answer U123's ruling rejected, arriving through a doc rather than through a lock. Corrected to the neutral half with every artifact name moved to `SessionCatalog`'s impl doc; **see U123 for why that is recorded there**. **Also from the review, dispositioned rather than fixed:** `CallerCaps`'s doc claimed `Default` \"grants both\" where the code derives `{false, false}` — on the §8.4 capability boundary, and mutation-proved load-bearing, so a future engineer aligning the *code* to that comment would have made `Default` the extension exemption and defeated §7.7's \"cannot gate a browser\" for any connection built from it; the asker-woken-before-`Ack` inversion is **U138** (M1.9's); the resumed session's unseeded controller cache is **U139** (M1.6's); and debt **d5** was discharged with a documented deviation — both *module-level* exemptions are deleted and `cargo check -p dacode-core --lib` is warning-free, but via **four narrow item-level** exemptions each naming a remover, because the modules' first production caller is C2 while individual items stay unused until M1.5/M1.7/M1.9/M2. The two item-level attributes that guarded nothing on their own were **removed** rather than kept: an exemption that is individually redundant reads as load-bearing to whoever deletes it next. **The three new modules are `pub(crate)` with a scoped exemption naming M3 as remover — debt d6** — because the alternative was widening `Session` back to `pub`, which both C1 reviewers had demanded against for a stated safety reason (a `pub Session` lets any dependent crate `session.log.take()` and drop the writer, turning M8's \"call `shutdown`, not drop\" into a workspace-wide review obligation). Design §4.1's \"the registry is the only thing `dacode-server` and the TUI touch\" is satisfied at **M3**, by widening the *registry* alone or by an opaque handle — a choice belonging to the row that has a caller. **Not verified, plainly:** no cross-compile build (**U14 stays open**); the three spawned-child tests ran on darwin only; **no assertion anywhere is timeout-bounded**, because tokio's `time` is M1.5's (§7.8 Q1), so every cross-task wait is a bounded yield loop whose bound appears in its failure message; R25/R26/R27 remain unpinned by declaration (U137 for the first two; R27's `biased` has **no** deterministic assertion, which the primary established by working the distribution rather than trusting the comment — without `biased` the extra commands processed are geometric with p=0.5, so \"≤1 record after `SessionClosed`\" holds ~25% of runs under the mutant at *any* queue depth); `SessionCreated` is deliberately not emitted at open and `SessionInfo.model` stays `None`, both because U115's string → `(provider, model)` join has no specified derivation *(✅ the **string → pair** half is now specified and built — doc 13 §13.7, `resolve.rs`; what remains unspecified is the **pair → string** inverse, which is the only reason this `None` still stands)*. **M1.9** inherits U130 + U131, §7.10 item 4's full crash form, **U134** (the two-token decision) and **U138** (the asker-woken-before-`Ack` inversion); **M1.6** inherits **U139** (seeding the controller cache from a reopened log's fold); **M3** inherits U132, **U137** (the two unpinned replay cancel races, and the `replay_tests.rs` split that must precede them) and U123's mechanism","status":"open","verify":"dual review ✅ A1, A2, t219, Pass B, C1, C2","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"M1.4","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U179","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E2","kind":"gate","title":"M1.5","body":"`dacode-providers`: `anthropic` + `openai-compat`, capabilities as values on `ModelInfo` rather than branches in §4.2, the `ToolDialect` reserved slot, B5's one XML grammar with stream holdback, and **`ExchangeRecord` emission** — an M1 deliverable by B11's signed condition, so the records exist before M5's analyzer is written rather than being retrofitted onto exchanges nobody recorded. ✅ **Its doc pre-flight landed 2026-09-24 (M1.1d) and this cell is now a pointer rather than an instruction: [13](part3-capabilities/13-providers.md) is the row's spec.** Both Ask-First gates are ruled — **g84** the hyper stack with bundled roots (+29 packages, zero new class-1 markers, bans clean, `reqwest` rejected on two measured policy costs), **g85** `CompletionTool` replacing §6's `Vec<ToolSpec>` so the row no longer depends on a 🧠-gated type — and the gate rows it owes are tabulated at doc 13 §13.2 (the `ring` allowlist row, §F.3's flagship update, `deny.toml`'s **CDLA-Permissive-2.0** entry, **no** `class1-exempt.txt` row, **no** `wrappers` entry, boundary edges **27 → 38 predicted**). ~~⚠️ **U140 is open and is the reason doc 13 keeps its depth marker** — `ModelInfo.window` for an untabled model — but it **does not block this row**, because `complete()` never reads `window`; only `models()` does, and M1.6 after it.~~ *(superseded at t255, 2026-09-25: U140 is ruled **and landed**, and doc 13's marker flipped in that same change — see the ✅ clause below. The \"does not block this row\" half was true and stays true as history.)* Also carried by doc 13 rather than here: the egress seam split with M1.7 (§13.5), U115's derivation and its three-sample correction (§13.7), tokio `time`'s zero-package cost and the ten loops it retires (§13.8), the local-listener test stack (§13.9), and §14's **six** provider tests with the two ordering-property ones named (§13.10). ✅ **Pass A LANDED 2026-09-24** (tracker t247/t252) — the crate's foundation and its real dependency set, under dual review: **secondary APPROVE at round 1** with a 74-row transcription table over doc 13 §§13.1–13.13 and zero findings; **primary REQUEST CHANGES (round 1: 4 MED / 3 LOW / 3 NIT) → REQUEST CHANGES (round 2: one MED and one NIT — this cell's own stale file/test counts, and the `registry.rs:507-511` cite the fix round had just invalidated) → REQUEST CHANGES (round 3: one MED and two LOW, all three inside the orchestrator's own board cell, zero crate changes) → APPROVE at round 4, no blocker remaining from any of the four rounds**. ⚠️ Rounds 2–4 carried **zero crate changes** — that is the fact that makes four rounds cheap rather than alarming: the code was settled at round 2 and the last two rounds were the orchestrator's prose., its four MEDs being two *stale-truth* sites (a refusal comment and a doc-07 sentence whose stated reason the pass itself had just discharged) and two credential-parser invariant gaps, **all nine items DISCHARGED at round 2 and mutation-proved** (4 mutations, 4 killed by named tests) — including the regression the orchestrator specifically asked it to hunt: `is_bare_ident` **does** admit `-` and `_`, so `[openai-compat]` — §6's own provider id — still parses, and a twelve-spelling sweep of the new predicate's edges found every one refused loudly rather than silently, and it **retracted its own initial HIGH** as self-inflicted (an unlocked `cargo tree` during a mutation rewrote the tracked `Cargo.lock` — P-LIVE-002 with a new artifact class). Five new files (`credentials` 660, `model` 524, `error` 290, `lib` 225, `resolve` 204 — 1 903 lines, all under the 1 000 cap though `credentials.rs` is over the 600 target), **38 tests**, workspace **334 → 372**, `dacode-protocol` still **121** and **byte-identical to HEAD**, corpus sha256 unchanged, boundary diff **empty at 38 exactly as §13.2 predicted**, `Cargo.lock` **106 → 137** (+31 lines / +29 distinct names — the gap is `ring`'s second versions of `getrandom` 0.2 and `windows-sys` 0.52, invisible to a name-subtraction), **one** class-1 attribution (`cc ← ring`, allowlist row landed, introducer never `cc`), zero `unsafe` with `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]`. **Four things Pass A established that the plan doc got wrong, all corrected in doc 13 rather than smoothed**: §13.2's duplicate-name parenthetical named `base64`, which is a duplicate in the *scratch probe's* deny output and not in this lock (`syn` + `windows-sys` are the real two); §13.7's sample-correction assignment had an **empty executable set** — the four sites are `Event::samples()` macro arms that *generate* six goldens, proved by mutation rather than argument, so the ruling is now \"leave all twelve alone\" with the corpus-re-bless cost that a full sweep would carry recorded; §13.13's `unsafe` gate needed the board's precise `rg` form because a bare `rg 'unsafe'` matches the `forbid` attribute that satisfies it; and §13.2's gate table omitted the **two `deny.toml` `[bans] skip` entries** `ring`'s own pins force. **Also ruled and landed**: `ModelRef` is single and providers-owned; §6's `StreamChunk::ToolCallArgs.delta` was **`RawJson`, which cannot hold a partial JSON fragment** — corrected in the spec to `String` with a do-not-fix-it-back note, and *not* a compatibility event precisely because §13.3's placement ruling made `StreamChunk` non-wire-visible; `#[async_trait]` is sketch shorthand and the trait is hand-desugared on `store/traits.rs`'s precedent, dyn-compatibility and `Send` both tested. ⚠️ **Rounds 2 and 3 both found defects in this cell, and both were the orchestrator's own — the f113/f117/U119b/f151 recorded-count class arriving again** — ⚠️ *unnumbered deliberately*: two prior sites already claim a \"fifth\" for different events ([33](part6-quality/33-benchmarking.md):321 and this file's own M1.1c cell), so an ordinal here would be a third competing count of a set no reader can reconstruct, and a recurrence number that cannot be reproduced is the same defect one level up. Pass B round 3's unnumbered \"arriving again\" is the form to reuse: **round 2's** MED was that this cell's first draft carried the *pre-fix-round* file counts and test totals, written before the last edit; **round 3's** MED was that the correction of those counts then missed a sibling numeral ~400 characters later in the same cell (the f208 clause's `549`, superseded by 660) — so the sweep followed the edit, twice, and the second instance arrived inside the sentence apologising for the first. Round 2's cause was P-LIVE-015's rule (\"measure after the last edit\") violated in a session that recorded two *new* `.agents/pitfalls-live.md` entries — P-LIVE-018 and P-LIVE-019, neither of them this class — while the entry that *does* cover it was already on the books from M1.4 Pass B (2026-09-23). That is the sharper form of the point rather than a softer one: **the rule predated the session instead of being written in it**, and was violated anyway, which is why the numbers above are the re-measured ones. **The no-amend rule makes a wrong count permanent once committed**, and a future pass derives its delta from it. ~~**Open, not blocking**: **U140** (so no model table, no window, no price is seeded anywhere in production code), **f208's** question of whether the hand-rolled `credentials.toml` subset parser should be replaced by a TOML crate at M2 (`toml_edit` is already blessed pure-Rust in §F.1, so the policy cost is zero and the trade is **660** lines of interim code), and~~ *(both closed at t255 — U140 ruled and landed, f208 answered by landing `toml_edit`; the no-seeded-product-data property **survives** the ruling and still holds, since `window` abstains rather than guessing. What remains open is)* **f207-c** — Pass D must choose between `dacode-json`'s write tier (~~38~~ **39** edges stand since t255's `toml_edit` row) and a `serde` derive (~~39~~ **40**) for `ExchangeRecord` and re-measure. ✅ **Both of the row's remaining user questions were ruled later the same day, so Passes B–E open with nothing pending on the user.** **U140 → arm (c)**, derived as `ModelInfo.window: Option<u32>` rather than a fallback number, on three spec facts (doc 13 §13.11 and the §6 entry both carry them): §6's own `supports_cache_breakpoints` *\"false ⇒ inert, not an error\"* idiom, A4's compaction-off-by-default, and §6's existing overflow path — a fallback would make `window` the one capability in that struct that **lies rather than abstains**. **f208 → `toml_edit`** replaces `credentials.rs`'s 660-line hand-rolled TOML subset, measured first (+7 packages default / **+6** parse-only, zero markers, `MIT OR Apache-2.0` so no license row, and its `serde` feature is **off by default** — which is why this crate and not `toml`, since `toml` would have forced a `serde` edge and broken §13.2's no-serde property). It also **deletes a defect class rather than patching it**: the two silent-`CredentialsMissing` spellings the review found are correct by construction under a real parser. ✅ **Both rulings LANDED at t255 (2026-09-25)** — one change under dual review, taking the boundary count **38 → 39** (doc 01 §1.6's verbatim diff re-run, **empty at 39**, 11 comment lines kept) and, because U140's Verify clause demands simultaneity, **flipping doc 13's depth marker and dropping §4's count 24 → 23 in that same change**. What landed: design §6's `pub window: Option<u32>` with the inert-not-error precedent cited in its trailing comment, §4.5's trigger sentence gained the *pointer* clause (no fraction to take when the window is `None`; §6's overflow path is the remedy — a pointer, not a compaction policy, which stays M1.6's), `model.rs`'s field/docs and the new `window: None` abstention test (`None ≠ Some(0)`, no default in the struct), `credentials.rs` rewritten on `toml_edit` parse-only under tracker g88's three-way discriminator — the hand-rolled subset's `is_bare_ident`/`parse_section_header`/`parse_quoted`/`seen_sections` machinery deleted, the quoted-key and single-quote/escape refusals **re-pointed to acceptance** (the parser implements TOML's grammar, so the refusal reason died), `[p]]` re-pointed to toml_edit's own parse error, the unknown-bare-key ignore KEPT (M1.11 forward-compat), and the never-log policy hardened — toml_edit's error `Display` quotes the offending line, so `problem` strings stay module-authored and only the span's line survives. Re-measured after the last edit: `dacode-providers` **41 tests** (38 at Pass A — ~~none deleted; three added~~ ⚠️ **corrected at review round 1: two DELETED and five ADDED, net +3**, `credentials` 14 → 16 and `model` 9 → 10, enumerated by counting `#[test]`/`#[tokio::test]` fns HEAD vs tree rather than by intent. **The code is right and the orchestrator's ruling was wrong**: tracker g88 said both \"no test is deleted\" and \"several change which side they assert\", and those two are in tension, because a test's *name* is its claim — `legal_toml_this_reader_does_not_speak_is_malformed_not_a_silent_miss` asserting that the quoted key now **resolves** would be a lie in the shape of a test. Coverage strictly **increased**: every refusal spelling survives in the replacement pair, two rows moved to acceptance exactly as g88's own case 3 directs, and the new suite asserts the **exact** line where the old asserted only `line >= 1`. Recorded as tracker f216, P-LIVE-018 arriving a second time inside one session — in the ruling written *about* that failure mode), file line counts after the round-1 fix `credentials` **695**, `model` 556, `error` 292, `lib` 229, `resolve` 204 (1 976 total; `credentials.rs` is over the 600 target and under the 1 000 cap, and its production half **shrank** 374 → 336 while its tests grew — the drift is coverage), workspace **372 → 375**, `dacode-protocol` still **121**, porcelain-clean, corpus sha256 unchanged (`c3b517c3…7097f5`), `Cargo.lock` **137 → 143 package entries / 133 → 139 distinct names (+6: `equivalent`, `hashbrown`, `indexmap`, `toml_datetime`, `toml_edit`, `winnow` — parse-only, no `toml_write`, no new duplicate-version)**, **zero** new class-1 markers and **no** `deny.toml`/`class1-allowlist`/`class1-exempt` edits (dep-audit, `--self-test`, `cargo deny` all exit 0), zero new dev-dependencies, zero `unsafe` (board grep form), one `struct ModelRef`. ~~t255's file set is disjoint from Pass B's, so the two can be dispatched in parallel~~ ⚠️ **(falsified pre-dispatch by tracker f213 — the shared file is `lib.rs`, which both passes write, and the derived artifacts `Cargo.lock`/`boundary-edges.txt` are what Pass B re-measures; dispatched sequentially, t255 first.)** **Remaining: Passes B–E** (Pass B **split 2026-09-25 on the user's ruling** into **t256** = `sse.rs` + the shared test responder and **t257** = `transport.rs` + the egress seam + retry, **sequential** because both write `lib.rs`; t249 the two `dto` adapters, t250 dialect+exchange, t251 the `dacode-core` bounded-yield retirement). ⚠️ **The split's seam is cross-task ordering, not size, and the size argument was measured and rejected**: Pass B's reading load is ~3 000–3 500 lines against `tasks.py budget`'s 500k work budget (roughly a tenth), and Pass A carried a comparable load *unsplit* with its code settling at round 2. ~~What the split buys is that P-LIVE-016's REPEAT/CONTENTION gates land on **t257 alone**, so t256 gets an ordinary review and t257 one that can be about concurrency~~ *(superseded at B1's landing by tracker f223, in this same cell's ✅ clause below: the f184 derivation put §13.9's responder — which B1 owns — on the list, so the gates ran at B1 too. What the split still buys is narrower and real: B2's concurrency is in **production** code, the retry path and the streaming pump §13.13's item 10 names, where B1's is confined to a test double)*; what it costs is two dispatches, two reviews and two `lib.rs` edits — **more** rounds in total, so a split is not a remedy for a spent orchestrator budget. Not four passes, because retry cannot ship apart from the transport that exposes it (`lib.rs`'s `Provider::complete` doc makes retry that method's contract, never the caller's — cited by anchor rather than line, because B1's own `lib.rs` growth moved it: f224) and the seam cannot ship apart from the connector it guards. `t248` is now the decomposition record, on the `t246` precedent. ✅ **Pass B1 LANDED 2026-09-26** (tracker t256) — `sse.rs` and doc 13 §13.9's shared test responder, under dual review: **secondary APPROVE at round 1** (a 14-row transcription table over doc 13 §§13.1–13.13 and design §6, one LOW and two NITs); **primary REQUEST CHANGES at round 1** (3 MED / 4 LOW / 4 NIT, **32 mutations — 26 killed by named tests, 6 survivors all explained**, and an explicit verdict that the holdback algorithm and the SSE decoder are *correct*) **→ REQUEST CHANGES at round 2** (5 MED / 2 LOW, and ⚠️ **every one of the seven was in the orchestrator's own fix-round prose, zero in the delivered code** — a falsified survey count, a line-count stale by 2 inside the paragraph correcting a falsified estimate, three asserted comparisons against predictions that exist nowhere, two board line-cites invalidated by this pass's own doc edits, and two panic messages naming causes that cannot occur) **→ round 3** (all seven discharged and verified true, every board number reproduced independently, and **one new MED** — `exchange`'s timeout message named a cause the reviewer then measured to be impossible on this host, i.e. round 2's defect class in the sibling helper, plus a LOW whose replacement wording claimed exhaustiveness while missing two reachable causes) **→ round 4, narrow** (both code edits discharged with **all six panic arms driven by probe**, and **two further MEDs, both the orchestrator's counts gone stale again** because the round-3 message fixes grew the files *after* the counts were written — 672/383 against a committing tree of 682/386, and a `lib.rs:103-108` cite that B1's own growth had moved to `:109-116`). ⚠️ **Four rounds, and rounds 2–4 found nothing in the delivered code.** Every finding from round 2 onward was orchestrator prose or an orchestrator measurement, which is the pattern worth carrying rather than the round count: the code was settled at round 1 and the last three rounds were a reviewer re-measuring the orchestrator's claims about it. **f224 is that finding**, and its corrective habit — a line cite is written last or re-grepped last, and a section reference survives an edit where a line does not — is why this cell's retry cite is now an anchor. ⚠️ **B1's implementing session was INTERRUPTED mid-run and the orchestrator finished it.** The artifact was a ~74-line block of test helpers written twice into `sse_holdback_tests.rs` (seven `E0428`s), which the compiler caught on the first gate run. **The generalisable half is that a compiler catches duplication and nothing catches deletion**: both reviewers were told, and both checked — the primary enumerated all seven helpers as present exactly once *and* load-bearing (each killed a mutant), and swept for duplicate fn names across all three test files. **The first act on resuming an interrupted tree is a build, not a review dispatch.** What landed: **5 new files / 2 384 lines** (`sse` 571, `sse_frame_tests` 324, `sse_holdback_tests` 421, `test_responder` 682, `test_responder_tests` 386 — largest 682, under the 1 000 cap and over the 600 target), the crate **5 files / 1 976 lines → 10 / 4 403**, tests **41 → 74** (+33: frame 14, holdback 10, responder 9; the five pre-existing files' counts unchanged at 16/6/2/10/7, enumerated by counting `#[test]`/`#[tokio::test]` fns HEAD vs tree rather than by intent), workspace **375 → 408**, `dacode-protocol` still **121** and porcelain-clean, corpus sha256 unchanged (`c3b517c3…7097f5`), **zero new dependencies and zero manifest edits** (`Cargo.lock` untouched at **143** entries / **139** names, `gates/`/`deny.toml`/root `Cargo.toml` untouched, boundary diff **empty at 39**), **zero dev-dependencies** as §13.9 rules, zero `unsafe` (board grep form) with `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` still at `lib.rs:43`. ⚠️ **Three things this pass settled or opened that a cold session must not get wrong.** **(1) The holdback seam was ruled before dispatch, not discovered in it (tracker g90)**: doc 13 §13.1's layout line puts \"§6's stream holdback\" in `sse.rs` while §6's third dialect bullet puts it inside \"one XML grammar\", which is `dialect.rs` — Pass D's file. Ruled: **`sse.rs` owns the mechanism, `dialect.rs` the grammar**, so the delimiter set is a **parameter** and no tag literal appears in the parser's production half (the only byte literals there are the BOM and SSE's own `data`/`event` field names). That is grounded in a spec fact rather than taste: **§6 specifies no tag spelling anywhere**, and U140's surviving principle is that nothing in this crate's production code seeds product data nobody chose. **(2) The hole behind that ruling is U144, filed pre-dispatch rather than at Pass D** — the grammar's tag set has no owner and no source, so `dialect.rs` cannot be written without ruling it, and the arm that edits design §6 is **Ask-First** (the g85 shape). g90's parameterization is what makes the deferral cost B1 nothing. **(3) The split's own recorded reason is false (tracker f223)**: this cell and both children's titles said P-LIVE-016's REPEAT/CONTENTION gates \"land on t257 alone\", and the f184 derivation the nodes themselves mandated falsified it — §13.9's responder **spawns** its accept task (`test_responder.rs:224`) and **awaits a channel that task feeds** (`:256`), two of f184's four criteria, in the module B1 owns. The gates were therefore run here: orchestrator **560/0** pre-review, **400/0** after review round 1's fixes and **300/0** (REPEAT 200 at 1 worker + CONTENTION 4 × 25) on the **final** tree; the review round independently ran **560/0** on the tree as delivered and **300/0** on the final one — floors 99.99 %/93.96 %/42.90 % at p = 1.6 %/0.5 %/0.1 % at N = 560, read as \"not detected at this N\". ⚠️ The four re-runs are not padding: the responder changed between each, and P-LIVE-016's gates are owed against the tree being committed (P-LIVE-015). **The split still stands on a narrower reason** — B2 carries the retry/backoff path and the streaming pump, which §13.13's item 10 names as M1.5's two qualifiers, and B1's concurrency is confined to a *test double* rather than to production code. Also opened: **U143** (the responder gives no *temporal* arrival separation — tokio's `TcpStream::poll_flush` is a no-op and every test driving it is a bare current-thread `#[tokio::test]`, so a client reads a whole multi-element body in **one** read; §6's `ExchangeRecord.first_byte_ms` is Pass D's and the streaming pump is t257's, and the fix is a test that *asserts* the separation, mutation-proved), and **d8**/**d9** (no length cap in the decoder — the bound belongs on t257's body reader, and a cap inside the decoder would need a number nothing specifies; one request per connection, which is what makes the queue order the request order). ⚠️ **The review round retracted its own R11 finding and the retraction is the useful half**: the surviving \"delete the per-write flush\" mutant was not a coverage gap but an **equivalent** mutant, because `poll_flush` returns `Ready(Ok(()))` unconditionally — so its survival was *evidence for* the prose defect, and the correct resolution was to delete the no-op (done) and file the property it was pretending to provide (U143). **Two findings were declined and both declinations were upheld by measurement**: the five `pub use sse::{…}` re-exports stay `pub`, because narrowing to `pub(crate)` produces **17 warnings** (1 unused-import + 16 `dead_code`) against §13.13's zero-warning gate — this repo's d4/d5/d6 shape a fourth time — and buys no invariant, these being pure byte transforms with no state a caller can corrupt, unlike d6's `Session`; and the responder's `#[path] mod tests;` carries no `#[cfg(test)]` because the file is reachable only through the crate root's own gate, on M1.4 Pass C2's precedent for deleting an attribute that guards nothing (documented at the declaration site rather than left as an inconsistency). **Doc edits this pass made**: §13.9's falsified 60–100-line estimate corrected with the measurement *and* an attribution of where the lines went (tracker f219), its \"the same trade this repo has already made twice\" → **three**; §13.13's item 10 gains the test-double qualifier, the counts with their provenance, and the size-REPEAT-by-connections rule (f220: the host's loopback `TIME_WAIT` ceiling is ~20 000 sockets, **not** the 16 384-port ephemeral range, because both endpoints are loopback so the 4-tuple binds — a burst past it fails with `AddrNotAvailable` on `connect` and reads exactly like a defect in the code under test); all three §13.14 cells. **Owed and scheduled, not forgotten**: `crates/dacode-providers` has **no `AGENTS.md`** and now meets skill-base's propose-it condition twice over (10 files / 4 390 lines), so tracker **t258** carries it to M1.5's end beside the \"new provider\" pattern article — a doc pass does not ride along in a code tree. **Not verified, plainly**: no provider call has ever been made — B1 proves a parser and a double, not a client, and \"works as an LLM client\" stays unmeasured until t257 and Pass C exist; no cross-compile build (**U14**, now with a third subject in nothing — B1 added no dependency); the two `debug_assert!` invariants added at review round 1 are debug-only by design, so a release build still degrades to the silent-`None` path they guard *(at B2's landing this narrows rather than closes: a **client** now exists and is proved against a local double, but still no call to a real endpoint has ever been made — **d13** carries that, and \"works as an LLM client\" stays unmeasured until Pass C's adapters exist)*. ✅ **Pass B2 LANDED 2026-09-27** (tracker t257, fix round t259) — `transport.rs`: the hyper/rustls client, doc 13 §13.5's guarded-resolver egress seam, §6's retry with jitter over an **abort-aware** sleep, the context-overflow case, §14's provider test #5 against a **test-supplied** predicate, and the streaming pump §13.13's item 10 names as an M1.5 qualifier. Under dual review: **primary REQUEST CHANGES at round 1** (4 MED / 3 LOW / 3 NIT; it ran 13 of its own mutations and killed 11, re-verified every third-party citation, and **upheld** the worker's refusal to add a second egress enforcement point) **→ APPROVE at round 2**, conditional on three MEDs that were all prose and all the orchestrator's; **secondary REQUEST CHANGES at round 1** (F1/F2/F5 comment precision + F3/F4/F6 doc consistency, over a clause-by-clause transcription table) **→ REQUEST CHANGES at round 2** (D1/D2, both orchestrator prose) **→ round 3 narrow** (one MED, F-R3, orchestrator prose again). ⚠️ **B1's pattern repeated exactly: the code settled at round 1 and every finding from round 2 onward was prose** — seven of them, six in the orchestrator's doc/board text and one in a knowledge entry's Scope line, zero in the delivered code. **Two worker declines were upheld on measurement, and both are worth more than the fixes** (P-LIVE-020's discipline run from the worker's side of the dispatch): (i) the prescribed `timeout(IO_BOUND, …)` around the pump's `.collect()` was measured **ineffective** — the mutant's spin is *inside a single poll* (every poll `Ready(None)`, never `Pending`), tokio cannot preempt a task, and on a current-thread runtime a task that never parks starves the timer driver that would fire the bound; the primary confirmed the root cause from tokio's own source (`Timeout::poll` polls the inner future first; the current-thread scheduler advances the time driver in `park_yield` *after* the ready queue drains) and re-measured the mutant dying by assertion in 12.6 s. The replacement is an **OS-timer observer**: the exchange runs on a dedicated `std::thread` with its own hand-built current-thread runtime, bounded from outside by `mpsc::recv_timeout` — which deliberately inverts §13.8's rule-3 warning, because there the `std` thread was the defect and here immunity to the observed *is* the requirement (**P-LIVE-021**); (ii) the premise behind the `select`-order finding was **false** on tokio 1.53.1 — `deadline_to_tick` rounds a deadline *up* to the end of a millisecond and the wheel fires at insertion only when the tick is already elapsed, so a sleep polled in the same expression that constructed it is `Pending` (measured 0/200 000 Ready for both a zero and a 1 ms delay). The swap landed anyway as **labelled hardening** with the refutation recorded in the test's doc, and the revert mutant is declared **equivalent** (survives 106/106) while a zero-skip-shortcut mutant is killed only by the new test — declaring an equivalent mutant *and* keeping the change being the better of the two options, since the rounding it would otherwise rest on is a third-party detail. **What landed**: 5 new files / **2 223 lines** (`transport` 680, `transport_retry_tests` 527, `transport_stream_tests` 480, `transport_egress_tests` 322, `transport_support` 214), `error.rs` **319 → 600** (five new variants — `HttpStatus`, `Exchange`, `EgressRefused`, `ContextOverflow`, `Cancelled` — plus one `retryable()` predicate behind both the backoff loop and `Event::Error{retryable}`), `lib.rs` 245 → 253, and `test_responder.rs` 682 → 684 for a **prose-only** reword of its U143 paragraph (B2's observer thread falsified \"every test driving this double is a bare `#[tokio::test]`\"; the *property* survives because that runtime is current-thread too). Crate **10 files / 4 403 lines → 15 / 6 917**; tests **74 → 106** (+32), workspace **408 → 440**; `dacode-protocol` still **121** and porcelain-clean; corpus sha256 unchanged (`c3b517c3…7097f5`); **zero new dependencies and zero manifest edits** (`Cargo.lock` untouched at **143** entries / **139** names, `gates/`/`deny.toml`/both manifests untouched, boundary diff **empty at 39**); zero dev-dependencies — on the **anchored** grep, because the loose form matches this manifest's own `# NO [dev-dependencies]` comment (**f225**, P-LIVE-019's shape in a gate pattern); zero `cfg_attr(not(test), …)` exemptions; zero `unsafe` on §13.13 item 12's **escaped** form, which the same item now carries because the unescaped brace the board and the doc both used exits **2** with a regex parse error rather than passing. **The seam** is a trait, `SseChunkMap`, so the byte/chunk plumbing is here and every event meaning is Pass C's — grep-verified that `transport.rs`'s *code* carries no provider id, event name or tag literal (its doc comments name providers as examples, which is what F5 corrected the claim to say). **g92's pre-dispatch ruling held**: no invented byte cap, and the five numbers/seams the spec owes instead of the code are filed as **U145–U149** with four debts (**d10**–**d13**) re-owned onto them. **The concurrency gates were derived, not inherited, and the derivation under-reported** (**f230**): the prescribed grep returns **zero** hits for `transport.rs`, yet every `send()` spawns connection-driver tasks because `TokioExecutor::execute` *is* `tokio::spawn` — a spawned task living in a *dependency*, which f184's four criteria cannot see. f223 was the derivation finding more than predicted; this is it finding **less**, the dangerous direction, and §13.13's item 10 now carries the widening grep. Counts, sized by **connections** (~40 per suite run, 35 s drains, `TIME_WAIT` peak 7 976 against the ~20 000 ceiling): worker **300/0** on the final tree, primary **400/0** on the delivered one (floors 99.8 %/86.5 %/33.0 % at p = 1.6 %/0.5 %/0.1 % — \"not detected at this N\"), secondary a reduced **100/0**. **Doc edits this pass made**: §13.5's annotation of the design §14 quotation it repeats (a plan doc may annotate a quotation of the spec, not rewrite it — and the *narrowing* is therefore the user's, arm (c) of U149 being two spec sentences, §4.4's and §14's); §13.2's `rustls` row carving **ALPN** out of \"the `ClientConfig` is ours\", because `hyper-rustls` asserts it empty and rewrites it (**f229** — pre-setting `[\"http/1.1\"]` panics at every client construction, so HTTP/1-only is expressed by leaving ALPN empty, the stronger property); §13.13's items 10 and 12; §13.14's Open cell swept for U145–U149 (**P-LIVE-006 in reverse** — the items were filed and the doc's own summary was not told); TODO §6's U143 and U147 corrections. ⚠️ **The round-2/3 finding worth carrying is f234**: three sites attributed an Ollama base URL of `http://127.0.0.1:11434` to design §6, which names **no URL spelling** (`11434` appears zero times in the spec) — and the invented spelling was load-bearing, because Ollama's own default `localhost` **is** a hostname and **is** guarded by the landed seam while a literal is not, so the false claim inflated the very gap it was filed to report. The conclusion survived; the recorded reason did not, which is P-LIVE-014's shape exactly. Beside it, §13.13 item 10's new clause was falsified **by this pass's own fix round** (a `spawn_blocking` observer added after the count was written), and its correction then miscounted a second time by counting `test_responder.rs` as one of the three transport test files — a grep count written beside its own correction inherits none of its verification, so the fix states the result **per file** rather than as a total. **Owed and scheduled, not forgotten**: `crates/dacode-providers` still has **no `AGENTS.md`** and now meets skill-base's propose-it condition three times over (15 files / 6 917 lines), so **t258** still carries it to M1.5's end beside the \"new provider\" pattern article. **Not verified, plainly**: no provider call has ever been made against a real endpoint (**d13**) — no real `getaddrinfo`, no TLS handshake, no ALPN on the wire, and `Transport::new(GaiResolver)` is proved to *construct* (a real fact: `with_safe_default_protocol_versions()` is fallible) and never to connect; `AbortSignal`'s `Send`-ness against a real `tokio_util` token is unmeasured and labelled so (**U148** — `tokio-util` is not an edge here); **no streaming test observes incremental arrival**, so **U143** stands and B2's pump tests assert the chunk *sequence* only, with one test existing to make that framing-insensitivity explicit; connection reuse is not asserted (**d9** stands, the double was not extended *(for reuse — at g96 it gained a `StallPoint` extension for stalled responses, which holds one request per connection and queue order, and reuse is still not asserted)*; the IP-literal egress path is untested because it is unguarded (**d10**/U149), and no test uses a literal-host URI so none can pass on it by accident; and a server that accepts and never answers still holds `send` open indefinitely (**d11**/U147) *(closed at g96 the same day — the ✅ clause below: `exchange_timeout` now bounds exactly that server, mutation-proved)*. **Remaining: Passes C (t249), D (t250) and E (t251)** — with **U144** (the XML grammar's tag set) owed before `dialect.rs` can be written ~~and **U146** on M1's critical path, because M1.9's loop must construct a `RetryPolicy` to call a provider at all while the row that owns those four numbers as config is M2's~~ *(U146 closed the same day it was filed — see the ✅ clause below; what Pass C still owes is d12's second half, a config surface to override the default, and that is doc 17's at M2)*. ✅ **U146 RULED BY THE USER AND LANDED 2026-09-27** (tracker g94, the row's second user ruling of the day after B2's five filings) — §6's four backoff numbers are **`retries: 2` (three total attempts) · `base: 500 ms` · `cap: 30 s` · jitter 75–100 % of the computed delay**, carried by `RetryPolicy::default()` so M1.9, Pass C and every test construct the *same* production policy rather than three call sites inventing one each. **The numbers come from a survey of the four reference harnesses in their checkouts beside this repo, not from taste** — §13.2's own method for the dependency set, and the reason U146's \"no number appears in the reference-harness surveys\" was true of the *surveys* and false of the *harnesses*: **two** of four default to three total attempts (openclaw and oh-my-pi), one runs at **eight** (forge — from its embedded `.forge.toml:39-45`, which `read_defaults()` layers unconditionally in production, *not* from the type's derived `Default` of 0 that no shipped run reaches) and one is **unbounded** (opencode's `policy()` is a `Schedule.fromStepWithMetadata` that retries while `retryable()` answers); **three** of four cap at exactly 30 s and all four double. ⚠️ This claim took **three** drafts, each falsified by measurement rather than by argument: the first said \"three of four pick three attempts\"; the second corrected the count but called forge \"disabled-by-default\", which is true of the *type* and false of the *harness*; the third is this one. Naming a value by its role in a mental model (\"the `Default`\") instead of enumerating what actually supplies it is P-LIVE-018's shape arriving inside the correction of the same class. The honest reading — a plurality of two against one at eight and one unbounded — is a **weaker** consensus than either earlier draft claimed, and therefore a better argument for choosing `retries: 2` on dacode's own grounds (a user watches this wait) than for inheriting a number because three harnesses agree on it. Doc 13 §13.8's new subsection carries the table with `path:line` per row, plus the two choices made **against** the survey and why — base 500 ms rather than opencode's 2 s, because opencode's default is tuned for a CLI whose common 429 is a free-tier limit while §6's own gloss on the abort-aware sleep — that it is what makes §4.2's `Cancel` *feel instant* — is a latency promise a long default would undercut, and a user watches this wait (§4.2's own cancellation text makes no latency claim, which is the misquote the review round caught in `transport.rs`'s doc); and `retry-after` staying a **floor bounded by `cap`** rather than opencode's override, because honouring a 90 s header verbatim would exceed the longest wait the caller declared. ⚠️ **Two disclosures rather than a clean number**: the cap is **inert** at the default `retries` (500 ms then 1 s, whole default backoff ≤ 1.5 s), so it is insurance for M2 raising `retries` and is live today only as the clamp on a server's `retry-after` — where the test that walks the steps is a **diagnostic** rather than the assertion that fires, because a mutant shrinking the cap trips the pinned-value test and the step-count assertion first (measured: `cap: 800ms` failed the value test 8/8 and never reached the loop); and **the jitter *source* is the one judgement call inside the ruling**, because §13.2's set carries no RNG and `ring` is not a direct edge (this crate reaches it through `rustls::crypto::ring`), so `ring::rand` would move the pinned boundary count **39 → 40** and is Ask-First. Landed instead: 18 lines over `SystemTime::now().subsec_nanos()` mixed with a process-local `AtomicU32` through an xorshift, on the **f70 ULID fork's** precedent, **not** a CSPRNG and documented as such — adequate because jitter's job is de-synchronisation and the counter is what separates concurrent retries inside one process, which is the case §5.3's parallel ≤ 4 subagents actually produce. The upgrade path is recorded rather than taken: one `ring` edge, zero new packages, M2's call — and it buys **spread quality, not security**, since `JitterFn`'s own doc already rules the source need not be cryptographic. Two measured consequences are disclosed in the function's doc rather than left to be discovered: the output is **quantised to 26 buckets** where oh-my-pi's `Math.random()` and openclaw's symmetric mode are continuous, and four siblings retrying in the same nanosecond get four distinct percentages **79.8 %** of the time against a birthday bound of 78.5 %. ⚠️ **And the clamp has a user-visible cost the first draft did not state**: a server sending `retry-after: 90` on both 429s makes the default wait 2 × 30 s and issue two requests guaranteed to fail again before the error surfaces — up to a minute of a TUI sitting still. Bounded, disclosed, and the arm against it is oh-my-pi's (abandon past the cap, `oneshot-retry.ts:215-218`), which is a one-line change plus a test. **Landed as**: `impl Default for RetryPolicy` + a private `default_jitter`/`jitter_entropy` pair in `transport.rs` (B2's **680** above is history, not the current file — measure it with `wc -l crates/dacode-providers/src/transport.rs` rather than trusting a number here. ⚠️ **This sentence deliberately carries no count, and the reason is f224 arriving six times in one pass**: it said 810, then 847, then 860, then 861, each written last and each falsified by a *later* review round growing the file above it. \"Write the count last or not at all\" is not sufficient when the last edit is not the last round — the corrective that actually holds is B1's anchor form: name the file and the command, and let the committing tree be its own authority. The count that matters is pinned once, below, after the final review round), the struct doc's \"deliberately no `Default`\" paragraph **replaced** rather than left contradicting the impl, and **two** docs corrected that had *asserted* the deferral rather than merely described it — `jitter_decides_the_delay`'s (\"the spec names no fraction and this crate has no randomness source\") and [`JitterFn`]'s own type doc (\"what this file must not do is hand-roll one\"), which the ruling falsified twice over since the landed source *is* hand-rolled. That is P-LIVE-006's shape and it took a sweep to find the second: `rg 'no randomness source|hand-roll|no .*Default.* impl' crates/dacode-providers/src/`. Crate **106 → 110** tests, workspace **440 → 444**, and — measured once after the **final** review round, with both sign-offs already in hand, so nothing is left to move it — `transport.rs` **861** lines, `transport_retry_tests` 533 → **664**, `transport_support` 214 → **215** for its own now-false \"this crate has none\" sentence, crate 15 files / **7 236** total; four new tests pin the values, the 75–100 % band **at both edges**, the variance and the clamp, and the mutation battery ran **40 mutants in total — 20 on the pre-fix tree (8 survivors, three of which the fixes killed) and 20 re-run from a verified-pristine baseline on the committing tree, where the tally is 15 killed / 5 survivors with every survivor accounted for** — the orchestrator's five (identity jitter, constant jitter, scale-*up* past the input, `retries: 4`, clamp removed) plus the three the review round proved were still surviving (the `Default`'s jitter field swapped for the identity, and the band narrowed to `% 25` and `% 12`), all killed and the tree restored md5-identical each time. ⚠️ **The five survivors are the more useful half, and an earlier draft of this sentence reported only the kills.** Three are documented as not load-bearing (dropping the golden-ratio multiply, dropping the zero-seed, dropping the xorshift — the first two measurably change nothing at `% 26`, and the third is annotated in the code as the stage a future edit must not delete). **Two are genuine regressions no test can see**: deleting or freezing the `AtomicU32` counter makes two same-nanosecond calls in one process produce *identical* delays — exactly the §5.3 sibling lockstep jitter exists to break — and both leave the suite 110/110 green. Pinning them would need a clock seam, i.e. production surface this crate does not have, so the defence is a comment in `jitter_entropy` saying *do not delete or freeze this counter* and naming the gap as disclosed rather than absent. That is the honest shape of a mutation tally: this cell's own B1/B2 precedents report survivors with their explanations (\"32 mutations — 26 killed, 6 survivors all explained\"), and reporting 8/8 killed while 5 of 20 survived would have been the f113 scope-stripping class again. ⚠️ **The review round's M11 is the finding worth carrying**: the band/variance test drove the *free function* rather than `(RetryPolicy::default().jitter)(…)`, so a mutant that left `default_jitter` correct and swapped the `Default`'s field for the identity survived 110/110 — §6's \"with jitter\" quietly unimplemented in the production policy with the whole suite green. A test must travel the path a caller travels. **Zero new dependencies and zero manifest edits**, `Cargo.lock` untouched at 143/139, boundary diff **empty at 39**. What the ruling does **not** close: ~~**U147** (no request-exchange timeout — a *different* number, still nobody's) and~~ **U148** (`complete()` has no channel for §4.2's `Cancel`); **d12** narrows to its second half. *(U147 closed hours later under g96 — the ✅ clause below.)* ✅ **U145/U147 RULED BY THE USER AND LANDED 2026-09-27** (tracker g96, the row's third ruling of the day, under the delegation *\"some params can be internal, some user-defined config (anyway, for user-defined config we should have internal defaults). Pick the best option.\"*) — the three transport bounds land as **`TransportLimits` with a `Default` in a new `limits.rs`**, d12's shape exactly, so doc 17 *substitutes* values and never restructures; **not** a `RetryPolicy` sibling, which U147's own arm text contemplated and g96 rejects — a retry policy governs *how many times we try*, a limits struct *how long one try may take*, and folding the second into the first would make `RetryPolicy::default()` the place a reader looks for a byte cap. **`exchange_timeout` 120 s** (request → headers, around each `send` attempt) · **`stream_idle_timeout` 300 s** (inter-chunk gap, restarted per arrival in the pump) · **`max_frame_bytes` 16 MiB** (per **undispatched** SSE frame, on a new `SseDecoder::buffered()` the pump reads after every chunk) — and **deliberately no total-body cap**, the five-harness survey's strongest finding: **zero of five** cap a general LLM response body, and a total-body cap on a stream truncates a legitimate long completion; the de-facto answer everywhere is the idle timeout, which is why (2) exists and why (1)+(2) together bound every stall — the argument that retires **d11** whole rather than half. The numbers were surveyed from the **five** reference harnesses on U146's method, and **every citation was re-verified in the resolved checkouts before landing (P-LIVE-014) — three of the ruling's own citations needed correcting**: the self-hosted constant is `SELF_HOSTED_LLM_IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS` (not the ruling's `SELF_HOSTED_DEFAULT_…` spelling), oh-my-pi's per-event timer restart is at `idle-iterator.ts:270`/`:424` (not `:27-28`, which is env resolution), and openclaw's no-built-in-default request timeout is a validated fall-through *function* (`resolveModelRequestTimeoutMs`, `provider-transport-fetch.ts:574-587`), not a literal `??` chain. Doc 13 §13.8's new limits subsection carries the corrected table with `path:line` per row. Both of the ruling's honesty labels survive in the field docs: the 120 s cites a **first-event** bound, which fires strictly *later* than a headers bound (generous rather than tight — open-code-review margins its `ResponseHeaderTimeout` past its deadline on the same reasoning, `client.go:46-54,72`), and the 16 MiB cites a **total-body** cap on three hardcoded paths where ours is **per-undispatched-frame**. **The cap's placement was decided by measurement, not by d8's note alone**: a probe over §13.9's double found body chunks are socket-read-sized — **14 chunks, max 286 089 bytes, for a 1 MiB single-write frame** — so d8's suggested per-chunk cap at 16 MiB could **never fire**, and it is the cross-chunk accumulation that the pump now bounds, with transient allocation capped at cap + one chunk. **The internal/user-facing split the delegation asked for**: both timeouts are **user-facing** at doc 17 (a real tuning story — cold local model versus warm cloud API — and openclaw literally tiers its idle value by provider class); `max_frame_bytes` is **internal only and doc 17 should not expose it** (a user who lowers it gets silent truncation reading exactly like a provider bug — U129's shape), which on an all-`pub` `Clone` struct is a **documented recommendation to M2, not a visibility restriction**, stated as such rather than left to look enforced. **Timeouts are not retries**: §6's set is closed (*\"Only 429 and 5xx are retried; anything else surfaces immediately\"*) and a timeout is not a status, so all three new `ProviderError` variants (`ExchangeTimeout`, `StreamIdleTimeout`, `FrameTooLarge`) classify `false` through the *existing* `retryable()` and are pinned there by test. The two timeouts sit on their existing sides of the delivered-output boundary — the exchange timeout pre-headers inside `send`'s loop (a return strands nothing), the idle timeout in the pump *after* chunks reached the caller (a re-issue would duplicate them, the second reason it cannot retry) — and **neither is abort-aware**, disclosed beside U148 rather than silently differing from §6's backoff sleep: covering the in-flight exchange with the signal is mechanically cheap from `send` but *which* signal arrives there is U148's Ask-First question, and the pump needs none because §4.2's drop kills the idle wait itself. **Landed as**: `limits.rs` + the three error variants + `send`/pump enforcement + `Transport::new`/`with_resolver`/`stream_sse` gaining the parameter + `test_responder`'s `StallPoint` extension (d9-disclosed: a stalled response holds its connection and never completes; queue order and one-request-per-connection survive) + **7 new tests**: the three defaults pinned **as data** on U146's split (behaviour tests drive `tight_limits()`, so nothing waits 120 s), each bound **proved to fire** against a stalled double and **mutation-proved** — the exchange mutant dies on its `IO_BOUND` guard at 10.0 s, the idle mutant on its guard at 10.0 s, the cap mutant on the variant assertion at 1.0 s (the idle bound ends the stream instead, which the assertion message names) and on the guard at 10.0 s with both stream-side bounds removed — every restore md5-identical and rebuild-verified; plus `buffered()` pinned in `sse_frame_tests` and both stalled shapes pinned byte-wise in `test_responder_tests`, whose `IO_BOUND` waits prove *absence* (their expiry is the pass path — the inversion is labelled in each doc). **P-LIVE-021 classified all three firing tests to the PARK side** — a silent server leaves a socket read `Pending`, the spin shape is an exhausted body re-polled to `Ready(None)` and a stalled body is the opposite — so runtime timers bound them and no OS-timer observer was needed; **U143 does not apply**: the stall *is* the arrival separation, structural rather than scheduled, so no test here needs `multi_thread` or a timing assertion. `transport.rs`'s \"Deliberately not here\" block had the U145/U147 entries **removed** rather than left contradicting the landed code, and the sibling sweep replaced them with the still-true **no-total-body-cap** entry; `sse.rs`'s no-cap entry, `error.rs`'s header and §13.14's Risks-cell d8 clause were re-pointed in the same change (P-LIVE-006). Crate **110 → 117** tests and workspace **444 → 451**, both measured pre-review — per f224 the committing tree is its own authority, re-measure with `cargo test -p dacode-providers` / `cargo test --workspace` after the final round; `transport.rs`'s line count is likewise an anchor (`wc -l`), deliberately not a number here. **Zero new dependencies, zero manifest edits** (`Cargo.lock` untouched at 143/139, boundary diff empty at 39 — `limits.rs` is a module, not an edge). **d8 and d11 are discharged**; d12's second half now covers both structs at doc 17; **U148/U149 are untouched** — neither is a number. ▶️ **AND THEN, BEFORE THE PASS C DISPATCH COULD GO OUT, DECOMPOSITION FOUND A SECOND FILE-OWNERSHIP CONTRADICTION — f236/g95's class arriving a second time in the same seat, which is the finding worth more than the ruling** (tracker **f238**, ruled **g97**, user-confirmed arm (i) 2026-09-28): **§6's `supports_tools: false` history demotion had no file owner anywhere in the plan**, and §14's provider **test #2** therefore had exactly one claimant — Pass C's `dto/` — whose own governing rule forbade it. Five measurements, all taken from the resolved sources: §6's dialect list carries **exactly five** bullets of which the **second** is the demotion; §13.1's layout line enumerated **four** and claimed \"(§6's five rules)\" by parenthetical; §13.10's test #2 cell named a **milestone and no file** in the same table whose test #3 cell does name `dialect.rs`; `rg 'demot'` across the plan finds **no** owner (the Appendix A/B hits are all about the *capability*); and the mechanism is **unreachable from `dto/`** because `ProviderMessage` carries only `role` and an opaque `content: RawJson`, so stripping `tool_calls` reaches inside provider-shaped bytes — which U150's own verify clause forbids in terms (\"no `dto/` file parses `ProviderMessage.content` … since that would pre-empt (a) by implementing (b)\"). **Dispatching as scoped gave the worker two bad outcomes**: write the defect §13.1 names in terms, or discover mid-pass that its test was unreachable. **RULING (arm i): the demotion is `dialect.rs`'s as §6's fifth dialect rule, and test #2 moves from t249 to t250 exactly as g95 moved test #1** — so **Pass C carries none of §14's six provider tests**, which is a legitimate outcome and not a shortfall (Pass A carried none; Pass B carried only test #5's seam half). **The decisive argument is that arm (i) is U150-agnostic**: the demotion lands in the shared module under *every* U150 arm, so if U150 later rules (a) the escaping and the demotion move to the engine **together** — which is how `model.rs`'s `ProviderMessage` doc already describes them, \"the escaping and demotion rules\" as one class — by substitution rather than rebuild, the same property g95's seam bought. **No spec edit ⇒ an orchestrator ruling, not Ask-First.** The two rejected arms are recorded on g97 rather than smoothed: (ii) `dto/`'s, contradicting §13.1's second layout rule and pre-empting U150's (a); (iii) the *engine's* per §6's \"the engine builds it, the provider only encodes it\", which turns on the **same sentence U150 turns on** and would move a §14 deliverable to a 🧠-gated row with no owner — so it is **deferred into U150, not ruled here**, and its cost if U150 goes that way is one more tracker edit and one more board cell. **THE CAUSE FIX IS THE ENUMERATION, NOT THE RULING**: §13.1's layout line now lists all five rules **in §6's own bullet order** so the count is checkable one-to-one against the spec, and §13.10's test #1 and #2 cells now name their files — because a rule *claimed by a count and named by no list* is precisely how this one stayed unowned through four landed passes, and no gate catches it (f96's class: every hygiene check verifies that references **resolve**, never that a count is **complete**). Also filed **f239**, whose first draft I falsified by measurement before it was acted on and which is worth keeping *because* of the correction: a pending gate is invisible to `tasks.py check` at **any** depth (it emits only BLOCK/OPEN/STAND/DEBT — census on this tree 7 DEBT / 2 STAND / 1 OPEN), but `attention.py` **does** render one as a `vote` — via `parse_tracker()` at `tools/attention.py:599`, gated on the word `user` appearing in title+verify (`_USER_GATE`, `:206`), which is what moved this session's open count **77 → 78**. **The real gap is tree position, not existence**: `parse_tracker()` iterates `data[\"nodes\"]` at `:623` **without recursing into `children`**, and that is the only tracker read in the file — measured, this branch has **45 top-level nodes against 610 recursive**, so **565 nested nodes (92.6 % of the tree) are invisible to it**. Of the five pending gates, **four (g4, g5, g93, g95) sit at depth 1 and all four contain the word \"user\", yet none renders**; only g97 does, and solely because I happened to add it at the root. So a pre-dispatch gate's visibility depends on *where it was added* rather than on whether it needs a decision — and **g93 and g95, the two gates whose entire purpose was to brief the next session before it dispatched Pass C, have never appeared in `attention.py` at all.** ⚠️ **The diagnostic trap is worth more than the bug: `git stash` is not a clean-tree control for anything that reads `.agents/cache/`.** I tried to attribute the 77 → 78 move to my doc edits by stashing them and re-running; the stashed run *still* reported 78, because the tracker is gitignored and survived the stash — so the control measured nothing and would have \"proved\" my doc edits innocent of a change they never caused. Any before/after comparison over a tool that reads gitignored state must vary *that* state, not the tree. Recorded with the counter-argument from the tool's own docstring (rendering every pending task would make `check` permanently red, \"and a gate that is always red gets ignored\") rather than \"fixed\", since changing a gate instrument mid-pass is how f237 happened — and because whatever is done about `attention.py` must **recurse**, which is a bigger change than a pass should make to its own gate mid-flight. ▶️ **AND SIZING THAT DISPATCH FALSIFIED A THIRD THING IN DOC 13 — f240**: §13.1's layout block gave each provider **one file** while the precedent the same doc cites for that boundary is a **directory per provider**, and the citation named a crate with no `dto/` in it at all. Measured in the resolved checkout: forge's `dto/<provider>/` lives at `crates/forge_app/src/dto/` (`anthropic/` 3 182 lines, `openai/` 6 905, `google/` 1 777), *not* at `forge_repo/provider` as the doc said, and the individual files inside run `anthropic/request.rs` 872, `anthropic/response.rs` 881, `openai/response.rs` **1 120** — **at or past this repo's 1 000-line cap before a test is written**, so a layout transcribed from a directory precedent into flat files described something this repo could not build. Corrected to directories with **the cap and the 600-line target as the rule rather than a fixed file count**, plus the structural reason dacode's *request* side is thinner than forge's and it is not optimism: `ProviderMessage.content` is already provider-shaped opaque `RawJson`, so a request DTO embeds those bytes rather than modelling content blocks — the *response* side has no such shortcut. ⚠️ **This milestone keeps finding the defect in a plan doc rather than in the code that followed it** — the class M1.4 Pass A1 named at §5's M1.4 cell (\"the third time\", by its own count, which is *not* re-derived here because the instances since it were written have not been enumerated and a stale ordinal is worse than none), and which U150/g95 then found in the spec and a plan doc *disagreeing with each other*. **What is new about this instance is the mechanism**: a layout transcribed at *summary* stage, where the survey's own granularity was lost and no gate could notice, because **nothing checks that a plan doc's file list is buildable under the repo's own size cap**. No spec edit — §6 says nothing about file layout. Also **f241**, from landing f240's own tracker edit: a `Files owned:[^.]*\\.` sentence-boundary regex stopped at the period *inside* `anthropic.rs`, so a ~1 500-character substitution left the old sentence's tail glued onto the new text in a node the next session reads as authoritative. Caught by re-reading from disk and asserting the **absence** of the old text — presence-of-new passes whether or not the old was removed. It is the same class as f225's loose dev-dep grep, §13.13 item 12's unescaped brace and this session's anchored-BLOCK trap, and in all four the failure direction was the dangerous one: it reported success rather than erroring. **`[^.]*` is not a sentence boundary in any text containing dotted identifiers, and this repo's tracker and board are almost entirely such text.** ✅ **Pass C LANDED 2026-09-28 (tracker t249, rulings g97/g98) — `dto/` is real: both wire adapters, both `impl Provider`, both `impl SseChunkMap`, and the two seams Pass D substitutes into.** 15 new files / 5 381 lines (production 2 258 across 7, largest 657; tests+support across 8, largest 694), **nothing in `crates/` over the 1 000-line cap**, crate **117 → 192** tests and workspace **451 → 526**, `dacode-protocol` unchanged at **121** with porcelain empty, **zero new dependencies and zero manifest edits** (`Cargo.lock` md5-identical, boundary diff **empty at 39**), zero `unsafe` with `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` intact. **Five review rounds** — the most at this milestone: secondary APPROVE at rounds 2 and 4, primary REQUEST CHANGES at 1 and 3 → APPROVE at 4, then a fifth residual round. **Mutations: the implementer's 61 (58 killed, 3 survivors all explained) plus the primary's independent 11 (9 killed, 2 survivors), and the two sets corroborate rather than duplicate** — the primary's P13 was a *pre-existing* unpinned invariant the implementer's set never touched. ⚠️ **THE FINDING WORTH CARRYING IS THAT BOTH REVIEWERS' VERDICTS TURNED ON A MEASUREMENT ONE OF THEM DID NOT MAKE.** Round 3 split — secondary APPROVE, primary REQUEST CHANGES — on whether an id-before-name provider shape was covered: the secondary had *read* the existing test and its disclosure and judged it covered, the primary *ran* the shape and got `[begin(name=\"\"), args({})]`, i.e. the real name dropped and the call silently lost. **A run beats a reading**, and the secondary retracted in terms in round 4 (\"*my method failed because I checked whether the words were there but not what the words were about*\"). That is the same failure as the **third inference-level citation defect in one file's family**: a `(path, first, last, substring)` checker verified the quoted words really were at openclaw `openai-completions-tool-calls.ts:132-133` — and they were, inside an **argument-byte-cap accumulator** whose own comment gives a different purpose, so the citation passed every mechanical check while supporting nothing. **Such a check verifies that a region CONTAINS a token and never that the token MEANS what the sentence claims.** ⚠️ **Three separate instruments broke in this one pass and all three reported green** (tracker f248/f255/f261, now **P-LIVE-019's amendment**): a mutation harness passed `--lib` — a *cargo* flag — to a libtest binary and scored 40 usage errors as 40 kills; a reviewer's `--exact` with a short name selected **0** tests so 40 green runs meant \"nothing ran\"; and a path dependency mid-edit in another process scored **46 mutants as does-not-compile**. The first two failed in *opposite* directions, which is why no \"be suspicious of green\" heuristic catches both — only a **companion quantity** does, and what caught the `--lib` defect was that 40 kills produced **zero test names**. ⚠️ **AND THE PASS'S OWN FIX ROUND REPRODUCED TWO CLASSES IT HAD JUST RULED ON**: after renaming a test, two doc comments still cited the **old** name as their *pinning* evidence (f241's rule — assert the **absence** of the old text, not merely the presence of the new), and two counts said \"five shapes\" beside a body pinning **six** (f238's mechanism, in the pass that ruled f238). **Two decisions recorded rather than left as code comments**: **g98** reversed the seam order to **demote-then-escape**, because §6's bullets are an *enumeration* and nowhere specify a sequence, so the landed \"§6's bullet order\" justification was not a reason — and sanitize-last is safe under every U150 arm; **U152** rules that §6's inability to amend a `ToolCallBegin` is **not** worth a protocol change, on a *durable reopen trigger* (\"a provider is observed sending arguments before the name\") rather than an argument. **U151** is the sibling gap and is **Ask-First**: `ProviderMessage` cannot express a message-level field, so `tool_call_id` is unencodable for `ProviderRole::Tool` and **every tool-capable `openai-compat` model** gets a rejected request. ⚠️ **Reproducibility caveat, stated because the gate list would otherwise imply otherwise**: `dacode-json` is a **path** dependency on `../../rust/json-tiers`, measured at **`33cf040` with 56 dirty files** at every gate run, and `Cargo.lock` pins a path dependency's *version string* and nothing about its content — so \"gates green at \\<hash\\>\" is **not re-derivable from this repo alone**. Judged not commit-blocking (blocking on a clean sibling would make this repo uncommittable whenever a sibling developer works) and recorded beside the results instead, which is f224's rule applied to a build. **Also corrected**: doc 13 §13.13 item 10's connection figure was stale a second consecutive time — the 192-test suite measures a **band of 65–70** across six 1-run deltas against the recorded ~46, and the +19 is accounted for exactly (17 new socket tests plus 2 retry requests), so **inheriting a recorded figure under-reports by about a third** in the direction that compounds; and §13.9 now carries f253's test-split rule, since a provider with a grammar suite *and* an end-to-end suite cannot satisfy both that and the cap. ✅ **Pass E LANDED 2026-09-29 (tracker t251; four review rounds, rulings g104/g107/g108) — `dacode-core`'s hand-rolled bounded-yield loops are real timeouts, and the pass's finding is that a yield count was never a wall-clock quantity.** Ten loops converted (nine `tokio::time::timeout` plus one unconditional `sleep(SETTLE)` absence window with **no bound that can fail** — **U154**), `dacode-core` now declares tokio's `time` **member-side** rather than inheriting it by feature unification, and doc 07 §7.8's Q1 note plus the root manifest's M1.5 note were corrected in the same commit (§2.3). **Zero production-code change**: `git diff --name-only` under `crates/dacode-core/src` is **six** files, not three — the `*_tests.rs` trio carrying the conversion plus `replay.rs`, `session.rs` and `store/writer_tests.rs` at **zero non-comment lines each** — proven by assertion-invocation counts identical base→head (84/84, 46/46, 69/69) and by a comment-only classifier with a known-bad companion. `dacode-core` **208 + 4 ignored** unchanged, workspace **526**, `Cargo.lock` md5 unchanged at 143 packages, boundary diff **empty at 39**, depth grep **23** both forms, no `unsafe`. ⚠️ **THE CONVERSION WAS NEVER THE PROBLEM. Across four rounds every single defect was in prose, and each round's was worse-placed than the last.** Round 1: three false *recorded reasons* on load-bearing decisions (a `~100 ns` yield cost that measures 14 µs, a bound size argued from it, and a manifest ruling). Round 2: **the primary ran my `g104` justification and both limbs were false** — dropping `time` from both crates still compiles, and the absent-feature error *does* name its cause; the conclusion survived on a different reason (declare-what-you-use, and a third party transitively forcing a feature is more fragile than a sibling declaring it). Round 3: a re-measurement harness resolved tokio **without `net`**, so its shape A built a time-only runtime instead of what `#[tokio::test]` builds here, and every figure came out **15× low** — which landed in doc 13 as \"neither campaign is settled\". Round 4 resolved it: **round 2 was right, by one cargo feature**, because tokio's `enable_all()` calls `enable_io()` only under `#[cfg(any(feature = \"net\", …))]` (`builder.rs:376-382`). ⚠️ **The fingerprint was printed in round 3's own output and nobody read it**: shape A measured *exactly equal* to shape B, which is what a `cfg`'d-out driver looks like. The harness now prints an `io_driver` column, because a property an argument rests on must be a labelled output and not a comment. ⚠️ **WHAT THE FOUR ROUNDS ACTUALLY DISCOVERED IS THE STRONGEST ARGUMENT FOR THE PASS.** A yield count is not a wall-clock quantity *at all*, and its value moved **33×** between M1.4 and M1.5 (the per-yield ratio, 14.15/0.43 = 32.9; doc 13's \"32×\" is the 200 000-loop ratio, 2.848/0.088 = 32.4 — same quantity, two bases, and nothing said so until a reviewer compared the files) on one machine with no load change, because `enable_all()` went from a **no-op** to two drivers. Measured, not argued: at M1.4 (`0c0c775`) `dacode-providers` was a stub and the lock had 69 packages with zero hyper-util/mio/socket2, so tokio had neither `net` nor `time` and M1.4's flake happened in a **driverless** runtime — its 200 000-yield sibling bound was worth **~0.088 s** and its 5 000 000-yield shutdown bound **~2.19 s**. So `SHUTDOWN_BOUND` = 30 s is **~341×** the bound that actually flaked and **~13.7× looser** than the bound it replaced, measured *in the runtime that bound really ran in*. **Pass E raised that bound; it did not lower it**, and rounds 1–3 all compared across eras — \"2.6× tighter\" and \"2.9× looser\" were both era-mismatches. The margin flips with the **resolved feature set** (stable, pinned, knowable) and not with machine load; the load-sensitive comparison is the era-*wrong* one. ⚠️ **THE PROCESS DEFECT IS MINE AND IS THE ONE TO CARRY.** I dispatched round 2's fixes to the **primary reviewer's** session id instead of the implementer's, so the reviewer wrote the fixes and then reviewed its own work. It disclosed that in the first line of its verdict and re-ran every experiment rather than trusting its own report — which is the only reason it was visible — but **a self-review is not a sign-off**, so round 2 produced one independent verdict where the gate requires two. The consequence was bounded and measurable rather than assumed: the two verdicts converged on the same class from different directions (each found a stale-cite site the other missed), and neither found a code defect. **The rule: a resumed `task_id` is opaque, so check which role owns it before dispatch — the tool will happily hand a reviewer an implementer's brief.** ⚠️ **AND FIVE ITEMS REACHED THE TREE FROM A DISPATCH RATHER THAN A DIFF, AND ALL FIVE WERE WRONG — two numbers, two attributions and one under-scoped command, so \"four numbers\" under-described it — the `hyper-util` `client-legacy` attribution and the host attribution are not figures, and counting them as such repeated the same conflation this list exists to correct.** Mine: \"9.4× headroom\" (it is 8.4×, and later ~341× against the era-correct baseline); the `hyper-util` `client-legacy` attribution for transitive `tokio/time` (it is hyper-util's own `tokio` feature — and **doc 07 already had it right**, so the error entered through a ruling that overrode a correct doc); a host attribution for a measurement I had not taken; four ratios derived from a per-yield cost instead of measured from loop cells; and an under-scoped `git show` that returns six where eight was claimed. **No reviewer checked any of them in the round they arrived, because they were in the prompt and not in the diff.** Every one was caught downstream by a worker or reviewer *re-measuring instead of transcribing* — and round 3's worker **refused** to certify my supplied host attribution, correctly identifying that adding it would have been f210's class committed inside the fix for f210's class, and would have looked authoritative *because* it named a host, a date and a mutant. **That refusal is the half of this pass that worked**, and it is now P-LIVE-023's causal chain. **Also landed:** **P-LIVE-023** (a proxy harness must resolve its subject's cargo features, and read the shape that was built to differ and did not); **P-LIVE-022** (`timeout(B, &mut handle)` *consumes* the handle's output, so the natural assert-then-await shape panics `JoinHandle polled after completion` — shipped twice in round 1 and caught by the suite); **P-LIVE-016's `~100 ns` corrected in place at its origin** with the mechanism, because that file is inlined into every worker prompt and a live normative claim gets corrected rather than annotated; **P-LIVE-009's fourth instance** — three rounds of a phrase sweep scoped to `crates/ docs/` each missed a site, and the fourth found one only by widening to `.agents/`, i.e. the highest-distribution prose in the repo was **unreachable by construction**; and the nine-plus-one correction swept to **four** sites, two of which no finding had named. ⚠️ **Two things this pass could not fix and did not hide.** (i) **The 1 000-line cap has no gate** — round 2 pushed `replay_tests.rs` to **1025** and *neither reviewer caught it*; the worker caught it itself and compressed to **993**, and checking the cap means remembering an `awk` one-liner. A stated hard rule enforced only by memory will be breached. (ii) **`replay_tests.rs` is 7 lines from the cap**, so U137's planned split (`replay_cancel_tests.rs`) is now the precondition for the next test added there, not a tidiness item. **Debt d16** records that `TASK_BOUND` is triplicated across three test modules — with the correction that **2 of 3 were dedup-able all along** via `super::support`, and all three already import from `registry.rs:545+`, so its remover is two independent steps rather than one blocked one. ✅ **Pass D LANDED 2026-09-30 (tracker t250, commit `7a99547`) — M1.5's ROW IS COMPLETE, all five passes under dual review.** `dacode-providers` **192 → 245** tests, workspace **526 → 579**. **The pass's shape was decided by a ruling rather than by its scope**: U150 came back as arm **(a1)** — §6 is the truth, the transport copy arrives *already escaped*, so the **engine** applies the escaping — and g103's consequence is that `complete()` receives an already-escaped copy, so a step inside `dto/` could not substitute into Pass C's seams; it could only be **inert**. **So this pass *removed* both seams across six files rather than using them** (g103 named one file; the tree had six, which the dispatch measured and stated before the worker started). The escaping *function* still lives here — §13.1 was wrong about **who calls it**, not **where it lives** — and only the call site moves, to `dacode-core` at **M1.9**. §13.1 now says which of §6's five rules the engine applies and which stay in this crate, because (a1) **splits** them and the layout rule was written assuming they travel together. **What landed**: `dialect/` (§6's five rules), `exchange.rs` (§13.6's four obligations including the redaction asymmetry — `exchanges.jsonl` **is** redacted, `events.jsonl` **never**), the **g90** substitution into `sse.rs` with the split-boundary property re-proved over the real marker set and still no tag literal in its production half, and §14's provider tests **#1–#4 and #6**. **U151 arm (iv)** rides the same commit: §6's `ProviderMessage` gains one opaque `RawJson` `sibling_fields` object, spliced beside `role`/`content` and **never parsed** — `git diff --numstat` is exactly **10 insertions / 0 deletions**, and U151's \"same commit\" clause is discharged by `git show --stat` rather than asserted. **U143 and d14 both closed** (`sse_paced` gives the responder temporal arrival separation; the stall-then-retryable variant kills the whole-loop-timeout mutant that survived Pass B2). ⚠️ **THE ENTRY-POINT TEST DIED IN BOTH PROVIDERS AND THAT IS SAID RATHER THAN BURIED**: it pinned the seam, and the seam is gone. Its replacement asserts on **socket bytes** at the same altitude — an already-escaped copy reaches the wire unchanged *and* a raw reserved spelling stays inert — which the primary verified really does reach the socket rather than stopping at the transform. ⚠️ **TWO FINDINGS OUTWEIGH THE CODE.** (i) **The escaping is idempotent, in and across both of its forms, and that is a named tested property rather than an observation.** It is what makes g103's \"double-escape **or** be inert\" arm *unreachable* — a `dto/`-side step could only be inert, the worse failure because it is silent — and it makes escaped-once versus escaped-twice **untestable on wire bytes**, so the defence is structural instead: zero escaping-function references in any production `dto/` file, as a greppable property. (ii) **g98's demote-then-escape order is now unobservable in this crate**: the two functions measurably **commute** and an order-swap mutant survives 244/244 (both the primary's measurements, attributed to it rather than to the pass so they stay reproducible). The composition that briefly re-expressed the order was removed at review for asserting an M1.9 caller that will not exist. ⚠️ **g98's own two-arm safety argument did not anticipate (a1)** — it said arm (a) makes the order question *disappear*, but (a1) only moved the call site, so the question **moved rather than dissolved**. The property therefore has **zero executable defence anywhere today**, which is filed as **d19** with M1.9 named as owner rather than left floating (f169's rule applied to a ruling). **Also filed, not settled**: **U155** (§6's `response: RawJson` cannot hold an SSE body; interim disclosed with both halves test-pinned, Ask-First flagged, `ExchangeRecord` is neither wire-visible nor fixture-pinned and **no golden fixture moved**) and **d18** (the scanner has no length caps — d8's shape one layer up, because `max_frame_bytes` caps one *undispatched frame* and not a construct assembled across many; **no number invented**, owner named). §13.10's **#1 and #6 are marked ◐ PARTIAL rather than ✅** with split ownership, because their `events.jsonl` halves are writable only in `dacode-core` — and the table gained a one-line legend defining both markers, since ✅ had only ever been convention. **The dead-code exemption the dispatch authorized came out VACUOUS**, and the reason is worth more than the exemption: under (a1) the escaping's caller is in *another* crate, so the items must be `pub` to be callable at all and a `pub` item is not dead — **zero** `allow(dead_code)` attributes exist in the crate. The widening has **two** reasons which f279's first draft conflated and the primary caught: **necessity** for rules 1–2 and `exchange`, **cost** for rules 3–5 (~20 exemptions avoided, on `sse.rs`'s precedent). **Both are now stated separately.** ⚠️ **THREE ROUNDS, AND THE DEFECT DISTRIBUTION IS PASS E's AGAIN: the code was approved at round 1 and every later defect was prose.** Round 2: a modality contradiction asserting a *non*-caller about an undispatched row, two stale load-bearing comments in frozen files, a visibility reason conflating necessity with cost, an unnamed duplicate-key obligation, a corrupted line in an unrelated pitfalls entry, and a helper **P-LIVE-024** promised that did not exist. Round 3: **the same contradiction relocated into the orchestrator's own tracker nodes**, where both reviewers independently found it surviving *after* the code was fixed. **Two of the sharpest catches were about attribution**: the primary refused to transcribe a nine-mutant list that existed only in chat (P-LIVE-017's shape), and refused to transcribe \"bit the implementer three times\" because no in-tree record existed — and the replacement enumeration came out **four** plus one labelled *probable-but-unattributable*, so the unsourced figure had also undercounted and mis-attributed one. ⚠️ **AND f278 WAS A FALSE REFUSAL, WHICH IS THE ROUND's MOST USEFUL RESULT**: the pass correctly observed that no `escapeControlTokens` identifier sat at five cited lines and wrongly concluded the citation was **stale**, when the lines are escape call sites one level *below* their gate. **The orchestrator's correction of f278 then repeated the same error class in three places** — naming \"three\" invocations where the checkout has **six across two files**, omitting a two-line ternary gate, and claiming \"the only knob is a boolean\" where a second file gates on a dialect comparison. **Both reviewers found defects in that paragraph in the same round, and not the same defects.** All three errors are recorded in sequence in doc 13 §13.15, whose generalisation is now stronger than the one it was written to carry: **a refusal to transcribe is itself a claim and needs the same verification as a transcription — and so does a correction.** The check that catches all three is identical: grep for the **subject** a citation names, not the nearest identifier in your own sentence. **P-LIVE-024 added** — a literal close-delimiter of the harness's *own* in-band grammar inside a tool argument truncates it in flight, which is `xml.md:20`'s reservation property held by this harness; it bit the orchestrator once and the worker four times (one of which **reported success and landed truncated**, caught only because `wc -l` disagreed), and the helpers that build those spellings from parts exist because of it. **P-LIVE-001 gained its eighth recurrence in this session** from a piped exit status, and the entry now records that a false number whose consequence was nil is a different record from a false green. **Gate matrix, re-run by the orchestrator after the last code edit**: `fmt` 0; `check --workspace --all-targets` and `clippy --workspace --all-targets` 0 with **zero** warning lines; `check -p dacode-providers --lib` 0 with zero warnings and **zero** exemption attributes; providers **245/0**, workspace **579/0**, protocol porcelain empty; no `[dev-dependencies]` (anchored grep), no `unsafe`, no control-token literal in `sse.rs` or `dto/` (wide pattern matching ASCII *and* fullwidth pipes); 1 000-line cap empty (largest in tree `event.rs` 996); `check-links` 0 both modes (796 links / 559 §-refs / 59 fences); `attention` hygiene all `ok`, §6 ids 155/155; `tasks.py check` 1 with **zero** `BLOCK` lines; `Cargo.lock` and `gates/` **unchanged**, boundary count still **39**, `Cargo.toml` diff comment-only. **REPEAT/CONTENTION were not re-run after rounds 2–3, on hash evidence rather than inference**: the primary set that bar in round 2, and round 3's answer is that *neither* file it could not prove comment-only was **edited at all** in rounds 2–3 — the only changes were transient mutation applications, restored from file copies and md5-verified, with both hashes confirmed against the primary's **own independent** pre-battery snapshot rather than the pass's figure, plus an mtime predating round 3's work window. So cycle #4's **300/0** (pass total **900/0**) ran on byte-identical copies and the same-line-count-code-edit residual is **excluded**. Disclosed as reasoned-not-measured for rounds 2–3.","status":"open","verify":"dual review ✅ A, B1, B2, U146, C, **E** (4 rounds), **D** (3 rounds)","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"M1.5","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U180","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E2","kind":"gate","title":"M1.6","body":"prompt caching: B14's `ContextBuffer`/`Segment` index and A4's four invariants, with the §14.1 tests that fail on a *byte*. ⚠️ **Compaction is opt-in, off by default, and does not ship here** — M1 ships the cache and not the compactor, and must not be written as though it ships both (**U97** stays open until §14.2's number exists). ▶️ ~~**Two things precede the code, both cheap and neither a decision.**~~ ⚠️ **Corrected at M1.1e (2026-09-30), and the correction is the point: one of the two was cheap and is now done, and the other turned out to be two user decisions.** ~~(i) **M1.1e = [09](part2-engine/09-context-caching.md)** is still a summary (46 lines), and §5's build order deepens a row's doc just-in-time ahead of it — §4's count falls 23 → 22 when its marker flips.~~ ✅ **(i) M1.1e LANDED — [09](part2-engine/09-context-caching.md) is deepened and is this row's instruction, but its marker is DELIBERATELY RETAINED and §4's count did not move, because the walk found two holes that need the user: **U156** (§4.5's `Segment.digest: [u8; 32]` names no hash function and no hashing crate is a `dacode-core` edge, so `Segment` is not constructible — a HARD GATE on this row, with both `sha2` arms measured at net +8 packages and `sha2` recommended) and **U157** (`ContextInfo.tokens_max: u32` cannot express §6's `window: None`, so the gauge cannot say \"unknown\"; `Option<u32>` recommended and cheap only until M1.3 publishes). Doc 09 also rules the four questions it can: R-a/R-b (the `Context`↔`ContextBuffer` split and the digest's version tag), R-c/R-d (the breakpoint unit and the rolling marker, measured across three reference harnesses) and R-e (§14.1's four caching tests, THREE of which have no subject at this row — #1 needs §5.6 (M2), #2 needs tool schemas (🧠 M1.10), #4 needs the compactor this row must not ship — so they land ◐ partial or deferred to doc 10 on doc 13 §13.10's precedent).** ⚠️ ~~**So M1.6 is BLOCKED on U156 and should not be dispatched before it is ruled**, while U157 can be ruled beside it or at the emit path.~~ ✅ **BOTH RULED BY THE USER 2026-09-30 (arm (a) each) AND LANDED AT STEP 0 UNDER DUAL REVIEW — the row is unblocked and ~~its remaining work is Passes A and B~~ ✅ **Pass A landed 2026-10-01, ~~so the remaining work is Pass B alone~~ and Pass B landed the same day, so the row is COMPLETE** (see the block at the end of this cell).** Step 0a (`dacode-protocol`): `ContextInfo.tokens_max` and the event variant's field are `Option<u32>`, `Default` is unknown-not-zero, two new tests pin the `None` encoding by exact bytes and the fold's `None` round-trip, and the measurement came out benign — **zero golden fixtures and zero corpus bytes moved**, so doc 33 §33.8's two pinned sha256 values still hold and needed no edit. Step 0b (`dacode-core`): the `sha2` edge with its decision comments, `src/context/{mod,digest,digest_tests}.rs` carrying `segment_digest` with **R-b's tag as a digest input** in two halves (const construction + per-call target encoding, both hashed before the payload), lock **143 → 151**, boundary **39 → 40**, and every gate run rather than reasoned about (`dep-audit` 0 at 8 attributions, `deny` clean, `check-lints` 0, the boundary diff verbatim empty, no unpredicted gate row). ~~Only the digest was built — **no `Context`, `ContextBuffer`, `Segment` or `SegmentKind`**, those being Pass A~~ ✅ **superseded by Pass A below; the strike is the point, because this was a present-tense claim in a live cell (the f96/M1.1e shape) and not a dated record**, and building API for an undispatched pass is a defect this repo has removed twice. **Review outcome: secondary APPROVE at round 1; primary REQUEST CHANGES with zero BLOCKERs and both MEDs in the board rather than the code** (this item's own stale citations and U156's unticked box), which the orchestrator discharged; the primary mutation-proved the digest (5 mutants, all killed, including the tag-dropped and framing-separator ones) and the gauge (4 mutants, all killed, including `None`-folded-as-0), and recomputed the golden vector with an independent implementation. **`dacode-core` 208 → 216 passed + 4 ignored, `dacode-protocol` 121 → 123, workspace 589.** ⚠️ **U157 is NOT closed by Step 0a** — its Verify clause also owes the emit path that handles `window: None` without inventing a number and doc 22/doc 21 naming the meter's rendering of \"unknown\", both **Pass B's**, and doc 09's depth marker is held by exactly that. ⚠️ **AND THE CAP HAS NO GATE, WHICH THIS PASS SPENT TWO MORE LINES OF**: `dacode-protocol/src/event.rs` is now **998** of 1 000 and the closest file in the workspace (`replay_tests.rs` 993, `transport.rs` 972 next), and Pass B's emit path is likely to touch that variant again. ✅ **It did not: Pass B added ZERO lines to `event.rs` — the emit lives in `dacode-core` and the under-run prose in `gauge.rs`'s header — so the cap stands at 998.** **(ii) The crate's map layer now exists**: [`crates/dacode-core/AGENTS.md`](../../crates/dacode-core/AGENTS.md) (2026-09-30, f222's finding discharged for the crate this row lands in rather than the one M1.5 did), carrying the row's **four inherited obligations** in one place — `Session.context` (§4.1's field, which doc 07 §7.2 schedules here and §7.9 forbade earlier), **U139**'s fold-at-open controller seed, the `cache_breakpoints` the provider dtos **deliberately do not encode** (`dto/mod.rs:57-61`: §4.5's markers go inside opaque content, so **the engine places them**), and `ModelInfo.window: None`'s **consumer** side (U140's abstention, not a fallback) — plus one naming warning measured while writing it: a bare `rg 'Segment' crates/` matches `dacode-providers`'s `HoldbackSegment`, which is the SSE holdback and not doc 09's history segment, so M1.6's `Segment` either gets qualified or shares a name with something else in the workspace. ✅ **Pass A discharged the second arm by measurement rather than by renaming** — it kept §4.5's name and verified that `rg '\\bSegment\\b'` over `*.rs` outside `src/context/` returns zero *type* references, that `dacode-providers` exports no bare `Segment`, and that the four `use dacode_providers` sites in `dacode-core` all import `ModelRef` alone, so no `use` site can see both. ⚠️ The bare grep's **count** stopped being the check: 39 hits when filed, **175** now. **▶️ PASS A LANDED 2026-10-01 under dual review — §9.2's items 1, 2, 4 and 5, with 3 and 6 left to Pass B (✅ both landed in the Pass B entry below, so this ▶️ is a dated record and not the row's state); the §1 banner of that date carries the narrative, the two new rulings (R-f, R-g), the review's four rounds and the verification matrix, and §6's U158 is its one new item. `src/context/` is nine files, `Session.context` is §4.1's tenth field, `dacode-core` 216 → 256 passed + 4 ignored, workspace 589 → 629, and zero new dependencies (lock 151, boundary 40, no fixture moved).** ✅ **PASS B LANDED 2026-10-01 — §9.2's items 3 and 6, and M1.6 IS COMPLETE; the §1 banner of that date carries the narrative, the three new rulings and the verification matrix. `src/context/` is **eleven files**: `gauge.rs` carries the estimate at the *measured* divisor — 4 per token over **bytes**, rounded **up**, both biases on the safe side of §4.5's under-run, the harness survey and its citations in the module header, oh-my-pi's `pi-natives` and open-code-review's `tiktoken-go` the two **refused** tokenizer arms (measured: Rust-via-N-API and Go, not C — neither is class 1; open-code-review's own *fallback* is `len(bytes)/4`, and the module header carries the refusal) — and `context_info`, **U157's consumer half**, where `window: None` passes **straight through** to `tokens_max: None` (pinned by `an_untabled_models_window_passes_through_as_none_and_never_as_zero` and mutation-proved against the `unwrap_or(0)` mutant), under **R-j**: no gauge field on `Context` — a function of the last reported usage and the delta, not state with no writer until M1.9. **U139 CLOSED** by `registry.rs`'s `folded_controller` in `open_session` — **R-h**: a chunked full forward fold from cursor 0 through `dacode_protocol::fold` (one fold per design principle 2, no hand-rolled `ControlChanged` scan), the backward-scan alternative deferred with an owner to M3's doc 20, and the error arm ruled **fail the open** (three reasons in `registry.rs`'s header) and pinned by the `FailSecondRange` tests at both the function and — through the round-2 `open_session_with` seam — the call site, whose undo (shutdown, not drop, on the M8 route) the retry half pins. The Verify-clause test — `a_session_closed_under_client_a_and_reopened_reports_a_until_take_control_transfers_it` — with the seed-precedence, chunk-walk (**520** records, FM13) and no-record-no-claim pins beside it, is in `registry_tests.rs`, never `replay_tests.rs` (untouched at 993). The fold's O(log-size) `Snapshot` transient is **U160**, M3's doc 20. **R-i** lands in doc 22/doc 21 — `used/?` with the bar suppressed, the omit-the-denominator alternative named beside it, both docs keeping their markers. **Doc 09's depth marker FLIPPED, §4's count 23 → 22, U157/U139 closed in §6.** `dacode-core` 256 → 269 passed + 4 ignored, workspace 629 → 642, **13 mutants / 13 killed across two rounds** (the reviewer's FM13/FM10 each survived the first tree and drove a new test and the `open_session_with` seam), zero new dependencies (lock 151, boundary 40, zero fixture/corpus bytes moved, `event.rs` untouched at 998).**","status":"open","verify":"dual review","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"M1.6","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U181","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E2","kind":"gate","title":"M1.7","body":"permissions: §4.4's resolution order, A22's `AllowPattern` subset bound checked at resolution time, A23's identifier injection and exact-`args` scan, A5's egress policy (hostname globs **and** the IP layer under them, since a glob cannot see what a name resolves to). Written knowing that §4.4's main `bash` allow-list is **advisory** and the real boundary is §4.7's worktree (**U63**, whose §4.4 wording is B7's owed transcription, and whose doc-14 side is M1.10's). ✅ **STEP 0 LANDED 2026-10-01 — all four gates ruled by the user and one already shipped, so what remains is ordinary code under dual review.** [A28](appendices/A-design-deltas.md) carries the spec half (§3.2's `DenyReason`, §4.4's three pattern domains, `.env` → `**/.env`, and a `deny` made a floor no grant or mode overrides); `dacode-protocol` carries U162's arm (a) refined to `Deny { reason: Option<DenyReason> }`, **123 → 125** and the register's **first golden diff** (`{\"type\":\"Deny\"}` → `{\"type\":\"Deny\",\"data\":{}}`, the pre-change bytes no longer decoding), with `event.rs` untouched at **998** because the variant lives in `perm.rs`. g128 ruled **arm 5** — hand-rolled, `Domain`-parameterised, **+0 packages** — with the user's added requirement that the matcher stay **adoptable by the strict sandbox** written into doc 16 §16.5 and onto U98 as a predecessor obligation. ⚠️ **Doc 16's marker is STILL RETAINED and §4's count stays 22**, because two of the four rulings have Verify clauses with code halves this row owes: U162's §14.1 distinguishability assertion (it cannot live in `dacode-providers`, which has no protocol edge, and lands where `EgressRefused` maps to `NetAddress`) and g130's two table tests. ⚠️ **And U166 arrived beside them**: an automatic decision has no `PermissionRequested`, so its `ReqId` names nothing — which `NetAddress`, produced at *connect* time after `net` already allowed automatically, makes unavoidable rather than theoretical. Doc 16 §16.16 names all five inherited items. ▶️ **DOC PRE-FLIGHT DONE (M1.1f, 2026-10-01) — and it turned this row's opening into a Step 0 rather than a pass.** Doc [16](part3-capabilities/16-permissions.md) is deepened and is the instruction; its marker is **retained** because three gates need the user (**g128** the matcher arm, **g129** three of §4.4's own words, **g130** whether a lower-rung `deny` is a floor) plus **U162**'s wire shape, which is cheap only until M1.3 publishes. ⚠️ **Three scope corrections this cell did not carry.** (i) **Of §4.4's five rungs M1.7 can build three** — the *project* and *user* rungs are §5.1's loader, which is `dacode-config`, which is doc 17 at **M2** — so the row must **not** build config discovery; two rungs are absent rather than stubbed, and inventing a TOML reader here is **U115's** named failure mode one row early. (ii) **A23's injection half is M1.10's and its escalation half is M1.9's**, so this row lands the scan as a *pure function with no production caller* — and `check()` itself has none until M1.9 either, which is d6's exemption shape a fifth time with M1.9 named as remover. (iii) **U63's ack now has a stronger mechanism than B7 recorded**: four of five semantically identical `rm -rf` argv spellings evade a glob deny **with no shell, subshell or pipeline involved**, and the join is not injective — so §4.7's worktree is the boundary for a reason that is *not* about shells, and dacode's own argv-not-shell property (§7.1, `PermScope::Exec`) does not save the allow-list. **The matcher itself is g128's**: no crate setting is correct for §4.4's three pattern domains at once, and the strict setting fails **open** (it voids `rm -rf *`), so R-c rules a `Domain` parameter and the recommendation is arm 5 hand-rolled at **+0 packages** — with `glob` refused outright on a measured non-UTF-8 deny bypass. ▶️ **PASS A LANDED 2026-10-02 under dual review** — `perm/pattern.rs` (the matcher: `Domain{Path,Command,Host}`, R-c/R-d/R-e, the four sandbox constraints, bytes-not-chars subjects), `pattern_tests.rs` + `pattern_prop_tests.rs` (19 table tests, the property test against an independent brute-force reference, a 14-mutant kill table), `perm/mod.rs` (d6's exemption a fifth time), `lib.rs`'s five-module sentence, and the crate `AGENTS.md` re-measured. **dacode-core 269 → 291 passed + 4 ignored; workspace 644 → 666; lock 151 and boundary 40 unmoved; `event.rs` untouched at 998.** Review round 1: both reviewers REQUEST CHANGES, **zero BLOCKERs and zero logic defects** — one measured-false ReDoS mitigation (§16.7's bound is circular), one surviving mutant (PM-X, R-d inside braces), one false ground (`Path` case-folding on APFS), five false counts/cites; all fixed at round 2 with PM14 added and killed. **Three rulings landed mid-review (R-h/R-i/R-j, §16.15): `Path` stays non-folding with the hole filed as U167 (M2/doc 17); the complexity cap is deferred while not a blocker for MVP (d22); consequence 3 disclosed unmet (U98/M5).** ▶️ **PASS B LANDED 2026-10-03 under dual review** — `perm/mod.rs` 59 → 477 (`PermissionState` + `check()` minting a `ReqId` per decision + the R-a(i) walk + g130's floor + the mode table), `perm/scope.rs` (R-c + R-b's anchoring bound), `Session.perm` (§4.1's eleventh field) at the registry's open path, `decide_resolve` wired (None/by enforced, bound-before-storage, `register_permission` carrying the scope), the U166 fold test, `id.rs`'s doc sync, and d23's corpus fix (2.85 % → 51.378 %). **dacode-core 291 → 308 + 4 ignored; protocol 125 → 126; workspace 666 → 684; lock 151 / boundary 40 / `event.rs` 998 all unmoved.** **U166 ruled arm (a) and CLOSED** (§3.2 amended in the same commit); **g130's Verify clause is met** (its two table tests landed); the fix round corrected an over-restrictive first cut of the floor (my dispatch's ambiguity — last-match-wins among config rules, the floor fences grants and modes only, §16.6 states the scope). **13/13 mutants killed.** ▶️ **PASS C LANDED 2026-10-03 under dual review** — `perm/net.rs` (356) + `net_tests.rs` (411): the four named sets (R-g), hand-rolled CIDR (R-f) with mapped-entry refusal, the `EgressGuard` producer, the `EgressRefused → NetAddress` mapping (U162's Verify clause), the `NetHost` producer, U149 arm (b)'s `require_name_host` literal-host refusal, and f313's identifier-swap kill. **dacode-core 308 → 320 + 4 ignored; workspace 684 → 699; 5/5 mutants killed; dead_code 16 → 30; doc 16's marker FLIPPED (§4's count 22 → 21).** ✅ **PASS D LANDED 2026-10-04 under dual review — THE ROW IS COMPLETE**: `perm/scan.rs` (167) + `scan_tests.rs` (307), A23's identifier scan as the pure detection half (the four ULID-backed needles, ASCII-case-insensitive per the site's ruling with both arms on record, leftmost, the six exclusions with §4.3's reasons, the `RawJson` compile-probe; no production caller until M1.9's dispatch). **dacode-core 320 → 336 + 4 ignored; workspace 699 → 715; 9/9 mutants killed; dead_code 30 → 34.** The ninth consecutive prose-only review round; both reviewers APPROVE (primary r3, secondary r2).","status":"open","verify":"✅ Step 0 · ✅ **Pass A** (matcher, 2026-10-02) · ✅ **Pass B** (resolver + the floor + U166 closed, 2026-10-03) · ✅ **Pass C** (egress IP layer + U149/U162 closed, 2026-10-03) · ✅ **Pass D** (identifier scan, 2026-10-04) — **the row is COMPLETE**","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"M1.7","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U182","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E2","kind":"gate","title":"M1.8","body":"~~the T1/T2 benchmark harness~~ → **the T1 benchmark harness — T2 cannot land at this row, and the cell said otherwise until the 2026-09-24 pre-flight** (see ⚠️ scope below) — over a committed `events.jsonl` corpus (doc 33, mapped onto §14.2's tiers). **Not brainstorm-gated**, and it should land early rather than last — but it benches fold, snapshot latency and log append/fsync, so it needs M1.2 and M1.4 first. Its corpus shape is already fixed by A1 (durable envelopes only, `LogHeader` on line 1), so nothing committed from here on needs a retrofit, and §14.2's publication rule binds every number it produces. ✅ **Dependency DECIDED 2026-09-24 (the user's approval closes the row's one Ask-First gate): `criterion` 0.8.2, `default-features = false, features = [\"cargo_bench_support\", \"async_tokio\"]`, as a *dev*-dependency of `dacode-protocol` and `dacode-core`.** Measured in a scratch crate on the pinned 1.98.1 with this repo's own `deny.toml` — never recalled, that being the P-LIVE-014 class: **+35 packages** against the current ~~68~~-**69**-package lock *(re-measured at M1.1c: `rg -c \"^\\[\\[package\\]\\]\" Cargo.lock` returns **69** and did before the pre-flight too — the f113/f117/U119b/f151 recorded-count class a fifth time; the +35 stands as the scratch estimate and the landing commit measures the authoritative delta, as §33.7 of [33](part6-quality/33-benchmarking.md) now requires)*; `licenses`/`advisories`/`sources` **clean with no new license rows** (the OR-licensed tail resolves to allowed terms); both 0.5.1 and 0.8.2 compile a real bench here, so nothing forced the older pin. **Why not 0.5.1**, since §6's inherited `dacode-json` methodology names it: that is inheritance and not a reason — 0.5.1 is edition 2018 with **no declared MSRV**, where 0.8.2 is MSRV 1.86 and current. ⚠️ **The `cc` objection to 0.8.2 was real but smaller than first stated, and the correction is the useful half**: `cc` v1.4.7 is **already in `Cargo.lock`** via `zstd-sys` → `zstd-safe` → `zstd` → `dacode-core` (B12's accepted class-1 exception), so 0.8.2 adds **no new toolchain requirement** and `cc` is **not** a new package — what it adds is a *second introducer* of that marker, `alloca` 0.4.0 (MIT, arrived in 0.8.0, **non-optional** under `cfg(any(windows, unix))` so it cannot be feature-gated off, and it genuinely compiles C: `build.rs` is `cc::Build::new().compile(\"calloca\")` and `calloca.o` is produced). **Two gate edits are therefore owed in the same commit as the dependency** (§2.3's rule): `deny.toml:59`'s existing `serde_json` ban gains **`wrappers = [\"criterion\", \"tinytemplate\"]`** — *both* parents, because the chain is `criterion` → `serde_json` **and** `criterion` → `tinytemplate` → `serde_json`, and measured, naming either one alone leaves `bans FAILED` with an `unmatched-wrapper` warning; and **Appendix F §F.3 gains a dev-only `alloca` row**, because the appendix's opening claim is that *every* C/C++ dependency is recorded there and layer 2 cannot see this one (⚠️ gate hole below). `gates/class1-allowlist.txt` needs **no** row — it is keyed on introducers the scan can find, and whether a row for an invisible crate is accepted or rejected must be settled by **running** `dep-audit.py`, not by reasoning about it. Also owed: correcting `gates/class1-exempt.txt`'s prediction that \"`winapi-sys` — legacy Windows API bindings\" arrives later, since what 0.8.2's `page_size` actually pulls is `winapi` 0.3 plus `winapi-i686/x86_64-pc-windows-gnu`, none of which matches the `*-sys` marker glob so no gate fires (that file has precedent for being corrected by measurement). **`async_tokio` is in the feature set because `LogWriter::append` is `async`**, and measured it does **not** widen tokio — it resolves to exactly `default,macros,rt,rt-multi-thread,sync,tokio-macros` with **no `mio` and no `socket2`** — so §7.8's Q1 feature discipline survives and the benches can use `b.to_async(runtime)` rather than hand-rolling `block_on`. Dropping `plotters`/`rayon` removes `web-sys` + `js-sys` (21 packages) for HTML reports nobody reads. ⚠️ **Wasm, settled so it is not re-asked: `alloca` cannot reach any wasm build, for two independent reasons** — it is target-gated out (`cargo tree --target wasm32-unknown-unknown` and `--target wasm32-wasip1` both resolve **zero** `alloca`~~`/cc`~~ *(⚠️ the `cc` half corrected at Pass B1's review — the f113 scope-stripping class: the measurement was the scratch crate's, which had no `zstd`; in this tree `cc` **does** resolve under both wasm targets via `zstd-sys`'s pre-existing normal edge, B12/U14's territory and not criterion's. The `alloca` half holds and is the only half this decision rests on)*, wasm carrying `family = \"wasm\"` and not `unix`), and it is a *dev*-dependency, which `cargo build --target wasm32-…` never compiles. This repo's only wasm posture is **resolution, never compilation**: `js-sys` sits in the exempt file because `ulid` → `web-time` surfaces under `dep-audit.py`'s `--target all` scan, and Appendix F §F.1's class-2 clause was widened 2026-09-19 purely so the gate does not demand a debt row for a crate that never compiles. That is the only reason \"wasm\" appears in the plan at all; **no wasm frontend is planned**, and B2's standing answer is a **TS SPA (Solid)** due by M3 with `protocol.d.ts` generated from M1.3's schema export — under which there is no Rust wasm build, and under the Leptos alternative `trunk` builds `dacode-web` while criterion stays a dev-dep of two other crates. ⚠️ **SCOPE — this row is T1-only, and the reason is a build-order fact rather than a preference** (the M1.4 \"a complete *list* is not a complete milestone\" class, arriving in a cell that looked complete): §14.2 computes T2's headline cache-hit rate **from §6's `ExchangeRecord`**, and `rg ExchangeRecord crates/` is **empty** — the record is **M1.5's** deliverable, which §5's build order places *after* M1.8, so the metric cannot be computed at this row even in principle. T2's other two numbers are worse placed: \"harness overhead before the first user token\" needs §5.6's six prompt layers (**M2** prompt assembly) and the tool schemas (**M1.10, brainstorm-gated**), and stable-prefix drift needs the assembled prefix. **What M1.8 builds is T1**, plus whatever T2 scaffolding needs no records. ⚠️ **REACHABILITY — §14.2's tier-1 list collides with debt d6, and the collision is measured against visibility rather than assumed**: `store::LogWriter::{spawn,append,flush,shutdown}` and `store::fanout::Fanout::{publish,subscribe,receiver_count}` are `pub`, so log append/fsync **including §7.4's cursor round trip** (the round trip *is* `append`'s return value) and fan-out-at-K-subscribers are benchable, and `DacodeJsonCodec`/`fold` are `pub` in `dacode-protocol` so fold throughput, snapshot latency and codec throughput are too. But `session`/`registry`/`actor` are `pub(crate)` (`lib.rs:55-59`, d6's remover named **M3**), so **\"memory per live session\" is not benchable at this row**, and **U116's Verify clause explicitly demands that \"M1.8's bench list names a registry-lookup case, or doc 20 records why one is unnecessary\"**. `store::compress::FieldEncoding::{encode,decode}` are `pub(crate)` as well (`compress.rs:339,361`), so the compression **level** bench — §14.2's own explicit knob — drives `zstd` directly over corpus leaves, which is legitimate because the store only forwards the level. TUI frame cost is out regardless (`dacode-tui` is a 15-line stub, **M1.11**). ~~**Proposed disposition, for M1.1c to record or overturn**~~ ✅ **recorded as proposed at M1.1c** ([33](part6-quality/33-benchmarking.md) §33.5, with U116 closed on the substitution stated — the record is doc 33's, not doc 20's, and §5's M3 item carries the revisit obligation): bench every reachable subject now, defer memory-per-session and registry-contention to **M3** with d6's widening, and close **U116** through its own second arm — doc 33 recording why the case is not here — rather than widening `registry` early, which would spend the visibility narrowing both C1 reviewers demanded for a bench that has no production caller either. ⚠️ **GATE HOLE found by this pre-flight, and this row is its first live instance**: `dep-audit.py` excludes dev edges **by design** (`TREE_FLAGS = [\"-e\",\"normal,build\",…]`, its own docstring reading \"dev-dependencies stay out of scope (they do not ship)\"), and `deny.toml`'s ban list has no `cc` entry because `cc` is legitimate for `zstd-sys` — so a class-1 marker arriving under a dev-dependency is invisible to **both** layers, and criterion 0.8.2's `alloca` → `cc` would have entered silently. Appendix F's \"never discovered in a `Cargo.lock` six months later\" has a dev-dep hole. The `alloca` §F.3 row is that hole **handled in the open rather than exploited**; ~~whether §F.2 should also state the dev-edge scope limit is M1.1c's call~~ ✅ **called at M1.1c: §F.2 states it** — landed as the third of §F.2's \"one limit of X\" paragraphs, with the by-hand practice (a dev-dep that compiles C owes a §F.3 row no gate asks for) and f189 closed on that second arm. ⚠️ **CI**: doc 31 §31.1 — **no CI provider exists** — so §14.2's \"tier 1 … is the only one that can gate a merge\" is a **hand-run** gate here, and doc 33 must give the command rather than imply a workflow. ~~**M1.1c is owed first**~~ ✅ **M1.1c LANDED 2026-09-24** (M1.1's cell: \"then 33 (M1.8)\"): ~~deepen~~ **[33](part6-quality/33-benchmarking.md) is deepened**, carrying this measured set exactly as [07](part2-engine/07-session-registry.md) §7.8 carried M1.4's and [03](part1-foundations/03-protocol.md) §3.8 carried M1.2's (§33.7, with the commands and the 69-package correction), plus the corpus shape/location/generator (§33.6 — `LogHeader` + **2 000 durable envelopes**, all 27 durable variants ≥ 1, realistic turn-weighted mix, leaves over the 1 024-byte floor, ≤ 2 MB at `crates/dacode-protocol/benches/corpus/events.jsonl`, generated by `tests/corpus.rs` on the golden **bless pattern** with the default suite run as the drift check — ⚠️ **overturning the `tools/`-script lean**: a Python generator would hand-mirror the wire format the derived-golden design exists to protect, and no **G.3 row** is owed for an in-crate test-suite mechanism, the bless precedent and the trap both recorded), the baseline-and-environment recording convention §14.2's publication rule requires (§33.8 — `[profile.bench] opt-level=3 lto=\"fat\" codegen-units=1` + `sample_size(30)` adopted deliberately from §6's inheritance, RESULTS.md-shaped environment block, baselines living in doc 33 itself per §14.2's *where the detail lives* sentence, and the compression-level procedure: a recorded curve, where a default ≠ 3 is a user decision + §3.6 precision edit), and the per-subject reachability table above with an owning row for every deferral (§33.5, incl. the hand-run gate command and P-LIVE-016's flake discipline at §33.9 — a bimodal or failing bench is a defect, never re-run-until-green before recording). **What remains at this row is the code, t241 + t242**: the two bench targets (`[[bench]] harness = false`), the corpus generator + drift check, the `[profile.bench]` stanza, `deny.toml`'s `wrappers = [\"criterion\", \"tinytemplate\"]`, t242's §F.3 `alloca` row and `class1-exempt.txt` winapi-prediction correction, and the first baseline recording — under g82's dual review, gate matrix re-run after the last edit. ✅ **Pass B1 LANDED 2026-09-24 (tracker t243; t242's two edits landed inside it, so t242 is done) — the protocol half, under dual review: secondary APPROVE round 1 (a 46-row transcription table over doc 33 §§33.5–33.10, every normative clause discharged); primary REQUEST CHANGES → APPROVE** after the orchestrator applied its two MED corrections. **Both MEDs were falsified facts in the NEW prose, not in code** — the review-gate value M1.4 established, arriving in a docs-heavy pass: (i) the §F.3 `alloca` row misattributed it \"via `page_size`\"; measured, `alloca` is criterion's **direct** `cfg(any(windows,unix))` dep (`cargo tree -i alloca`; `page_size` is a separate direct dep whose own deps are only `libc`/`winapi` — the winapi prediction-correction above stays right, the attribution was conflated); (ii) \"both wasm trees resolve zero `alloca`/**`cc`**\" was the **scratch crate's** measurement transcribed de-scoped — in this tree `cc` DOES resolve under both wasm targets via `zstd-sys`'s pre-existing normal edge (B12/U14's territory, unrelated to criterion), so the claim was corrected in all three published sites (§F.3's row, doc 33 §33.7, this cell) as the f113 class, while the `alloca` half holds and is the only half the decision rests on. **What landed**: root `Cargo.toml` gains the `criterion` 0.8.2 workspace entry (decision comment per g81) + `[profile.bench] opt-level=3 lto=\"fat\" codegen-units=1`; `dacode-protocol` gains the dev-dep + explicit `[[bench]] fold`/`[[bench]] codec` (`harness = false`); **the corpus** — `benches/corpus/events.jsonl`, `LogHeader` + 2 000 durable envelopes, **1 088 730 B**, sha256 `c3b517c3fe214d74cbc785b5cbcf26213914a158e5532a4f5748deceeb7097f5`, all 27 durable variants derived from `EventTier` (not hardcoded), 24 turn-weighted rounds, 88 leaves >1 024 B (44 compressible / 44 noisy) from an inline fixed-seed xorshift, byte-identical across bless runs; **the generator** `tests/corpus.rs` (646 lines) on the golden bless pattern — the DEFAULT suite run byte-compares and fails naming `DACODE_CORPUS_BLESS=1 cargo test -p dacode-protocol --test corpus`, mutation-proved (a 1-byte flip kills it); `benches/{fold,codec}.rs` + a `benches/common` loader — fold at prefixes 100/1 000/2 000 (`Throughput::Elements`), snapshot cold-fold with a fresh `Snapshot` per iteration (`iter_batched`), codec encode/decode over the corpus bytes (`Throughput::Bytes`, divisor cross-checked against the recorded medians by the primary). **`cfg(test)` IS set for `harness=false` bench targets on rustc 1.98.1** (probe-measured twice), so the bench roots carry the canonical relaxation block verbatim — and **f190** records that `check-lints` does not *mechanically* walk `benches/*.rs` (loud-failing via clippy instead). `deny.toml`'s `serde_json` ban gained `wrappers = [\"criterion\", \"tinytemplate\"]` (both-parents requirement mutation-proved: either alone leaves `bans FAILED`); §F.3's `alloca` row landed making §F.2's forward reference true; `class1-exempt.txt`'s winapi prediction corrected in its comment block only. **The dep-audit invisible-crate question was settled by RUNNING, not reasoning**: an allowlist row for `alloca` is **accepted blindly** by both modes — nothing cross-checks allowlist rows against the visible tree — so the row stays out as decoration; the outcome is recorded in §F.3's watch cell. **Lock 69 → 106 (+37 vs the pre-flight's +35** — §33.7's \"the landing commit measures\" rule paying for itself immediately); the production graph is untouched: `-e normal,build --target all` package set byte-identical to a HEAD worktree, no `mio`/`socket2`, tokio features unchanged, boundary diff **empty at 27**. One observation, no action: criterion's chain adds `syn` 2.0.119 beside the lock's existing `syn` 3.0.6 (via `zerocopy-derive`); `cargo deny` exits 0 with no multiple-versions warning. **Baselines recorded in doc 33 §33.8** (six rows, median [95 % CI], measured after the last edit): fold 28.081/9.2793/5.1105 Melem/s at 100/1 000/2 000 (per-event cost grows with N — the scaling shape the prefixes exist to show), snapshot cold-fold@2k 386.95 µs (consistent with fold/2000's 391.35 µs — cross-checked), codec encode 763.39 µs (1.3257 GiB/s), decode 1.2715 ms (815.01 MiB/s). The primary's independent re-run found 3/6 medians marginally outside the recorded CIs (encode −3.2 %, the rest ≤ ±2.7 %) — single-run jitter on a shared machine, exactly §33.9's wide-band regime; the orchestrator's own gate run agreed (+1.0 %/−1.1 % on the two rechecked). **Gate matrix, all re-run by the orchestrator after the last edit**: `fmt` 0; `check`/`clippy --workspace --all-targets` 0 with zero warning lines; `dacode-protocol` **121** green (114 + 7 corpus) with `tests/fixtures` porcelain empty (goldens byte-identical); `dacode-core` **208** green and porcelain clean (untouched); `cargo bench -p dacode-protocol` exit 0 suite-clean; `cargo deny check` 0; `dep-audit.py` + `--self-test` 0; `check-lints` 0 (11 members, 3 test roots); boundary diff empty at 27; `check-links` 0 both modes (748 links); `attention.py` 0 zero FAIL — **including its two-form depth grep catching the M1.1c doc's own header masking the short form** (hard rule 5 applies to the writer's own prose: \"carry the migrated summary forward\" reworded before commit). Corpus-data choices recorded rather than invented: `SessionCreated`/`SessionClosed` carry no `origin` although §3.3 makes them command-caused — the goldens set zero origins, fold ignores the field, and no committed convention exists (primary's LOW, accepted). **What remains of M1.8: Pass B2 (tracker t244)** — `dacode-core`'s three subjects (append + fsync incl. the cursor round trip, fan-out at K + queue-depth behaviour, the compression-level curve = B12's number), the flake-class half per doc 33 §33.9, with its own dual review and baseline rows. ✅ **Pass B2 LANDED 2026-09-24 (tracker t244) — M1.8's row is COMPLETE** — under dual review: secondary **APPROVE** at round 1 (a second 46-row transcription table, every clause discharged), primary **APPROVE** with five low notes, two of which were fixes folded into the commit (a *recalled* `−7..=22` zstd level range in a bench comment measured at **−131 072..=22** — P-LIVE-014's class reaching bench prose; and the bytes-per-1 000 note's \"file size equals byte for byte\" clarified by probe: the 2 000 envelope lines are byte-identical, line 1 is not — the writer's fresh `LogHeader` carries a wall-clock `created`) plus three precision clauses (deflate's level-invariant base64 wrapper, the lagging-publish attribution labelled subtraction-not-isolation, level 1's weak-dominance reading recorded beside argmin=19). **Code**: `dacode-core`'s criterion dev-dep (**lock 106 → 106**, one edge line, zero packages), three bench targets (`append`/`fanout`/`compress`, 140–238 lines each, canonical relaxation blocks byte-identical, `[[bench]] harness = false`) + a `benches/common` whose `TempSession` follows `store/mod.rs`'s no-tempfile precedent; **no src/ change** (git diff under `crates/dacode-core/src` empty), no visibility widening, protocol porcelain empty, corpus sha256 unchanged. **f194, verified sentence-by-sentence by the primary against the vendored sources**: async per-iteration setup inside criterion's `to_async` loop needs `block_in_place(|| Handle::current().block_on(spawn))` — criterion runs setup inside its own `block_on`, nested `Runtime::block_on` panics on the enter guard, and the multi-thread runtime's `block_on` thread is tokio's one `allow_block_in_place` case (worker.rs:418-429, `exit_runtime` clearing only `ENTERED`). **Numbers, recorded in doc 33 §33.8 with B2's own environment block**: append **21.233 [21.013–21.474] ms** per iteration = 2 000 `append().await` round trips **plus the one `shutdown()`** (disclosed in the bench and the doc: it consumes the writer and criterion has no teardown hook; exactly one §3.6 durability boundary inside), derived **10.62 µs/append** and **544 328.5 B per 1 000 events** (deterministic, printed every run); fan-out at K ∈ {1, 8, 64} = **54.479 µs / 299.17 µs / 1.4625 ms** per 1 000 publishes (marginal **+35.0/+20.8 µs** per subscriber — the wake path, sub-linear in K), the lagging-subscriber drop-oldest path **15.913 µs** (~3.4× *cheaper* than K = 1: a pinned client never blocks the engine — §3.7's promise, measured); the compression curve over the corpus's **88 leaves / 610 379 B** at ten levels — ratio **level-invariant 0.4154–0.4188**, so **B12's number confirms `level = 3`** (argmin(ratio) = 19 at 0.25 pp for ~108× latency recorded rather than rounded away; no adoption, no user decision owed; `DEFAULT_LEVEL` and §3.6 untouched). **f191**: criterion 0.8.2's console `time:` is `typical()` = slope-or-mean, **not the sample median** — source-verified against `estimate.rs`/`report.rs` and `estimates.json`; §33.8's estimator naming corrected across both recordings, no number moved, one statistic identity preserved. **f192**: the two-run divergence (four fast compress rows up to +8.9 %) was §33.9's investigate-event — resolved as shared-machine/estimator noise on recorded evidence (mean-vs-slope pairs disagreeing in *opposite* directions, the byte-identical deterministic ratio arm, slow rows agreeing ≤1.3 %, `fold/2000` +1.8 % on an untouched crate), with a **±10 % wide-band advisory** for the fast rows; not re-run-until-green. **P-LIVE-016 discipline**: zero bounded waits in the bench tree — every await structurally completes (primary swept all six sites); the fan-out `published == 1 000 × K` guard mutation-proved (killing a drain task fires it); the suite ran cleanly **four consecutive times** on one binary (implementer ×2, primary ×1, orchestrator ×1 after the review corrections — a comment-only edit cannot move codegen, and the run confirmed it: append inside its recorded CI). **Orchestrator gate matrix after the last edit**: `fmt`/`check`/`clippy --workspace --all-targets` 0 with zero warning lines; `dacode-core` **208** + 4 ignored; `dacode-protocol` **121**; `cargo bench` both crates exit 0; `deny`, `dep-audit` + `--self-test`, `check-lints`, `check-links` both modes, `attention` all 0; boundary diff **empty at 27**. **The build order resumes at M1.5** (providers) — the row that lands tokio's `time` feature (§7.8's Q1) and can finally replace the hand-rolled bounded-yield loops with real timeouts (P-LIVE-016's obsoleted-by note)","status":"open","verify":"dual review ✅ B1, B2","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"M1.8","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U183","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E2","kind":"gate","title":"M1.9","body":"🧠 **the agentic loop** (§4.2) — the guard set with real *numbers* behind its config keys, what drives continuation, streaming/execution overlap, synthetic tool results on abort, steering vs hard interrupt. ✅ **Pre-session survey LANDED (2026-10-05, tracker g155): [the-agentic-loop](../../.agents/knowledge/reference-harnesses/the-agentic-loop.md)** — all five harnesses, every guard's default measured (turn ceiling **100** in both harnesses that have one; pause continuations **8**; soft escalations **3**; tool-error budget **3**; stuck-loop **3**-nudge vs **10/20/30**-terminate), U70's two barrier answers (per-mutation sequential-only vs turn-spanning concurrency-safe), **U96's hand-over green field**, U69's two independent stored≠context convergences, and the four guard-firing behaviors (interrupt+persist / disable-tools+tell / grace-round / throw) — the session opens from it, on the U136/U93 precedent of a choice among measured options rather than a blank page. ▶️ **User steer (2026-10-05, on seeing the survey): lean away from compaction, into \"alert the user → handover summary → new session\"** — recorded on U96 and U97; **U96's annotation now carries the two-arm shape** (model-initiated bail-out first, agent-side hard threshold as fallback, one default-on option with run-to-end and compaction as alternates); the session opens with it, and the survey's Part 9 thresholds become the *fallback* threshold's candidates under it. Deepen [08](part2-engine/08-agentic-loop.md) **after** the session, not before. Carries **U96** (the hand-over resume: two normative sections cite a mechanism nobody has designed) and **U69** (the delta fan-out is unbounded while the stored copy is capped), and needs **U70 decided first** — §4.6's checkpoint barrier has named no well-defined call since A2 made calls concurrent and A3 made dispatch incremental. ⚠️ **Inherited from M1.1g (2026-10-04), two TUI-side obligations** (doc 22 §22.5, doc 23 §23.5): **the transcript's markdown rendering** (R-b — this is the row that makes assistant text live, and therefore the row where markdown stops being polish; `pulldown-cmark`, pure Rust, is the recorded candidate) and **the `IdleReason → ExitCode` mapping's live producer** (the function lands tested at M1.11; this row's loop emits the `AgentIdle` the mapping reads). ⚠️ **And M1.1g NARROWS this row's d6 obligation rather than violating it**: M1.11 serves the `SendMessage` handler (append-only, no turn — R-h's first half, because both front doors need it and the actor's `decide` is refuse-by-default today), so d6's \"M1.9 for the actor's command surface\" now means **`Cancel` and the turn-driving half** — the loop's own commands — rather than the whole surface","status":"open","verify":"**🧠 brainstorm**, then dual review","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"M1.9","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U184","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E2","kind":"gate","title":"M1.10","body":"🧠 **the built-in tools** (§4.3) **and the task tree + completion gate** (§4.8) — one session, because `todo` is one of the tools and its arg schema is decided there. Which tools ship, each arg schema, its `Concurrency` key, its permission scope, what `bash:readonly` may actually claim, which item kinds ship *(⚠️ **clarified 2026-09-19 at M1.1a: as a *tool-schema* question — which kinds `todo`'s arguments accept. `TaskKind`'s five variants have been normative since A19 and are fixtured at M1.2, so read as a *wire* question this cell would promise a change to a set M1.2 has already pinned, which is a compatibility event and would have to be raised before M1.3 publishes.** See [03](part1-foundations/03-protocol.md) §3.9.)*, the default tree for a plain vs an orchestrated session, and `task.reminders_max` — the cap being the whole difference between a gate and a hang. **B17's `finding` variant lands from here**, which is what unblocks M1.3's publication. Deepen [14](part3-capabilities/14-tools.md) and [32](part2-engine/32-task-tracking.md) **after**; B6's deferred half (the offline schema generator's write-up) lands with doc 14. ⚠️ **SCOPE INPUT 2026-09-28, AND IT NARROWS THIS SESSION WITHOUT CLOSING IT** (tracker g102): the user is building **`/Users/mouse/RPM/dacode/dacode-todo-mcp`** — a *separate* Rust workspace (`todo-cli`, `todo-domain`, `todo-mcp`, `todo-store`; `rmcp`, stdio transport, its own M1) whose stated purpose is to **replace markdown TODO boards with a deterministic task graph**: \"tasks are typed nodes, dependencies are typed edges … and *what is next in repo X under epic Y* is a query, not a prose parse.\" So the **`todo` arg-schema half of this session largely moves out** — but **§4.8's completion gate does not**, because the `check` semantics, the `Standing` status and `task.reminders_max` are **engine** properties: an MCP server can *store* the graph, while the gate that decides a turn is complete lives in `dacode-core`. This session still owes the gate, §4.3's other built-in tools, `bash:readonly`'s claim, the default trees and `reminders_max`. ⚠️ **AND IT CREATES A CROSS-REPO QUESTION WITH A HARD DEADLINE — the ⚠️ above is no longer hypothetical.** This cell already warned that *which item kinds ship* is a **tool-schema** question because `TaskKind`'s five variants are **normative since A19 and were fixtured at M1.2**, so read as a *wire* question it would promise a change to a set M1.2 has already pinned — a **compatibility event** that \"would have to be raised before M1.3 publishes.\" `todo-mcp-server` has its **own** typed node kinds, so **either it maps onto dacode's five `TaskKind` variants or two repos under one owner have divergent task graphs.** That must be answered **before M1.3**, and M1.3 is *already* ordered after this row for an independent reason (B17's `finding` lands here and unblocks M1.3's publication). **The two constraints compound.** One more ordering fact: §2 puts the **MCP client in `dacode-tools`, this row's crate**, so nothing can integrate the two repos before M1.10 regardless of how the schema question is answered. **Neither item is on the critical path yet** — see g102's queue: Pass D, Pass E, the five g101 rulings, M1.6, M1.7 and M1.11-minus-the-exit-table are all dispatchable without a session. ⚠️ **Inherited from M1.1g (2026-10-04): the diff-preview format constraint** (doc 22's R-c) — M1.11's diff viewer parses `PermissionRequested.preview`'s text as a *unified diff*, so the edit tool this session designs must either emit unified diffs or that parser widens; the same shape as the `TaskKind` constraint above, recorded so the brainstorm sees it","status":"open","verify":"**🧠 brainstorm**, then dual review","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"M1.10","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U185","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E2","kind":"gate","title":"M1.11","body":"the hosts: ratatui TUI (diff viewer, A9's mouse and collapse-on-paste rules, §14.1's `Clients` tier as their test), `run -p`, and §11's exit codes. ⚠️ **U93 is owed before the exit-code table is written** — a refused lock cannot be an `IdleReason`, so giving it a code ends the property that the exit-code space has one source of truth, *and* it overturns B21's signed sub-answer 5 (\"both: error plus `attach`\"). Doc 23 keeps its depth marker until then. ⚠️ **U136 is owed at this row's opening, before the sessions sidebar is built**: doc 22's sidebar and design §10's layout box assume a session list, but `Command` has no `ListSessions` variant, `Snapshot` carries one `SessionInfo`, and `dacode-tui` may depend on `dacode-protocol` only — so either a protocol surface lands here (cheap before M1.3 publishes) or doc 22 and §10 record that the sidebar shows registry-resident sessions only. **U123's mechanism owner is whichever row that turns out to be.** ✅ **DOC PRE-FLIGHT DONE (M1.1g, 2026-10-04) — and the walk re-measured U136, changing its weight.** Docs [22](part4-hosts-clients/22-tui.md) and [23](part4-hosts-clients/23-cli.md) are deepened and are the row's instruction, both markers retained (doc 22's held by U136, doc 23's by U93). **U136's arm (a) is heavier than its filing implied**: `Ack` is `Result<(), String>` so no command returns data, and the event stream is per-session so a cross-session list has no stream to ride — arm (a) is a *new protocol concept* (a data-carrying `Ack` or a session-less channel), not \"one variant self-registers\"; arm (b) is refined the same way (§10's three sources are all in one `Snapshot`; the real list consumers have non-protocol routes — the dashboard over `/api/sessions` at M3, `dacode session list` reading the `pub` `store` catalog). **Recommendation arm (b), four grounds in doc 22 §22.11; the user rules, on the M1.7 Step 0 precedent.** What the row's code passes inherit, all ruled in the docs: **R-a** the `Engine` facade (d6's widening choice made by the row that has a caller, with f160's review obligation at the first callers), **R-b** plain-text transcript (markdown is M1.9's inheritance), **R-c** the diff viewer over `Preview` (M1.10 inherits the format constraint), **R-d/R-e** mouse and paste (crossterm's five-mode superset recorded; the 1 MiB cap), **R-f** scrollbox + coalescing, **R-g** the `SendMessage` handler (append-only, no turn — the actor's `decide` is refuse-by-default, so the one command both front doors need is this row's to serve) + the slash dispatch set, **R-h** the Clients-tier harness (`TestBackend` + command-buffer assertions, plus the §14.1 precision edit). Doc 23's `run -p` is the live harness whose turn is M1.9's, with the `IdleReason → ExitCode` mapping landed as a tested function. **No `/serve`, no `attach`, no markdown, no live turns at this row** — doc 22 §22.2's must-not list is the scope contract. Dependency sets measured (ratatui 0.30.2 + crossterm 0.29 = 74 packages, ~46 net-new names; clap 4.6.7 = 21, ~11 net-new; `cargo deny` green, zero C markers, the new class-2 names predicted for the exempt file with the scan deciding). ✅ **STEP 0 LANDED (2026-10-04): U136 ruled arm (b) by the user on g147's recommendation** — the sidebar is the fold's tree (the current session + sub-agents + tasks + steps, all from the one `Snapshot`); design §10 carries the scoping sentence (no protocol surface enumerates sessions; the cross-session list belongs to §9.2's dashboard over `/api/sessions` and §11's `dacode session list`, both reading the store directly); **U123's owner is M3**; doc 22's marker flipped with the ruling (§4: 21 → 20) and U136 closed citing it. **The row's remaining work is ordinary code under dual review** — the engine half first (R-a's `Engine` facade, R-g's `SendMessage` handler — f160's review event at the facade's first callers), then the TUI passes, then doc 23's `run -p`; U93 still gates the exit-code table at the row's tail. ✅ **PASS A LANDED (2026-10-04, tracker t312, dual review — three rounds, zero logic defects): the engine half both front doors need.** `engine.rs` (R-a's `pub` `Engine` facade, the registry staying `pub(crate)`), `actor_send.rs` (R-g's `SendMessage` handler — the canonical user-turn **pair**, `TurnStarted { role: User }` + `UserMessage`, both reviewers upholding the implementer's ruling that the fold drops a bare message), and **f160's deletions** (the `registry`/`actor`/`replay` module-level exemptions gone with their first production callers, 8 fallout items item-level with named removers). **dacode-core 336+4 → 348+4; workspace 715 → 727; lock/boundary/`event.rs`/fixtures all unmoved.** **U158 closed** (the false remover comment deleted outright); **U169 filed** (`SessionCreated`/`WriterEpoch`/`sandbox` have no producer — the binary-bridge pass owns them); the command-`session`-field routing recorded for **M3's** connection layer. ✅ **PASS B LANDED (2026-10-04, tracker t319/g152, dual review — two rounds, the fix round trimming ratatui's unused `macros`/`underline-color`): the `dacode-tui` crate.** 11 production files + 10 test siblings (5 446 lines), 99 tests including both §14.1 Clients-tier properties (mutation-verified 5/5) and the corpus driver; the crate is **executor-free** (no `tokio` normal dep — §2.2's letter; the `App` is a sync drain machine, `TuiClient` async-fn-in-trait, crossterm re-exported for the binary's loop); **lock 151 → 211** (the M1.1g feature-set prediction superseded: `underline-color`'s optional tail was 63 packages, trimmed as unused — f332), **boundary 40 → 42**, zero new exempt rows (the scan decided), §14.1's precision edit landed, workspace 727 → 826. **f333 filed** (§10's red-`check` indicator + `Verify::Manual` close key — owner M1.10, no code owed); d26/d27 recorded. ✅ **BINARY-BRIDGE LANDED (2026-10-04, tracker g153, dual review — two rounds + an orchestrator fix round): the `dacode` binary's two front doors.** 10 files / 1 533 lines, 16 unit + 3 integration tests incl. §23.8's gate (the spawned binary's jsonl stream asserted envelope-by-envelope, byte-identical to the log's prefix); **U169's three producers** at the registry's open path (`WriterEpoch { folded+1 }` then `SessionCreated`, origins `Some(created_by)`/`None`, `model` the canonical join, `sandbox` on `Session.sandbox` — a recorded supersession of the M2 schedule); **clap 4.6.7 in the tree (lock 211 → 220, +9 names), boundary 42 → 45** (`dacode -> clap/tokio/futures-util`), ratatui a `dacode-tui` re-export; workspace 826 → 851. f334 (`SessionClosed` is log-truth, no row yet), f335/f336 (M3), f337 (fixed), d28 (PTY debt), **U170** (`DACODE_HOME`'s §12 note) filed; **U169 closed**. **What remains of M1.11 is U93's exit-code table alone** — the row's tail, the user's; doc 23's depth marker stays until it lands. ✅ **TAIL LANDED (2026-10-04, tracker t334/g149): U93 ruled arm (b), value `4`** — §11's table extended with the pre-session value and the one-source-of-truth property **restated** (`0–3` the `IdleReason` mapping, `4` the single refusal — one value, not a family), doc 23 §23.5's pending shape became the ruled record and **the marker flipped** (§4: 20 → 19), B21's sub-answer 5 annotated at both its sites (exit-code half superseded, the fail-fast/holder-named/`attach` machinery intact), the fork-of-held-parent behavior question deferred to M3's fork row with the owner named, and **no code moved** (`exit.rs`'s six-arm mapping untouched; `4`'s first producer is M3's `session fork`/resume — `run` creates a new session and cannot hit the lock). **THE M1.11 ROW IS COMPLETE** — Step 0 + Passes A/B + the binary-bridge pass + the tail; the §1 banner of this date carries the narrative","status":"open","verify":"dual review ✅ Step 0 · ✅ **Pass A** (facade, 2026-10-04) · ✅ **Pass B** (the TUI crate, 2026-10-04) · ✅ **binary-bridge** (2026-10-04) · ✅ **U93 tail** (2026-10-04) — **the row is COMPLETE**","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"M1.11","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U186","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"the two-tier event model","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-18**, on [B3](B-decisions.md)'s sign-off of 2026-09-17, with\n> **A25's M1 half riding along** as its own banner required and **§16 item 6's\n> `Standing` status** landing where it is normative. A25 is the entry after A22 below,\n> cited as plain text rather than by anchor, because an applied heading gains a suffix\n> that moves the slug. **The \"before any golden fixture\" precondition held**: no\n> fixture exists, because the crates are still stubs. Ten spec sections were edited —\n> §3.1 (`HostId`, `SessionRef`, `Cursor` restated), §3.2 (`EventTier`, `tier()`,\n> `seq: Option<Cursor>`, `TextEnded`/`ThinkingEnded`, `WriterEpoch`, the tier rule),\n> §3.5 (`Snapshot.epoch`, one-fold-two-tiers), §3.6 (durable-only log, `LogHeader`,\n> the three fencing obligations), §3.7 (both resync triggers), §4.3 (`HostId`\n> excluded from the scan, for now), §4.8 (`Standing`), §12 (the header in the\n> layout), §13 + §13.2 (the persistence claim and the `Json` alias), §14 (fixture\n> rule + three tests) — plus §1's principle 2, which asserted that *every* event is\n> in the log.\n>\n> **Six scoping decisions taken while transcribing. Four were put to the user and\n> answered 2026-09-18; two are fold-ins the delta did not mention.**\n>\n> 1. **`seq` became `Option<Cursor>`, which the delta as drafted did not say.** It\n>    said \"add a tier marker\" and was silent on the cursor of an event that is never\n>    appended. A18's §3.7 makes a missing `seq` the *only* gap signal, so numbering\n>    deltas would have put permanent holes in the log's sequence and made clients\n>    resync forever — precisely the failure §3.6 names as worse than a corrupt log,\n>    because the bytes still parse. A18's own step 1 (\"the engine assigns `seq` when\n>    it appends to the log\") already implied durable-only; this states it. The\n>    self-heal that makes a cursor-less delta safe is the boundary **replacing**\n>    rather than extending a block's text, so a duplicated or dropped delta costs\n>    nothing.\n> 2. **The tier is a rule, not a list: `Durable` iff the fold consumes it.** Chosen\n>    over enumerating ~30 variants so that A14's session-organization events and\n>    B17's `finding` classify themselves rather than needing this entry reopened. It\n>    leaves exactly four `Live` variants. Two classifications needed an argument\n>    rather than an inspection and are written into §3.2 for that reason:\n>    `ContextInfo` is durable (it is in `Snapshot.context`, so the rule decides it),\n>    and **`Error` is live** — every error that *changes state* already has a durable\n>    carrier in `TurnFinished.stop` or `AgentIdle.reason`, so one that changes\n>    nothing is a diagnostic. `Error` is the only classification in this delta that\n>    is expensive to reverse, and the user confirmed it explicitly.\n> 3. **Only the epoch is wire-visible** of A25(e)'s three mechanisms. `WriterEpoch`\n>    as a durable event plus `Snapshot.epoch`, with an epoch *decrease* as a second\n>    resync trigger beside the `seq` gap. The **idempotency key** and the **leaf\n>    CAS** are stated in §3.6 as obligations on the store API, with the mechanism\n>    left to doc 05 — neither is something a client renders, so putting them on the\n>    wire would have grown `Envelope` for no reader. B21's `flock` is **restated** as\n>    the local mechanism, not weakened or replaced.\n> 4. **A25(a) took a third answer, and the user approved the deviation.** Its entry\n>    lists \"a location component or an opaque handle\"; both break A23's requirement\n>    that the four scanned ids stay **exact-matchable**. What landed is neither:\n>    `SessionId(Ulid)` is byte-identical, and the *route* is separate —\n>    `HostId` + `SessionRef { host, session }`, used only where a command may name a\n>    remote session. A ULID is already globally unique, so it can name a session\n>    anywhere; what it lacked is a route, and a route is a host attribute — the same\n>    shape A25 itself gives the model endpoint. §4.3 gained one clause recording that\n>    `HostId` joins the scanned set when a tool argument can name a host (M5).\n> 5. **U66 folded in: the log gains a header record.** Not in the delta, but §6 owed\n>    it to A1 *by name* (\"A1 is the natural place … cheaper before the first fixture\n>    exists\"), and the epoch needed a durable initial record anyway, so the two share\n>    a mechanism. Line 1 of `events.jsonl` is now a `LogHeader` carrying\n>    `format_version` — **the same counter §7.2's `Hello` sends**, so a log and a wire\n>    version cannot drift. ⚠️ **It edits a sentence A24 landed one day earlier**: the\n>    header carries no cursor and occupies a line, so §3.6 no longer says the cursor\n>    \"equals line number + 1\" but that it is \"derivable by arithmetic from the\n>    record's position\". The invariant A24 was protecting — do not weld the cursor to\n>    the JSONL representation — is strengthened by the change, not weakened, since a\n>    fixed `+1` was exactly such a weld.\n> 6. **U27's §3.2 half discharged:** `ToolCall { args: Json }` → `args: RawJson`.\n>    §13.2's alias note is **narrowed, not deleted** — §4.3's `perm_scope`/`call`\n>    signatures still spell `Json` and are A2's to rename, so U27 stays open on that\n>    half alone.\n>\n> **Debt this creates, recorded rather than left to the next reader.**\n> **A12** strikes §16 item 6: `Standing` is normative in §4.8 now (goals only — a\n> non-`Goal` in `Standing` is rejected, because applied to a task it is a way to dodge\n> the checker), and §16's text is deliberately *left in place* so the bookkeeping pass\n> owns the removal in one piece rather than piecemeal. Until then `attention.py`\n> renders §16.6 as an open vote — **U95's false positive, accepted by the user.**\n> ~~**A14** owes three *log* fixtures and needs no tier decision, the rule supplying it.~~\n> ✅ **Discharged the same day** — A14 applied 2026-09-18 and took exactly that path:\n> the rule classified all three events `Durable` with no tier decision to take, and §14's\n> derivation put them in the log fixture set with no list to update.\n> **Doc 05** (already expanded) should carry the `LogHeader` and the\n> epoch/idempotency/CAS mechanism when M1 starts. **Doc 03 stays a summary** despite\n> its `[DESIGN DELTA]` marker now being stale — flipping it would deepen an unstarted\n> milestone's doc, which hard rule 1 forbids; the marker is corrected when M1 starts.\n\n**Current.** §3.2 (`:89-124`) is a single flat `Event` enum mixing live deltas\n(`TextDelta`, `ThinkingDelta`, `ToolOutputDelta`) with durable events\n(`TurnStarted`, `ToolCall`, `ToolResult`, `TurnFinished`, `Compacted`) and no\ntier marking. §3.7 (`:212-219`) reconnects via `GetSnapshot` → `Subscribe\n{ since }` and asserts \"No gap, no duplicate\" but specifies no gap *detection*.\n\n**Proposed.**\n- Add a tier marker so the log writer and replay path can filter without\n  matching every variant: `Event::tier() -> Tier { Durable, Live }`, or a\n  `durable: bool` on `Envelope`. Prefer the **method on `Event`** — the tier is\n  a property of the variant, not of the envelope instance, and a method cannot\n  be set inconsistently by a producer.\n- State the replay rule: **live deltas are never persisted**; on replay a client\n  reconstructs the final value from the durable boundary, never from deltas.\n- Add the **full-value boundary variants** that B3 requires and §3.2 lacks:\n  `TextEnded { turn, text }` and `ThinkingEnded { turn, text }`. `ToolResult`\n  already serves as the boundary for tool output. Without these, a client that\n  misses the deltas cannot render the turn from the log alone — and since live\n  deltas are never persisted, *replay has nothing else to render from*.\n  *(Originally justified here as breaking §3.7's \"no gap\" claim; A18 restated\n  that claim as a property of `seq` continuity rather than of value\n  completeness, so the two are distinct guarantees and this bullet stands on its\n  own.)*\n- ~~§3.7: adopt **seq-numbered events + client-side gap detection → resync**\n  (openclaw `gateway-client/src/protocol-client.ts:413-425`) as the reconnect\n  contract, so a silently half-open WS is recovered rather than rendering stale\n  state.~~ **→ moved to A18 (§3.7/§4.1 below), ✅ applied 2026-09-12 — this\n  bullet is discharged.** A18 expanded it into the full bounded-fan-out contract\n  and was signed off independently of B3/B4, so this delta now carries only the\n  tier marker and the `*Ended` boundary variants.\n  *(Deliberately a plain-text cross-reference, not an in-page anchor: these\n  headings gain a resolution suffix when the delta is applied, which changes the\n  slug and silently breaks any anchor pointing at them. See [TODO](../TODO.md)\n  §6's repo-hygiene traps.)*\n\n**Depends on.** B3 (which this delta effectively resolves). ~~and B4 (the resync\ncontract is what makes WS safe to keep)~~ — **B4 resolved 2026-09-12** (WS kept),\nand its resync half landed in A18, so neither blocks this any more.\n\n**Recommend accept.** This is the most structural delta in the set: it changes\nthe `Event` enum and the fold, and everything downstream ([03](../part1-foundations/03-protocol.md),\n[05](../part2-engine/05-event-log-storage.md), [08](../part2-engine/08-agentic-loop.md))\nassumes it. Apply it before any golden fixtures are written (§14) — retrofitting\na tier marker into existing fixtures is exactly the \"compatibility event\" §14\nwarns about. ✅ **Done 2026-09-18** — applied with the six scoping decisions above,\nand the precondition held: the crates are still stubs, so no fixture was retrofitted.\nThe `Current.` paragraph below describes a §3.2 that no longer exists and is kept as\nthe record of what changed.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-18","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§12","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§13","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§14","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.1","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.2","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.5","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.6","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.7","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.3","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.8","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A1","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U187","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"concurrency policy instead of a bool","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-18**, the first of the engine group. The amendment landed\n> exactly as signed — `parallel_safe() -> bool` became\n> `fn concurrency(&self, args: &RawJson) -> Concurrency` over `Shared`, `Exclusive`\n> and `Keyed(ConcurrencyKey)`, with the **tool computing its own key** rather than\n> returning a closure. **Its obligation on the transcription is discharged**: §4.3 now\n> carries the author guidance — what each variant means, how to compute a key, why the\n> key must be canonical, and what the default is — because an undocumented policy enum\n> gets defaulted to `Exclusive` everywhere and the parallelism is silently lost.\n>\n> **Four scoping decisions taken while transcribing.**\n>\n> 1. **No wire variant was added, so the audit-trail benefit is stated rather than\n>    built.** The amendment's argument for a value over a closure is that the key is\n>    \"loggable in the `PermissionResolved`/audit trail\". Adding a field to\n>    `PermissionResolved` would have been a wire change with a fixture, for something\n>    the delta argued *for* but never proposed. What landed is the property — a key is a\n>    value, so it **can** be written into the trail, where a closure can only be\n>    described — with §4.3 saying so and the actual recording left to §4.4's audit path\n>    and doc 08 when it is deepened. If a concurrency decision turns out to need its own\n>    durable event, that is a new delta, not a reading of this one.\n> 2. **Which tool gets which policy is brainstorm-owned content and is marked pending,\n>    not invented.** §1's deferral banner allows a transcription to land the approved\n>    *shape* while marking content for the session, and names \"which tools ship\" as\n>    exactly that class. So §4.3 specifies the enum, the default, the canonical-key\n>    requirement and the three tests that pin the semantics — and **does not assign a\n>    policy to `read`, `edit`, `bash` or anything else.** A2's own proposal used two\n>    `edit`s as its worked example, so §14's tests use it too, but that is a test of the\n>    mechanism and not a policy assignment. §4.8 written self-contained by A19 is the\n>    precedent that this works.\n> 3. **U27's second half discharged, and §13.2's alias note dropped rather than\n>    narrowed again.** A1 renamed §3.2's `ToolCall.args`; this renames §4.3's\n>    `perm_scope` and `call` (and `concurrency`, which is new). With no bare `Json` left\n>    anywhere in the spec, the note that defined the alias has no site — verified by\n>    `rg '\\bJson\\b'` over the spec returning nothing outside `RawJson`/`dacode-json` —\n>    so it is replaced by the rule it was covering for: opaque sub-JSON is always\n>    spelled `RawJson`, and spelling it out at each site is the point. U27 closes on\n>    both halves.\n> 4. **Two files outside the plan were stale the moment this landed, and one of them\n>    reaches every coding agent in the repository.** The banner named\n>    `.agents/roles/_rust.md`, which told every agent that \"tools that can run\n>    concurrently declare `parallel_safe`\"; it is corrected, and\n>    `.agents/skills/dacode/SKILL.md`'s trait snippet with it, and the four **generated**\n>    `.opencode/agent/*.md` adapters regenerated by `tools/agents-sync.py`. This is the\n>    same class as A16's `_rust.md` false-type caveat: a role file is loaded into every\n>    subagent's context, so a stale line there is not a doc lag, it is an instruction\n>    being given to every worker. **Doc 08 needed more than a marker flip** — it\n>    specified `Concurrency::Dynamic(fn(&args) -> Key)`, the closure arm the amendment\n>    *rejects*, so a reader would have implemented the rejected design. Corrected with a\n>    strike-and-annotate, **marker and depth untouched**: doc 08 is still a migrated\n>    summary and M1 has not started, and removing one false line adds no depth. **Doc\n>    14's content was already correct** (it names `concurrency`), so only its\n>    `[DESIGN DELTA]` marker is stale and it stays, per A1's doc-03 precedent.\n>\n> **Debt this creates, recorded rather than left to the next reader.** **Doc 08** owes\n> the dispatch algorithm when it is deepened — §4.2's pseudocode now says calls are\n> partitioned by policy and scheduled, but *how* an `Exclusive` call interleaves with a\n> stream still arriving, and whether a `Keyed` group may start while an `Exclusive`\n> drains, is loop content and belongs to the 🧠 session. **A3 owns reconciling its guard\n> set with both §4.8's gate and this dispatch order**, since all three now sit in §4.2's\n> pseudocode. **Doc 14's** marker is corrected when M1 deepens it, and it owes the\n> per-tool policy table the brainstorm produces. **A3/A4 still owe §4.8 tightened\n> cross-references** (A19's scoping decision 1), unchanged by this.\n\n**Current.** §4.3 (`:287`) `fn parallel_safe(&self) -> bool { false }`; §4.2\n(`:259`) \"parallel where tools are marked `parallel_safe`\".\n\n**Proposed.** Replace with a per-tool **concurrency policy** resolved at\ndispatch (oh-my-pi `agent-loop.ts:2903-2929`): `Shared` runs concurrently with\nother shared calls; `Exclusive` serializes behind the running shared set;\nanything mutating the working tree defaults to `Exclusive`. Two `edit`s on\ndifferent files are shared, on the same file exclusive.\n\n**Amendment.** [08](../part2-engine/08-agentic-loop.md) sketches the third arm\nas `Concurrency::Dynamic(fn(&args) -> Key)` — a closure, which is awkward to\nexpress as a trait return value and cannot be `Debug`/logged. Have the **tool\ncompute the key itself**:\n\n```rust\npub enum Concurrency { Shared, Exclusive, Keyed(ConcurrencyKey) }\n// in trait Tool:\nfn concurrency(&self, args: &RawJson) -> Concurrency { Concurrency::Exclusive }\n```\n\n`ConcurrencyKey` is an opaque interned string (the file path, the host, the\nlock name). Same expressiveness, no closure in the signature, and the key is\nloggable in the `PermissionResolved`/audit trail. §4.2's pseudocode line 259\nchanges with it.\n\n**Depends on.** Nothing. Independent of every B decision — and it turned out to be\nindependent of A1 too, since it adds no `Event` or `Command` variant and so needs no\nfixture and no tier decision.\n\n**Recommend accept, amended as above.** **Done 2026-09-18**, with the amendment and\nits documentation obligation both discharged, and with the four scoping decisions in\nthe banner. **The \"Current.\" and \"Proposed.\" text below is historical**: the line\nnumbers predate four applied deltas, and the `Dynamic(fn)` arm it quotes from doc 08\nis the one the amendment rejects.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-18","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§13.2","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§14","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.2","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.3","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A2","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U188","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"loop guards, overlap, synthetic tool results, steering","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-18**, the second of the engine group. Everything the sign-off\n> approved landed as proposed: the guard set, continuation driven by work remaining\n> rather than a step count, streaming/execution overlap with the transcript fragment\n> committed **before** a tool's side effects start, the pairing invariant with\n> **synthetic tool results** on every abandoning path, and steering as distinct from a\n> hard cancel. **Six spec sections, against the one the delta named** — because the\n> guard set needs types §3.2 never defined, a commit boundary §3.6 never listed, an\n> exit-code mapping §11 never stated, and the reconciliation with §4.8 that A19 left\n> owed.\n>\n> **Four scoping decisions taken while transcribing.**\n>\n> 1. **Two types the spec used but never defined had to be created, and A3 could not\n>    land without them.** `StopReason` appears in `TurnFinished { stop }` and §4.2's\n>    pseudocode branches on `stop == EndTurn`, but no enum existed anywhere — and this\n>    delta's synthetic-result rule fires \"on any abort / **`length`** stop / error tail\",\n>    which is a `StopReason` value that did not exist. `IdleReason` was the same, its five\n>    variants listed only in a trailing comment. Worse, §4.2 already called\n>    `emit AgentIdle(NeedsInput, \"turn limit\")` — a **two-argument shape the one-field\n>    variant could not hold** — so the spec had been describing an event it did not have.\n>    Both are now real enums, with `MaxTokens`/`Paused`/`Cancelled`/`Error` named in\n>    `StopReason` and `ToolOutput` + `SyntheticReason` defined beside them. This is the\n>    same class as `SessionInfo` being a comment until A14 needed it: a type mentioned in\n>    a signature and never defined is invisible until a delta has to add a variant, and\n>    that delta pays for every earlier one that did not.\n> 2. **`AgentIdle` gained a typed `guard: Option<GuardKind>` — a wire change to an\n>    existing variant, recorded rather than folded in.** A guard termination has to reach\n>    the client, and there were three shapes. Extending `IdleReason` with six guard\n>    variants **breaks §11**, whose four exit codes map onto it, and §9.2's five status\n>    chips. An untyped detail string **violates principle 3** — a reason a UI must parse\n>    to render distinctly is hidden state behind a fold. So `IdleReason` stays coarse and\n>    aligned to the exit codes and chips, and the guard is a separate typed field. It is\n>    `Option` and omitted when `None` (§13.5's convention), so an ordinary `Done` is\n>    byte-identical to before and the change is additive on the wire. **`GuardKind`\n>    growing is a config-and-code change; `IdleReason` growing is a contract change in\n>    two places** — that asymmetry is the point of the split and is now written into both\n>    sections.\n> 3. **The guard numbers are marked pending, not invented.** §1's deferral names \"the\n>    guard numbers\" as brainstorm-owned content explicitly, so what landed is the *shape*\n>    — a table of eight guards, each with a config key, a firing condition and the\n>    `IdleReason` it exits through — and the reference-harness values\n>    (`MAX_PAUSED_TURN_CONTINUATIONS=8`, `MAX_SOFT_TOOL_ESCALATIONS=3`) stay in the plan\n>    as candidates. What is normative is that a guard is a **named, configured, reported**\n>    termination, so tuning one is a config change and adding one is never a wire change.\n> 4. **The hand-over resume is a slot, not a mechanism.** `GuardKind::HandOver` exists so\n>    the termination has somewhere to report through, and §4.2 says plainly that what the\n>    resume contains, whether it is durable, whether bail-out is a guard or a tool call,\n>    and how it meets `Fork` and §3.6's single-writer lock are **not specified here**.\n>    Inventing them would have pre-empted the 🧠 session the sign-off explicitly did not\n>    bypass, and would have created an `Event` variant needing a fixture for a design\n>    nobody has done. **U96 stays open and is annotated, not closed.**\n>\n> **Two debts discharged, both owed by other deltas.** **A19's**: §4.8 was written\n> self-contained because it landed before the guard set existed, and its gate is now\n> reconciled — §4.8 still owns *when* `check` is evaluated and the reminder escalation,\n> while §4.2 owns the termination, which fires `GuardKind::CompletionGate`. So \"the gate\n> is hard but bounded\" became a typed event rather than a property of pseudocode, and\n> `task.reminders_max` is one row in the guard table. **A2's**, created yesterday: its\n> dispatch ordering sits in the same pseudocode block, so §4.2 now shows scheduling under\n> §4.3's policies *and* guard evaluation in one flow, with the pairing step between them\n> — which is where the two interact, since a guard firing with calls outstanding is\n> exactly the case that needs synthetic results.\n>\n> **Debt this creates, recorded rather than left to the next reader.** **The 🧠\n> agentic-loop session** owns the guard numbers, the stuck-loop detector's actual\n> heuristic (`loop.stuck_loop_*` is a key with no content behind it), and everything in\n> **U96**. **A4** must land compaction as *one mechanism* with the hand-over — §4.2 now\n> says so in the section rather than only in this banner — and `compact()` no longer\n> appears in the pseudocode at all, so A4 has to put it back deliberately rather than find\n> it. **Doc 08** owes the dispatch algorithm and the steering/interrupt detail when it is\n> deepened; its `[DESIGN DELTA]` marker for this delta is now stale and stays, per A1's\n> doc-03 precedent. **Doc 03** is now five deltas out of date (M1.1). **§3.2's `Live` set\n> is unchanged** — no variant was added, only typed, so A1's \"exactly four\" still holds.\n\n**Current.** §4.2 (`:251-267`) terminates on `if turn_count > agent.max_turns`;\ncancellation at `:276-278` drops the provider stream and kills tools by process\ngroup.\n\n**Proposed.**\n- **Reframe termination as a guard set**, not one counter. Keep `max_turns` as a\n  hard ceiling and add: a **deadline** timer; a **pause-continuation cap** for\n  non-terminal `pause_turn`; a **soft-tool-escalation cap**; a **per-tool error\n  budget**; and a **loop-recovery intervention** that escalates warning →\n  terminate on a detected stuck loop. Sources: oh-my-pi\n  (`config.deadline`, `MAX_PAUSED_TURN_CONTINUATIONS=8`,\n  `MAX_SOFT_TOOL_ESCALATIONS=3`), forge (`ToolErrorTracker`), openclaw\n  (`critical-tool-loop`).\n- **Continuation is driven by \"more tool calls, or pending steering/follow-up\n  messages?\"**, not by a step count (oh-my-pi\n  `while hasMoreToolCalls || pendingMessages`; openclaw's outer follow-up queue +\n  inner tool loop).\n- **Overlap streaming with execution**: enqueue async tool batches while the\n  model is still streaming, and **commit the transcript fragment that owns a tool\n  call before its side effects start**, so a crash never loses the record of\n  what was attempted (openclaw `agent-stream-response.ts:178-218,296-333`).\n- **The tool-pairing invariant is sacred.** On any abort / `length` stop / error\n  tail, **synthesize placeholder tool results** so every `tool_use` has a\n  matching `tool_result` — providers reject unpaired histories (oh-my-pi\n  `createSyntheticToolResultMessage` with a `__synthetic` discriminator). This\n  needs a representation in §3.2 too: a synthetic marker on `ToolResult`.\n- **Steering vs interrupt**: a cooperative steering signal (a mid-batch user\n  message that skips and releases remaining calls) is distinct from a hard\n  cancel that kills side-effecting tools cleanly. §4.2 has only the hard one.\n\n**Depends on.** Nothing, though the synthetic-result marker rides in on A1 — ✅ **and\nit did**: A1 landed first, so the marker arrived as a `ToolOutput::Synthetic` variant\nclassified `Durable` by the tier rule with no tier decision to take, and §14's\nderivation gives it a *log* fixture for free. It also turned out to depend on **A2**\nin the other direction — not for its content but for its *site*: A2 had rewritten\n§4.2's dispatch ordering the day before, so this delta had to reconcile the guard set\nwith it rather than with the pseudocode it was drafted against.\n\n**Recommend accept.** Every one of these is a bug class dacode would otherwise\nrediscover in production; all four harnesses converged on them independently. **Done\n2026-09-18**, to six sections rather than the one named here, with the four scoping\ndecisions in the banner. **The \"Current.\" and \"Proposed.\" text below is historical** —\nits line numbers predate five applied deltas, and its \"this needs a representation in\n§3.2 too\" is now the `ToolOutput`/`SyntheticReason` pair plus two enums the spec had\nbeen using without defining.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-18","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§11","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§14","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.2","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.6","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.2","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.8","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A3","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U189","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"prompt caching + expanded compaction","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-18**, the engine group's last. **The caching half landed as\n> proposed** — the stable fingerprinted prefix with breakpoints, append-only message\n> growth, longest-stable-prefix resync, volatility hygiene as engine invariants, and\n> `Usage` gaining both cache counters. **Both amendments to the compaction half landed\n> as amended**, not as originally proposed: compaction is opt-in and *not* v1's\n> default, and partial compaction is a candidate awaiting a measurement rather than an\n> adopted feature. **B14 folded in** as the buffer representation. Six spec sections\n> against the one the delta named.\n>\n> **Four scoping decisions taken while transcribing.**\n>\n> 1. **The dependency this delta's own banner warns about twice was already\n>    discharged, five days before it was signed.** Both the banner and the\n>    `Depends on` line say A4 \"must be applied **together with B6**'s canonical\n>    byte-stable tool schema or the ordering invariant is unenforceable\". But §13.3 has\n>    carried exactly that since **A16 landed on 2026-09-12** — \"Generated schemas must be\n>    **canonical and byte-stable**: `title` stripped, key order pinned. Tool-definition\n>    churn, *including pure reordering*, destroys the cache-stable prefix (§13.4), so\n>    byte-stability is a caching requirement and earns a golden fixture (§14), not a\n>    comment\" — and §13.4 independently states that \"anything volatile — timestamps,\n>    per-request ids, **tool ordering** — stays out of it\". §14's `xtask` bullet already\n>    promises the golden-output test §13.3 says the schema earns. **So A4 cites §13.3\n>    and §13.4 rather than re-adding them.** Transcribing the warning literally would\n>    have duplicated normative text in a second section and left the next reader to\n>    reconcile two copies. The generalisation is the mirror image of A15's experience:\n>    a delta's `Depends on` cell describes the world when it was *drafted*, so the\n>    pre-flight must check whether each named dependency has already been discharged by\n>    an earlier application.\n> 2. **Three items composed into a hole none of them stated, and the resolution is\n>    forced by amendments already signed.** Amendment (a) makes compaction opt-in and\n>    says v1's answer to a filling context is A3's hand-over. But **A3 landed the\n>    hand-over as a slot, not a mechanism** (`GuardKind::HandOver`, deliberately —\n>    designing it would have pre-empted the 🧠 session). And **U68** says a token-estimate\n>    under-run has no first-class failure path, prescribing \"treat overflow as retryable,\n>    **compact**, and re-issue\" — which amendment (a) has just made non-default. So v1\n>    had *no* automatic response to a filling context, and both candidates were\n>    unavailable. What landed: overflow is a **retryable provider error with two\n>    branches** — compact-and-re-issue when compaction is enabled, otherwise terminate\n>    through §4.2's guard set **with a remedy the user can act on** (`/compact`, a\n>    hand-over, a fork) rather than failing opaquely mid-turn after the tool work is\n>    already paid for. §6 states it as a provider-level path and §4.5 as the estimate's\n>    consequence, and §6 records why overflow is *not* a 429: backing off and retrying\n>    the same request cannot succeed. **U68 closes**; the honest statement it forced is\n>    that **v1's default answer to a filling context is \"stop and tell the user\"**.\n> 3. **`Usage` was referenced by two events and never defined — the same defect A3\n>    fixed twice the day before.** `TurnFinished { usage: Usage }` and\n>    `SubAgentFinished { usage: Usage }` both named a type with no definition anywhere,\n>    so A4's cache fields had nowhere to go. It is now a real struct with four counters,\n>    and the reason all four are on the *event* rather than being a §6 computation detail\n>    is stated: pricing a cached read at the input rate is not imprecision but a wrong\n>    number that overstates exactly the saving the cache exists to deliver, and a wrong\n>    cost is worse than an absent one because it is believed. **This closes U71**, which\n>    said precisely that and offered \"either A4 first, or `cost_usd` stays `None`\".\n> 4. **Compaction's trigger survives but its default does not.** `compact_at` and\n>    `/compact` stay, because an opt-in feature still needs a threshold; what changed is\n>    that automatic compaction is **off by default** and §4.5 now says so in terms rather\n>    than leaving \"compacts at 80 %\" standing as v1's behaviour. The five rules the\n>    proposal listed all landed (append-only boundary, turn-aligned tool-pair-safe cut,\n>    prune-before-summarize with a protected window and savings floor, an incremental\n>    structured summary that later compactions *update* under a hard cap, and a retention\n>    floor). **Partial compaction is recorded as a candidate with both hazards named** —\n>    it rewrites bytes already sent, so it must go through truncate-and-re-emit and never\n>    a byte-rewrite; and it destroys the cache prefix from the cut point, trading a cache\n>    miss for context space, a trade §14.2's hit-rate metric is the instrument for. **U97\n>    stays open and is not adopted before that number exists.**\n>\n> **One debt discharged, owed jointly with A3.** A19 wrote §4.8 self-contained because\n> it landed before the sections it needed existed, and recorded that A3 and A4 own\n> tightening those into real cross-references. A3 did the guard-set half; this did the\n> other two — §4.8's \"context rendering never rewrites bytes already sent\" now names\n> itself as §4.5's append-only rule applied to one producer, says why a status flip costs\n> **one segment** in B14's index rather than a rewrite, and places the rendering *outside*\n> the fingerprinted prefix under §5.6's rule for volatile content. **B14 is thereby\n> applied too**, and its own sign-off's second consequence landed with it: §5.6's\n> environment block is placed after the prefix and **excluded from the fingerprint**, so\n> switching branches costs one segment rather than the whole prompt.\n>\n> **Debt this creates, recorded rather than left to the next reader.** **U96 is now\n> load-bearing in a way it was not yesterday**: §4.5 names the hand-over as v1's answer to\n> a filling context and §6's overflow path offers it as a remedy, so an undesigned\n> mechanism is now cited by two normative sections. It stays open and the 🧠 agentic-loop\n> session owns it — but the session is no longer refining an idea, it is filling a slot\n> the spec points at. ~~**§14.2 does not exist yet** (A21 is unapplied), so the cache-hit-rate\n> metric that partial compaction and the T2 tier both depend on is cited by section number\n> rather than by content; **A21 must place it**~~ ✅ **A21 applied later the same day and\n> §14.2 exists**, naming the hit-rate metric, gating partial compaction on its number, and\n> naming `ExchangeRecord` as what the rate is computed from — so the two citations this\n> banner made by section number now resolve to content. **B18's T2** needs `Usage`'s four\n> counters, which now exist. ⚠️ **Those two citations were dangling references while this\n> banner was being written**, and no gate could see them: the spec forbids markdown links,\n> so nothing checked its internal `§N.M` cross-references. A21's pass added\n> `check_section_refs()` to `check-links.py` for exactly that reason. **Doc 09 and doc 10** carry the plan-side caching and\n> compaction detail and are both still summaries; **doc 03** is now six deltas out of date\n> (M1.1). **A4 added no `Event` or `Command` variant** — `Usage` is a field type, not a\n> variant — so it costs no fixture of its own, though `TurnFinished`'s existing fixture\n> now has four counters in it.\n\n> **✅ SIGNED OFF 2026-09-17 — approved for application; NOT yet transcribed into the\n> spec.** The **caching half is approved as proposed**: stable fingerprinted prefix with\n> breakpoints, append-only message growth, longest-stable-prefix resync on any rewrite, and\n> volatility hygiene as engine invariants (no per-request timestamps/UUIDs; **stable tool\n> ordering, since reordering is a cache kill even when the set is identical**), with `Usage`\n> gaining `cache_read_input_tokens` / `cache_creation_input_tokens`. **B14** folds in as the\n> buffer representation, and this must be applied **together with B6**'s canonical\n> byte-stable tool schema or the ordering invariant is unenforceable.\n>\n> **Two amendments to the compaction half, in the user's words:** *\"compaction could be a\n> later feature (opt-in). Also it could be partial compaction (by stripping some later tools\n> output that doesn't affect the following conversation, but requires some experiments while\n> could affect caching).\"* What each means, precisely: **(a)** compaction is **opt-in and\n> later**, so §4.5's \"compact at 80 % of the window\" is **not v1's default answer** — v1's\n> answer to a filling context is A3's hand-over/resume, and the transcription must not leave\n> §4.5 implying that automatic compaction ships at M1. **(b) Partial compaction** (stripping\n> later tool output that the following conversation does not depend on) is recorded as a\n> **candidate requiring experiment, not an adopted feature**, with both hazards named: it\n> rewrites bytes already sent, so it must go through the truncate-and-re-emit resync path\n> and never a byte-rewrite (§4.8 forbids rewriting sent bytes for exactly this reason); and\n> it destroys the cache prefix from the cut point onwards, so it trades a cache miss for\n> context space — a trade **B18's T2 cache-hit-rate metric is the instrument for**.\n\n**Current.** §4.5 (`:318-328`) covers context, a token estimate, and a\nsingle-paragraph compaction description. No caching discipline at all.\n\n**Proposed — new \"Prompt caching\" subsection.**\n- **Stable fingerprinted prefix**: freeze system prompt + tool definitions,\n  fingerprint them, place cache breakpoints at the end of the prefix\n  (Anthropic `cache_control`, OpenAI explicit breakpoints, Gemini implicit).\n  The prefix must be **byte-stable** across turns or the cache is defeated.\n- **Append-only message growth.** Prefix caching is content-addressed from the\n  front: *any* edit to an earlier message invalidates everything after it.\n- **Longest-stable-prefix resync**: when history must be rewritten (compaction,\n  prune, model switch, field injection), truncate to the longest byte-stable\n  digest prefix and re-emit only the suffix — never rebuild the whole array.\n  Version-tag message digests to detect incoherence.\n- **Volatility hygiene** as engine invariants: no per-request\n  hashes/timestamps/UUIDs in the system prompt; **stable tool ordering —\n  reordering is a cache kill even when the set is identical**; dedup repeated\n  tool output; `ttl: \"1h\"` breakpoints for slow interactive sessions.\n- **`Usage` gains cache-token fields**: `cache_read_input_tokens`,\n  `cache_creation_input_tokens`, so hit-rate is measurable rather than inferred.\n  Source: oh-my-pi `append-only-context.ts` (`StablePrefix` fingerprint `:355`,\n  `syncMessages` `:226,:296`); validated by the api-introspect cache-failure\n  taxonomy ([25](../part5-outreach/25-introspection.md)).\n\n**Proposed — expand the compaction paragraph.**\n- The boundary is a **new append-only entry, never a byte-rewrite** (openclaw).\n- Cut only at a **turn-aligned, tool-pair-safe cut point** (`findCutPoint`).\n- **Prune before summarizing**, with a protected recent window and a\n  minimum-savings floor (oh-my-pi protects the newest ~40 k tool-output tokens,\n  needs ≥20 k savings, floor 50 tokens; never cuts at a `toolResult`).\n- **Incremental structured summary** (Goal / Progress / Decisions / Next-Steps)\n  that later compactions **update** rather than replace, hard-capped (openclaw\n  caps at 16 k chars).\n- A **retention floor** on recent tokens (openclaw keeps ~20 k).\n- An optional **method chain** with fallthrough (`remote → snapcompact →\n  handoff → shake → soft`), recorded as future work, not v1.\n\n**Depends on.** Nothing — but it *creates* a dependency: **B6's canonical,\nbyte-stable tool schema is required by A4's stable-tool-ordering invariant.**\n~~Apply A4 and B6 together or the caching guarantee is unenforceable.~~ ⚠️\n**That condition was already satisfied five days before this delta was signed**:\nB6's half has been normative in §13.3 since **A16 applied on 2026-09-12**, with\n§13.4 independently ruling tool ordering out of the cached prefix, so A4 cites both\nrather than re-adding them. See scoping decision 1 in the banner — a `Depends on`\ncell describes the world at drafting time, not at application time.\n\n**Recommend accept.** [09](../part2-engine/09-context-caching.md) calls this\n\"the highest-leverage performance/cost work in the whole system\", and the spec\ncurrently has no hook for it at all. **Done 2026-09-18**, to six sections rather\nthan the one named here, with the four scoping decisions in the banner. **The\n\"Current.\" and \"Proposed.\" text below is historical**: §4.5 is now *Context, prompt\ncaching and compaction* and carries all of it. **One proposal was deliberately not\ntranscribed** — the optional method chain with fallthrough\n(`remote → snapcompact → handoff → shake → soft`) — because this entry itself records\nit as \"future work, not v1\", and three of its five stages are the hand-over whose\nmechanism **U96** leaves to the 🧠 session. Specifying a fallthrough chain over\nundesigned stages would have been invention, not transcription.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-18","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§14","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.2","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.5","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.8","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§5.6","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§6","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A4","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U190","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"the capability model as data, B5's subset, egress policy","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-18**, the capability group's first. **The redirect is the\n> substance and it landed as redirected**: native `gemini` is *not* in v1, and §6\n> instead says why the capability model is the stronger position — a provider that\n> needs something unusual is a **value on `ModelInfo`**, not a `match` in §4.2, so\n> onboarding a non-openai-compatible provider means implementing `complete` and\n> reporting honest capabilities. B5's subset, the SSRF/egress policy and the retry\n> detail all landed unchanged. **Four spec sections against the one named**, because\n> the egress policy pairs with §4.4's `net` scope and reaches §4.3's `fetch`, and\n> because §14 owes tests for rules whose failure is silent.\n>\n> **Four scoping decisions taken while transcribing.**\n>\n> 1. **A4 had already built most of the capability model, one day earlier.** The\n>    redirect asks for \"capabilities expressed as **data** (`supports_tools`, caching\n>    semantics, dialect/grammar choice)\". Three of those four are already `ModelInfo`\n>    fields — `supports_tools`, `supports_thinking`, `supports_cache_breakpoints` —\n>    because A4's four-rate cost rule needed `ModelInfo` to be a real struct rather than\n>    a trailing comment. So A5 **extends** a capability model rather than adding one, and\n>    the only genuinely new capability is the dialect. This is the second delta in six\n>    whose named dependency turned out to be already discharged (A4/B6 was the first),\n>    and the pre-flight is what caught it: transcribing the redirect literally would have\n>    re-added fields that were normative since yesterday.\n> 2. **`ToolDialect` is a reserved slot, and in v1 it is *derived* rather than chosen.**\n>    B5's subset makes the in-band grammar reachable **only when `supports_tools` is\n>    false** — a model with native tool use needs no grammar at all — so with exactly one\n>    grammar shipping there is no choice to make and the value follows from the\n>    capability (with a config override for a provider that reports dishonestly). What the\n>    redirect actually needs is the **slot**: `ToolDialect { Native, XmlGrammar }`, so\n>    un-deferring B5's ten dialects adds variants rather than restructuring the engine.\n>    That is the inert-stub pattern B7 and A22 both use, applied to a capability model,\n>    and it is what makes \"onboarding is cheap\" a property of the types rather than an\n>    intention.\n> 3. **B5's instruction to add a numbered §16 decision was stale and was NOT followed.**\n>    Both B5's banner (\"§16 has no B5 entry today\") and this entry's `Current.` paragraph\n>    ask for one. But §16 is titled **Open decisions** and B5 is **resolved**, so adding\n>    it would put a closed decision in the spec's open list — and **A19 set the precedent\n>    explicitly**: \"§16 gained no B15 row (it lists *open* decisions)\". The concern behind\n>    the instruction is legitimate and is answered differently: the spec assumed native\n>    tool use everywhere with no record that dialects were *considered and deferred*, so\n>    **the deferral now lives in §6's prose** where a reader of the provider section meets\n>    it, and Appendix B keeps the decision. A12 owns §16's bookkeeping and applies last.\n> 4. **`CompletionRequest` and `StreamChunk` were referenced by §6's trait and never\n>    defined** — the same defect class A3 and A4 each hit, in the very section this delta\n>    owns, and A5 cannot specify transport-copy escaping or stream holdback without saying\n>    what the request and the chunk are. Both are now real types. `StreamChunk`'s variants\n>    are deliberately the *provider-side* granularity and carry no cursor, because §3.2's\n>    two tiers decide durability and §3.6 assigns a cursor at log append — a chunk is not\n>    an envelope.\n>\n> **Debt this creates, recorded rather than left to the next reader.** **U10 stays open\n> and A5 deliberately does not touch it** — malformed tool-argument JSON repair is a\n> separate axis belonging to the tools, and this entry's own \"explicitly out of scope\"\n> said so; §6 now records the exclusion where a reader will meet it. **Doc 13** is the\n> provider plan doc and is still at summary depth, so B5's and A5's machinery has no\n> plan-side write-up; it is the natural home for the dialect matrix if it is ever\n> un-deferred. **The ten deferred dialects and per-request history re-encoding** remain\n> deferred, and `ToolDialect` is where they land. **Doc 03** is now seven deltas out of\n> date (M1.1), and **U100** gains two more types that are *defined* rather than\n> comment-only, which narrows it slightly.\n\n**Current.** §6 (`:479-494`) defines the `Provider` trait and lists v1\nimplementations as `anthropic` + `openai-compat`. §16 has **no** numbered\ndecision for in-band dialects — the spec currently assumes native tool use\neverywhere.\n\n**Proposed.**\n- Add a **native `gemini`** provider (Google Generative AI / Vertex). All three\n  TS harnesses carry it separately from openai-compat because tool-calling and\n  caching semantics differ (oh-my-pi `google-generative-ai` / `-gemini-cli` /\n  `-vertex`; openclaw `google/vertex`).\n- Add the **B5 v1 subset**: Harmony **control-token escaping** on the transport\n  copy (required for *native* tool use on gpt-5.x / gpt-oss, which reject\n  reserved control-token spellings in data); a `supports_tools: false`\n  capability flag driving **history demotion**; **one XML grammar** with stream\n  holdback; a **tool-name allowlist** checked before promotion; and the rule\n  that repair **never scrubs inside fenced or indented code regions**.\n- Add **SSRF/egress `net-policy`**: IP-range deny + URL secret redaction for any\n  provider or `fetch` egress, paired with §4.4's `net` scope (openclaw\n  `packages/net-policy/src/ip.ts`).\n- Add retry detail already implied by §6: backoff **with jitter** and\n  **abort-aware sleep** (openclaw `packages/retry/src/index.ts:9`).\n\n**Explicitly out of scope for this delta.** The ten remaining dialects and\nper-request history re-encoding (deferred by B5), and **malformed tool-arg JSON\nrepair** — that is a separate axis (forge carries a distinct 3 694-LoC\n`forge_json_repair` crate for it) belonging in\n[14](../part3-capabilities/14-tools.md), not §6. Record it as a follow-up in\n[TODO](../TODO.md) rather than folding it in here.\n\n**Depends on.** ~~B5~~ ✅ **signed 2026-09-17**, and — as the pre-flight found — on\n**A4** in a way nobody recorded: A4's four-rate cost rule made `ModelInfo` a real\nstruct carrying three of the four capabilities this redirect asks for, so A5 extends a\ncapability model rather than adding one. It also turned out to depend on **A3** for\n`StopReason`, which `StreamChunk::Done` carries.\n\n**Recommend accept**, scoped exactly as above. **Done 2026-09-18**, to four sections\nrather than the one named here, with the four scoping decisions in the banner. **The\nfirst proposed bullet (native `gemini`) was not transcribed**, as the redirect\nrequired, and §6 now records *why* its absence is the stronger position rather than\nleaving a reader to wonder whether it was forgotten. **The \"Explicitly out of scope\"\nparagraph below was honoured rather than merely noted**: malformed tool-argument JSON\nrepair stays **U10**'s and belongs to the tools, and §6 says so where a reader meets\nthe dialect rules — so the exclusion is visible in the spec and not only in this\nappendix.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-18","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§14","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.3","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.4","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§6","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A5","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U191","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"the channel-plugin model","body":"\n> **✅ SIGNED OFF 2026-09-17 — approved for application; NOT yet transcribed into the\n> spec.** *\"is okay\"* — approved as proposed: the **plugin** owns account/security, DM\n> pairing, session grammar and outbound formatting; **core owns the single shared `message`\n> tool** and its dispatch, so no bridge grows its own send tool and the audit trail does not\n> fragment. Canonical conversation resolution, editable draft-stream outbound and DM pairing\n> codes stand as written. §7.2 still ships four bridges as independent binaries, and M4\n> scope is unchanged.\n\n**Current.** §7.2 (`:532-539`) ships bridges as separate binaries\n(`dacode-ext-telegram`, `-discord`, `-slack`, `-desktop`), each independently\nposting messages on `AgentIdle`/`QuestionAsked`/`PermissionRequested`.\n\n**Proposed.** Adopt openclaw's **plugin/core split** (`docs/plugins/sdk-channel-plugins.md:19-33`,\n`src/channels/AGENTS.md`), which runs ~50 channel plugins on one contract:\n- The **plugin** owns account/security, DM pairing, session grammar, and\n  outbound formatting/threading.\n- **Core owns the single shared `message` tool and its dispatch.** Without this,\n  every bridge grows its own send tool and the audit trail fragments.\n- **Canonical conversation resolution**: `channel + account + thread → session`\n  (`src/channels/conversation-resolution.ts`), with DM/group scoping.\n- **Editable draft-stream outbound** (`src/channels/draft-stream-loop.ts`) —\n  streamed replies edit one message rather than spamming many.\n- **DM pairing codes** gating unknown senders (`README.md:75-80`) — a security\n  control dacode's §7 currently lacks entirely.\n\n**Depends on.** Nothing. M4 scope.\n\n**Recommend accept.** dacode's §7 is the differentiator no reference harness\nhas end-to-end (oh-my-pi has **no** external messaging bridge at all), so getting\nthe model right early is cheap insurance.\n\n---\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§7","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A6","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U192","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"config-write endpoints, the scaling axis, and the one rule for every non-session write","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-19.** Signed off 2026-09-17 (*\"accept\"* — as proposed, with both\n> caveats, the post-A19 amendment and the multi-user-is-architecture-not-v1-scope\n> statement, released by **B10** and **B11**). **Ten spec sections were edited against the\n> two this entry named** — §8.2, §8.3, §8.4, §8.5, §3.6, §3.7, §5.1, §9.2, §12, §14 —\n> because each of its three parts needed a section the entry did not list: caveat 1 needed\n> §8.5's allowlist *and* §8.2's write rule, caveat 2 needed §5.1 (which is where §12\n> already cited it), the amendment needed §8.4 and §9.2, and **U99**'s freshness channel\n> needed a wire frame in §3.6 plus a field in §12.\n>\n> **Six scoping decisions.**\n>\n> 1. **The scaling axis landed in §8.2 rather than in a new section, and the reason is\n>    that two sections already cited it there.** §2.2 (via A13) and §3.7 (via A14) both\n>    forward-reference \"§8.2's scaling axis\" for storage and auth traits that §8.2 did not\n>    contain — a **resolvable reference with false content**, which is the A21/`§14.2`\n>    hazard class one level subtler, because `check_section_refs()` passes: the heading\n>    exists. Creating a §8.6 would have fixed the content and left both citations pointing\n>    at the wrong place, so §8.2 carries it (it is not purely a route list — the redaction\n>    and content-disposition rules were already prose there) and **§8.6 stays free**, which\n>    matters because doc 25 proposes exactly that number for introspection. Both\n>    trait families are stated with their M1-only implementations, `BlobStore` is\n>    **reconciled in as the third storage trait** (§2.1 named it, this entry did not), the\n>    method lists are left to the plan, and the placement A13 recorded is cited rather than\n>    restated. **`Authenticator`/`Tenant` are placed in `dacode-server` with no new crate**,\n>    on the test §2.1 applies to the storage traits: no engine rule depends on identity,\n>    because the engine sees a caller only as a `ClientId` and a controller status.\n> 2. ⚠️ **This entry's own gate was not transcribed, because it is incoherent.** The\n>    proposal says config writes are \"gated on controller status (§8.4)\". §5.1's four\n>    layers are built-in defaults, user config, repo config and env/CLI — **none\n>    session-scoped** — while §8.4's controller rule reaches only commands that *mutate a\n>    session*. A14 had already ruled on exactly this for the registry (\"not session-scoped,\n>    so the controller rule does not reach it\") and §8.4 said the rule \"should be decided\n>    once for every non-session write rather than per command\"; A22 then assigned A7 that\n>    single answer. So §8.4 now states **one rule for all three surfaces** — registry,\n>    config, board — as instances of *a client changed something durable outside any\n>    session*, with tightening named per principal kind. **The seam had to be corrected on\n>    the way**: §8.4 said the rule \"lands\" on `Hello`'s reserved capability flags, but\n>    **`Hello` is not a `Command`** (it is §7.2's extension handshake), so a flag there\n>    cannot gate a browser and a tenant cannot gate a bridge. Both seams are now named and\n>    required to express one policy.\n> 3. **Two rules this entry does not contain were added, because the surface is unsafe\n>    without them and both are the \"too permissive\" class no gate can catch.**\n>    **(a) The writable root is derived server-side and never supplied.** One server\n>    legitimately hosts sessions in several repositories (`CreateSession` carries a `cwd`,\n>    §4.7 maps worktrees), so \"`<repo>/.dacode/`\" has no single referent: a client-named\n>    root would let a browser working in one repository write another's config, and an\n>    ambiguous root would make §8.5's allowlist decorative. **(b) A write may not add an\n>    exec surface** — `[[extensions]]`' `cmd` (§7.2) and a hook's `run`/`post` (§7.1) are\n>    refused *although the file holding them is inside the allowlist*, because at those keys\n>    editing configuration **is** executing code, as the server's user, at every later\n>    start, from a file that outlives the request. The composition is A22's shape again:\n>    §9.2 renders model-authored markdown on the authenticated origin, so a script reaching\n>    that origin could turn one transcript into persistent command execution. It costs the\n>    *operator* nothing — `dacode config edit` in a terminal still reaches those keys — and\n>    constrains only a client acting on their behalf, which is the distinction the user\n>    settled A22's item 2 on.\n> 4. **Writes are conditional: `ETag` on every read, `If-Match` required on every write,\n>    `412` on mismatch.** Four writers can reach one file — a browser, `dacode config edit`,\n>    the user's own editor, and for a board §4.8's promotion — and last-writer-wins between\n>    them is silent data loss. \"A human reviews the diff\" is not a reconciliation mechanism\n>    for a clobber: the diff shows the result, not the bytes that were overwritten.\n> 5. **The board route is repo-scoped, following the file.** §12 puts boards at\n>    `<repo>/.dacode/plans/<board>.md`, so `GET /api/plans` and `GET`+`PUT\n>    /api/plans/:board` resolve against the **server's** cwd and return `409` outside a\n>    repository rather than writing into whatever directory happens to be current. The\n>    \"plausibly a project-level artifact\" reading survives as **presentation** (§9.2 groups\n>    boards) and not as a second routing axis. **`promote` is two checks**: controller-gated\n>    at the session owning the item, allowlist- and `If-Match`-gated at the path — and from\n>    the model a promotion is a **write through §4.4** (§4.8's `Verify::Command` rule is the\n>    precedent), while from a browser it is not a tool call at all, so neither door inherits\n>    the other's check. **No `POST`**: the client names the file, so create and replace are\n>    one idempotent operation — §3.3's caller-allocated-id reasoning applied to HTTP.\n> 6. **U99 landed, and its socket half is a compatibility event.** `org_version` on all\n>    four tree reads (§12's monotonic `version`, stored rather than derived from mtime\n>    because mtime is not monotonic across a rename-on-write, a restore, or two hosts\n>    sharing a home directory) plus **§3.6's third down-frame kind, `Hint`**. It is not an\n>    `Envelope` and gets **no golden fixture**, because §14.1's fixture set derives from\n>    `Event`'s variants: no `session`, no `seq`, no cursor, never written, never replayed,\n>    and safe to lose, since the authoritative version travels on the read it prompts. That\n>    is exactly the line §3.7 draws, and it is why the registry does not become an event\n>    stream here.\n>\n> **One gap recorded rather than filled.** §3.3 has **no task-mutation `Command`**, so the\n> client→engine path a browser would use to `promote` — or to close a `Verify::Manual` item,\n> which §4.8 says requires a controller action recorded as its own event — does not exist in\n> the protocol; `promote` is a `TaskOp` on the `TaskUpdated` **event** only. Inventing a\n> variant would have been a wire change in a section this entry does not own, for a shape the\n> task-tree brainstorm holds, so it is a new §6 item instead.\n>\n> **Debt this creates.** **B10 is now half-applied in A25's shape**: its comment-preserving\n> writes are normative at §5.1 (where §12 already cited them and no section carried them),\n> while its generated `dacode.schema.json` stays owed to A12/doc 17 — so B10 must not be\n> marked applied. **Doc 20** is the scaling axis's plan-side home and is still a summary;\n> **docs 19, 21, 17, 18 and 32** each carried a line A7 falsified and were corrected under\n> hard rule 1 with their markers untouched. **`xtask`** now has a third thing to specify if\n> the config schema is generated (B6's tool schema, B2's protocol schema, B10's config\n> schema), and doc 01 §1.5's register row owes that. **`toml_edit`** is pure Rust, so it\n> needs no Appendix F row — checked rather than assumed.\n\n**Current.** *(historical — the line numbers are from the pre-A7 spec)* §8.2\n(`:565-584`) exposes `GET /api/config`, `/api/agents`, `/api/skills`,\n`/api/models` — read-only, **16 routes in total**. §8.3 (`:589-596`) is\nsingle-user and states plainly: \"No users, no roles.\" **After application §8.2\ncarries 23 routes and the scaling axis, and §8.3 carries the trait pair.**\n\n**Proposed.**\n- **Config-write endpoints** for UI-driven agent/skill/workflow customization\n  (`PUT`/`POST` over `/api/{config,agents,skills,workflows}`), ~~gated on\n  controller status (§8.4)~~ → ⚠️ **this gate was NOT transcribed; see scoping\n  decision 2.** It is incoherent as written, and A14's pattern was applied\n  instead. **`PUT`/`DELETE` landed rather than `PUT`/`POST`** — the client names\n  the file, so create and replace are one idempotent operation.\n- The **scaling axis** from [00-overview §0.3](../00-overview.md): `EventStore`/\n  `SessionStore` and `Authenticator`/`Tenant` as **pluggable traits from day\n  one**, with `JsonlStore` + `SharedToken` as the only M1 implementations and\n  `SqliteStore`/`PostgresStore`/`MultiUser` later. → ✅ **landed at §8.2, with\n  `BlobStore` reconciled in as the third storage trait** — §2.1 named three and\n  this entry named two, so transcribing it literally would have left the spec\n  disagreeing with itself about how many storage traits exist.\n\n**Two caveats that must be written into the delta, not left implicit.** ✅ **Both\ndischarged 2026-09-19, and each needed a section this entry did not name.**\n1. **Config-write is a new write surface outside the session cwd.** §8.5's path\n   safety canonicalizes and rejects anything outside the session's\n   cwd/worktree — but these endpoints write to `~/.config/dacode/` and\n   `<repo>/.dacode/`. §8.5 must be extended with an explicit allowlist of\n   writable config paths, or the endpoints bypass the only path guard the spec\n   has. → ✅ **§8.5 is now split Reads/Writes**, with a three-class allowlist,\n   five exclusions and the three read-side properties carried over. **What this\n   caveat could not see is that \"`<repo>`\" has no single referent** on a server\n   hosting sessions in several repositories, so the allowlist alone does not\n   close the hole — the root has to be *derived* (scoping decision 3a).\n2. **UI-driven writes make B10's comment-preservation load-bearing.** A CLI\n   `dacode config edit` eating comments is annoying; a web UI silently\n   destroying a user's annotated `config.toml` is a data-loss bug. Format-\n   preserving writes (`toml_edit`) become a requirement of *this* delta, not\n   just of B10. → ✅ **landed at §5.1**, which is where §12 already cited it and\n   no section carried it: before this pass the requirement existed in the spec\n   only as a *citation*. **B10's other half (the generated `dacode.schema.json`)\n   stays owed**, so B10 is now half-applied rather than done.\n\n**Scope discipline to state explicitly:** multi-user is **architecture, not v1\nscope**. The traits ship in M1; `MultiUser` and `PostgresStore` do not. §8.3's\n\"No users, no roles\" stays true of the shipped default and should be reworded\nas \"no users or roles *in the default single-user configuration*\" rather than\ndeleted. → ✅ **reworded exactly as instructed**, with the addition that it now\nnames what the design *does* permit (§8.2's scaling axis), so the sentence cannot\nbe read later as a prohibition. The two other \"§8.3 has no users and no roles\"\nclaims in §8.4 were swept to match.\n\n**Depends on.** ~~B10, B11.~~ ✅ **both signed 2026-09-17**; B11's crate\nplacement had already been transcribed by **A13** (2026-09-18), which is why this\nentry cites §2.1 rather than restating it — and why A13's §2.2 sentence about \"the\nscaling axis §8.2's storage and auth traits describe\" was a real obligation rather\nthan prose.\n\n**Recommend accept**, with both caveats and the scope statement. **Done\n2026-09-19**, with the six scoping decisions in the banner. **The `Current.` and\n`Proposed.` text above is historical**: §8.2 was read-only and carried 16 routes,\n§8.3 said \"No users, no roles\" flatly, and §8.5 was two sentences about reads.\n\n**Amended after A19 landed — caveat 1 gains a third path, and a new surface\narrives with it.** §4.8's promoted task board lives at `<repo>/.dacode/plans/<board>.md`\n(§12), which is exactly the class of path caveat 1 describes: a write surface\noutside the session cwd. So:\n\n✅ **All four bullets discharged 2026-09-19.**\n\n- **`.dacode/plans/` must be on §8.5's writable-path allowlist** if any UI is to\n  save a board. Without it the endpoints cannot write there, or they bypass the\n  only path guard the spec has. → ✅ **the third of the three writable classes**,\n  and §9.2's config editor is where a user reaches it.\n- **A board is cross-session, which this delta's §8.2 framing does not cover.**\n  Its endpoints are scoped `/api/sessions/:id/…`; a board aggregates items promoted\n  from *several* sessions, so it needs either a project-scoped route (which makes\n  it a §6/B13 concern) or a repo-scoped one. ~~Unspecified today, and recorded in\n  [32](../part2-engine/32-task-tracking.md) and [TODO](../TODO.md) §6 rather than\n  left for M3.~~ → ✅ **repo-scoped, following the file**: §12 puts a board at\n  `<repo>/.dacode/plans/<board>.md`, so `GET /api/plans` and `GET`+`PUT\n  /api/plans/:board` resolve against the **server's** cwd and `409` outside a\n  repository. The project-level reading survives as **presentation** (§9.2 groups\n  boards) and not as a second routing axis — which is the answer\n  [32](../part2-engine/32-task-tracking.md) and [TODO](../TODO.md) §6 were holding\n  the question open for.\n- **`promote` from a browser is a controller action.** Writing a *committed* file\n  from a UI is a different trust level from a model writing it, so §8.4's\n  controller/observer split has to say who may. → ✅ **answered as two checks\n  rather than one**, because the act is session-scoped while its effect is not:\n  the controller of the session owning the item may promote, *and* the write is\n  bounded by §8.5's allowlist and §8.2's `If-Match`. **The asymmetry this bullet\n  did not state is now in §8.4**: from the model a promotion is a write through\n  §4.4 (§4.8's `Verify::Command` rule is the precedent), while from a browser it is\n  not a tool call at all — so neither door inherits the other's check. ⚠️ **And the\n  browser door does not exist in the protocol yet**: §3.3 has no task-mutation\n  `Command`, recorded as a new §6 item rather than invented here.\n- **The live tree needs none of this.** It is folded into `Snapshot.tasks` (§3.5),\n  so every client already renders it over the existing channel — worth stating so\n  the board question is not read as larger than it is. → ✅ **and it stayed true**:\n  the board work added one file route family and no `Event` variant, so the tree's\n  rendering path is untouched.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-19","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§8.2","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§8.3","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§8.4","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§8.5","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A7","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-19T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-19T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U193","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"the web stack, as a deferral with a committed prerequisite","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-18.** B2's split transcribed, and **the framework was not\n> picked** — which is the whole discipline of this delta, since the split *is* the\n> decision. §9.1 now says the framework is an **open decision with a deadline** (due by\n> **M3**) rather than \"Decision pending\", with **TS SPA (Solid)** as the *standing\n> recommendation* and both reasons recorded so the choice is not relitigated from\n> scratch: all three reference web UIs chose TypeScript, and the four hard parts of this\n> UI (streaming markdown, highlighting, virtualised lists, diff rendering) have mature TS\n> solutions and weak WASM ones. A recommendation is not a decision, so §9.2's screens and\n> §9.3's behaviour stay framework-neutral and the server is unaffected either way. The\n> Leptos/TS/HTMX table is kept as the record of what was weighed.\n>\n> **Three scoping decisions taken while transcribing.**\n>\n> 1. **The JSON-Schema export is now unconditional, and the old conditionality was\n>    backwards.** B2 made it a non-optional **M1** deliverable, but §9.1 carried it *only*\n>    inside the \"If TS:\" branch — so an M1 obligation was conditional on an M3 decision.\n>    §9.1 now states the inversion explicitly: **the export is what makes the TS branch\n>    cheap**, so requiring the branch first would have made the framework decision harder\n>    rather than easier. Its framework-independent uses are listed (extension authors\n>    validating a `Command`, a future SDK, `run --output jsonl` consumers, and §14.1's\n>    golden fixtures, which already pin the wire format in JSON and so are half a schema).\n> 2. ⚠️ **A live contradiction between §9.1 and §13.3/§2.4 was resolved, and it was not a\n>    choice.** §9.1's old text had \"a `build.rs` step emits `protocol.d.ts` from the Rust\n>    types (via a small JSON-Schema export **in `dacode-protocol`**)\" — but §13.3 confines\n>    `schemars` to an offline generator because it depends on `serde_json`\n>    non-optionally and says **\"never in a shipped crate\"**, and §2.4 puts build-time\n>    codegen in **`xtask`** on the test of whether its output ships. So the generator lives\n>    in `xtask`, `dacode-protocol` neither depends on `schemars` nor grows a `build.rs`,\n>    and `protocol.d.ts` is generated **in CI with a drift check that fails the build** —\n>    layered on the golden fixtures, which catch a change to the serialized shape while the\n>    drift check catches the generated types falling out of step with it (the failure a\n>    second language cannot otherwise see). §9.1 names this as §14.1's existing `xtask`\n>    rule applied to a second generator rather than as a new rule.\n> 3. **Doc 03's line was a false claim, not a stale marker, so it was corrected under hard\n>    rule 1 rather than left.** It read \"Optional: a JSON-Schema export … **if** the web UI\n>    is a TS SPA\" — the exact conditionality this delta removes, in the plan doc for the\n>    crate that owes the export. Strike-and-annotate, **marker and depth untouched**:\n>    removing one false line from a summary adds no depth. Same argument that justified\n>    correcting doc 08's `Dynamic(fn)` arm under A2.\n>\n> **Debt this creates.** **The framework decision is due by M3** and §9.1 names the\n> deadline, so the deferral cannot drift; **doc 21** is the web-UI plan doc and is still a\n> summary, so §9.1/§9.2/§9.3 are its normative source. **`xtask`** now has two generators\n> to specify (the tool-schema one from §13.3 and this one), and doc 01 §1.5's register row\n> should say so when M1 deepens it. **No wire change, no `Event` variant, no fixture** —\n> the export is an artifact *derived from* the fixtures, not a new surface.\n\n**Current.** §9.1 (`:619-631`) gives the Leptos/TS/HTMX table, says **\"Decision\npending.\"**, and notes that either way the server is unaffected.\n\n**Proposed (per B2's split).** Do **not** resolve the framework. Instead record:\n- `dacode-protocol` **ships a JSON-Schema export of its own types as an M1\n  deliverable** — non-optional, and useful regardless of framework (extension\n  authors, a future SDK, `run --output jsonl` consumers, golden fixtures).\n  [03](../part1-foundations/03-protocol.md) currently calls this \"Optional … if\n  the web UI is a TS SPA\"; that conditionality is removed.\n- The **framework decision is due by M3**, with **TS SPA (Solid)** as the\n  standing recommendation and the reason recorded (all three reference web UIs\n  chose TS; streaming markdown, highlighting, virtualization and diffs all have\n  mature TS solutions and weak WASM ones).\n- If TS: `protocol.d.ts` is **generated in CI with a drift check that fails the\n  build**, layered on the golden fixtures that already pin the wire format.\n\n**Depends on.** ~~B2~~ ✅ **signed 2026-09-17**.\n\n**Recommend accept as amended** — this delta records a *deferral with a\ncommitted prerequisite*, which is a real resolution, not a punt. **Done\n2026-09-18**, with the three scoping decisions in the banner and the framework left\nopen exactly as instructed. **The \"Current.\" text below is historical** — §9.1 no\nlonger says \"Decision pending\", and its `build.rs`-in-`dacode-protocol` clause was\ncontradicted by §13.3 and §2.4 and is gone. **The prerequisite half of \"a deferral\nwith a committed prerequisite\" is what needed the care**: the prerequisite was\nsitting *inside* the conditional branch it was carved out of, so it had inherited\nthe very conditionality the split removed. That is the fourth time a signed\ndecision's M1 half was found inside a conditional (A25's wire half, A10's\ncarve-out, A21's `§14.2` citations), which makes it a pattern worth checking for\nrather than a run of bad luck.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-18","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§9.1","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A8","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U194","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"one tree component, TUI mouse, collapse-on-paste","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-18**, and with it **M1's delta *list* is complete** — of\n> `A1–A5, A9, A14, A15` every one has landed. ⚠️ **That claim was true of the list and\n> false of the milestone, and the correction belongs here because this is the entry that\n> made it**: **B11's signed condition also made A10's exchange-record format an M1\n> deliverable**, A10's entry assigned its §6 side to **A5**, and A5 had landed without it.\n> No gate could see that — A5's own `Depends on` is correct, and a complete list looks\n> complete — so it took an **inbound-assignment sweep** (grep both appendices for what\n> *other* deltas assign to each applied one) to find it. The record is now landed and\n> **A10 is half-applied** in A25's shape. It was taken **out of queue order** for\n> that reason: the application order puts A6 next, but A6 is §5's **M4** dependency\n> while A9 is M1's last, and the queue and the milestone lists are different axes. Its\n> own **sequencing constraint is satisfied** — A3 and A4 both landed earlier the same\n> day, as its sign-off required.\n>\n> **Four scoping decisions taken while transcribing.**\n>\n> 1. **§9.2's half was already normative, so A9 extends it rather than adding it.**\n>    \"One tree component, three sources\" landed with **A19** on 2026-09-13, verbatim in\n>    shape: the task tree, the sub-agent tree and declarative workflow steps render in one\n>    component because they are one shape. What A9 contributes is §10's **matching**\n>    component — the TUI sidebar now names the same three sources, which it previously did\n>    not, having listed sessions/sub-agents and the task tree as two bullets — plus the two\n>    TUI rules. This is the third delta in eight whose named work turned out to be partly\n>    done (A4/B6 and A5/A4 were the others), and as with those the pre-flight is what\n>    caught it: transcribing the proposal literally would have duplicated a bullet that has\n>    been normative for five days.\n> 2. **The mouse rule landed with its *reason*, not just its condition.** The signed text\n>    says \"SGR tracking enabled only while a fullscreen overlay holds the alt screen\",\n>    which is a mechanism. §10 now also says why the condition is the point: a TUI that\n>    captures the mouse all the time takes **scroll-back and text selection** away from the\n>    user's own terminal, the two things a terminal does better than any UI. Without the\n>    reason the rule reads as an implementation convenience and the next person enables\n>    mouse support globally.\n> 3. **Collapse-on-paste is recorded as a §10 rule and *not* shared client behaviour, and\n>    that is substantive.** In a browser a large paste into a textarea is the browser's\n>    problem and already works; in a terminal a big bracketed paste can be misread as a\n>    burst of keystrokes. So §9.3 correctly grows no paste or mouse rule, and §10 says so\n>    explicitly — otherwise the next reader generalises a terminal workaround into the web\n>    UI. The signed parameters landed unchanged: 10 lines / 1000 chars, an **indivisible**\n>    chip backed by a paste map, expanded on submit, a host hook to intercept\n>    (wrap-in-codeblock / attach-file), bracketed-paste buffering under a size cap.\n>    **Indivisible** is stated with its reason: a chip the cursor can half-edit is worse than\n>    either alternative, because it looks like text and is not.\n> 4. ⚠️ **A gap was found and deliberately recorded rather than filled.** §9.2's bullet\n>    promises three sources in one tree, but **nothing on the wire correlates a declarative\n>    workflow step with the child session it spawned**: `SubAgentSpawned` carries the\n>    child, the agent *name* and a task *description*, not a step identity. The engine\n>    knows the correlation — §4.2's `task` tool carries the parent task-item id, which is\n>    what scopes the child's write access — but does not say so. The minimal fix is a wire\n>    change plus a `TaskId` identifier (currently undefined; it is in **U100**'s list) plus\n>    a §4.3 scan-exclusion clause, since `TaskId` *is* tool-nameable through `todo` and so\n>    cannot silently join the scanned set. That is three sections A9 does not name, for a\n>    correlator whose right shape depends on §5.4's step model — **which A25's M5 half owns\n>    and has not designed**. Inventing it here would have been exactly the error avoided\n>    for U96's hand-over. So §9.2 states the limitation openly, names it as an *edge*\n>    problem rather than a *shape* problem, and leaves the correlator to the §5.4 step\n>    model. **Neither signed text requires it**: A19's bullet and this entry's proposal\n>    both claim the sources are the same *shape*, and shape is a property of the node, not\n>    of the linkage.\n>\n> **Debt this creates, recorded rather than left to the next reader.** **U101 (new)**:\n> §14 has **no UI test tier at all**, so A9's two client rules are normative with nothing\n> that can fail on them — a mouse rule whose violation silently costs the user their\n> terminal's scroll-back, and two paste thresholds that are numbers and will drift. Adding\n> a tier means choosing a harness for a crate that does not exist yet, which is **A21**'s\n> §14 restructuring, so it is recorded with A21 (or M1.1) as owner and A9's two rules\n> named as the tier's first content. **U19** is annotated: the mouse and paste specifics\n> are now normative in §10, so when doc 22 is deepened its source is the spec rather than\n> the survey — and doc 22 stays a summary, now three deltas out of date (M1.1). **Doc 21**\n> (web UI) is likewise still a summary and §9.2 is its normative source. **The step-linkage\n> correlator** belongs to A25's §5.4 half.\n\n**Current.** §9.2 (`:649-650`) renders a sub-agent tree from\n`SubAgentSpawned`. §10 (`:671-689`) lists diff viewer, session tree, `/serve`,\n`attach` — no mouse, no paste handling.\n\n**Proposed.**\n- **Unify the workflow/pipeline view with the sub-agent tree** (§9.2 and §10):\n  declarative workflow steps (§5.4) and spawned sub-agents are the same shape —\n  a node with live status that expands to a transcript. One component, not two.\n- **TUI mouse support** (§10): SGR tracking (`?1000h ?1003h ?1006h`) enabled\n  only while a fullscreen overlay holds the alt screen, with 0-based row/col\n  hit-testing (oh-my-pi `tui.ts:88,:2775`, `mouse.ts:39,:85,:107`). Directly\n  portable to crossterm.\n- **Collapse-on-paste** (§10): a paste over **10 lines or 1000 chars** collapses\n  to an indivisible chip backed by a paste map, expanded on submit, with a host\n  hook to intercept (wrap-in-codeblock / attach-file) (oh-my-pi\n  `components/editor.ts:598,:633,:885`). Bracketed-paste buffering with a size\n  cap (64 MiB there).\n\n**Depends on.** Nothing — but the sign-off attached a **sequencing constraint** (\"A3\nand A4 must not land later than A9, since A9 unifies the tree views A19's task tree\njoins\"), and ✅ **it held**: A3 and A4 both landed earlier on 2026-09-18. It also\nturned out to depend on **A19** in a way nobody recorded — §9.2's \"one tree\ncomponent, three sources\" bullet was already normative, so half of this delta's first\nproposal was done five days before it was applied.\n\n**Recommend accept.** Both TUI items come with concrete, tested parameters\nrather than aspirations, and both are cheap in ratatui/crossterm. **Done\n2026-09-18**, with the four scoping decisions in the banner — including one gap\n**deliberately not filled** (nothing on the wire correlates a workflow step to its\nspawned child, and the correlator's shape depends on a §5.4 step model A25 has not\ndesigned). **The \"Current.\" and \"Proposed.\" text below is historical**: §9.2 no longer\n\"renders a sub-agent tree\" alone, and §10 now carries mouse and paste rules it did not\nhave. The reference-harness line citations are the survey's, not the spec's, and stay\nhere rather than moving into it.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-18","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§10","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§9.2","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A9","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U195","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"API introspection & caching analyzer","body":"\n> **⚠️ HALF-APPLIED 2026-09-18, in A25's shape — and the half that landed is the one\n> that was nearly missed, which is the interesting part.** The **M1 carve-out is\n> APPLIED**: `ExchangeRecord` is normative in §6, `exchanges.jsonl` is in §12's layout\n> with the redaction asymmetry stated against `events.jsonl`, and §14 has the test that\n> can fail on getting that asymmetry wrong in either direction. **The §17 body is still\n> owed** — the transparent logging proxy, SSE reassembly, the cache-failure taxonomy,\n> and the standalone mode that does not involve `dacode-server` at all. That is M5 and\n> stays in the queue.\n>\n> **How the M1 half was missed, because the mechanism matters more than the miss.** This\n> entry assigned the §6 side to **A5** — twice, in the banner (\"A5 carries the §6 side\n> of it\") and in the proposal (\"A5 should carry the §6 side\"). A5 was applied without\n> it. Nothing caught that: A5's own `Depends on` cell says \"B5\" and is correct, its\n> gates were green, and the board's claim \"**M1's delta list is complete**\" was *true of\n> §5's list* (`A1–A5, A9, A14, A15` all applied) while being **false in substance**,\n> because B11's signed condition — *\"if the provider layer does not emit it from the\n> start, retrofitting instrumentation across every provider in M5 is far more expensive\n> than emitting it in M1\"* — was unmet. What found it was an **inbound-assignment\n> sweep**: for each applied delta, grep both appendices for other entries assigning work\n> *to* it. Eight deltas, one unfulfilled assignment, one line of shell.\n>\n> **The generalisation is the reason this banner is long.** Every delta's pre-flight\n> checked what it *depended on* and never what *depended on it*. A list of deltas is\n> what its author remembered; cross-delta obligations are precisely what lists do not\n> carry, and a complete list looks complete — which is what makes this failure invisible\n> in a way a wrong milestone attribution (recorded separately) is not. The sweep is now\n> part of the standing gate set beside the type-definition sweep and the two-form depth\n> grep.\n>\n> **Two scoping decisions on the record itself.** Its fields map **one-to-one onto\n> B11's signed list** — endpoint kind, request/response bodies, extracted `usage`\n> including cache-token fields, timings, redaction applied — and no further, since\n> inventing fields is the error the type sweep exists to catch. **One field beyond that\n> list is `turn`**, and it earns its place: the record's purpose is to check §4.5's\n> invariants against what actually happened in a turn, which needs the exchange and the\n> event log to be joinable. The session id is *not* a field, because the record lives in\n> that session's directory and a field would duplicate the path. Two deliberate absences:\n> **`api_kind` is a string rather than an enum**, because the set is open and an enum\n> would need a variant per dialect — the per-provider branching A5's redirect exists to\n> remove — and **there is no cache-hit field**, because `usage.cache_read_input_tokens`\n> *is* the hit signal and a second field would be derivable state that eventually\n> disagrees with its source.\n>\n> **The redaction asymmetry is the part most likely to be got backwards, so it is stated\n> in three places.** `events.jsonl` is append-only and is **never** redacted — §4.3 makes\n> prevention the only control available for a stored log, since redacting it would be a\n> rewrite. `exchanges.jsonl` is a **derived diagnostic copy** and **is** redacted, under\n> §8.2's rule for copies. A log that is redacted is a rewritten log; a diagnostic copy\n> that is not redacted is a leak of the entire conversation. §6 states the rule and its\n> two failure directions, §12 states it where the two files sit side by side (because a\n> file that looks like a log invites log treatment), and §14 tests both directions.\n>\n> **Debt this creates.** **Doc 13** is the provider plan doc B11 named for the record\n> shape and is still a summary, so its deepening at M1.1 should take §6 as its source\n> rather than restating it. **§17 remains owed** and its content is unchanged by this\n> pass. **B18's T1/T2 and A21's §14.2** gain their instrument: the exchange record is\n> what a cache-hit-rate metric is computed *from*, so until now §14.2 would have had\n> nothing to read.\n\n**Current.** Absent from the spec entirely.\n[25](../part5-outreach/25-introspection.md) proposes \"§8.6 or a new §17\".\n\n**Proposed.** Add a new section — **§17 \"Debugging & introspection\"**, not §8.6.\nRationale: the subsystem has two modes, and one of them (a standalone logging\nproxy that *any* CLI can point at via `ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL`) does not involve\n`dacode-server` at all. Burying it under §8 Server would misdescribe it. §16 is\n\"Open decisions\" and will shrink as decisions land, so appending §17 after it is\nnot an ordering problem.\n\nContent: the transparent logging proxy (bind local ports → upstream, forward\nverbatim, tag endpoints with an `api_kind`); **SSE reassembly** into a final\nmessage object while forwarding chunks with no added latency; **secret\nredaction** in logs but not upstream; **usage extraction** including\n`cache_read_input_tokens` / `cache_creation_input_tokens`; one JSON line per\nexchange; the **cache-failure taxonomy** (missing/too-small `cache_control`\nmarkers, measured hit-rate, volatile system prompts by first-changed-bytes\nclassification, tool-definition churn *including pure reordering*, non-append-only\nhistory transitions, oversized or byte-identical repeated tool results,\ncache-TTL gaps, mid-session model switches); and **in-process record emission**\nfrom dacode's own provider layer so a session is self-introspecting without an\nexternal proxy.\n\n**The part that cannot wait for M5.** Per B11, the *crate* is M5, but the\n**exchange-record format is an M1 deliverable** in §6 — retrofitting\ninstrumentation across every provider later costs far more than emitting it from\nthe start. This delta should say so explicitly, and A5 should carry the §6 side.\n\n**Depends on.** B11.\n\n**Recommend accept**, as a new §17 with the M1 record-format carve-out.\n\n---\n","status":"in_work","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"new §17 (M5) + §6/§12/§14 (M1 carve-out)","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A10","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U196","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"reconcile the JSON crate's reality","body":"\n> **✅ Superseded and applied as part of A16, 2026-09-12** — exactly as this\n> entry recommended. Retained below as the record of *how* the discrepancy was\n> found (tracker finding `f1`) and of the rename's measured blast radius. The\n> `Value`-does-not-exist half is now resolved: §13.1 states it, and no dacode\n> doc outside these appendices names `dacode_json::Value` any more.\n\n**Current.** §13 (`:746-760`) names a type **`dacode_json::Value`** that does not\nexist. The crate is published as **`dacode-json`** — correctly named since the\nrename sweep — and has **no owned value type**: `Value<'a>`/`Doc<'a>` are\nborrowed from the input slice plus a `Pool`.\n\n**Half of this finding is now resolved.** A11 originally covered two defects: the\nspec's crate name (`dacodec-json`, a crate that never existed) and its `Value`\ntype. The **name half was fixed by the `dacodec` → `dacode-json` rename**, which\nswept the spec, the role files, the appendices and the generated adapters\ntogether. The **`Value` half stands unchanged** — a rename cannot make a type\nexist — and is the reason A16 is still needed.\n\n**Recommend: do not apply separately.** A16 rewrites §13 wholesale and\nsubsumes this. A11 stays in the index as the record of *how the discrepancy was\nfound* (tracker finding `f1`), struck as superseded.\n\n**Blast radius — measured before the sweep, kept as the audit trail.** Counting\nonly the two *wrong forms* (`dacodec-json` and `dacodec_json`), they appeared\n**37 times across 17 files**, not just in the spec:\n\n| Where | Occurrences |\n|---|---|\n| `docs/dacode-design.md` | 8 |\n| `.agents/skills/dacode/SKILL.md` | 4 |\n| `.agents/config/agent-rules.yaml` | 3 |\n| `.opencode/agent/*.md` (**generated**) | 9 across 4 files |\n| `AGENTS.md` | 2 |\n| `.agents/roles/_rust.md`, `.agents/implement.md`, `.agents/reviewer.md`, `.agents/roles/implementer.md`, `.agents/skill-base.md`, `.agents/task-templates/write-tests.md` | 7 |\n\nThat table undercounts what the sweep actually touched, because it excludes the\n*bare* `dacodec` and the `dacodec::` Rust paths: the full figure was **145\noccurrences across 31 files**. All of it has landed, including the\n`tools/agents-sync.py` regeneration of the `.opencode` adapters.\n\n**What the sweep could not fix.** `.agents/roles/_rust.md`'s rule \"All\nprotocol/tool/provider JSON goes through `dacode_json::Value`\" still names a type\nthat does not exist, and that file is loaded into **every coding agent's\ncontext**. Renaming the crate made the sentence accurate about the crate and left\nit false about the type, so it now carries an inline caveat pointing at B1 and\nA16. Applying A16 is what removes the need for the caveat — which remains a\nstronger argument for accepting A16 promptly than spec hygiene alone.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-12","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§13","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A11","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-12T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-12T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U197","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"resolve the open decisions","body":"\n> **✅ SIGNED OFF 2026-09-17 — approved for application; NOT yet transcribed into the spec,\n> and it APPLIES LAST, after every other delta.** *\"ok\"* — approved as proposed: resolve\n> §16.1–§16.5, add native GUI nodes (thin gateway nodes, not engine forks) and ACP, keep §16\n> listing what remains open. **Its scope grew today:** besides those, it now also carries the\n> resolution of **§16 item 6** — the standing-goal gap A19 created — which the user signed as\n> a **`Standing` status, goals only**. A task cannot be standing, because applied to a task it\n> would be a way to dodge the gate; that is exactly how `tools/tasks.py --status standing`\n> already behaves and refuses. **This is a wire-format change** (a `TaskStatus` variant),\n> hence a compatibility event under §14, and it settles **with A1** rather than separately.\n\n**Current.** §16 (`:801-810`) lists five open decisions: web stack, TOML vs\nJSON, `bash:readonly` sandboxing, ACP, checkpoint strategy.\n\n**Proposed** — record the B-pass resolutions:\n- **§16.1 web stack** → *partially resolved*: schema export committed for M1,\n  framework due by M3 (B2). **Stays open** in reduced form.\n- **§16.2 TOML vs JSON** → *resolved*: TOML on disk, JSON on wire, plus\n  comment-preserving writes and a generated `dacode.schema.json` (B10).\n- **§16.3 `bash:readonly` sandboxing** → *resolved*: deny-list in v1, documented\n  as advisory, `SandboxBackend` seam reserved, interpreters disqualifying, real\n  containment via §4.7 worktrees (B7).\n- **§16.4 ACP** → *resolved as deferred*: implement in M5; two M1 constraints\n  recorded (log-derivable state; no client-capability branching in tools) (B9).\n- **§16.5 checkpoint strategy** → *resolved*: shadow git, with the six edge-case\n  additions and a pruning policy (B8).\n- **Add** native GUI nodes (macOS Swift / Linux Tauri as **thin gateway nodes**,\n  not engine forks) as a recorded M6 direction — openclaw's model, per\n  [27](../part5-outreach/27-native-gui.md).\n- **Add** the two decisions the plan surfaced that §16 does not currently list:\n  **in-band tool dialects** (B5, now resolved as phased) and the **templating\n  choice** for prompt partials and workflow `when` conditions\n  ([17](../part3-capabilities/17-config-agents-skills.md) — forge uses\n  handlebars compiled at build time; still genuinely open).\n\n**Do not empty §16.** It should keep listing what remains open — after this pass\nthat is the web framework (B2's second half) and templating. An empty §16 would\nfalsely imply the design is settled.\n\n**Depends on.** B2, B7, B8, B9, B10 — apply **last**.\n\n**Recommend accept.**\n\n---\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§16","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A12","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U198","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"the two approved-with-dates crates, and in-tree vendoring","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-18.** Both crates recorded as **approved-with-dates** — M3 and M5\n> — and **neither was added to §2.1's tree**, which still lists eleven members, exactly as\n> this entry required. The vendored-in-tree settlement is now explicit in §2.1 rather than\n> implied by the tree.\n>\n> **Three scoping decisions taken while transcribing.**\n>\n> 1. **The instruction to \"update Appendix D to mark both deferred\" was already\n>    satisfied, and Appendix D needed something else instead.** Its graph already carried\n>    both crates with an asterisk and a deferral note, so what was actually stale was the\n>    *legend* — it called them \"**proposed** new crates\" when B11 had approved them with\n>    dates — and it carried **no milestone at all**. So the legend now says\n>    approved-with-dates, names M3 and M5, and records the reasons (the trait lives in\n>    `dacode-core::store` from M1 so the boundary arrives when a second implementor makes\n>    it pay; the introspect *record format* is already normative in §6 via A10's carve-out,\n>    so the crate arrives to read records written since M1). **This is the fifth delta in a\n>    row whose named work was partly done before it started** — A4/B6, A5/A4, A9/A19,\n>    A21/A4, A8/B2-and-doc-03 — which is what eleven transcriptions in one day does to a\n>    queue: each entry's `Depends on` and instruction list describes the world as it was\n>    when drafted.\n> 2. ⚠️ **A phantom-edge hazard was found and written into the spec rather than left to\n>    the reader.** `gates/boundary-edges.txt` declares itself an **exact** diff against\n>    `cargo tree`, not a subset test — \"a violating edge fails, and so does a legal new\n>    one, until this file is updated in the same commit\". A13 asks §2.2 to state where\n>    `dacode-store` sits *when it arrives*, so the spec now names dependency edges for\n>    crates that do not exist. **Those edges must not be added to the pinned list**, and\n>    §2.2 says so in the same breath as the rules, with the reason: adding an edge for a\n>    crate with no directory does not describe the graph, it breaks the gate — and it\n>    *looks* like diligence to someone reading §2.2 and the gate file side by side. The\n>    file was verified unchanged at 29 lines with zero store/introspect edges.\n> 3. **§2.2's enforcement sentence was stale and was corrected.** It said the rules are\n>    \"Enforced by review; consider a `cargo-deny`/workspace-lint\" — but the pinned\n>    exact-diff gate **already exists**, so the spec was describing as aspirational the\n>    mechanism that enforces it. Rewritten to name the mechanism and its same-commit rule,\n>    which is §2.3's rule applied to the crate graph.\n>\n> **One coupling named rather than discovered later.** A13's placements must agree with\n> **A7's** scaling axis (`EventStore`/`SessionStore` and `Authenticator`/`Tenant` as\n> pluggable traits from day one, `JsonlStore` and `SharedToken` the only M1\n> implementations). A7 is still unapplied, so §2.2 records the requirement that the two\n> agree — pluggable backends from day one, crate boundary when a second implementor makes\n> it pay for itself — instead of leaving M3 to find a contradiction.\n>\n> **No wire change, no fixture, no new type, and no gate-file change** — which is what\n> makes this the cheapest delta in the set: it records two decisions and corrects one stale\n> sentence about how they are enforced.\n\n**Current.** §2 (`:37-58`) lists ten crates and four dependency rules.\n\n**Proposed (per B11).** Record both crates as **approved with dates**, not as\npresent-day additions:\n- **`dacode-store`** — `EventStore`/`SessionStore`/`BlobStore` traits + backends\n  + compression. **Traits live in `dacode-core::store` from M1; extract the crate\n  at M3** when `SqliteStore` becomes the second implementor. Rationale: a crate\n  boundary with one implementation is indirection without payoff, and the trait\n  risk is fixed by having a second implementor, not by where the trait lives.\n- **`dacode-introspect`** — proxy + cache analyzer. **Created at M5**; its\n  exchange-**record format** is an M1 deliverable in §6 (see A10).\n- Update the dependency rules to state where each sits when it arrives\n  (`core` → `store`; `server` → `core`, `protocol`, `store`, never UI crates),\n  and update [Appendix D](D-crate-graph.md) to mark both **deferred**.\n\n**Depends on.** ~~B11~~ ✅ **signed 2026-09-17**. It also turned out to depend on\n**A10** in one direction: this entry says `dacode-introspect`'s \"exchange-**record\nformat** is an M1 deliverable in §6 (see A10)\", and that record **landed earlier\ntoday**, so §2.1 now cites it as normative rather than owed.\n\n**Recommend accept as amended.** If you prefer dependency isolation from day\none, say so and `dacode-store` becomes an M0 crate instead — that is the\nrespectable dissent, and the cost is designing the trait against one\nimplementor. **Done 2026-09-18**, with the dissent not taken and the three\nscoping decisions in the banner. **The \"Current.\" text below is historical** — §2\nnow lists eleven crates rather than ten (`xtask` arrived with A20) and has more\nthan four dependency rules.\n\n**Settled alongside sign-off: all crates are vendored in-tree.** Every crate —\nincluding `dacode-store` and `dacode-introspect` when they arrive — lives under\n`crates/` in this repository with its full name (`dacode-protocol`,\n`dacode-tools`, …). **One repo, not a workspace of repos**; the only external\ncrate is the JSON library, `dacode-json` (renamed from `dacodec`, landed). So B11's\ndeferral is about *when a directory appears under `crates/`*, never about\npublishing anything. §2's tree already implies this; the amendment makes it\nexplicit, and it means [Appendix D](D-crate-graph.md) needs no repo/path column.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-18","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§2.1","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§2.2","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A13","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U199","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"session organization","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-18**, on [B13](B-decisions.md)'s sign-off of 2026-09-17 and a\n> scoping session with the user on 2026-09-18. **The delta as signed covered two\n> sections; ten were edited**, because the question it left unanswered — what a folder\n> *is*, as opposed to which folder a session is in — has no home in §3.2 or §3.5. The\n> three signed events landed verbatim; everything around them is new and is listed as a\n> scoping decision below rather than smuggled in.\n>\n> **Four scoping decisions, all four put to the user and answered 2026-09-18.**\n>\n> 1. **The registry is authoritative for names; membership stays a fold.** The signed\n>    delta carried three *membership* events and nothing that carries what a folder\n>    **is** — name, parent, order. A folder is a cross-session entity while every\n>    `Envelope` is scoped to one session, so a folder tree has no log to live in; and\n>    B13's own motivation defeats pure membership, since a folder for a many-step\n>    feature must be creatable *before* the sessions that fill it. What landed:\n>    `org.json` (§12) is authoritative for names and is the one file in that layout\n>    that is **not** a projection, with B13's Fix-1 carve-out stated explicitly —\n>    membership is rebuildable, names are not. **v1 accepts that a rename reaches no\n>    client but its issuer**, which learns it from the `Ack`. §3.7's \"never silently\n>    stale\" promise is therefore **scoped to a session** with the registry named as\n>    the one exception, and the freshness channel (a version on the tree reads plus a\n>    hint on the socket) is **A7's**, tracked as **U99** — it is §8.2 surface, not\n>    replay semantics. **Rejected and recorded:** widening `Envelope.session` to make\n>    the registry an event stream, which would re-open §3.6's cursor, lock and fencing\n>    questions for a second stream that is not append-only anyway; and folder-as-path-\n>    string, which cannot represent an empty folder and so cannot represent B13's\n>    create-then-fill case at all.\n> 2. **Registry mutation is a `Command`, and §8.4's controller rule does not reach\n>    it.** A `Command` rather than an HTTP route because §8.1's default `dacode` is a\n>    TUI with an in-process engine and **no socket** — an HTTP-only registry would be\n>    unreachable from the client that ships first, and principle 1 would be false for\n>    it. `CreateSession` is the precedent that the enum holds session-less commands.\n>    §8.4 gained the reason the controller rule cannot apply (no session, and an empty\n>    folder has no controller at all), that **any authenticated client** may mutate the\n>    registry in v1 since §8.3 has no users and no roles and inventing a principal here\n>    would be a capability system in all but name, and that `Hello`'s reserved flags are\n>    where a rule lands if multi-user auth arrives — decided once for every non-session\n>    write rather than per command. The three *membership* commands joined §8.4's\n>    controller-only list.\n> 3. **`org.json`, atomic, in §12.** Authoritative, written by temporary file plus\n>    rename because a torn registry loses names nothing else holds, and bounded by the\n>    number of folders rather than of sessions. `meta.json`'s comment corrected — it\n>    *caches* `SessionInfo`, which the log remains the truth about. Conflict rule stated\n>    where both files are described: names → the registry, membership → the log.\n> 4. **One op-carrying `Org { op: OrgOp }` rather than seven registry commands**, on\n>    `TaskUpdated`'s own stated argument: one golden fixture covers the subsystem at no\n>    loss of expressiveness. **The three membership events were left exactly as\n>    signed** — collapsing them into an op-carrying variant too would have deviated\n>    from a signed delta, which needs the user in the same change. Sub-decision forced\n>    by the existing `Ack`: **ids are caller-allocated**. `Ack` has no payload slot and\n>    the registry has no log, so there is no channel to return a new `ProjectId` in;\n>    both ids are ULIDs and `cmd_id` is already caller-chosen, so this costs nothing and\n>    keeps `Ack` payload-free.\n>\n> **Three smaller items the user approved by not objecting, all landed:**\n> `ProjectId(Ulid)` + `FolderId(Ulid)` in §3.1, **excluded from §4.3's `args` scan**\n> with the clause shape `HostId` got; `SessionInfo` promoted from a comment to a real\n> struct in §3.5; tags given a normalization rule (trimmed, case-folded, interior\n> whitespace collapsed, empty rejected) and a config cap (`session.tags_max`, default\n> 64) in §3.2.\n>\n> **Two fold-ins the delta did not mention.** **§4.7 now carries Fix-2 as normative\n> spec text**: B13 assigned the worktree derivation to doc 06, but the spec may not\n> cite a plan doc, and a rule that §14 must test has to be normative somewhere — so\n> §4.7 states that derivation maps back through `--git-common-dir`, and §14 gains the\n> test that can fail on it. **§14's fixture obligation is discharged by derivation\n> rather than by a list**: A1's rule makes the durable subset self-maintaining, so the\n> three events acquire *log* fixtures with nothing to update, and `Org` needs exactly\n> one command fixture. Two engine tests added: *a rename is not an event* and *a\n> sandboxed session derives to the main repository*.\n>\n> ⚠️ **This delta falsified a prediction a signed delta had already made.** A23's\n> banner said twice that \"A14 will add `ProjectId` to the scanned set when it lands\".\n> It does not, and cannot usefully: §4.3's scan catches ids the engine **allocated and\n> injected**, and nothing injects a project id — `DACODE_RT_` starts at three\n> (session, call, tmp) — nor can any v1 tool argument name one, so the scan could never\n> fire. §4.3 now says so in `HostId`'s clause shape, and **both A23 sites carry an\n> annotation** rather than being left as live claims the spec contradicts.\n>\n> **Debt this creates, recorded rather than left to the next reader.** **A7** owns the\n> registry freshness channel (**U99**) and should answer \"who may write a non-session\n> resource from a browser\" once, for `Org`, for config writes and for `promote`\n> together. **Doc 03 stays a summary** despite now being further out of date — A14\n> adds five `Command` variants and three `Event` variants to the crate it describes —\n> because flipping it would deepen an unstarted milestone's doc; M1.1 corrects it, as\n> A1's banner already recorded. **Doc 05** gained one sentence: its `SessionStore` row\n> called the organization mappings a projection, which is now true of membership and\n> false of names. **Closed: U16, U17.**\n\n**Current.** §3.2 has `SessionCreated`, `SessionRenamed`, `SessionClosed`.\n§3.5's `SessionInfo` is \"id, agent, model, cwd, title, parent, children,\nperm_mode\".\n\n**Proposed.**\n- Three new **durable** events plus matching **controller-only** Commands (§8.4):\n  `SessionProjectSet { project: Option<ProjectId> }`,\n  `SessionTagged { add: Vec<String>, remove: Vec<String> }`,\n  `SessionMoved { folder: Option<FolderId> }`.\n- `SessionInfo` gains `project`, `tags`, `folder`; the fold applies them so TUI,\n  web and every observer converge. Golden fixtures for each new variant (§14).\n- **Include B13's Fix-1 wording verbatim**, because §6.2 and §6.3 of\n  [06](../part2-engine/06-session-organization.md) currently contradict each\n  other: organization changes are durable events **in the session event log**,\n  are **never part of `Context`** (the provider never sees them), and the\n  `SessionStore` mapping tables are a **rebuildable projection** for querying,\n  never authoritative.\n- Record B13's Fix-2 consequence for project auto-derivation: in a §4.7 worktree\n  sandbox, `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` returns the **worktree** path, so\n  derivation must map back through `--git-common-dir` to the main repository or\n  every sandboxed session splits into its own project.\n\n**Depends on.** ~~B13~~ ✅ signed, and on ~~A1~~ ✅ **applied first, as required** —\nthe three new events are durable and their fixtures are *log* fixtures, which only\nmeans something once `tier()` exists.\n\n**Recommend accept**, including both fixes. **Done 2026-09-18**, with both fixes and\nwith the scope widened as the banner records: the proposal below describes two\nsections and three events, and what landed is ten sections, three events, four\ncommands, an `OrgOp` enum, two new identifiers and an authoritative registry file.\n**The \"Current.\" paragraph above describes the spec as it was before this pass** —\n§3.5's `SessionInfo` is a real struct now, and §3.2's lifecycle group has three more\nvariants.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-18","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§12","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§14","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.1","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.2","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.3","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.5","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.7","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.3","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.7","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§8.4","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A14","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U200","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"selective compression","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-18**, on [B12](B-decisions.md)'s resolution of 2026-09-17\n> (`zstd`, the dependency policy's first accepted class-1 exception) and the sign-off\n> of the same day. **The mechanism landed exactly as signed** — per-field `codec`\n> discriminator, config-driven allowlist, ~1 KiB floor, configurable level, the one\n> `compress = \"off\" | \"blobs\" | \"allowlist\"` knob, and the explicit non-searchability\n> trade. **Three of its enumerations did not survive contact with deltas that landed\n> after it was drafted**, and each correction is recorded below as a deviation rather\n> than folded in silently. Seven spec sections were edited against the two the delta\n> named, because two of them were carrying claims B12 and B21 had already falsified.\n>\n> **Four scoping decisions. The banner's own instruction covered one; three are\n> deviations from a signed enumeration and are put on the record for that reason.**\n>\n> 1. **The codec is named in the `LogHeader`, not in the discriminator.** The signed\n>    variant list was `plain | zstd | flat | flat+zstd`; what landed is\n>    **`plain | compressed`** plus `{\"codec\":\"zstd\"}` in the header A1 created for\n>    exactly this kind of file-level property. The instruction to \"generalize the\n>    variant names\" pre-authorized removing `zstd` from the enum, but generalizing\n>    naively — `plain | compressed` with the codec in *config* alone — would have\n>    opened a hole: change the configured codec and every already-written record\n>    tagged `compressed` becomes undecodable. The log is append-only, so existing\n>    records are never re-encoded and one file is written under one codec for its\n>    whole life; the header states which. A per-field codec name would duplicate that\n>    in every compressed record and grow the enum by a variant per codec, while still\n>    obliging a reader to support every codec any surviving log used — the same\n>    obligation at a per-record cost. **Blobs are the exception and must be**: they\n>    live outside the log (§12), cannot inherit its header, and so each is\n>    self-describing.\n> 2. **`flat` is not a field codec and was removed from the enum.** A15 listed it\n>    beside the codec names, but `flat` is a representation of a *record* while\n>    `codec` is an encoding of one *field* inside a record — two axes. Keeping both in\n>    one per-field discriminator would make a **format** change expressible as a\n>    **compression** change, which is how a reader ends up unable to tell whether a log\n>    was re-encoded or re-formatted. `flat` stays where §13.5 already put it: a\n>    possible future binary `Codec` for the log, announced by `LogHeader` — the\n>    reserved-not-implemented pattern B7 and A22 both use.\n> 3. **Two compression targets were invalidated by A1 and are restated, not\n>    deleted.** The signed allowlist named `ToolOutputDelta` chunks as \"the dominant\n>    cost\" and `ThinkingDelta`/reasoning content beside them. **Both are `Live`** under\n>    A1's tier rule — broadcast and never written (§3.2, §3.6) — so compressing them\n>    saves zero bytes on disk. The cost *is* dominant, but in the stream, not in the\n>    log. The durable carriers of the same content replaced them: `ToolResult.output`\n>    (already listed) and **`ThinkingEnded.text`**, the whole-block boundary A1 added,\n>    which is where reasoning content becomes durable. This is the clearest case of a\n>    delta drafted before another one that reordered its premises.\n> 4. **§13.2 asserted `flat` as the log's representation, and B21 had already\n>    overruled it.** Its per-surface table said \"On-disk event log → `flat` buffers\",\n>    landed with A16 on 2026-09-12; **B21 was signed five days later** with \"the log\n>    stays append-only JSONL and stays the truth\", and §3.6, §12 (`events.jsonl`,\n>    `LogHeader` on line 1, `index.bin` as `(seq, offset)`) and §8.2 (`/export` →\n>    \"events.jsonl download\") all agree with B21. So the table row was the defect. A15\n>    is the delta that specifies log compression and therefore the one that had to\n>    resolve it — it could not name a per-field codec inside a record whose format was\n>    stated two sections away as something else. §13.1's tier table and §13.2's row are\n>    both corrected; §13.5 keeps `flat` as the reserved future codec.\n>\n> **Three stale claims corrected in the same pass, none of them in a section A15\n> names.** **§2.3** still listed \"the session compression codec\" among the C\n> dependencies that are \"open choices to be settled on measurement\" — B12 settled it by\n> decision, and Appendix F's row had been moved to *accepted* while the spec still\n> called it open. Per A17's applied note (only TLS is named in the spec as unavoidable)\n> the fix moves the codec *out* of the open-choices list and records it as the policy's\n> first accepted exception with its condition still open, rather than adding a `zstd`\n> paragraph to §2.3. **§13.5** said the log's compression codec \"is still open\". And\n> **nothing anywhere said the provider transcript is never compressed** — A15's\n> \"thinking/reasoning content\" target could be read as the transcript, which §13.4\n> forbids re-encoding because the cached prefix must stay byte-identical. §3.6 now says\n> so explicitly, and it is a §14 test.\n>\n> **Debt this creates, recorded rather than left to the next reader.** **U14 stays\n> open and A15 does not discharge it** — it is the *condition* on B12's exception\n> (verify `libzstd`'s cross-compile cost against the CI matrix), and `zstd-sys` is\n> rowed in Appendix F in the commit that adds the dependency, per §2.3's same-commit\n> rule. **A21** (§14 → §14.1/§14.2) must place the three new storage-codec tests in\n> the correctness half, and **B18's T1** benches tune the *level* rather than choosing\n> the codec, so §14.2 should cite the level as the measured knob. **Doc 05** §5.2/§5.3\n> corrected in the same change: its `flat`-compose bullet, its two invalidated targets,\n> its write path and its stale `[DECISION B12]` marker. **Doc 03 stays a summary** and\n> is now four deltas out of date (M1.1). **Closed: U25** — its transcription obligation\n> was exactly the generalization instruction, and it is discharged with a sharper reason\n> than the one it was written with.\n\n**Current.** §3.6 (`:200-210`) specifies JSON Lines of `Envelope`, one file per\nsession, fsync'd at boundaries — no compression, no codec tagging. §12\n(`:716-742`) gives the on-disk layout.\n\n**Proposed.**\n- A **per-field `codec` discriminator** on compressible fields:\n  `plain | zstd | flat | flat+zstd`. The envelope skeleton (ids, `seq`, `ts`,\n  `type`) stays plain so it remains indexable and cheap to scan.\n- A **config-driven allowlist** of compression targets: `ToolResult.output` and\n  `ToolOutputDelta` chunks (the dominant cost), thinking/reasoning content, and\n  blob-store segments (always). **Never** the fields clients filter or search on:\n  ids, `seq`, `ts`, event `type`, tool `name`, permission decisions, `usage`,\n  titles, tags.\n- A **size floor** (~1 KiB) below which compression is skipped, and a\n  configurable level (default ~3).\n- One knob: `[storage] compress = \"off\" | \"blobs\" | \"allowlist\"`. `JsonlStore`\n  defaults to `blobs` so JSONL stays grep-able; `Sqlite`/`Postgres` default to\n  `allowlist`.\n- The **explicit searchability trade**: a compressed field is not SQL-`LIKE`\n  searchable, and that is intended. If full-text search over tool output is ever\n  wanted, either exclude the field or maintain a separate FTS index built at\n  write time.\n- §12 gains the blob/compressed-segment layout.\n\n**Depends on.** ~~B12 (zstd)~~ ✅ **resolved 2026-09-17**, and on ~~A1~~ ✅ **applied\nfirst** — which is why two of this delta's targets had to be restated rather than\ntranscribed. Note B12's open check: `libzstd` is C and touches\nthe cross-compile matrix in\n[31](../part6-quality/31-ci-milestones.md) — verify before M1, since `ruzstd` is\ndecode-only and cannot replace it for writes. **That check is U14, it is still open,\nand applying this delta does not discharge it.**\n\n**Recommend accept.** **Done 2026-09-18**, with the mechanism exactly as proposed and\nthree enumerations corrected against deltas that landed after this was drafted. **The\n\"Proposed.\" list above is now historical** — its variant list, its two `*Delta` targets\nand its silence on where the codec is named are all superseded by the banner, which is\nthe record of what landed and why.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-18","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§12","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§13.1","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§13.2","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§13.5","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§14","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§2.3","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.6","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A15","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U201","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"the `dacode-json` hybrid strategy + `RawJson`","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-12**, on sign-off of [B1](B-decisions.md) **and B6**\n> (the latter delegated by the user alongside B1). All seven items below landed;\n> §13 is now **§13.1 tiers · §13.2 per-surface table · §13.3 the serde rule\n> re-read · §13.4 byte-exactness · §13.5 `Codec` + conventions**. The\n> `_rust.md` caveat item 1 motivated is **deleted**, and the unqualified\n> \"serde types never leak\" rule was corrected to *third-party* at all eight\n> sites that carried it (`AGENTS.md` ×2, the `dacode` skill ×2,\n> `implement.md` ×3, `reviewer.md`, `roles/implementer.md`, `agent-rules.yaml`\n> ×4), with the adapters regenerated.\n>\n> **Three scoping decisions taken while applying it — recorded so a reviewer can\n> check them rather than infer them:**\n>\n> 1. **§13.2 defines what the spec's bare `Json` alias denotes** (`RawJson`)\n>    instead of editing the signatures that use it. `ToolCall.args` (§3.2) and\n>    `Tool::perm_scope`/`call` (§4.3) both say `Json`; rewriting §13 alone would\n>    have left the spec self-contradictory, but those signatures belong to\n>    **A1** and **A2**, which are unsigned. Defining the alias in the section\n>    that owns JSON representation removes the contradiction without pre-empting\n>    either delta.\n> 2. **§4.3's `ToolSpec` comment was fixed** — \"JSON schema (dacode-json Value)\"\n>    named a type that does not exist, the same defect as §13's. Corrected to\n>    \"(canonical RawJson, §13.2)\". The *signature* was left alone; that is A2's.\n> 3. **The log-codec wording is deliberately codec-agnostic.** Item 6's\n>    \"`flat`-based binary codec\" is kept, but §13.5 does **not** name a\n>    compression codec, because [B12](B-decisions.md) was reopened by A17 and\n>    `zstd` is no longer assumed. Doc 02 §2.4's table still says \"zstd\" and is\n>    now stale — logged in [TODO](../TODO.md) §6 rather than folded in here.\n\n**Current.** §13 (`:746-760`), five bullets: a `Codec` trait with\n\"`dacode-json` as the sole implementation\"; \"Tool schemas and provider\nrequest/response bodies are `dacode_json::Value`\"; the serde-isolation rule\n(\"Do not let serde types leak into `dacode-protocol` or `dacode-core`\"); and the\nencoding conventions.\n\n**Proposed — a full rewrite of §13** (it roughly triples in length). Contents:\n1. **Correct the facts.** The crate *name* is now right everywhere — the\n   `dacodec` → `dacode-json` rename landed and was swept through this repo — so\n   this item reduces to its other half: there is **no owned value type**.\n   `Value<'a>`/`Doc<'a>` are borrowed from the input slice plus a `Pool`, so\n   §13's `dacode_json::Value` cannot exist as written and an `Event` cannot hold\n   it. State the tiers that do exist: typed (serde), DOM (`Parser`/`Doc`),\n   on-demand (`pull`), zero-copy (`flat`), builders (`write`/`builder`) — the\n   survey is [02 §2.1](../part1-foundations/02-json-strategy.md).\n2. **Adopt the hybrid per-surface strategy** ([02 §2.4](../part1-foundations/02-json-strategy.md)):\n   serde-derived structs for control types with adjacent tagging; **`RawJson`**\n   for opaque sub-JSON (`ToolCall.args`, `ToolSpec.schema`, provider raw\n   bodies); typed decode for a tool reading its own args; `pull`/DOM for engine\n   inspection; `flat` (+zstd) for the on-disk log; base64 for image bytes; raw\n   `reqwest`/`hyper` + `dacode-json` for provider HTTP (no vendor SDK).\n3. **Re-read the serde-isolation rule.** Its intent is that **third-party** serde\n   types do not leak into `dacode-protocol`/`dacode-core`. dacode's **own** types\n   deriving `serde::{Serialize,Deserialize}` and going through `dacode-json` is the\n   intended path, not a violation. Without this re-reading the rule forbids the\n   only workable strategy.\n4. **Record `schemars`' isolation boundary as a consequence** (B6): `schemars`\n   depends on `serde_json` non-optionally (verified from forge's `Cargo.lock`,\n   both 0.9.0 and 1.2.2), so it is confined to **`dacode-tools`**, converting\n   `schemars::Schema` → `RawJson` at the boundary — the same adapter precedent\n   §13 already sets for MCP crates. Generated schemas must be **canonicalized\n   and byte-stable** (strip `title`, pin key order) because A4 makes tool-order\n   churn a cache kill.\n5. **State byte-exactness as a correctness property.** Opaque JSON is preserved\n   end-to-end (provider → event → log → wire) so the prompt-cache stable prefix\n   survives round-trips. An owned-tree `Value` would reorder keys and reformat\n   floats and silently defeat caching. Include the measured justification:\n   splice ≈110× faster than re-encoding, capture 1.2–1.5× cheaper than a tree\n   with 2 inherent allocations vs 114 080.\n6. **Keep the `Codec` trait** with `dacode-json` as sole impl, leaving room for a\n   `flat`-based binary codec for the log (A15).\n7. Keep the encoding conventions bullet unchanged (adjacent tagging, `Option`\n   omitted, bytes base64).\n\n**Depends on.** B1, B6. **Supersedes A11.**\n\n**Recommend accept.** This is the largest single rewrite in the set, and per\nA11's blast-radius table it also triggers edits to `AGENTS.md`, the role/config/\nskill files and a `tools/agents-sync.py` regeneration. The misleading\n`dacode_json::Value` reference in `.agents/roles/_rust.md` is loaded into every\ncoding agent's context, which makes this the most urgent delta in the set\n*despite* being nominally blocked on B1.\n\n**The upstream rename has landed — this delta is no longer held on it.** The JSON\ncrate was renamed **`dacodec` → `dacode-json`** (module path `dacode_json`,\nupstream commit `e739d6c`), and the whole tree was swept in the same pass: the\nspec, both appendices, the two cross-harness digests, `TODO.md`, `AGENTS.md`, the\nrole and skill files, and the regenerated `.opencode` adapters.\n[Appendix E](E-dacode-json-cheatsheet.md) and\n[02](../part1-foundations/02-json-strategy.md) were renamed with it, since both\nwere named after the old crate.\n\nWhat the sweep deliberately did **not** do is apply items 1–7 below. That is\nstill gated on **B1**, and the `Value`-does-not-exist half of the finding is\nuntouched by a rename — renaming a type that does not exist gives you a\ncorrectly-spelled type that does not exist. Two consequences for whoever applies\nthis:\n\n- Write `dacode-json` / `dacode_json` throughout. The names are already correct\n  everywhere else, so the delta no longer has to introduce them, and item 1's\n  \"correct the facts\" reduces to the `Value` half plus the tier survey.\n- `.agents/roles/_rust.md`'s \"JSON goes through `dacode_json::Value`\" line now\n  carries an inline caveat pointing here and at B1. Applying A16 is what lets\n  that caveat be deleted.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-12","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§13","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A16","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-12T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-12T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U202","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"the dependency policy","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-12**, on the user's sign-off. All four proposed bullets\n> landed; the policy is now normative at **design §2.3**, and\n> [Appendix F](F-c-dependencies.md) is the register §2.3 points at rather than a\n> proposal.\n>\n> **Five scoping decisions taken while applying it — recorded so a reviewer can\n> check them rather than infer them:**\n>\n> 1. **§2 was restructured into §2.1 crate tree · §2.2 internal dependency rules\n>    · §2.3 external dependency policy**, rather than the policy being appended\n>    to §2's existing body. Every other multi-part section of the spec uses\n>    `### N.M` (see §13.1–§13.5, which A16 created the same way), and a lone\n>    `### 2.1` with no §2.2 would leave a numbering gap. §2 had no subsections\n>    before, so nothing referenced §2.x; references to \"§2\" stay valid. The line\n>    numbers in *Current* below are pre-restructure.\n> 2. **The spec gained no link to Appendix F.** `docs/dacode-design.md` contains\n>    **zero** markdown links — the plan cites the spec, never the reverse — so\n>    §2.3 says \"a debt register maintained alongside the implementation plan\"\n>    instead of naming the file. Appendix F carries the back-reference.\n> 3. **`§1.5` is written as \"principle 5\" in the spec.** §1 is a five-item\n>    numbered list with no subsections, so the `§1.5` shorthand this entry uses\n>    reads as a dangling section reference inside the spec itself.\n> 4. **The avoidable consequences are deliberately not enumerated in the spec.**\n>    Only TLS — the one genuinely unavoidable class-1 dependency — is named.\n>    Listing B12's codec, M3's store and `syntect`'s feature flags in the\n>    normative doc would enshrine choices B12 has not made and A15 has not\n>    applied; §2.3 instead states that they remain register rows settled on\n>    measurement. The full register stays in Appendix F, which is where a\n>    reviewer looks for it.\n> 5. **A13/B11 content was kept out**, though it also concerns §2 (in-tree\n>    vendoring, `dacode-store`, `dacode-introspect`). Both are unsigned, so\n>    applying them would have ridden along on A17's approval.\n>\n> **Unrelated edit landing in the same commit:** the spec header gained a\n> one-line **versioning convention** (the version moves at milestone boundaries;\n> a delta application moves the date). That settles the TODO §6 follow-up raised\n> during the A16 pass, which A17 made load-bearing — it is not part of this delta.\n\n**Current.** §2 (`:35-58`) lists the crates and the dependency rules *between\nthem*, but says nothing about what may enter the tree from outside. The only\nrelated statements are §1.5's single-binary principle and §13's \"no vendor SDK\"\nfor provider clients.\n\n**Proposed.** Add a dependency policy to §2:\n- **Pure Rust by default.**\n- A dependency that compiles or links vendored C/C++ — detectable as a `build.rs`\n  invoking `cc`, `cmake` or `bindgen`, or a published `*-sys` companion —\n  requires a row in [Appendix F](F-c-dependencies.md) with a reason and a\n  replacement plan, **and** a CI allowlist entry, in the same commit.\n- FFI bindings to the platform's own ABI (`libc`, `windows-sys`) are **exempt** —\n  no C is compiled or vendored, and banning them would ban the standard library's\n  own platform layer.\n- Enforced by `cargo-deny [bans]` plus a `cargo tree` scan for those markers\n  against the allowlist, so the register is the machine-checkable form of the\n  policy rather than a document nobody re-reads.\n\n**Why adopt it before M0 rather than after M3.** forge's `Cargo.lock` — a\nshipping Rust agent harness — carries `cc`, `cmake`, `openssl-sys`,\n`libsqlite3-sys`, `ring`, `aws-lc-sys`, `aws-lc-rs`, `onig_sys`, `openssl`,\n`rustls` and `native-tls`. That is a good approximation of what dacode\naccumulates without a policy. Adopting it first means the initial `Cargo.toml`\nfiles are written under it.\n\n**Consequences already identified** (full detail in Appendix F):\n- **TLS is unavoidable C.** There is no production pure-Rust TLS: `rustls`'\n  default `aws-lc-rs` provider needs `cmake`, and its `ring` fallback also ships\n  `cc`-compiled C/asm. Prefer `ring` (smaller build, no `cmake`); never\n  `native-tls`/`openssl-sys`. This is the register's flagship entry and it is\n  known-unavoidable on day one, which is itself the argument for having the\n  register.\n- **B12 is reopened.** `zstd` links C via `zstd-sys`, and `ruzstd` is\n  **decode-only** so it cannot serve the write path. Pure-Rust alternatives:\n  `lz4_flex`, `brotli`, `miniz_oxide` / `flate2` with `rust_backend`.\n- **M3's `SqliteStore` conflicts.** `rusqlite`/`sqlx` bundle SQLite C. `redb` is\n  pure Rust and ACID but has **no SQL**, and the session filters in\n  [06 §6.4](../part2-engine/06-session-organization.md) assume queryability.\n  Decide at M3; `JsonlStore` (pure Rust) is the default until then.\n- **`syntect`** needs `default-features = false, features = [\"regex-fancy\"]` to\n  avoid pulling `onig`.\n- **Confirmed clean:** `dacode-json` (only non-optional dep is `zmij`, pure-Rust\n  `no_std`; `cc` is used solely by the opt-in `cbench` baselines),\n  `ratatui` + `crossterm`, `gitoxide`, `grep`/`regex`/`ignore`, `toml_edit`.\n\n**Depends on.** Nothing.\n\n**Recommend accept, early.**\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-12","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§2","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A17","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-12T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-12T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U203","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"bounded fan-out and the seq→gap→resync contract","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-12**, on the user's sign-off, together with **B4** (which\n> this delta had already absorbed the robustness half of). The §3.7 and §4.1\n> blocks below were applied **verbatim** — they were drafted as paste-ready spec\n> text, so proposal and spec should diff clean against each other.\n>\n> **Three scoping decisions taken while applying it — recorded so a reviewer can\n> check them rather than infer them:**\n>\n> 1. **§14 was extended, which the proposal only mentioned under *Interactions*.**\n>    §3.7's step 5 asserts the fold is idempotent over `seq`, but §14's protocol\n>    test list did not cover it, and nothing tested the bounded drop-oldest path\n>    at all. Added both: fold-idempotence as a property test beside the existing\n>    fold-equivalence one, and a deliberately lagged subscriber in the server\n>    integration test that must detect the `seq` gap and resync to the same folded\n>    state as a client that never lagged. Stating an invariant in §3.7 without a\n>    test that can fail on it is how §3.7 got its false guarantee in the first\n>    place.\n> 2. **§3.7's heading was left as \"Reconnect / replay semantics\"** even though the\n>    section now leads with fan-out. The bounded queue *is* what makes reconnect\n>    and replay sound, so the title still describes the content, and renaming\n>    would exceed the signed-off scope. (No doc anywhere anchors to a spec\n>    heading — verified — so a rename would be safe if it is ever wanted.)\n> 3. **Version stays `v0.1` and the date stays 2026-09-12**, per the versioning\n>    convention recorded under the spec's `Status` field: a delta application\n>    moves neither. This is the first application *under* that convention.\n>\n> **No new `Event`/`Command` variant**, so no new golden fixture — as the\n> proposal argued when rejecting an explicit `Lagged` event. §14 gains tests, not\n> fixtures.\n>\n> **Consequence for A1:** its §3.7 resync bullet is now *discharged*, not merely\n> repointed, so A1 narrows to the tier marker and the `*Ended` full-value\n> variants — and its B4 dependency is gone along with B4's resolution. A1 is the\n> last structural blocker on M1's golden fixtures.\n\n**New, raised after A17 landed.** Found by a client-transport question — \"how do\nTUI/web clients talk to `serve`, and what is best for fast notifications?\" —\nwhich exposed that §3.7's central guarantee is asserted rather than earned.\nRecorded first as an amendment to [B4](B-decisions.md); split out as its own\ndelta because **it is transport-independent and should not wait on B4**, which\nis by its own account the most taste-driven decision in the set.\n\n**Current.** §3.7 (`:212-219`) is three bullets ending in an unqualified\nassertion:\n\n> - A client that reconnects does `GetSnapshot` → `Subscribe { since: snapshot.cursor }`.\n>   No gap, no duplicate.\n\n§4.1 (`:306-308`) says the registry is a `DashMap` \"plus a broadcast channel per\nsession\" and stops. Neither mentions what happens when a client cannot keep up.\n\n**The defect.** `tokio::sync::broadcast` — the obvious reading of \"a broadcast\nchannel per session\" — **drops the oldest messages when a receiver lags**,\nsurfacing `RecvError::Lagged`. So the primitive §4.1 already picked is lossy\nunder a slow consumer, and §3.7's \"No gap, no duplicate\" is **false as written**.\n[07-session-registry](../part2-engine/07-session-registry.md):16 does call for\n\"a bounded queue and explicit drop/backpressure policy … a slow client must not\nstall the engine\" (from openclaw's `allBounded` capacity-256 subscriber queue;\nopencode's SSE fan-out queue is also capacity-256), but a summary-depth plan doc\nis not where an implementer looks for a correctness invariant.\n\nNote this is **not** an argument for unbounded queues. Unbounded fan-out means\none stalled browser tab applies backpressure to the engine, which is worse: it\ntrades a recoverable staleness for an unrecoverable hang. Bounded-and-lossy is\nthe right choice — it just has to be *stated*, together with what makes the loss\nrecoverable.\n\n**Proposed §3.7 — replace the three bullets with:**\n\n> **Fan-out is bounded; a slow client never stalls the engine.** Each session's\n> broadcast channel (§4.1) has a bounded per-subscriber queue (default 256\n> envelopes). A subscriber that falls behind loses its oldest queued envelopes.\n> The engine does not block on any client, and one client's backpressure is\n> invisible to every other.\n>\n> **Losing envelopes is safe because every `Envelope` carries a monotonic `seq`\n> (§3.2) and clients detect discontinuities instead of trusting the stream.** The\n> contract, end to end:\n>\n> 1. The engine assigns `seq` when it appends to the log — strictly increasing\n>    within a session, no holes.\n> 2. Fan-out is bounded; a lagging subscriber loses envelopes.\n> 3. A client tracks the highest `seq` it has folded and treats any\n>    discontinuity as a resync trigger. **Detection needs no extra protocol\n>    surface: a missing `seq` is itself the signal.**\n> 4. Resync is `GetSnapshot` → `Subscribe { since: snapshot.cursor }`, replaying\n>    the missed range from the log.\n> 5. The fold is idempotent over `seq`, so a resync whose range overlaps what the\n>    client already applied converges instead of double-counting.\n>\n> A client is therefore never *silently* stale: it either holds every envelope or\n> knows it missed some and is fetching them. **\"No gap, no duplicate\" is a\n> property of this contract, not of the transport** — it holds identically over\n> the WebSocket (§3.6) and over stdio extensions (§7.2).\n>\n> - `Subscribe { since: Some(c) }` streams every event with `seq > c` from the\n>   log, then live events. `since: None` means live only, and is an explicit\n>   opt-out of gap recovery: such a client must re-snapshot on reconnect rather\n>   than assume continuity.\n> - The server keeps the last N (default 5 000) events per session in memory;\n>   older ranges are read from the log file. **N must exceed the subscriber queue\n>   depth**, or a client lagging far enough to be dropped would also fall outside\n>   the in-memory window and pay a disk read to recover. The two limits are one\n>   invariant, not two independent tunables.\n> - A client that reconnects does `GetSnapshot` → `Subscribe { since: snapshot.cursor }`.\n\n**Proposed §4.1 — append one sentence to the registry paragraph:**\n\n> Each session's broadcast channel is bounded with an explicit drop-oldest policy\n> (§3.7); the engine never blocks on a subscriber.\n\n**An explicit `Lagged` event was considered and rejected.** `seq` discontinuity\nalready carries the information, and a new `Event` variant would be a §14\ncompatibility event plus a golden fixture for no gain. If an implementation wants\nto signal lag eagerly it may do so as a transport-level control frame — that is\nan optimisation, not protocol, and clients must not depend on it.\n\n**Depends on.** Nothing. Independent of every B decision, including B4.\n\n**Interactions.**\n\n- **Shrinks A1.** A1's §3.7 bullet (\"adopt seq-numbered events + client-side gap\n  detection → resync\") is subsumed. Applying A18 first leaves A1 carrying only\n  the tier marker and the `*Ended` full-value variants — welcome, since A1 is the\n  most structural delta in the set and must land before any golden fixtures.\n- **Settles part of B4 in advance.** The robustness contract B4 recommends is\n  exactly this one, and it is identical under WS and SSE (opencode reaches it with\n  `?after=<seq>` plus `EventSource` reconnect; openclaw with WS-side detection).\n  B4 then reduces to the transport taste call it should have been.\n- **Promotes doc 07 and doc 19 from recommendation to spec.** Doc 07's bounded\n  queue and doc 19's \"adopt seq-numbered events + gap detection → resync\" both\n  become statements of what §3.7 requires.\n- **§14 gains one test, not one fixture.** No new variant, so no new golden\n  fixture. But fold-idempotence-over-`seq` (step 5) deserves a property test\n  alongside the existing `fold(events) == fold(snapshot_at(k)) + fold(events[k..])`\n  — specifically that folding a range twice is a no-op, which is what makes\n  overlapping resync safe.\n\n**Recommend accept, and accept before A1.** It is small, depends on nothing,\nremoves a false guarantee from the spec, and reduces the blast radius of the\ndelta that everything else in M1 is waiting on.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-12","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.7","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.1","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A18","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-12T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-12T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U204","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"built-in task tracking & gating","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-13**, on the user's sign-off of **B15** (all five\n> recommendation points and all four consequences, none redirected). A new design\n> **§4.8** plus nine further edits: §3.2's `TaskUpdated` variant and its\n> one-variant-carrying-an-op rationale, §3.5's `Snapshot.tasks`, §4.2's gate in\n> the loop pseudocode *and* its special-tool list, §4.3's `todo` entry, §5.4's\n> `gate` → `approve` rename, §9.2's unified tree component, §10's TUI sidebar,\n> §12's `plans/<board>.md`, and §14's two engine tests.\n>\n> **Four scoping decisions taken while applying it — recorded so a reviewer can\n> check them rather than infer them:**\n>\n> 1. **§4.8 was written self-contained, NOT transcribed verbatim, because three\n>    references in the proposed text were dangling.** It cited \"the cache-stable\n>    prefix … its fingerprint (§4.5)\" — §4.5 has no caching content, **A4**\n>    creates it; \"rendered as a segment\" — **B14**'s `Segment`/`ContextBuffer`,\n>    unsigned; and \"joins the guard set … the same shape as A3's per-tool error\n>    budget\" — §4.2 terminates on `if turn_count > agent.max_turns`, **A3**\n>    creates the guard set. Landing those verbatim would have put three references\n>    to nonexistent constructs into the normative spec: the `dacode_json::Value`\n>    defect class again. §4.8 therefore states its own requirement in its own\n>    words (\"never rewrites bytes already sent … a provider's cached prefix\n>    survives\") and describes the gate without naming a guard set. **This is A16's\n>    move** — it defined §13.2's `Json` alias locally rather than editing unsigned\n>    signatures. A3/A4/B14 own tightening the cross-references when they land.\n> 2. **This delta's own `Depends on` line was wrong and is corrected rather than\n>    left in force.** It claimed *\"A1 must land first — the tier marker is what\n>    lets §3.2 say `TaskUpdated` is durable.\"* The spec has **no tier concept at\n>    all** today (§3.6 persists *every* `Envelope`, deltas included — the defect A1\n>    exists to fix), so §4.8 declaring durability in prose is consistent before and\n>    after A1. A1 was never the blocker; A3/A4/B14 were, and decision 1 dissolved\n>    them. The application-order paragraph below still places A19 after the engine\n>    group, but for the *collision* reason (it edits §4.2 and §4.5) rather than the\n>    dependency reason.\n> 3. **§16 gained no B15 entry, though the text below asks for one.** §16 is the\n>    *open*-decisions list and A12's proposal says not to empty it, so a closed\n>    decision never listed there needs no row. What §16 *did* gain is a genuinely\n>    open one — see decision 4.\n> 4. **A gap found while applying was recorded, not patched.** `check` is red while\n>    a `Goal` is unclosed with only `Blocked`/`WontFix` exempt, so a legitimately\n>    *standing* goal reddens the gate on every turn until the reminder cap burns —\n>    this repo's own `tools/tasks.py` failure mode (finding f21) reproduced in the\n>    product. The fix is a `Standing` status or treating `Goal` like `Debt`, and\n>    either invents beyond the signed-off text, so it is **§16.6** with a note that\n>    it must settle before the gate is implemented and that it is a §14\n>    compatibility event.\n>\n> **One golden fixture** for `TaskUpdated` (it is an `Event` variant, so §14's\n> \"every variant\" rule already claims it) and **two engine tests** — rollback\n> rewinds the tree, and a red `check` blocks then escalates rather than looping.\n> The second is the only thing that can fail on the claim that the gate is\n> *bounded*. Version stays **v0.1**, `Date` moves to 2026-09-13.\n\n**New, raised by the user after the original pass.** The requirement: dacode's own\ndevelopment already runs on a two-tier task discipline — `tools/tasks.py` (a\nbranch-keyed tree of goal/task/gate/finding/debt nodes, each with a mandatory\nverification method, and a `check` that exits non-zero on a done-without-\nverification item or an unclosed goal) plus the committed board at\n[TODO](../TODO.md) — and that should be a **product feature**, not a repo-local\nscript. Decision dossier: **B15**. Evidence:\n[task-tracking-and-gating](../../../.agents/knowledge/reference-harnesses/task-tracking-and-gating.md).\n\n**Current.** Four fragments, none of them a design:\n\n- §4.3's built-in list ends `task`, `todo` (per-session scratch list shown in\n  UI)`. One line. No tree, no kinds, no verification, no gate.\n- §4.2's loop terminates on its guard set (A3) and knows nothing about work items.\n- §12 lists `<repo>/.dacode/plans/` and **specifies nothing about it**.\n- §5.4's declarative workflows use the word **`gate`** for `ask_user` step\n  approval — an unrelated concept wearing the same word.\n\n**Proposed — a new §4.8, plus six edits that follow from it.**\n\n> ### 4.8 Task tracking and completion gating\n>\n> Work state is a **fold, not a table**. Every mutation of the task tree is a\n> durable event in the session log (§3.2) folded into `Snapshot` (§3.5), so\n> replay, fork, undo and every client converge on the same tree, and a rollback\n> (§4.6) rewinds it with no reconciliation code.\n>\n> ```rust\n> pub enum TaskKind   { Goal, Task, Gate, Finding, Debt }\n> pub enum TaskStatus { Pending, InProgress, Done, Blocked, WontFix }\n> pub enum Verify {\n>     Command { argv: Vec<String> },   // the engine runs it; the exit code is the gate\n>     Manual  { what: String },        // only a controller (§8.4) may close it\n> }\n> pub struct TaskItem {\n>     pub id: TaskId,\n>     pub parent: Option<TaskId>,\n>     pub kind: TaskKind,\n>     pub title: String,\n>     pub status: TaskStatus,\n>     pub verify: Verify,               // mandatory at creation\n>     pub evidence: Option<String>,     // recorded on close\n>     pub blocked_on: Option<String>,   // required when status == Blocked\n> }\n> ```\n>\n> **`verify` is decided when the item is created, never retrofitted at close.** An\n> `add` without one is rejected. The two flavours differ in what the engine can do\n> with them: `Command` is *run* on close and its exit code is the gate — the only\n> kind that can be enforced rather than asserted; `Manual` is not\n> machine-checkable, so closing it requires a controller action (§8.4) recorded as\n> its own event, and the model cannot self-certify.\n>\n> **`Verify::Command` is an exec surface and is treated as one.** Running it goes\n> through the permission engine (§4.4) exactly as `bash` does — `PermScope::Exec`,\n> the same deny-list, and the active sandbox (§4.7) when there is one. **The gate\n> never auto-approves its own verification command.** A `verify` that needs\n> approval blocks the close until the user grants it; the alternative is\n> model-authored argv executed with no prompt, which is a privilege-escalation path\n> rather than a feature.\n>\n> **The completion gate.** The loop (§4.2) may not emit a terminal turn while\n> `check` is red. `check` is red when an item is `Done` whose `Verify::Command`\n> failed or was never run, when a `Goal` is unclosed, or when a `Debt` is\n> untriaged. The gate is **hard but bounded**: on a red check the engine appends a\n> reminder and continues, up to `task.reminders_max` (default 3), after which it\n> surfaces the red gate to the user and stops rather than looping forever. Items in\n> `Blocked` — which must carry `blocked_on` — and `WontFix` are exempt from every\n> reminder, because work genuinely waiting on a human must not be nudged\n> indefinitely.\n>\n> **Context rendering is append-only.** The tree is rendered into the *new inbound*\n> context as a droppable segment placed **after** the cache-stable prefix and\n> excluded from its fingerprint (§4.5), so a status flip never rewrites bytes a\n> provider has already cached. Tool results echo the **diff**, not the whole tree.\n> The rendered segment is excluded from any export or review path that needs a\n> stable prefix, since its counters change every turn.\n>\n> **Two tiers.** The session tree lives in the event log: durable, rewindable,\n> per-session. Cross-session continuity is an **explicit promotion** to a committed\n> board under `<repo>/.dacode/plans/`, written as markdown a human reviews like any\n> other change. This is deliberately *not* cross-session memory: the board is a\n> reviewed repository artifact and nothing accumulates silently.\n>\n> **Subagents** may update items they own — a `task` spawn (§4.3) carries the\n> parent item id — and may not modify the parent's `Goal`s or `Gate`s.\n\n**The six consequential edits.**\n\n1. **§3.2** gains one durable variant, not five:\n   `TaskUpdated { op: TaskOp, item: TaskItem }`, where `TaskOp` is\n   `add | set | close | block | promote`. One variant carrying an op means one\n   golden fixture for the whole subsystem instead of five, at no loss of\n   expressiveness. Durable per A1's tier marker — a task tree that did not survive\n   replay would be the whole point missing.\n2. **§3.5** `Snapshot` gains `tasks: TaskTree`, so the fold is shared by TUI, web\n   and extensions like every other state (§1 principle 2). No client keeps a\n   private task list.\n3. **§4.2** the completion gate joins the guard set as one more guard — it is the\n   same shape as A3's per-tool error budget and stuck-loop intervention: a\n   condition, a bounded number of interventions, then escalate to the user.\n4. **§4.3** the `todo` entry becomes `todo` (the durable task tree and its\n   `check`, §4.8)`. **The tool keeps the name `todo`** despite doing far more than\n   a scratch list, because every reference harness uses that name and a familiar\n   tool name is worth more to a model than an accurate one; `task` is already\n   taken by subagent spawning.\n5. **§12** `.dacode/plans/` is specified: `plans/<board>.md` — the promoted,\n   committed, human-reviewed board. It joins the `.dacode/.gitignore` rule as\n   *not* ignored, unlike `shadow/` and `worktrees/`.\n6. **§9.2 / §10** the task tree renders in **the same component as the workflow\n   and sub-agent trees** — a node with live status that expands to detail. A9\n   already unifies those two on exactly that description; this is its third arm,\n   one component rather than three.\n\n**One separable cleanup, included but independently rejectable.** §5.4's workflow\nstep attribute `gate` is renamed **`approve`**, which is literally what it does\n(*\"pause for approval of the plan\"*), leaving `gate` unambiguous for the task kind.\nOne word in one TOML example. Reject this bullet and nothing else in A19 changes,\nbut the two senses of \"gate\" then coexist in the spec.\n\n**§16** gains the decision as resolved (B15), and A12 must record it.\n\n**Depends on.** ~~**B15**, and **A1 must land first** — the tier marker is what\nlets §3.2 say `TaskUpdated` is durable rather than live-only.~~ **Corrected at\napplication: B15 only.** The spec has no tier concept at all yet (§3.6 persists\nevery `Envelope`, deltas included), so §4.8 stating durability in prose is\nconsistent before *and* after A1 — see scoping decision 2 in the banner. It does\n*edit* §4.2 and §4.5, which is a collision reason to sequence it after **A3** and\n**A4**, and it must precede **A9** so the tree unification is written knowing the\nthird arm exists. **Applied 2026-09-13 ahead of A3/A4 by making §4.8\nself-contained** (scoping decision 1), which is what removed the collision rather\nthan the ordering.\n\n**Interactions.**\n\n- **B14 supplies the mechanism §4.8 relies on.** \"Rendered as a segment placed\n  after the stable prefix and excluded from the fingerprint\" is only expressible\n  because B14's appender + segment index makes placement a decision rather than a\n  rewrite. Without B14, §4.8's caching paragraph has nothing to operate on.\n- **A14 is the structural precedent.** Session-organization events are durable,\n  folded into `SessionInfo`, never part of `Context`, with store tables as a\n  rebuildable projection. `TaskUpdated` → `Snapshot.tasks` is the same pattern —\n  with one deliberate difference: the task tree **is** rendered into `Context`\n  (append-only), because the model needs to see its own work, whereas organization\n  metadata is exactly what the provider should never see.\n- **§14 gains a fixture and two tests.** One golden fixture for `TaskUpdated`, a\n  fold test that a rollback rewinds the tree, and a gate test that a red `check`\n  blocks a terminal turn up to the reminder cap and then escalates rather than\n  looping. The gate test is the important one: it is the only thing that can fail\n  on the claim that the gate is bounded.\n\n**Recommend accept.** The model, the persistence and the caching are all\nprecedent-backed and point the same way; the executable-`verify` gate is the one\npart with no precedent anywhere, which is the reason to specify it carefully\nrather than the reason to skip it.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-13","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§12","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§14","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§16","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.2","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.5","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.2","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.3","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.8 (new)","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§5.4","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§9.2/§10","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A19","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-13T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-13T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U205","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"repository tooling language","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-16**, on sign-off of **B16** — but **narrowed on the way\n> in**, and the narrowing is the substance of the decision. The user's rule was\n> *\"the python scripts that relate to work on the repo/design docs, that are not\n> shipped with the final harness and not required by harness for work — we can\n> leave as python.\"* Applied to all five tools, that rule leaves **nothing to\n> port**, so §2.4 is not \"Rust by default with recorded exceptions\"; it is **two\n> classes with a scope boundary between them**.\n>\n> **Six scoping decisions taken while applying it — recorded so a reviewer can\n> check them rather than infer them:**\n>\n> 1. **The proposal's principle-5 sentence was false as drafted and is rewritten.**\n>    It claimed *\"Principle 5's no-external-runtime claim then covers the project's\n>    own development and not only its output: one toolchain for contributors and\n>    for CI.\"* Under the narrowed policy contributors and CI still need Python, so\n>    §2.4 now **states the boundary** — principle 5 scopes to the product and its\n>    build, not the authoring environment — and names the cost as chosen rather\n>    than leaving an unqualified claim that silently covers half the project. Same\n>    defect class as §3.7's \"No gap, no duplicate\" before A18, and as B7's advisory\n>    `bash` allow-list.\n> 2. **Authoring tooling is *out of scope*, not an exception awaiting a port.** The\n>    proposal's framing (\"a script in another language is permitted only while it\n>    has no Rust home … and is deleted when that disposition lands\") would have made\n>    all five Python tools temporary. They are not. §2.4's last-paragraph exception\n>    survives only in its genuine case: tooling that must run on an unbuilt\n>    checkout, which is why doc validation stays a script.\n> 3. **§2.1 and §2.2 were edited as the proposal's items 2 and 3 specify**, plus one\n>    rule the proposal did not contain: **no crate depends on `xtask`**. Without it\n>    a shipped crate could import build-time codegen, and the \"ships nothing\"\n>    property would be unenforced.\n> 4. **§14 gained two bullets** (the proposal's item 4). One makes `xtask` ordinary\n>    Rust with a **golden-output test** for generated bytes, on the same reasoning\n>    as the protocol's fixtures — silent drift in generated bytes is not caught by\n>    the consumer. The other states plainly that authoring tooling is outside §14,\n>    so the gap is recorded rather than discovered.\n> 5. **The disposition table was deliberately NOT written into the spec.** It names\n>    five repo scripts, which are not product surface. §2.4 requires a register and\n>    points at one maintained alongside the implementation plan — the same move §2.3\n>    makes for the class-1 dependency register — and ~~the table itself lives in B16's\n>    dossier~~ **the table itself now lives in [Appendix G](G-authoring-tools.md)**,\n>    the register §2.4 points at (TODO §6's U80, closed 2026-09-16 — a dossier is an\n>    audit trail, not a home for current state). The spec carries zero markdown links, so the register is named in\n>    prose, not linked.\n> 6. **Doc 01 was not deepened.** M0 has not started, so the just-in-time rule\n>    holds and the depth count is unchanged. ~~The proposal's item 3 (\"doc 01 gains\n>    the disposition table when it is deepened\") is therefore still owed, and lands\n>    at M0 rather than here.~~ **Item 3 is superseded by Appendix G:** doc 01 gains\n>    a *pointer* to the register when it is deepened, not a copy of the table.\n>\n> **Version stayed v0.1 and the `Date` moved to 2026-09-16**, per the convention\n> under the spec's `Status` field.\n\n**New, raised by the user after the original pass** — *\"for the harness, we might\nnot want to use/rely on python in the system (for internal tooling) as much as we\ncan — build rust-based functions.\"* Decision dossier: **B16**.\n\n**Current.** Nothing. The spec governs what may enter the Rust dependency tree\n(§2.3) and what the crates are (§2.1) and how they depend on each other (§2.2),\nbut says **no word about what language the repository's own tooling is written\nin**. The only statement anywhere is `AGENTS.md`'s tech-stack row — \"Python 3 +\nPyYAML for repo scripts (`tools/`)\" — which records a habit as though it were a\npolicy. That habit is now ~~1 738~~ **2 073** lines across five tools, three of\nwhich parse YAML *(recounted at sign-off; the original figure was already stale, and\nthe growth — +335 in one session, all of it untested — is itself part of the\nargument)*.\n\n**Proposed — a new §2.4, sibling to §2.3.** Both answer the same class of\nquestion (*what does this project permit itself*), and §2 already uses the `### N.M`\nconvention that A16 and A17 established.\n\n> ### 2.4 Repository tooling\n>\n> Tooling that operates on this repository — link checking, agent-adapter\n> generation, doc indexing, work tracking — is **Rust, in an `xtask` crate inside\n> the workspace**. Principle 5's no-external-runtime claim then covers the\n> project's own development and not only its output: one toolchain for\n> contributors and for CI.\n>\n> A script in another language is permitted only while it has no Rust home. Each\n> one carries a recorded disposition — port, retire, or replace by dogfooding a\n> product feature — and is deleted when that disposition lands. **Once `xtask`\n> exists, a new tool is never written in another language.**\n>\n> Where a tool duplicates a dacode feature, the feature wins: the repository uses\n> dacode on itself rather than maintaining a parallel implementation.\n>\n> The one documented exception is tooling that must run on a checkout that has not\n> been built. Doc validation is the case in point: if it requires a compiled\n> workspace, the docs cannot be checked until the code builds, which inverts the\n> intended order. An exception is recorded here with its reason, never left\n> implicit.\n\n**Why a spec section and not just an `AGENTS.md` line.** Because the claim it\nmakes is architectural, not stylistic: principle 5 currently holds for the shipped\nbinary and not for the project that ships it, and §2.3 set the precedent that\n\"what this repo permits itself\" is spec content. It also gives the disposition\ntable a normative home, so a lingering Python script is a recorded exception\nrather than an oversight.\n\n**The four edits that follow from it.**\n\n1. **`AGENTS.md`'s tech-stack row is corrected**, since it states the habit as\n   policy. It becomes \"Rust `xtask` (Python only where §2.4 records an\n   exception)\".\n2. **§2.1's crate tree gains `xtask`** as a workspace member that ships nothing —\n   the same seam B6's offline schema generator already assumes, so this does not\n   invent a location.\n3. **M0 scaffolds it.** [01](../part1-foundations/01-workspace-scaffolding.md)\n   gains the disposition table when it is deepened; the crate itself is part of\n   M0's workspace, not a later addition.\n4. **§14's testing strategy** should say whether repo tooling is tested. Today the\n   five Python scripts have no tests, which is how `attention.py` shipped a\n   milestone heuristic that over-triggered and an edit that deleted a function\n   definition while still passing a syntax check. Rust tooling gets `cargo test`\n   for free; that is an argument for the port independent of the toolchain count.\n\n**Depends on.** **B16**, and it must be **read alongside B10** — three of the five\ntools parse YAML, so porting them either adds a YAML crate (`serde_yaml` is\ndeprecated upstream, and no YAML crate appears in\n[Appendix F](F-c-dependencies.md)) or moves `agent-rules.yaml` and the tracker to\nTOML, which is B10's subject. Deciding B16 without B10 lets B16 settle B10 by\naccident.\n\n**Interactions.**\n\n- **B15/§4.8 turns two of the five tools into retirements, not ports.**\n  `tasks.py` *is* the task tree and `attention.py` is a view over it, so porting\n  them would build a second implementation of a spec'd product feature. The\n  disposition is retire-and-dogfood — cheaper than porting, and the stronger form\n  of the user's instinct. **Retiring `tasks.py` is blocked on §16.6** (standing\n  goals), because that bug was found in `tasks.py` and an unfixed §4.8 would\n  inherit it.\n- **A17 (§2.3) is the precedent for the section's shape and its enforcement\n  style**: a stated default, a named exception class, and a machine-checkable\n  register. §2.4's register is the disposition table; its enforcement is that a\n  script with no row is out of policy.\n- **B6's `xtask`-style schema generator** already assumes this crate exists. A20\n  makes that assumption explicit rather than incidental, and the two should share\n  one crate rather than two.\n\n**Recommend accept**, with the `check-links.py` dissent recorded in B16 — it is\nthe one tool where \"must run on an unbuilt checkout\" is a genuine argument for\nkeeping Python permanently, and §2.4's final paragraph exists to let that\nexception be stated rather than fought over later.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-16","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§2.4 (new)","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A20","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-16T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-16T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U206","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"benchmarking as a first-class concern","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-18.** *\"yes, let's do split\"* — §14 is now **§14.1 Correctness** and\n> **§14.2 Benchmarks and evaluation**, with all six proposed items landed and every link\n> stripped as the transcription warning required. **It arrived later than planned and that\n> changed what it was for**: A21 was drafted when §14 was a flat five-bullet list and\n> nothing cited a subsection, and by the time it landed **the spec already referenced\n> `§14.2` twice** — §3.2's `Usage` paragraph and §4.5's partial-compaction paragraph, both\n> written by A4 — so those were **dangling forward references in the spec**, and applying\n> A21 is what made them resolve.\n>\n> **Three scoping decisions taken while transcribing.**\n>\n> 1. **§14.1 is not \"the existing text unchanged\", because the existing text more than\n>    doubled since this was drafted.** A3, A4, A5, A9, A15 and A10's carve-out each added\n>    tests, so §14.1 carries **twelve** bullets rather than five — the eleven that had\n>    accumulated plus the `Clients` bullet this delta itself adds (**thirteen once A22\n>    added `Trust boundary` later the same day, fourteen once A7 added `Config writes\n>    and the registry hint`**). The instruction was\n>    right in intent (do not rewrite the correctness suite while adding a benchmarking one)\n>    and wrong in fact, so it was followed as intent.\n> 2. **A twelfth bullet was added that this delta does not propose: `Clients`.** **U101**\n>    recorded that §14 had **no UI test tier at all**, so A9's two client rules were\n>    normative with nothing that could fail on them, and named A21 as owner. Both properties\n>    are now stated as tests — mouse tracking off unless an overlay holds the alternate\n>    screen, and collapse-on-paste honouring its thresholds with an indivisible chip —\n>    **with the harness deliberately not named**: the TUI crate does not exist and §9.1's\n>    framework decision is open, so choosing a backend now would be choosing it for a crate\n>    that has not been written. What is fixed is the properties, which outlive any harness.\n>    **U101 closes.**\n> 3. **The four inbound assignments were discharged inside §14.2 rather than left as\n>    notes.** A15 required the compression **level** be named as a measured knob, since B12\n>    settled the codec and what remains is which level trades ratio against latency — so the\n>    default is a number tier 1 produces rather than a value someone reasoned about. A10\n>    required §6's **`ExchangeRecord`** be named as what hit rate is computed *from*, which\n>    is the sentence that explains why the record was an M1 deliverable while the analyzer\n>    is M5: a metric cannot be retrofitted onto exchanges nobody recorded. U97 required\n>    §4.5's partial compaction be gated on the hit-rate number existing, so its \"candidate\n>    not feature\" status now has a named instrument. And B18's publication rule is stated\n>    **normatively and first**, because everything below it depends on it.\n>\n> ⚠️ **This delta's own `Current.` list is now stale in three places, and was corrected\n> rather than transcribed.** It describes §3.6/§12's codec as \"deliberately unnamed and\n> stalled on a measurement nobody can take\" — **A15 named it `zstd` and B12 resolved it**.\n> It cites §4.5's \"compaction at 80 % of the window\" as an unmeasured assertion — **A4 made\n> that opt-in and explicitly not v1's default**. And it says §14 is a flat five-bullet list;\n> it was eleven before this pass touched it. Its knock-on claim that \"**B12 stops being a\n> stalled vote**\" is likewise historical: B12 was resolved by *decision* on 2026-09-17, so\n> §14.2 tunes the **level** rather than producing the codec number B12 was waiting for.\n>\n> **One new gate came out of this pass rather than out of the delta.** The dangling `§14.2`\n> references were invisible to every existing check, and the reason is structural: the spec\n> is forbidden markdown links, and `check-links.py` validates links and anchors — so **the\n> convention that keeps the spec clean had also removed the only mechanism that could catch\n> a bad internal cross-reference**, in a file carrying 290 of them. `check-links.py` now\n> has `check_section_refs()`, bound to the same `NO_LINK_FILES` list for exactly that\n> reason, and it was **tested by injecting three dangling references** (`§14.9`, `§99.3`,\n> `§2.7`) and confirming it named those three, ignored the two valid ones beside them, and\n> exited 1 — then passed again on restore. A gate nobody has watched fail is a gate nobody\n> can trust.\n>\n> **Debt this creates.** **Doc 33** carries the corpus contents, the scorer implementations\n> and the recorded environments, and is still a summary — §14.2 says so explicitly and\n> names it in prose rather than by link. **B18's tiers** are named in §14.2 by *property*\n> (deterministic-in-CI, token/cost, task-success-out-of-CI) rather than by their T1/T2/T3\n> labels, so doc 33 owns the mapping. **No wire change, no fixture, no new type** — which is\n> why this was the cheapest delta in the set, as its own entry predicted.\n\n**Depends on [B18](B-decisions.md).** ~~T2's cache-hit-rate metric additionally\nneeds **A4**'s `Usage` cache fields to exist; T1, T3 and the publication rule do\nnot, so A21 can land ahead of A4 with T2 stated as a requirement rather than a\nformula.~~ ✅ **The ordering this anticipated did not happen, and the result is\nbetter**: A4 landed *first*, so §14.2's hit-rate tier is stated against fields that\nexist (`Usage`'s four counters) rather than as a requirement awaiting them. It also\ngained a dependency nobody recorded — **A10's `ExchangeRecord`**, which is what the\nhit rate is computed *from*, and which is why that record was an M1 deliverable while\nthe analyzer that reads it is M5.\n\n**Current.** §14 is titled *Testing strategy* and is a flat five-bullet list —\nprotocol fixtures, engine `MockProvider` sequences, tool sandboxes, a server\nintegration test, the echo extension. Every item is a **correctness** test: it\npasses or it fails. There is no notion of a measured property anywhere in the\nspec, and yet the spec asserts several:\n\n- §13.4 — byte-exactness exists so \"the prompt-cache stable prefix survives\".\n  Nothing measures whether the cache hits.\n- §13.3 — tool-schema churn \"*including pure reordering*\" destroys that prefix.\n  A golden fixture catches a schema *change*; nothing catches the cache\n  *consequence*.\n- §3.7 — a 256-envelope queue and a 5 000-event window, \"one invariant, not two\n  independent tunables\". Neither number is derived.\n- §4.5 — compaction at 80 % of the window, on a token *estimate*.\n- §4.8 — `reminders_max` default 3.\n- §3.6/§12 — the log's compression codec, deliberately unnamed and stalled on a\n  measurement nobody can take.\n\n**Proposed.** Restructure §14 as `### 14.1` / `### 14.2`, matching the `### N.M`\nconvention §2 and §13 adopted when A17 and A16 landed. §14.1 is the existing text\nunchanged. §14.2 is new:\n\n1. **State the distinction.** Correctness tests pass or fail; benchmarks produce\n   numbers that must be compared against a recorded baseline. Both are required;\n   neither substitutes for the other.\n2. **Name the deterministic tier as a CI gate.** Benchmarks that need no provider\n   — fold throughput, snapshot latency, log append and codec throughput, fan-out\n   cost, memory per session — run against a **committed event-log corpus**, since\n   the log is already the fixture format and `MockProvider` already replays canned\n   chunks.\n3. **Name prompt-cache hit rate as a measured property of the engine**, alongside\n   the harness's own token overhead before the first user token. This is the one\n   addition that changes behaviour rather than recording intent: it converts\n   §13.4's rationale from an assertion into something that can fail.\n4. **State the publication rule normatively**: *a number may not be published,\n   quoted in a specification, or used to justify a decision unless its scorer is\n   in the tree, re-runnable, and its environment recorded alongside it.*\n5. **Record task-success evaluation as deliberately out of CI** — pinned model and\n   date, N repetitions reported as a distribution, invoked by hand — and note that\n   its driver is `run -p --output jsonl` and its pass/fail primitive is §4.8's\n   `Verify::Command`, so it needs **no new protocol surface**.\n6. **Record cross-harness comparison as a non-goal**, so it is not relitigated.\n\n**Why a spec change rather than a plan doc.** Three of the six numbered assertions\nabove are *in the spec*, and two of them (§13.3's reordering hazard, §3.7's paired\nlimits) are stated as invariants the design depends on. An invariant the spec\nasserts and no artefact checks is the exact defect A18 was written to fix in §3.7\n— the \"No gap, no duplicate\" claim that was asserted rather than earned. §14 is\nwhere that gets fixed for the numeric claims, and the plan doc\n[33](../part6-quality/33-benchmarking.md) then says *how*.\n\n**Blast radius.** §14 only; no `Event` variant, no wire change, no fixture. §15's\nroadmap gains nothing — the work attaches to existing milestones. ~~The one knock-on\nis that **B12 stops being a stalled vote**: §14.2's deterministic tier names codec\nthroughput as a measured property, which is the number B12 has been waiting for.~~\n⚠️ **Historical**: B12 was resolved by *decision* on 2026-09-17 (`zstd`, the\npolicy's first class-1 exception) and A15 applied it, so tier 1 tunes the **level**\nrather than producing a codec number. **The blast radius was also understated in one\ndirection**: §14 gained a `Clients` bullet this entry does not propose, because\n**U101** named A21 as the owner of the missing UI test tier — so the radius is \"§14\nplus one debt discharged\", still with no protocol surface.\n\n**Watch the link convention.** The proposed text above is *plan* text and cites\nAppendix F, doc 33 and B12 by link. **Strip every link when transcribing** —\n`grep -c '](' docs/dacode-design.md` must return 0. Name doc 33 as \"the\nimplementation plan's benchmarking document\" in prose, as A17 did for Appendix F.\n✅ **Honoured** — `grep -c ']('` returns 0, and §14.2's closing paragraph points at\nthe plan's benchmarking document in prose. **This warning turned out to be pointing\nat a bigger hole than the one it names**: the convention that keeps the spec\nlink-free had also removed the only mechanism that could catch a bad *internal*\ncross-reference, and A4 had left two dangling `§14.2` citations that nothing could\nsee. `check-links.py` now checks those too; see the banner.\n\n**Recommend accept.** It is the cheapest delta in the set — one section, no\nprotocol surface — and it is the only one that makes an existing spec claim\nfalsifiable. **Done 2026-09-18**, with the three scoping decisions in the banner and\n**one new standing gate** as a by-product. **The `Current.` and `Proposed.` text below\nis historical** — it describes a five-bullet §14 and a codec nobody had chosen, and its\nlist of unmeasured spec assertions has since been shortened by A4 and A15 landing.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-18","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§14 → §14.1/§14.2","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A21","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U207","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"close four capability seams","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-18, with B7's `yolo` confirmation landing in the same pass as this\n> banner required** — so §4.4 was edited once rather than twice. All four seams are closed\n> and **ten spec sections** carry the result against the six this entry named, because\n> item 4 reaches §9.2's renderer and every item needs §14 tests. **Two questions this\n> banner had left open were put to the user and answered 2026-09-18**, and one of the\n> answers reverses this banner's own reading.\n>\n> **Three scoping decisions, the first of which is a reversal.**\n>\n> 1. ⚠️ **The `AllowPattern` bound IS applied — this banner's orchestrator reading was\n>    wrong and the user corrected it.** The banner recorded the pattern-subset bound as\n>    part of what \"permissive\" declines, reserved with the capability flags rather than\n>    applied, and flagged it for redirect *\"since it costs a user nothing and only\n>    constrains what an extension may grant on the user's behalf.\"* **Redirected: the bound\n>    is applied.** The argument that settled it is the one the flag anticipated —\n>    *permissive* is about the **user's** experience, and the bound changes none of it,\n>    since a user at their own terminal can still grant `**` explicitly. What it constrains\n>    is a **client acting on the user's behalf**: without it, §3.3's `AllowPattern(String)`\n>    plus §4.4 putting session grants at the *top* of the resolution order plus §8.4 letting\n>    extensions resolve permissions compose into \"one bridge answering one prompt can grant\n>    `**` for the session\", which makes any connected extension root-equivalent. That is not\n>    permissiveness, it is the unreviewable three-section composition this delta was\n>    promoted out of §6 prose to fix. Mechanically checkable at resolution time because the\n>    request is already in `PermissionRequested { scope }`; a pattern outside it is an\n>    `Ack Err`.\n> 2. **The capability split stays reserved, as redirected.** §8.4 now states the trust model\n>    **explicitly** — *a connected extension is as trusted as the local terminal* — with the\n>    reason it is deliberate (a phone that can report a session blocked but cannot unblock\n>    it is a pager, not a client) and the reason it was a defect before (it was true only by\n>    composition of three separately-written sections, which is unreviewable). §7.2's `Hello`\n>    **reserves** `answer_questions` and `resolve_permissions`, both granted by default in\n>    v1, so tightening later is additive and not a wire break — B7's inert-stub pattern\n>    applied to a trust model. The two flags are separate because the acts differ in kind:\n>    answering a question supplies data, resolving a permission authorises an effect.\n> 3. **B7 confirmed A23's `yolo` carve-out, and the user added a notice.** The carve-out\n>    stands: under `yolo` a detection is recorded without escalating, because a mode whose\n>    whole meaning is that the user opted out of being asked cannot start asking, and\n>    auditability is not lost since the detection is still on `PermissionResolved`. **But it\n>    does not stay silent either** — `yolo` is the mode where a leak is *most* likely\n>    (everything runs automatically, so nothing passes a human on the way to exec) and\n>    therefore the one mode where an escalation-based warning would never fire. So the first\n>    detection in a session raises a **non-blocking notice**: rendered once, does not pause\n>    the turn, requires no answer, not repeated. Once per session rather than per detection,\n>    because a notice on every occurrence in a mode chosen for not being interrupted *would\n>    be* the interruption.\n>\n> **Items 1, 3 and 4 landed as confirmed, each with the reason it is a rule rather than a\n> preference.** **Item 1**: `run` is an argv array executed without a shell, and\n> **templating survives into argv elements** — deleting it would have been safer and less\n> useful, while the array form keeps the convenience and removes the surface. A hook that\n> wants a shell must write `[\"sh\",\"-c\",…]`, which is the point: the dangerous case becomes\n> visible in the config rather than being the default behaviour of any hook that\n> interpolates. §7.1 now states *why* this is a rule and not an example-level detail — the\n> example's own fields are client-controlled, but the natural fields to interpolate on\n> `QuestionAsked`/`PermissionRequested` (the prompt, a tool name, an error message) are\n> **model-authored**, so `\"{{ prompt }}\"` in a shell string is a model-reachable\n> command-injection bug and nothing about the field name warns the author. **Closes U61.**\n> **Item 3**: inheritance is an **intersection** — the child's effective scopes are its own\n> resolved scopes ∩ the parent's, mode `min(child, parent)` — which makes §5.3's *existing*\n> promise true on both axes instead of only the mode axis, and is not a restriction on an\n> operator who can set the parent wide. **Item 4**: `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer`\n> origin-wide plus `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"` as a *renderer* rule so it covers any surface\n> rendering model output; `?token=` documented as bootstrap-only and stripped once the\n> cookie is set; `/api/sessions/:id/file` and `/blob/:blob_id` served as `text/plain` with\n> `Content-Disposition: attachment` under a CSP forbidding inline script, because both return\n> arbitrary repository or tool content **on the authenticated origin** and are therefore a\n> stored-XSS path. §8.3 records that none of this costs capability, which is why it is not\n> part of the permissive trade-off: what stops leaking is dacode's **own** bearer token.\n>\n> **Debt this creates, recorded rather than left to the next reader.** **A25's M5 half**\n> inherits §8.4's trust model and must argue remote principals **on its own merits**, since\n> the capability split no longer arrives from here — the reserved flags are the mechanism it\n> should use. **A7** owns the §8.5 writable-path allowlist and \"who may `promote` from a\n> browser\", and should answer it together with A14's **U99** registry question rather than\n> separately, because §8.4 now says the rule for non-session writes should be decided once.\n> **Doc 16** (permissions) and **doc 19** (server gateway) are the plan-side homes for items\n> 2 and 4 and are both still summaries. **U62, U63 and U65** were checked against this pass\n> rather than assumed: see the board for which it discharges and which it does not.\n> **No `Event` variant and no fold change, so no golden fixture** — item 2 changes a\n> `Command`'s *validation*, not its shape, and the reserved flags are an additive field on\n> `Hello`, exactly as this entry predicted.\n> ---\n>\n> **The signed-off record follows, preserved verbatim except where it now contradicts\n> what landed.** It is kept because the reasoning is the audit trail — but read it against\n> the banner above, which supersedes it on one point.\n>\n> ~~**Item 2 (`AllowPattern` bound + capability split) is NOT taken for v1.**~~ ⚠️\n> **Superseded on the bound, 2026-09-18: the capability split is indeed reserved rather\n> than applied, but the `AllowPattern` subset bound IS applied** — the user redirected\n> exactly the reading this paragraph flagged. The rest of this block stands as the reason\n> §8.4 needed the trust model written down. A\n> permissive first version means a connected extension may resolve permissions and may\n> grant a session-wide pattern, which is what §8.4 already says. But the defect A22\n> named was never the *choice* — it was that the choice is **implied by three\n> separately-written sections composing badly**, and therefore unreviewable. So the\n> transcription owes two things instead of a restriction: **(a)** §8.4 states the trust\n> model **explicitly** — *a connected extension is as trusted as the local terminal; it\n> may answer questions, resolve permissions and grant session-wide patterns; this is\n> deliberate for v1 and is the reason a bridge is not a notification-only surface* — and\n> **(b)** `Hello { capabilities }` **reserves** the `answer_questions` /\n> `resolve_permissions` flags now, granted-by-default in v1, so tightening later is\n> additive and not a wire break. That is B7's inert-stub pattern applied to a trust\n> model: permissive now, no refactor later. *(Recorded as the orchestrator's reading:\n> the pattern-subset bound — `AllowPattern` ⊆ the requested scope — is part of what\n> \"permissive\" declines, and is reserved with the flags rather than applied. Redirect\n> this if the bound was meant to survive, since it costs a user nothing and only\n> constrains what an extension may grant on the user's behalf.)* ✅ **Redirected, and the\n> redirect is the better answer: the bound costs the *user* nothing because they can still\n> grant `**` from their own terminal, and it constrains only what a client acting on their\n> behalf may infer from one request. \"Permissive\" was about the user's experience, and the\n> bound does not touch it.** Note (a)'s phrase \"grant session-wide patterns\" is now bounded\n> by §4.4 — session-wide, but never wider than the request that opened the grant.\n>\n> **Items 1, 3 and 4 were each confirmed individually** (\"1. Hooks - ok. 2. Sub-agents -\n> ok. 3. Ok.\"), in the forms proposed here, and none of them is a permission-model\n> choice:\n>\n> - **1 — hooks:** `run` takes an **argv array executed without a shell**, and\n>   **templating survives into argv elements** rather than being deleted. With no shell\n>   there is nothing to inject into, so the convenience stays and the injection surface\n>   goes; a hook that genuinely wants `sh -c` must say so explicitly, which is the point\n>   of making it visible. `post` gains the redaction rule (or the plain statement that a\n>   `post` hook sees the whole envelope and must target a trusted endpoint).\n> - **3 — sub-agent inheritance:** the rule becomes an **intersection** — a child's\n>   effective scopes are its own resolved scopes ∩ the parent's, and its mode is\n>   `min(child, parent)`. This is not a restriction on the operator, who can set the\n>   parent wide; it makes §5.3's **existing promise** (\"a child cannot exceed its\n>   parent\") true for scopes and not only for modes, closing the case where a child's own\n>   agent file carries `edit.allow = [\"**\"]` under a parent scoped to `src/**`.\n> - **4 — web-UI hygiene:** `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer` plus `rel=\"noopener\n>   noreferrer\"` on rendered links, `?token=` stripped once the cookie is set,\n>   `/api/sessions/:id/file` and `/blob/:blob_id` served as `text/plain` with\n>   `Content-Disposition: attachment` under a CSP that forbids inline script, and `Bearer`\n>   documented as the primary path with `?token=` as the bootstrap-only convenience it\n>   is. No capability cost: this stops dacode's **own** bearer token leaking through the\n>   `Referer` of a page that renders model-authored markdown.\n>\n> ~~**Still owed by the transcription:** the §4.4 half is **B7's**, not A22's, and B7 is\n> now signed — so A22's application and B7's confirmation of A23's `yolo` carve-out\n> should land together rather than twice.~~ ✅ **They did land together, 2026-09-18** —\n> §4.4 was edited once, carrying both the `AllowPattern` bound and B7's confirmation of the\n> `yolo` carve-out plus the user's non-blocking-notice addition. **A23's debt is thereby\n> discharged** and B7's entry records it. No `Event` variant and no fold change, so **no\n> golden fixture**; item 2's reserved flags are an additive field on `Hello` — ✅ **and that\n> prediction held exactly**.\n\n**Depends on nothing.** Every item below is a *correction* — the spec presents\nsomething as a boundary that does not hold — so there is no fork to sign off\nfirst, only whether the correction is right. That makes A22 the same shape as A17\nand A18, both of which landed on their own.\n\n**Provenance.** Found in the session-8 third-party review and first recorded as\nprose bullets in [TODO](../TODO.md) §6. **They are promoted here because a §6\nbullet is not sign-off-able**: it appears in no delta sweep, has no id to cite or\nclose, and is exactly how §16.6 became invisible. A spec change belongs in this\nappendix; the §6 entries now point here.\n\n**These are not hypotheticals.** The same class was already caught once, in\n§4.8's `Verify::Command` — model-authored argv the engine would have run. That one\nwas patched back into the artefact before sign-off. These four were not caught,\nand three of them predate it.\n\n#### 1. §7.1 — hooks interpolate into a shell command\n\n**Current.** *\"`run` gets the envelope on stdin and templated args\"*, with the\nexample `run = \"notify-send 'dacode' '{{ session.title }}: {{ reason }}'\"` — a\nstring interpolated into a command line.\n\n**Stated precisely, because the strength of the claim matters:** the example's own\nfields are client-controlled, not model-controlled. But the **mechanism is\ngeneral**, §7.1's second example fires hooks on `QuestionAsked` and\n`PermissionRequested`, and the natural fields to interpolate *there* — the\nquestion `prompt`, a tool name, an error message — **are model-authored**. A hook\nauthor who writes `{{ prompt }}` has written a command-injection bug, and nothing\nin §7.1 warns them.\n\n**Proposed.**\n- `run` takes an **argv array, not a shell string**, executed without a shell.\n- **Delete templated args entirely.** The envelope already arrives on stdin,\n  which is the complete mechanism; templating adds an injection surface and no\n  capability. Hook scripts parse stdin, as `dacode-ext-*` already must.\n- `when` keeps its expression language — it is evaluated, never executed.\n- State a redaction rule for `post`, which currently ships the **whole\n  `Envelope`** to an arbitrary URL while §8.2 redacts secrets on `/api/config`.\n  Either the same redaction applies, or §7.1 says plainly that a `post` hook sees\n  everything and should only target a trusted endpoint.\n\n#### 2. §3.3 / §4.4 — `AllowPattern` is unbounded, and §8.4 hands it to extensions\n\n**Current.** §3.3 has `ResolvePermission { decision: AllowPattern(String) }`;\n§4.4 puts session grants at the **top** of the resolution order, above agent,\nproject and user config; §8.4 says extensions may answer permissions *\"regardless\nof control\"*, deliberately, *\"because that is their purpose\"*.\n\nCompose those three and a connected bridge answering one prompt can grant `**`\nfor the session. Any extension is therefore root-equivalent, which is not what\n§8.3's \"no users, no roles\" model intends — it intends *one trusted operator*, and\na Telegram chat is a weaker authentication context than the local terminal.\n\n**Proposed, two separable edits.**\n- **Bound the pattern.** `AllowPattern` must be a **subset of the scope actually\n  requested**. The request is already in `PermissionRequested { scope }`, so this\n  is mechanically checkable at resolution time; a pattern outside it is an\n  `Ack Err`. This keeps the feature — \"allow all `cargo *` for this session\" —\n  and removes the escalation.\n- **Split the capability.** §7.2's `Hello { capabilities }` gains separate flags\n  for *answer questions* and *resolve permissions*. Answering a question is\n  supplying data the model asked for; resolving a permission is authorising an\n  effect. Bridging the first does not imply bridging the second, and the default\n  for a chat bridge should be questions only.\n\n#### 3. §5.3 — sub-agent inheritance caps the mode, not the scopes\n\n**Current.** *\"its own permission state (child cannot exceed parent's mode)\"* —\nand `PermMode` is only `Plan | Ask | AutoEdit | Yolo`. The allow/deny **lists**\nare per-agent config, so a child whose own agent file carries\n`edit.allow = [\"**\"]`, spawned from an `auto-edit` parent scoped to `src/**`, is\nconstrained by neither.\n\n**Proposed.** State the rule as an **intersection**: a child's effective\npermissions are the intersection of its own resolved scopes with the parent's,\nand its mode is `min(child, parent)`. One sentence, and it makes §5.3's existing\npromise true rather than partial.\n\n#### 4. §8.3 / §8.2 — the browser token travels in a URL, on the origin that renders model output\n\n**Current.** *\"Accepted as `Authorization: Bearer` or, for browsers, `?token=` on\nfirst visit which sets an HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict cookie.\"* The cookie handling\nis right; the delivery is not.\n\nA query token lands in browser history, in every proxy and server log, and in the\n`Referer` of **any outbound link the page renders** — and §9.2 renders\nmodel-authored markdown, so a transcript containing an external link is one click\nfrom leaking it. Separately, §8.2's `/api/sessions/:id/file` returns arbitrary\nrepository content **on the authenticated application's own origin**, which is a\nstored-XSS delivery path.\n\n**Proposed.**\n- `Referrer-Policy: no-referrer` on the app, and `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"` on\n  every rendered link.\n- Strip `?token=` from the URL once the cookie is set.\n- Serve `/api/sessions/:id/file` and `/blob/:blob_id` as `text/plain` with\n  `Content-Disposition: attachment`, under a CSP that forbids inline script.\n- §8.3 keeps `Bearer` as the primary path and documents `?token=` as the\n  bootstrap-only convenience it is.\n\n#### Not in A22, on purpose\n\n**§4.4's `bash` allow-list is advisory for exactly the reason B7 gives for\n`bash:readonly`** — a shell command is a program, and a glob over its text cannot\ndecide what it does (`git diff; git push`, `cargo run` invoking anything,\n`$(...)`). That is not a correction to write here, because **B7 already owns the\nadvisory-versus-enforced wording and is unsigned**; A22 would be pre-empting it.\nB7's dossier has been widened to cover §4.4 instead, so one decision answers the\nwhole question rather than two artefacts answering halves of it.\n\n**Blast radius.** No `Event` variant and no fold change, so **no new golden\nfixture**. Item 2 changes a `Command`'s *validation*, not its shape. Item 1\nchanges a config schema that no code reads yet — which is the argument for landing\nit now: every one of these is free before the thing exists and awkward afterwards.\nItem 4 is server configuration only. ✅ **All four held, and the blast radius was\nunderstated in one direction only**: ten sections were touched rather than the six\nthis entry names, because item 4's `rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"` is a *renderer* rule\nbelonging in §9.2 (so it covers any surface rendering model output, not just one\nview), item 2's bound needed §3.3's `PermDecision` comment as well as §4.4, and\nevery item needs §14 tests. No new section, no new type, no wire shape changed.\n\n**Watch the link convention.** The text above is *plan* text and cites TODO and\nB7 by link. **Strip every link when transcribing** — `grep -c '](' docs/dacode-design.md`\nmust return 0. ✅ **Honoured** — returns 0, and the §-reference check that A21's pass\nadded reports 307 resolving.\n\n**Recommend accept.** If any single item is contentious it is item 2's capability\nsplit, because §8.4 grants that power deliberately and the user may consider the\nthreat model out of scope for a single-operator tool. Say so explicitly if that is\nthe call — *\"a bridge is as trusted as the terminal\"* is a defensible position, and\nwriting it down is much better than leaving it implied by a composition of three\nsections that were each written separately. **Done 2026-09-18, and the user did make\nthat call explicitly**: the position is defensible and is now written down, with the\ncapability split reserved rather than applied — but the `AllowPattern` bound was\n**redirected back in**, on the argument that \"permissive\" describes the user's\nexperience and the bound does not touch it. **The `Current.`/`Proposed.` text below is\nhistorical**; the banner is the record of what landed and why.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-18","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§14","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.3","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.4","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§5.3","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§7.1","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§7.2","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§8.2","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§8.3","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§8.4","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§9.2","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A22","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-18T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U208","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"identifier privacy","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-16**, on [B19](B-decisions.md)'s sign-off. All seven\n> proposed edits landed and the rule is normative across six sections: §4.3\n> carries the three-surfaces argument, the injection, the opaque handle and the\n> ULID-only scan; §4.4 places the scan; §5.1 reserves the prefix; §7.1 redacts a\n> `post` body; §8.2 names the redaction points; §12 gains `sessions/<id>/tmp` and\n> `scratch/<token>`. No `Event` variant, so **no golden fixture**, and it landed\n> independently of A1 exactly as predicted. The spec gained no markdown link and\n> no B- or U-number citation.\n>\n> **One correction and five scoping decisions taken while transcribing — recorded so\n> a reviewer can check them rather than infer them. The correction changed what the\n> spec asserts; scoping decisions 1 and 2 changed behaviour; the last three are\n> wording an implementer would otherwise have guessed.**\n>\n> 0. **⚠️ CORRECTION, caught on self-review after a first draft had already landed:\n>    the redaction half is about *secrets*, not identifiers, and the draft said\n>    both.** Item 5 proposed \"redaction on the four projection endpoints\" without\n>    naming what is redacted, and the first transcription read that as covering\n>    §4.3's identifier set as well. It cannot, for two reasons. `/export` and\n>    `/events` serve **envelopes**, whose structural ids — `session`, `seq`, `call` —\n>    are how the fold correlates events (§3.5), so redacting them makes `/export`\n>    produce a file nobody can replay and `/events` a page nobody can render. And it\n>    contradicts [B19](B-decisions.md) itself, which says of the identifier control\n>    *\"it is not a redaction layer\"* and rests on prevention precisely because\n>    neither the log nor the cached prefix may be rewritten. **All three sites now\n>    say secrets explicitly and state why identifiers are excluded**: §4.3's closing\n>    paragraph, §7.1 and §8.2. Worth recording as a class: a signed decision does not\n>    protect against a mis-transcription of it, and nothing but a re-read against the\n>    decision's own text catches one.\n> 1. **§4.4's automatic-decision path had to be written, and the `yolo` carve-out\n>    in it is the one to check.** The proposal said only that the scan runs \"after\n>    the permission decision but before exec\" and that a detection is surfaced\n>    \"inside the permission prompt\" — which is silent on the case where the\n>    decision was *automatic* and there is no prompt. Leaving it silent would have\n>    made the scan advisory on exactly the paths where nothing else stops a call,\n>    which is the defect B7 was widened to name. So §4.4 now says: record the\n>    detection on `PermissionResolved` and **escalate to `ask`**. `yolo` records\n>    without escalating, justified in spec-internal terms rather than by citing an\n>    unsigned decision. ⚠️ **B7 owns the mode's polarity and is unsigned** — when\n>    it lands it must confirm or overturn that carve-out. This is the same shape\n>    of debt A19 created for A3/A4.\n> 2. **§5.1's \"rejected rather than ignored\" was scoped to authoring surfaces, or\n>    it breaks nested `dacode`.** Taken literally — rejected on *presence* in the\n>    environment — the rule would break the case the reservation exists to\n>    protect, because the engine injects `DACODE_RT_*` into every tool process and\n>    a nested `dacode` launched from a tool call inherits them. That nested case\n>    is not hypothetical: it is what a [B20](B-decisions.md) swarm member *is*.\n>    So the spec makes a key **defined** in the reserved namespace a load-time\n>    error, while mere presence in the environment is expected and not an error —\n>    stated explicitly so an implementer does not \"fix\" it into a startup failure.\n> 3. **Item 7 said the scratch directory is \"gitignored\"; it is not, because it is\n>    not in a repository.** [B19](B-decisions.md)'s own worked example puts it at\n>    `~/.local/share/dacode/sessions/<id>/tmp`, which is outside any repo, so\n>    there is nothing to gitignore. §12 says so rather than carrying an\n>    inapplicable instruction — the same reason A17's application refused to\n>    enumerate avoidable consequences in the normative doc.\n> 4. **The alias mechanism is deliberately left open, with the property stated\n>    instead.** B19 offered \"a symlink, a bind, or a directory named by a derived\n>    token\". A symlink *resolves* to the id-bearing path, so had the property been\n>    written as \"no resolution may reveal the id\" a symlink would fail it and the\n>    offer would be self-contradictory. §12 therefore makes the property about the\n>    **exported path string**, with the reasoning recorded (an argv is free for\n>    any local user to read; resolving a symlink is neither free nor accidental).\n>    Naming a mechanism would have put an M1 implementation choice into the spec.\n> 5. **`run` hooks are explicitly *not* redacted.** Item 4 said only \"hook payload\n>    redaction\", but §7.1 has two hook kinds and only `post` crosses a network.\n>    Saying which is redacted and why prevents \"payload\" being read as both —\n>    redacting a local `run` hook would break the legitimate case of a user's own\n>    script needing the real id.\n>\n> **Still open, unchanged:** how wide the injected set goes. It is written as a\n> **config list starting at three** (`DACODE_RT_SESSION`, `DACODE_RT_CALL`,\n> `DACODE_RT_TMP`), so widening is a configuration change and not a spec edit —\n> which is what discharges the low-confidence part without settling it.\n> [B18](B-decisions.md)'s T2 prompt-overhead number is what argues the rest.\n> ~~**A14 will add `ProjectId`** to the scanned set when it lands, as predicted: one\n> line, not a dependency.~~ ⚠️ **This prediction did not fire, and A14's banner\n> records why (2026-09-18).** A14 added `ProjectId` and `FolderId` to §3.1 and\n> **excluded both from the scan**, in the clause shape `HostId` got. The prediction\n> assumed a new ULID-backed id is a new scan target; the scan's actual subject is\n> narrower — ids the engine **allocated and injected**, where the risk is a tool\n> echoing one into an argv. Nothing injects a project id (`DACODE_RT_` starts at\n> three) and no v1 tool argument can name one, so the check could never fire and\n> adding it would have made the enumeration look complete when it is not. §4.3 now\n> states the exclusion with its condition, so it widens by the same one line if a\n> tool ever can name a project.\n\n**Status.** **B19 signed off 2026-09-16** (\"if you recommend (2)+(4) — then is\nfine\"), so this delta is a transcription of a decision already made, not a proposal.\nIt is written as a delta rather than edited straight into the spec because the spec\nmoves only through one (B4's rule), and because six sections is too many to change\nwithout a recorded blast radius.\n\n**Current.** The spec has exactly one redaction concept — §8.2's `GET /api/config`\nreturning \"secrets redacted\" — and no general one. §4.3 defines the tool trait and its\n`args`; §4.4 the permission resolution order; §5.1 four config layers, the fourth\nbeing \"environment (`DACODE_*`) and CLI flags\"; §7.1 hooks, where `post` ships the\nwhole `Envelope` to an arbitrary URL; §8.2 the HTTP surface including four endpoints\nthat serve *copies* of the log; §12 the on-disk layout, whose session directory is\n`~/.local/share/dacode/sessions/<session_id>/`.\n\n**Proposed.**\n\n1. **§4.3 — runtime injection.** The engine sets the identifiers a tool needs as\n   environment variables in the tool's own process, under a **reserved prefix that is\n   not `DACODE_`** (that namespace is §5.1's config-override layer, so sharing it\n   would make a model-authored `export` a config-write surface). Minimum set: one\n   variable per id class, plus a scratch directory. Tool descriptions and the\n   built-in agents' prompts reference the variables rather than literals.\n2. **§4.3 — the scratch directory is an opaque handle.** §12's session directory\n   *embeds the session id*, so exporting it would put the id straight back into argv\n   — the surface being protected. Export a path whose name is a derived token that is\n   not the id; keep the id-bearing path engine-internal.\n3. **§4.3/§4.4 — an exact `args` check before execution**, restricted to the **four\n   ULID-backed ids** of §3.1 (`SessionId`, `ClientId`, `CallId`, `ReqId`).\n   `TurnId(u32)` and `Cursor(u64)` are excluded on purpose: substring-matching small\n   integers fires on ordinary numbers. Matching a known finite set is exact, not\n   heuristic — which is the difference between this and B7's deny-list. Where it sits\n   in §4.4's resolution order is part of the delta.\n4. **§7.1 — hook payload redaction.** `post` currently ships the whole `Envelope` with\n   no rule at all while §8.2 redacts secrets. This closes U61's second half.\n5. **§8.2 — redaction on the four projection endpoints** (`/export`, `/events`,\n   `/blob/:blob_id`, `/diff/:call`). These serve *copies*, so redacting them rewrites\n   nothing — the distinction that makes prevention necessary for the log and the\n   transcript but not for these.\n6. **§5.1 — the reserved prefix is excluded from the `DACODE_*` layer**, stated where\n   the layer is defined rather than only where it is used.\n7. **§12 — the scratch directory** appears in the layout, gitignored, with its\n   opaque-name rule.\n\n**What this delta does not do.** No redaction of the log or of the provider\ntranscript: the log is append-only (§3.6, principle 2) and the cached prefix must stay\nbyte-identical (§13.4), so for those two surfaces prevention is the only control that\nexists. **No new `Event` variant, so no new golden fixture** — which is also why it\ncan land independently of A1.\n\n**Depends on.** Nothing. B19 is signed; A1 is not required (no wire-shape change);\n~~A14 would add `ProjectId` to the checked set when it lands, which is a one-line\naddition rather than a dependency~~ ⚠️ **superseded 2026-09-18**: A14 landed and\n**excluded** `ProjectId` (and `FolderId`) from the scan instead, because nothing\ninjects either and so the check could never fire — see the annotation in this\nentry's banner and §4.3's exclusion clause. It was never a dependency in either\ndirection, which is the part that mattered for sequencing.\n\n**Left open by the sign-off, with the variant recommended for it.** How wide the\ninjected set goes — B19's *low*-confidence part. **Recommend exactly three**, and make\nthe set a config list rather than a hardcoded enum so widening is a config change:\n`DACODE_RT_SESSION`, `DACODE_RT_CALL`, `DACODE_RT_TMP`. Those are the three a tool\ncommand plausibly needs; `ClientId` and `ReqId` are engine-internal, so injecting them\nbuys nothing and costs prompt surface that B18's T2 number measures. Three reliability\ndetails: the reserved prefix must be **rejected** by §5.1's config loader rather than\nignored, so a mistyped key fails loudly instead of silently doing nothing; the\nvariables go into the **tool process environment only**, never the environment block\nthe model can see, or the ids are straight back in the prompt; and the `args` check\nruns **after** the permission decision but **before** exec, so a denied call does not\npay for the scan and a detected literal can be surfaced *inside* the permission prompt\n(\"this command contains the session id\") rather than as a separate failure mode.\n\n**Recommend accept as a transcription.** ✅ **Done 2026-09-16** — the seven edits are\napplied, and U61 and U65 in [TODO](../TODO.md) §6 are widened to cite them rather than\nrestating the argument a third time. **Still owed:** deepen\n[16](../part3-capabilities/16-permissions.md) when M1 reaches it. Doc 16 is M1 and still\nat summary depth, so hard rule 1 holds it — and it now has more to carry than it did\nwhen B19 was drafted, because §4.4's escalation-on-detection behaviour is permission-\nengine work that no plan doc describes yet.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-16","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§12","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.3","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.4","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§5.1","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§7.1","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§8.2","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A23","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-16T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-16T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U209","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"session-log concurrency and the index sidecar","body":"\n> **✅ APPLIED 2026-09-17**, on [B21](B-decisions.md)'s sign-off, the same day the\n> delta was written. All six proposed edits landed: §3.6 carries the single-writer\n> invariant with the lock's three reliability properties and the kernel-ownership\n> argument; §4.1 makes the registry the lock holder; §8.1 names `attach` as the\n> remedy; §12 gains `lock`, `index.bin` and the locality rule; §14 gains the two\n> tests. [Doc 05](../part2-engine/05-event-log-storage.md) gained **§5.5** with the\n> implementation obligations. **No `Event` or `Command` variant, so no golden\n> fixture.** The spec gained no markdown link and no B- or U-number citation.\n>\n> **Five scoping decisions taken while transcribing — recorded so a reviewer can\n> check them rather than infer them. The first is the one that changed the delta's\n> scope; the rest are placement.**\n>\n> 1. **U67 was folded in, and the delta as drafted did not mention it.** B21's\n>    on-sign-off list said §3.6 gets \"the single-writer invariant and the lock file\"\n>    and nothing about the cursor. But §3.6 also said `Cursor = line number + 1`, and\n>    an index of `cursor → byte offset` is **only worth having if the cursor is not\n>    already the line number** — so applying the delta as scoped would have left the\n>    spec asserting an equivalence its own new index contradicts. [U67](../TODO.md)\n>    had independently identified the same sentence as a wrong invariant a store\n>    implementer would inherit, and owed it to M1. **Both are discharged here.** This\n>    is P-LIVE-005's lesson applied rather than repeated: re-read the edit against\n>    what else in the same section it invalidates, not only against the decision that\n>    authorized it.\n> 2. **The index's three invariants went to doc 05, not the spec.** B21 asked for\n>    both (§12 for the files, doc 05 for the deepening) without drawing the line. The\n>    split taken: §12 carries the two files and the **rebuildable-projection\n>    asymmetry** — what a reader of the layout needs in order to know which artifact\n>    is the truth. Doc 05 §5.5 carries the same-code-path, log→fsync→index write\n>    order, and validate-and-truncate-on-open invariants, plus the crate choice, the\n>    refusal message and the NFS warning — what an implementer needs. That is A17's\n>    precedent (refuse to enumerate consequences in the normative doc) applied to a\n>    section that already had a doc to hold them.\n> 3. **`fd-lock` is named in doc 05 and deliberately *not* in the spec.** §3.6 says\n>    \"advisory lock (`flock` on POSIX, `LockFileEx` on Windows)\" — **syscalls**, which\n>    are the semantics being relied on and will not change. The crate is an M1\n>    dependency decision, and naming it in the spec would pre-empt the ask-first rule\n>    for external crates. Doc 05 names it with its §2.3 class-2 justification, which\n>    is where a dependency choice belongs. The spec does name `reqwest`/`hyper` and\n>    `schemars`, so this is a judgement about *this* crate's status, not a convention.\n> 4. **The locality rule went into §12 rather than a new section.** Sub-answer 2 is\n>    about where the session directory may live, and §12 is the on-disk layout, so it\n>    sits next to the paths it constrains — stated as a rule plus a startup warning,\n>    not as a restructure.\n> 5. **§14's two tests are specified as needing a second *process*,** with the second\n>    named as the only thing that can fail on the kernel-ownership claim. That is the\n>    rhetorical move §14 already uses for §4.8's bounded gate (\"the only thing that\n>    can fail on the claim that the gate is *bounded*\"), so it matches the section's\n>    existing style rather than inventing a new one.\n>\n> **Still open, unchanged:** the medium confidence on the offset sidecar lasting\n> through M1, now carrying its exit route — **the index grows a second table rather\n> than the log changing format.** And **[B20](B-decisions.md) must re-read this\n> entry**: B21 is B20's constraint (d) at local scale, and a swarm whose members each\n> held their own lock would satisfy B21 and violate B20.\n>\n> **One gap the application surfaced and deliberately did not fill — [U93](../TODO.md).**\n> §3.6 now guarantees a refusal is fast, named and carries a remedy, but §11 documents\n> exit codes *only* for `run`, and B21's sub-answer 5 says the commands that hit the\n> lock are `session fork` and resume — so a refusal falls through to a generic `1`,\n> indistinguishable from a crash. That matters for the same reason `run` already has\n> **3** for \"blocked on permission\": a held session is a *state a caller must branch\n> on*, not a failure. Assigning a number was not this delta's to invent, and the\n> honest fix extends §11's table to the other subcommands in one edit rather than\n> bolting a code onto a section that documents one command. Owed by M1 and doc 23.\n\n**Status.** **B21 signed off 2026-09-17**, all five sub-answers taken as\nrecommended, so this delta is a transcription of a decision already made rather than\na proposal. It is written as a delta because the spec moves only through one (B4's\nrule), and because five sections is too many to change without a recorded blast\nradius.\n\n**Current.** The spec says nothing at all about concurrent access to a session —\nverified by grep rather than recall: `flock`, lock file, single-writer and \"two\nprocesses\" appear nowhere in it. §3.6 gives an append-only JSONL log fsync'd at\nthree boundaries and states `Cursor = line number + 1`; §4.1 gives one\n`SessionRegistry` **per process**; §8.1 lists four process models, three of which can\nbe running at once. So when a TUI and a `run -p` open the same session directory,\ntoday's answer is *both append*.\n\n**Why that is worse than it sounds, and the reason this delta exists.** It is not a\ntorn-line problem — a single `write()` of one line is atomic on a local filesystem,\nso the JSONL stays parseable, and that is exactly what makes the failure quiet. What\nbreaks is `seq`: §3.7 says the engine assigns it on append and that it is strictly\nincreasing, so two writers each produce a valid sequence whose union is neither.\n§3.7's gap detection then reports phantom discontinuities, clients resync forever,\nand `Cursor = line number + 1` makes the two writers' cursors *collide* on the same\nline. A log corrupt in its **ordering** rather than its bytes still parses, which is\nwhy the invariant has to be stated rather than assumed.\n\n**Proposed.**\n\n1. **§3.6 — single-writer as an invariant, enforced by an advisory lock.** Exactly\n   one process appends to a session's log: the holder of `flock`/`LockFileEx` on a\n   dedicated `<session>/lock`. Three details carry the reliability, and all three are\n   from B21's sub-answer 1: held **for the whole session, not per append** (per-append\n   locking admits a second writer *between* appends, which is precisely the `seq`\n   collision); acquired with a **non-blocking try**, never a blocking lock (a second\n   engine should fail in milliseconds with a remedy, not hang behind a session that\n   may run for hours); and on a **dedicated lock file, not `events.jsonl`** (locking\n   the data file conflates \"I own this session\" with \"I have this file open\", and\n   breaks the moment anything opens the log read-only to export it). The decisive\n   property is that **the kernel owns the lock** and releases it when the process\n   dies, including on `SIGKILL` and on crash — so there is no stale-lock recovery code\n   to write and no `--force-unlock` flag to grow. The pid and start time written\n   inside the lock file are **informational only**: correctness rests on the `flock`,\n   never on the contents, which is the mistake PID-file locking makes.\n2. **§3.6 — the cursor is assigned by the store, not defined as a line number.** This\n   discharges **U67**, which is coupled rather than merely adjacent: an index of\n   `cursor → byte offset` is only worth having if the cursor is not *already* the line\n   number, and B11 plans a SQLite store by M3 where the equivalence is simply false.\n   Restated as: the cursor is assigned by the store and is strictly increasing; for\n   the JSONL store it happens to equal line + 1. One sentence, and it stops a store\n   implementer inheriting a wrong invariant. §3.7's fold is over `seq` and was never\n   over a line count, so nothing downstream changes.\n3. **§4.1 — the registry is the lock holder.** Acquiring the lock is part of opening\n   a session and releasing it is part of closing one, so the invariant has exactly one\n   owner per process and one process per session. A second opener is an **error, not a\n   second writer**.\n4. **§8.1 — `attach` is the remedy.** Principle 1 is what makes single-writer cheap:\n   the protocol exists so that a second consumer never needs write access. Naming\n   `attach` in the process model is what turns a refusal into a usable answer.\n5. **§12 — `lock` and `index.bin` beside `events.jsonl`, plus the locality rule.** The\n   index is a **rebuildable projection, never authoritative**: fixed-width\n   `(u64 seq, u64 offset)` records, append-only like the log, so a torn tail truncates\n   to a valid prefix by arithmetic alone and a corrupt or stale index is recovered by\n   one scan of the log — while a corrupt log is not recoverable at all. That is the\n   rule A14/B13's Fix-1 already states for organization data, extended. §12 also gains\n   the **locality rule**: the working tree may be remote, the log may not, because\n   advisory lock semantics are version-dependent over NFS and the failure is silent.\n6. **§14 — the two tests that make the invariant falsifiable.** A second process\n   opening a held session fails cleanly, names the holder and offers `attach`, and\n   never appends; and a holder killed with `SIGKILL` leaves no unusable lock, so the\n   next opener acquires it. Neither is reachable from a single-process unit test, so\n   both spawn a second process.\n\n**The index's three invariants live in doc 05, not in the spec.** Reconstruction and\ninitial construction are the **same code path** (scan the log, emit records), so there\nis nothing to drift; write order is **log line → fsync → index record**, never the\nreverse, because an index entry for a line that did not land is a hole while a missing\nentry for a line that did is merely a rebuild; and **on open, validate the last record\nagainst the log and truncate to the last good one** — the WAL-recovery pattern, and\nthe reason \"rebuildable\" is load-bearing rather than decorative. Those are\nimplementation obligations, and doc 05 is already expanded, so it is where an\nimplementer will look.\n\n**What this delta does not do.** It does not make the log anything but JSONL: the log\nstays plain, grep-able, append-only and the truth, and the index stays a sidecar. It\ndoes not add SQLite — that is B11's M3 extraction, and Appendix F already records the\n`libsqlite3-sys` trade. It does not add lock stealing or handover, which is a\nconsensus problem wearing a UX costume. **No `Event` variant and no `Command`\nvariant**, so **no golden fixture** — the lock is below the protocol, which is the\npoint of it.\n\n**Depends on.** Nothing. B21 is signed; A1 is not required, since the tier marker does\nnot touch storage concurrency. **It must be written against the current §12**, which\nA23 amended on 2026-09-16 to add `sessions/<id>/tmp` and `scratch/<token>` — the\nsequencing the board predicted when it landed A23 ahead of B21.\n\n**Coupling recorded, not resolved.** This is **B20's constraint (d) at local scale**:\n\"one `seq` assigner per session log\" was written there as a distributed-systems\ncommitment, and a file lock is the same rule enforced on one host. Recording them as\none rule is the point — **a swarm whose members each held their own lock would satisfy\nB21 and violate B20.** B21's sub-answer 2 dissolves the NFS collision with B20's Q1\nrather than solving it: Q1 was about where a remote member gets the *repository*, and\nnothing requires the *session directory* to be remote.\n\n**Left open, as B21 left it.** Confidence is **medium** on the offset sidecar being\nsufficient through M1. It is, until the session-list filters land; if `dacode session\nlist` needs more, **the index grows a second table rather than the log changing\nformat** — which is the property that makes the medium confidence safe to sign.\n\n**Recommend accept as a transcription**, and apply it before M1 writes its first\n`EventStore`. ✅ **Done 2026-09-17** — accepted, applied, and doc 05 deepened in the\nsame pass, so the invariant is normative before any storage code exists rather than\nretrofitted into it. That timing was the point: §3.6's single-writer rule is the kind\nof constraint that is free to state and expensive to add once two writers have shipped.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-17","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§12","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§14","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.6","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.1","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§8.1","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A24","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U210","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"remote-execution constraints","body":"\n> **✅ SIGNED OFF 2026-09-17 with [B20](B-decisions.md) — approved for application; NOT\n> yet transcribed into the spec.** This entry did not exist before B20's session: it is\n> the transcription B20 owes, and B20's own \"on session\" list named the sections. It is\n> **split by milestone**, because half of it is wire format and half is not, and the two\n> halves have opposite urgency.\n\n**M1 half — wire format, and it rides with A1.** ✅ **APPLIED 2026-09-18, inside A1's\ntranscription** — see A1's banner for the scoping decisions, two of which are this\nhalf's: (a) took a **third answer** beyond the two options listed below, and (e) put\n**only the epoch** on the wire. Free before golden fixtures exist, a rewrite after:\n\n- **(a) A `SessionId` that can name a session on another host** — a location component or\n  an opaque handle. §3.1's ids are ULID-backed and A23's scan depends on that, so\n  whatever is chosen must keep the four ids exact-matchable.\n- **(e) A fencing token on the session writer.** Not \"one `seq` assigner\" but **one\n  committer, fenced by an epoch, deduped by an idempotency key, with a CAS on the leaf** —\n  what openclaw's transcript commit does (`worker-rpc-transcript-client.ts:31-56`,\n  `transcript-commit.ts:44,188-216`) and what opencode states in prose\n  (`sync/README.md:31-33`). **B21's kernel-owned `flock` cannot do this remotely**: there\n  is no shared filesystem to lock, and B20's Q1 permits a network FS where `flock`\n  semantics are NFS-version-dependent. B21's single-writer invariant is *restated*, not\n  weakened — the lock stays the local mechanism, and the epoch is what a partitioned\n  remote writer cannot forge.\n\n**M5 half — behaviour, none of it wire format.**\n\n- **§5.3** — a child session's `cwd`/sandbox inheritance is meaningless across hosts, so\n  the child gains a **location**; and the model endpoint becomes a **host/session\n  attribute**. That attribute is B20's Q5 = **option A**: the member runs\n  `dacode-providers` and points its base URL at a controller-side **credential broker**\n  (A10/doc 25's proxy) which injects the key and **forwards verbatim**. The\n  attribute form is what keeps option B (controller-executed inference) reachable later\n  with no wire change, and *forward verbatim* is non-negotiable either way — a proxy that\n  re-serialises destroys the cache prefix it exists to measure (§13.4).\n- **§5.4** — a workflow is a **DAG**: a step gains `host` (absent = local), `needs` and\n  `parallel`; **the swarm shares one §4.8 task tree**, because a gate per host is not a\n  gate; remote fan-out gets **its own cap**, separate from `max_parallel_subagents`; and\n  `task` may also name a host for ad-hoc delegation. A step that opts into\n  **retry-on-host-loss must declare its effect class**, honoured only when the step is\n  idempotent or its `verify:` can detect a double run — the default is *block and ask*,\n  because no surveyed harness has idempotence keys.\n- **§8.4** — capabilities for a **remote principal**, and this is the part that must be\n  argued rather than inherited: **A22's item 2 was redirected to permissive for v1**, so\n  the split no longer arrives from there. The minimum set the survey supports is\n  *observe / prompt / execute / spawn / grant-permissions*, with **spawn and\n  grant-permissions never held by a remote principal** — openclaw gateway-proxies both\n  (`src/worker/tool-authority.ts:1-28`). Local permissiveness is untouched; this is about\n  a process on another machine.\n- **§4.8** — `Verify::Command` evidence records the **executing host**, without which a\n  green gate is unattributable and Q6's block-and-ask cannot tell the human where to look.\n- **§4.7** — sandbox backends become **declared host capabilities negotiated at\n  admission** (`worker-admission.ts:47-69` is the precedent; openclaw's\n  `backend-handle.types.ts:4` is B7's reserved seam already built), jointly with **B7**. A\n  host that cannot declare one **refuses members** — and since B7 ships the seam as an\n  **inert stub**, nothing can declare one until it is filled (**U98**).\n\n**Not in this delta.** The build itself (M5, beside doc 20). Forge's *remote services,\nlocal effects* form — declined, because A10 covers the only remote service dacode plans\nand **hosted semantic indexing is an explicit non-goal**. And anything that would let a\nmember see less of the conversation: the survey is blunt that neither brokering form\nhides the transcript from the member, so an untrusted host is a **containment** question,\nnot an inference-placement one.\n\n**Depends on.** ~~B20~~ ✅ resolved. ~~The **M1 half must land with A1**~~ ✅ **it did,\n2026-09-18**; the M5 half needs\n**A3** (synthetic tool results plus an *aborted / effects unknown* outcome in §3.2,\nwithout which Q6's default cannot be expressed) and **A4** (`Usage` cache fields, so a\nmember's spend rolls up correctly rather than pricing cached reads at full rate — U71).\n\n**Watch the link convention.** The text above is *plan* text and cites B20 by link.\n**Strip every link when transcribing** — `tools/check-links.py --conventions-only` is the\nauthority, not a raw grep.\n\n**Recommend accept** — signed with B20 rather than separately, because the two are one\ndecision: B20 chose the shape, A25 writes it down.\n\n---\n\n## Deltas applied\n\nApplied entries stay in place above, struck through in the index with a\nresolution banner in the detail section — nothing is deleted.\n\n| # | Section | Applied | Date | Note |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ~~**A28**~~ | §3.2, §3.3, §4.3, §4.4 | **A denial carries a typed reason, and §4.4's pattern language is corrected** — `DenyReason` with five arms, `Deny { reason: Option<DenyReason> }`, the three pattern *domains* stated, `.env` → `**/.env`, and a `deny` made a floor no grant or mode overrides | 2026-10-01 | The register's third entry to arrive already applied, and its **first with a golden diff**: one string moved and the pre-change bytes no longer decode. Landed before M1.3 publishes, which is [U162](../TODO.md)'s deadline rather than M1.7's convenience |\n| ~~**A27**~~ | §3.2, §3.5, §4.5, §8.2, §14.1 | **Four durable events with no fold destination, given one** | 2026-09-20 | The register's **second** entry to arrive already applied, found by **M1.2 Pass C1's fold walk** — the first time `fold` was actually written, which ran A1's tier rule as a diagnostic: *every `Durable` variant must have a fold destination, or the rule's own \"iff\" is false*. Four did not, and each fix is the minimum that makes an existing spec claim true rather than a new mechanism: **`TurnBlock::User { content, from }`** appended per `UserMessage` — without it a G4-bootstrap or replay client renders every transcript with no user side, and §9.2's \"reconciled by `UserMessage{from}`\" line presupposed the destination; **`SettingsChanged { model?, agent?, perm_mode? }`** (Event 30 → 31, `Durable`) plus `SessionCreated`'s two new fields — §3.3's `Set*` commands promise \"resulting events\" and §3.5 says `SessionInfo` is \"all derived from the log\", and both were false for `model`/`perm_mode`; **`SessionInfo.closed: Option<CloseReason>`**, first close wins — §8.2's list annotation has always said \"(**status**, …)\" and U109's rationale (\"the session list *says* a session is over\") presupposed it; **`Compacted` folds `context.tokens_used = after`**, with §4.5's divider made explicitly a live-rendering affordance. Plus **two fold rules stated where a second implementer (M1.3's TS client) meets them**: openness is a convention, not a flag — the open block is the turn's trailing same-kind block, exact under §6's flat stream (tracker f124); and \"no open block\" splits by turn existence — unknown turn ignored (totality), started turn *creates* the block (the half log-only replay depends on — tracker f126, correcting G5's own §14.1 transcription). **Three verified non-gaps** recorded rather than fixed: `SubAgentFinished` (summary flows via the parent's `ToolResult`, §5.3), `TaskUpdated{Promote}` (repo-side write; content is M1.10's), `Checkpoint` (**U112**, filed with M2/doc 12 as owner — no route, no `Snapshot` field, and a `Rollback` UI needs the ids from somewhere). Approved in-session on the recommendations; **again pre-fixture**, so the wire change costs zero golden diff — A26's sequencing rule earning its keep a second time |\n| ~~**A26**~~ | §3.2, §3.5, §5.3, §14.1 | **A turn is an ordered block list, and a child session closes with `Completed`** | 2026-09-19 | The register's first entry to arrive **already applied**: it came out of M1.1a's review of the three protocol types `bc52e50` *designed* rather than transcribed, which the previous session's handover flagged as needing a second pair of eyes before M1.2 fixtures them — and **two of the three changed**, which is the gate earning its keep, since no structural check can tell whether a variant *set* is the right one. `TurnView.text: String` could not hold a turn with more than one text block while §6's `StreamChunk` is a flat sequence, and §3.2's replace-at-boundary rule then destroyed the first block — **a loss in durable state, not a mis-render**, because deltas are `Live` and never persisted. It is now `blocks: Vec<TurnBlock>` with the rule reworded to *closes the open block*, which needs **no block index on the events**: arrival order *is* block order, the boundary is authoritative, and a dropped close is a §3.7 `seq` gap rather than a silent merge. `CloseReason` gained `Completed` because a sub-agent whose task finished matched none of its four producers, and §5.3 — which never said whether a child session closes at all — now says so, carrying the **child-specific** warning that makes the variant safe (a primary session goes `AgentIdle(Done)` and stays open, and §11's exit codes map from `IdleReason`, so a leak would be visible only to a user reading a session list). **Neither change cost a fixture**: no `Event` variant changed shape, and `Snapshot` is §8.2's payload rather than a log record. Two §14.1 tests added, the first invisible to the two pre-existing two-tier tests because each uses a single-block turn. **The sequencing rule that falls out** is now in doc 03 §3.7: an `Event`/`Command` variant is fixture-pinned from M1.2, a folded type route-pinned until M1.3 — so review the first kind earlier |\n| ~~**A7**~~ | §8.2, §8.3, §8.4, §8.5, §3.6, §3.7, §5.1, §9.2, §12, §14 | **Config writes, the scaling axis, and one rule for every non-session write** | 2026-09-19 | **Ten sections against the two this entry named**, because caveat 1 needed §8.5 *and* §8.2's write rule, caveat 2 needed §5.1 (where §12 already cited it and no section carried it), the post-A19 amendment needed §8.4 and §9.2, and **U99**'s freshness channel needed a wire frame (§3.6) plus a field (§12). **§8.2 grew from 16 routes to 23** and now carries the scaling axis, which is what makes §2.2's and §3.7's existing citations of \"§8.2's scaling axis\" resolve to *content* rather than to a heading — a resolvable reference with false content is the `§14.2` hazard class one level subtler, and `check_section_refs()` cannot see it. Both trait families are normative with `BlobStore` **reconciled in as the third** (§2.1 named three, this entry named two), `JsonlStore`+`SharedToken` the only M1 implementations, and **`Authenticator`/`Tenant` placed in `dacode-server` with no new crate** on §2.1's own test (no engine rule depends on identity). ⚠️ **Its stated gate was NOT transcribed**: \"gated on controller status (§8.4)\" is incoherent, since §5.1's four layers include no session-scoped one and the controller rule reaches only commands that mutate a session — so **A14's pattern was applied instead** and §8.4 now states one rule for the registry, the config writes and a board write together, discharging A22's assignment. **The seam was corrected on the way**: §8.4 said the rule lands on `Hello`'s capability flags, but `Hello` is not a `Command`, so a flag there cannot gate a browser — §8.3 now names two seams and requires one policy. ⚠️ **Two rules the entry does not contain were added, both in the \"too permissive\" class no structural gate can catch**: the writable root is **derived server-side and never supplied** (one server hosts sessions in several repos, so a client-named root would let a browser in repo A write repo B's config), and **a write may not add an exec surface** — `[[extensions]]`' `cmd` and a hook's `run`/`post` are refused although the file is inside the allowlist, because §9.2 renders model-authored markdown on the authenticated origin and one transcript could otherwise become persistent command execution as the server's user. Plus `ETag`/`If-Match` with `412` (four writers reach one file) and a board route that `409`s outside a repository. **U99 closed**; **B10 is now half-applied** (comment-preserving writes normative at §5.1, its `dacode.schema.json` still owed); **one gap recorded rather than filled** — §3.3 has no task-mutation `Command`, so the browser path to `promote` does not exist in the protocol. **The sixth delta in a row whose named work was partly pre-done or partly wrong** |\n| ~~**A13**~~ | §2.1, §2.2 | **Two crates approved-with-dates rather than added**: `dacode-store` extracted at M3, `dacode-introspect` created at M5, with every crate vendored in-tree | 2026-09-18 | The cheapest delta in the set — no wire change, no fixture, no new type, **and no gate-file change** — and its whole discipline was *not* adding things: §2.1's tree still lists **eleven members**, because the banner's instruction was that neither crate may look like an M0 directory. Both are recorded as approved-with-dates with the reasons that make the dates principled rather than arbitrary: `dacode-store`'s traits live in `dacode-core::store` **from M1** so the boundary arrives when `SqliteStore` becomes the second implementor (a boundary with one implementation is indirection without payoff, and the trait risk is retired by a second implementor rather than by where the trait lives — extracting later is a *move*, not a redesign, precisely because M1 wrote against the trait); `dacode-introspect`'s **exchange-record format is already normative in §6** via A10's carve-out, so the crate arrives to read records written since M1. ⚠️ **A phantom-edge hazard was found and written into the spec rather than left to the reader**: `gates/boundary-edges.txt` is an **exact** diff against `cargo tree`, not a subset test, so §2.2's new rules name edges for crates that do not exist and state in the same breath that those edges **must not** be pinned until the directory does — adding one does not describe the graph, it breaks the gate, and it looks like diligence to someone reading both side by side. Verified unchanged at 29 lines with zero store/introspect edges. **Two of its own instructions were already stale**: Appendix D's graph already carried both crates, so what was actually wrong was its *legend* (it called them \"proposed\" and carried no milestone), and §2.2 said the rules are \"enforced by review; consider a workspace-lint\" when the pinned exact-diff gate **already exists** — the spec was calling its own enforcement mechanism aspirational. One coupling named rather than discovered at M3: A13's placements must agree with **A7's** scaling axis, still unapplied. **The fifth delta in a row whose named work was partly pre-done** |\n| ~~**A8**~~ | §9.1 | **The web stack as a deferral with a committed prerequisite**: the framework open but deadline-bound, the JSON-Schema export unconditional | 2026-09-18 | B2's split transcribed with the framework **deliberately not picked**, which is the whole discipline of this delta — §9.1 now says the framework is an open decision **due by M3** with **TS SPA (Solid)** as the *standing recommendation* and both reasons recorded (all three reference web UIs chose TS; the four hard parts of this UI have mature TS solutions and weak WASM ones), rather than a bare \"Decision pending\". A recommendation is not a decision, so §9.2 and §9.3 stay framework-neutral. **The JSON-Schema export became unconditional**, with the inversion stated in the spec: it was carried only inside the \"If TS:\" branch, so an **M1** obligation was conditional on an **M3** decision — and the export is what makes the TS branch *cheap*, so requiring the branch first would have made the decision harder rather than easier. ⚠️ **A live contradiction was resolved, and it was not a choice**: the old text had a `build.rs` emitting the export *inside `dacode-protocol`*, while §13.3 confines `schemars` to an offline generator (\"never in a shipped crate\") and §2.4 puts build-time codegen in `xtask`. So the generator is `xtask`'s, the protocol crate grows no `build.rs` and no `schemars` dependency, and `protocol.d.ts` is generated **in CI with a drift check that fails the build** — layered on the golden fixtures, which catch a change to the serialized shape while the drift check catches the generated types falling out of step with it, the failure a second language cannot otherwise see. **Doc 03's \"Optional … if the web UI is a TS SPA\" was a false claim rather than a stale marker**, corrected under hard rule 1 with its marker and depth untouched. **The fourth time a signed decision's M1 half was found sitting inside a conditional** (A25's wire half, A10's carve-out, A21's `§14.2` citations), which makes it a pattern to check for rather than a run of bad luck |\n| ~~**A22**~~ | §3.3, §4.4, §5.3, §7.1, §7.2, §8.2, §8.3, §8.4, §9.2, §14 | **Four capability seams closed**: hooks take an argv array with no shell, `AllowPattern` is bounded by the request it answers, sub-agent inheritance is an intersection, and the browser token stops leaking through `Referer` and stored content | 2026-09-18 | All four seams closed across **ten** sections rather than the six named, with **B7's confirmation of A23's `yolo` carve-out landing in the same pass** as this delta's banner required, so §4.4 was edited once rather than twice — discharging the debt A23's resolved-table row named as B7's. Items 1, 3 and 4 landed as individually confirmed, each with the *reason* it is a rule rather than a preference: templating survives **into argv elements** because deleting it would be safer and less useful while the array form keeps the convenience and removes the surface, and a hook wanting a shell must now write `[\"sh\",\"-c\",…]` so the dangerous case is visible in the config; inheritance is an **intersection** because §5.3's existing promise was true for modes and false for scopes; and item 4 is a **renderer** rule in §9.2 so it covers any surface rendering model output, not one view. ⚠️ **Item 2 was redirected at sign-off and then re-answered by the user**: the capability split stays **reserved** (`Hello` carries `answer_questions`/`resolve_permissions`, granted by default in v1, so tightening later is additive and not a wire break — B7's inert-stub pattern applied to a trust model) and §8.4 now states the trust model **explicitly** rather than leaving it implied by three separately-written sections composing badly, which was the actual defect. But the **`AllowPattern` subset bound IS applied**, reversing this entry's own orchestrator reading, on the argument that *permissive* describes the **user's** experience and the bound does not touch it — a user may still grant `**` from their own terminal, and what the bound constrains is a client acting on their behalf, which without it could widen permissions for a whole session from a single prompt. **The `yolo` carve-out was confirmed and then improved**: it stands (a mode meaning \"do not ask\" cannot start asking, and the detection is still recorded so the audit trail is complete), but `yolo` is also the mode where a leak is *most* likely and the one mode where an escalation-based warning would never fire, so the first detection in a session raises a **non-blocking notice** — once per session, because a notice on every occurrence in a mode chosen for not being interrupted would be the interruption. **Closes U61.** No `Event` variant, no fold change, no fixture, exactly as predicted; §14 gained a **Trust boundary** bullet with one test per seam |\n| ~~**A21**~~ | §14 → §14.1 Correctness / §14.2 Benchmarks and evaluation | **Measurement separated from correctness**: a deterministic CI tier over a committed log corpus, prompt-cache hit rate as a measured property, the publication rule normative, task-success deliberately out of CI, cross-harness comparison a non-goal | 2026-09-18 | The cheapest delta in the set, as its own entry predicted — no wire change, no fixture, no new type — and the only one that makes existing spec claims falsifiable. **It arrived late enough that the spec already cited `§14.2` twice**, both written by A4 (§3.2's `Usage` paragraph and §4.5's partial-compaction paragraph), so applying it is what made those resolve: they were **dangling forward references in the spec**, invisible to every gate, because the convention that forbids markdown links had also removed the only mechanism that checks an internal cross-reference — in a file carrying 290 of them. **That hole is now closed by a new standing gate**, `check_section_refs()` in `check-links.py`, bound to the same `NO_LINK_FILES` list for exactly that reason and **tested by injecting three dangling refs** (`§14.9`, `§99.3`, `§2.7`) to confirm it fails on those, ignores the two valid ones beside them, and passes on restore. **§14.1 is twelve bullets rather than the five \"existing text unchanged\" this delta assumed** (thirteen once A22 landed, fourteen once A7 added `Config writes and the registry hint`), because A3/A4/A5/A9/A15/A10 each added tests — the instruction was right in intent and wrong in fact, so it was followed as intent. **A twelfth bullet it does not propose was added**: `Clients`, discharging **U101**'s finding that §14 had no UI test tier at all and so A9's mouse and paste rules were normative with nothing that could fail on them; the two *properties* are fixed and the **harness deliberately unnamed**, because the TUI crate does not exist and §9.1's framework decision is open. All four inbound assignments discharged inside §14.2 — the compression **level** as a measured knob (A15), **`ExchangeRecord`** as what hit rate is computed *from* (A10, and the reason that record was an M1 deliverable), partial compaction gated on the number existing (U97), and B18's publication rule stated normatively **first** because everything below depends on it. ⚠️ **Its own `Current.` list was stale in three places** and was corrected rather than transcribed: the codec is no longer unnamed (A15/B12), §4.5's 80 % is no longer the default (A4), and §14 was eleven bullets not five; its \"B12 stops being a stalled vote\" knock-on is historical, since B12 resolved by *decision* and §14.2 tunes the level |\n| ~~**A9**~~ | §9.2, §10 | **One tree component across three sources**, **conditional TUI mouse**, **collapse-on-paste** — and with it M1's delta *list* is complete (⚠️ the milestone was not: A10's M1 carve-out, assigned to A5, was still missing and landed later the same day) | 2026-09-18 | Taken **out of queue order** and for a reason worth keeping: the application order puts A6 next, but A6 is §5's **M4** dependency while A9 was **M1's last**, and the queue and the milestone lists are different axes. Its own sequencing constraint held — A3 and A4 both landed earlier the same day. **§9.2's half was already normative**, landed by A19 on 2026-09-13 as \"one tree component, three sources\", so A9 *extends* it: what it contributes is §10's **matching** component (the TUI sidebar previously listed sessions/sub-agents and the task tree as two bullets and never named workflow steps) plus the two TUI rules. Each rule landed with its **reason** and not only its mechanism — a TUI that captures the mouse all the time takes scroll-back and text selection away from the user's own terminal, and a large bracketed paste can be misread as a burst of keystrokes — because a conditional rule with no stated reason reads as a convenience and gets \"simplified\" later. Both are recorded as **§10 rules, not shared client behaviour**, so §9.3 correctly grows neither. ⚠️ **One gap found and deliberately recorded rather than filled**: §9.2 promises three sources in one tree, but nothing on the wire correlates a declarative workflow *step* to the child session it spawned — `SubAgentSpawned` carries the child, the agent *name* and a task *description*. The engine knows (§4.2's `task` tool carries the parent task-item id) but does not say. The minimal fix is a wire change plus a `TaskId` identifier (still undefined — **U100**) plus a §4.3 scan-exclusion clause, since `TaskId` *is* tool-nameable via `todo`; that is three sections A9 does not name, for a correlator whose shape depends on §5.4's step model, **which A25's M5 half owns and has not designed**. Neither signed text requires it — both claim the sources are the same *shape*, and shape is a property of the node, not of the edge. **U101 opened**: §14 has no UI test tier at all, so both new client rules are normative with nothing that can fail on them; A21's §14 restructuring owns it |\n| ~~**A5**~~ | §4.3, §4.4, §6, §14 | **The capability model as data** (`ToolDialect`, `CompletionRequest`, `StreamChunk`), B5's in-band subset as normative rules, SSRF/egress `[net_policy]`, retry with jitter and abort-aware sleep | 2026-09-18 | Applied **as redirected**, which is the substance: native `gemini` is not in v1, and §6 states why that is the stronger position rather than leaving a reader to wonder whether it was forgotten — a provider needing something unusual is a **value on `ModelInfo`**, not a branch in §4.2. **A4 had already built most of the capability model** one day earlier (its four-rate cost rule needed `ModelInfo` to be a real struct, and put `supports_tools`/`supports_thinking`/`supports_cache_breakpoints` on it), so A5 extends rather than adds; the second delta in six whose named dependency was already discharged. `ToolDialect` landed as a **reserved slot** — B5's grammar is reachable only when `supports_tools` is false and v1 ships exactly one, so the value is *derived* and the point of the enum is that un-deferring the ten dialects adds variants rather than restructuring the engine (the B7/A22 inert-stub pattern applied to a capability model). **B5's instruction to add a numbered §16 decision was deliberately not followed**: §16 lists *open* decisions, B5 is resolved, and A19 set that precedent explicitly — so the deferral lives in §6's prose where a reader meets it. **`CompletionRequest`/`StreamChunk` were referenced by §6's trait and never defined**, and are now real types, with `StreamChunk` deliberately cursor-less since a chunk is not an envelope. The egress policy needed two sections A5 does not name: §4.4 gains an **IP layer under the hostname globs** (a glob matches the wrong thing, so it cannot close SSRF) and §4.3's `fetch` becomes the second of three egress paths through one policy. **U10 stays open** and §6 now records the exclusion |\n| ~~**A4**~~ | §3.2, §4.5, §4.8, §5.6, §6, §14 | **Prompt caching** as four engine invariants (fingerprinted prefix, append-only growth, longest-stable-prefix resync, volatility hygiene) + **compaction** opt-in with five rules + **B14's segment-index buffer** + `Usage`'s four counters | 2026-09-18 | The caching half landed as proposed and both compaction amendments as amended, so automatic compaction is **off by default** and §4.5 no longer implies it ships at M1. **Its own `Depends on` was already satisfied**: B6's canonical byte-stable schema has been normative in §13.3 since A16 (2026-09-12), five days *before* this was signed, with §13.4 independently ruling tool ordering out of the prefix — so A4 cites both rather than duplicating normative text in a second section. **Three items composed into a hole none of them stated**: amendment (a) defers compaction, A3 landed the hand-over as a *slot* not a mechanism, and U68's prescribed fix was \"compact and re-issue\" — leaving v1 with no automatic response to a filling context. Resolution forced by the signed amendments rather than a new choice: overflow is a **retryable provider error with two branches** (compact-and-re-issue when enabled; otherwise terminate through §4.2's guard set with an actionable remedy), and §4.5 now states plainly that **v1's default answer is \"stop and tell the user\"**. **`Usage` was referenced by two events and never defined** — the same defect A3 fixed twice the day before — and is now a real struct whose four counters are what closes **U71**: pricing a cached read at the input rate is a wrong cost, not an imprecise one. Partial compaction recorded as a **candidate** with both hazards named, not adopted before §14.2's number (**U97 open**). Discharged the other half of **A19's** cross-reference debt and applied **B14**, including its §5.6 consequence: the environment block sits outside the fingerprint. The optional method chain was deliberately **not** transcribed — this entry records it as future work and three of its five stages are the undesigned hand-over |\n| ~~**A3**~~ | §3.2, §3.6, §4.2, §4.8, §11, §14 | **Loop guards**: termination as a named, configured, reported guard *set* rather than one counter; streaming/execution overlap with the owning transcript fragment committed before side effects; the sacred tool-pairing invariant with typed synthetic results; steering as distinct from a hard cancel | 2026-09-18 | Six sections against the one named, because the guard set needed types the spec had been **using without defining**: `StopReason` (branched on as `EndTurn` in §4.2, and A3's own rule fires on a `length` stop that did not exist) and `IdleReason` (five variants in a trailing comment), plus a §4.2 pseudocode line calling `AgentIdle(NeedsInput, \"turn limit\")` — **a two-argument shape the one-field variant could not hold**. Both are real enums now, with `ToolOutput`/`SyntheticReason` beside them. **`AgentIdle` gained `guard: Option<GuardKind>`**, a wire change recorded rather than folded in: growing `IdleReason` instead would break §11's four exit codes and §9.2's five chips, and an untyped detail string would violate principle 3. `Option` omitted when `None` keeps an ordinary `Done` byte-identical. **The guard numbers are marked pending**, since §1's deferral names them as brainstorm-owned content; what is normative is the shape, so tuning a guard is a config change and adding one is never a wire change. **Discharged two debts owed by other deltas** — A19's (§4.8's gate is now `CompletionGate`, one guard among eight) and A2's (its dispatch ordering shares the pseudocode block). §3.6 gained the pre-side-effect fsync, §11 the explicit `IdleReason` mapping, §14 three tests. **U96 deliberately not closed** |\n| ~~**A2**~~ | §4.2, §4.3, §13.2, §14 | **Concurrency policy**: `Concurrency { Shared, Exclusive, Keyed(ConcurrencyKey) }` resolved per call from `args`, replacing `parallel_safe() -> bool`, with the author guidance the amendment made an obligation | 2026-09-18 | The amendment landed as signed — the tool computes its own key rather than returning a closure, so the key is a value that can be logged — and §4.3 now documents how an author chooses, why the key must be **canonical**, and that the `Exclusive` default is deliberately pessimistic (an unthought-through tool costs parallelism rather than causing a race). **No wire variant added**, so the \"loggable in the audit trail\" argument landed as a *property* rather than as a new field on `PermissionResolved`; if a concurrency decision needs its own durable event that is a new delta. **Per-tool assignments are marked pending**, not invented — §1's deferral names \"which tools ship\" as brainstorm-owned content, on A19's §4.8 precedent. **U27 closes on both halves**: with no bare `Json` left in the spec, §13.2's alias note was dropped rather than narrowed again. Touched two files outside the plan — `.agents/roles/_rust.md` and the `dacode` skill, plus four **generated** adapters — because a role file is loaded into every subagent's context, so a stale line there is an instruction being given to every worker. **Doc 08 needed more than a marker flip**: it specified `Concurrency::Dynamic(fn(&args) -> Key)`, the arm the amendment rejects, so a reader would have built the rejected design; corrected with the marker and depth untouched |\n| ~~**A15**~~ | §2.3, §3.6, §12, §13.1, §13.2, §13.5, §14 | **Selective compression**: a per-field `codec` discriminator, a config-driven allowlist over durable fields, a size floor and level, one `[storage] compress` knob, the searchability trade, and the codec named per *file* | 2026-09-18 | The mechanism landed **exactly as signed**; three enumerations did not survive later deltas, and each is recorded as a deviation rather than folded in silently. The codec moved **into `LogHeader`** (`plain | compressed`) because generalizing it into *config alone* would orphan every record already written — the log is append-only, so one file lives under one codec and the header says which; blobs are self-describing since they cannot inherit it. **`flat` left the enum**: it is a *record representation*, not a field encoding, and keeping both axes in one discriminator would make a format change expressible as a compression change. **`ToolOutputDelta`/`ThinkingDelta` are `Live`** under A1's rule — never written, so compressing them saves nothing — and were restated to `ToolResult.output` and `ThinkingEnded.text`. It also closed a contradiction it did not own: **§13.2 said the log is `flat` buffers**, which B21 overruled five days after A16 wrote it, and §2.3/§13.5 still called the codec an open choice after B12 settled it. §3.6 gained the rule that **the provider transcript is never compressed** (§13.4's byte-identical prefix), and §14 three tests. **U25 closed; U14 deliberately not discharged** |\n| ~~**A14**~~ | §3.1, §3.2, §3.3, §3.5, §3.7, §4.7, §4.3, §8.4, §12, §14 | **Session organization**: three durable membership events, `SessionInfo` as a real struct, `ProjectId`/`FolderId`, an `Org { op: OrgOp }` registry command, `org.json`, B13's Fix-1 carve-out and Fix-2 derivation | 2026-09-18 | The signed delta named two sections; **ten were edited**, because nothing in it carried what a folder *is* as opposed to which folder a session is in. Applied with **four scoping decisions, all the user's**: the registry is authoritative for **names** and membership stays a fold, so v1 accepts that a rename reaches only its issuer and §3.7's promise is **scoped to a session** with the freshness channel left to A7 (**U99**); registry mutation is a **`Command`**, since §8.1's default TUI has no socket, and §8.4's controller rule does not reach a session-less resource; **one op-carrying variant** on `TaskUpdated`'s argument, with **caller-allocated ids** because `Ack` has no payload slot; `org.json` atomic and non-rebuildable. Fix-2 landed normatively in **§4.7** rather than only in doc 06, because the spec may not cite a plan doc and §14 must be able to test it. **Falsified A23's prediction** that `ProjectId` would join the `args` scan — nothing injects one, so it could never fire — and annotated both A23 sites. **U16, U17 closed** |\n| ~~**A1**~~ | §3.1, §3.2, §3.5, §3.6, §3.7, §4.3, §4.8, §12, §13, §14 | **The two-tier event model**: `Event::tier()` over an `EventTier`, the rule *`Durable` iff the fold consumes it*, `seq: Option<Cursor>`, `TextEnded`/`ThinkingEnded` full-value boundaries, live deltas never persisted — **plus A25's M1 wire half** (`HostId`/`SessionRef`, `WriterEpoch`, `Snapshot.epoch`) and **§16 item 6's `Standing`** | 2026-09-18 | Applied with **six scoping decisions**, four of them the user's: `seq` *had* to become `Option`, because A18 made a missing `seq` the only gap signal and numbering deltas would make clients resync forever; the tier is a **rule** so A14's events and B17's `finding` classify themselves; only the **epoch** is wire-visible, the idempotency key and leaf CAS being store obligations; and A25(a) took a **third answer** that keeps A23's scan exact-matchable. **U66 folded in** (`LogHeader`), which reworded a cursor sentence A24 had landed the day before. **U27's §3.2 half discharged**, its §4.3 half left to A2. No fixture existed, so nothing was retrofitted |\n| ~~**A19**~~ | §4.8 (new), §3.2, §3.5, §4.2, §4.3, §5.4, §9.2/§10, §12, §14, §16 | **Built-in task tracking**: a fold-derived task tree, item kinds, a `verify:` mandatory at creation and typed by checkability, a hard-but-bounded completion gate, append-only context rendering, and `.dacode/plans/` finally specified | 2026-09-13 | Applied **self-contained**, not verbatim: three references in the proposal were dangling (§4.5 has no caching content until A4, no `Segment` until B14, §4.2 has no guard set until A3), so §4.8 states its own requirements — the A16 move. Its `Depends on: A1` claim was **wrong** and is corrected. §16 gained no B15 row (it lists *open* decisions) but did gain **§16.6**, the standing-goal gap found while applying. §5.4's `gate` → `approve`. One fixture, two engine tests |\n| ~~**A18**~~ | §3.7, §4.1, §14 | Bounded fan-out + drop-oldest, and the seq→gap→resync contract as one invariant, replacing §3.7's unqualified \"No gap, no duplicate\" | 2026-09-12 | §3.7/§4.1 applied verbatim from the paste-ready proposal. §14 extended beyond the literal proposal (fold-idempotence + a lagged-subscriber test) because an untested invariant is how §3.7 became false. No new variant, so no new fixture. Discharged A1's resync bullet and its B4 dependency |\n| ~~**A17**~~ | §2 | Dependency policy; §2 restructured as **§2.1 crate tree · §2.2 internal rules · §2.3 external policy** | 2026-09-12 | All four bullets landed. Only TLS is named in the spec as unavoidable; the avoidable consequences stay in Appendix F so unsigned choices are not enshrined. No link added — the spec carries zero markdown links by convention |\n| ~~**A16**~~ | §13 | Hybrid JSON strategy + `RawJson`; §13 rewritten as **§13.1–§13.5** | 2026-09-12 | Items 1–7 all landed. §13.2 defines what the spec's bare `Json` alias denotes, so §3.2/§4.3 stop contradicting §13 without pre-empting A1/A2 |\n| ~~**A11**~~ | §13 | Superseded by A16, as its own entry recommended | 2026-09-12 | Kept as the record of how the discrepancy was found (tracker finding `f1`) |\n\n~~**21 of 25 deltas are applied and the other 4 are approved and owed a transcription**~~\n~~**22 of 26 deltas are applied and the other 4 are approved and owed a transcription\n— none is unsigned.**~~ **23 of 27 deltas are applied and the other 4 are approved and\nowed a transcription — none is unsigned.** ⚠️ **The total grew without the owed count moving, which is new**:\n**A26** arrived *already applied* on 2026-09-19, and is the register's first entry not to\ncome from a B-decision — it came out of M1.1a's review of the three protocol types `bc52e50`\ndesigned rather than transcribed ([TODO](../TODO.md) §6 **U108**/**U109**), was approved\nin-session by the user, and landed the same day. **A27 followed on 2026-09-20 in the same\nshape** — out of M1.2 Pass C1's fold walk (**U111**), approved in-session, applied the same\nday — so the class that arrives already applied, from a board finding rather than a\nB-decision, now has two members, and both were free *because* they landed pre-fixture. Two of the four are *half*-applied and count in both columns:\n**A25** (M1 wire half landed inside A1, M5 behaviour half owed) and **A10** (its M1\nexchange-record carve-out landed 2026-09-18 in §6/§12/§14, its §17 body owed at M5).\nWhat remains owed is therefore **A6** (M4), **A10's §17 body** (M5), **A12** (last)\nand **A25's M5 half** — and since A7 landed 2026-09-19, *no* owed delta gates M1, M2\nor M3.** ~~**16 of 22 deltas remain unapplied.**~~ Both halves of that\nsentence were stale: the denominator predated A23 and A24, and the numerator hides\nthe distinction that now matters — an *approved* delta is work an agent can start,\nan *unsigned* one is not. ~~**A21 and A22 are both outside the sequence**, like\nA20.~~ **A21 was signed 2026-09-17**, ~~so A22 is the only one left outside the\nsequence;~~ **and A22 followed it the same day**, so the sequence no longer has an\nexception waiting on a vote. ~~A22 depends on nothing at all and is still cheapest\nnow, because every seam it closes is free before the code exists and awkward\nafterwards — though its item 2 was **redirected** rather than accepted, so what it\nowes §8.4 is an explicit trust model and a reserved capability field, not a\nrestriction.~~ ✅ **Both applied — A21 and then A22, on 2026-09-18.** A22's\n\"cheapest now\" argument turned out to be right in a stronger sense than written: the\nconfig schema its item 1 changes is still read by no code, so `run` became an argv\narray for the cost of one edit rather than a migration. **Its item 2 landed as\nredirected *and* as re-answered** — §8.4 does state the trust model explicitly and\n`Hello` does reserve the capability flags, but the `AllowPattern` subset bound was\n**redirected back in** on 2026-09-18, so §4.4 carries a restriction after all. Next in the\napplication order: ~~the protocol/storage group **A1** and **A14**~~ → **A1 applied\n2026-09-18**, ~~so the group's remainder is **A14** and **A15**~~ → **and A14\nfollowed it the same day**, so the group's remainder is **A15 alone**. A14 inherited\nexactly what A1's banner promised: the tier rule classified its three events\n`Durable` with no tier decision to take, and §14's derivation gave them *log*\nfixtures without a list to update. It also **grew well past its own proposal** — two\nsections became ten, because the signed text answered *which folder a session is in*\nand nothing in it carried *what a folder is* — which is the second time a delta's\nreal scope was only visible on application (A19 was the first, and it wrote §4.8\nself-contained for the same reason). ~~**A15 is held\nbehind [B12](B-decisions.md)**~~ → **the hold is gone entirely**: B12 resolved to\n`zstd` on 2026-09-17 as an accepted class-1 exception, and A15's mechanism was\napproved the same day. The reason to generalize the variant names anyway survives\nthe resolution — the discriminator carries the codec as a **serialized wire value**,\nso a name baked into it makes the *next* switch a §14 compatibility event rather than\na config change, and the proposal hardcodes `plain | zstd | flat | flat+zstd`.\n~~**A1 is now the last structural blocker on M1's golden\nfixtures**~~: A18 narrowed it to the tier marker and the `*Ended` variants, and\nboth of its decision dependencies are gone (B4 resolved, ~~B3 effectively resolved\nby A1 itself~~ **B3 signed 2026-09-17 outright**). ~~It must still land *before* any\nfixture is written.~~ ✅ **It did, 2026-09-18** — and the precondition held rather\nthan being waived: the crates are still stubs, so no fixture existed to retrofit.\n**M1's golden fixtures are now unblocked**, which is the last structural gate that\nwas ahead of them.\n\n**A19 landed out of order and left A1 one more thing to do.** It added\n`TaskUpdated` to §3.2's `Event` enum, so A1's tier marker now has one more variant\nto classify — durable, and §4.8 says so in prose in the meantime. That does not\nweaken the \"A1 before any fixture is written\" rule: no fixture exists yet (there\nis no code), and `TaskUpdated`'s will be written after A1 lands like every other.\nIt does mean A1 can no longer be applied by editing only the variants that\nexisted when it was drafted. ✅ **Done 2026-09-18, and the warning generalised\nbetter than expected**: `TaskUpdated` is classified `Durable`, and because the tier\nlanded as a **rule** rather than a list, the variant A19 added and the `finding`\nvariant B17 will add both classify themselves with no edit here. §4.8's prose claim\nis now a property of the enum. **U48 closes.**\n\n---\n","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.1","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.6","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.7","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.7","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.8","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§5.3","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§5.4","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§8.4","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A25","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U211","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"a turn is an ordered block list, and a child session closes with `Completed`","body":"\n**Origin, and why this entry exists in a register of proposals.** Not a B-decision. It came\nout of **M1.1a's review of the three protocol types `bc52e50` *designed* rather than\ntranscribed** — `CloseReason`, `Preview`, `TurnView`/`CallView` — which the previous\nsession's handover flagged as worth a second pair of eyes before M1.2 fixtures them, on the\nargument that no gate can tell whether a variant *set* is the right one. Two of the three\nchanged; `Preview` and `CallView` stood (the one refinement wanted for `Preview` was already\nin §3.2's own field comment). The findings are [TODO](../TODO.md) §6 **U108** and **U109**,\nboth approved in-session by the user and applied the same day — so **this entry records an\napplied edit rather than proposing one**, the first in this register to arrive that way.\nThat is worth saying out loud, because the alternative was a change to a `Durable` event's\nenum and to `Snapshot`'s shape with no register entry at all, and a reader auditing \"what\nchanged in §3.2/§3.5 after A25?\" would have found nothing.\n\n**1. `CloseReason` gained a fifth variant, `Completed` (§3.2) — a wire change to a `Durable`\nevent.** Each of the four named a producer: an explicit `CloseSession`, a writer fence, a\nparent that closed, a host that exited. **A sub-agent whose task finished was none of them**,\nand §5.3 returned the child's final message as the tool result and emitted\n`SubAgentFinished` on the parent without ever saying whether the child session closes, by\nwhom, or with what reason — so the alternative to a new variant was child sessions\naccumulating in a session list forever with no state marking them finished. §5.3 now carries\nboth the rule and the constraint that makes it safe: **`Completed` is child-specific**, since\na *primary* session that finishes emits `AgentIdle(Done)` and **stays open** for more input\n(§4.2), §11's exit codes map from `IdleReason` rather than `CloseReason`, and the fold renders\nwhatever it is given — so a leak would be visible only to a user reading a session list that\nsays a session is over while the engine still accepts messages. `Completed` and `ParentGone`\nstay separate variants because \"the work finished\" and \"the parent went away\" are different\nfacts to someone reading a session tree, and only one of them is a success.\n\n**2. `TurnView.text: String` + `thinking: Option<String>` became `blocks: Vec<TurnBlock>`\n(§3.5) — *not* a wire change, which is the part worth recording.** §6's `StreamChunk` is a\nflat sequence with no block index and no end-of-block chunk, so one completion can carry\ntext → tool call → text, and with interleaved thinking\n`Thinking → Text → ToolCall → Thinking → Text`. §4.2 makes each completion one turn, so those\nblocks share a `TurnId`. With one accumulator per kind, §3.2's boundary rule — the `*Ended`\nevent **replaces** the accumulated text — destroyed the first block, and since deltas are\n`Live` and never persisted the log could not recover it: **a loss in durable state rather\nthan a rendering defect**, and replay reproduced it. §3.2's rule is therefore reworded to\n***closes the open block, replacing the text the deltas accumulated***, and a new paragraph\nstates that a turn may carry several text and thinking blocks, with the mechanism. **A1's\nrule is narrowed in wording and unchanged in substance**: the self-heal property it exists\nfor survives verbatim under close-semantics, and §14.1's duplicated-delta test passes\nunmodified.\n\n**Two consequences were made spec rather than left as inference**, both placed where an\nimplementer of `fold` meets them: a delta arriving *after* a boundary **opens a new block**,\nwhich is why **no block index is needed on the four delta/boundary events** — arrival order\n*is* block order and the boundary is authoritative; and a *dropped* boundary is **not** a\nsilent merge but a `seq` discontinuity that §3.7 resyncs. `CallView`'s comment gained the\nthird: match a `ToolResult` to its call **by `CallId`, never by position**, because §4.2\ndispatches calls concurrently and results arrive in no order a block position predicts.\n\n**§14.1 gained one test for each**, and the first is the only thing that can fail on the fix:\nstream text → call → text inside one completion and assert both blocks survive, in order and\nwith the call between them. The two pre-existing two-tier tests cannot see it, because each\nuses a single-block turn — so a fold written against the old shape would have passed both.\nThe Engine bullet gained the paired lifecycle test: a primary session driven to\n`AgentIdle(Done)` emits **no** `SessionClosed`, while a sub-agent whose final message is\nreturned closes with `Completed`; paired because either half alone passes under the wrong\nrule.\n\n**Depends on.** Nothing. **Gates** [03](../part1-foundations/03-protocol.md) (which now\ncarries the fold's three block rules), [08](../part2-engine/08-agentic-loop.md) (the loop\nemits the boundaries that close blocks), [22](../part4-hosts-clients/22-tui.md) and\n[21](../part4-hosts-clients/21-web-ui.md) (both render a block list now, not a `text`\nfield).\n\n**Recommend accept.** ✅ **Accepted and applied 2026-09-19**, before M1.2 wrote a line of\nthe crate — and the user's question before approving it (*does this mean dropping work\nalready done, and restoring it later?*) was answered by counting rather than by reassurance:\n**neither**. Both types had been added two commits earlier and had never been compiled,\nfixtured, generated into `protocol.d.ts`, or rendered by a client; each appeared in the spec\nat exactly **two** lines, measured before editing. Both fixes are forward edits, `CallView`\nis unchanged and is *reused* as `TurnBlock::Call`, and six of `TurnView`'s eight fields\nsurvive. **The sequencing rule that falls out is now in doc 03 §3.7**: an `Event`/`Command`\nvariant is fixture-pinned from M1.2, while a folded type is route-pinned until M1.3 — so\nreview the first kind earlier, and a review of the second kind stays cheap right up until the\ncrate exists.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-19","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§14.1","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.2","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.5","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§5.3","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A26","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-19T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-19T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U212","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"four durable events with no fold destination, given one","body":"\n**Origin, and the method that found it.** Not a B-decision and not a type review: **the\nfirst fold walk** — M1.2 Pass C1 writing `fold` itself, variant by variant. The diagnostic\nwas A1's own tier rule run backwards: *an event is `Durable` iff the fold consumes it*, so a\n`Durable` variant the fold has nowhere to put is either a misclassification or a missing\ndestination, and every one found was the latter. That is a stronger form of the per-field\nwalk that produced U100/U107 — the field walk asks whether each type *resolves*; the fold\nwalk asks whether each event *lands*. It ran once, over one module, and found four. Filed as\n[TODO](../TODO.md) §6 **U111**, approved in-session by the user on the recommendations\nbelow, applied the same day — the register's second entry to arrive already applied, and\nagain **pre-fixture**, so the one wire change costs zero golden diff.\n\n**1. `TurnBlock::User { content: Vec<ContentBlock>, from: ClientId }` (§3.5).**\n`TurnStarted { role: User }` opens a user turn, but `UserMessage` — durable, and the *only*\ncarrier of the user's content blocks — had no destination: `TurnBlock` was\n`Text`/`Thinking`/`Call`. A client bootstrapping per §3.7's message-only form, or replaying\na log, would render **every conversation with the user's side missing**, and §9.2's\n\"optimistic rendering … reconciled by `UserMessage { from }`\" presupposed a fold that lands\nit. One block appended per `UserMessage`, fields copied verbatim — the fold copies, it does\nnot interpret — and a turn may hold several when steering interleaves (§4.2). `TurnView`'s\nbuild list gains `UserMessage`; it had been incomplete since the type was written.\n\n**2. `SettingsChanged` + `SessionCreated`'s two new fields (§3.2) — the one wire change.**\n`SessionInfo.model`/`.perm_mode` are rendered by §9.2's header and promised by §3.5 as \"all\nderived from the log\", yet no event ever carried them: `SessionCreated` had neither, and\n§3.3's `Set{Model,Agent,PermMode}` promise \"resulting events\" that did not exist.\n`SessionCreated` now seeds both (resolved from agent config at creation, §5.2 — never read\nfrom config at render, which would be hidden state), and one new `Durable` variant\n`SettingsChanged { model?, agent?, perm_mode? }` mirrors what the three commands change.\n**Event 30 → 31 (4 `Live`, 27 `Durable`)**; one variant rather than three because the\ncommands are one family and the fields say which fired. Doc 03 §3.4's counts and §3.7's\nfixture budget (80 → **82**) are re-measured, not incremented.\n\n**3. `SessionInfo.closed: Option<CloseReason>` (§3.5).** `SessionClosed` was durable with no\nfold effect, so no `Snapshot`-derived surface could say a session is over — while §8.2's\nlist annotation has always read \"(**status**, agent, cwd, cost, last_activity)\" and U109's\nown rationale was phrased in exactly those terms (\"a session list saying a session is over\nwhile the engine still accepts messages\"). The field is the mechanism that rationale\npresupposed; first close wins, a second close ignored — totality (G5) applied to a field.\n§8.2 gains the companion sentence that `status`/`cost`/`last_activity` are **route-derived\ndisplay values, not `SessionInfo` fields** — closing the annotation↔type mismatch without\ngrowing the type.\n\n**4. `Compacted` folds `context.tokens_used = after` (§4.5).** The event's `after` *is* the\npost-compaction token count and `Snapshot.context` *is* the meter, so the fold is a copy;\nwhat was missing was the sentence saying so. §4.5's \"UIs can render a divider\" is now\nexplicitly a **live-rendering** affordance: a replay-only client sees the meter drop and the\nturns intact, because the log lost nothing — the divider was never reconstructible from\n`Snapshot` and no longer claims to be.\n\n**Two fold rules stated, both because a second implementer is coming.** M1.3 generates\n`protocol.d.ts` and the web UI folds in TS, so conventions that live only in this crate's\ncode would be reimplemented from guesswork. **(a) Openness is a convention, not a flag**\n(§3.2): no event carries it and the log cannot store it, so the open block is *the turn's\ntrailing same-kind block* — exact rather than heuristic under §6's flat stream, because a\nsame-kind run ends only where another kind intervenes or the turn ends (tracker f124).\n**(b) \"No open block\" splits by turn existence** (§14.1): a boundary for an unknown turn is\nignored (totality), while a boundary inside a started turn with no deltas behind it\n**creates** its block — the half log-only replay depends on, and the correction of G5's own\n§14.1 transcription, which had listed it as an ignore case (tracker f126; both halves are\none test).\n\n**Three verified non-gaps, recorded so the next walk does not re-find them.**\n`SubAgentFinished` — the summary reaches the parent's transcript via the `task` tool's\n`ToolResult` (§5.3), and the child's usage lives in the child's own log. `TaskUpdated\n{ Promote }` — a repo-side write to `.dacode/plans/` (§4.8); the tree's *content* is\nM1.10's brainstorm. `Checkpoint` — genuinely unexposed (no §8.2 route, no `Snapshot`\nfield, and a `Rollback` UI needs the ids from somewhere), but checkpoints are doc 12/M2\nand the id's *contents* are f107's carve-out, so it is filed as **U112** with M2 as owner\nrather than invented inside a protocol pass.\n\n**Depends on.** Nothing; composes with A26 (the block list `User` joins), A1 (the tier rule\nthat diagnosed it), A14 (`SessionInfo`), A15 (`Compacted`'s shape). **Gates**\n[03](../part1-foundations/03-protocol.md) (counts, budget, fold rules — all updated in the\nsame pass), and at M1.3 the schema export and `protocol.d.ts`, which is why the sequencing\nrule mattered: applied pre-fixture, this bundle cost **zero** rework; after C2 it would\nhave cost two goldens and a fold rewrite; after M1.3, a published wire change.\n\n**Recommend accept.** ✅ **Accepted and applied 2026-09-20** on the user's in-session\napproval of all four recommendations, with the §14.1 additions (the user-turn replay test,\nthe creates-its-block positive, `SettingsChanged`/`SessionClosed` landing in `SessionInfo`)\nlanding in the same pass.\n\n---\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-09-20","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§14.1","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.2","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.5","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.5","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§8.2","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A27","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-09-20T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-20T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U213","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E9","kind":"task","title":"a denial carries a typed reason, and §4.4's pattern language is corrected","body":"\n**Origin.** Not a B-decision and not a fold walk: the **M1.1f doc pass** — the\nfield-by-field walk of §4.4 behind [16](../part3-capabilities/16-permissions.md),\nplus a five-arm dependency measurement and a survey of the same question in the five\nreference checkouts. It found four holes needing the user rather than an\nimplementer; the user ruled all four the same day (**g128** the matcher arm, **g129**\nthree of §4.4's own words, **g130** the `deny` floor, **U162** the wire shape), and\nthis delta is the spec half of those rulings. Applied in the same change as the\nprotocol code, on A27's precedent and because the two must not drift.\n\n**1. `DenyReason`, and `Deny { reason: Option<DenyReason> }` (§3.2, §3.3, §4.3) —\nU162's arm (a), refined.** §4.3 has required since A5 that *\"which of the two layers\nrefused is recorded on `PermissionResolved` … distinguishable in the audit trail and\nin the prompt shown to the user\"*, and the landed `Deny` was fieldless, so the\nrequirement was **not expressible on the wire**. Five arms: `Rule`, `Mode` and\n`Timeout` (four from §4.4 and one from §4.2's ask timeout), plus `NetHost` and\n`NetAddress` — §4.3's two egress layers, which is the requirement that forced the\nenum. Typed rather than a string on §3.2's own criterion and A3's `GuardKind`\nprecedent: a client branches on it (`NetHost` and `NetAddress` have different\nremedies) and a fold cannot match a rendering.\n\n⚠️ **The refinement is the part worth keeping, and it came from the pass that built\nit rather than from the ruling.** Arm (a) as recommended was `Deny { reason:\nDenyReason }`, non-optional — which puts a *diagnostic* on the **command** path:\nevery client answering `ResolvePermission` with a denial would have to name an\nengine taxonomy whose only correct value there is a constant. `Option` removes the\nburden without a sixth `Client` arm, because `None` **is** the answerer case,\ncorroborated by `PermissionResolved.by`. The reason travels with the decision rather\nthan sitting on the event for two measured reasons: `dacode-core`'s\n`PermissionResolution` carries a decision to its waiter **without** the event, and a\nsibling field on `PermissionResolved` would have spent lines `event.rs` does not have\n— it is at **998 of the ungated 1 000-line cap**, so that shape would have dragged a\nfile split into a Step 0. ⚠️ **A third reason was given for arm (a) and was measured\nfalse**: that a `Snapshot` fold could not see an event-side field. `fold.rs` handles\n`PermissionResolved` as `answered(snapshot, req)` — it **discards** the decision — and\n`Snapshot` retains no permission decisions at all. The arm survives on the two\nreasons that are true; the false one is recorded because it was the recommendation's\nown stated ground, and a deferral's reason is what the next pass acts on.\n\n**This is the register's first entry with a golden diff.** `{\"type\":\"Deny\"}` →\n`{\"type\":\"Deny\",\"data\":{}}`, because adjacent tagging emits `data` even when every\nfield inside it is omitted — so the answerer case is **not** wire-compatible with the\npre-change bytes, which no longer decode (`missing field data`). One string, pinned in\nboth directions. It lands before **M1.3** publishes the schema export and\n`protocol.d.ts`, which is §1's ordering constraint doing its job: the same reason\nB17's `finding` variant gates M1.3 is the reason this shape is cheap now and a\ncompatibility event later.\n\n**2. §4.4's three pattern *domains*, stated (g129 ii).** One glob vocabulary was\nwritten over three keys whose correct `*` semantics **contradict**: `edit` needs it to\nstop at `/` (or `src/*` allows `src/a/b.rs`), `bash` needs it to cross `/` (or\n`rm -rf *` fails to deny `rm -rf /tmp/x`), `net` needs it to stop at `.` (or\n`*.example.com` allows `evil.a.example.com`). Measured across five matcher arms:\n`glob` and `globset` expose **one** knob for all three, and **the strict setting is\nthe worse failure** — `literal_separator(true)` turns `rm -rf *` into a no-op. So the\nspec now names the domain per key, which is what makes [16](../part3-capabilities/16-permissions.md)\n§16.4's `Domain`-parameterised matcher (ruling **R-c**) a transcription rather than an\ninvention.\n\n**3. `.env` → `**/.env`, and the anchoring rule (g129 i).** §4.4's own example was\nwrong in every arm: patterns anchor to the **whole** subject, so `deny = [\".env\"]`\nmatches `.env` and **not** `config/.env` — uniformly across `glob`, `globset`,\n`wildmatch`, `regex` and a hand-rolled matcher. The corrected example is accompanied\nby the anchoring sentence, so the next reader does not re-derive it from a bypass.\n\n**4. A `deny` is a floor (g130).** §4.4 put session grants at the *top* of the\nresolution order and A22 bounded `AllowPattern` to a subset of its request — but that\nbound limits how **wide** a grant may be, not **which rung it overrides**, so a client\nacting on the user's behalf (which §8.4's trust model makes reachable) could lift an\noperator's `deny` for the rest of a session, and §14.1's \"a grant may not exceed its\nrequest\" test would pass on the escalation. One sentence closes it, plus the mode\nmapping the section had left implicit (`plan` turns `ask` into `deny` for\n`edit/bash/net`, `yolo` turns `ask` into `allow`, `auto-edit` likewise for `Edit`) and\nthe ruling that **`deny` is never mode-overridable, including under `yolo`** — a `deny`\nthat voids in the one mode where nothing passes a human voids exactly where it matters\nmost. openclaw's measured shape corroborates the floor (most-restrictive-wins,\n`minSecurity`/`maxAsk`); forge's and opencode's contradict it, both deriving a grant\n*wider* than the request that produced it.\n\n**Deliberately NOT in this delta.** **g128's** arm (hand-rolled, `Domain`-parameterised,\n**+0 packages**) is a plan-side choice with no spec text — adding no crate, it does not\nengage §2.3, and the user's added requirement (that the matcher stay adoptable by the\nstrict sandbox) is recorded on [U98](../TODO.md) rather than here, since its owner is\nthe row that fills B7's stub. **g129 (iii)** — `bash`'s advisory wording — is **B7's\nowed transcription, still owed**: U63 was acked with a measurement that *strengthens*\nit (four of five semantically identical `rm -rf` argv spellings evade a glob deny with\nno shell involved, so §7.1's argv-not-shell property does not save the allow-list), but\nthe §4.4 honesty sentence belongs to B7's A12-adjacent pass and is not folded in here.\n**R-a**, the concatenation direction §4.4 leaves unstated, is ruled plan-side in doc 16\nrather than spec'd: the approved edit was narrow, and the direction is enforceable by a\ntable test where the prose would be enforceable by nothing.\n","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"APPLIED 2026-10-01","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.2","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§3.3","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.3","note":null},{"kind":"spec","target":"§4.4","note":null},{"kind":"other","target":"A28","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-01T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-01T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U214","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"task","title":"B1a","body":"B1a (`dacode_json::RawJson`) is **done** — implemented, benchmarked, dual-reviewed,\ncommitted in the `dacode-json` repo.","status":"closed","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":"B1a (`dacode_json::RawJson`) is **done** — implemented, benchmarked, dual-reviewed,\ncommitted in the `dacode-json` repo.","note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"B1","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U215","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"finding","title":"U14 gates M1","body":"      `attention.py` shows it P0 / `review` / \"gates M1\", which reads as agent-actionable","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"U14","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U216","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"finding","title":"U78 gates M1","body":"      names all three under *Blocking M1*. A delta inherited its milestone from the","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"U78","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U217","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E8","kind":"finding","title":"U165 gates M1","body":"      it should be *declared*.** This item renders **P0 \"gates M1\"** and it gates no milestone. The cause is","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"U165","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:U218","repo":"dacode","epic":"dacode:E2","kind":"finding","title":"M1 gates M1","body":"      is normative~~, and no delta gates M1 any more~~.** ⚠️ **That last clause was wrong","status":"open","verify":"imported: the board's checkbox and discharge marker","evidence":null,"note":null,"references":[{"kind":"other","target":"M1","note":null}],"priority":null,"until":null,"created":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-10-06T00:00:00.000000000Z"}],"decisions":[{"id":"dacode:D1","repo":"dacode","title":"JSON strategy","status":"signed","options":[],"recommendation":null,"resolution":"hybrid adopted","confidence":"medium","references":[{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S4","note":null},{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S5","note":null}],"created":"2026-09-12T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-12T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:D2","repo":"dacode","title":"Web stack","status":"signed","options":[],"recommendation":null,"resolution":"the split — schema export a non-optional M1 item, framework due by M3","confidence":"medium","references":[{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S31","note":null},{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S32","note":null}],"created":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:D3","repo":"dacode","title":"Delta vs full message updates","status":"signed","options":[],"recommendation":null,"resolution":"pure deltas, full value at the durable `*Ended` boundary","confidence":"medium","references":[{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S24","note":null},{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S5","note":null}],"created":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:D4","repo":"dacode","title":"WS vs SSE for the live channel","status":"signed","options":[],"recommendation":null,"resolution":"keep WS","confidence":"medium","references":[{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S31","note":null},{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S5","note":null}],"created":"2026-09-12T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-12T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:D5","repo":"dacode","title":"In-band tool dialects in v1","status":"signed","options":[],"recommendation":null,"resolution":"the ~700 LoC subset ships, the dialect matrix deferred","confidence":"medium","references":[{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S11","note":null}],"created":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:D6","repo":"dacode","title":"Tool schema generation","status":"signed","options":[],"recommendation":null,"resolution":"offline codegen","confidence":"medium","references":[{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S25","note":null},{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S4","note":null}],"created":"2026-09-12T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-12T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:D7","repo":"dacode","title":"`bash` sandboxing and honesty","status":"signed","options":[],"recommendation":null,"resolution":"deny-list + advisory honesty; the seam ships as an inert stub","confidence":"medium","references":[{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S12","note":null},{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S25","note":null}],"created":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:D8","repo":"dacode","title":"Checkpoint strategy","status":"signed","options":[],"recommendation":null,"resolution":"shadow git kept with all six additions; workdirs via §5.4 workflow instructions","confidence":"medium","references":[{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S29","note":null}],"created":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:D9","repo":"dacode","title":"ACP commitment","status":"signed","options":[],"recommendation":null,"resolution":"build deferred to M5, the two M1 constraints committed","confidence":"medium","references":[{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S38","note":null}],"created":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:D10","repo":"dacode","title":"Config on disk","status":"signed","options":[],"recommendation":null,"resolution":"TOML confirmed, both consequences recorded","confidence":"medium","references":[{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S30","note":null}],"created":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:D11","repo":"dacode","title":"New crates `dacode-store` / `dacode-introspect`","status":"signed","options":[],"recommendation":null,"resolution":"store deferred to M3, introspect approved for M5 with its record format at M1","confidence":"medium","references":[{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S20","note":null},{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S37","note":null},{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S7","note":null}],"created":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:D12","repo":"dacode","title":"Session compression codec","status":"signed","options":[],"recommendation":null,"resolution":"`zstd`, accepted as a class-1 exception — APPLIED 2026-09-18 via A15","confidence":"medium","references":[{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S7","note":null}],"created":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:D13","repo":"dacode","title":"Session organization model","status":"signed","options":[],"recommendation":null,"resolution":"confirmed with both fixes; virtual folders promoted to a first-class requirement","confidence":"medium","references":[{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S8","note":null}],"created":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:D14","repo":"dacode","title":"Prompt/context buffer representation","status":"signed","options":[],"recommendation":null,"resolution":"appender + segment index, never re-parse","confidence":"medium","references":[{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S10","note":null},{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S30","note":null}],"created":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:D15","repo":"dacode","title":"Built-in task tracking & gating","status":"signed","options":[],"recommendation":null,"resolution":"fold-derived tree with an executable `verify:`","confidence":"medium","references":[{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S24","note":null},{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S25","note":null},{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S26","note":null},{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S35","note":null},{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S5","note":null}],"created":"2026-09-13T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-13T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:D16","repo":"dacode","title":"Repository tooling language","status":"signed","options":[],"recommendation":null,"resolution":"two classes, narrowed on sign-off","confidence":"medium","references":[{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S15","note":null},{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S3","note":null}],"created":"2026-09-16T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-16T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:D17","repo":"dacode","title":"The built-in `reviewer`","status":"signed","options":[],"recommendation":null,"resolution":"option (c), the split — usage defined by shipped workflows; the delta stays brainstorm-gated","confidence":"medium","references":[{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S25","note":null},{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S28","note":null},{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S30","note":null}],"created":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z","updated":"2026-09-17T00:00:00.000000000Z"},{"id":"dacode:D18","repo":"dacode","title":"Harness benchmarking","status":"signed","options":[],"recommendation":null,"resolution":"four tiers, three adopted; the publication rule normative","confidence":"medium","references":[{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S15","note":null},{"kind":"doc","target":"dacode:S16","note